The second level is the OT-vs-NT material. Matthew 25:34 speaks of a kingdom ''prepared… from the foundation of the world'' (apo kataboles kosmou), and John 14:2 has Jesus departing to ''prepare a place.'' These do not assert a creation date for the physical sky at all; they speak of an eschatological dwelling/kingdom in a metaphorical-spatial register. Apologists such as Gleason Archer resolve the whole cluster by distinguishing senses of ''heaven'': the atmospheric sky, the stellar heaven, and the abode of God — only the last of which the NT passages address. On the NT half this distinction is sound and the verses are not really in competition with Genesis; they are answering a different question. To that extent the contradiction is overstated and partially harmonizes on semantic grounds.

Where Archer''s framework over-reaches is in retrojecting the tripartite cosmology into Genesis itself. The ''third heaven'' language Paul uses in 2 Cor 12:2 reflects a Second Temple apocalyptic schema (developed in texts such as 2 Enoch and the Testament of Levi, which multiply the heavens to three, seven, or ten) and not the flat-earth-under-a-solid-dome model the Priestly writer actually held. Mark Smith and Jon Levenson document that P''s cosmos is a single-vaulted structure with waters above the raqia and the abyss below — there is no ''God''s throne room as uncreated heaven'' category in Genesis 1 waiting to absorb the harmonization. The Priestly cosmography is continuous with broader ancient Near Eastern models (compare the Enuma Elish, where Marduk splits Tiamat to form the sky-vault), as John Walton emphasizes. Reading the later three-heavens scheme back into Genesis to dissolve the day-one/day-two problem is therefore anachronistic.

Raymond Brown notes that John 14:2''s ''dwelling places'' (monai) are eschatological and almost certainly do not envision an ongoing post-ascension construction project; the language is reassurance, not cosmology. The net assessment: the NT verses do not contradict Genesis on when the sky was made — that part is a category confusion the harmonizers correctly identify — but the Genesis-internal repetition of shamayim at two different points remains a real artifact of how the Priestly cosmogony was composed. Verdict: partly overstated (NT vs. OT), but a genuine intra-Genesis tension rooted in P''s vocabulary.', 'Mark S. Smith, *The Priestly Vision of Genesis 1* (Fortress, 2010), pp. 71-79, on the raqia and Priestly cosmology, at Genesis 1:6-8
John H. Walton, *The Lost World of Genesis One* (IVP Academic, 2009), pp. 55-71, on ancient cosmic geography
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 59-60, for the harmonizing three-heavens response
Jon D. Levenson, *Creation and the Persistence of Evil* (Princeton University Press, 1994), pp. 3-13, on the Priestly cosmogony
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John* (AB 29A; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 620-621, on the eschatological ''mansions'' of John 14:2', 0, NULL, 1, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Genesis, Matthew, John', 2, 3, 3, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

The New Testament then attributes creation to Christ. John 1:3 says ''all things came into being through him (di'' autou), and without him not one thing came into being,'' and Colossians 1:16 — drawing on the personified-Wisdom traditions of Proverbs 8:22-31, Sirach 24, and Wisdom of Solomon 7:22-8:1 — declares ''in him all things were created… all things have been created through him and for him.'' James Dunn reads Colossians as Wisdom Christology: the church applied to Jesus the cosmological role Hellenistic Judaism had already assigned to personified Wisdom/Logos, a figure said to be ''with'' God at creation and the agent through whom the world was ordered. This was a move Larry Hurtado traces in the earliest devotional practice — the inclusion of Jesus in cultic worship — and Bart Ehrman frames as the progressive elevation of Jesus to divine status across the first century.

The strongest harmonization is not the popular Trinitarian appeal but Richard Bauckham''s ''divine identity'' thesis. Bauckham argues that Second Temple Judaism defined God''s uniqueness not primarily through abstract metaphysics but through two functional markers: sole sovereignty over all things and sole agency in creation. The NT writers, he contends, deliberately include Jesus within that unique identity — so attributing creation ''through'' Christ is not adding a second creator alongside God but specifying the mode of the one God''s single creative act. The internal NT engine for this is 1 Corinthians 8:6, a likely pre-Pauline confessional formula that carefully splits the prepositions: all things are ''from'' (ex) the Father and ''through'' (dia) the Lord Jesus Christ. Source (ex) and agency (dia) are distinguished, not divided between two creators, and the formula appears to be a Christological reworking of the Shema itself (''one God… one Lord''). On Bauckham''s reading the tension within the NT genuinely dissolves, and the popular Trinitarian harmonization (Father creating through Son by Spirit) is a later systematic articulation of the same first-century intuition rather than its source.

Where this leaves Isaiah 44:24 is the crux. The verse does not merely under-specify the agency; it actively denies that anyone was with YHWH — the Ketiv reading even poses it as a rhetorical question, ''who was with me?'', expecting the answer ''no one.'' Bauckham''s solution works only by reading the later Christological ''inclusion within the divine identity'' back into a text whose own logic is the exclusion of any second party. That is a coherent theological synthesis but a developmental one: the categories that make ''the Logos was within YHWH''s single act'' intelligible are not present in Deutero-Isaiah and emerge through the Wisdom traditions and their first-century Christological appropriation. So the NT is internally harmonizable, and the popular ''both did it'' framing of 1 Cor 8:6 is a misreading — it does not split the job between two beings but distinguishes source from agency within one creative act. But the diachronic move — from a text whose entire point is ''no one was with me'' to a confession that creation occurred through Christ — reflects theological evolution, not a doctrine latent in Genesis and Isaiah from the start. Verdict: harmonizable within the NT and partly overstated, but a genuine developmental tension with Isaiah''s sole-creator monotheism.', 'Larry W. Hurtado, *Lord Jesus Christ: Devotion to Jesus in Earliest Christianity* (Eerdmans, 2003), pp. 27-78, on early exaltation Christology
Bart D. Ehrman, *How Jesus Became God* (HarperOne, 2014), pp. 251-282
James D. G. Dunn, *Christology in the Making* (Eerdmans, 1996), pp. 187-196, on Colossians and Wisdom Christology
Richard Bauckham, *Jesus and the God of Israel* (Eerdmans, 2008), pp. 18-31, for the divine-identity harmonization
John J. Collins, *Introduction to the Hebrew Bible*, 3rd ed. (Fortress, 2018), pp. 388-393, on Deutero-Isaiah''s monotheism', 0, NULL, 1, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Genesis, Isaiah, John, 1 Corinthians, Colossians', 5, 3, 4, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The strongest harmonization, advanced by Gleason Archer and John Walton, makes two moves. First, genre: Job 38 is hymnic theophany, not a sequential creation account, and pressing its imagery for chronology mistakes its rhetorical purpose, which is to overwhelm Job with the inaccessibility of creation''s origins (''Where were you?''), not to date the stars. The whole speech is a barrage of unanswerable questions designed to expose the limits of human knowledge, and the founding-of-the-earth imagery (architectural language — foundations, measuring line, cornerstone, 38:4-6) is metaphor drawn from human building, not a cosmological timetable. Second, referent: the parallelism ''morning stars // sons of God'' strongly suggests that ''morning stars'' is a poetic designation for the angelic host rather than literal astronomical bodies. Marvin Pope''s Anchor Bible commentary notes that the pairing reflects the widespread ancient association of stars with divine beings (compare Isa 14:12, helel ben shachar, ''Day Star, son of Dawn,'' and the astral deities of Ugaritic myth, where the divine assembly is regularly imagined among the stars). On this reading the verse is not asserting that physical stars were manufactured before the earth; it is depicting the heavenly court rejoicing at creation, and the chronological clash with Genesis 1 largely dissolves into figurative language.

The versional evidence reinforces that both registers were heard in the line from antiquity. The Old Greek of Job 38:7 reads hote egenethe astra ainesan me phone megale pantes angeloi mou — ''when the stars came into being, all my angels praised me with a loud voice.'' The verb egenethe (aorist of ginomai, a third-person singular in the ordinary Greek concord by which a neuter-plural subject astra takes a singular verb) renders the first colon in astral terms, and on the lexical sense of ginomai (''come into being, be made'') it favors a literal/created reading of the stars rather than a purely figurative angelic one; it is the second colon, ''all my angels'' (pantes angeloi mou), that translates the Hebrew parallel ''sons of God'' and supplies the part that supports the angelic association. The LXX thus splits the difference: it keeps astral language in the first colon while making the angelic identity explicit in the second, illustrating that ancient translators already heard both registers in the verse rather than collapsing them into one. The point cuts in both directions — it weakens the apologetic claim that ''morning stars'' simply means angels, while confirming the broader ancient overlap of stars and divine beings.

This is a fair and largely persuasive harmonization, and it is why the status is partial rather than full contradiction. Two residual points keep the tension from fully evaporating. First, even granting the angelic reading, the passage still presents the heavenly beings (whether called stars or sons of God) as already in existence and active at the moment the earth is founded — which sits awkwardly with the Priestly insistence that the entire cosmos, heavenly bodies included, is created within the six-day frame, and with the later notion that the angelic host is itself a created order. Job nowhere narrates a creation of these beings; they are simply there. Second, the Genesis 1 placement of the luminaries on day four is itself a deliberate theological maneuver rather than a naive observation. Mark Smith argues that P intentionally demotes the sun, moon, and stars from the divine status they held throughout the ancient Near East (where they were astral deities), reducing them to functional timekeepers ''to separate day from night'' and ''for signs and seasons'' (Gen 1:14) — notably refusing even to name ''sun'' and ''moon,'' calling them only the ''greater'' and ''lesser light.'' That polemical demotion is precisely why they are introduced late, as manufactured objects. Job 38, working in an older and more comfortably mythopoeic idiom, still treats the astral/divine beings as primordial celebrants. Nahum Sarna''s JPS commentary likewise stresses that the day-four placement is a calculated literary and theological choice within P''s seven-day frame, not a chance ordering. So the two texts reflect different theological postures toward the heavenly bodies even if the genre argument neutralizes the surface chronology. Verdict: largely overstated/harmonizable on genre and the angelic referent, with a residual tension over the primordial status of the heavenly host.', 'Mark S. Smith, *The Priestly Vision of Genesis 1* (Fortress, 2010), pp. 83-85, on the demotion of luminaries
Nahum M. Sarna, *Genesis* (JPS Torah Commentary, 1989), pp. 8-9
John H. Walton, *Genesis 1 as Ancient Cosmology* (Eisenbrauns, 2011), pp. 104-119
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 60-61
Marvin H. Pope, *Job* (AB 15; Doubleday, 1965), pp. 293-294, on ''morning stars'' in Job 38:7', 0, NULL, 1, 'OT_internal', 'Genesis, Job', 2, 2, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The traditional harmonization, run by Archer and many conservative commentators, posits two phases: day one introduces a primordial, non-solar light — sometimes identified with the divine glory or an undifferentiated cosmic luminescence — and day four institutes the regular, mechanically governed alternation of day and night through the sun and moon. This reading has ancient pedigree. The notion of a primordial light distinct from the sun appears in rabbinic tradition (the or ganuz, the ''hidden light'' that b. Chagigah 12a says was created on day one and then stored away for the righteous in the world to come), and the difficulty of pre-solar light was felt across Second Temple literature (4 Ezra 6:38-41 dwells on the brightness commanded forth at the beginning of creation). As a piece of theology the primordial-light reading is coherent and old. As an account of what the text describes, it strains, because the verb and object are identical in both verses; the text does not say day four creates a new kind of light-cycle but that the luminaries perform the same dividing already reported in 1:4.

The more economical explanation, and the mainstream critical one, is literary-structural. Mark Smith, Gordon Wenham, and Claus Westermann all observe that Genesis 1 is organized as a deliberate two-panel framework: days one through three create the realms or domains (light/day, sky and sea, dry land with vegetation), and days four through six populate or govern those domains (luminaries to rule day and night, birds and fish, land animals and humans). Day four corresponds to day one precisely because the luminaries are the ''rulers'' (the verb m-sh-l, ''to rule,'' Gen 1:16, 18) installed in the light-domain established on day one. The repetition of the separating vocabulary is therefore not a claim about two distinct physical events on a single timeline but a literary signal of the day-1/day-4 correspondence. Wenham presents this framework as the key to the chapter''s architecture; Westermann reads the luminaries'' ''separating'' as their ongoing governing function rather than a redundant re-creation of the day-one division. The polemical thrust reinforces the reading: by delaying the sun and moon to day four and assigning them a merely functional, subordinate role, P strips the great astral deities of the surrounding cultures of their divinity — refusing even to name them, calling them only the ''greater'' and ''lesser light'' (Gen 1:16) — while still acknowledging the obvious fact that it is the sun that now governs the day.

It is worth adding that the framework reading does not require denying that P thought light somehow existed before the sun; the ancient cosmology that produced this text plainly did distinguish light as a created reality (1:3) from the luminaries as later furniture (1:14-19), and that distinction is itself a marker of the chapter''s pre-scientific astronomy rather than a covert two-phase apologetic. The day-one light is the cosmic condition that makes day and night possible; the day-four luminaries are the bodies installed to administer that already-instituted cycle. On the author''s own terms the repetition is functional, not contradictory.

So the assessment depends on the question being asked. If one reads Genesis 1 as the carefully patterned liturgical cosmology its author composed — with its sevenfold rhythm, its refrains (''and God saw that it was good''), and its panelled symmetry — the repeated b-d-l language is intelligible and not self-contradictory; the structure is the point, and the author very likely was not narrating sequential natural history at all. If one insists on reading it as a strict chronological report, then the text does separate light from darkness twice, once with no luminaries and once with them, and that is incoherent. The contradiction is thus real under a flat-literal reading and dissolves under the literary reading the text''s own design invites. Verdict: harmonizable as deliberate framework structure; a genuine contradiction only under a chronological-literalist construal the author probably did not intend.', 'Mark S. Smith, *The Priestly Vision of Genesis 1* (Fortress, 2010), pp. 87-92, on the domain/inhabitants parallel structure
Gordon J. Wenham, *Genesis 1-15* (WBC 1; Word, 1987), pp. 6-7
Claus Westermann, *Genesis 1-11: A Continental Commentary* (Fortress, 1984), pp. 112-114
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 60-61, for the two-phase harmonization', 0, NULL, 1, 'OT_internal', 'Genesis', 10, 2, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The principal harmonization comes in two forms. The first, found in Archer and Kenneth Mathews, treats ''out of the ground'' as a loose phrase for terrestrial origin that need not specify the moment or medium of creation, so that 2:19 simply reaffirms in general terms what 1:20-21 narrated in detail. The difficulty is that 2:19 uses yatsar, the very verb J uses two verses earlier for forming the man ''of the dust of the ground'' (Gen 2:7) — the parallel is deliberate and material, not vague. J is making a specific claim that the birds, like the animals and like Adam, were molded from the adamah; the verse is not gesturing at generic ''terrestrial origin'' but naming a fabrication medium.

The second and more consequential move is grammatical: the NIV and some conservative commentators render wayyitser as a pluperfect, ''Now the LORD God had formed,'' relocating the event back into Genesis 1 so that 2:19 merely recalls an already-completed creation. This is grammatically strained. The form is a standard waw-consecutive imperfect (wayyiqtol), the default Hebrew construction for foregrounded narrative sequence — ''and then he formed.'' Hebrew can express anteriority, but it normally does so with a fronted subject and a qatal (perfect) verb (the construction in, e.g., Gen 2:8''s ''the LORD God had planted'' is itself debated), not with a plain wayyiqtol in a chain of sequential actions. Pushing wayyitser to pluperfect is therefore a translation driven by the need to harmonize rather than by the syntax. Speiser, Westermann, and Wenham read it as straightforward sequence. The narrative logic confirms this: in J the formation of the animals is God''s response to the judgment ''it is not good that the man should be alone'' (2:18) — they are formed and paraded before the man as candidate helpers, and only when none proves suitable does God build the woman. If the animals had all been created days earlier, the experiment of forming them to relieve the man''s solitude becomes incoherent; the scene presupposes that the animals are being made then and there.

The contradiction is thus built into the redactional seam, not an artifact of translation. Two independent cosmogonies — one in which birds rise from the waters as part of an ordered cosmic filling, another in which they are molded from soil as part of a search for human companionship — were set side by side, and the difference in the birds'' origin is exactly the kind of doublet the Documentary Hypothesis was developed to explain. The harmonizations succeed only by overriding both the verb J chose and the plain sequence of the narrative. Verdict: genuine contradiction reflecting the P and J sources.', 'Richard Elliott Friedman, *The Bible with Sources Revealed* (HarperOne, 2003), pp. 33-37, on J vs. P in Genesis 1-2
James L. Kugel, *How to Read the Bible* (Free Press, 2007), pp. 50-57
John J. Collins, *Introduction to the Hebrew Bible*, 3rd ed. (Fortress, 2018), pp. 73-83
Joel S. Baden, *The Composition of the Pentateuch* (Yale University Press, 2012), pp. 193-206
Kenneth A. Mathews, *Genesis 1-11:26* (NAC 1A; B&H, 1996), pp. 214-216, for the pluperfect harmonization
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 68-69', 0, NULL, 1, 'OT_internal', 'Genesis', 10, 2, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The leading harmonization, developed by John Walton, is the functional-versus-material distinction: Genesis 1, Walton argues, is concerned with assigning function and ordering the cosmos for habitation, not with describing material manufacture, so its ''bringing forth'' need not specify a substance, leaving Genesis 2 free to supply the material detail (''from the ground'') without contradiction. Walton''s broader thesis — that ancient cosmogony was about function rather than material origins — is genuinely illuminating for Genesis 1 read on its own terms. But it does not neutralize this particular clash, for two reasons. First, Genesis 2:19 is not silent about material in a way that ''fills a gap'' left by Genesis 1; it makes an affirmative and specific material claim using yatsar, the same verb J uses in 2:7 for forming the man ''of the dust of the ground (afar min ha-adamah).'' The author is explicitly paralleling the animals'' formation to the man''s: both are molded from the same soil. That is a positive ontological statement about origin, not a complementary note about function. Second, Genesis 1 is not as functionally indifferent as the harmonization needs: it specifies that the waters and the earth themselves bring forth the creatures, which is a claim about source. Water-origin and ground-origin are not the same source, and saying one account is ''about function'' does not make them agree on where the animals came from.

Speiser and Wenham read 2:19 as straightforward narrative in which YHWH forms the animals from the ground, and the source-critical consensus — Friedman, Baden — assigns 1:20-25 to P and 2:19 to J. The contradiction is precisely the kind of doublet that motivated the Documentary Hypothesis: two creation traditions, preserved intact by a redactor unwilling to discard either, lying side by side with incompatible details. The redactor evidently valued conserving the received traditions over smoothing them into a single consistent sequence, which is why the seam at Genesis 2:4 produces not one creation story but two. The functional reading explains why Genesis 1 looks the way it does; it does not erase the fact that J says the beasts were molded from dirt while P says the earth and waters brought them forth. Verdict: genuine contradiction reflecting the P and J sources.', 'Richard Elliott Friedman, *The Bible with Sources Revealed* (HarperOne, 2003), pp. 33-37
Joel S. Baden, *The Composition of the Pentateuch* (Yale University Press, 2012), pp. 193-206
John H. Walton, *The Lost World of Genesis One* (IVP Academic, 2009), pp. 85-97, for the functional-ontology harmonization
E. A. Speiser, *Genesis* (AB 1; Doubleday, 1964), pp. 18-20
Gordon J. Wenham, *Genesis 1-15* (WBC 1; Word, 1987), pp. 55-56', 0, NULL, 1, 'OT_internal', 'Genesis', 10, 2, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard harmonization is the pluperfect translation, popularized by the NIV: ''the LORD God had formed'' the animals, relocating their creation to Genesis 1 so that 2:19 merely recalls a prior act. Archer and Mathews defend this. The grammar resists it. The Hebrew verb is wayyitser, a waw-consecutive imperfect (wayyiqtol), which is the workhorse form for sequential past narration — ''and he formed,'' the next event in the chain. Biblical Hebrew does have ways to mark anteriority, chiefly a disjunctive clause with a fronted subject and a qatal (perfect) verb (as in Gen 2:8, where the garden-planting is followed by the disjunctive note about the man whom God ''had formed''); Victor Hamilton, in his NICOT commentary, notes the possibility of a pluperfect nuance, but the construction in 2:19 is not the disjunctive-perfect pattern that normally signals it. It is a plain wayyiqtol embedded in a chain of wayyiqtol verbs (he said… he formed… he brought). Translating it as pluperfect is therefore a permissible-but-disfavored reading adopted for harmonistic reasons rather than one the syntax demands; Speiser and the critical commentators read it as simple sequence, and the major modern critical translations (NRSV, JPS) do not adopt the pluperfect.

The decisive objection is narrative logic rather than grammar. In J the animals are formed as God''s response to the man''s solitude — they are candidate companions, paraded before the man so he can name them and so God can see whether any is a fit ''helper corresponding to him'' (ezer kenegdo). The experiment fails (''but for the man there was not found a helper as his partner,'' 2:20), and that failure motivates the creation of the woman. If the animals had already been created in Genesis 1, days before, the entire scene collapses: there would be no reason to form them in response to the man''s need, and no sense in which their creation belongs to the search for a helper. The pluperfect reading salvages the surface chronology only by gutting the logic of the very story it is interpreting — it answers the chronological objection at the cost of rendering the narrative motivation unintelligible.

The broader pattern reinforces the source-critical reading. The two accounts diverge not only in order but in the divine name (Elohim in Genesis 1, YHWH Elohim in Genesis 2), in the cosmological starting point (a watery chaos to be separated in P; an arid waterless earth in J), and in the mode of creating humanity (a simultaneous, image-bearing dual creation versus a sequential crafting from dust and rib). These are the very criteria — vocabulary, theology, and narrative doublets — by which Wellhausen and his successors distinguished the sources. James Kugel puts the point bluntly: these are not two complementary angles on a single event but two distinct traditions placed side by side, each internally coherent and mutually incompatible on the order of creation. Friedman and Baden assign them to J and P, and the reversal of human/animal order is exactly the kind of contradiction the source theory predicts and explains in a single move, where the harmonization must strain both grammar and narrative sense. Verdict: genuine contradiction reflecting the J and P sources.', 'James L. Kugel, *How to Read the Bible* (Free Press, 2007), pp. 50-57
Richard Elliott Friedman, *The Bible with Sources Revealed* (HarperOne, 2003), pp. 33-37
Joel S. Baden, *The Composition of the Pentateuch* (Yale University Press, 2012), pp. 193-206
E. A. Speiser, *Genesis* (AB 1; Doubleday, 1964), pp. 15-20
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 68-69, for the pluperfect defense
Kenneth A. Mathews, *Genesis 1-11:26* (NAC 1A; B&H, 1996), pp. 214-216
Victor P. Hamilton, *The Book of Genesis 1-17* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 1990), commentary on Genesis 2:18-20', 0, NULL, 1, 'OT_internal', 'Genesis', 10, 2, 5, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Four explanations of the plurals compete. (1) A royal ''we'' — but Biblical Hebrew attests no such majestic plural for a single speaker, and this option is generally abandoned by Hebraists. (2) A plural of deliberation or self-address — grammatically defensible and favored by some, including conservative commentators, though it strains in 3:22''s ''like one of us,'' which compares the man to a plurality. (3) Address to the angelic/heavenly court — God announcing his intention before the assembled bnei elohim; this is the reading of many critical and some traditional scholars and fits the council scenes elsewhere (1 Kgs 22:19-22, where YHWH consults the host of heaven; Job 1-2, where the bnei elohim present themselves before YHWH). (4) Trinitarian anticipation — a Christian dogmatic reading that postdates the text by centuries and which even most conservative OT scholars decline to defend as the original sense. The serious exegetical contenders are (2) and (3).

The council reading draws strength from Psalm 82, which is explicit: ''God (elohim) has taken his place in the divine council (adat-el); in the midst of the gods (elohim) he holds judgment'' — and proceeds to sentence those gods to die ''like mortals'' (82:6-7), an oddity if they were merely human judges, since human judges already die. Apologists, including Archer and Daniel Block, argue that these elohim are human judges or, alternatively, angels, never genuine deities, preserving strict monotheism. The strongest piece of evidence against that reduction is Deuteronomy 32:8-9. The Masoretic Text reads that Elyon apportioned the nations ''according to the number of the sons of Israel (bnei yisrael),'' but the older reading is preserved in the Qumran scroll 4QDeut^j (4Q37), which reads bny ''lhym (bene elohim, ''sons of God''). The Greek tradition splits at exactly this point: the best-attested Old Greek reading is angelon theou (''angels of God''), but a significant variant reads hyion theou (υἱῶν θεοῦ, ''sons of God''), attested in pre-Christian Greek witnesses associated with the Fouad-266 group, and it is this latter reading — ''sons of God'' — that corresponds directly to the 4QDeut^j Hebrew bene elohim and is widely held to reflect the more original Vorlage. The form bene el (''sons of El'') is not the scroll''s actual text but the cognate divine-council phrase known from Ugaritic, which scholars adduce to illuminate the same conception. Both non-Masoretic forms point back to a divine-council Vorlage, with YHWH then receiving Israel as his allotted portion (32:9) — a tableau of a high god distributing the nations among subordinate deities, with YHWH as one of them receiving Israel. The MT ''sons of Israel'' is widely regarded as a later theological correction smoothing out the polytheistic implication.

The critical reconstruction, associated with Mark Smith and Patrick Miller, sees a trajectory from an early period in which YHWH presided over (or was situated within) a pantheon, through monolatry (worship of YHWH alone while other gods are conceded to exist, as in ''who is like you, O LORD, among the gods?'', Exod 15:11), to the full ontological monotheism of Deutero-Isaiah and later texts. On this view the canon preserves the seams of that development: the divine plurals and council scenes are fossils of the older cosmology, and Deutero-Isaiah''s polemic is the mature endpoint. Michael Heiser shares the council reading of the textual data — he too takes the other elohim to be real spiritual beings and Deut 32:8-9 as a genuine council text — but, unlike Smith and Collins, he rejects the evolutionary polytheism-to-monotheism trajectory; he reads the divine council within a synchronic Israelite ''monotheism'' of a distinctive kind, in which the lesser elohim are created beings who never rival YHWH''s unique ontological status. So Heiser and the critical scholars agree on the exegesis of the council passages while disagreeing on the developmental conclusion drawn from them.

The harmonizing readings are not impossible for any single verse — ''judges'' for Psalm 82 and ''deliberation'' for Genesis 1:26 can each be argued — but they must be applied selectively and against the grain of the Deut 32:8-9 textual evidence to keep every passage monotheistic. Because individual passages genuinely underdetermine the question while the textual and comparative data lean toward an earlier non-monotheistic stratum, the matter is best classed as disputed. Verdict: disputed, with the weight of textual and comparative evidence favoring a developmental account.', 'Mark S. Smith, *The Origins of Biblical Monotheism* (Oxford University Press, 2001), pp. 27-40, 135-148
Mark S. Smith, *The Early History of God*, 2nd ed. (Eerdmans, 2002), pp. 32-43
Michael S. Heiser, *The Unseen Realm* (Lexham, 2015), pp. 23-37, on the divine council
John J. Collins, *Introduction to the Hebrew Bible*, 3rd ed. (Fortress, 2018), pp. 112-115
Patrick D. Miller, *The Religion of Ancient Israel* (Westminster John Knox, 2000), pp. 1-45
