The decisive harmonization is Jacob Milgrom''s, and it is widely accepted in critical scholarship, not merely an apologetic device. Milgrom argues that the chatat is misnamed in English: functionally it is a purification offering whose object is the contamination of the sanctuary by ritual impurity, not the moral guilt of the offerer. In the Priestly worldview certain natural conditions — childbirth, menstruation, abnormal genital discharges, skin disease, contact with a corpse — generate tum''ah (ritual impurity) that, if unaddressed, pollutes the sacred precinct; the chatat decontaminates the sanctuary, not the conscience. On this analysis childbirth is not sinful in any moral sense; it is ritually defiling in the same way that contact with death is defiling, and the offering restores the woman''s access to the holy. Jonathan Klawans''s distinction between ritual impurity (natural, often unavoidable, not sinful) and moral impurity (generated by grave sin) sharpens the point: childbirth belongs squarely to the former category. Apologists such as Archer, and many evangelical commentators following Walton, adopt precisely this purity-versus-sin framework, and on the central question — is childbearing a sin? — they are correct, and the contradiction largely dissolves as an artifact of the ''sin offering'' translation.

Two features keep the resolution from being complete. First, the text uses kipper, ''make atonement,'' the same verb deployed across Leviticus for the expiation associated with wrongdoing (e.g., Lev 4-5); even if its object here is sanctuary pollution rather than personal guilt, the vocabulary blurs the ritual/moral line that the harmonization needs to keep sharp, and ancient and later readers (including some rabbinic and patristic interpreters) did sense a connection to sin. Second, the asymmetry of the purity periods resists a purely neutral ''ritual mechanics'' explanation: a mother of a son is impure seven days plus thirty-three of purification (forty total), but a mother of a daughter fourteen plus sixty-six (eighty total) — double. No purely physiological or neutral-ritual account fully explains why bearing a female child should double the impurity, and the asymmetry suggests the system encodes a gendered valuation of impurity that is not reducible to ''childbirth is simply defiling like corpse-contact.'' Kugel and others note that the Priestly source treats the bodily processes of reproduction as intrinsically defiling, which sits in a certain ideological tension — though not a flat logical contradiction — with the creation theology that commands reproduction as blessing. The honest assessment: the strong sense of the alleged contradiction (childbearing is a sin) is overstated and resolves on Milgrom''s reading; a weaker tension between the celebratory mandate to multiply and the ritual cost the Priestly system attaches to the act remains, and the kipper language and gendered asymmetry keep it from vanishing entirely. Verdict: largely overstated/harmonizable as a ritual-purity matter, with a residual ideological tension.', 'Jacob Milgrom, *Leviticus 1-16* (AB 3; Doubleday, 1991), pp. 253-258, 742-768, on chatat as purification offering
Jonathan Klawans, *Impurity and Sin in Ancient Judaism* (Oxford University Press, 2000), pp. 21-42
James L. Kugel, *How to Read the Bible* (Free Press, 2007), pp. 339-350
Tikva Frymer-Kensky, ''Pollution, Purification, and Purgation in Biblical Israel,'' in *The Word of the Lord Shall Go Forth: Essays in Honor of David Noel Freedman*, ed. C. L. Meyers and M. O''Connor (Eisenbrauns, 1983), pp. 399-414
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 124-125', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Genesis, Leviticus', 6, 3, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

What keeps a tension in view is source-critical rather than purely logical. The two statements belong to different creation accounts with different settings, audiences, and theological purposes, and they were not composed as a permission-plus-exception unit. In Genesis 1 the grant is universal and unconditional, spoken to humanity collectively — ''to them,'' male and female created together (1:27-29) — as part of the benediction that orders the cosmos; there is no garden, no test, no prohibition anywhere in the Priestly horizon. In Genesis 2 the prohibition is given privately to the man, formed first and alone, before the woman exists (2:16-17, 2:22), in the specific setting of the Eden garden and as the narrative hinge that sets up the transgression of chapter 3. The food grant of 1:29 and the prohibition of 2:17 thus operate in different literary worlds: one a cosmic blessing to humankind, the other a probationary command to an individual in a garden. Friedman assigns them to P and J; Westermann and Wenham treat the two creation pericopes as distinct compositions with distinct concerns.

The harmonization therefore succeeds only on the assumption that the final redactor intended 2:17 to function as a silent qualification of 1:29 — that the reader should carry the universal grant of chapter 1 into the garden of chapter 2 and subtract the one forbidden tree. That is a reasonable way to read the canonical text as a unified whole, and nothing in the logic forbids it; this is why the status is partial harmonization rather than contradiction. But it is a redactional harmonization imposed on two originally independent traditions, not a feature the texts themselves coordinate. The Priestly writer shows no awareness of a forbidden tree, and the Yahwist shows no awareness of a prior universal grant; the seam between them is real even though the resulting juxtaposition can be read coherently. Compared with the bird-origin or order-of-creation doublets, the friction here is mild — the statements can coexist — and the honest verdict is that the alleged contradiction is largely overstated: it dissolves under a unified reading and survives only as a marker of the underlying source division. Verdict: overstated as a contradiction; harmonizable as general-grant-plus-exception, with a genuine but minor source-critical seam.', 'Richard Elliott Friedman, *The Bible with Sources Revealed* (HarperOne, 2003), pp. 33-37
Claus Westermann, *Genesis 1-11: A Continental Commentary* (Fortress, 1984), pp. 220-222
Gordon J. Wenham, *Genesis 1-15* (WBC 1; Word, 1987), pp. 33-34, 62-63
Kenneth A. Mathews, *Genesis 1-11:26* (NAC 1A; B&H, 1996), pp. 211-213, for the harmonization
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 68-69', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Genesis', 10, 2, 3, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

Three framings sit on the table. Dispensationalists such as Charles Ryrie and Robert Saucy read a single progressive revelation across successive covenants — Edenic vegetarianism, the Noahic concession to meat, the Mosaic restriction marking Israel as a separated people, and Christian freedom under the New Covenant. This reading is internally coherent and answers why the rules differ by book. What it concedes, however, is exactly the skeptic''s point: the rules change. ''Eat only plants'' and ''eat anything that moves'' are not one instruction, and naming the shift a covenantal stage-transition labels it rather than dissolving it.

From a historical-critical angle the rules differ because the authors differ. Genesis 1:29 is Priestly (P), an idealized portrait of a peaceable original order in which no creature kills — a vision echoed in the eschatological peaceable kingdom of Isaiah 11. P then accounts for real-world meat-eating with the post-flood grant of Genesis 9:3, hedged immediately by the blood prohibition (9:4) — P softening its own ideal while introducing the first dietary limit. The kashrut of Leviticus 11 belongs to Priestly and Holiness Code material doing systematic purity work; Jacob Milgrom''s Anchor Bible commentary traces how the distinctions function within P''s holiness logic, tying the permitted/forbidden taxonomy to Israel''s vocation to ''be holy'' (Lev 11:44-45), and Mary Douglas read the clean/unclean taxonomy as a system policing categorical boundaries — animals that do not conform to their class''s defining locomotion or anatomy are anomalous and therefore impure. Deuteronomy 14 is a parallel but not identical list from the Deuteronomic tradition; its catalogue of named clean game animals (Deut 14:4-5) has no counterpart in Leviticus 11, a divergence that itself signals separate hands working the same body of food law.

The Markan declaration is, by near-universal critical consensus, a Markan gloss. Joel Marcus is explicit that the participial clause ''cleansing all foods'' (katharizon panta ta bromata) is the evangelist''s editorial aside, not a quotation of the historical Jesus; Marcus also observes that Matthew, working from Mark, lacks the clause at the parallel point (Matt 15:17-20). Davies and Allison''s ICC commentary on Matthew likewise treats Matthew''s reworking of the pericope as deliberately retaining a more Torah-positive frame. Critics widely note this omission, and it is plausibly — though not as a matter of consensus certainty — connected to a more Torah-conservative redactional tendency in Matthew, a Gospel elsewhere notable for retaining Jesus'' affirmation that not one stroke of the law will pass away (Matt 5:17-19). Jonathan Klawans sharpens the matter through the distinction between moral and ritual impurity: the dispute in Mark 7 concerns handwashing and the priority of moral defilement, not the abrogation of the kosher categories themselves. The historical Jesus argues that what comes out of a person defiles more than what goes in; the Markan community, writing decades later for a mixed Gentile audience, universalized the saying into a cancellation of food law.

Bart Ehrman''s narrative argument is forceful here. Had Jesus actually abolished kosher law during his ministry, the Acts narratives become difficult to read: Peter would not need a threefold rooftop vision (Acts 10) to be persuaded that no food is unclean — and would not protest ''I have never eaten anything profane or unclean'' (10:14) — and the Jerusalem council (Acts 15) would have no live dispute to resolve. The early church plainly did not remember Jesus having settled the food question; the very persistence of the controversy is evidence that the Markan gloss is a later interpretive development rather than a remembered ruling. This argument depends on taking Luke-Acts as preserving genuine memory of the early Jerusalem community''s practice, which is itself a reconstruction, but it is a well-grounded one: the food-law controversy is independently attested in Paul (Gal 2:11-14; Rom 14), confirming that the question remained open across the first Christian generation.

The harmonization works theologically for a reader already committed to progressive revelation; it partially resolves the canonical tension by supplying a coherent storyline in which each mandate belongs to its own covenantal epoch. The source-critical reading is more parsimonious, explaining the divergences — and the editorial seam in Mark 7:19 — without retrojecting a covenantal scaffold. The evidence points to multiple authors working out food law across centuries, not a single divine menu dispensed in stages. Verdict: harmonizable as theology, genuine diversity of sources as history.', 'Jacob Milgrom, *Leviticus 1-16* (AB 3; Doubleday, 1991), pp. 643-742, on the dietary laws and the holiness system.
Mary Douglas, *Purity and Danger* (Routledge, 1966), pp. 51-71, on the logic of clean and unclean.
