The Lotan''s-sister verses (Gen 36:22; 1 Chr 1:39) are sometimes folded in as a third, competing claim about Timna''s identity, but they do not contradict Genesis 36:12 at all. A single woman can be both the sister of Lotan within her Horite family of origin and, later, the concubine of Eliphaz within her marital household. These are two biographical data points about the same person seen from two genealogical vantage points — the Horite line of Seir and the Edomite line of Esau. Pulling them into the contradiction dilutes the case by conflating a genuine textual problem with a non-problem. The spotlight belongs on Genesis 36:12 versus 1 Chronicles 1:36.

Apologists meet the real contradiction chiefly with an elliptical reading. Archer and Keil-Delitzsch argue that the Chronicler''s ''and Timna and Amalek'' is compressed shorthand for ''and by Timna, Amalek'' — i.e., that Timna is named not as a son but as the mother through whom Amalek came, the Chronicler presupposing the reader''s knowledge of Genesis 36:12. Stated at its strongest, this is a plausible scribal economy: Chronicles routinely abbreviates its Vorlage, and a genealogist working from Genesis could telescope ''Timna bore Amalek to Eliphaz'' into the bare sequence ''Timna, Amalek.'' If granted, it harmonizes the two lists. The difficulty is that the harmonization must supply a preposition (''by/from'') that the Masoretic consonantal text does not contain, and must read a woman''s name embedded in a string of unambiguous male names as a covert maternity note rather than a list item.

The text-critical evidence cuts against the smooth reading. Sara Japhet observes that the Chronicler frequently condenses and occasionally garbles inherited genealogical material, and the Edomite list is a known trouble spot. Gary Knoppers documents that the Septuagint preserves variant readings in precisely this passage, with the Greek witnesses diverging over names and arrangement — a signature of transmission instability. Ralph Klein, in the Hermeneia volume, treats the Genesis 36 / 1 Chronicles 1 relationship as a case where the Chronicler''s source already differed from the received Genesis text, or where copying introduced corruption. The most economical conclusion is that the Chronicler worked from a form of the Edomite genealogy that handled Timna differently, or that the list suffered corruption in transmission — perhaps the haplographic loss or displacement of a relational term. Either way, as the two passages now stand in the MT, a woman appears as a son. The elliptical fix is possible but requires importing words and reinterpreting a name against the plain syntax. Verdict: genuine contradiction, most likely rooted in the Chronicler''s source or scribal corruption.', 'Sara Japhet, *I & II Chronicles* (OTL; WJK, 1993), pp. 58-60, on the Chronicler''s genealogical method.
Gary N. Knoppers, *1 Chronicles 1-9* (AB 12; Doubleday, 2003), pp. 275-280, on the Edomite list and LXX variants.
Ralph W. Klein, *1 Chronicles* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2006), pp. 63-72, on the Genesis 36 / 1 Chronicles 1 problem.
Nahum M. Sarna, *Genesis* (JPS Torah Commentary, 1989), pp. 250-252, on Genesis 36.
Roddy L. Braun, *1 Chronicles* (WBC 14; Word, 1986), pp. 23-26.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 214-215, for the elliptical harmonization.
C. F. Keil and F. Delitzsch, *Commentary on the Old Testament*, vol. 3 (Eerdmans, reprint 1982), pp. 46-47.', 0, NULL, 6, 'OT_internal', 'Genesis, 1 Chronicles', 3, 2, 1, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard harmonization distinguishes two kinds of divine work. Genesis 1-2 concerns creative labor — the bringing-into-being of the cosmos — which concluded when creation was ''finished'' (Gen 2:1). John 5 concerns ongoing providential and redemptive activity: the sustaining of the world, the giving and taking of life. D. A. Carson and Herman Ridderbos run this reading in their Johannine commentaries, and they are on firm historical ground, because Second Temple and rabbinic Judaism had already faced the problem. If God rested absolutely every Sabbath, who keeps the world running, grants births, and ordains deaths on the seventh day? Philo (De Cherubim 86-90) insists that God never ceases acting, since to act is proper to God''s nature, and that the ''rest'' of Genesis means the effortless ease of one who is never wearied. Genesis Rabbah preserves rabbinic discussion to similar effect — that God ''rested'' from creating new worlds but continues to govern this one — and the rabbinic reasoning that a man may carry within his own private domain on the Sabbath was extended to God, whose ''domain'' is the whole cosmos, so that his providential acts are not Sabbath ''work'' in the prohibited sense.

The Hebrew of Genesis 2 supports the distinction. The verb is shabat (''ceased, desisted'') from the specific melakhah (''work, occupation'') of creation; it asserts the cessation of the creative project, not divine inactivity in general. It is worth noting that the Pentateuch''s two retellings of the rest use a different verb: in the Sabbath commandments of Exodus 20:11 and 31:17 God nuah (''rested, was refreshed''), a more anthropomorphic term — Exod 31:17 even says he ''was refreshed'' (wayyinnafash). This is the more vulnerable formulation for the contradiction, since it implies recuperation; yet even there the object of the rest is the completed work of the six days, not a claim that God thereafter does nothing. The two verbs together frame Genesis'' point as the institution of the Sabbath rhythm and the completion of creation, not a doctrine of perpetual divine idleness.

On this basis the tension is genuinely reconcilable at the theological level, and it is almost certainly what the Fourth Gospel intends. The Johannine Jesus is not denying Genesis; he is invoking a live exegetical commonplace — that God works on the Sabbath in sustaining and life-giving ways — to license his Sabbath healing as participation in the Father''s own ceaseless activity.

What the harmonization does not erase is the rhetorical and Christological edge of John''s deployment. Critically, the point of John 5:17 is not a tidy systematic distinction between creative and providential work; it is the assimilation of the Son to the Father. That is precisely why John 5:18 reports that ''the Jews'' sought all the more to kill him — ''because he not only broke the Sabbath but also said that God was his own Father, making himself equal with God.'' The Evangelist uses the saying to blur the Father-Son boundary and to ground a high Christology, a theological program foreign to the Priestly author of Genesis 2, who was framing the etiology of the Sabbath institution, not anticipating debates about a divine Son''s prerogatives. Raymond Brown and C. K. Barrett both read the verse as a deliberate Johannine assertion of Jesus'' equality with God, and Craig Keener situates it within the contemporary Sabbath-work debates, noting that the form of Jesus'' argument — God''s Sabbath activity as warrant for his own — presupposes exactly the rabbinic discussion Philo and the midrashim attest. So the contradiction is reconciled at the level of doctrine — completed creation versus ongoing providence — while remaining a real instance of a later author reinterpreting an earlier text for new theological ends. Verdict: harmonizable theologically; a reinterpretive tension at the textual-historical level.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John* (AB 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 216-218, on John 5:17-18.
C. K. Barrett, *The Gospel According to St. John*, 2nd ed. (Westminster, 1978), pp. 255-258.
D. A. Carson, *The Gospel According to John* (PNTC; Eerdmans, 1991), pp. 246-249, for the creative-vs-providential distinction.
Herman N. Ridderbos, *The Gospel of John* (Eerdmans, 1997), pp. 192-195.
Philo, *De Cherubim* 86-90, on God''s ceaseless activity and the meaning of divine ''rest''.
*Genesis Rabbah* 11, on rabbinic discussion of divine activity on the Sabbath.
Craig S. Keener, *The Gospel of John*, vol. 1 (Baker, 2003), pp. 645-648.', 0, NULL, 2, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Genesis, John', 5, 3, 2, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

The differences are not cosmetic. The order is reversed — in Genesis 1 humans come last, after the animals; in Genesis 2 the man is formed first, then the animals, then the woman. The method differs — simultaneous male-and-female creation ''in the image of God'' versus a sequential, anthropomorphic crafting of one body from dust and another from a rib. The divine name differs — Elohim throughout Genesis 1, the compound YHWH Elohim throughout Genesis 2. The cosmological premise differs — Genesis 1 begins with a watery deep (tehom) that must be separated and bounded, while Genesis 2 begins with a dry, unwatered ground awaiting a mist or stream. And the register differs — Genesis 1''s stately, formulaic, repetitive liturgical cadence versus Genesis 2''s earthy, narrative, anthropomorphic storytelling in which God walks, plants, molds, and breathes. These are precisely the criteria by which Wellhausen and his successors separated P from J, and Friedman and Baden assign the two chapters to those two sources.

The dominant apologetic move treats Genesis 2 as a ''zoom-in'' on day six — an aerial photograph followed by a close-up — so the two are complementary, not contradictory. Archer, Mathews, Hamilton, and Walton all argue this. To rescue the creation order, Archer and the NIV translators render the verb in Genesis 2:19 as a pluperfect, ''the LORD God had formed'' the animals, making them already exist before the man. Stated fairly, this is a genuine grammatical option: Hebrew lacks a dedicated pluperfect tense, and the waw-consecutive imperfect (wayyiqtol) can occasionally bear an anterior sense in context. The trouble is twofold. First, wayyitser here is the standard narrative waw-consecutive that drives sequence forward, and reading it as anterior is contextually motivated by the harmonization rather than by the syntax — anteriority in Hebrew is normally signaled by a disjunctive clause with a fronted subject and a perfect verb, which is not the construction in 2:19; most grammarians (so Westermann, Speiser) take it as straightforward sequence. Second, and more decisively, the pluperfect does not save the logic of the J narrative even if granted: in Genesis 2 the animals are formed and brought to the man specifically so that he might find a helper among them, and only when none proves suitable is the woman made. One does not parade pre-existing animals before a lonely man to discover that they will not do; the narrative beat requires that the animal-forming be a fresh, purposive act within the search for a companion.

James Kugel and Joel Baden both stress that harmonizing the two requires overriding the texts'' own literary signals. Mark Smith''s study of the Priestly vision shows how thoroughly Genesis 1 is a self-contained theological composition with its own cosmology and its own conclusion in the Sabbath, not a summary awaiting Genesis 2''s detail. Claus Westermann and E. A. Speiser read the chapters as independent traditions, and the very fact that the second account opens with its own toledot-style heading and its own back-to-zero cosmology (''in the day that the LORD God made earth and heaven,'' with no rain, no plant, no man) confirms that it is not narrating the interior of day six but starting the story over from a different vantage. The parsimonious account, with Friedman and Baden, is that a later redactor placed two originally separate creation stories side by side and left the seams visible — most plainly at Genesis 2:4, where P''s colophon (''these are the generations of the heavens and the earth'') meets J''s incipit. The harmonization is theologically motivated and grammatically strained; the two-source reading explains every divergence — order, method, name, cosmology, and register — at once. Verdict: genuine compositional contradiction, two sources juxtaposed.

(Data note: this entry''s DB-level question text and anomalous answer IDs reflected a scrape/join error; the questionUrl, source page, and verse references all confirm the topic is the two Genesis creation accounts, and the question has been corrected accordingly. The scope overlaps id=7, which treats the order of humans relative to the animals, and id=17, which treats the male-and-female-versus-rib sub-tension; this entry addresses the meta-question of the two-accounts problem itself.)', 'Richard Elliott Friedman, *The Bible with Sources Revealed* (HarperOne, 2003), pp. 33-37, on P and J in Genesis 1-3.
