The harmonization here is sound on the lexical level, and its strongest defenders state it precisely. Blomberg (ZECNT James) and Carson read James 1:13 in the moral-solicitation sense: God is constitutionally incapable of being lured into evil, which is exactly why James insists temptation does not originate from God (1:14, the agent is one''s own ἐπιθυμία, desire). Deuteronomy 6:16, by contrast, recalls Massah (Exod 17:7), where Israel "tested" God by demanding proof — "Is the LORD among us or not?" To "tempt" God in that sense is to put him on trial, to demand he validate himself on command, which is precisely the second of Jesus'' wilderness temptations he refuses to enact. The two texts are therefore not denying and asserting the same proposition; one concerns God''s immunity to moral seduction, the other concerns Israel''s (and the tempter''s) presumption in demanding divine self-justification. Johnson (AB James) and Hartin (Sacra Pagina) confirm that James'' apeirastos belongs to a moral, not an experiential, register. On the narrow lexical charge, the harmonization succeeds.

The deeper tension survives the lexical fix, and it is worth stating carefully. James 1:13 is not merely denying that God can be seduced; in its diaspora-Jewish and early-Christian setting it leans toward a doctrine of divine impassibility — that God is not internally disturbed or moved by external stimuli. Yet the Hebrew Bible repeatedly depicts YHWH being "tested" (nāsâ) by Israel and reacting: Exodus 17:2, Numbers 14:22, and the recital of Psalm 78 (vv. 18, 41, 56) and 95:9 all describe Israel testing God, and in the narrative he responds with anger, grief, and altered plans. That is not solicitation-to-sin, but it is divine susceptibility to provocation, which sits awkwardly beside James'' absolute "untemptable" framing. Kugel (How to Read the Bible) stresses how thoroughly responsive the Hebrew God is — he regrets making humanity (Gen 6:6), relents from threatened judgment (Exod 32:14; Jonah 3:10), and changes his mind about Saul (1 Sam 15:11). Fretheim (The Suffering of God) builds an entire Old Testament theology on this responsiveness, arguing the impassible-God reading imports later philosophical categories. James 1:13 reflects a Hellenistic-Jewish and early-Christian theology — visible in Philo and later patristic appropriations of Greek divine perfection — that stands in real tension with the older Hebraic portrait of a God who can be moved.

Weighing it: the verse pairing the SAB chose is semantically blunt, because "tempt" in Deuteronomy 6:16 simply does not mean what "tempt" means in James 1:13, and any responsible exegesis separates the two. To that extent the contradiction is overstated. But the underlying question the pairing gestures toward — whether God is genuinely susceptible to being affected by his creatures — is a live one, and on it the canon does not speak with one voice. Verdict: lexically harmonizable, but the broader tension between divine impassibility and a responsive YHWH is real.', 'Luke Timothy Johnson, *The Letter of James* (AB 37A; Doubleday, 1995), on Jas 1:13-15.
Patrick J. Hartin, *James* (Sacra Pagina 14; Liturgical Press, 2003), on Jas 1:13-15.
Craig L. Blomberg, *James* (ZECNT; Zondervan, 2008), on Jas 1:13.
D. A. Carson, *How Long, O Lord?* (Baker, 1990).
Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; JPS, 1996), on Deut 6:16.
James L. Kugel, *How to Read the Bible* (Free Press, 2007).
Terence E. Fretheim, *The Suffering of God: An Old Testament Perspective* (Fortress Press, 1984).', 0, NULL, 2, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Deuteronomy, James', 5, 3, 3, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

The harmonizing case has two parts and a strong form. On Exodus vs. Numbers, Archer''s two-events reading takes the differing commands as exactly what one expects of two distinct occasions forty years apart: God commanded a strike the first time and a word the second, and Moses'' fault lay in repeating the older gesture when a new one was required. On Deuteronomy 33:8, Craigie (NICOT), Merrill (NAC), and Tigay (JPS) read the verse not as prescribing a divinatory water-method but as a poetic statement that the Levites — or Moses himself — were proved and vindicated at Massah and Meribah, with the Urim and Thummim naming Levi''s priestly oracular office rather than the mechanism of the miracle. That reading of 33:8 is sound; the verse is Levitical blessing, and nothing in it claims water was drawn by lot. The strongest form of the harmonization therefore concedes only two events with two commands and denies that Deuteronomy supplies a third method at all.

The critical reading presses the strike/speak difference into service as evidence rather than coincidence. Milgrom (JPS Numbers) argues explicitly that Numbers 20 is P''s reworking of the older JE Rephidim story, relocated to Kadesh and recast so that Moses'' striking — the action commanded and praised in Exodus 17 — becomes the disqualifying sin. Levine, Budd, Friedman, and Baden converge on the same doublet analysis. On this view the contradiction in method is the literary fingerprint of redaction: P needed an etiology for Moses'' exclusion from the land, and it generated one by changing the divine instruction from "strike" to "speak" so that Moses'' habitual gesture would now constitute disobedience. The near-identical plot — water shortage, rebellious congregation, rod, rock, the name Meribah — makes independent origination strained, and the poetry of Deuteronomy 33:8 again treats Massah/Meribah as a single proving rather than two.

Weighing it: the apologetic reading of Deuteronomy 33:8 is correct and the SAB''s implication of a third "Urim-and-Thummim method" is an overreading (the answer text''s "Umim and Thumim" is moreover a misspelling of Urim and Thummim). To that extent one of the three legs of the contradiction falls away. But the core strike-versus-speak divergence between Exodus 17 and Numbers 20 is not dissolved by the two-events defense; it is precisely what the source-critical reading explains more economically as a single tradition redeployed for a new theological purpose. The two readings remain in genuine dispute, with the redactional account having the better explanation for why the command changed exactly where the narrative needed Moses to fail. Verdict: disputed — Deut 33:8 is a non-issue, but the strike/speak conflict is real and best explained as P''s reframing of the older miracle.', 'Jacob Milgrom, *Numbers* (JPS Torah Commentary; JPS, 1990), on Num 20:7-13.
Baruch A. Levine, *Numbers 1-20* (AB 4A; Doubleday, 1993), on Num 20:1-13.
Philip J. Budd, *Numbers* (WBC 5; Word Books, 1984), on Num 20:1-13.
