The harmonizing tradition offers two main strategies, and in their strongest forms they are not frivolous. The first treats Reuel as a clan name or grandfather, Jethro as the personal name, and Hobab as a separate man — Moses'' brother-in-law rather than father-in-law. This leans on the genuine breadth of the Hebrew חֹתֵן (ḥōtēn), which derives from a root for marriage-alliance and can in principle denote a range of in-law relations, so that "Hobab…Moses'' father in law" in Numbers 10:29 might be parsed as referring to Reuel as the in-law and Hobab as the son. The second strategy holds that one man bore several names: Reuel ("friend of God") as a given name, Jethro ("his excellency," from a root for abundance/pre-eminence) as a priestly title or honorific, and Hobab as an additional name or epithet. Multiple naming is well attested in the world of the text — Abram/Abraham, Jacob/Israel, Gideon/Jerubbaal, Solomon/Jedidiah — so the phenomenon itself is unobjectionable. Archer, Kaiser, and Cole (TOTC Exodus) deploy these moves; the lexical observation about ḥōtēn is real and the multi-name parallels are genuine.

The source-critical reading accounts for the same data without the strain. Propp (AB Exodus) and Friedman (Who Wrote the Bible?; The Bible with Sources Revealed) assign the names to different documents: J uses Reuel (Exod 2:18), E uses Jethro (Exod 3:1; 4:18; ch. 18), and Numbers 10:29 — itself reckoned to J — gives "Hobab son of Reuel," while Judges 4:11, a separate (likely Deuteronomistic) tradition, simply calls Hobab the father-in-law. On this view the variation is exactly what one expects when independent traditions about a peripheral Midianite figure are stitched together: each source preserved its own name, and the redactor harmonized them only partially, leaving the seams visible. Baden (The Composition of the Pentateuch) treats the case as a textbook instance of distinct source-vocabulary.

Weighing the two: the multi-name defense is logically available and cannot be disproved, since people in this milieu did carry several names. But it pays in complexity. It must read ḥōtēn in Numbers 10:29 against the surface grammar (which calls Hobab, not Reuel, the father-in-law), it must coordinate Judges 4:11 (which also makes Hobab the father-in-law) with that reparsing, and it must explain why one man would be called by three different names in three different documentary contexts with no text ever noting the identification. The source-critical reading explains all of this at once: different traditions, different names, incomplete redaction. Neither is demonstrable, but parsimony favors the documentary account; the harmonization survives only by multiplying assumptions. Verdict: disputed — reconcilable by the multi-name/in-law route, but the distribution of names across sources is more economically explained as conflated traditions.', 'William H. C. Propp, *Exodus 1-18* (AB 2; Doubleday, 1999), on Exod 2:16-22; 3:1; 18:1.
Richard Elliott Friedman, *Who Wrote the Bible?* (HarperOne, 1987).
Richard Elliott Friedman, *The Bible with Sources Revealed* (HarperSanFrancisco, 2003), on Num 10:29.
Joel S. Baden, *The Composition of the Pentateuch* (Yale University Press, 2012).
Baruch A. Levine, *Numbers 1-20* (AB 4; Doubleday, 1993), on Num 10:29.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982).
R. Alan Cole, *Exodus* (TOTC; InterVarsity Press, 1973), on Exod 3:1.', 0, NULL, 6, 'OT_internal', 'Exodus, Numbers, Judges', 3, 2, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The harmonization is strong and has internal biblical warrant. Its best form, defended by Sarna (JPS Exodus), Propp (AB), and Archer, reads 20:4 in light of the clause that immediately follows it: "thou shalt not bow down thyself to them, nor serve them" (20:5). On this reading the prohibition is governed by cultic intent — the issue is images made as objects of worship, not iconography as such. The cherubim of the mercy seat and the curtains are theologically functional and decorative; no one bows to them, and the ark is precisely where God meets Moses rather than a worshipped object. The pattern recurs elsewhere: the bronze serpent of Numbers 21:8-9 is a divinely commanded image for healing, and when Israel later begins burning incense to it, Hezekiah destroys it (2 Kings 18:4) — confirming that the offense lay in veneration, not manufacture. Solomon''s temple likewise displayed cherubim, lions, oxen, and pomegranates (1 Kings 7) without censure. On the worship-versus-making distinction, the apologetic reading is correct, and the Exodus 20 / Exodus 25 pairing genuinely resolves.

What the worship-versus-making reading does not erase is an internal diversity within the Pentateuch itself. The Deuteronomic strand, especially Deuteronomy 4:16-18, reads less like a prohibition on cultic images and more like a near-total ban on fashioning likenesses of any creature, grounded in the claim that at Horeb Israel "saw no manner of similitude" (4:15). Halbertal and Margalit (Idolatry) and Moberly (Old Testament Theology) argue that the Hebrew Bible carries at least two distinct iconic theologies: a permissive priestly strand in which divinely sanctioned images (cherubim, the bronze serpent) are unproblematic, and a stricter Deuteronomic strand tending toward thoroughgoing aniconism. Second-Temple Judaism inherited the stricter reading and radicalized it — hence the largely aniconic Hasmonean coinage, the violent removal of the golden eagle Herod set over the temple gate (Josephus, War 1.648-655), and later rabbinic unease with figural art. That the rule was never absolute even within Judaism is shown by the third-century CE synagogue at Dura-Europos, whose walls are covered with biblical figural scenes.

Weighing it: the apologetic reading wins the specific contradiction the SAB poses, because the Second Commandment, read with its own following clause and against the bronze-serpent and Solomonic precedents, plainly targets worship rather than craftsmanship. The SAB''s framing — that God forbids and then commands images of heavenly beings — flattens that distinction. But the contradiction the SAB stumbles toward is real at a deeper level: the canon contains both a strand that comfortably commands cherubim and a strand that reads as a near-blanket prohibition on creaturely likenesses, and those strands are not fully harmonizable into a single consistent iconic policy. Verdict: partially harmonized — the worship/making distinction resolves Exod 20 vs. Exod 25, but the priestly and strict-Deuteronomic iconic theologies remain in genuine tension.', 'Nahum M. Sarna, *Exodus* (JPS Torah Commentary; JPS, 1991), on Exod 20:4.
William H. C. Propp, *Exodus 19-40* (AB 2A; Doubleday, 2006), on Exod 20:4 and 25:18-22.
Moshe Halbertal and Avishai Margalit, *Idolatry* (Harvard University Press, 1992).
R. W. L. Moberly, *Old Testament Theology* (Baker Academic, 2013).
Michael B. Hundley, *Gods in Dwellings* (SBL Press, 2013).
Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; JPS, 1996), on Deut 4:16-18.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982).', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Exodus, Deuteronomy', 6, 2, 3, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

Apologists offer three routes, and each deserves its strongest form. First, the Semitic-idiom move: the Greek miseo ("hate") in Luke 14:26 renders a Hebrew idiom of comparative preference, so "hate" means "love less." This has real support — Genesis 29:31 describes Leah as "hated" (senu''ah) where the parallel simply means less-loved than Rachel (29:30), and Deuteronomy 21:15-17 uses "loved" and "hated" wives as legal shorthand for preferred and less-preferred. Matthew''s parallel rewrites the saying as "He that loveth father or mother more than me is not worthy of me" (Matt 10:37), supplying a comparative gloss. Second, the life-stages move: the commandment binds the dependent child, whereas the discipleship sayings address adults choosing a vocation, so the two never govern the same situation. Third, the radicalizing move: Jesus does not abrogate the commandment but relativizes all earthly loyalties under the kingdom — and indeed in Mark 7:9-13 he attacks the corban practice precisely for using a religious vow to dodge the duty of supporting one''s parents, which shows him defending the fifth commandment, not overturning it.

The idiom argument is the strongest, and Matthew''s softening of Luke''s wording is telling evidence in its favor — but it cuts both ways. The fact that an evangelist felt the need to recast "hate" as "love less" shows that the early church already registered friction between the saying and the honor ethic; Davies and Allison (ICC Matthew) note the redactional softening explicitly. And "love less" does not dissolve the problem so much as relocate it: a commandment that enjoins honoring parents is in real tension with a teacher who makes loving them less than himself the price of discipleship. Fitzmyer (AB Luke) grants the idiom but stresses the saying''s deliberate shock value. The corban appeal in Mark 7 genuinely shows Jesus defending parental support and is the apologist''s best card; the John 2:4 "Woman" address is largely a non-issue, since the vocative gynai is not pejorative in Koine (Jesus uses it tenderly from the cross in John 19:26) and the same Gospel has him providing for his mother there.

Where the harmonizations break is Luke 9:59-60. Burial of one''s father ranked among the most sacred filial obligations in Second Temple Judaism — it overrode even priestly purity rules (Lev 21:2, which permits a priest to defile himself for a parent) and counted among the deeds of covenant loyalty (hesed) — and Jesus'' "let the dead bury their dead" overrides it outright. The idiom defense cannot help here, because refusing to let a man bury his father is not "loving him less"; it is suspending a primary duty of the fifth commandment for the sake of immediate discipleship. Nolland (WBC Luke) and Hengel (The Charismatic Leader and His Followers) both read this saying as a deliberate breach of ordinary filial piety, characteristic of the radical, eschatological demand of the kingdom; Hengel sets it against the backdrop of prophetic call narratives (Elijah permits Elisha to take leave of his parents in 1 Kings 19:20, whereas Jesus does not).

Weighing it: the "hate" saying and the Johannine address harmonize cleanly; the corban passage shows Jesus is not simply anti-family. But Luke 9:59-60 leaves a genuine residual collision with the honor commandment that the harmonizations soften rather than remove — and the Gospels'' own editorial fingerprints show the early church felt that collision too. Verdict: partially harmonized — the idiom resolves most of it, but the unburied-father saying preserves a real tension.', 'Brevard S. Childs, The Book of Exodus: A Critical, Theological Commentary (OTL; Westminster, 1974), pp. 417-421, on Exod 20:12.
Jeffrey H. Tigay, Deuteronomy (JPS Torah Commentary; JPS, 1996), pp. 69-70, on Deut 5:16.
