The harmonization is well grounded and widely held: Hosea 1 and 3 are prophetic sign-acts, not endorsements of sexual immorality. Stuart (WBC Hosea), Garrett (NAC), and Dearman (NICOT) all read the marriage to Gomer as a divinely commanded living parable of Israel''s covenant infidelity to YHWH. The genre is well attested. Isaiah walks naked and barefoot three years as a sign against Egypt (Isa 20:2-4); Ezekiel lies bound on his side 390 days and bakes bread over dung as a siege-enactment (Ezek 4); Jeremiah wears a wooden yoke (Jer 27) and buries a loincloth (Jer 13). Such acts are meaningful precisely as embodied prophecy, carrying rhetorical force that ordinary speech could not. Andersen and Freedman (AB Hosea) lay out the symbolic structure exhaustively, and on this reading Hosea is not himself committing adultery; he is marrying a woman of promiscuous character as a public, walking metaphor for YHWH''s bond with faithless Israel. The blanket prohibitions on porneia therefore stand untouched: a unique, divinely commissioned symbolic marriage is not a general license, and the SAB''s flat "fornication is OK when God commands it" badly misstates the genre.

What the sign-act reading does not fully dissolve is the divine-command problem. The command in 1:2 is not "marry a once-promiscuous woman now reformed"; it is to take a "wife of whoredoms" because "the land hath committed great whoredom." Hosea 3:1 commands love for a woman who is presently an adulteress, and 3:2 has the prophet acquiring her for fifteen pieces of silver and a quantity of barley — language of purchase that gives the transaction a morally untidy texture. Heschel (The Prophets) and Brueggemann (The Prophetic Imagination) both observe that the sign-acts characteristically implicate the prophet in conduct that stretches or contravenes ordinary ethical categories; that strain is part of their rhetorical power, not an accident. So even granting that this is not garden-variety fornication, the texts do depict God commanding behavior — marriage to and continued love of a sexually unfaithful partner — that his law elsewhere condemns, and doing so for a higher pedagogical purpose.

Weighing it: the apologetic reading correctly resolves the surface charge. The sign-act genre is real, the symbolic intent of Hosea is explicit, and nothing in the prophet''s marriage licenses general sexual license; the SAB framing is blunt to the point of distortion. But the residual point is genuine and is itself a theologically interesting concession: the harmonization works only by affirming that God may suspend or override the ordinary application of his own moral commands when a greater revelatory purpose is in view. That is a coherent position with deep roots (compare the binding of Isaac, Gen 22), but it is not nothing — it concedes that the divine command and the divine law are, in this instance, in real if explicable tension. Verdict: partially harmonized — the sign-act genre defeats the crude reading, but a real divine-command tension remains.', 'Francis I. Andersen and David Noel Freedman, *Hosea* (AB 24; Doubleday, 1980), on Hos 1:2-3 and 3:1-5.
J. Andrew Dearman, *The Book of Hosea* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 2010), on Hos 1:2.
Douglas Stuart, *Hosea-Jonah* (WBC 31; Word Books, 1987), on Hos 1 and 3.
Duane A. Garrett, *Hosea, Joel* (NAC 19A; Broadman & Holman, 1997), on Hos 1:2.
Abraham J. Heschel, *The Prophets* (Harper & Row, 1962).
Walter Brueggemann, *The Prophetic Imagination* (Fortress Press, 1978).', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Exodus, Deuteronomy, Hosea, Acts, 1 Corinthians, 2 Corinthians, Galatians, Ephesians, Colossians, 1 Thessalonians, Hebrews, Jude', 6, 2, 3, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The harmonization is therefore not a clever maneuver but simple lexicography, and the strongest apologetic statement of it is also the correct one. Archer notes the distinction; Fee (NICNT 1 Corinthians) and Thiselton (NIGTC) both render zēloûte in 14:39 as "earnestly desire" or "be zealous for," with no semantic overlap with the epithymeō of the Decalogue prohibition. Modern translations remove the appearance of contradiction by translating the words differently: the ESV and NIV give "earnestly desire" or "eagerly desire" for 1 Corinthians 14:39 while retaining "covet" for the Decalogue citations and "covetousness"/"greed" for the Ephesians and Colossians vice lists. There is no textual variant in play and no manuscript dispute; the underlying Greek is stable. The issue is purely one of seventeenth-century English vocabulary.

It is worth being precise about what is and is not contradictory. As a matter of the New Testament Greek, there is no tension at all: Paul commands zeal for prophecy and condemns illicit desire and greed, which are wholly distinct dispositions. As a matter of the KJV''s English, the same word "covet" does appear to be both commanded and forbidden — but that is an artifact of translation choice, not of anything the authors wrote. A reader working only from the King James and not checking the Greek will perceive a contradiction that the source text does not contain. This is the textbook profile of a translation contradiction: it appears in one English version, is invisible in the Greek and in modern renderings, and requires only minimal exegesis to dissolve. The SAB''s framing is King James-centric and would not register the problem in any contemporary translation. Verdict: harmonized — the text does not contradict itself; the seventeenth-century translation does.', 'Gordon D. Fee, *The First Epistle to the Corinthians* (NICNT, 2nd ed.; Eerdmans, 2014), on 1 Cor 14:39.
Anthony C. Thiselton, *The First Epistle to the Corinthians* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 2000), on 1 Cor 14:39.
Douglas J. Moo, *The Epistle to the Romans* (NICNT; Eerdmans, 1996), on Rom 7:7.
Robert Jewett, *Romans* (Hermeneia; Fortress Press, 2007), on Rom 7:7 and 13:9.
Harold W. Hoehner, *Ephesians: An Exegetical Commentary* (Baker Academic, 2002), on Eph 5:3.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982).', 0, NULL, 5, 'NT_internal', 'Romans, 1 Corinthians, Ephesians, Colossians', 6, 2, 2, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

The harmonization has solid Second-Temple texture and is the strongest part of the apologetic case. Exodus 19-20 nowhere explicitly denies angelic participation, and a well-developed Jewish tradition by Paul''s day populated Sinai with angels. The clearest evidence is the Septuagint of Deuteronomy 33:2, which in Rahlfs reads "at his right hand were angels with him" (ek dexiōn autou angeloi met'' autou) where the Masoretic Hebrew has the obscure ʾesh dat ("a fiery law," or possibly "fire was a law"); the LXX text-form here is itself variable across witnesses, but it plainly introduces angels absent from the plain MT. Jubilees has the Angel of the Presence mediate the Sinai revelation and dictate the tablets to Moses (Jub. 1:27-2:1). Longenecker (WBC), Moo (BECNT), Betz (Hermeneia), and Dunn (BNTC) all observe that Paul is invoking an established interpretive framework rather than inventing one. (Josephus, Antiquities 15.136, is sometimes cited here for Herod''s claim that the Jews learned their laws and the holiest of their ordinances "through angels" — the phrase is usually paraphrased rather than quoted, and the Greek angeloi in that passage is widely taken to mean human envoys or messengers, so its value as evidence for angelic mediation of the law is contested and considerably weaker than Jubilees or LXX Deut 33:2.) On this reading Galatians 3:19 is not denying Exodus but supplementing it from a tradition that filled in the heavenly retinue Exodus leaves implicit. That much is historically accurate and softens the apparent contradiction.

What the harmonization does not erase is that the plain sense of Exodus knows no angelic mediators of the law. In the narrative, YHWH speaks, the people tremble and beg not to hear the divine voice directly (20:18-19), and Moses alone becomes the mediator. The mediator in Exodus is Moses, not angels-plus-Moses; the angelic layer is a Second-Temple development, not an exegetical entailment of the Pentateuchal text. More important, Paul is not merely reporting the tradition — he is putting it to polemical work. Martyn (AB Galatians) argues that Paul deliberately minimizes the law''s divine directness: the more layers of mediation between God and the law (angels, then a human mediator), the lower the law''s ontological standing, and the more room for Christ as the superior and direct revelation. Galatians 3:20''s cryptic remark that "a mediator is not a mediator of one, but God is one" pushes in the same direction, subordinating the mediated law to the unmediated promise given to Abraham. Betz reads the verse as part of Paul''s argument that the law is provisional and inferior to the promise — a downgrade of Torah, not a neutral report.

The pliability of the tradition is itself telling. Acts 7:53 and Hebrews 2:2 invoke the same angelic mediation to different rhetorical ends — Stephen to magnify Israel''s guilt in disobeying a law so gloriously delivered, Hebrews to argue from lesser to greater (if the angel-mediated word was binding, how much more the word spoken through the Son). In none of these is angelic mediation treated as a diminishment except in Paul''s hands. That Paul alone bends it downward shows the angelology is theologically loaded, chosen because it serves his argument against requiring Torah-observance of Gentile converts. Hannah''s study of Michael and angel traditions documents how flexible and contested the Sinai-angelology remained in this period, and how readily it could be turned to opposite theological purposes.

Weighing it: the contradiction is not flat, because Exodus does not say "no angels were present" and Paul is drawing on a real interpretive tradition rather than fabricating one. To that extent the two can be held together. But they are not saying the same thing. Exodus portrays direct divine speech with Moses as the sole mediator; Galatians portrays a law ordained through angels and handed over by a mediator, deployed precisely to relativize that law. The difference is genuine, and it reflects developing Second-Temple theology and Paul''s particular polemical needs rather than a single unified account of Sinai.

Verdict: partially harmonized — reconcilable through the angelic-mediation tradition, but a real difference in portrayal, sharpened by Paul''s polemic, remains.', 'J. Louis Martyn, Galatians (AB 33A; Doubleday, 1997), pp. 354-359, on Gal 3:19-20.
James D. G. Dunn, The Epistle to the Galatians (BNTC; Hendrickson, 1993), pp. 189-191, on Gal 3:19.
Richard N. Longenecker, Galatians (WBC 41; Word Books, 1990), pp. 139-143, on Gal 3:19.
Douglas J. Moo, Galatians (BECNT; Baker Academic, 2013), pp. 235-239, on Gal 3:19.
Hans Dieter Betz, Galatians (Hermeneia; Fortress Press, 1979), pp. 168-170, on Gal 3:19-20.
William H. C. Propp, Exodus 19-40 (AB 2A; Doubleday, 2006), pp. 154-170, on Exod 19-20.
Darrell D. Hannah, Michael and Christ: Michael Traditions and Angel Christology in Early Christianity (Mohr Siebeck, 1999), on the angelic Sinai traditions.', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Exodus, Galatians', 7, 3, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The harmonization rests on a real lexical and legal distinction, and its strongest form is precise. The word in Leviticus 19:34 and Deuteronomy 10:19 is גֵּר (gēr), the resident alien — a foreigner dwelling within Israel under its authority and substantially incorporated into its covenantal and legal order, distinct from the נָכְרִי (nokrî), the transient foreigner. Biblical law treats the gēr as a protected class alongside the widow and orphan (Exod 22:21; 23:9; Deut 24:17-21). The populations marked for destruction in Deuteronomy 7:2 are not gērîm at all but the named Canaanite nations — Hittites, Girgashites, Amorites, Canaanites, Perizzites, Hivites, Jebusites — placed under the חֵרֶם (ḥērem), the ban of devoted destruction, on the stated theological rationale of preventing idolatrous contamination of the land (7:4). Copan (Is God a Moral Monster?), Merrill (NAC Deuteronomy), and Tigay (JPS) all press this point, and they are right: the love-command and the extermination-command do not address the same people, and the SAB''s flat "love them / kill them" framing collapses a distinction the text carefully maintains. To that extent the contradiction is overstated.

What the lexical distinction does not resolve is the moral tension the texts embed. The Canaanite ḥērem in Deuteronomy 7:2 and, still more starkly, 20:16-18 ("thou shalt save alive nothing that breatheth…thou shalt utterly destroy them") is not displacement or expulsion but the total killing of men, women, children, and livestock, justified theologically as a prophylactic against religious apostasy. Niditch (War in the Hebrew Bible) classifies this as a "ban ideology" that is internally coherent within Ancient Near Eastern warfare categories — the Mesha Stele records an analogous Moabite ḥērem of Israelite towns devoted to the god Chemosh — but morally toxic by any modern standard. Collins (Does the Bible Justify Violence?) argues that even granting the gēr / Canaanite distinction, the Deuteronomic command to annihilate whole populations has functioned across history as a biblical warrant for sacralized violence, repeatedly invoked by later readers. Brueggemann (Theology of the Old Testament) likewise treats the extermination texts as an unresolved problem the canon does not soften. The point is not that Leviticus 19:34 and Deuteronomy 7:2 contradict each other on the same referent — they do not — but that the same legal tradition holds together an ethic of love for the incorporated outsider and an ethic of devoted destruction for the designated outsider, and these are not reducible to a single coherent stance toward foreigners.

Weighing it: the apologetic reading wins the narrow lexical charge cleanly, and the SAB oversimplifies by treating two distinct Hebrew categories as one. But the harmonization purchases that resolution only by reframing the question; it does not dissolve the embedded tension between hospitality and extermination, which remains textually present and morally acute. Verdict: partially harmonized — the gēr / Canaanite distinction defeats the surface contradiction, but the deeper tension between welcome and ḥērem is real and unresolved.', 'John J. Collins, *Does the Bible Justify Violence?* (Fortress Press, 2004).
Susan Niditch, *War in the Hebrew Bible: A Study in the Ethics of Violence* (Oxford University Press, 1993).
Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; JPS, 1996), on Deut 7:2 and 10:19.
Jacob Milgrom, *Leviticus 17-22* (AB 3A; Doubleday, 2000), on Lev 19:33-34.
Richard D. Nelson, *Deuteronomy: A Commentary* (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 2002), on Deut 7:2.
Walter Brueggemann, *Theology of the Old Testament* (Fortress Press, 1997).
Paul Copan, *Is God a Moral Monster?* (Baker Books, 2011).', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Leviticus, Deuteronomy', 6, 2, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The core datum is that the underlying terms are inherently relational. Hebrew qadosh and Greek hagios both carry the primary sense of ''set apart,'' ''consecrated,'' ''belonging to the deity'' rather than denoting an intrinsic moral-metaphysical quality possessed in isolation. On this analysis holiness is not a zero-sum property: a thing or person becomes holy precisely by being set apart to YHWH, who is the source and standard of holiness. Jacob Milgrom''s work on the Holiness Code (Leviticus 17-26, the ''H'' source) demonstrated that P and H deliberately extend holiness outward in concentric circles — from the inner sanctuary, to the priests, to the whole people (''be holy, for I am holy,'' Lev 19:2), and even to the land. Holiness in this system is scalar and derivative, radiating from the divine center. Israel Knohl and Baruch Schwartz develop the same point: the Holiness School''s innovation was precisely to democratize sanctuary holiness to the laity. The command in Lev 19:2 is structurally mimetic — ''be holy because I am holy'' models human consecration on the divine pattern, much as imitatio Dei undergirds the whole ethical program of the chapter. 1 Peter 1:16 quotes Lev 19:2 directly, transposing this covenantal call onto the church; the citation formula (''it is written'') signals that the author is consciously extending the Levitical logic, not contradicting it.

Revelation 15:4 operates in an entirely different key. It is a doxology — the Song of Moses and of the Lamb — built from a mosaic of Septuagintal phrases echoing Psalm 86:9-10, Jeremiah 10:6-7, and Deuteronomy 32. The Greek of Rev 15:4 reads hoti monos hosios, ''because you alone are holy/devout,'' using hosios rather than hagios. The point should not be overstated: hosios connotes ''devout, pious, holy'' and overlaps semantically with holiness language in the LXX and NT, so the choice of word is not by itself a decisive lexical lever that dissolves the tension. What carries the argument is the genre. Liturgical absolutes of this kind — ''you alone are holy,'' ''you alone are good,'' ''you alone are wise'' (cf. Rom 16:27; 1 Tim 6:15-16) — are the standard idiom of biblical praise. They assert God''s incomparability and unique transcendence, not a metaphysical census of who may bear the adjective. Mark 10:18, ''none is good but God alone,'' uses precisely the same exclusive construction without anyone concluding that Scripture forbids calling people good. The ''alone'' in a doxology functions to magnify, not to legislate the distribution of a property.

The harmonization here is therefore not a strained apologetic dodge but the natural reading of both texts. Gleason Archer and Andreas Köstenberger frame it as a distinction between God''s unique ontological holiness and creaturely derivative holiness, and on the generic and lexical evidence they are correct; Grant Osborne''s commentary on Revelation reads 15:4 as Septuagintal doxology celebrating divine incomparability. The Skeptic''s Annotated Bible framing produces the contradiction only by atomizing both verses from their settings: flattening a hymn of incomparability into a propositional denial, and flattening a covenantal vocation into a competing ontological claim. Once the doxological genre of Revelation 15:4 and the scalar, participatory grammar of biblical holiness are recognized, the appearance of conflict dissolves. The Bible does say God alone is holy in the sense of being holiness''s source and supreme exemplar; it also says creatures become holy by consecration to him. These are not rival answers to one question but two coordinated theological claims. Verdict: overstated — a weak contradiction that rests on reading a hymn and a law code as flat, competing propositions.', 'Jacob Milgrom, *Leviticus 17-22* (Anchor Bible 3A; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 1397-1400, on Lev 19:2 and the democratization of holiness.
Israel Knohl, *The Sanctuary of Silence: The Priestly Torah and the Holiness School* (Fortress, 1995), pp. 180-186.
Baruch J. Schwartz, "Israel''s Holiness: The Torah Traditions," in *Purity and Holiness*, ed. Poorthuis and Schwartz (Brill, 2000), pp. 47-59.
Grant R. Osborne, *Revelation* (BECNT; Baker Academic, 2002), pp. 564-566, on Rev 15:4.
Andreas J. Köstenberger, *A Theology of John''s Gospel and Letters* (Zondervan, 2009).
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982).', 0, NULL, 2, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Exodus, Leviticus, 1 Peter, Revelation', 5, 3, 2, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

The strongest harmonization turns on genre and illocutionary force. Exodus 23 is casuistic law — a binding prescription for the Israelite''s own conduct in a concrete property dispute. Psalm 35 is an individual lament in the imprecatory subgenre, in which a sufferer under unjust attack appeals to God for vindication. Erich Zenger''s study of the psalms of divine wrath argues that this is theologically significant rather than embarrassing: the imprecation hands vengeance over to YHWH precisely so the petitioner does not take it himself, consonant with the principle ''vengeance is mine, says the LORD'' (Deut 32:35, echoed in Rom 12:19). David Firth''s monograph on surrendering retribution makes the same structural point: the psalmist relinquishes personal violence by transferring the matter to the divine court. On this reading the two texts address different acts — what the Israelite does with his hands versus what he asks God to do — and so are not strict legal contradictions. That distinction is real and it does substantial work.

But it harmonizes only partially, for several reasons. First, the imprecatory tradition is broad and severe — Psalms 58, 69, 109, and 137 ask God to break teeth, blot names from the book of life, orphan children, and dash infants against the rocks. The New Testament does not quarantine these as sub-Christian: Paul cites the imprecation of Psalm 69:22-23 with apparent approval in Romans 11:9-10, and Revelation''s martyrs cry ''how long?'' for vindication (Rev 6:10). The canon treats imprecation as legitimate scriptural speech, which is harder to reconcile with the benevolence ethic than the simple genre-distinction suggests. Second, the Pentateuch itself is not univocal on enemies. Exodus 23:4-5 enjoins kindness toward a personal enemy-neighbor in a restitution setting, and Leviticus 19:18, 34 extend love to the neighbor and even the resident alien — but Deuteronomy 23:3-6 permanently excludes Ammonites and Moabites from the assembly and forbids ever seeking their welfare, while the herem texts mandate extermination. The Torah''s enemy-ethic is a spectrum, not a single rule.

Third, the reception history is telling. Jesus'' antithesis in Matthew 5:43-48 — ''you have heard it said, hate your enemy ... but I say, love your enemies'' — presents itself as a corrective intensification, not a neutral summary of a unified Old Testament teaching. Craig Keener notes that the cited maxim (''hate your enemy'') is not a direct Torah quotation but reflects a current interpretive trajectory that the imprecatory and exclusionary texts could plausibly support; Jesus'' formulation gains rhetorical force precisely because the prior tradition was mixed. The honest conclusion is that the contradiction is softer than a flat reading implies, because law and lament differ in what they enjoin, but it is not dissolved: the canon preserves both an ethic of concrete enemy-kindness and a robust, scripturally sanctioned tradition of cursing one''s enemies. Verdict: partially harmonizable by genre, but the imprecatory tradition''s canonical authority leaves a real ethical tension.', 'Erich Zenger, *A God of Vengeance? Understanding the Psalms of Divine Wrath* (Westminster John Knox, 1996), pp. 13-22, 63-79.
David G. Firth, *Surrendering Retribution in the Psalms: Responses to Violence in the Individual Complaints* (Paternoster, 2005).
Hans-Joachim Kraus, *Psalms 1-59: A Continental Commentary* (Fortress, 1988), pp. 391-396, on Ps 35.
John Goldingay, *Psalms, Volume 1: Psalms 1-41* (BCOTWP; Baker Academic, 2006), pp. 491-502, on Ps 35.
Craig S. Keener, *The Gospel of Matthew: A Socio-Rhetorical Commentary* (Eerdmans, 2009), pp. 197-205, on Matt 5:43-48.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982).', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Exodus, Psalms', 6, 2, 3, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The most serious harmonization, advanced by Copan, Flannagan, and Kitchen, is layered. First, the herem ("ban") commands are ancient Near Eastern war rhetoric — hyperbole not meant as literal demography. Second, the drive-out texts describe the normal means, with killing reserved for those who resisted. Third, Ezekiel 47 is eschatological restoration, not Mosaic law. Assembled, this is meant to yield a single escalating program with stylized exaggeration in the middle.

Each plank is vulnerable. The hyperbole defense collides with Deuteronomy 20:16-17''s explicit limiting clause: in the cities of the land "you shall save alive nothing that breathes." That is not a rhetorical flourish but a legal specification, set in deliberate contrast to the terms-of-peace rules for distant cities (20:10-15). Deuteronomy 20 establishes two warfare regimes — siege-with-tribute for far cities, total herem for the cities of the seven nations — and the contrast is the opposite of careless exaggeration; it is a juridical distinction. The ANE-parallels argument cuts against its own user: the Mesha (Moabite) Stele records Mesha devoting the inhabitants of Nebo to Ashtar-Chemosh using the cognate root ḥrm, and it describes an actual massacre of seven thousand, not a literary trope. Lauren Monroe and Jacob Wright have shown that such devotion-rhetoric was understood programmatically, as describing real cultic destruction, not mere bombast. The comparative evidence thus makes the herem texts more literal, not less.

Source and redaction criticism offers a cleaner account. The "driving out" tradition reflects older non-Priestly strata: the drive-out promises of Exodus 23:20-33 and 34:11-16 belong to the Covenant Code and the so-called cultic decalogue (J/E material), not to P, and they envision gradual dispossession over time — Exod 23:29-30 even explains the delay ("lest the land become desolate, and the beast of the field multiply"). The total-extermination commands are Deuteronomic, composed centuries later and framed by the ideology of cult centralization and purity; the herem rationale in Deut 7 and 20 is explicitly to prevent religious contamination ("lest they teach you to do after all their abominations," Deut 20:18), the central anxiety of the Josianic program. Joshua''s own narrative oscillates and then concedes failure: Josh 15:63, 16:10, and 17:12-13, and especially Judges 1:19-36, repeatedly admit that the Canaanites were not driven out but reduced to forced labor and continued to dwell in the land. Ezekiel 47:22, finally, represents a late prophetic inclusivism that grants the gēr a permanent landed inheritance "as born in the country" — a complete reversal of the herem. Friedman, Baden, and Römer treat these as literarily and historically distinct layers; Konrad Schmid''s work on the formation of the Pentateuch (Genesis and the Moses Story; and the Gertz/Levinson/Rom/Schmid volume) likewise locates the conquest materials in separate compositional horizons. John Collins frames the resulting diversity as one of the clearest internal tensions in the canon.

No single Mosaic policy is recoverable; the final text stitches four programs from four periods. The harmonization succeeds only by treating the redacted whole as one authorial voice — the very assumption source criticism has dismantled. Even granting some hyperbole in conquest summaries, hyperbole cannot convert an explicit legal command to exterminate into a command merely to expel, nor can it reconcile expulsion with naturalized inheritance. Daniel Block, treating Ezek 47:21-23, notes how startling the inclusion of resident aliens as landed heirs is against the background of the conquest traditions; it is not an extension of the earlier program but a deliberate departure from it. The programs differ in agent (God vs. Israel), in fate (death vs. expulsion vs. coexistence), and in status (annihilated enemy vs. landholding citizen).

Verdict: a genuine contradiction reflecting compositional layering, not a coherent escalating policy.', 'John J. Collins, Does the Bible Justify Violence? (Fortress, 2004).
Joel S. Baden, The Composition of the Pentateuch: Renewing the Documentary Hypothesis (AYBRL; Yale University Press, 2012).
Lauren A. S. Monroe, Josiah''s Reform and the Dynamics of Defilement: Israelite Rites of Violence and the Making of a Biblical Text (Oxford University Press, 2011).
Jacob L. Wright, "Warfare and Wanton Destruction: A Reexamination of Deuteronomy 20:19-20," JBL 127 (2008): 423-458.
Thomas Römer, The So-Called Deuteronomistic History: A Sociological, Historical and Literary Introduction (T&T Clark, 2007).
Richard Elliott Friedman, The Bible with Sources Revealed (HarperOne, 2003).
Konrad Schmid, Genesis and the Moses Story: Israel''s Dual Origins in the Hebrew Bible (Siphrut 3; Eisenbrauns, 2010).
Paul Copan and Matthew Flannagan, Did God Really Command Genocide? Coming to Terms with the Justice of God (Baker, 2014).
K. A. Kitchen, On the Reliability of the Old Testament (Eerdmans, 2003).
Daniel I. Block, The Book of Ezekiel, Chapters 25-48 (NICOT; Eerdmans, 1998), pp. 718-721, on Ezek 47:21-23.', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Exodus, Numbers, Deuteronomy, Joshua, Ezekiel', 6, 3, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The oldest harmonization, going back at least to Keil and Delitzsch and defended by Walter Kaiser and Gordon Wenham, reads the Hebrew negation ki lo comparatively rather than absolutely: ''I did not speak to them primarily/merely about sacrifices, but about obeying my voice.'' The appeal is to the well-attested Semitic idiom of dialectical negation, where ''not X but Y'' means ''Y rather than X,'' as in Hosea 6:6 (''I desired mercy and not sacrifice'') and Deuteronomy 5:3. On this reading Jeremiah is ranking covenant priorities — obedience over ritual — not denying that the sacrificial Torah was ever given. This is a genuine grammatical option, and Jer 7:23 (''Obey my voice ... and walk in all the way that I command you'') supplies a plausible ''but Y'' clause that supports the comparative sense.

The difficulty is that the comparative reading must override a fairly emphatic absolute construction reinforced by a precise temporal anchor — ''in the day that I brought them out of the land of Egypt.'' That clause locates the denial at the exodus itself, exactly where the Priestly narrative places the giving of the sacrificial cult (Sinai, weeks later). The flat sense of the verse denies that cultic legislation belonged to the foundational covenant at all. William Holladay and Jack Lundbom both treat 7:22 as a deliberate rhetorical denial rather than an idiomatic understatement, noting that the verse stands in a unit (7:21-26) whose entire thrust is that obedience, not offerings, was the original demand.

The wider evidence favors reading this as more than rhetoric. Jeremiah belongs to a prophetic stream with a documented ambivalence toward sacrifice: Amos 5:21-25 (which likewise denies that Israel offered sacrifices in the wilderness), Hosea 6:6, Micah 6:6-8, and Isaiah 1:11-17 all subordinate or reject cult in favor of justice and covenant loyalty. These texts arguably reflect a tradition that either did not possess the developed Priestly legislation or regarded it as a later accretion. Wellhausen famously made Jer 7:22 (with Amos 5:25) a keystone for dating the Priestly source after the exile, on the reasoning that a prophet could not have denied a sacrificial Torah he knew to be Mosaic. Moshe Weinfeld''s analysis of the Deuteronomic school shows that Jer 7''s framing of the exodus emphasizes ''my voice'' and covenant obedience with no reference to Sinai cultic law — precisely what one expects if the prophet''s authoritative tradition resembled proto-Deuteronomy rather than the final Priestly Pentateuch. Patrick Miller and, from a more conservative angle, Moshe Greenberg (''Did Jeremiah Reject the Cult?'') debate how far the denial extends, but both acknowledge a real prophetic critique of the cult. The honest conclusion: the comparative-negation reading can smooth the contradiction at the level of intent, but the simpler and better-attested explanation is that Jeremiah stands in a prophetic tradition genuinely at odds with the Priestly account of the cult''s origins, and that disagreement is itself evidence of the Torah''s compositional layering. Verdict: partially harmonizable by grammar, but the prophetic-vs-Priestly tension is real and better explains the data.', 'William L. Holladay, *Jeremiah 1: A Commentary on the Book of the Prophet Jeremiah, Chapters 1-25* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1986), pp. 261-264, on Jer 7:21-26.
Jack R. Lundbom, *Jeremiah 1-20* (Anchor Bible 21A; Doubleday, 1999), pp. 481-485, on Jer 7:22.
Julius Wellhausen, *Prolegomena to the History of Ancient Israel* (1883; repr. Scholars Press, 1994).
Moshe Weinfeld, *Deuteronomy and the Deuteronomic School* (Oxford University Press, 1972).
Moshe Greenberg, "Did Jeremiah Reject the Cult?" in *Studies in the Bible and Jewish Thought* (JPS, 1995).
Walter C. Kaiser Jr., *Hard Sayings of the Old Testament* (InterVarsity, 1988).
Gordon J. Wenham, *The Book of Leviticus* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 1979).', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', 'Exodus, Jeremiah', 7, 3, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The strongest harmonization, defended by Keil and by Eugene Merrill, distinguishes ordination liturgy from standing cult law. Exodus 29 stands inside the seven-day consecration of Aaron and his sons (29:35, ''thus shall you do to Aaron ... seven days''), and the daily bullock in 29:36-37 is tied specifically to making atonement for and consecrating the altar. Numbers 28, by contrast, legislates the permanent tamid binding on all generations. On this reading the bullock was a one-time, seven-day feature of the inauguration, not a perpetual daily duty, so there is no conflict between the inaugural rite and the standing ordinance. The seven-day limiter in 29:35 gives this real textual support, and the rabbis reached the same conclusion: b. Menachot 49b-50a explicitly rules that the Exodus 29 bullock applied only during the days of consecration.

The difficulty is that Exodus 29 does not cleanly partition the bullock from the perpetual offering. Verse 36 uses la-yom, ''for/each day'' — the same construction governing the continual tamid in 29:42 (''a continual burnt offering throughout your generations'') and in Numbers 28:3. Verse 38 then pivots: ''Now this is what you shall offer upon the altar: two lambs ... day by day continually,'' which reads as introducing the standing daily rite rather than merely concluding the ordination. Read on its own terms, Exodus 29 plausibly prescribes a richer daily ritual — bullock plus two lambs — than Numbers 28, and the rabbinic ruling functions as a halakhic resolution imposed on an ambiguous text rather than its plain sense. Thomas Dozeman and William Propp both note the seam here between consecration material and perpetual-offering material.

The broader picture from Priestly source criticism is that the tamid legislation was not fixed in a single revelation. Milgrom and Knohl trace multiple Priestly strata behind the daily-offering rules, progressively coordinated. Crucially, Ezekiel 46:13-15 preserves yet a third version: a single lamb daily, not two, with an accompanying grain offering — a Second-Temple-adjacent prophetic blueprint that diverges from both Exodus and Numbers. Baruch Levine and Gordon Wenham conclude that these inconsistencies are best explained as different cultic traditions crystallized at different phases of temple practice (plausibly First- vs. Second-Temple usage) rather than as a seamless Mosaic code. The honest assessment: the ordination-vs-perpetual distinction is a viable and ancient harmonization that resolves the surface arithmetic, but it depends on a categorization the Exodus text does not clearly make, and it cannot account for the Ezekiel variant. Verdict: partially harmonizable by distinguishing inaugural from standing rites, but the differences more likely reflect layered Priestly cultic traditions.', 'Jacob Milgrom, *Numbers* (JPS Torah Commentary; JPS, 1990), pp. 237-242, on Num 28:1-8.
Baruch A. Levine, *Numbers 21-36* (Anchor Bible 4A; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 365-380.
Israel Knohl, *The Sanctuary of Silence: The Priestly Torah and the Holiness School* (Fortress, 1995).
Thomas B. Dozeman, *Exodus* (ECC; Eerdmans, 2009), pp. 651-660, on Exod 29:38-42.
William H. C. Propp, *Exodus 19-40* (Anchor Bible 2A; Doubleday, 2006), pp. 461-470.
Gordon J. Wenham, *Numbers: An Introduction and Commentary* (TOTC; InterVarsity, 1981).
Eugene H. Merrill, "Numbers," in *The Bible Knowledge Commentary* (Victor, 1985).', 0, NULL, 7, 'OT_internal', 'Exodus, Numbers', 1, 2, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

First the misreadings. Habakkuk 2:16 ("drink thou also, and let thy foreskin be uncovered") is not a command to drink; it is an imprecatory taunt within the woe-oracles against Babylon (2:15-16), reversing onto the oppressor the cup of staggering it forced on others. Lamentations 4:21 ("Rejoice ... O daughter of Edom ... thou shalt be drunken, and shalt make thyself naked") is bitter sarcasm announcing Edom''s coming humiliation. Both belong to the genre of prophetic judgment, where the "cup" is a stock metaphor for divine wrath (cf. Isa 51:17; Jer 25:15-29). Reading them as endorsements of drunkenness mistakes the rhetorical force; these two SAB answers should be revised or dropped.

Second, a popular apologetic claim must also be rejected: that the wine Jesus made at Cana, or the wine the texts commend, was unfermented grape juice. The lexical evidence is against it, though it needs to be stated carefully. Hebrew yayin and Greek oinos denote fermented, intoxicating wine; the Cana steward''s remark that hosts serve the good wine first "and when men have well drunk (methysthōsin), then the worse" (John 2:10) presupposes inebriating wine. Hebrew tirosh, usually rendered "new wine" or must, is a different and contested case: lexicography (e.g. HALOT) commonly treats it as the freshly pressed product whose degree of fermentation is debated, and it is frequently distinguished from yayin precisely on that ground. Yet tirosh is not simply innocent grape juice either — Hosea 4:11 pairs "wine and new wine" (yayin and tirosh) as together "taking away the heart," attributing an intoxicating effect to it. Robert Stein''s survey of wine in New Testament times documents that ancient wine, though often diluted, was genuinely alcoholic. Deuteronomy 14:26 settles the question for the Torah: tithe-money may be spent "for whatsoever your soul desires ... for wine, or for strong drink (shekar)" and consumed in rejoicing before YHWH. Shekar specifically denotes a strongly intoxicating beverage, so this is a positive festival mandate, not mere tolerance — Christensen reads it as authorizing celebratory consumption at the central sanctuary.

With those clarified, the bulk of the catalogue harmonizes by context. The prohibitions are role-specific and temporary. The Nazirite vow (Num 6:1-3) brackets wine for the duration of a consecration, and the mother of Samson abstains as a Nazirite-birth extension (Judg 13:3-4) — Daniel Block notes this belongs to the birth-annunciation type-scene, not general ethics. Priests abstain only while on duty in the tent (Lev 10:9, in the immediate aftermath of Nadab and Abihu), even though wine libations are commanded at the sanctuary (Num 15:5; 28:7); Milgrom reads the priestly ban as a sobriety requirement for cultic discernment, not a general prohibition. Isaiah 28:7 condemns priests and prophets who err through drink — an abuse, not a categorical ban. The Proverbs cluster (20:1; 23:20-21, 29-35) and the NT vice-lists (Eph 5:18; Gal 5:21; Rom 13:13) target drunkenness, not consumption; Van Leeuwen notes the wisdom tradition''s careful distinction between the gift of wine and the folly of the "winebibber." Romans 14:21 is situational abstention for the sake of a weaker conscience.

Against these stand the positive evaluations: wine as covenant blessing (Gen 27:28), wisdom''s feast (Prov 9:5), and the divine command to "eat your bread with joy and drink your wine with a merry heart" (Eccl 9:7). The Song of Songs likewise celebrates wine in an erotic-domestic register ("thy love is better than wine," Song 1:2; cf. 5:1), which Athalya Brenner analyzes as part of the poem''s sensory food-and-drink imagery; Köstenberger and Bauckham read Cana as deliberately affirming the goodness of wine within celebration. Even the prophetic woes that the SAB enlists under "don''t do it" mostly indict excess and oppression rather than drinking as such: Amos 6:1-6, for instance, condemns the complacent elite "that drink wine in bowls" — its target is luxurious, callous overindulgence by those "at ease in Zion," not the moderate use of wine commended elsewhere. Hosea 4:11 ("wine and new wine take away the heart") is a proverbial warning against intoxication''s effect on judgment, again an abuse-critique.

What survives all this contextualization is a real but narrower tension within the canon''s social locations: the priestly and Nazirite ideal treats wine as something to be set aside for holiness, while the festival law of Deuteronomy and the wisdom and celebration traditions treat it as a divine gift to be enjoyed. These reflect different streams — priestly, wisdom, prophetic, festival — with differing evaluations, and no single rule fully reconciles abstention-as-holiness with drink-as-blessing. The materials come from diverse traditions and social settings, and the canon preserves all of them side by side without homogenizing them.

Verdict: largely harmonizable by genre and role, with two SAB prooftexts (Hab 2:16; Lam 4:21) misframed as commands, but a genuine residual tension between priestly/Nazirite abstention and the Torah''s positive festival drinking mandate remains.', 'Robert H. Stein, "Wine-Drinking in New Testament Times," Christianity Today 19.19 (1975): 9-11.
Duane L. Christensen, Deuteronomy 1:1-21:9 (WBC 6A; Thomas Nelson, 2001), on Deut 14:26.
Daniel I. Block, Judges, Ruth (NAC; Broadman & Holman, 1999), on Judg 13.
Jacob Milgrom, Leviticus 1-16 (Anchor Bible 3; Doubleday, 1991), pp. 611-614, on Lev 10:9.
