The standard harmonization, defended by Archer, Kaiser, and Eugene Merrill, fastens on Exodus 30:12: when the people are numbered, ''every man shall give a ransom for his soul ... that there be no plague among them.'' On this reading the Torah permits censuses provided the atonement money is collected; David''s sin was either omitting the ransom or, more commonly, conducting the census in royal pride and reliance on military strength rather than on YHWH. There is a real textual hook here, and the motif of plague-upon-numbering in Exod 30 superficially matches the plague in 2 Sam 24.

But the harmonization imports a cause the narratives never state. Nowhere does 2 Samuel 24 or 1 Chronicles 21 mention the half-shekel ransom or attribute the disaster to its omission. More damaging, 2 Samuel 24:1 makes YHWH himself the instigator: ''the anger of the LORD was kindled against Israel, and he moved David against them to say, Go, number Israel and Judah.'' God incites the census, then punishes the people for it — a theological tangle the pride-explanation does not touch. The clearest evidence that ancient interpreters did not read the offense as a missing ransom is the Chronicler''s rewrite: 1 Chronicles 21:1 replaces YHWH with ''Satan stood up against Israel, and provoked David to number Israel.'' This is one of the textbook cases of inner-biblical theological revision — the Chronicler, reading Samuel and finding it intolerable that YHWH would incite a sin he then avenges, substituted a different inciter. Sara Japhet and Gary Knoppers treat this as a deliberate redactional softening; Ryan Stokes''s study of the figure argues ''the satan'' here is still an adversarial agent, but in any case the change is to the identity of the instigator, not to the addition of a forgotten ritual. Peter Enns highlights the episode as a paradigm of how Scripture preserves competing theological renderings of one event. Had the ransom-omission been the operative issue, the Chronicler could simply have noted it; instead he changed who moved David, which shows that the earliest interpreters located the problem in the divine incitement and the act of numbering itself, not in a cultic technicality.

The better explanation invokes the ancient Near Eastern wariness about censuses. A census rendered a population legible for taxation and military conscription and was widely felt to court divine displeasure by treating the people as a royal possession; McCarter notes parallels and the danger associated with numbering. The Priestly legislation of Exodus 30 represents a later cultic rationalization that creates a sanctioned procedure (with ransom) to defuse that very danger, whereas the older Samuel tradition preserves the sense that a royal census undertaken without explicit divine mandate trespassed on YHWH''s prerogative over his people. The Chronicler then sanitizes the theology a further step. These are three layers — an old narrative of dangerous numbering, a Priestly procedure, and a later theological correction — not three harmonious teachings. Verdict: a genuine contradiction; the harmonization works only by reading the unstated ransom motive into a Samuel narrative whose own difficulty the Chronicler felt compelled to rewrite.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *II Samuel* (Anchor Bible 9; Doubleday, 1984), pp. 508-518, on 2 Sam 24.
Sara Japhet, *I & II Chronicles: A Commentary* (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 374-378.
Gary N. Knoppers, *1 Chronicles 10-29* (Anchor Bible 12A; Doubleday, 2004), pp. 740-752.
Ryan E. Stokes, "The Devil Made David Do It . . . Or Did He? The Nature, Identity, and Literary Origins of the Satan in 1 Chronicles 21:1," *JBL* 128 (2009): 91-106.
Peter Enns, *Inspiration and Incarnation: Evangelicals and the Problem of the Old Testament* (Baker Academic, 2005).
Walter C. Kaiser Jr. et al., *Hard Sayings of the Bible* (InterVarsity, 1996).', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Exodus, Numbers, 2 Samuel, 1 Chronicles', 6, 3, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Exodus 30:22-33 specifies a single consecrated compound — the holy anointing oil of liquid myrrh, sweet cinnamon, fragrant calamus, cassia, and olive oil — reserved exclusively for anointing the tent of meeting, the ark and its furniture, and Aaron and his sons. A parallel passage (30:34-38) governs the sacred incense (qetoret). Verses 32-33 and 37-38 forbid compounding either formula ''like it'' for ordinary use, and the penalty is karet, being ''cut off from his people'' — the severe sanction reserved for trespass on the holy. The prohibition is explicitly about sanctuary exclusivity: these specific recipes are qodesh, set apart for YHWH, and replicating them profanes the holy. It says nothing about cosmetic or domestic perfume in general.

The lexical detail confirms the point, though it must be stated carefully. Exodus 30 designates its anointing product mishchah qodesh, ''holy anointing,'' a cultic term, and 30:37 forbids reproducing the consecrated incense mixture. Proverbs 27:9 (MT) reads shemen u-qetoret — shemen (''oil'') and qetoret. Notably, qetoret is itself the word used for the sacred incense of Exodus 30:34-38, not a generic term for cosmetic perfume; so the contrast between the two passages cannot rest on wholly different vocabulary, as if Proverbs used a ''secular'' word. The decisive distinction is contextual rather than purely lexical: Exodus regulates a specific consecrated recipe reserved for the sanctuary, whereas Proverbs describes the ordinary, everyday enjoyment of fragrant oil and incense in normal social life. One text governs cultic exclusivity; the other commends a commonplace sensory pleasure. The two are doing different cultural work with overlapping vocabulary.

The wider canon corroborates that ordinary perfume was unremarkable and even celebrated: the Song of Songs is saturated with myrrh and spices in an erotic-domestic register (Song 1:3, 13; 4:10-14), Esther 2:12 describes a year of cosmetic perfuming, and Psalm 45:8 praises the king''s myrrh-scented robes. Negative perfume imagery (e.g., Isa 3:24; 57:9) attaches to luxury-as-pride or idolatry, not to fragrance per se.

Scholars of ancient perfume economics, including Victor Matthews''s survey and Nili Fox''s work on Israelite officialdom, observe that perfumed oils were ubiquitous in elite ancient Israelite life and that the biblical distinction between cultic and profane fragrance reflects a priestly boundary-drawing program (protecting the uniqueness of sanctuary substances) rather than a categorical prohibition of scent. Bruce Waltke''s commentary on Proverbs reads 27:9 as commending ordinary fragrant oil as a sensory pleasure, and Hamilton and Propp both locate the Exodus 30 prohibition squarely within sanctuary-exclusivity law. The only residual interest in the SAB''s pairing is the genuinely real tension between priestly sanctuary-exclusivity and the wisdom/royal celebration of sensory culture — but that is a difference of domain, not a contradiction in commandment. Verdict: overstated. Exodus 30:37 bans copying one holy recipe; Proverbs 27:9 commends ordinary perfume and incense. They do not conflict.', 'Victor P. Hamilton, *Exodus: An Exegetical Commentary* (Baker Academic, 2011), pp. 519-528, on Exod 30:22-38.
William H. C. Propp, *Exodus 19-40* (Anchor Bible 2A; Doubleday, 2006), pp. 480-487.
Bruce K. Waltke, *The Book of Proverbs: Chapters 15-31* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 2005), pp. 376-378, on Prov 27:9.
Victor H. Matthews, "Perfumes and Spices," in *Anchor Bible Dictionary*, ed. Freedman (Doubleday, 1992).
Nili S. Fox, *In the Service of the King: Officialdom in Ancient Israel and Judah* (Hebrew Union College Press, 2000).
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982).', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Exodus, Proverbs', 6, 2, 1, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

The apologetic response runs on two tracks. First, a physical/metaphorical distinction: Isaiah 40:28 denies that God ever suffers creaturely physical exhaustion, whereas the prophetic ''weariness'' is anthropopathic — figurative speech for God''s moral exasperation with covenant infidelity. Second, the Exodus ''refreshed'' is read as covenantal rest establishing the Sabbath, not recovery from depletion. John Oswalt and Walter Kaiser defend both moves. The anthropopathism track has genuine force: Isaiah 40 is plainly a doxology asserting transcendence over creaturely limitation, and no competent reader takes it as a claim about divine stamina; the prophetic ''I am weary'' is recognizably the idiom of an offended covenant partner. So far the surface tension does ease.

The distinction carries a cost, however, and it does not cleanly cover Exodus 31:17. The verb there is nafash, ''was refreshed'' or, more literally, ''took breath'' / ''caught one''s breath.'' The same verb appears in Exodus 23:12 and 2 Samuel 16:14 of humans and animals recuperating from labor. The Priestly Sabbath etiology explicitly grounds human rest in divine rest (''for in six days the LORD made heaven and earth, and on the seventh day he rested and was refreshed''); if the divine refreshment is drained of all content as ''mere metaphor,'' the very analogy on which the Sabbath command rests loses its footing. The text models the creature''s recuperation on God''s, which presupposes that God''s ''rest'' is more than an empty figure.

More broadly, critical scholarship cautions against the wholesale quarantining of divine affect. Once weariness, anger, regret (Gen 6:6), surprise, and even apparent learning (''now I know,'' Gen 22:12) are all reclassified as anthropopathism, large tracts of the Priestly and Deuteronomistic portrayal of YHWH cease to mean what they say. Terence Fretheim''s study of divine suffering and Benjamin Sommer''s work on the bodies of God argue that ancient Israelite theology had robust anthropomorphic and anthropopathic dimensions — a God who genuinely responds, changes course, and is affected — which later classical theism, under philosophical pressure for divine impassibility, smoothed over. Abraham Heschel''s analysis of the divine pathos makes the prophetic ''weariness'' central rather than incidental to Israel''s God-concept. The most economical reading is developmental and source-critical: Isaiah 40:28 is a Deutero-Isaian, exilic universalization of divine transcendence, framed to answer exilic despair (''have you not heard?''), whereas the Priestly Exod 31:17 and the earlier prophetic oracles preserve an older, more anthropomorphic register. Second Isaiah''s lofty theology is not the only theology in the canon; the Tanakh contains a spectrum of God-concepts. Verdict: partially harmonizable — the physical/metaphorical distinction dissolves much of the conflict, but it strains against nafash in the Sabbath etiology and reflects a real diversity of Israelite portrayals of God across the canon.', 'Terence E. Fretheim, *The Suffering of God: An Old Testament Perspective* (Fortress, 1984).
Benjamin D. Sommer, *The Bodies of God and the World of Ancient Israel* (Cambridge University Press, 2009).
Abraham J. Heschel, *The Prophets* (Harper & Row, 1962).
John Goldingay, *The Message of Isaiah 40-55* (T&T Clark, 2005).
Mark S. Smith, *The Origins of Biblical Monotheism: Israel''s Polytheistic Background and the Ugaritic Texts* (Oxford University Press, 2001).
John N. Oswalt, *The Book of Isaiah, Chapters 40-66* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 1998), pp. 67-72, on Isa 40:28.
