The oldest harmonization simply identifies them: Sinai and Horeb are two names for one site, or Horeb names the wider massif while Sinai names the specific peak (or vice versa). Archer, Alec Motyer, and Carmen Imes defend versions of this, and Jewish tradition reached it long ago — b. Shabbat 89a-b offers a midrashic list of the mountain''s several names tied to its functions. Given the irrecoverable geography of the Sinai peninsula and the fluidity of ancient toponyms, the bare lexical identification cannot be ruled out, and at the level of reference the two names may well point to one place. To that extent the tension is not a factual contradiction about two distinct mountains.

What the harmonization does not explain is the distribution of the names, which has been a cornerstone of Pentateuchal source criticism since Astruc and Wellhausen. The usage is not random or freely mixed; it segregates with striking consistency. ''Sinai'' clusters in material assigned to the Yahwistic and Priestly sources — the Sinai pericope of Exodus 19-24 (excluding D-edited seams), Leviticus, Numbers, and the Priestly-influenced sections of later texts like Nehemiah 9. ''Horeb'' clusters in the Elohistic and Deuteronomic material: Deuteronomy uses Horeb consistently and never says Sinai, and the Deuteronomistic History follows suit (1 Kings 8; the Elijah-at-Horeb narrative of 1 Kings 19), as does Malachi, whose theology is markedly Deuteronomistic. Richard Friedman lays out the distribution in detail and Joel Baden''s documentary analysis confirms it; crucially, the Sinai/Horeb split correlates with other independent source markers — divine-name usage, characteristic vocabulary, theological emphasis, and narrative doublets. If a single author were merely using two interchangeable names for one mountain, one would expect them to appear mixed within the same documents; instead they are sharply partitioned along precisely the lines drawn by other criteria.

This is what makes the case a textbook source-critical marker rather than a trivial synonym. The apologetic fallback — that different biblical authors simply preferred different names for the same place — is true as far as it goes, but it concedes the decisive point: the Pentateuch and the historical books were composed by multiple writers with distinct vocabularies and traditions. Once that is granted, one is already doing source criticism, only under a different label. Thomas Dozeman''s redaction study of Exodus 19-24 and the standard treatments by Weinfeld and Schmid all locate the Horeb tradition in the Deuteronomic stream and the Sinai tradition in J/P. Verdict: partially harmonizable at the level of geographic reference (the two names may denote one mountain), but the rigorously segregated distribution of the names is strong positive evidence of the Torah''s composite authorship — the real significance of the datum.', 'Richard Elliott Friedman, *The Bible with Sources Revealed* (HarperOne, 2003).
Joel S. Baden, *The Composition of the Pentateuch: Renewing the Documentary Hypothesis* (AYBRL; Yale University Press, 2012).
Thomas B. Dozeman, *God on the Mountain: A Study of Redaction, Theology and Canon in Exodus 19-24* (Scholars Press, 1989).
Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; JPS, 1996).
Moshe Weinfeld, *Deuteronomy 1-11* (Anchor Bible 5; Doubleday, 1991).
Carmen Joy Imes, *Bearing YHWH''s Name at Sinai: A Reexamination of the Name Command of the Decalogue* (Eisenbrauns, 2018).
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982).', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', 'Exodus, Leviticus, Deuteronomy, 1 Kings, 2 Chronicles, Nehemiah, Malachi', 4, 3, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard conservative harmonization — Kitchen, Howard, Hess — reads Joshua''s summaries as conventional ancient Near Eastern conquest rhetoric describing a decisive but not exhaustive victory, with Judges 1 narrating the subsequent failure to consolidate. K. Lawson Younger''s *Ancient Conquest Accounts* gives this its scholarly muscle: Egyptian, Hittite, and Assyrian royal annals routinely claim total destruction (''I slew them all,'' ''none escaped'') of populations that demonstrably survived, because the genre serves ideological celebration, not census accuracy. On this reading, Joshua 21:44 is hyperbole of the same family, and no honest ancient reader would have heard it as a body-count claim contradicted by Judges 1.

This has genuine force, and Joshua itself supports part of it: Joshua 13:1-6 explicitly inventories unconquered territory, and 15:63 and 17:12-13 record the same gaps that Judges 1 will repeat. So the triumphal summary in 21:43-45 is not naive about the exceptions — it sits in a book that already names them. That is precisely the difficulty. The hyperbole defense explains a single rhetorical summary; it does not explain a corpus that asserts both completion and non-completion in adjacent breath, then supplies a divine speech (Judges 2:1-5) to account for why the promised dispossession did not happen: ''I will not drive them out from before you.''

Source and redaction criticism account for this better than genre alone. Martin Noth''s Deuteronomistic History thesis, refined by Richard Nelson and Thomas Römer, treats Joshua 21:43-45 as a DtrH editorial frame asserting the theology of fulfilled promise, while Judges 1 preserves an older, more localized — often called Ephraimite or Judahite — register of who actually held which towns. Carolyn Pressler notes that Judges 1''s material reads as pre-Deuteronomistic source data later fitted under the theological frame of Judges 2, and Susan Niditch treats Judges 1 as a competing tradition of incomplete conquest. The redactor who joined the streams did not erase the tension; he theologized it, converting military failure into a moral lesson about disobedience and a test of Israel (Judges 2:22; 3:4).

The external data align with the composite, gradualist picture rather than a single sweeping conquest. The Merneptah Stele (c. 1207 BCE) already places an entity called ''Israel'' in Canaan as a people, not an invading army. Excavation at Jericho (Kenyon) and Ai (et-Tell) found no fortified cities destroyed in the relevant Late Bronze window; Hazor and Lachish do show destruction layers, but their dating and causation remain debated and cannot be assigned to a single swift campaign without controversy. The broader archaeological discussion — Finkelstein, Dever — favors a protracted emergence of Israel within Canaan, which matches Judges far better than the triumphal Joshua summaries, though the precise model is contested.

Verdict: partially harmonizable. The ANE hyperbole convention legitimately softens the rhetorical summaries, and Joshua itself flags the exceptions, so the language need not be read as a literal census; but it cannot dissolve a corpus that holds completion and incompletion together and must invent theodicy to bridge them. The cleanest account is two literary traditions stitched by the Deuteronomistic editor.', 'K. Lawson Younger Jr., *Ancient Conquest Accounts* (JSOTSup 98; Sheffield Academic Press, 1990), pp. 190-237, on the hyperbolic conventions of ANE conquest reports applied to Joshua 21:43-44
Martin Noth, *The Deuteronomistic History* (JSOTSup 15; Sheffield Academic Press, 1981), on the DtrH framework underlying Joshua-Judges
Richard D. Nelson, *Joshua* (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 1997), pp. 245-250, on the editorial summary of Joshua 21:43-45
Thomas Römer, *The So-Called Deuteronomistic History* (T&T Clark, 2007), on the redactional layering of Joshua-Judges
Susan Niditch, *Judges* (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 2008), on Judges 1 as a competing tradition of incomplete conquest
Carolyn Pressler, *Joshua, Judges, and Ruth* (Westminster Bible Companion; Westminster John Knox, 2002), on Judges 1 source predating the DtrH triumph frame
K. A. Kitchen, *On the Reliability of the Old Testament* (Eerdmans, 2003), pp. 159-190, on the conservative successful-then-backslid harmonization
