The classic harmonization, traceable to Maimonides (*Guide* I.37) and renewed by Gleason Archer, parses panim el-panim as an idiom for direct, intimate verbal communication rather than literal visual reception of the divine face. On this reading 33:11 describes the manner of speech—unmediated, conversational—while 33:20 governs visual perception of the divine glory. The idiom genuinely carries this sense elsewhere: Deuteronomy 5:4 says YHWH spoke to all Israel "face to face" at Horeb, yet Deuteronomy 4:12 and 4:15 stress that the people "saw no manner of similitude" (temunah), only heard a voice. So the phrase can denote presence and address without implying a seen face. The apologist has a real lexical handle.

Two features resist a clean resolution, however. First, Exodus 33:11 adds the intensifier "as a man speaketh unto his friend," which strengthens rather than attenuates the immediacy. Second—and more damaging—Numbers 12:8 explicitly extends Moses'' privilege into the visual register, contrasting him with ordinary prophets who receive visions and dreams: with Moses YHWH speaks "mouth to mouth (peh el-peh), even apparently (mar''eh), and not in dark speeches; and the similitude of the LORD (temunat YHWH) shall he behold." That is the very word, temunah, that Deuteronomy 4 denies the people saw—now affirmed of Moses, and affirmed visually. The biblical writers themselves do not speak with one voice about whether Moses'' access was uniquely visual or merely verbal.

Source criticism explains the layering. The older Yahwistic and pre-Priestly strata are robustly anthropomorphic: YHWH walks in Eden (Gen 3:8), eats with Abraham at Mamre (Gen 18), and in Exodus 24:9-11 the seventy elders "saw the God of Israel" and "did eat and drink"—a striking statement that no harm came to them. Against this, the Deuteronomic and Priestly programs aggressively guard divine transcendence and invisibility (Deut 4:12-15; the prohibition in Exod 33:20). Exodus 33 reads as a composite in which an older notice of face-to-face intimacy (v. 11) has been overlaid with a transcendence-protecting theophany (vv. 17-23) that concedes only the "back parts." Benjamin Sommer''s *The Bodies of God* situates this precisely as the friction between an embodied-deity tradition and an anti-anthropomorphic correction, both surviving in the canon. Propp''s Anchor Bible commentary likewise reads vv. 18-23 as a deliberate qualification of the bald claim in v. 11.

The editor did not delete the older material; he juxtaposed correction with it. That is not a unified doctrine of divine visibility but two competing theologies kept in uneasy proximity. The Maimonidean idiom reading is the right tool for Deuteronomy 5:4, but applied to Exodus 33:11 it must absorb both the "as a friend" intensifier and the contrary visual claim of Numbers 12:8, which it cannot do without special pleading. Verdict: not harmonized—a genuine tension produced by anthropomorphic and anti-anthropomorphic strands the redactor left side by side.', 'Benjamin D. Sommer, *The Bodies of God and the World of Ancient Israel* (Cambridge University Press, 2009), pp. 58-68, on the anthropomorphic vs. anti-anthropomorphic strands behind Exodus 33:11, 20
William H. C. Propp, *Exodus 19-40* (Anchor Bible 2A; Doubleday, 2006), pp. 601-608, on Exodus 33:11-23
