Jacob Milgrom''s extended treatment in *Leviticus 1-16* establishes the distinction the harmonization depends on. Karet ("cutting off") is a divine penalty—variously understood as premature death, the extinction of one''s line (dying childless), or exclusion from being "gathered to one''s kin" in the afterlife—imposed by God, not adjudicated or carried out by a human court. Mot yumat ("he shall surely be put to death") is the human judicial death penalty, executed by the community, typically by stoning. They operate at different levels of enforcement: one is heaven''s sanction, the other the court''s. The rabbis devoted substantial discussion to karet''s exact mechanism (e.g., b. Mo''ed Katan 28a; b. Sanhedrin 64b), debating whether it meant early death, childlessness, or a postmortem penalty—proof that the tradition never equated it with exile.

Once karet is correctly identified, the two-tier structure of the Sabbath law becomes legible. Exodus 31:14-15, 35:2, and the case in Numbers 15:32-36 all attach mot yumat to active, public, intentional Sabbath labor; the karet clause covers the broader, often unwitnessed category of "that soul" who profanes the day. These are complementary sanctions for graded circumstances, not competing answers to one question. The plain-reading apologetic harmonization is therefore exegetically solid here.

The Numbers 15:32-36 episode is nonetheless revealing and worth weighing fairly. A man is found gathering sticks on the Sabbath; he is placed in custody "because it was not declared what should be done to him," and Moses must inquire of YHWH, who then prescribes stoning by the whole congregation. The narrative''s own admission that the penalty was not yet specified suggests the Priestly Sabbath legislation was not experienced as a self-evidently complete code; the stick-gatherer story functions as case-law supplement, fixing the judicial death penalty for a witnessed, intentional violation. Baruch Levine and Milgrom both read Numbers 15:32-36 as precisely this kind of legal precedent narrative appended to the karet legislation of Exodus-Leviticus.

The deeper historical question—whether karet and the judicial death penalty were ever distinct in lived practice, or whether the distinction is a later scribal systematization—remains open, and Donald Wold''s study of the karet penalty in P canvasses the possibilities. But that is a question about the social reality behind the texts, not about whether the texts contradict.

Verdict: harmonized. The supposed exile-versus-execution contradiction dissolves once karet is recognized as a distinct divine sanction rather than banishment; Exodus 31:14 prescribes both penalties in a deliberate two-tier scheme, and Numbers 15:32-36 supplies the judicial-death case-law for intentional, witnessed labor.', 'Jacob Milgrom, *Leviticus 1-16* (Anchor Bible 3; Doubleday, 1991), pp. 457-460, on the karet penalty as a divine sanction, with reference to Exodus 31:14
Donald J. Wold, "The Kareth Penalty in P: Rationale and Cases," *SBL Seminar Papers* (1979): pp. 1-45
Baruch A. Levine, *Numbers 1-20* (Anchor Bible 4; Doubleday, 1993), on Numbers 15:32-36 as case-law supplement
William H. C. Propp, *Exodus 19-40* (Anchor Bible 2A; Doubleday, 2006), on Exodus 31:12-17
Nahum M. Sarna, *Exodus* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1991), on the distinction between karet and judicial death at Exodus 31:14', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Exodus, Numbers', 6, 2, 2, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard apologetic resolution—Archer, Merrill, Hamilton—reads "made" in Deuteronomy as the language of the commissioning authority: as a king "builds" a palace his masons actually construct, Moses "made" the ark by directing Bezalel''s work. Deuteronomy gives the overseer''s perspective; Exodus 37 names the artisan. The move is legitimate idiom in many languages and is not implausible on its face.

The Hebrew, however, offers no causative marker to support it. Deuteronomy 10:3 uses the qal wa''a''as ("and I made") with Moses as direct subject; Exodus 37:1 uses the qal wayya''as bezal''el ("and Bezalel made") with Bezalel as direct subject. Neither verb is in the causative hiphil ("caused to be made"). The commissioning reading must be imported semantically rather than read off the grammar; both texts present their named subject as the literal maker.

The more economical explanation treats Deuteronomy as an independent literary stratum that retells the wilderness narrative in compressed, Moses-centered form. Deuteronomy 10:1-5 streamlines the whole tablet-and-ark sequence: Moses ascends, hews the tablets himself, makes the ark, deposits the tablets, descends—a single human actor throughout. The Priestly account in Exodus 25-40 instead builds an elaborate sanctuary narrative in which YHWH designates Bezalel of Judah and Oholiab of Dan, fills them with the divine spirit and skill (Exod 31:1-11; 35:30-35), and assigns the craft to this gifted artisanal caste under Moses'' authority. Notably, the chronology also differs: in Deuteronomy 10 the ark is made before Moses'' second ascent to receive the tablets, whereas in Exodus the ark is constructed afterward, as part of the tabernacle project—an ordering difference the commissioning reading does not address.

The two presentations serve different theological programs. Deuteronomy concentrates mediation in Moses, the sole covenant intermediary; P distributes cultic labor among spirit-endowed specialists, consistent with its pervasive interest in ordained ritual expertise and the dignity of the sanctuary craft. Tigay''s JPS commentary on Deuteronomy 10 treats this directly as a case where D''s version diverges from P''s, and Weinfeld and Friedman situate Deuteronomy 10:1-5 as a D recasting that does not coordinate with the Priestly Bezalel material. Sarna, more conservative, still registers the divergence. Even Kitchen, defending overall reliability, concedes the accounts derive from distinct literary settings.

Verdict: partially harmonizable. The commissioning-authority idiom is formally available and lets a harmonizing reader hold both statements together, but it is imported against the qal grammar of both verbs and leaves the differing sequence and theological emphasis unexplained; source criticism more simply accounts for why D credits Moses and P credits Bezalel.', 'Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1996), pp. 100-103, on Deuteronomy 10:1-5 and its divergence from P
Moshe Weinfeld, *Deuteronomy 1-11* (Anchor Bible 5; Doubleday, 1991), on the Deuteronomic recasting of Deuteronomy 10:1-5
Richard Elliott Friedman, *The Bible with Sources Revealed* (HarperOne, 2003), on the D and P accounts of the ark at Deuteronomy 10:1-5 and Exodus 37:1
William H. C. Propp, *Exodus 19-40* (Anchor Bible 2A; Doubleday, 2006), on Bezalel and the Priestly sanctuary craft at Exodus 31:1-11 and 37:1
Victor P. Hamilton, *Exodus: An Exegetical Commentary* (Baker Academic, 2011), on the commissioning-authority reading of Exodus 37:1 and Deuteronomy 10:3
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), on Moses as commissioning maker at Deuteronomy 10:3', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', 'Exodus, Deuteronomy', 7, 2, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The apologetic case, developed by Wenham and Kaiser and partly anticipated by Milgrom, distinguishes the cases. Numbers 15:22-23 frames its scenario as inadvertent failure to keep "all these commandments"—the whole of the Torah YHWH spoke through Moses—whereas Leviticus 4 addresses inadvertent transgression of a particular prohibition ("any of the commandments... concerning things which should not be done"). On this reading the two texts legislate two different offenses: comprehensive communal apostasy in Numbers, a specific communal lapse in Leviticus, each with its appropriate rite. The distinction has textual purchase, since Numbers 15 does generalize its protasis broadly.

The difficulty is that the two passages differ not merely in scope but in ritual architecture. Leviticus 4 mandates the elders'' hand-leaning, interior blood manipulation against the veil, and the carcass-burning outside the camp—features absent from Numbers 15, which instead pairs a wholly different function, an olah (burnt offering), with the hattat (sin offering). One animal versus two; one cultic function versus two; one ritual procedure versus another. These are not naturally two grades of a single ceremony but two distinct rites, and the case-distinction reading does not by itself explain why the rituals themselves diverge so thoroughly.

Source and stratum analysis offers the cleaner historical account. Israel Knohl''s *The Sanctuary of Silence* argues that Numbers 15 belongs to the Holiness School (H), a later Priestly stratum that revised and supplemented the earlier Priestly Torah (P) preserved in Leviticus 4—here expanding the purification offering to include a burnt offering as an added atoning element. Milgrom disputes Knohl on the direction of dependence but agrees the passages stem from distinct Priestly layers. The final redactor incorporated both without fully coordinating them, leaving two procedures for the communal inadvertent sin.

The rabbis themselves felt the need to harmonize. The Mishnah and Talmud (m. Horayot 1-3; b. Horayot 6b) resolve the doubling by assigning Numbers 15''s two-animal rite specifically to inadvertent communal idolatry and Leviticus 4''s single-bullock rite to other inadvertent communal sin. That is a workable legal distinction—but it is one the biblical text does not itself state; the rabbis supplied it precisely because the plain texts were not transparently compatible.

Verdict: genuine literary tension. The scope-distinction harmonization is partly grounded in Numbers 15''s broad framing, but it cannot account for the divergent ritual structures; the rabbinic idolatry/other-sin division is a coherent post-hoc legal harmonization, while source criticism (H revising P) best explains why two different rites for the same case stand in the Torah.', 'Jacob Milgrom, *Leviticus 1-16* (Anchor Bible 3; Doubleday, 1991), pp. 264-270, on Leviticus 4:13-21
Jacob Milgrom, *Numbers* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1990), on Numbers 15:22-31
Israel Knohl, *The Sanctuary of Silence: The Priestly Torah and the Holiness School* (Fortress, 1995), on Numbers 15:22-26 as a Holiness-School revision of P
Baruch A. Levine, *Leviticus* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1989), on the ritual of Leviticus 4:13-21
Gordon J. Wenham, *The Book of Leviticus* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 1979), on the case distinction between Leviticus 4:13-21 and Numbers 15:24-26
Walter C. Kaiser Jr., *Hard Sayings of the Bible* (InterVarsity, 1996), on the differing communal sin offerings', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Leviticus, Numbers', 6, 2, 1, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The Greek of Hebrews 10:4 is categorical: adynaton ("impossible") fronts the clause for emphasis, and the verb aphairein ("take away") is denied absolutely, not relativized. The author is not saying the sacrifices were temporary or anticipatory placeholders that genuinely worked at a lower level; he is asserting an ontological incapacity. Leviticus 17:11 makes the opposite ontological claim: blood does atone, by divine gift. One text says the mechanism is impossible; the other says it is mandated and effective.

The apologetic resolution is supersessionist typology, and it is the self-understanding of Hebrews itself: F. F. Bruce, William Lane, and Gareth Cockerill develop it fully. On this reading the Levitical sacrifices effected real but provisional cultic purification within the covenant, while functioning as types pointing forward to the once-for-all sacrifice of Christ, which alone deals with sin definitively (Heb 10:10, 10:14). "Take away sins" in Hebrews is read in an eschatological-final register (full, permanent removal of conscience-guilt, Heb 9:9, 10:2), distinct from the ritual sense of "atone" (kipper) in Leviticus.

The distinction works only if one accepts Hebrews'' own framework. The Priestly theology itself knows nothing of a deeper atonement behind the sacrificial system: kipper (to purge, cover, ransom) is presented as genuinely effective—it cleanses the sanctuary and secures forgiveness (Lev 4:20, 4:26, 4:31)—not as a shadow awaiting a future reality. Hebrews'' claim that animal blood was always categorically incapable is a theological retrojection, not a reading native to the Torah it reinterprets.

Historically, this fits Hebrews'' setting. Harold Attridge''s Hermeneia commentary documents how the author constructs an elaborate typological argument—Melchizedek priesthood, heavenly sanctuary, new covenant—precisely to justify a community''s displacement from the Jerusalem cult. This is theological innovation in the late first century. Notably, post-70 CE Judaism, which also lost the Temple, developed substitutes for sacrifice (Torah study, prayer, almsgiving; cf. Avot de-Rabbi Natan 4, the famous Yohanan ben Zakkai tradition) without ever declaring that the sacrifices had never worked. Hebrews takes the far more radical step of denying the system''s efficacy in principle.

Even the prophetic critiques that Hebrews echoes stop short of this. Amos 5:21-24, Hosea 6:6, Micah 6:6-8, Isaiah 1:11-17, and Jeremiah 7:21-23 subordinate sacrifice to obedience and justice, and some sharply devalue cultic offerings, but none asserts that sacrifice cannot in principle atone—their target is sacrifice divorced from righteousness. Hebrews'' flat denial is genuinely novel within the canon. Jon Levenson and Stephen Finlan both treat it as a distinct development in Second Temple atonement thinking, and David Moffitt''s work shows how dependent Hebrews'' logic is on Christ''s resurrection and heavenly offering to make its case work.

Verdict: genuine contradiction at the literal level. The typological harmonization is Hebrews'' own argument and coheres within its Christological frame, but it does not reconcile with the Priestly theology of kipper as effective atonement; it supersedes that theology by reinterpreting it.', 'Harold W. Attridge, *The Epistle to the Hebrews* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1989), pp. 270-290, on Hebrews 10:1-18
Jacob Milgrom, *Leviticus 17-22* (Anchor Bible 3A; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 1471-1474, on Leviticus 17:11
William L. Lane, *Hebrews 9-13* (WBC 47B; Word, 1991), on the Christological supersession at Hebrews 10:4
Gareth Lee Cockerill, *The Epistle to the Hebrews* (NICNT; Eerdmans, 2012), on the typological reading of Hebrews 10:1-14
Jon D. Levenson, *Resurrection and the Restoration of Israel* (Yale University Press, 2006), on Second Temple atonement developments
Stephen Finlan, *Problems with Atonement* (Liturgical Press, 2005), on Hebrews'' distinctive atonement argument at 10:4
David M. Moffitt, *Atonement and the Logic of Resurrection in the Epistle to the Hebrews* (NovTSup 141; Brill, 2011), on the resurrection logic underpinning Hebrews 10:4-14', 0, NULL, 2, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Leviticus, Numbers, Hebrews', 5, 3, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Leviticus 4 calibrates the offering to the offender''s rank: a bullock for the anointed (high) priest whose sin implicates the whole people (4:3); a bullock for the entire congregation (4:14); a male goat for a chieftain or ruler (4:23); and a female goat or a lamb for a common Israelite (4:28, 4:32). The hierarchy is deliberate and theologically weighted—the more consequential the sinner, the costlier the purification. Leviticus 5:14-19 then treats a different category altogether: me''ilah, sacrilege or trespass against "the holy things of the LORD," for which a ram of guilt-offering value (with restitution plus a fifth) is required. These are not three competing answers to one question but a differentiated system keyed to offender status and offense type, and Milgrom''s analysis of the purification and reparation offerings develops exactly this typology. Against this part of the charge, the case-refinement harmonization is exegetically strong.

The harmonization meets its limit, however, in the overlap between Leviticus 4 and Numbers 15. Leviticus 4:27-31 prescribes for the common person''s inadvertent sin a female goat—se''irat izzim—and 4:32 permits a lamb as an alternative. Numbers 15:27-28 prescribes for the same offender and the same offense, the inadvertent sin of an individual "soul," a female goat—again se''irat izzim, "of the first year." The two laws address an identical case with overlapping but non-identical prescriptions: Leviticus offers a goat-or-lamb choice; Numbers fixes the goat and specifies its age. The surrounding rituals also differ, since Numbers 15 embeds the individual case within its broader (and differently structured) communal legislation. This is not a difference of status-tier or offense-category; it is two versions of the same provision.

Source-critical analysis explains the overlap. Israel Knohl''s Holiness-School thesis treats Numbers 15:27-31 as a later H reworking of the earlier Priestly purification-offering legislation in Leviticus 4. Milgrom contests Knohl on which stratum is prior but agrees the two passages derive from distinct Priestly layers later compiled together. The redactor preserved both formulations of the common-person case without fully reconciling the lamb-alternative of Leviticus 4:32 with the fixed year-old goat of Numbers 15:27.

Verdict: partially harmonizable. The supposed three-way contradiction is largely an artifact of collapsing Leviticus 4''s status hierarchy and Leviticus 5''s distinct sacrilege category—on those the case-refinement reading is solid. The residual Leviticus 4 versus Numbers 15 overlap for the common Israelite, however, is best explained as distinct Priestly strata rather than as cleanly complementary cases.', 'Jacob Milgrom, *Leviticus 1-16* (Anchor Bible 3; Doubleday, 1991), pp. 226-292, on the status-graded purification offerings of Leviticus 4 and the reparation offering of 5:15-16
Jacob Milgrom, *Numbers* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1990), on Numbers 15:27-31 and its relation to Leviticus 4
Israel Knohl, *The Sanctuary of Silence: The Priestly Torah and the Holiness School* (Fortress, 1995), on Numbers 15:27-31 as a Holiness-School reworking of P
John E. Hartley, *Leviticus* (WBC 4; Word, 1992), on the graded sin-offering schedule of Leviticus 4-5
Baruch A. Levine, *Leviticus* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1989), on the purification and reparation offerings of Leviticus 4-5
Gordon J. Wenham, *The Book of Leviticus* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 1979), on the case-refinement reading of Leviticus 4 and 5', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Leviticus, Numbers', 6, 2, 1, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The apologetic resolution—Kaiser, Craigie, Merrill—proposes that the two texts use a narrower and a broader category. The Hebrew sheretz ha-of ("swarming/creeping thing that flies") in Deuteronomy 14:19 supposedly denotes only the crawling winged insects that Leviticus also forbids, while the jumping locusts of Leviticus 11:21-23 form a separate sub-class that Deuteronomy is not addressing. The reading has real exegetical support from within Leviticus itself: Leviticus 11:20 first declares "all fowls that creep, going upon all four, shall be an abomination," and only then (vv. 21-23) excepts the leaping insects—so Leviticus does treat jumpers as a distinguished sub-category with their saltatorial hind legs as the marker. If Deuteronomy 14:19 is using sheretz ha-of in that restricted Levitical sense, the laws need not conflict.

The weakness is that this requires Deuteronomy to silently deploy the same technical taxonomy as the Priestly source while omitting the very exception that taxonomy exists to specify. Deuteronomy''s dietary code (14:3-21) is conspicuously simpler and broader than Leviticus 11—it lists clean and unclean quadrupeds and the fish criterion, names forbidden birds, and then dismisses winged insects in a single sweeping clause with no jumping-insect carve-out. Tigay''s JPS commentary notes precisely this: Deuteronomy''s list streamlines the Priestly system and drops categories. The omission is significant, because eating locusts was a real and enduring practice—attested across the ancient Near East, presupposed in Leviticus'' own exception, and reflected later in the Gospel notice that John the Baptist ate "locusts and wild honey" (Mark 1:6; Matt 3:4). A law that forbade all flying insects without exception would be at odds with a known and permitted food.

The more economical reading is that Deuteronomy 14 is a Deuteronomistic simplification of the dietary laws, derived from but not identical to Leviticus 11. Weinfeld, Tigay, and Wright treat Deuteronomy 14:3-21 as a D recension of the purity system that did not preserve every Priestly subdivision. Mary Douglas and Walter Houston situate both lists within the broader logic of Israelite classification, but neither erases the difference in scope between the two codes.

The reception history confirms the texts were not trivially compatible. A community holding only Deuteronomy would naturally conclude that all flying insects were forbidden; one holding only Leviticus would know the locust exception. The Mishnah (m. Hullin 3:7) had to specify the marks of a kosher locust to coordinate the canon''s two voices—harmonizing labor that would be unnecessary had the texts plainly agreed.

Verdict: partially harmonizable but overstated as a flat contradiction. The narrow-category reading is grammatically possible and is anchored in Leviticus 11:20-23''s own sub-classification, so a harmonizing reader can hold the laws together; but it must read Deuteronomy''s silence as tacit agreement with a Priestly taxonomy Deuteronomy nowhere states, and source criticism more simply explains the difference as a Deuteronomic streamlining of the Levitical dietary code.', 'Jacob Milgrom, *Leviticus 1-16* (Anchor Bible 3; Doubleday, 1991), pp. 665-668, on Leviticus 11:20-23 and the locust exception
Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1996), pp. 138-140, on Deuteronomy 14:19
Moshe Weinfeld, *Deuteronomy and the Deuteronomic School* (Oxford University Press, 1972), on the Deuteronomic recension of the dietary laws at Deuteronomy 14:3-21
Walter J. Houston, *Purity and Monotheism: Clean and Unclean Animals in Biblical Law* (JSOTSup 140; Sheffield Academic Press, 1993), on the relation of Leviticus 11 and Deuteronomy 14
Peter C. Craigie, *The Book of Deuteronomy* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 1976), on the narrow-category reading of Deuteronomy 14:19
Christopher J. H. Wright, *Deuteronomy* (NIBCOT; Hendrickson, 1996), on Deuteronomy 14:3-21 as a reworking of Leviticus 11
Mary Douglas, *Leviticus as Literature* (Oxford University Press, 1999), on the classificatory logic of Leviticus 11:20-23', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Leviticus, Deuteronomy', 6, 2, 1, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The strongest apologetic harmonization, developed by Gleason Archer in his *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* and by the contributors to *Hard Sayings of the Bible*, has three moving parts. First, Samuel was a divinely commissioned prophet operating in an unsettled premonarchic period before any central altar existed; the patriarchs (Abraham, Isaac, Jacob) sacrificed freely, and Samuel continues that pattern. Second, 1 Chronicles 6:33-38 (MT 6:18-23) reckons Samuel''s line through Kohath, making him a Levite after all, so he is not strictly a ''stranger.'' Third, the Deuteronomistic Historian himself concedes that high-place sacrifice was tolerated ''because there was no house built unto the name of the LORD'' (1 Kings 3:2), so the centralization restriction simply was not yet in force.

The high-place point carries genuine exegetical weight — the bamot were demonstrably in legitimate use throughout the premonarchic and early monarchic periods (Samuel at Mizpah and Ramah, Elijah at Carmel, Solomon at Gibeon), and the Deuteronomistic narrator does not condemn them before the temple. The genealogical retrojection in Chronicles is likewise a recognized phenomenon. But the harmonization founders on what the Priestly texts actually say, and it is worth being precise about why the conceded force of the bamot point does not rescue it. The toleration of high places is a Deuteronomistic datum drawn from the book of Kings; it is a concession internal to that corpus''s own theology of centralization-once-the-temple-exists. The Priestly and Holiness laws in Numbers 18 and Leviticus 17 operate on entirely different premises and attach no temporal qualifier. They do not say ''until the temple is built''; Leviticus 17:7 grounds the rule in a permanent statute ''throughout their generations,'' and the penalties — death for the encroaching layman, karet for the unauthorized slaughterer — are framed as unconditional and immediate. One cannot import the Deuteronomistic loophole into the Priestly law that explicitly excludes it; the two corpora simply do not share the assumption that local sacrifice was ever legitimate. That is precisely why the harmonization that works within the Deuteronomistic History does not bridge to the Priestly material — and why the verdict is non-harmonization rather than partial harmonization.

Critical scholarship reads this as a textbook source seam. Numbers 18 and Leviticus 17 are Priestly/Holiness material reflecting a centralizing cultic ideology with the Aaronide priesthood at its apex; the Samuel narratives are older traditions embedded in the Deuteronomistic History that presuppose a decentralized cult with sacrifice at many local sites. Julius Wellhausen''s *Prolegomena* set out the basic developmental scheme, in which P''s centralization is a late projection backward; Israel Knohl''s *The Sanctuary of Silence* refined the relative dating of P and H without overturning the core insight that the Priestly vision projects later cultic centralization back into the Mosaic age. P. Kyle McCarter''s Anchor Bible *I Samuel* treats Samuel''s Mizpah offering as wholly unremarkable within its own narrative world — precisely because that world has not yet imagined the Aaronide monopoly. Jacob Milgrom''s and Baruch Levine''s Anchor Bible volumes on Leviticus and Numbers locate the encroachment-and-slaughter statutes squarely within the Priestly system, treating the death penalty for lay encroachment as constitutive of P''s theology of graded holiness.

The Chronicler''s move is itself the best evidence that ancient editors felt the problem. By relocating Samuel into a Levitical genealogy, 1 Chronicles 6 quietly resolves the difficulty the older Ephraimite framing of 1 Samuel 1:1 creates — but the two cannot both be the original datum, and the Ephraimite notice is the earlier text. Reading editorial intent always carries some subjectivity, yet the direction of harmonization (lay Ephraimite becomes Levite) points one way and reflects a postexilic anxiety to fit Samuel''s recognized cultic role into the now-canonical Aaronide-Levitical system. The tension is not dissolved by appeal to prophetic exception, because the Priestly law admits no such exception.

Verdict: genuine contradiction, generated by competing source traditions over who could legitimately approach the altar.', 'Julius Wellhausen, *Prolegomena to the History of Israel* (A. & C. Black, 1885), on the late dating of the Priestly cultic centralization.
Israel Knohl, *The Sanctuary of Silence: The Priestly Torah and the Holiness School* (Fortress, 1995), on the Holiness School''s redaction of Priestly law.
P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *I Samuel* (Anchor Bible 8; Doubleday, 1980), on 1 Samuel 7:9.
Jacob Milgrom, *Leviticus 17-22* (Anchor Bible 3A; Doubleday, 2000), on Leviticus 17:3-4.
Baruch A. Levine, *Numbers 1-20* (Anchor Bible 4; Doubleday, 1993), on Numbers 18:7.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), on Samuel''s sacrifice at Mizpah.
Walter C. Kaiser et al., *Hard Sayings of the Bible* (InterVarsity, 1996), on pre-temple high-place sacrifice.', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Leviticus, Numbers, 1 Samuel', 6, 2, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard contextual harmonization — found in Geisler and Howe''s *When Critics Ask* and in virtually every critical commentary alike — is the correct reading here, which is why this is a case of genuine resolution rather than special pleading. Isaiah 1 is a unified prophetic indictment. The Hebrew of v. 13 does not condemn the Sabbath as an institution; it condemns the *cultic act performed by the unjust*. The decisive evidence is internal: the very next breath (vv. 16-17) issues the positive demand — "cease to do evil, learn to do well, seek judgment, relieve the oppressed, judge the fatherless, plead for the widow." The complaint is that bloody hands (v. 15, "your hands are full of blood") render the offerings repugnant, not that offerings and Sabbaths are themselves wrong. This is the well-attested prophetic-cultic critique that runs through Amos 5:21-24 ("I hate, I despise your feast days"), Hosea 6:6 ("I desired mercy, and not sacrifice"), Micah 6:6-8, and Jeremiah 7. John Goldingay, Joseph Blenkinsopp''s Anchor Bible *Isaiah 1-39*, and Brevard Childs all read Isaiah 1:13 as rhetorical hyperbole targeting ritual hollowed of ethics. Isaiah is no cessationist: the same book blesses the Sabbath-keeper in 56:2. So the OT-internal contradiction the entry advances is weak.

Where a real and sharper contradiction lives is in territory the entry''s framing never reaches — the New Testament''s relativization of the very obligation the Torah calls perpetual. Paul tells the Colossians (2:16-17) to let no one judge them "in respect of an holyday, or of the new moon, or of the sabbath days," which he relegates to "a shadow of things to come." Romans 14:5 makes the matter adiaphora: "One man esteemeth one day above another: another esteemeth every day alike. Let every man be fully persuaded in his own mind." Hebrews 4:9-10 transmutes the Sabbath into an eschatological "rest" entered by faith rather than a weekly cessation of labor. James Dunn''s NIGTC *Colossians* reads 2:16 as Paul''s deliberate dismantling of the calendar obligations of the Mosaic law for gentile believers. This produces the genuine internal-Bible tension: a Torah and prophetic tradition that calls Sabbath an "everlasting" sign of the covenant (Exodus 31:16-17) standing against a Pauline-Hebrews stream that treats its observance as optional and superseded. Christian apologists who invoke Isaiah 56 to defend a perpetual Sabbath while invoking Paul to set it aside are running both sides of that contradiction at once.

The usual harmonization of the NT side — that Christ "fulfills" the Sabbath, transferring its substance to a new-covenant rest — is theologically coherent within a supersessionist framework but does not erase the legal-textual fact that the Torah marked the institution as non-terminable. "Perpetual" (Hebrew ʿolam, Exodus 31:16) is precisely the word a later relativization has to reinterpret.

Verdict: overstated as cited (Isaiah 1:13 is hyperbolic prophetic critique, harmonizable with Sabbath law), but a stronger, unaddressed contradiction stands between the Torah''s perpetual Sabbath and Pauline-Hebrews freedom from it.', 'Norman L. Geisler and Thomas Howe, *When Critics Ask* (Victor Books, 1992), on Isaiah 1:13.
John Goldingay, *Isaiah* (New International Biblical Commentary; Hendrickson, 2001), on Isaiah 1:13-17.
Joseph Blenkinsopp, *Isaiah 1-39* (Anchor Bible 19; Doubleday, 2000), on Isaiah 1:13.
Brevard S. Childs, *Isaiah* (Old Testament Library; Westminster John Knox, 2001), on Isaiah 1:13.
Jacob Milgrom, *Leviticus 23-27* (Anchor Bible 3B; Doubleday, 2001), on Leviticus 23:3.
James D. G. Dunn, *The Epistle to the Colossians and to Philemon* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 1996), on Colossians 2:16-17.', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Exodus, Leviticus, Deuteronomy, Isaiah', 6, 2, 3, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

The core of the harmonization, advanced by D. A. Carson, R. T. France''s NICNT *Matthew*, and Craig Blomberg, turns on the semantic range of Greek krinō, which spans ''to evaluate/discern,'' ''to decide,'' and ''to condemn'' — much as English ''judge'' does. The immediate Matthean context settles which sense is in view: the same Sermon that says ''judge not'' in 7:1 commands believers in 7:6 not to give what is holy ''unto the dogs'' nor cast pearls ''before swine,'' and in 7:15 to ''beware of false prophets'' — both of which require precisely the discerning evaluation 7:1 supposedly forbids. The resolution is that 7:1 targets censorious, self-exalting condemnation, not moral discernment. John 7:24 makes the distinction explicit in a single verse: ''Judge not according to the appearance, but judge righteous judgment'' — superficial condemnation is barred, righteous evaluation commanded. Davies and Allison''s ICC *Matthew* defend this reading, as does Hans Dieter Betz''s Hermeneia *Sermon on the Mount*. On the Pauline side, Anthony Thiselton''s NIGTC *1 Corinthians* distinguishes the eschatological verdict on hidden motives that Paul defers in 1 Corinthians 4:5 (''judge nothing before the time'') from the present-tense disciplinary action Paul mandates in 5:12 (''do not ye judge them that are within?''). These are different objects: ultimate verdicts on the heart belong to God; ordering the visible life of the community belongs to the church. The lexical and contextual work genuinely softens the contradiction.

Where the tension does not fully dissolve is at the level of moral consistency in application. Matthew 7:1-2 does not merely forbid hypocrisy; it grounds the prohibition in a reciprocal principle — ''with what measure ye mete, it shall be measured to you again'' — that reads as a general norm, not a narrow rule about the censorious. And Paul''s handling of the Corinthian offender is hard to square with his counsel to the Romans. In 1 Corinthians 5:3-5 he has ''judged already, as though I were present,'' the man who has his father''s wife, and commands the assembly to ''deliver such an one unto Satan'' — a verdict pronounced at a distance, on a single report, with severe consequence. That is not gentle discernment; it is sentence. The same Paul who tells the Romans ''why dost thou judge thy brother?'' (14:10) judges a brother in Corinth from Ephesus. Dale Allison, in *The Sermon on the Mount*, observes the standing tension between Jesus'' radical prohibition and the early communities'' practical need for discipline, and grants that the churches were already negotiating it in real time. It is worth noting that Romans 14 and 1 Corinthians 5 address different objects too — disputable matters of conscience (food, days) in Romans versus flagrant public immorality in Corinth — which softens but does not erase the inconsistency, since the underlying principle Jesus articulates is framed as general.

Verdict: weak-to-moderate, and largely a translation-and-context issue. The semantic range of krinō and the immediate contexts resolve most of the surface collision, distinguishing forbidden condemnation from commanded discernment; a residual ethical inconsistency remains in how Paul himself applies the principle.', 'D. A. Carson, *The Sermon on the Mount: An Evangelical Exposition of Matthew 5-7* (Baker, 1978), on Matthew 7:1-6.
R. T. France, *The Gospel of Matthew* (NICNT; Eerdmans, 2007), on Matthew 7:1.
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew* (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988-1997), on Matthew 7:1.
