Dale C. Allison, *The Sermon on the Mount: Inspiring the Moral Imagination* (Crossroad, 1999), on Matthew 7:1-2.
Hans Dieter Betz, *The Sermon on the Mount* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1995), on Matthew 7:1.', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Leviticus, Matthew, Luke, John, Romans, 1 Corinthians, James', 6, 2, 3, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard harmonization is the Semitic-idiom defense, deployed by D. A. Carson, Darrell Bock''s BECNT *Luke*, and most Lukan commentators, and it has real linguistic warrant. The Greek miseō here renders an underlying Semitic sanēʾ that frequently functions comparatively — "to love less," "to prefer over." Genesis 29:30-31 is the paradigm: Jacob "loved also Rachel more than Leah," and the very next verse says Leah was "hated" (senûʾah) — clearly meaning less-loved, not despised, since she is simultaneously the bearer of his firstborn. Deuteronomy 21:15-17 uses the identical "loved/hated" pairing for two wives in a purely comparative legal sense. Decisively, Matthew 10:37 preserves what is almost certainly the same Q logion in the softer comparative form: "He that loveth father or mother more than me is not worthy of me." Joseph Fitzmyer''s Anchor Bible *Luke* and François Bovon''s Hermeneia *Luke* both acknowledge the comparative function of miseō in 14:26, and the Matthean parallel suggests that Matthew (or his tradition) already heard the absoluteness of "hate" as needing rephrasing. At the lexical level, then, the contradiction softens substantially: Luke 14:26 demands that allegiance to Jesus outrank every kinship tie, not literal emotional hatred.

What the idiom defense cannot fully neutralize is the rhetorical and theological force of Jesus'' kinship teaching across the tradition. Even granting "love less," the demand is for a radical subordination — and elsewhere outright rupture — of family bonds that the Levitical and Johannine love-ethic frames as incompatible with covenant love. John Meier''s *A Marginal Jew* (vol. 1) argues that the historical Jesus genuinely demanded a break with conventional kinship structures: "let the dead bury their dead" (Matthew 8:21-22), the redefinition of family in Mark 3:31-35 where Jesus sets aside his mother and brothers ("Whosoever shall do the will of God, the same is my brother"), and the explicit prediction of family-against-family division in Matthew 10:34-36 ("I came not to send peace, but a sword"). Dale Allison''s *Constructing Jesus* corroborates the disruptive social profile of the early Jesus movement. So the entry is reaching for something real: the movement''s ethic genuinely strained against the household-centered piety of its world. And 1 John 3:15 — equating hatred of a brother with murder, denying such a one eternal life — leaves no semantic slack; Raymond Brown''s Anchor Bible *Epistles of John* reads it as a flat absolute. There is no smooth path from "hate father and mother" to "love thy neighbour as thyself" without interpretive labor on the verb.

Verdict: moderate contradiction. The Semitic-comparative reading, secured by Genesis 29, Deuteronomy 21, and the Matthew 10:37 parallel, resolves the surface tension — apologists are right that miseō is hyperbolic and comparative. But the underlying ethic of kinship-rupture in the Jesus tradition still pulls against the Levitical-Johannine love command once one stops softening the Greek; critics are right about that remainder.', 'D. A. Carson, *The Sermon on the Mount: An Evangelical Exposition of Matthew 5-7* (Baker, 1978), on the comparative sense of ''hate.''
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (Anchor Bible 28A; Doubleday, 1985), on Luke 14:26.
François Bovon, *Luke 2: A Commentary on the Gospel of Luke 9:51-19:27* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2013), on Luke 14:26.
John P. Meier, *A Marginal Jew, Volume One: The Roots of the Problem and the Person* (Doubleday, 1991), on Jesus and family relations.
Dale C. Allison, *Constructing Jesus: Memory, Imagination, and History* (Baker Academic, 2010), on the kinship-disrupting demands of Jesus.
Raymond E. Brown, *The Epistles of John* (Anchor Bible 30; Doubleday, 1982), on 1 John 3:15 and 4:20-21.
Darrell L. Bock, *Luke 9:51-24:53* (Baker Exegetical Commentary; Baker, 1996), on Luke 14:26.', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Leviticus, Luke, 1 John', 6, 2, 3, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The strongest harmonization works the covenantal and genre angles. Paul Copan (*Is God a Moral Monster?*), Christopher Wright (*The God I Don''t Understand*), and Norman Geisler argue that Deuteronomy 13 is a unique theocratic statute for Israel as a covenant-bound nation, not a perpetual ethical norm binding on individuals or on the church. Bernard Levinson and Jeffrey Tigay both situate it firmly within the ancient Near Eastern vassal-treaty tradition: in the Vassal Treaties of Esarhaddon and earlier Hittite treaties, incitement to break loyalty to the suzerain — including the duty to report and even kill a family member who conspires against the great king — is a capital matter. Deuteronomy reframes YHWH as the divine suzerain and apostasy as treason; the death penalty fits that genre. On this reading, the Sinai covenant constituted a holy polity charged with maintaining cultic fidelity, whereas the new covenant in Christ shifts enforcement from the civil sword to persuasion, which is why the apostolic writings move to love-of-enemy ethics and limit "separation" (2 Corinthians 6) to cultic non-entanglement rather than hostility. This genre identification and the canonical distinction between theocratic civil law and church discipline have real merit and keep the texts from a flat contradiction.

Where the harmonization runs into difficulty is the moral-philosophical level. If "thine hand shall be first upon him to put him to death" was ever the divinely right response to a family member''s religious dissent, then either the moral standard changed or it was never absolute — and either horn strains the claim of a unified, immutable divine character. C. S. Cowles, in *Show Them No Mercy*, concedes the difficulty and takes a near-Marcionite route: Jesus reveals what God is truly like, and the Deuteronomic herem reflects accommodation to Iron Age categories rather than God''s settled will. Eric Seibert, in *Disturbing Divine Behavior*, presses the same move further, distinguishing the "textual God" from the "actual God." The cost is explicit: the Deuteronomic author is conceded to have misrepresented God. Christopher Wright resists that concession and tries to hold both — Deuteronomy 13 was right then, the cross is right now — but this leaves a deity whose ethics are situationally relative, which is its own theological problem and does not so much resolve the contradiction as relocate it from the text to the doctrine of God.

Verdict: genuine contradiction at the ethical level, partially masked by the covenantal-genre framing. The vassal-treaty identification and the theocracy/church distinction save the passages from direct collision, but only by conceding that what counts as faithful treatment of the unbeliever shifted from execution to love — a shift that the harmonization purchases at the price of either a changing or an accommodated divine ethic.', 'Bernard M. Levinson, *Deuteronomy and the Hermeneutics of Legal Innovation* (Oxford University Press, 1997), on Deuteronomy 13.
Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1996), on Deuteronomy 13:6-10.
Paul Copan, *Is God a Moral Monster?* (Baker, 2011), on the theocratic context of Deuteronomy 13.
Christopher J. H. Wright, *The God I Don''t Understand* (Zondervan, 2008), on Old Testament law and divine character.
Eric A. Seibert, *Disturbing Divine Behavior* (Fortress, 2009), on accommodation in violent Old Testament texts.
C. S. Cowles et al., *Show Them No Mercy: 4 Views on God and Canaanite Genocide* (Zondervan, 2003), on the moral problem of commanded killing.', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Leviticus, Deuteronomy, Matthew, Mark, Luke, Romans, 2 Corinthians, Galatians, James', 6, 2, 3, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

First, the passage cited against Leviticus 20:10 is almost certainly not original to the Gospel of John. The pericope adulterae (John 7:53-8:11) is absent from the earliest and best Greek witnesses — Papyrus 66 and Papyrus 75 (both later second/early third century), Codex Sinaiticus, and Codex Vaticanus — and from the early Syriac, Coptic (Sahidic), and several Old Latin traditions. Where it does appear in later manuscripts it floats: some place it after John 7:52, some after John 7:36 or 21:25, and a family of minuscules inserts it into Luke after 21:38. Its vocabulary and style are markedly un-Johannine. Bruce Metzger''s *Textual Commentary* assigns the omission an {A} certainty and the Committee printed the passage in double brackets; the NA28 critical apparatus likewise marks it as a later insertion. Bart Ehrman (*Misquoting Jesus*) and Chris Keith (*The Pericope Adulterae, the Gospel of John, and the Literacy of Jesus*) accept the manuscript verdict: a free-floating oral tradition, plausibly authentic in some kernel, that was secondarily woven into John. To cite it as Scripture''s verdict on adultery against Leviticus 20:10 is therefore to cite a text the critical consensus regards as a later interpolation — a fact the entry''s framing passes over.

Second, granting the passage as canonical (as it functions in the received text and most printed Bibles), the strongest harmonization comes from D. A. Carson and, similarly, Andreas Köstenberger''s BECNT *John*: Jesus does not abrogate the Mosaic penalty but exposes a procedural defect. The Torah''s own evidentiary rule (Deuteronomy 17:7) requires that "the hands of the witnesses shall be first upon him to put him to death," and Leviticus 20:10 demands the death of *both* parties — yet only the woman is produced, the male partner conspicuously absent though the act was "in the very act." The accusers are explicitly described as "tempting him, that they might have to accuse him" (John 8:6), so the case is a legal trap, not a regular prosecution. "He that is without sin among you, let him first cast a stone" can be read as challenging the moral standing of the would-be executioner-witnesses. On this reading Jesus operates within the law rather than against it, and it is a defensible construal of the narrative as it stands.

What even Carson''s reading leaves unresolved is the remainder in Jesus'' own words. He does not say, "this prosecution is procedurally defective; retry it with proper witnesses and the second offender." He says, "Neither do I condemn thee: go, and sin no more" (8:11). That is a refusal to apply the penalty, not merely a delay of it, and the story has functioned in Christian tradition precisely as a softening of Levitical severity. James Dunn, in *Jesus Remembered*, observes that whatever the historical Jesus taught about adultery — and Matthew 5:27-30 internalizes and intensifies the standard rather than abolishing the external sanction — the cultural memory of Jesus that generated and preserved this pericope was already moving away from capital punishment. The Levitical death penalty for adultery left no significant trace in early Christian practice.

Verdict: structurally moderate but textually weak. The contradiction leans on a passage the critical consensus considers non-original to John; granting the passage, the procedural-defect harmonization is reasonable yet does not erase the de facto suspension of the death penalty. The honest core is that early Christianity, whoever authored the pericope, was already abandoning Levitical execution for adultery.', 'Bruce M. Metzger, *A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament*, 2nd ed. (United Bible Societies, 1994), on John 7:53-8:11.
Bart D. Ehrman, *Misquoting Jesus: The Story Behind Who Changed the Bible and Why* (HarperOne, 2005), on the pericope adulterae.
Chris Keith, *The Pericope Adulterae, the Gospel of John, and the Literacy of Jesus* (Brill, 2009), on the textual transmission of the passage.
D. A. Carson, *The Gospel According to John* (Eerdmans, 1991), on John 8:3-11.
Andreas J. Köstenberger, *John* (BECNT; Baker Academic, 2004), on John 8:3-11.
James D. G. Dunn, *Jesus Remembered* (Eerdmans, 2003), on Jesus'' teaching on adultery.
Jacob Milgrom, *Leviticus 17-22* (Anchor Bible 3A; Doubleday, 2000), on Leviticus 20:10.', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Leviticus, John', 6, 2, 3, 3, 1, 'John 7:53-8:11 (pericope adulterae) is absent from the earliest and best manuscripts (P66, P75, Sinaiticus, Vaticanus) and floats to varying locations, judged a later insertion by critical consensus.', 'Metzger, Textual Commentary on the Greek NT, 2nd ed.; NA28 apparatus at John 7:53-8:11');

The identification of qedeshim with male homosexual temple prostitutes is a translation tradition, propagated through the Septuagint''s renderings and codified by the King James translators, who read their own moral categories back into an obscure cultic term. Modern critical scholarship has largely abandoned that equation. Phyllis Bird''s study ''The End of the Male Cult Prostitute'' argues that the evidence for qedeshim as homosexual prostitutes is wholly inferential and overdetermined; Mayer Gruber''s work on the qedeshah and her cognates shows that the Hebrew and Akkadian terms denote a cultic office whose duties are not demonstrably sexual, let alone homosexual. Saul Olyan''s analysis of Leviticus 18:22 and 20:13 treats those verses as addressing a sex act between men in their own right, with no necessary connection to cult prostitution. The upshot is that 1 Kings 15:12 and Leviticus 20:13 are not talking about the same thing: the Kings text reports the removal of syncretistic cultic functionaries (the Deuteronomistic Historian''s recurring concern with purging foreign worship — compare Jehoshaphat removing the remnant of the qedeshim in 1 Kings 22:46, and Josiah breaking down the houses of the qedeshim in 2 Kings 23:7), while the Leviticus text legislates a sexual prohibition. Even Robert Gagnon, writing from a conservative position in *The Bible and Homosexual Practice*, agrees that the two verses operate on different objects. This is why the contradiction as cited dissolves: it is built on a KJV lexical artifact.

There is, however, a genuine tension in the vicinity that the entry''s chosen verse fails to demonstrate. Nowhere in the Deuteronomistic History or Chronicles is a single Israelite executed for same-sex intercourse under Leviticus 20:13. The narrative response to the qedeshim is consistently expulsion (Asa, Jehoshaphat) or destruction of cultic infrastructure (Josiah), never capital punishment of individuals for the sex act per se. Jacob Milgrom argues in his Anchor Bible *Leviticus* that the Holiness Code''s death penalties were often more rhetorical-legislative — declaring the gravity of an offense — than judicially executed, and the broader observation that biblical capital statutes rarely show evidence of enforcement is well established. So the tension the entry gropes toward — between the lethal Levitical statute and the milder, non-capital practice recorded in the historical books — is real; 1 Kings 15:12 is simply the wrong hook for it, because that verse is not about the Levitical offense at all. Mordechai Cogan''s Anchor Bible *I Kings* reads Asa''s removal of the qedeshim straightforwardly as a measure of cultic reform, not as the punishment of a sexual offense.

Verdict: weak as cited, owing to the qedeshim/''sodomites'' mistranslation that modern critical scholarship has set aside. A distinct and more defensible tension exists between the Holiness Code''s death penalty and the narrative books'' actual practice, but it is not demonstrated by the verse the entry pairs against Leviticus 20:13.', 'Phyllis A. Bird, "The End of the Male Cult Prostitute: A Literary-Historical and Sociological Analysis of Hebrew Qadesh-Qedeshim," in *Congress Volume Cambridge 1995*, ed. J. A. Emerton (VTSup 66; Brill, 1997), on the meaning of qedeshim.
Saul M. Olyan, "''And with a Male You Shall Not Lie the Lying Down of a Woman'': On the Meaning and Significance of Leviticus 18:22 and 20:13," *Journal of the History of Sexuality* 5 (1994), on Leviticus 20:13.
Mayer I. Gruber, "Hebrew Qedesah and Her Canaanite and Akkadian Cognates," *Ugarit-Forschungen* 18 (1986), on the cultic term qedeshah/qedeshim.
Jacob Milgrom, *Leviticus 17-22* (Anchor Bible 3A; Doubleday, 2000), on Leviticus 20:13.
Robert A. J. Gagnon, *The Bible and Homosexual Practice* (Abingdon, 2001), on the qedeshim and Leviticus 20:13.
Mordechai Cogan, *I Kings* (Anchor Bible 10; Doubleday, 2001), on 1 Kings 15:12.', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Leviticus, 1 Kings', 6, 3, 4, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard apologetic harmonization, advanced by Gleason Archer and by R. K. Harrison''s Tyndale *Leviticus*, distinguishes the two as different scenarios: Leviticus 15:24 covers inadvertent contact (the man did not know, or the period began unexpectedly during intercourse), while 20:18 covers the deliberate act, signaled by the verb galah ("to uncover") and the phrase "he hath discovered her fountain," taken to imply willful exposure. The distinction is not invented from nothing — early rabbinic interpretation faced the same tension. The Mishnah lists intercourse with a niddah among the karet offenses (cf. Keritot 1:1), and the rabbinic tradition develops the inadvertent/intentional distinction within the framework of sin-offering versus karet liability, which Jacob Milgrom notes in his Anchor Bible *Leviticus 17-22*. So the harmonizing move has a long pedigree.

What the distinction cannot overcome is the source-critical evidence that Leviticus 15 and Leviticus 20 belong to different layers of the Priestly material. Leviticus 15 is part of the older Priestly purity code (P), which handles menstrual contact as a matter of ritual impurity managed by waiting periods and washing — a contamination, not a crime. Leviticus 20 belongs to the Holiness Code (H), which Israel Knohl (*The Sanctuary of Silence*) and Jacob Milgrom argue is a later stratum that systematically re-frames Priestly purity matters as moral-covenantal violations bearing the severe sanctions of karet or death. Knohl''s specific thesis is that H takes P''s ritual categories and re-reads them as offenses against the holiness of the land and people. On this analysis the two texts are not addressing "different scenarios" at all; they are two legal traditions legislating the same scenario, with H escalating P''s penalty. Decisively, the harmonizing distinction has no textual anchor in 15:24 itself: nothing in that verse signals inadvertence, surprise, or lack of intent. The word "inadvertent" must be supplied to save the consistency. The galah/"discovered her fountain" language of 20:18 is the ordinary idiom for sexual relations throughout Leviticus 18 and 20, not a special marker of premeditation absent in chapter 15.

The rabbinic resolution thus performs precisely the harmonizing function Knohl identifies as covering the seam between the two strata — a coherent later systematization, but one that presupposes the unity it sets out to demonstrate. Verdict: genuine contradiction within Leviticus, partially resolvable if one stipulates an inadvertent/willful distinction the text does not state, and most economically explained by the Holiness Code''s redactional escalation of the older Priestly purity rule.', 'Israel Knohl, *The Sanctuary of Silence: The Priestly Torah and the Holiness School* (Fortress, 1995), on the Holiness School''s reframing of Priestly purity law.
Jacob Milgrom, *Leviticus 1-16* (Anchor Bible 3; Doubleday, 1991), on Leviticus 15:24.
Jacob Milgrom, *Leviticus 17-22* (Anchor Bible 3A; Doubleday, 2000), on Leviticus 20:18.
Tikva Frymer-Kensky, *Reading the Women of the Bible* (Schocken, 2002), on menstrual impurity in the Priestly law.
R. K. Harrison, *Leviticus* (Tyndale Old Testament Commentary; InterVarsity, 1980), on Leviticus 15:24 and 20:18.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), on Leviticus 15:24 and 20:18.', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Leviticus', 6, 2, 1, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Two harmonizations dominate. The Reformed version, developed at length in D. A. Carson''s *The Difficult Doctrine of the Love of God*, distinguishes multiple senses of divine love and argues that God can hold a general, providential benevolence toward all creation (John 3:16) while reserving a special elective love for the elect; "hating" Esau then means preterition — passing him over for covenant love — not emotional malice. The second, the Semitic-comparative reading favored by Walter Kaiser and others, treats "hate" (Hebrew sanēʾ, Greek miseō) in Malachi 1:3 and Romans 9:13 as "love less" or "reject as covenant heir," the same idiom seen in Genesis 29:30-31 (Leah "hated" = less-loved) and Deuteronomy 21:15-17 (the law of the "hated" wife''s firstborn). Both moves have real warrant for the texts they fit. The comparative use of sanēʾ is well documented, and James Dunn''s WBC *Romans 9-16* together with Douglas Moo''s NICNT *Romans* both read Romans 9:13 as a statement about the election of Jacob/Israel over Esau/Edom as peoples — note Malachi addresses Edom as a nation, not Esau the individual — not about God''s emotional disposition toward a man.

The difficulty is that these mechanisms do not cover the full evidentiary base. The categorical Psalms are not elective contrasts: Psalm 5:5 says God hates "all workers of iniquity," Psalm 11:5 that his soul hates "the wicked" and "him that loveth violence," Hosea 9:15 that YHWH came to hate Israel "for the wickedness of their doings." These are straightforward declarations of divine antipathy toward classes of people, with no Jacob-over-Esau comparative structure to relativize them. Patrick Miller''s *Interpreting the Psalms* treats this divine-hatred language as morally charged judgment, not relational shorthand. The comparative reading simply does not reach them.

Not all of the cited proof-texts are equally strong, and intellectual honesty requires saying so. Job 16:9 ("He teareth me in his wrath, who hateth me") is the weakest of the lot: it is not a divine self-declaration nor a narrator''s assertion but a line of Job''s own complaint within the poetic dialogue, a sufferer''s accusatory description of how God seems to be treating him. The book''s own framing (Job 1:1, 8; 42:7) explicitly denies that God hates Job; the prologue presents him as blameless and the epilogue vindicates him against the friends. So Job 16:9 reports a character''s anguished perception, not the text''s theological claim, and a critic should not lean on it. The core of the tension does not need it.

Meanwhile 1 John 4:8 is not one mode of love among several but an ontological predication — "God is love" (ho theos agapē estin) — which Raymond Brown''s Anchor Bible *Epistles of John* reads as identifying love with the divine nature itself, not merely describing one of God''s activities. The Reformed harmonization preserves both poles, but at the cost of asserting that God simultaneously loves and hates the same persons in different senses, which is coherent only inside the prior dogmatic framework and is not self-evident from the texts. Jon Levenson''s *The Death and Resurrection of the Beloved Son* situates the Jacob-over-Esau motif within the wider biblical pattern of the chosen and rejected son, which lends weight to the elective reading of Malachi/Romans precisely by showing it is about lineage and inheritance rather than affect.

One of the entry''s proof-texts lies outside the Protestant canon: "Wisdom 14:9" is from the Wisdom of Solomon, a deuterocanonical book, so a Protestant reader may set it aside without touching the argument. The core tension rests entirely on protocanonical material — Psalm 5:5, Psalm 11:5, Hosea 9:15, Malachi 1:3, Romans 9:13 — and survives that subtraction intact.

Verdict: disputed, leaning genuine at the level of biblical-theological coherence. The texts reflect distinct theological streams — a Deuteronomic-Wisdom tradition of divine hatred toward the wicked and a Johannine identification of God with love — that must be harmonized by an external dogmatic scheme rather than read together as a single, natural voice. The comparative reading resolves Romans 9:13 but not the categorical Psalms; the elective-love reading resolves the whole only for those who already grant its framework.', 'D. A. Carson, *The Difficult Doctrine of the Love of God* (Crossway, 2000), on the distinct senses of divine love.
James D. G. Dunn, *Romans 9-16* (Word Biblical Commentary 38B; Word, 1988), on Romans 9:13.
