Raymond E. Brown, *The Epistles of John* (Anchor Bible 30; Doubleday, 1982), on 1 John 4:8 and 4:16.
Jon D. Levenson, *The Death and Resurrection of the Beloved Son* (Yale University Press, 1993), on election and the chosen/rejected son.', 0, NULL, 2, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Leviticus, Job, Psalms, Proverbs, Hosea, Malachi, Wisdom, John, Romans, 1 John', 5, 3, 4, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard harmonization runs along progressive-revelation and differing-scope lines, and it is partly correct. Deuteronomy 23 legislates membership in the wilderness/conquest-era covenant assembly; Leviticus 21 addresses the narrower question of priestly officiants at the altar, not the standing of all eunuchs before God; and Isaiah 56 announces an eschatological reopening of the boundaries of YHWH''s people. Joseph Blenkinsopp''s Anchor Bible *Isaiah 56-66* and Brevard Childs''s OTL *Isaiah* both read Trito-Isaiah as deliberately re-opening the tightly drawn Deuteronomic and Priestly boundaries; Walter Brueggemann concurs that 56:3-5 is a conscious, polemical reversal. The crucial datum, acknowledged across the harmonizing and critical camps alike, is that Isaiah 56 knows precisely what it is contradicting and does so on purpose — it is not addressing an unrelated topic but re-litigating the very exclusion of Deuteronomy 23. By placing the texts on a temporal sequence (wilderness assembly law, then eschatological reversal), the harmonization keeps them from applying simultaneously and so avoids a flat collision.

Where that move incurs a cost is the question of whether Deuteronomy 23 was true when written. If "he that hath his privy member cut off shall not enter into the congregation of the LORD" was YHWH''s word at Moab, then Isaiah 56 announces a change in divine policy — which sits awkwardly with classical theism''s doctrine of divine immutability. If it was not true when written, the Deuteronomist misascribed the exclusion to God. Either horn concedes that the criteria for belonging to YHWH''s people shifted substantively over time, and that concession is precisely the contradiction the entry presses. The Christian appropriation in Acts 8:26-39, where the Ethiopian eunuch is baptized without hesitation, implicitly takes the first option — the exclusion was provisional, now superseded. James Kugel, in *How to Read the Bible*, argues that this layered structure — later texts engaging and revising earlier ones while the editorial frame asserts continuity — is the basic mechanism of biblical theology. Bernard Levinson''s *Legal Revision and Religious Renewal in Ancient Israel* documents Deuteronomy itself revising earlier law in just this way; Trito-Isaiah then performs the same operation on Deuteronomy. The inner-biblical evidence is thus not of a single harmonious voice but of an ongoing legal-theological argument carried out across the canon.

Verdict: genuine contradiction, openly staged by Isaiah 56 as a reversal of Deuteronomic policy. The progressive-revelation harmonization spares the texts a head-on collision by sequencing them, but only by conceding that the boundaries of YHWH''s people were redrawn — which is itself the substance of the contradiction.', 'Joseph Blenkinsopp, *Isaiah 56-66* (Anchor Bible 19B; Doubleday, 2003), on Isaiah 56:3-5.
Bernard M. Levinson, *Legal Revision and Religious Renewal in Ancient Israel* (Cambridge University Press, 2008), on the revision of earlier law by later texts.
James L. Kugel, *How to Read the Bible: A Guide to Scripture, Then and Now* (Free Press, 2007), on inner-biblical interpretation and revision.
Brevard S. Childs, *Isaiah* (Old Testament Library; Westminster John Knox, 2001), on Isaiah 56:3-5.
Jacob Milgrom, *Leviticus 17-22* (Anchor Bible 3A; Doubleday, 2000), on Leviticus 21:17-21.
Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1996), on Deuteronomy 23:1.
Walter Brueggemann, *Isaiah 40-66* (Westminster Bible Companion; Westminster John Knox, 1998), on Isaiah 56:3-5.', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Leviticus, Deuteronomy, Isaiah', 6, 3, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard apologetic harmonization, deployed by R. T. France, D. A. Carson, and Craig Blomberg, distinguishes the statute''s domain. On this reading Leviticus 24 was always a courtroom standard governing proportional judicial penalty — and, crucially, a *limiting* principle that capped retribution at parity rather than licensing escalation — while Jesus targets its misuse as a charter for private vengeance. France''s NICNT *Matthew* develops this in detail; Matthew Henry was making the same move three centuries earlier. The harmonization has real grounding in the Hebrew and comparative material. Jacob Milgrom and Bernard Jackson both argue that talionic formulae in Israelite law functioned to limit blood-feud escalation, and that in practice compensation was frequently monetary rather than literal mutilation. The cuneiform parallels confirm the pattern: in the Laws of Hammurabi (§§196-201) talion applies between social equals but converts to fixed payments across class lines, and the Hittite Laws operate almost entirely through compensation. Raymond Westbrook''s comparative work treats the talionic clause as a ceiling on vengeance, not a mandate to maim. So Jesus'' Matthean correction has a genuine target if first-century hearers were extending the statute to personal retaliation.

Where the harmonization runs short is the rhetorical architecture of Matthew 5. The six antitheses (5:21-48) are structured "You have heard that it was said... but I say unto you," and the thing said is the Torah text itself — "an eye for an eye" is Scripture, not a rabbinic gloss the evangelist flags as error. Hans Dieter Betz, in his Hermeneia *The Sermon on the Mount*, argues the antitheses do not merely correct misinterpretation; they advance a "higher righteousness" (5:20) that in places revises the Torah''s plain demand. Ulrich Luz, in his Hermeneia *Matthew*, concedes that 5:38-39 raises a genuine question of whether Jesus fulfills or supersedes, and notes the church has negotiated that ambiguity since the second century. The reception history makes the point concrete: Marcion read the antitheses as Jesus replacing the inferior law of the Creator; the Reformers read them as Jesus intensifying and internalizing it. Both readings are textually defensible, which is itself evidence that the text does not settle the matter cleanly.

The sharper difficulty is internal to Matthew. Just twenty verses earlier, 5:17-18 has Jesus declare he came not to abolish (*katalysai*) the Law or the Prophets but to fulfill (*plērōsai*) them, insisting not one jot or tittle will pass away. The antitheses then appear to do precisely what 5:17 disavows — at least with the divorce permission (5:31-32, against Deut 24:1-4) and the talion. Davies and Allison, in their ICC *Matthew*, treat "fulfill" as Jesus bringing the Law to its intended eschatological completion, which absorbs some of the tension; but "resist not evil" plus "turn the other cheek" relocates the moral standard somewhere the talionic ceiling never went — from limiting retaliation to renouncing it. That is not a clarification of Leviticus 24 but a transposition of the whole question from justice to non-resistance.

Verdict: partially harmonizable. The civil-versus-personal distinction genuinely softens the surface clash, and the limiting-principle reading of Leviticus is sound. But the antithetical form, the direct citation of the Torah statute, and the unresolved tension with Matthew 5:17-18 mean Jesus is best read as moving the ethical bar, not merely restating where Moses set it.', 'R. T. France, *The Gospel of Matthew* (NICNT; Eerdmans, 2007), pp. 217-223, on Matthew 5:38-39
Hans Dieter Betz, *The Sermon on the Mount* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1995), pp. 275-285, on Matthew 5:38-39
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 1-7* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 271-282, on Matthew 5:17-18, 38-39
