Raymond Westbrook, ed. (with Gary M. Beckman), *A History of Ancient Near Eastern Law* (Handbook of Oriental Studies; Brill, 2003), pp. 415-436, on ANE talionic formulae', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Leviticus, Matthew', 6, 2, 4, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard apologetic harmonization is the two-senses-of-fear move, deployed by John Calvin, Wayne Grudem, and essentially every post-Reformation systematic theology. Hebrew *yir''ah* (verb *yare''*) and Greek *phobos* span a semantic range from "reverential awe" to "terror." The fear of Proverbs 9:10 — "the beginning of wisdom" — is reverential submission before the Creator, whereas the fear cast out by 1 John 4:18 is the servile dread of punishment that the atonement removes. Believers thus fear God in sense one (awe) and not in sense two (terror). C. S. Lewis works the distinction in *The Problem of Pain*; D. A. Carson develops it in *The Difficult Doctrine of the Love of God*.

This lexical move is real, not ad hoc. Bruce Waltke (NICOT *Proverbs*) and Michael Fox (Anchor Bible *Proverbs*) both show the wisdom tradition''s "fear of YHWH" is weighted heavily toward reverent loyalty and moral submission, not panic — it stands parallel to "knowledge" and "the discipline of wisdom" (Prov 1:7; 15:33), which is incompatible with mere terror. And 1 John 4:18 is precise: it links the fear that love expels with *kolasis* — "punishment, torment" — the same lexeme used for eschatological punishment at Matthew 25:46 (*kolasin aiōnion*, "everlasting punishment"). Raymond Brown''s Anchor Bible *Epistles of John* reads the verse as concerning the believer''s confidence before judgment (cf. 4:17, "boldness in the day of judgment"), i.e. the removal of dread *of condemnation*, not the abolition of reverence. So for the wisdom passages the harmonization has genuine textual purchase.

Where it breaks down is precisely the Synoptic and Hebrews material the entry cites. Matthew 10:28 / Luke 12:5 command fear of "him which is able to destroy both soul and body in hell (*gehenna*)" — the verb is *phobeisthe* and the object is annihilating divine judgment, not contemplative awe. Hebrews 10:31, "a fearful (*phoberon*) thing to fall into the hands of the living God," sits inside the most severe warning passage in the epistle (10:26-31), explicitly invoking "judgment and fiery indignation." Harold Attridge''s Hermeneia *Hebrews* and Luke Timothy Johnson''s NTL *Hebrews* both read these as deliberate fear-of-punishment appeals. That is the very *kolasis*-dread 1 John 4:18 says perfect love eliminates. The two-senses move that rescues Proverbs cannot rescue Matthew 10:28: either it commands the punitive fear love supposedly removes, or one must read "destroy soul and body in hell" as reverential awe, which the language resists.

A further layer the entry rightly flags lies inside the Hebrew Bible. The wisdom passages attach material rewards to fear: Proverbs 22:4 promises "riches, and honour, and life"; 19:23 says the God-fearer "shall not be visited with evil"; 10:27 says it "prolongeth days." Job — whose protagonist is introduced as one who "feared God" (Job 1:1) yet is devastated — and Qoheleth (Eccl 8:14, where the righteous get what the wicked deserve) are sustained internal protests against exactly this retributive calculus. It is worth noting that the entry''s own proof-text Job 16:9 ("He... hateth me") is the weakest member of the fear/hate stream cited elsewhere: it is Job''s anguished complaint, a character''s perception within the dialogue, not a divine or narratorial declaration, and the book''s frame (1:1, 8; 42:7) repudiates the idea that God hates him. Its evidentiary value for any divine-disposition claim is therefore minimal. Choon-Leong Seow''s Anchor Bible *Ecclesiastes* treats Qoheleth as a deliberate destabilizing of the confident wisdom equation. So the tension is not only OT-versus-NT but internal to the wisdom dialogue itself.

Verdict: partially harmonizable, leaning toward genuine. The reverence-vs-dread distinction dissolves the wisdom-literature conflict and the Pauline statements about adoption. It does not neutralize Matthew 10:28 and Hebrews 10:31, which command the fear of punishment that 1 John 4:18 says love casts out — a real cross-corpus theological tension within the New Testament.', 'Bruce K. Waltke, *The Book of Proverbs: Chapters 1-15* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 2004), pp. 100-101, 181, on Proverbs 1:7; 9:10
Michael V. Fox, *Proverbs 1-9* (Anchor Bible 18A; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 67-69, on Proverbs 1:7
Raymond E. Brown, *The Epistles of John* (Anchor Bible 30; Doubleday, 1982), pp. 528-531, on 1 John 4:18
Harold W. Attridge, *The Epistle to the Hebrews* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1989), pp. 290-296, on Hebrews 10:31
Luke Timothy Johnson, *Hebrews: A Commentary* (New Testament Library; Westminster John Knox, 2006), pp. 266-270, on Hebrews 10:31
Choon-Leong Seow, *Ecclesiastes* (Anchor Bible 18C; Doubleday, 1997), pp. 287-289, on Ecclesiastes 8:14
