The decisive evidence is cumulative and internal. There are post-Mosaic notices a Late Bronze Age author could not have written: Genesis 12:6 and 13:7 remark that "the Canaanite was then in the land," presupposing a time when they no longer were; Genesis 36:31 lists Edomite kings "before there reigned any king over the children of Israel," presupposing the Israelite monarchy; Deuteronomy 3:11 treats Og''s bedstead as a museum curiosity; and Numbers 21:14 quotes "the Book of the Wars of the LORD" as an external source. Deuteronomy 34 narrates Moses'' death, burial, and the verdict that "there arose not a prophet since in Israel like unto Moses" (34:10) — a retrospect requiring distance. Beyond anachronism lies the documentary evidence: doublets (two creation accounts, Gen 1 and 2; two interwoven flood narratives; three wife-sister episodes, Gen 12, 20, 26), alternating divine names (YHWH vs. Elohim) that correlate with other stylistic features, and legal codes that diverge (the Covenant Code of Exod 20-23, the Deuteronomic Code of Deut 12-26, and the Priestly legislation of Leviticus).

The traditional defense, made by Gleason Archer (*Survey of Old Testament Introduction*) and Duane Garrett (*Rethinking Genesis*), runs three moves: the post-Mosaic notices are minor scribal updatings of place names; Numbers 12:3 is a later insertion or inspired transcription; and Deuteronomy 34 was appended by a successor — a view already conceded in the Talmud (b. Bava Batra 14b-15a), which assigns the last eight verses to Joshua. These concessions are revealing: once one grants editorial activity for the death notice and the anachronisms, the claim of unitary Mosaic authorship has already been abandoned in substance; what remains is a question of degree.

The critical position is the Documentary Hypothesis and its descendants. Julius Wellhausen''s *Prolegomena* synthesized the four-source model — J (Yahwist), E (Elohist), D (Deuteronomist), P (Priestly) — woven by post-exilic redactors. Contemporary scholarship debates the details vigorously: Joel Baden (*The Composition of the Pentateuch*) defends a renewed Documentary Hypothesis; John Van Seters and others favor supplementary or fragmentary models; the dating of the sources remains contested. But the *multi-authored, multi-stage* character of the Pentateuch is not seriously disputed in mainstream Hebrew Bible scholarship. Richard Elliott Friedman''s *Who Wrote the Bible?* gives the accessible version; James Kugel''s *How to Read the Bible* shows how the doublets and divine-name patterns drive the analysis. Even conservative critical scholars grant layered composition: Kenneth Kitchen (*On the Reliability of the Old Testament*) argues for substantial second-millennium material with later editing, and Garrett concedes editorial layering while resisting Wellhausen''s dating. The full "every word from Moses" position now survives chiefly in apologetic and young-earth circles.

No harmonization rescues unitary Mosaic authorship, because the traditional defenders themselves concede the load-bearing point — that parts of the Pentateuch postdate Moses and reflect later hands. Once Deuteronomy 34 and the anachronistic notices are granted to editors, the doublets, divergent legal codes, and alternating divine names supply an independent and convergent case for composite authorship that no appeal to a single Mosaic pen can absorb.

Verdict: genuine and well-established. Numbers 12:3 alone is a thin reed and the framing undersells the case, but the cumulative textual evidence against unitary Mosaic authorship is overwhelming and represents the settled consensus of critical scholarship, with only dating and source-divisions seriously in dispute.', 'Julius Wellhausen, *Prolegomena to the History of Israel* (Black, 1885), on the documentary sources of the Pentateuch
