Kenneth A. Kitchen, *On the Reliability of the Old Testament* (Eerdmans, 2003), on the dating of Pentateuchal material', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', 'Numbers, Deuteronomy', 7, 3, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The apologetic harmonization is sequential-events theory: Caleb spoke first and alone in 13:30, immediately after the spies'' report; Joshua then joined him in 14:6 once the congregation''s rebellion had escalated to the point of proposing to stone Moses and return to Egypt (14:4, 10). On this reading the two passages capture different moments in a single deteriorating crisis. Gleason Archer, Walter Kaiser, and Timothy Ashley (NICOT *Numbers*) all advance it, and it is textually defensible: 13:30 sits at the report stage, 14:6 at the open-rebellion stage, so a temporal progression — Caleb''s solo counter, then the joint Joshua-Caleb intervention — fits the narrative arc that runs report → rebellion → joint plea → forty-year judgment.

The harmonization works well as a reading of the *final form*. It does not strain the text to picture Joshua joining Caleb only when matters worsened. The difficulty surfaces at the compositional level, where Numbers 13-14 shows clear signs of interwoven traditions. Baruch Levine (Anchor Bible) and Jacob Milgrom (JPS) identify the seams. The spy list in 13:1-16 is Priestly, and it is there — at 13:16 — that "Moses called Hoshea the son of Nun Jehoshua (Joshua)," the editorial renaming that installs the future conquest leader into the scout party. Beneath the Priestly framework runs an older JE narrative in which Caleb appears as the lone dissenting spy. The decisive tell is YHWH''s own verdict: at 14:24 He singles out "my servant Caleb... him will I bring into the land," with no mention of Joshua; Joshua''s exemption is added separately at 14:30 ("save Caleb... and Joshua"), which most source critics read as a Priestly supplement harmonizing the two figures. Most telling of all, the Deuteronomic retelling at Deuteronomy 1:36 names only Caleb as the faithful spy who will enter the land; Joshua appears at 1:38 in a *separate* notice, exempted not as a faithful spy but as Moses'' designated successor. Joel Baden''s *The Composition of the Pentateuch* traces these doublets and argues the original tradition had Caleb as the sole faithful scout, with Joshua''s pairing a later editorial move tying the conquest leader into the wilderness-faithfulness story.

So the two readings operate at different levels and do not cancel each other. As a literary harmonization of the received text, the sequential read is sound; one can narrate 13:30 and 14:6 as successive scenes. But the underlying tradition-history points to a genuine duality: an early Caleb-only account (echoed by Deuteronomy 1:36 and YHWH''s solo commendation of Caleb in 14:24) overlaid by a Priestly account that brings Joshua alongside him.

Verdict: weak-to-moderate, largely a compositional rather than a narrative contradiction. The final form harmonizes as two moments in one crisis; the source-critical evidence and the Caleb-only Deuteronomy retelling indicate the earliest tradition knew only Caleb, with Joshua added later.', 'Baruch A. Levine, *Numbers 1-20* (Anchor Bible 4; Doubleday, 1993), pp. 347-380, on Numbers 13:30; 14:6-9
Jacob Milgrom, *Numbers* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1990), pp. 100-114, on Numbers 13:16; 14:24
