Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1996), pp. 18-20, on Deuteronomy 1:36
Thomas B. Dozeman, *The Pentateuch: Introducing the Torah* (Fortress, 2017), on the composition of Numbers 13-14', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', 'Numbers', 7, 2, 1, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The decisive harmonizing point is internal to Ecclesiastes 11:9 itself, and here the critical and traditional readings converge. The verse does not stop at "walk in the ways of thine heart"; it continues, "but know thou, that for all these things God will bring thee into judgment." That second clause reframes the first. Tremper Longman (NICOT *Ecclesiastes*) and Choon-Leong Seow (Anchor Bible *Ecclesiastes*) both read it as either a sincere balancing qualification or as Qoheleth''s characteristically ironic deflation — the apparent permission set up precisely so the judgment-warning can land. James Crenshaw (OTL *Ecclesiastes*) argues that Qoheleth''s permissions are typically rhetorical scaffolding for the larger frame''s insistence on divine judgment and the limits of human knowledge; Michael Fox (*A Time to Tear Down and a Time to Build Up*) makes the same case. So the verse, read as written, is closer to a warning than a license, and the contradiction with Numbers 15:39 and Proverbs 28:26 largely dissolves once the cherry-picked first clause is read together with its own conclusion.

The two counter-texts also have narrower scope than the framing implies. Numbers 15:39 is a Priestly instruction attached to the law of the *tzitzit* (fringes): the tassels are a mnemonic "that ye seek not after your own heart and your own eyes, after which ye use to go a whoring" — i.e., a command against apostasy and idolatrous wandering, not a general epistemology of the emotions. Proverbs 28:26 and Ecclesiastes 11:9 are, moreover, both wisdom literature; once 11:9 is read with its judgment clause, the two are not even sharply opposed — both counsel against the unreliability of the unexamined heart, in different registers.

What remains genuinely in tension is something the heart-following framing does not actually capture: the broader Qoheleth voice. Even granting that 11:9 is qualified, Ecclesiastes repeatedly commends enjoyment of food, drink, and labor as God''s portion (2:24; 3:13; 5:18-19; 8:15), and its relationship to Torah obedience is famously uneasy — uneasy enough that the rabbis debated withdrawing the book from circulation (b. Shabbat 30b) on account of its internal contradictions, and the orthodox epilogue (12:13-14, "Fear God, and keep his commandments") is widely regarded as a later frame added to bring the work within bounds. Seow and Crenshaw both treat this canonical disputation as evidence that ancient readers themselves felt the friction between Qoheleth''s wisdom-skepticism and the Deuteronomic-prophetic mainstream. But that tension is about the book''s stance toward conventional piety, not about whether one should "follow one''s heart."

Verdict: weak / overstated. The apparent contradiction depends on quoting the first half of Ecclesiastes 11:9 while dropping its own judgment clause; read whole, the verse warns rather than permits, and Numbers 15:39 is a narrower anti-apostasy command. A real tension exists between Qoheleth''s voice and the Deuteronomic mainstream, but the heart-following pairing does not land it.', 'Choon-Leong Seow, *Ecclesiastes* (Anchor Bible 18C; Doubleday, 1997), pp. 348-352, on Ecclesiastes 11:9
Tremper Longman III, *The Book of Ecclesiastes* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 1998), pp. 259-262, on Ecclesiastes 11:9
