The classic harmonization, running from Keil and Delitzsch through Gleason Archer''s *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties*, identifies Moserah as the encampment at the foot of Mount Hor, so that Numbers names the peak and Deuteronomy names the adjacent staging area. The proposal is unfalsifiable but not absurd on its own terms — wilderness toponyms are notoriously unlocatable, and a death ''on'' a mountain could be reported by the camp beneath it. That is the strongest form of the case.

The itineraries, however, refuse to cooperate. Numbers 33:31-38 is a structured travel log, and within it Moseroth and Mount Hor are not adjacent — they are separated by seven intervening stations: from Moseroth the people go to Bene-jaakan, Hor-haggidgad, Jotbathah, Abronah, Ezion-geber, then Kadesh in the wilderness of Zin, and only then to Mount Hor. Deuteronomy 10:6 not only collapses the death into Moserah but reverses the direction of march: it has Israel travel from Bene-jaakan TO Moserah, whereas Numbers 33 has them go from Moseroth (the plural form of the same name) TO Bene-jaakan. This is not a peak-versus-base distinction. It is two itineraries running in opposite directions and placing Aaron''s death at different stops.

The source-critical reading is straightforward and well established. The Numbers death scene belongs to the Priestly source (P), for which the episode is theologically load-bearing: it is the narrative vehicle for the transfer of the high priesthood from Aaron to Eleazar. The vestment-stripping and re-vesting in Numbers 20:28 functions as P''s investiture liturgy cast as narrative — the cultic etiology behind the legitimacy of the Aaronid-Eleazarite line, as Jacob Milgrom emphasizes. Deuteronomy 10:6-7 reads as an intrusive itinerary fragment awkwardly embedded in Moses'' speech; Martin Noth flagged it as a secondary insertion precisely because it does not match the surrounding hortatory material or the Numbers itinerary. Thomas Dozeman and others have argued that the wilderness itineraries themselves carry compositional weight, marking the seams where Priestly and non-Priestly travel traditions were stitched together. Jeffrey Tigay''s JPS commentary on Deuteronomy notes the conflict candidly and does not pretend the two place-names can be simply equated.

To harmonize, one must assume Moserah is loose shorthand for the whole Hor region and silently delete the seven encampments Numbers 33 inserts between Moseroth and Mount Hor — while also explaining away the reversed direction of travel. Read as written, the texts preserve two distinct geographical memories of where the first high priest died, one anchored to a cultic mountain that serves P''s priestly succession theology and one anchored to an itinerary station that the Deuteronomic tradition received independently. Verdict: genuine contradiction, a visible seam between source traditions.', 'Jacob Milgrom, *Numbers* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1990), pp. 169-171, 281-282, on Aaron''s death as a Priestly investiture etiology and the Numbers 33 itinerary.
Martin Noth, *Numbers: A Commentary* (Old Testament Library; Westminster, 1968), pp. 242-247, on the itinerary tradition and Deuteronomy 10:6-7 as a secondary intrusion.
Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1996), pp. 104-106, on the difficulty of Deuteronomy 10:6-7.
