The apologetic reading, advanced by Archer and by Kaiser et al. in Hard Sayings of the Bible, is that Deuteronomy charges Moab and Ammon with the intent and commission to curse, not with a curse that actually took effect. This is grammatically well grounded. The operative form in Deut 23:4 (Eng.) / 23:5 (Heb.) is the piel infinitive construct of qalal with the lamed of purpose — le-qalel, "to curse," i.e. "in order to curse" — naming the purpose of the hire rather than asserting a completed, effective imprecation. The decisive point in favor of the harmonization is the very next verse, Deuteronomy 23:5 (Eng.) / 23:6 (Heb.): "Nevertheless the LORD thy God would not hearken unto Balaam; but the LORD thy God turned the curse into a blessing unto thee, because the LORD thy God loved thee." Far from contradicting Numbers, the Deuteronomist explicitly affirms the Numbers outcome — Yahweh refused to let the curse stand and converted it to blessing. On the question as posed — did Balaam succeed in cursing Israel? — both texts answer no. The indictment in Deuteronomy is the hostile hiring, not a magical success.

This substantially dissolves the surface contradiction. The earlier intuition that the Deuteronomist "clearly believes a curse was uttered and Yahweh transformed it" actually cuts against the contradiction: a curse that Yahweh turns into a blessing is precisely the Numbers picture, where Balaam''s mouth produces blessing instead of cursing. The idiom "turned the curse into a blessing" describes the thwarting of an intended imprecation, not the reversal of one that had already taken hold.

What does not fully harmonize is the historical-traditional substratum. Deuteronomy 23 implicates the Ammonites alongside the Moabites, whereas Numbers 22-24 knows only Balak the Moabite (with Midianite elders in Numbers 22:4, 7), never Ammon. The Deuteronomic tradition is therefore working with an expanded or variant memory of the affair, a point Tigay registers in noting the formulaic pairing of Moab and Ammon as the archetypal hostile neighbors of the Transjordan. Moreover, the Balaam cycle in Numbers is itself composite: the talking-donkey pericope (Numbers 22:22-35) sits in tension with its frame, and the oracles in chapters 23-24 are widely regarded as older independent poetry. That judgment is reinforced by the eighth-century Deir Alla plaster inscription, which attests a non-Israelite seer named Balaam son of Beor functioning in a quite different religious milieu — evidence that the figure circulated in Transjordanian tradition independently of the Israelite story. Levine and Milgrom treat the prose and poetry as distinct layers, and the wider early-Jewish reception, surveyed by John Levison, pulls Balaam''s reputation in opposite directions — sometimes a genuine prophet, sometimes the archetypal corrupt diviner of Numbers 31:16.

So the contradiction as a bare yes/no is overstated. Read with Deut 23:5''s (Eng.) blessing-conversion clause and the purpose-infinitive le-qalel, both texts agree the curse never landed. What remains is a real but lesser tension at the level of tradition: Ammon''s inclusion in Deuteronomy and the different shaping of the Balaam memory across the sources. Verdict: largely harmonizable on the surface claim; residual source-traditional divergence over who hired him and how the episode was remembered.', 'Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1996), pp. 211-213, on Deuteronomy 23:3-6.
Baruch A. Levine, *Numbers 21-36* (Anchor Bible 4A; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 137-234, on the composite Balaam cycle and the Deir Alla inscription.
