The traditional harmonization, developed by Augustine and Aquinas on the problem of concursus and codified in Geisler and Howe''s When Critics Ask and Geisler''s Systematic Theology, distinguishes God directly asserting falsehood — which the denial texts exclude — from God judicially commissioning deception as punishment upon those who have already chosen to reject the truth. On this reading the lying spirit, the deceived prophet of Ezekiel, and the Thessalonian delusion are all instruments of judgment visited on the already-guilty (Ahab''s prophets; idolaters who "set up idols in their heart," Ezek 14:4; those who "received not the love of the truth," 2 Thess 2:10), not God speaking a personal falsehood. This is a genuinely coherent position and should not be dismissed as mere word-play; it tracks a recognizable moral distinction between deceiving an innocent and abandoning a hardened deceiver to his chosen lie. Frame articulates the Reformed version through concursus, in which God ordains the deception without being its proximate moral agent.

The weight of the critical objection falls on the verbs. The Hebrew of Ezekiel 14:9, pittiti (piel of patah, "to seduce, entice, deceive"), gives Yahweh the active causative — the same root that describes the seducer of a virgin in Exodus 22:16 (English) / 22:15 (Hebrew MT). 2 Thessalonians 2:11 has God as subject of pempei, "sends," with the delusion (energeian planēs) as direct object; this is causative, not bare permission. 1 Kings 22 does not depict permission either: Yahweh solicits a volunteer, ratifies the plan, and guarantees its success. So the harmonization cannot rest on softening these to permissive constructions; the grammar resists it. The defender must instead hold the line on definition — that commissioning a deceiving agent against the guilty is not "lying" in the sense the denial texts deny.

Three contextual observations frame the dispute. First, 1 Kings 22 belongs to a divine-council worldview, well documented by Patrick Miller and others, in which Yahweh presides over a heavenly assembly that deliberates and dispatches agents — a setting the later philosophical-theological tradition no longer shared. Second, the Micaiah episode is a prophetic vision-report whose point is to explain, after the fact, why the court prophets unanimously and wrongly predicted victory; it is a theodicy of false prophecy, not a treatise on divine veracity. Third, the "cannot lie" affirmations cluster in later strata — Titus and Hebrews are post-Pauline and articulate divine constancy in terms congenial to Hellenistic notions of an immutable, impassible deity, while Numbers 23:19 and 1 Samuel 15:29 pair "lie" with "repent / change his mind" and primarily assert the reliability of covenant promise, not a metaphysical incapacity for any deception. Brueggemann and Crenshaw stress that the older Israelite texts are comfortable with Yahweh employing deception within judgment, and Greenberg''s treatment of Ezekiel 14:9 reads the deceived prophet as caught up in Yahweh''s own act of judicial enticement rather than merely permitted to err.

The upshot: there is no flat formal contradiction once "lie" is restricted to direct assertion of falsehood to the innocent, because the deception texts uniformly target the already-guilty within a judgment frame. But that restriction is doing all the work, and it is contestable — Ezekiel''s first-person "I have deceived" strains it, and the denial texts make no such carve-out. Verdict: disputed — real tension that turns on the contested scope of "lie" rather than on a demonstrable formal inconsistency.', 'Simon J. DeVries, *1 Kings* (Word Biblical Commentary 12; Word, 1985), pp. 264-268, on the lying spirit in the divine council.
Moshe Greenberg, *Ezekiel 1-20* (Anchor Bible 22; Doubleday, 1983), pp. 247-257, on Ezekiel 14:9 and the verb pittiti.
Patrick D. Miller, "The Divine Council and the Prophetic Call to War," *Vetus Testamentum* 18 (1968): 100-107.
