Two harmonizations have long standing. The first, favored by Archer and Calvin, leans on Paul''s own qualifier, ''in one day'' (en mia hemera), which has no counterpart in Numbers: 23,000 may be the number who fell on the first day of the plague, with the remainder bringing the eventual total to 24,000. Numbers gives a final death count without a time index; Paul reports a single day''s casualties. The two need not collide. The second is simpler: Paul, writing rapidly and citing from memory or from a homiletic tradition rather than checking the scroll, rounds or misremembers. Either route renders the numbers compatible without forcing the text.

The first option is reasonable but supplies a distinction the texts do not assert. Nothing in Numbers 25 marks 23,000 as a first-day subset, and nothing in Paul indicates he is consciously carving a subtotal out of a larger figure. There is also an internal complication in Numbers itself: 25:4-5 has Yahweh and Moses order the execution of the ringleaders (''hang them up before the LORD against the sun''), a separate count from the 24,000 plague deaths in 25:9, which shows the chapter already distinguishing modes and tallies of death — but it does not yield a clean 23,000/1,000 split that would underwrite Paul''s number.

The history of reception confirms that ancient readers felt the discrepancy. Hellenistic and rabbinic exegesis circulated varying tallies and rationales for the Baal-Peor deaths (cf. the discussions reflected in the Talmudic and midrashic traditions on the apostasy), and within the Christian transmission some witnesses to 1 Corinthians 10:8 read 24,000, an evident scribal accommodation to Numbers — the textual fingerprint of a noticed problem. The Septuagint of Numbers 25:9 reads 24,000 with the MT, so the variation is not a translation artifact in the source text; it is located in Paul and in those who later tidied him. Fitzmyer''s Anchor Yale commentary treats Paul as drawing on a homiletic/exegetical tradition rather than precisely reproducing Numbers, and Thiselton and Fee likewise read the citation as paraenetic and approximate rather than archival. It is worth noting Paul is loose with the wilderness data more broadly in this very passage, telescoping several episodes.

The upshot is modest. The ''one day'' qualifier and the recognized inexactness of Paul''s typological citation make the figures reconcilable: there is no contradiction at the level of what each writer is claiming, since Paul is not asserting the total plague death toll and Numbers is not reporting a single day. What the discrepancy does establish, against any doctrine of mechanical numerical precision, is that Paul is citing inexactly — from memory, tradition, or rounding — not transcribing the Hebrew figure. Verdict: harmonizable as an approximate citation; the divergence reflects Paul''s inexact homiletic use, not a factual conflict between the events described.', 'Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *First Corinthians* (Anchor Yale Bible 32; Yale University Press, 2008), pp. 386-388, on the Baal-Peor citation.
Anthony C. Thiselton, *The First Epistle to the Corinthians* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 2000), pp. 739-742, on 1 Corinthians 10:8.
Gordon D. Fee, *The First Epistle to the Corinthians* (NICNT; Eerdmans, 1987), pp. 455-457, on the wilderness typology.
Jacob Milgrom, *Numbers* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1990), pp. 211-216, on the Baal-Peor episode.
