Roy E. Ciampa and Brian S. Rosner, *The First Letter to the Corinthians* (Pillar New Testament Commentary; Eerdmans, 2010), on Paul''s use of the Numbers tradition.', 0, NULL, 7, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Numbers, 1 Corinthians', 1, 1, 3, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The conservative harmonization (Archer, Keil and Delitzsch, and most evangelical commentaries) holds that the swallowing claimed in chapter 16 took those physically present in the rebel encampment, while Korah''s own sons — ancestors of the later Korahite temple singers — were not in their father''s company at the moment of judgment and so survived. This preserves the Levitical lineage the Chronicler and the psalm superscriptions treat as legitimate, and 26:11 is read as confirming exactly that.

The surface objection is that 16:27 explicitly stages ''their sons'' at the tent doors and 16:32 has the earth swallow ''all the men that pertained unto Korah.'' Taken flatly, the household of Korah perishes entire, leaving no room for surviving sons. The harmonization must suppose Korah''s sons were elsewhere — which the narrative does not say.

The decisive context, however, is that Numbers 16 is itself a redactional fusion of at least two rebellion stories, a point on which Noth, Milgrom, Levine, and Knohl broadly converge. One strand is the Dathan-Abiram revolt (Reubenites contesting Moses'' civil authority, older non-Priestly material), in which the rebels are swallowed by the earth at their tents (16:25-34). The other is the Korah revolt (a Priestly account of a Levitical-priestly challenge over cultic prerogative), in which the 250 censer-bearers are consumed by fire from Yahweh (16:35). The text has interwoven a ''swallowing'' death belonging to Dathan and Abiram with a ''fire'' death belonging to Korah''s faction; the seams are visible in the doubled locations and the shifting cast. On this reading, the death of Korah''s household is not the single, unambiguous datum the question assumes — the composite narrative is precisely where ambiguity about Korah''s own fate and that of his sons enters.

The 26:11 clause then functions as a Priestly notice safeguarding the Korahite guild, whose descendants controlled a major share of the temple''s musical liturgy — the ''sons of Korah'' psalms (42, 44-49, 84-85, 87-88). The Chronicler relies on the same legitimacy (1 Chronicles 6:22-38; 9:19). Mark Leuchter''s work on Levitical identity situates such notices within the inner-Israelite politics of priestly and Levitical lineage. So the clause is doing real ideological work, not merely correcting an oversight.

Where does this leave the contradiction? On the text''s own composite terms it is internally reconcilable: the Korahite line survives because the sons of Korah were not part of their father''s doomed company — a reading the canonical form actively asserts in 26:11 and the genealogies presuppose. The tension is therefore not a flat self-contradiction so much as a seam between traditions that the redactors have smoothed with an explicit clause. What it does NOT support is a harmonization that pretends 16:27''s ''their sons'' and 26:11 sit together without strain; the smoothing is audible. Verdict: harmonizable on the canonical text via the explicit 26:11 notice and the composite source history, while clearly bearing the marks of redactional layering.', 'Jacob Milgrom, *Numbers* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1990), pp. 129-137, 414-423, on the composite rebellion narrative and Numbers 26:11.
Baruch A. Levine, *Numbers 1-20* (Anchor Bible 4; Doubleday, 1993), pp. 405-432, on the composite rebellion account.
Israel Knohl, *The Sanctuary of Silence: The Priestly Torah and the Holiness School* (Fortress, 1995), on the Priestly shaping of the Korah narrative.
Martin Noth, *Numbers: A Commentary* (Old Testament Library; Westminster, 1968), pp. 120-135, on the two rebellion traditions.
Mark Leuchter, *The Levites and the Boundaries of Israelite Identity* (Oxford University Press, 2017), on Korahite Levitical lineage.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), on the survival of Korah''s sons.', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', 'Numbers', 7, 2, 1, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard harmonization invokes the elasticity of Hebrew ben, which denotes "son" but routinely extends to "grandson" or "descendant" — the same usage that lets Matthew 1:1 call Jesus "son of David, son of Abraham," collapsing a millennium of generations. On this reading Chronicles telescopes the genealogy, naming Zelophehad as a Manassite descendant without intending to deny the fuller line preserved in Numbers and Joshua. This is a legitimate and well-attested feature of biblical genealogical style. Against it, the intuitive objection — that Chronicles calls Asriel and Zelophehad "sons" in one breath, so both must mean immediate son — does not hold, because telescoping within a single list is precisely what Chronistic genealogies do; the genre mixes immediate and remote descent freely and without flagging the shift, often for the purpose of asserting tribal-territorial claims rather than strict paternity.

The more serious point is textual. 1 Chronicles 7:14-19 is among the most corrupt passages in the Hebrew Bible. The Masoretic Text is grammatically broken — verse 14 opens with a string of names whose syntactic relationships are unclear, and the Aramaizing and Septuagintal witnesses preserve a partly different arrangement. Virtually every modern critical edition and commentary reorders or emends the verses to extract sense: Japhet''s OTL commentary, Knoppers'' Anchor Bible, and Klein''s Hermeneia all devote extended discussion to reconstructing the Manassite list, and they do not agree on the result. Given that the received text here cannot be read with confidence, it cannot bear the weight of a precise genealogical contradiction. What the corruption does suggest is that the Chronicler (or his sources) worked from fragmentary, possibly variant Manassite traditions that were never fully reconciled with the Pentateuchal line; Knoppers reads the passage as reflecting genuine tension with the Numbers/Joshua genealogy at the level of source material rather than a clean alternative pedigree.

This is also a notably weak "contradiction" even at the surface. Numbers and Joshua are explicit and mutually consistent; Chronicles is abbreviated and textually unstable. Reading Chronicles'' ben as "descendant" — the ordinary genre convention — removes any formal clash, because nothing in the Chronistic notice positively asserts that Hepher was not Zelophehad''s father; it simply does not name the intervening links, or names them in a damaged sequence. The Zelophehad tradition is significant for an independent reason: it underwrites the daughters''-inheritance ruling (Numbers 27, 36; Joshua 17), a legal etiology the Chronicler had every reason to preserve, since Zelophehad''s daughters are explicitly named in the surrounding Manassite material and the clan''s territorial standing depended on it. Milgrom traces how the inheritance case generated supplementary legislation about intratribal marriage in Numbers 36, which is why the line was remembered at all.

The upshot: read with the standard understanding of telescoped genealogy and due caution about a badly corrupted text, the passages are reconcilable — Numbers and Joshua give the full pedigree, Chronicles an abbreviated or garbled one — and the apparent disagreement about "who was Zelophehad''s father" dissolves into the difference between a complete and a compressed list. The residual uncertainty is text-critical, not doctrinal. Verdict: largely harmonizable via the ben="descendant" convention and telescoping; the real datum is a corrupt, variant Manassite genealogical tradition, not a factual conflict over paternity.', 'Sara Japhet, *I & II Chronicles* (Old Testament Library; Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 178-184, on the corrupt Manassite genealogy.
Gary N. Knoppers, *1 Chronicles 1-9* (Anchor Bible 12; Doubleday, 2003), pp. 456-470, on the Manassite genealogy and its tension with Numbers/Joshua.
Ralph W. Klein, *1 Chronicles* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2006), pp. 226-235, on the text-critical problems of 1 Chronicles 7:14-19.
Jacob Milgrom, *Numbers* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1990), pp. 230-234, on Zelophehad''s line and the inheritance ruling.
Trent C. Butler, *Joshua* (Word Biblical Commentary 7; Word, 1983), pp. 190-192, on Joshua 17:3.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), on the flexible use of ben.', 0, NULL, 6, 'OT_internal', 'Numbers, Joshua, 1 Chronicles', 3, 2, 1, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The principal harmonization, found in Lamar Cooper''s NAC volume and treated more cautiously by Daniel Block, assigns Ezekiel 40-48 to a future, visionary temple distinct from the Mosaic tabernacle/temple cult, so that the two recipes regulate two different cultic orders and need not match. This is a serious and widely held reading: Ezekiel 40-48 is presented as a guided vision of a restored sanctuary with its own architecture, calendar, priesthood, and prince (nasi), and few scholars think it was meant to be enacted as a literal supplement to the Torah''s sacrificial code.

The cost of the move is what keeps the tension alive. Numbers 28 labels its prescriptions perpetual — olam, ''throughout your generations.'' If Ezekiel''s temple program lawfully alters the new-moon recipe (and not only this: Ezekiel also diverges from the Pentateuch on the Passover/Unleavened Bread offerings in 45:21-25, on the daily tamid, which Ezekiel 46:13-15 gives as a single morning lamb against the twice-daily lamb of Numbers 28:3-8, and on the role and offerings of the nasi), then the Pentateuch''s ''perpetual statute'' is not in fact unalterable. One trades a numerical discrepancy for a conflict about the permanence of Torah law — which is why the difficulty was felt acutely within Judaism itself.

The rabbinic reception is the strongest external evidence that this is a real conflict and not a modern invention. The Babylonian Talmud (b. Shabbat 13b; cf. b. Menahot 45a; b. Hagigah 13a) preserves the tradition that the book of Ezekiel was nearly ''stored away'' (i.e. excluded from public use) because its prescriptions contradicted the words of the Torah, and that Hananiah ben Hezekiah ben Garon reconciled them only through a prodigious labor of harmonizing study, consuming three hundred jars of oil in lamplight. The rabbis explicitly classified Ezekiel''s sacrificial law as conflicting with the Mosaic law; their solution was to defer the contradictions to the eschatological age, when Elijah would explain them — an admission that they could not resolve them on the texts as they stood.

The critical reading converges with the rabbinic instinct while declining the eschatological deferral. Zimmerli''s Hermeneia commentary and Block''s NICOT, along with Tuell and Duguid, treat Ezekiel 40-48 as a priestly restoration program — plausibly a Zadokite-oriented blueprint formed in the exile — that diverges from the Pentateuchal P/H system at numerous points. On this view the divergence is not a tidy ''different temple'' rule but the trace of competing priestly traditions about the proper cult, neither of which was edited into agreement with the other in the canon.

So the status is genuinely contested. The ''eschatological temple'' harmonization is internally coherent and intellectually respectable; it does not so much resolve the contradiction as relocate it from arithmetic to the question of whether a ''perpetual'' Torah statute can be superseded. Verdict: disputed — the recipes conflict on their face; the future-temple reading can absorb the conflict only at the price of qualifying the Torah''s claim of permanence, a tension that Jewish tradition itself recognized rather than dissolved.', 'Walther Zimmerli, *Ezekiel 2: A Commentary on the Book of the Prophet Ezekiel, Chapters 25-48* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1983), pp. 482-499, on Ezekiel 46 and the divergences from Pentateuchal law.
