Jacob Milgrom, *Numbers* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1990), pp. 238-242, on the Numbers 28 offerings.', 0, NULL, 7, 'OT_internal', 'Numbers, Ezekiel', 1, 2, 1, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The apologetic harmonization (Archer and most commentaries on Judges) has two prongs and is, in this case, unusually strong. First, Numbers 31 is a strike against a particular Midianite coalition implicated at Peor, not the whole Midianite people, who ranged widely from northwest Arabia through the Transjordan to the fringes of Sinai; Midianites elsewhere were untouched. Second, the chapter itself records that the Israelites ''took all the women of Midian captives, and their little ones'' (31:9) — so some Midianites demonstrably survived the campaign, and Israelite soldiers were assigned the virgin captives (31:18). The decisive internal point, which the original commentary rightly conceded, is that 31:9 makes a literal total extermination self-contradictory within the same chapter; ''all the males'' (kol-zakhar) must therefore be read as the combatants who were engaged, not every male Midianite in existence. Two centuries of demographic recovery, plus untouched Midianite groups, comfortably account for Judges 6.

This is reinforced by genre. Ancient Near Eastern war reports — and the biblical herem tradition specifically — habitually employ totalizing, hyperbolic language (''all,'' ''utterly destroyed,'' ''left none remaining'') that the same texts then contradict by recording survivors. The Merneptah stela''s ''Israel is laid waste, his seed is not'' is the classic comparandum: annihilation rhetoric, not census. Susan Niditch''s *War in the Hebrew Bible* and Lawson Younger''s work on conquest accounts document this convention extensively, and both Niditch''s and Sasson''s Judges commentaries note that ''Midian'' is a fluid ethnic-geographic label covering several distinct groups across centuries. Read within the convention, ''they slew all the males'' does not assert a biological extinction event.

What remains is not really a factual contradiction but a tonal and traditional tension. The rhetoric of Numbers 31 reaches for total erasure of the targeted coalition, and the Pentateuchal herem ideology and the Deuteronomistic oppression narrative of Judges 6 derive from different literary streams that were never coordinated into a single demographic history of Midian. One tradition remembers Midian annihilated for Peor; the other remembers Midian as a formidable camel-borne regional power humbled by Gideon. Those are different portraits, but they are not logically incompatible once the captives clause and the hyperbolic idiom are granted.

The upshot: on the texts'' own terms the contradiction substantially dissolves. Numbers 31:9 forecloses a literal universal slaughter, the herem idiom is recognized hyperbole, and ''Midian'' denotes a dispersed and resilient population. The Judges 6 horde needs no special pleading. Verdict: largely harmonizable as a localized punitive campaign described in conventional totalizing war-rhetoric; the residual tension is literary and traditional, not a factual conflict about whether any Midianites survived.', 'Susan Niditch, *War in the Hebrew Bible: A Study in the Ethics of Violence* (Oxford University Press, 1993), pp. 28-77, on the rhetoric of total destruction.
Jack M. Sasson, *Judges 1-12* (Anchor Yale Bible 6D; Yale University Press, 2014), pp. 322-335, on Midian in Judges 6.
K. Lawson Younger Jr., *Ancient Conquest Accounts: A Study in Ancient Near Eastern and Biblical History Writing* (JSOTSup 98; Sheffield Academic Press, 1990), on the conventions of conquest rhetoric.
Baruch A. Levine, *Numbers 21-36* (Anchor Bible 4A; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 446-466, on the war against Midian.
Philip D. Stern, *The Biblical Herem: A Window on Israel''s Religious Experience* (Brown Judaic Studies 211; Scholars Press, 1991), on the herem tradition.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), on the survival of Midianite groups.', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', 'Numbers, Judges', 7, 2, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The lexical harmonization (Archer and the standard commentaries) is decisive on the apparent contradiction. The Hebrew is anav, which does not mean weak, passive, or behaviorally mild; it denotes the humble, lowly, God-submissive disposition — the opposite of the self-asserting and the proud. In the Psalms anav and the cognate anavah characterize the righteous poor and the pious sufferer (e.g. the anavim whom Yahweh exalts), not the temperamentally gentle. The note in Numbers 12:3 sits within the Miriam-Aaron challenge to Moses'' unique prophetic standing (Numbers 12:1-2): the point is that Moses did not grasp at his own status or retaliate against his siblings, leaving vindication to Yahweh. A man can be anav before God — claiming nothing for himself, deferring entirely to the divine — while executing severe judgment when, in the narrative''s own terms, God commands it. There is therefore no contradiction between being anav and ordering the Midianite executions; the two operate on different axes (posture before God versus action toward enemies of the covenant). The SAB''s pairing trades on the modern English connotation of ''meek'' as ''mild,'' which the Hebrew does not carry. On the lexical question the tension dissolves.

What the verse does raise — and what the harmonization does not touch — is a literary and authorial problem of long standing. A first-person Mosaic author declaring himself the humblest man on earth is self-undermining: the claim of supreme humility is itself a boast. Medieval and early-modern readers saw this clearly. Ibn Ezra, in his guarded comments hinting at post-Mosaic material in the Torah, noted verses that Moses could not naturally have written about himself; Spinoza, in the *Theological-Political Treatise* (1670), pressed such observations into a direct argument against Mosaic authorship of the Pentateuch. Numbers 12:3 became a stock exhibit. Critically, the half-verse reads as an editorial gloss — a narratorial aside defending Moses'' character precisely at the moment his authority is challenged by Aaron and Miriam — rather than as Moses'' self-report. The surrounding Numbers corpus then preserves a far less serene Moses: the rash striking of the rock at Meribah (Numbers 20:10-12), for which he is barred from the land; the commissioning of the Midian war (Numbers 31); and, in Exodus 32, the order that the Levites cut down kinsmen after the golden calf. The portrait is composite, and the eulogistic gloss does not fully cohere with it.

So two things are true at once. As a doctrinal contradiction the entry is overstated: anav is theological humility, compatible with commanded severity, and the lexical case is sound. As a literary observation it is real and historically consequential: the verse is a parenthetical self-encomium that the tradition itself (Ibn Ezra, Spinoza, modern source criticism) has long read as betraying a hand other than Moses''. Verdict: overstated as a contradiction once anav is understood; genuine as evidence of editorial glossing and against Mosaic authorship.', 'Baruch A. Levine, *Numbers 1-20* (Anchor Bible 4; Doubleday, 1993), pp. 328-334, on Numbers 12:3 and the meaning of anav.
Jacob Milgrom, *Numbers* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1990), pp. 93-96, on Numbers 12:3.
Benedict de Spinoza, *Theological-Political Treatise* (1670), ch. 8, citing Numbers 12:3 against Mosaic authorship.
Richard Elliott Friedman, *Who Wrote the Bible?* (Summit Books, 1987), on editorial additions and the question of Mosaic authorship.
Martin Noth, *Numbers: A Commentary* (Old Testament Library; Westminster, 1968), pp. 92-96, on the secondary character of Numbers 12:3.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), on the meaning of meekness in Numbers 12:3.', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', 'Numbers', 7, 2, 1, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

The harmonization here is strong and, on the canonical text, decisive. Numbers 13-14 is a single continuous narrative: the spies'' mission (13), the report and the people''s revolt (14:1-10), Moses'' intercession (14:11-19), Yahweh''s verdict (14:20-35), and the death of the faithless spies (14:36-38). Joshua is named within this very scene as one of the two faithful scouts who tore their clothes and urged trust (14:6), and he is explicitly listed among the exempted in 14:30, only six verses after the Caleb-only oath. Reading 14:24 as a deliberate exclusion of Joshua therefore requires ignoring the immediate context, which names him. The natural reading is rhetorical focus: 14:24 singles out Caleb for special commendation — ''because he had another spirit with him, and hath followed me fully'' — as the most conspicuous dissenter from the majority report, with Joshua''s parallel exemption stated in the surrounding verses. Wenham''s Tyndale and Cole''s NAC commentaries read it precisely this way, and the cumulative narrative unambiguously preserves both men. There is no formal contradiction.

What the singling-out of Caleb does reflect — and what the harmonization correctly leaves room for — is a discernible compositional history. Numbers 14:24 is shaped as a self-contained divine oath about Caleb and his seed, with no mention of Joshua, and several scholars (Levine; Knierim and Coats) detect behind it an originally independent Calebite tradition. Caleb is identified as a Kenizzite (Numbers 32:12; Joshua 14:6, 14) — that is, of Edomite/Kenizzite rather than strictly Israelite stock — and the Calebite clan''s claim to Hebron is preserved as a distinct settlement narrative in Joshua 14:13-15 and 15:13-19 (with Judges 1:20). The Caleb-only material plausibly circulated in southern, Judahite/Calebite circles before being integrated into the national conquest story led by Joshua, the Ephraimite hero of the Deuteronomistic narrative. On this reading Joshua''s exemption was coordinated with the older Caleb oath as the traditions were woven together — which is exactly why 14:24 still reads as Caleb-centric while the frame names both.

The upshot: as a stand-alone contradiction the entry is weak. The text as transmitted names Joshua and Caleb together in the same chapter and repeatedly thereafter; 14:24''s solo mention of Caleb is emphasis, not an exhaustive census of survivors, and the apologetic harmonization is sound. The genuine residue is not a contradiction but a fingerprint of textual layering — an independent Calebite tradition partly visible beneath the Joshua-led conquest narrative. Verdict: harmonizable; the surface tension resolves as rhetorical focus, with the real datum being an absorbed Calebite source tradition.', 'Baruch A. Levine, *Numbers 1-20* (Anchor Bible 4; Doubleday, 1993), pp. 360-380, on the Caleb tradition in Numbers 13-14.
Rolf P. Knierim and George W. Coats, *Numbers* (Forms of the Old Testament Literature IV; Eerdmans, 2005), on the structure of Numbers 13-14.
Gordon J. Wenham, *Numbers* (Tyndale Old Testament Commentaries; InterVarsity Press, 1981), pp. 122-126, on the focus on Caleb in 14:24.
R. Dennis Cole, *Numbers* (New American Commentary 3B; Broadman & Holman, 2000), on the exemption of Joshua and Caleb.
Trent C. Butler, *Joshua* (Word Biblical Commentary 7; Word, 1983), pp. 170-180, on the Calebite Hebron tradition.
Jacob Milgrom, *Numbers* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1990), pp. 110-115, on Caleb in the spy narrative.', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', 'Numbers', 7, 2, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The strongest apologetic harmonization is a systematic-theology framework: divine anger is short toward covenant people who repent, longer where corporate judgment is required, and terminal toward the unrepentant and the cursed. Different durations attach to different objects under different conditions. Geisler and Howe develop this recipient-based reading. As a theological grid it is coherent and absorbs most of the duration verses. Psalm 30:5 explicitly frames the brevity of anger against the lifetime of favor ("his anger is but a moment, but his favor is for a lifetime"), and Psalm 103:9 and Micah 7:18 are doxological statements about God''s disposition toward the penitent, not absolute metaphysical claims; Gerstenberger''s form-critical reading of Psalm 30 underscores that these are liturgical declarations of thanksgiving, not propositions about anger''s intrinsic span. Numbers 32:13''s forty years is a specific historical judgment, not a statement about anger''s duration as such. The Matthew 25 material concerns final judgment of the unrepentant, a categorically different object — and Block''s discussion of wrath and judgment in the Judges narratives illustrates, at least for the Deuteronomistic material, that corporate divine anger is typically bounded and tied to the people''s conduct.

The framework nonetheless has two cracks it cannot close. First, Judith 8:15 — admittedly a deuterocanonical text — states that God "will not threaten as a man, nor be inflamed to anger as a son of man." That sits in obvious tension with the dozens of passages where Yahweh''s anger flares. The recurrent Hebrew idiom is charah aph-Yahweh, literally "the nostril/face of Yahweh burned," alongside charon aph, "burning of anger" — concrete, bodily, affective language. The "not inflamed to anger" line in Judith reflects a Hellenistic-Jewish appropriation of divine apatheia and impassibility, a philosophical conception increasingly at home in Second Temple Judaism and at odds with the older Hebrew picture of an emotionally responsive deity; Moore''s Anchor Bible Judith reads the line against precisely that Hellenistic backdrop. Brueggemann, Heschel, and Crenshaw all describe this as a development internal to the biblical concept of God: an older anthropopathic deity whose anger is a genuine affective flare, progressively rephilosophized into an immutable being whose "wrath" is reinterpreted as judicial action. Heschel in particular insists that prophetic anger is real divine pathos, not metaphor.

Second, and more decisively, Jeremiah contradicts itself in nearly identical vocabulary. Jeremiah 3:12 has Yahweh promise "I will not keep anger forever" (using netor, to retain or bear a grudge); Jeremiah 17:4 says "ye have kindled a fire in mine anger, which shall burn forever" (le-''olam). The audience-distinction move — 3:12 addresses penitent returning Israel, 17:4 addresses apostate Judah — does real work, since the objects differ. But the same prophetic book applies opposite durations to Yahweh''s aph using the same temporal marker, and the resolution depends on supplying conditions the verses themselves do not flag. Holladay''s Hermeneia Jeremiah and Lundbom''s Anchor Bible Jeremiah both attribute the divergence to different redactional layers — the hope-and-restoration material against the unrelenting-judgment material — rather than to a single coherent doctrine of bounded versus eternal wrath.

The apologetic systematization therefore functions as a theology but only by stitching together verses that arose in different worldviews: Hebrew anthropopathism (the burning nostril), Hellenistic impassibility (Judith), prophetic restoration theology (Jer 3; Mic 7), and apocalyptic eternal punishment (Matt 25). On the bounded-versus-conditional axis the texts largely cohere; on the deeper question of whether Yahweh is the sort of being who is "inflamed to anger" at all, and on Jeremiah''s own internal split, they do not. Verdict: partially harmonizable by recipient and condition, with a genuine residual tension rooted in distinct theological eras.', 'Norman Geisler and Thomas Howe, *When Critics Ask* (Victor Books, 1992), on the duration of divine anger
Daniel I. Block, *Judges, Ruth* (NAC 6; Broadman & Holman, 1999), on divine wrath and judgment in the Judges narratives
