The standard apologetic harmonization, found in Block''s NAC and echoed across evangelical commentaries, reads Deuteronomy 2:9 as a one-time wilderness travel policy and treats Judges 3 and Jeremiah 48 as later judgments triggered by Moabite aggression. In Judges 3 Eglon king of Moab oppresses Israel first (Judg 3:12-14), so Ehud''s revolt and the ensuing slaughter constitute defensive liberation, not unprovoked conquest of Moabite homeland; the engagement is at the fords of the Jordan, on Israelite-controlled ground, not a westward seizure of Ar. Jeremiah 48 is a prophetic oracle of judgment against Moab for pride ("we have heard the pride of Moab," Jer 48:29) and trust in Chemosh, delivered centuries later. On this reading nothing technically violates Deuteronomy 2:9, since both later episodes arise under different circumstances and neither annexes the protected Lot-grant territory. The chronology is not absurd and covers most of the surface friction.

The difficulty is the rhetorical totality of the later material set against the form of the Deuteronomic clause. Deuteronomy 2:9 is not framed as a temporary courtesy but as a divine land grant: the verb is the perfective "I have given" (*natatti*), the same grant-language used for Israel''s own inheritance, and it is grounded in kinship—Moab as Lot''s descendants. Jeremiah 48:2 then has Yahweh resolving to "cut it off from being a nation" (*nakhritennah mi-goy*), a direct negation of national existence for the very people whose territory was earlier secured by divine gift. Younger''s NIVAC Judges and Carroll''s OTL Jeremiah both observe that the oracles against Moab assume a posture irreconcilable with the Deuteronomic protection.

The critical-scholarship explanation is that the Hebrew Bible preserves multiple memory traditions about Moab reflecting different periods and agendas. The Deuteronomic "kinship" tradition treats Moab as Abrahamic relatives whose land Yahweh has assigned and protected. The Deuteronomistic History casts Moab as a regional rival caught in cycles of conflict and tribute. The classical prophets fold Moab into eschatological judgment against the nations. Weinfeld and Tigay both discuss how "Moabite" identity in biblical discourse shifts with era and theological purpose; the protective Deuteronomic stance likely reflects a period of stable relations, the oracles a period of hostility, with the Mesha Stele attesting the real political seesaw between Israel and Moab. Mark Smith''s work on the layered self-understanding of Israel vis-à-vis its neighbors fits the same pattern.

The harmonization succeeds at the chronological-narrative level—different eras, different provocations—but the unconditional grant language of Deuteronomy 2:9 contains no expiry or conditionality clause. Either Yahweh''s gift to Lot''s descendants is irrevocable, in which case Jeremiah 48 contradicts it, or it is revocable on the recipients'' conduct, in which case Deuteronomy 2:9 misstates the actual policy by omitting the condition. The cleaner account is that these are traditions in tension, smoothed by sequential reading. Verdict: partially harmonizable by chronology, with a genuine residual reversal of the land-grant theology.', 'Daniel I. Block, *Judges, Ruth* (NAC 6; Broadman & Holman, 1999), on Judges 3:28-30
K. Lawson Younger Jr., *Judges, Ruth* (NIVAC; Zondervan, 2002), on Judges 3
Robert P. Carroll, *Jeremiah* (OTL; Westminster, 1986), on Jeremiah 48
Moshe Weinfeld, *Deuteronomy 1-11* (Anchor Bible 5; Doubleday, 1991), on Deuteronomy 2:9
Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; JPS, 1996), on Deuteronomy 2:9
Jack R. Lundbom, *Jeremiah 37-52* (Anchor Bible 21C; Doubleday, 2004), on Jeremiah 48:2
Mark S. Smith, *The Early History of God: Yahweh and the Other Deities in Ancient Israel* (Eerdmans, 2002)', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Deuteronomy, Judges, Jeremiah', 6, 3, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The apologetic harmonization mirrors the Moab argument. Deuteronomy 2:19 is a one-time wilderness travel directive constraining Israel''s march to Canaan, not a permanent treaty of non-aggression; Judges 11 and Jeremiah 49 are later responses to Ammonite aggression. Jephthah''s war is, on this reading, defensive—Ammon presses a territorial claim to the land between the Arnon and the Jabbok (Judg 11:13), and the diplomatic exchange in Judges 11:14-27 frames Israel''s action as a response to Ammonite encroachment rather than a violation of a Lot-grant homeland. Jeremiah''s oracle is then judicial: a response to Ammon''s idolatry (the cult of the national god) and its seizure of Israelite territory after the fall of the northern kingdom. Block and Butler handle the Judges material this way, and the chronological framework genuinely covers most of the surface narrative—the Deuteronomic prohibition concerns the wilderness itinerary, whereas Jephthah and Jeremiah address centuries-later hostilities. To the extent the question imagines a single static rule for "how the Ammonites should be treated," that reading flattens a layered textual history, and the chronological case dissolves much of it.

What the chronology does not dissolve is the land-grant theology. The structure of the Deuteronomic clause is identical to the Moabite one: the perfective grant-language "I have given" (Hebrew *natatti*), grounded in Lot-kinship, with no stated condition or expiry. The grant is framed as Yahweh''s own apportionment of the earth, parallel to the Edom (Esau) and Moab notices in Deuteronomy 2, and the Deuteronomist offers no clause making it revocable on the recipients'' conduct. Jeremiah 49:1 does not merely announce punishment—it attacks the premise of the grant: "Hath Israel no sons? hath he no heir? why then doth their king inherit Gad, and his people dwell in his cities?" The rhetorical questions imply that the children of Lot have no legitimate title to the territory in question, and that Gad''s land rightfully belongs to Israel. A textual note is in order here: the consonantal *mlkm* admits two readings, vocalized either as *malkam* ("their king," so KJV) or as *milkom* (the Ammonite national deity Milcom, so NRSV, ESV, and most modern editions, supported by the parallel "his people" and the LXX''s treatment). The wordplay is almost certainly deliberate—king and god collapse into one—but the Milcom reading is a recognized translation crux rather than a settled datum, and the argument here does not depend on which vocalization one adopts. Either way, the verse is a reversal of the Deuteronomic land theology, not just a penalty for misbehavior. Lundbom and Holladay both flag the tension in their treatments of Jeremiah 49.

The critical-scholarship reading is the same as for Moab: two traditions about Ammon coexist in the canon—a "children of Lot" tradition treating them as protected kin (cf. the Genesis 19 origin etiology) and an "enemy nation" tradition subjecting them to *herem*-style judgment—reflecting different periods and theological frameworks. The protective Deuteronomic stance likely reflects a period of relatively stable relations and a theology that read the Trans-Jordanian neighbors as recipients of their own divine allotment; the prophetic oracles reflect later conflict, including the post-722 Ammonite expansion into vacated Gadite land that Jeremiah 49:1 protests. Weinfeld and Tigay note that the varied biblical stance toward the Trans-Jordanian neighbors is itself a product of this multi-tradition layering, and Carroll reads the oracle as part of the prophetic oracles-against-the-nations collection rather than a coherent legal supplement to Deuteronomy.

The harmonization patches the surface narrative if one grants that Yahweh''s land grants are revocable on the recipients'' conduct. But Deuteronomy 2:19 includes no such condition; it is an unconditional "I have given" clause. To invalidate it later requires precisely the theological revision Jeremiah 49:1 performs ("Hath Israel no heir?"), and that revision is itself the seam. Verdict: partially harmonizable by chronology, with a genuine residual contradiction over the irrevocability of the Lot land-grant, carrying a clear redactional fingerprint.', 'Daniel I. Block, *Judges, Ruth* (NAC 6; Broadman & Holman, 1999), on Judges 11:32
Trent C. Butler, *Judges* (WBC 8; Thomas Nelson, 2009), on Judges 11
Jack R. Lundbom, *Jeremiah 37-52* (Anchor Bible 21C; Doubleday, 2004), on Jeremiah 49:1-2
William L. Holladay, *Jeremiah 2: A Commentary on the Book of the Prophet Jeremiah, Chapters 26-52* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1989), on Jeremiah 49
Robert P. Carroll, *Jeremiah* (OTL; Westminster, 1986), on Jeremiah 49
Moshe Weinfeld, *Deuteronomy 1-11* (Anchor Bible 5; Doubleday, 1991), on Deuteronomy 2:19
Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; JPS, 1996), on Deuteronomy 2:19, 37', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Deuteronomy, Judges, Jeremiah', 6, 3, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard apologetic harmonization, articulated by Geisler and Howe and developed in Grudem''s Systematic Theology, distinguishes "forever" as "for the duration of the present age / an extremely long time" from "forever" as "truly eternal." On this read the abiding-earth verses describe the present cosmos''s relative permanence over against human transience, while the eschatological verses describe its eventual transformation. Grudem adds a second move favored by many evangelical eschatologists: the "new earth" of 2 Peter 3 and Revelation 21 is a *renewed* earth, not a destroyed-and-replaced one, so the earth abides in transformed continuity rather than perishing. That is the strongest harmonization available, and Romans 8:21''s groaning creation "delivered from the bondage of corruption" lends it some intracanonical support.

Both moves run into the texts. The Hebrew *''olam* in Psalm 104:5 and Ecclesiastes 1:4 does not carry the English "long but finite" flexibility in these wisdom-poetic settings; it denotes permanence, indefinite and unchanging duration. Ecclesiastes 1:4 builds an explicit contrast between human generational mortality ("one generation passeth away, and another generation cometh") and the earth''s stability—the rhetorical force depends on the earth being genuinely permanent against transient humanity. Reduce *''olam* to "a long time" and Qoheleth''s point collapses. Seow''s Anchor Bible Ecclesiastes makes exactly this argument: the pessimism about human transience is anchored in the perceived absolute permanence of the cosmic order; Longman''s NICOT reads the verse the same way.

The renewal harmonization is contradicted by the Greek of 2 Peter 3:10. The verse has the heavens pass away "with a great noise" (*rhoizēdon*), the elements (*stoicheia*) "dissolved" (*luthēsetai*), and—on the Byzantine and TR reading behind the KJV "burned up"—*katakaēsetai*. The earliest and best manuscripts (Sinaiticus, Vaticanus) actually read *heurethēsetai*, "shall be found/discovered," the *lectio difficilior* adopted by NA28; Bauckham''s WBC treats this as one of the hardest textual cruxes in the New Testament, but neither reading supports gentle refurbishment—dissolution language dominates either way, and the parallel imagery of cosmic conflagration is Stoic *ekpyrōsis* adapted to Jewish apocalyptic. Revelation 21:1 is more decisive still: "the first heaven and the first earth were passed away" uses *apēlthon*, the same verb of abolition used for the old order''s removal, and 20:11 has earth and heaven flee "and there was found no place for them." Aune''s WBC Revelation reads this as genuine cosmic dissolution, not cosmetic renewal.

Beneath the lexical point is a real theological development. The older Israelite cosmology of Psalm 104 and Ecclesiastes—earth as a permanently founded order that "cannot be moved"—is creation theology, and Psalm 102:25-26''s "they shall perish" already registers an internal counter-voice. The destruction-and-replacement view in Daniel, the Synoptic Apocalypse, 2 Peter, and Revelation is a later apocalyptic framework, shaped in part by Persian and Hellenistic eschatology, as Collins traces in The Apocalyptic Imagination. The two cosmologies do not fully reconcile. The apologetic requires either "forever" to mean "temporary" or "dissolved/burned" to mean "renewed"—in each case redefining ordinary language to rescue the harmonization. Verdict: genuine contradiction reflecting two distinct theological eras.', 'Norman Geisler and Thomas Howe, *When Critics Ask* (Victor Books, 1992), on the duration of the earth
Wayne Grudem, *Systematic Theology* (Zondervan, 1994), on the new heavens and new earth
Choon-Leong Seow, *Ecclesiastes* (Anchor Bible 18C; Doubleday, 1997), on Ecclesiastes 1:4
Tremper Longman III, *The Book of Ecclesiastes* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 1998), on Ecclesiastes 1:4
Richard J. Bauckham, *Jude, 2 Peter* (WBC 50; Word, 1983), on 2 Peter 3:10
David E. Aune, *Revelation 17-22* (WBC 52C; Thomas Nelson, 1998), on Revelation 21:1
John J. Collins, *The Apocalyptic Imagination* (Eerdmans, 1998)
Hans-Joachim Kraus, *Psalms 60-150* (Continental Commentary; Fortress, 1989), on Psalm 104:5', 0, NULL, 4, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Deuteronomy, Psalms, Ecclesiastes, Isaiah, Matthew, Mark, Luke, Hebrews, 2 Peter, Revelation', 5, 3, 3, 1, 1, '2 Peter 3:10''s final verb is textually unstable, with major witnesses reading heurethēsetai (''will be found/discovered'') against the TR/KJV katakaēsetai (''will be burned up''), bearing on whether the verse describes destruction.', 'NA28 apparatus at 2 Peter 3:10; Metzger, A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament, 2nd ed.');

The standard apologetic harmonization, articulated by Tigay in the JPS Deuteronomy and gestured at in Block''s NIVAC, reads "face to face" (*panim be-panim*) as an idiom for direct, immediate communication rather than literal visual encounter. The phrase elsewhere shifts between visual and non-visual senses: Genesis 32:30 (Jacob "I have seen God face to face"), Numbers 14:14 (Yahweh "seen face to face"), and Exodus 33:11 (Yahweh spoke to Moses "face to face, as a man speaketh unto his friend"). On this read Deuteronomy 5:4 means "directly addressed," fully compatible with 4:12''s "no form, only a voice." The argument is lexically defensible and is the strongest move available.

Its limit is that the idiom is not reliably non-visual, and the very passage that grounds the metaphor undercuts it. Exodus 33:11 has Yahweh speak to Moses "face to face," yet only nine verses later Yahweh says "thou canst not see my face: for there shall no man see me, and live" (33:20), and Moses is permitted to see only Yahweh''s "back parts" (33:23). Exodus 33 thus deploys *panim* in both a metaphorical sense (intimate address) and a literal-visual sense (what may or may not actually be seen) within a single chapter—proof that the idiom does not by itself settle the visual question. Deuteronomy 5:4 then adds the spatial-sensory frame "in the mount out of the midst of the fire," weighting the description toward a real, situated encounter. Childs on Exodus and Weinfeld on Deuteronomy both note that 5:4 reads the Horeb event as unmediated, while 4:12 reads it as mediated entirely through voice without form.

The critical-scholarship explanation is straightforward and well attested. Deuteronomy 4 belongs to a later redactional layer—Weinfeld places it in the late Deuteronomistic stratum—whose program polices against any visual representation of Yahweh. The "no form, ye saw no similitude" framing is a deliberate de-visualization of the Horeb theophany, set up precisely to ground the image prohibition that follows in 4:15-19 ("ye saw no manner of similitude... lest ye corrupt yourselves, and make you a graven image"). Deuteronomy 5 preserves the older, more anthropomorphic "face-to-face" tradition that the chapter-4 redactor was working to neutralize. Two layers, two theological positions, side by side. Sommer''s The Bodies of God and Smith''s Origins of Biblical Monotheism document this broader canonical contest over whether Yahweh possesses visible form, with Deuteronomy 4 versus 5 as a textbook instance.

The idiomatic harmonization is therefore genuinely partial: *panim be-panim* can mean direct address, and on that narrow point the two verses can be read compatibly. But the visual anchoring of 5:4 and the polemical de-visualizing purpose of 4:12 mean the chapters are advancing different theologies of divine perceptibility, not merely using one idiom in two registers. Verdict: partially harmonizable as idiom, with a real redactional seam between an older anthropomorphic theophany tradition and a later anti-iconic one.', 'Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; JPS, 1996), on Deuteronomy 5:4
Daniel I. Block, *Deuteronomy* (NIVAC; Zondervan, 2012), on Deuteronomy 5
Moshe Weinfeld, *Deuteronomy 1-11* (Anchor Bible 5; Doubleday, 1991), on Deuteronomy 4:12, 15-19
Brevard S. Childs, *The Book of Exodus* (OTL; Westminster, 1974), on Exodus 33:11, 20
Benjamin D. Sommer, *The Bodies of God and the World of Ancient Israel* (Cambridge University Press, 2009)
Mark S. Smith, *The Origins of Biblical Monotheism* (Oxford University Press, 2001)
Bernard M. Levinson, *Deuteronomy and the Hermeneutics of Legal Innovation* (Oxford University Press, 1997)', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', 'Deuteronomy', 7, 3, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard apologetic harmonization, articulated by Walter Kaiser in Toward Old Testament Ethics, runs along Reformed and dispensationalist lines. The Law divides into moral, civil, and ceremonial categories; only the moral law binds Christians, while civil and ceremonial law were provisional and fulfilled in Christ. Matthew 22:37-40 does not abolish but summarizes—Jesus identifies the two greatest commands on which "hang all the law and the prophets," so the Law''s substance is upheld, not voided. Luke 16:16 marks a salvation-historical transition point: the era of the Law and Prophets gives way to the kingdom proclamation, while the moral content endures. This is a sophisticated harmonization with a long pedigree, and on the two-commandment point it is plainly correct: Matthew 22:40''s verb *krematai* ("hang/depend") presents the two loves as the hermeneutical hinge of the whole Torah, not its replacement, exactly as Matthew 5:17 has Jesus come "not to destroy, but to fulfil."

The difficulty is twofold. First, the moral/civil/ceremonial trichotomy is a later Christian theological construction—Aquinas formalized it in the Summa (I-II q.99)—not a category the Pentateuch or the post-exilic texts endorsing it operate with. It conveniently retains the Sabbath as moral "principle" while discarding dietary law as "ceremony," but Deuteronomy 11:1 lumps "his charge, his statutes, his judgments, and his commandments" together with no internal sorting mechanism for which subset is permanent. Sanders'' Paul, the Law, and the Jewish People and Dunn''s New Perspective work both dismantled the assumption that Second Temple Jews ranked Torah obligations this way; for them the covenant was a single indivisible whole.

Second, the New Testament does not speak with one voice on the Law''s continuing force. Luke 16:16 reads as dispensational displacement—"the law and the prophets were until John; since then the gospel of the kingdom is preached"—and Fitzmyer''s Anchor Bible Luke takes it as articulating Luke''s two-stage salvation history in which the Law''s primary function yields to kingdom proclamation. Matthew, by contrast, has Jesus insist that "not one jot or tittle" shall pass from the Law until heaven and earth pass (Matt 5:18), a position in real tension with Luke 16:16. Luz''s Hermeneia Matthew and Bovon''s Hermeneia Luke both register the divergent stances on the Law within the canon itself. Paul sharpens the displacement further: in Galatians 3:23-25 the Law was a "schoolmaster" (*paidagōgos*) holding Israel in custody "until Christ," after which "we are no longer under a schoolmaster"—and Räisänen''s Paul and the Law shows how unstable and contested Paul''s own statements about the Law''s status are.

The two-commandment summary is thus genuinely reconcilable with the "forever" texts. But Luke 16:16 and the Pauline pedagogue argument read as explicit dispensational shifts, and harmonizing them with the Old Testament''s repeated permanence language requires importing the tripartite-law framework that the original writers would not have recognized. Verdict: the summary axis harmonizes cleanly; the supersession axis is a partial harmonization resting on a later, externally supplied theological category.', 'Walter C. Kaiser Jr., *Toward Old Testament Ethics* (Zondervan, 1983), on the categories of Mosaic law
E. P. Sanders, *Paul, the Law, and the Jewish People* (Fortress, 1983)
James D. G. Dunn, *The New Perspective on Paul* (Eerdmans, 2008)
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (Anchor Bible 28A; Doubleday, 1985), on Luke 16:16
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 21-28* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2005), on Matthew 22:37-40
François Bovon, *Luke 2: A Commentary on the Gospel of Luke 9:51-19:27* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2013), on Luke 16:16
Heikki Räisänen, *Paul and the Law* (Mohr Siebeck, 1983)
Moshe Weinfeld, *Deuteronomy 1-11* (Anchor Bible 5; Doubleday, 1991), on Deuteronomy 7:9 and 11:1', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Deuteronomy, 1 Chronicles, Psalms, Matthew, Luke', 6, 3, 4, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard apologetic harmonization, articulated by Grudem''s Systematic Theology and developed in Bruce Ware''s work on classical theism, distinguishes God''s exhaustive knowledge from the *demonstration* or *manifestation* of what He already knows. God does not test Israel to acquire information but so that Israel will know its own heart, or so the test manifests in history what is eternally known to God. The "to know" language is anthropomorphic accommodation—scripture speaking of God in human terms—and the harmonization has been the standard answer since Augustine. There is genuine intracanonical support for the accommodation hermeneutic: scripture frequently ascribes human features to God (eyes, hands, repenting, walking), and few read those literally.

The difficulty is that the Hebrew of Deuteronomy 8:2 resists the accommodation read on its own terms. The verb is *la-da''at*, the infinitive construct of *yada''* ("to know") governed by the purpose preposition—"in order to know what was in thine heart." The grammar presents the testing as serving an epistemic function for Yahweh, not merely a pedagogical one for Israel. Tigay''s JPS Deuteronomy notes the natural reading is that the older Hebrew theology did not presuppose the exhaustive, eternal omniscience that later Hellenistic-Jewish and Christian theology developed. The pattern recurs across the older Pentateuchal strata: in Genesis 18:21 Yahweh says of Sodom, "I will go down now, and see whether they have done altogether according to the cry of it... and if not, I will know"; in Genesis 22:12, after the Akedah, the angel says "now I know that thou fearest God"—the temporal "now" marking a gain in knowledge contingent on Abraham''s action. The 2 Chronicles 32:31 notice that God "left" Hezekiah "to try him, that he might know all that was in his heart" sits in the same idiom.

Mark Smith and Benjamin Sommer have documented at length how the early Israelite deity is conceived as embodied, anthropomorphic, and spatially located in ways incompatible with the philosophically perfect God of later theology: Yahweh walks in the garden "in the cool of the day" (Gen 3:8) and "comes down" to investigate Babel (Gen 11:5) and Sodom (Gen 18:21). Brueggemann and Crenshaw extend the point to the epistemic dimension—Yahweh uses tests to gain knowledge. The omniscience-affirming verses, by contrast, cluster in poetic-hymnic and later or post-exilic contexts (Ps 139, Ps 44, the Lukan Acts 1:24) where the philosophical conception of God has matured. The contradiction is thus between two strata of biblical theology, not merely two surface verses.

The accommodation argument works only if one assumes the doctrine of omniscience was already in place when Deuteronomy 8 was written and the author was speaking metaphorically. The critical reconstruction reverses the order: divine omniscience is a later development, and Deuteronomy 8, like Genesis 18 and 22, means what its grammar says. Crenshaw''s A Whirlpool of Torment and Brueggemann''s Theology of the Old Testament both treat this kind of internal tension as evidence of theological development across the canon. Verdict: genuine contradiction rooted in the development of the doctrine of God; the accommodation reading is a later theological overlay, not the plain sense of the testing texts.', 'Wayne Grudem, *Systematic Theology* (Zondervan, 1994), on divine omniscience and testing
Bruce A. Ware, *God''s Greater Glory: The Exalted God of Scripture and the Christian Faith* (Crossway, 2004), on divine foreknowledge
