Benjamin D. Sommer, *The Bodies of God and the World of Ancient Israel* (Cambridge University Press, 2009)
Walter Brueggemann, *Theology of the Old Testament* (Fortress, 1997)
James L. Crenshaw, *A Whirlpool of Torment* (Fortress, 1984)
Erhard S. Gerstenberger, *Psalms, Part 2* (FOTL XV; Eerdmans, 2001), on Psalm 139:2-3', 0, NULL, 2, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Deuteronomy, 2 Chronicles, Psalms, Acts', 5, 3, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard apologetic harmonization distinguishes two senses of divine sonship: a creation sense (every human is God''s offspring as a created being, Acts 17:29) and a covenant/adoptive sense (some are God''s children as heirs adopted into the family). Deuteronomy and the Psalms operate in the first; Paul in Galatians 3:26 in the second; the categories address different relationships and do not conflict. N. T. Wright develops this in Paul and the Faithfulness of God, and James Scott''s Adoption as Sons of God gives the granular case for *huiothesia* as a Pauline-specific adoptive category distinct from generic creation-sonship. This is one of the stronger harmonizations in the corpus because it tracks a real distinction in usage. Acts 17:29 in the Areopagus speech invokes explicitly Stoic creation language—"we are God''s offspring" (*tou gar kai genos esmen*) quoting the poet Aratus''s Phaenomena—a manifestly different mode of sonship than Galatians intends. Dunn''s BNTC Galatians and Betz''s Hermeneia Galatians both read 3:26 within a specific argumentative thread about Abrahamic descent and incorporation into the people of God through Christ, not a denial that all humans are creatures of God.

The tension is not fully neutralized, however, because the canon deploys more than two senses, with unstable referents. Psalm 82:6 in its original setting is not fictive-kinship language at all: it is a divine-council scene in which Yahweh judges the lesser *elohim* and calls them "sons of the Most High," which Smith and others read as residual Israelite polytheism. When John 10:34 has Jesus cite the verse against blasphemy charges, He reapplies it to humans, expanding the reach of "sons of God" yet again. Across the canon the phrase therefore denotes divine-council members, all Israel, faithful Israel, all humanity as creatures, and specifically those with faith in Christ—no single stable referent.

More pointedly, Deuteronomy 14:1''s "children of the LORD your God" is addressed to all Israel by virtue of corporate covenant membership, not by individual faith, whereas Galatians 3:26 makes sonship contingent on faith in Christ and thereby excludes anyone outside that faith—including unbelieving ethnic Israel. Paul''s argument *requires* redefining who counts as God''s children, which is precisely why Romans 9-11 grows so labored, distinguishing "children of the flesh" from "children of the promise" (Rom 9:8) and wrestling with the standing of ethnic Israel. Caroline Johnson Hodge''s If Sons, Then Heirs shows how Paul reconstructs kinship and ethnicity around Christ rather than around birth. Fitzmyer''s commentary on Acts confirms the creation-sonship register of the Areopagus speech.

The creation-sense/adoptive-sense distinction is theologically clean and dissolves the bluntest form of the contradiction. But it does not erase that Paul reframed the covenant relationship in a way the older texts did not anticipate, narrowing corporate-covenantal sonship to faith-defined sonship. Verdict: largely harmonizable by recognizing distinct senses of sonship, with a genuine residual tension over which sense is authoritative for membership in God''s people.', 'N. T. Wright, *Paul and the Faithfulness of God* (Fortress, 2013), on divine sonship in Paul
James M. Scott, *Adoption as Sons of God: An Exegetical Investigation into the Background of HUIOTHESIA in the Pauline Corpus* (WUNT 2/48; Mohr Siebeck, 1992), on Galatians 3:26
Hans Dieter Betz, *Galatians* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1979), on Galatians 3:26
James D. G. Dunn, *The Epistle to the Galatians* (BNTC; Hendrickson, 1993), on Galatians 3:26
Caroline Johnson Hodge, *If Sons, Then Heirs: A Study of Kinship and Ethnicity in the Letters of Paul* (Oxford University Press, 2007)
Mark S. Smith, *The Origins of Biblical Monotheism* (Oxford University Press, 2001), on Psalm 82:6
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Acts of the Apostles* (Anchor Yale Bible 31; Yale University Press, 1998), on Acts 17:29', 0, NULL, 4, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Deuteronomy, Psalms, Hosea, Matthew, Luke, John, Acts, Galatians', 5, 3, 3, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The apologetic harmonization runs two lines. First (Archer, Kaiser): Deuteronomy 20:19 prohibits only *fruit* trees—the Hebrew rationale is "for the tree of the field is man''s life" (or "is man," a famously difficult clause), specifying food-producing trees—whereas 2 Kings 3:19''s "every good tree" may include non-fruit trees of strategic value. Second: 2 Kings 3 is a special divine command for a specific war, and special commands may override general statutes when Yahweh authorizes them. Most evangelical harmonizers combine the two, and Hobbs''s WBC 2 Kings softens the tension along these lines.

The first move runs into the text itself. Deuteronomy 20:20 already draws exactly the distinction the apologist needs and resolves it the other way: "Only the trees which thou knowest that they be not trees for meat, thou shalt destroy and cut them down" for siegeworks. The statute therefore explicitly permits felling non-fruit trees; the prohibition is targeted at fruit trees precisely so that the besieger does not destroy the food supply he might later need. The 2 Kings 3:19 command to fell "every good tree" (*kol-etz tov*) does not naturally restrict to non-fruit trees—in agricultural Hebrew *tov* characteristically modifies productive, fruit-bearing trees—and it is paired with stopping all wells and ruining every good field with stones, a comprehensive scorched-earth program. Cogan and Tadmor''s Anchor Bible 2 Kings read the verse as describing total devastation of Moab''s agricultural base, an ecological war policy directly at odds with the Deuteronomic restraint.

The second move—special-command override—is more candid but concedes the surface contradiction. If Yahweh through Elisha commands what Deuteronomy forbids, then either the Deuteronomic statute is not actually permanent (contradicting its own claims to lasting validity) or Yahweh''s directives are inconsistent across time. Invoking divine prerogative to override statute is a meta-rule that presupposes the texts disagree at face value; it resolves nothing at the level of the commands themselves.

The critical-scholarship reading is cleaner. Niditch''s War in the Hebrew Bible locates Deuteronomy 20:19 within a developed humanitarian war-ethic distinctive to the Deuteronomic tradition, not shared by the older prophetic narratives. The 2 Kings 3 account belongs to the older Elisha cycle, where prophetic authority and Yahweh-war operate without Deuteronomic constraint. Sweeney''s OTL I & II Kings flags this as one of the places the Deuteronomistic editors did not fully harmonize inherited source material with their own statutory program, leaving the seam visible. Weinfeld''s analysis of the Deuteronomic school similarly treats 20:19-20 as a deliberate, comparatively late humanitarian innovation in ancient Near Eastern siege practice. The contradiction reflects the layering of Israelite war ethics across centuries rather than a single coherent policy. Verdict: genuine contradiction; the fruit-tree distinction actually cuts against the harmonization, and the special-command rescue concedes the surface tension.', 'Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), on 2 Kings 3:19
Walter C. Kaiser Jr., *Toward Old Testament Ethics* (Zondervan, 1983), on Deuteronomy 20:19
T. R. Hobbs, *2 Kings* (WBC 13; Word, 1985), on 2 Kings 3:19
Mordechai Cogan and Hayim Tadmor, *II Kings* (Anchor Bible 11; Doubleday, 1988), on 2 Kings 3:19
Marvin A. Sweeney, *I & II Kings* (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 2007), on 2 Kings 3
Susan Niditch, *War in the Hebrew Bible* (Oxford University Press, 1993), on Deuteronomy 20:19-20
Moshe Weinfeld, *Deuteronomy and the Deuteronomic School* (Oxford University Press, 1972), on Deuteronomy 20:19-20
Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; JPS, 1996), on Deuteronomy 20:19-20', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Deuteronomy, 2 Kings', 6, 3, 1, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard apologetic harmonization, articulated in Davies and Allison''s ICC Matthew and across critical and evangelical Matthew commentaries alike, reads "eunuchs for the kingdom" as a metaphor for voluntary lifelong celibacy, not literal castration. The saying climaxes a discourse on marriage and divorce (Matt 19:3-12); the third class of "eunuch" is figurative, describing those who renounce marriage for the sake of the kingdom—precisely the figure Paul commends in 1 Corinthians 7. On this reading Jesus endorses celibacy, not self-mutilation. The metaphor reading commands near-consensus: Davies-Allison, Luz, and Keener all adopt it, and the church near-universally repudiated Origen''s literal self-castration (reported by Eusebius) as a misreading of the verse. So Matthew 19:12, properly construed, is not an endorsement of physical castration, and as a Matthew-versus-Deuteronomy contradiction the SAB framing is weak.

But the deeper tension the question gestures at—whether God''s stance toward eunuchs is exclusion or favor—survives the metaphor reading, because it runs across the canon rather than between these two verses. Deuteronomy 23:1 treats the physically emasculated as categorically excluded from the *qahal Yahweh*, the cultic assembly, with no remedy; the exclusion is a matter of bodily integrity and ritual wholeness, consonant with the priestly concern for unblemished bodies. Isaiah 56:3-5 then explicitly reverses it: "Neither let the eunuch say, Behold, I am a dry tree... unto them will I give... a name better than of sons and of daughters: I will give them an everlasting name, that shall not be cut off." Blenkinsopp''s Anchor Bible Isaiah 56-66 and Levinson''s work on Deuteronomic legal innovation both read Isaiah 56 as a deliberate, knowing revision of Deuteronomy 23—the post-exilic prophet overturning the older statute, even punning on "cut off" against the eunuch''s condition. The New Testament then builds on the Isaian trajectory: Acts 8 has Philip baptize the Ethiopian eunuch without hesitation (and the eunuch''s reading-matter is Isaiah itself), and Fitzmyer''s commentary on Acts notes the deliberate fulfillment of the Isaian promise of eunuch inclusion.

The canon therefore exhibits a clear arc from exclusion (Deut 23:1) to inclusion (Isa 56) to figurative commendation (Matt 19:12, Acts 8)—and the first two stages are in genuine, irreducible tension, since Isaiah 56 does not reconcile Deuteronomy 23 but overrides it. Kuefler''s The Manly Eunuch traces how late-antique Christianity inherited and reworked these competing valuations of the eunuch body. So the harmonization succeeds narrowly: Matthew 19:12 is metaphor, not literal castration, and the precise contradiction the SAB poses is overstated. What it does not dissolve is the documented reversal between Deuteronomy and Isaiah/Acts. Verdict: the Matthew-vs-Deuteronomy framing is overstated, but it points to a real canonical contradiction in the treatment of eunuchs that resolves only by one text superseding another.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew, Vol. III* (ICC; T&T Clark, 1997), on Matthew 19:12
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 8-20* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2001), on Matthew 19:12
Craig S. Keener, *A Commentary on the Gospel of Matthew* (Eerdmans, 1999), on Matthew 19:12
Joseph Blenkinsopp, *Isaiah 56-66* (Anchor Bible 19B; Doubleday, 2003), on Isaiah 56:3-5
Bernard M. Levinson, *Deuteronomy and the Hermeneutics of Legal Innovation* (Oxford University Press, 1997), on Deuteronomy 23:1
Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; JPS, 1996), on Deuteronomy 23:1
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Acts of the Apostles* (Anchor Yale Bible 31; Yale University Press, 1998), on Acts 8 and eunuch inclusion
Mathew Kuefler, *The Manly Eunuch: Masculinity, Gender Ambiguity, and Christian Ideology in Late Antiquity* (University of Chicago Press, 2001)', 0, NULL, 2, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Deuteronomy, Matthew', 5, 4, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

Genesis 38 then hands us Perez (Pharez), born to Judah by his daughter-in-law Tamar — a union the narrator frames as both deceptive and incestuous (Lev 18:15 explicitly bars a man from his daughter-in-law). Under the rabbinic incest definition of *mamzer*, Perez is a textbook case. Matthew 1:3-6 and Luke 3:31-33 both route David''s line through Perez. The arithmetic must be stated carefully. Perez is the *mamzer* — generation zero, the disqualified ancestor. His descendants run Hezron, Ram (Aram), Amminadab, Nahshon (Naasson), Salmon, Boaz (Booz), Obed, Jesse, David. Counting only Perez''s descendants, David is the *ninth* in line; counting names inclusive of Perez himself, David is the tenth name in the list. The contradiction depends on the first count: David, the messianic fountainhead, enters the assembly as the ninth generation issuing from the *mamzer* Perez — one short of the tenth-generation bar that would clear his line. (Listing ten names is not the same as nine generations of descendants; Perez is excluded from the count of those barred-then-cleared, since he is the *mamzer* himself, not one of his ten generations of descendants.)

The apologetic harmonization runs three ways. First, narrow the definition: Gleason Archer''s *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* restricts *mamzer* to the child of cultic prostitution or mixed-cult marriage, not ordinary illegitimacy. Second, dismiss the count as schematic: Matthew''s genealogy is openly stylized into three sets of fourteen (Matt 1:17), telescoping generations, so it cannot be pressed arithmetically — and indeed Matthew demonstrably omits three Davidic kings (Ahaziah, Joash, Amaziah) between Joram and Uzziah in 1:8, so the list is selective by design. Third, the chronological move: the law post-dates Sinai and could not retroactively bind Perez.

Each has weaknesses. The cult-prostitute reading lacks lexical support; the Hebrew gives no such qualifier, and the LXX''s "born of a prostitute" is itself a gloss, not a cultic specification. The schematic-genealogy defense is double-edged: conceding that Matthew''s count is unreliable undercuts the very genealogical argument used to certify Davidic (and thus messianic) descent, and in any case the law''s clock runs from Perez regardless of how many names the evangelist omits — telescoping would only make the qualifying descendant arrive sooner, not later, and Luke''s fuller list (Luke 3:31-33) likewise yields nine descendants. The chronological defense ignores that Boaz, Jesse, David, and Solomon all post-date Sinai in the biblical timeline yet fall inside ten generations of Perez; the Deuteronomist presents the law as binding from Sinai forward, and a perpetuity reading ("forever," per the *ad olam* parallel) would bar the line regardless.

The more economical critical read is that the Deuteronomic exclusion and the Davidic genealogy belong to different traditions that were never harmonized. Tigay''s JPS commentary notes that Deuteronomy 23 reflects a purity ideology later intensified in Ezra-Nehemiah''s campaign against mixed marriage, while the genealogy preserves an older inclusive memory. Christine Hayes situates the *mamzer* rule within evolving Second Temple intermarriage halakhah, where the boundaries of "who may enter" were precisely the contested question. Raymond Brown and Davies-Allison observe that the evangelists'' inclusion of Tamar (and Rahab, Ruth, and Bathsheba) is theologically pointed — flagging irregular unions as part of a deliberate motif, not policing them against Deuteronomy 23. The tension is real but "disputed": whether it counts as a contradiction depends on which reading of *mamzer* one adopts and how literally one presses a genealogy the evangelist openly stylized. Verdict: overstated as bare arithmetic, but genuine as an unreconciled clash of traditions — an exclusionary purity law and an inclusive messianic genealogy bound into one canon without anyone harmonizing the count.', 'Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1996), pp. 210-211, on Deuteronomy 23:2
Christine E. Hayes, *Gentile Impurities and Jewish Identities: Intermarriage and Conversion from the Bible to the Talmud* (Oxford University Press, 2002), on Deuteronomy 23:2-3
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), on Deuteronomy 23:2
Raymond E. Brown, *The Birth of the Messiah* (Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, updated ed. 1993), pp. 71-74, on Matthew 1:3-6
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 1 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 170-172, on Matthew 1:17
Mishnah Yebamot 4:13; HALOT s.v. *mamzer*, on Deuteronomy 23:2', 0, NULL, 7, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Genesis, Deuteronomy, Matthew, Luke', 1, 3, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

Then Ruth. Ruth 1:4 introduces "Ruth the Moabitess"; she marries Boaz (Ruth 4:13), bears Obed, and the book closes with a genealogy making her the great-grandmother of David (Ruth 4:17, 22). Matthew 1:5 carries her into the line of Jesus. A Moabite woman is thus not merely admitted to the assembly but stands at the headwaters of the Davidic monarchy.

The harmonizations are several. The dominant rabbinic move (m. Yebamot 8:3; b. Yebamot 76b–77a) distinguishes *Moabi* (a male Moabite) from *Moabiyyah* (a female Moabite), restricting the ban to men: "an Ammonite but not an Ammonitess, a Moabite but not a Moabitess." Walter Kaiser and much evangelical literature adopt this. A second move appeals to Ruth''s confession ("thy people shall be my people, and thy God my God," Ruth 1:16) as a functional conversion that naturalizes her. Daniel Block, in his NAC volume, reads the law as excluding Moabites *qua* Moabites while permitting full proselytes.

None holds well. The masculine-singular forms in Deuteronomy 23:3 are the standard collective idiom of Pentateuchal legislation; they nowhere else restrict a law to males — and Nehemiah 13:1-3 demonstrably applies the same text to expel foreign *wives*, treating women as covered. The male/female distinction is transparently *ex post facto* halakhah engineered to rescue Davidic (and messianic) legitimacy; the Talmud''s own discussion frames it as resolving precisely the David problem. The conversion defense is anachronistic: the institutional category of the proselyte (*ger tsedek*) with a conversion rite is a later development, and Ruth undergoes no ritual the text describes — she is repeatedly called "the Moabitess" even after Obed''s birth (Ruth 4:10).

The critical consensus is that Ruth is a literary counter-text. Tamara Cohn Eskenazi and Tikva Frymer-Kensky, Adele Berlin, and Kirsten Nielsen argue the novella was composed (or at least deployed) in deliberate tension with the exclusionary nationalism of Ezra-Nehemiah, whose program forced the dissolution of foreign marriages (Ezra 9-10; Neh 13). The book''s repeated emphasis on Ruth''s Moabite identity is not incidental but polemical: its thesis is that a Moabite *can* be incorporated and even mother the dynasty. On this reading the tension with Deuteronomy 23 is the point — an inner-biblical argument about who counts as Israel. Reading the canon as a single harmonious voice flattens what is in fact a debate between Israelite factions. Verdict: genuine contradiction, and likely a deliberate one on the part of Ruth''s author.', 'Tamara Cohn Eskenazi and Tikva Frymer-Kensky, *Ruth* (JPS Bible Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 2011), on Ruth 1:4; 4:13-17
Kirsten Nielsen, *Ruth: A Commentary* (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 1997), on Ruth 1:16
Daniel I. Block, *Judges, Ruth* (NAC; Broadman & Holman, 1999), on Ruth 1:16
Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; JPS, 1996), pp. 211-212, on Deuteronomy 23:3
Christine E. Hayes, *Gentile Impurities and Jewish Identities* (Oxford University Press, 2002), on Deuteronomy 23:3
Adele Berlin, "Ruth and the Continuity of Israel," in *Reading Ruth: Contemporary Women Reclaim a Sacred Story*, ed. Judith A. Kates and Gail Twersky Reimer (Ballantine, 1994), on Ruth 4:13-17
Walter C. Kaiser Jr., Peter H. Davids, F. F. Bruce, and Manfred T. Brauch, *Hard Sayings of the Bible* (IVP, 1996), on Deuteronomy 23:3
