Daniel I. Block, *The Book of Ezekiel, Chapters 25-48* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 1998), on Ezekiel 25:13
Mordechai Cogan and Hayim Tadmor, *II Kings* (AB 11; Doubleday, 1988), on 2 Kings 14:7
Iain W. Provan, *1 and 2 Kings* (NIBC 7; Hendrickson, 1995), on 2 Kings 14:7', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Deuteronomy, 2 Kings, Ezekiel, Obadiah', 6, 3, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The vagueness of *ervat davar* generated the first-century rabbinic dispute Jesus is implicitly addressing. The House of Shammai read it narrowly (sexual misconduct); the House of Hillel read it broadly enough to license divorce "even if she spoiled his dish" (m. Gittin 9:10). David Instone-Brewer''s influential reconstruction is that Jesus targets the Hillelite "any cause" divorce — the lax abuse of Deuteronomy 24 — rather than abolishing all remarriage; Craig Blomberg similarly reads Jesus as intensifying the Mosaic intent for the kingdom era, not contradicting it.

In Matthew, this harmonization is strong. The *porneia* clause, the explicit framing as a Hillel-Shammai debate ("Is it lawful... for *any cause*?" Matt 19:3), and the appeal back to Genesis 1-2 over against Deuteronomy''s concession (Matt 19:8, "because of your hardness of heart") together make Matthew''s Jesus a participant in an inner-Jewish halakhic argument rather than a flat contradictor of Moses. To that extent the tension is partially harmonized.

But Luke 16:18 — the verse actually cited — carries none of this apparatus. It is an unqualified declarative: marrying a divorced woman *is* adultery, full stop. If Jesus were merely correcting a Hillelite abuse, Luke''s redactor either did not preserve that nuance or did not intend it. Read on its own terms, Luke 16:18 brands as adultery precisely the remarriage Deuteronomy 24:2 permits.

The critical reconstruction sharpens this. John P. Meier (*A Marginal Jew*, vol. 4) argues at length that the historical Jesus prohibited divorce absolutely — a position stricter than both Deuteronomic and Pharisaic law and one of the few rulings multiply attested (Mark, Q/Luke, Paul in 1 Cor 7:10-11) — and that the Matthean *porneia* exception is a secondary softening by the Matthean community accommodating communal realities. Joseph Fitzmyer reaches the same conclusion on Luke, and his earlier study connected the exception to the Qumran prohibition of certain unions. On this reconstruction the contradiction with Deuteronomy is real and deliberate on Jesus'' part: a prophetic abrogation of a Mosaic concession, not a clarification of it. Verdict: harmonizable in Matthew via the Hillel-Shammai frame; genuinely contradictory in Luke, where the unqualified ruling overturns the Deuteronomic permission.', 'John P. Meier, *A Marginal Jew: Rethinking the Historical Jesus*, Vol. 4: *Law and Love* (Anchor Yale Bible Reference Library; Yale University Press, 2009), on Luke 16:18
David Instone-Brewer, *Divorce and Remarriage in the Bible: The Social and Literary Context* (Eerdmans, 2002), on Deuteronomy 24:1; Matthew 19:3
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (AB 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1119-1124, on Luke 16:18
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, "The Matthean Divorce Texts and Some New Palestinian Evidence," *Theological Studies* 37 (1976): pp. 197-226, on Matthew 19:9
Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; JPS, 1996), pp. 220-222, on Deuteronomy 24:1-4
Craig L. Blomberg, *Matthew* (NAC 22; Broadman & Holman, 1992), on Matthew 5:32
Raymond F. Collins, *Divorce in the New Testament* (Liturgical Press, 1992), on Mark 10:11-12; Luke 16:18
