Paul''s premise (3:10a) is the reverse of Deuteronomy''s plain force: "as many as are of the works of the law are under a curse." On the surface Deuteronomy promises curse for *non*-compliance (with blessing for compliance in ch. 28), while Paul deploys the same verse to place those who *rely* on law-compliance under curse.

The logical gap is the famous "missing premise." Paul''s syllogism only works if one supplies an unstated middle term — that no one in fact keeps the whole law, so the curse of Deuteronomy 27:26 falls on all who attempt justification by it. N. T. Wright, Daniel Fuller, and many others reconstruct exactly this: Paul is not contradicting Deuteronomy but invoking its own curse-sanction against a humanity that cannot meet the "all things" standard. James Dunn''s New Perspective variant relocates the curse — it falls not on Torah-obedience as such but on the law used as an ethnic boundary marker excluding Gentiles, so Paul critiques covenantal exclusivism, not Torah-keeping. Both forms are exegetically serious and cohere with Paul''s surrounding argument, where he immediately cites Habakkuk 2:4 and Leviticus 18:5 in 3:11-12 to oppose faith and law as competing principles of life.

The implicit-premise reading does substantial exegetical work and is defensible at the level of Paul''s rhetoric. But it requires importing a premise Paul never states in Galatians and which he elsewhere appears to deny: in Philippians 3:6 Paul calls himself, "as to righteousness under the law, blameless" (ἄμεμπτος) — implying the law *can* be kept at the relevant level. If perfect law-keeping were impossible by definition, that self-description would be incoherent. E. P. Sanders and Heikki Räisänen press exactly this point. Sanders''s verdict in *Paul, the Law, and the Jewish People* (the key "reasoning backward" discussion falls in the law chapter, roughly pp. 20-25 of the 1983 Fortress edition) is that Paul reasons backward: convinced first that righteousness is in Christ (and therefore *not* in the law), he then assembles scriptural proofs that strain against their plain sense. The Deuteronomic curse was a sanction for covenant infidelity, not a verdict on the whole enterprise of Torah; Paul re-reads it midrashically to serve a conclusion the Deuteronomist would not recognize. Räisänen documents the resulting inconsistencies across the Pauline corpus, treating Galatians 3:10 as one node in a pattern rather than an isolated slip.

Hans Dieter Betz and Martinus de Boer treat Galatians 3:10 as a deliberate, rhetorically charged reappropriation rather than neutral exegesis — Betz reading the verse within the forensic-rhetorical architecture of the letter, de Boer within Paul''s apocalyptic antithesis between the present evil age and the new creation. Tigay''s reading of Deuteronomy 27 underscores the point from the Hebrew side: the dodecalogue of curses at Shechem is a covenant-ratification liturgy targeting specific covenant breaches, not a meditation on the impossibility of obedience. The Deuteronomic curse presupposes that obedience is achievable — "this commandment is not too hard for you" (Deut 30:11) — which is the inverse of the premise Paul''s argument requires.

Whether to call this a "contradiction" or a "creative reappropriation" is partly a judgment about how much hermeneutical license one grants Paul. The surface tension is undeniable — Deuteronomy curses non-compliance; Paul effectively curses reliance on compliance — and the standard harmonization rests on a silent premise that sits awkwardly with Philippians 3:6 and with Deuteronomy''s own optimism about obedience. Hence "disputed." Verdict: real surface contradiction, resolvable only by importing an unstated and arguably un-Pauline premise about the impossibility of law-keeping.', 'E. P. Sanders, *Paul, the Law, and the Jewish People* (Fortress, 1983), pp. 20-25, on Galatians 3:10
Heikki Räisänen, *Paul and the Law* (WUNT 29; Mohr Siebeck, 2nd ed. 1987), on Galatians 3:10
N. T. Wright, *The Climax of the Covenant: Christ and the Law in Pauline Theology* (T&T Clark, 1991), on Galatians 3:10
James D. G. Dunn, *The Epistle to the Galatians* (BNTC; Hendrickson, 1993), on Galatians 3:10
Hans Dieter Betz, *Galatians* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1979), on Galatians 3:10
Martinus C. de Boer, *Galatians: A Commentary* (NTL; Westminster John Knox, 2011), on Galatians 3:10
Daniel P. Fuller, *Gospel and Law: Contrast or Continuum? The Hermeneutics of Dispensationalism and Covenant Theology* (Eerdmans, 1980), on Galatians 3:10
Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; JPS, 1996), p. 254, on Deuteronomy 27:26', 0, NULL, 4, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Deuteronomy, Galatians', 5, 3, 3, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The leading harmonization is form-critical. Moshe Weinfeld demonstrated in granular detail that Deuteronomy 28''s curse catalogue is patterned on Neo-Assyrian vassal treaties (notably the Vassal Treaties of Esarhaddon) and earlier Hittite treaty forms, where extravagant, hyperbolic curses are a genre convention designed to terrify the vassal into loyalty. John Walton and Daniel Block accordingly read "YHWH will rejoice to destroy you" not as a literal psychological report but as covenant-curse rhetoric. Paul Copan, in *Is God a Moral Monster?*, generalizes the move: ANE treaty curses are deliberately over-the-top, so the verse dramatizes the gravity of covenant breach rather than disclosing divine sadism. On this reading Ezekiel 18 gives the "real" divine disposition and Deuteronomy 28 the rhetorical warning.

The genre observation is sound and partially defuses the tension: the two verses operate in different registers — covenant-curse hyperbole versus prophetic pastoral appeal — and need not be read as flatly competing psychological claims. To that degree the contradiction is partially harmonized.

But the defense relocates as much as it resolves. If Deut 28:63 is hyperbole, the portrait of God it offers is, on the apologist''s own terms, not to be trusted as descriptive — which concedes the critical point that the canon''s God-language is shaped by rhetorical context rather than uniform. Moreover, the pleasure-in-destruction motif is not isolated to one treaty curse. Proverbs 1:26 has personified Wisdom laugh (אֶלְעַג) at calamity; Psalm 2:4 and Psalm 37:13 have YHWH laugh at the wicked; Deuteronomy 28:63''s symmetry is echoed conceptually elsewhere. The motif has too many independent attestations to be quarantined as a single stylized curse, and Ezekiel''s emphatic threefold denial reads as a deliberate counter-claim, not a complementary nuance.

The critical read frames this as competing theological streams. Walther Eichrodt, Jon Levenson, and Yochanan Muffs note that the Hebrew Bible sustains divergent portraits of YHWH — a wrathful covenant-enforcer in the Deuteronomistic tradition and a longsuffering, repentance-seeking God in much prophetic material (Ezekiel, Jonah 4, Joel 2:13). Zimmerli and Block both situate Ezekiel 18 within the exilic crisis over individual responsibility, where the prophet stresses God''s desire for repentance against a fatalistic populace. The two portraits cannot be fully fused without softening Deuteronomy''s *sus* past what it says or hardening Ezekiel''s *chaphets* past what it says. Verdict: partially harmonizable through genre once the treaty-curse register of Deuteronomy 28 is recognized, but a genuine residual tension remains between the Deuteronomistic and prophetic characterizations of God.', 'Moshe Weinfeld, *Deuteronomy and the Deuteronomic School* (Oxford University Press, 1972), pp. 116-129, on Deuteronomy 28:63
Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; JPS, 1996), p. 268, on Deuteronomy 28:63
