Harmonizations split denominationally. The Reformed reading (Grudem, Piper, Frame) takes the predestination texts at face value and treats the choice-language as describing the genuine *experience* of deciding, which is compatible with an underlying divine decree (compatibilism). The Arminian reading (Olson, Oden) takes the choice-texts at face value and recasts predestination as God''s foreknowledge of free responses (the prescience view), or as corporate rather than individual election. Molinism (William Lane Craig) interposes "middle knowledge": God knows what each free creature *would* do in every possible circumstance and actualizes the world he wills from among those, preserving both libertarian freedom and meticulous providence.

Each harmonization deploys philosophical machinery the biblical authors plainly lacked. The compatibilist reading drains the imperatives of force — if the choice is decreed, "choose life" addressed to the non-elect is, at best, a means and at worst theater. The prescience reading collides with Romans 9:11, which expressly locates election *before* any deeds and "not because of works," making foreseen choices an awkward basis. Molinism resolves the puzzle by stipulating a metaphysical category (counterfactuals of creaturely freedom grounded prior to the divine creative decree) that Scripture nowhere articulates.

The more economical critical read is that the biblical writers held genuinely different views. Deuteronomistic theology presumes covenantal moral agency; Pauline theology, especially Romans 9-11, develops hard-edged election language partly to address an acute historical problem — why most Jews had not accepted Jesus as messiah. James Dunn, E. P. Sanders, and Heikki Räisänen read Romans 9 as Paul wrestling with that specific crisis using deterministic categories he does not apply consistently — note that within the same argument Romans 10:9-13 hinges salvation on confessing and believing, and 11:23 envisions the hardened being grafted back "if they do not persist in unbelief." Fitzmyer''s Anchor commentary similarly resists collapsing Paul into a systematic predestinarian. The Hebrew Bible is not a single author, and Paul is not a systematic theologian; the determinism-versus-free-will frame was imposed later, by Augustine, the Reformers, and their opponents, on texts that were not composed to answer that question. Verdict: disputed — a genuine and unresolved tension that the canon contains rather than reconciles, and that each systematic harmonization can address only by importing extrabiblical philosophical apparatus.', 'E. P. Sanders, *Paul, the Law, and the Jewish People* (Fortress, 1983), on Romans 9:11-22
James D. G. Dunn, *Romans 9-16* (WBC 38B; Word, 1988), on Romans 9:11-22
