The key to the tension is the idiom "go out and come in" (*tset wabo*). Across the Hebrew Bible this phrase denotes active leadership, especially military command, not general mobility: Numbers 27:17 uses it for the leader who will "go out before them and come in before them"; 1 Samuel 18:13-16 describes David "going out and coming in" before the people as a war-leader; 1 Kings 3:7 has Solomon confess he does not know how to "go out or come in" as a ruler. On this well-attested usage, Moses in 31:2 is not lamenting physical decay but announcing the end of his leadership tenure — reinforced by the immediately following clause, which gives the operative reason: YHWH has barred him from crossing the Jordan (cf. Num 20:12; Deut 3:27). The disqualification is juridical-theological, not medical.

The apologetic harmonization (Gleason Archer, Daniel Block, Peter Craigie) draws precisely this distinction: 34:7 describes Moses'' preserved physical vigor, while 31:2 concerns his leadership office and the divine prohibition. Rashi and Ibn Ezra likewise read 31:2 in light of its second half — Moses cannot lead the conquest because God has forbidden him to enter, not because his body has failed. This reading is not special pleading; it follows the standard semantics of *tset wabo* and respects the syntax of 31:2, where the divine prohibition is supplied as the explanation. The two statements are therefore logically compatible: a man of preserved vigor can nonetheless be debarred from leading the conquest by divine decree. The tension is genuinely dissolved at the narrative level.

Worth noting, though, that the harmonization operates within the final-form text and does not depend on historicity. Source critics (Noth, Friedman, Tigay) assign Deuteronomy 34 — the death-and-burial notice with its eulogistic "his eye was not dim" — to a different, later editorial hand (a Priestly or post-Deuteronomistic redactor) than the older Deuteronomic material in chapter 31. On this view the vigor-at-120 line is the kind of laudatory embellishment that accrues to founder figures, and the two passages were composed by different writers who were not measuring Moses for medical consistency. That the verses *also* harmonize semantically is convenient for the redactor''s seam: the leadership idiom in 31:2 leaves the eulogistic claim of 34:7 untouched. Verdict: harmonizable — "go out and come in" is leadership/military idiom, and 31:2 expressly grounds Moses'' withdrawal in the divine prohibition, not in physical decline, so it does not contradict the eulogy of preserved vigor in 34:7.', 'Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; JPS, 1996), pp. 290, 338, on Deuteronomy 31:2; 34:7
Peter C. Craigie, *The Book of Deuteronomy* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 1976), on Deuteronomy 31:2
Richard Elliott Friedman, *The Bible with Sources Revealed* (HarperSanFrancisco, 2003), on Deuteronomy 34:1-12
Martin Noth, *The Deuteronomistic History* (JSOTSup 15; Sheffield Academic Press, 2nd ed. 1981), on Deuteronomy 34:7
Daniel I. Block, *Deuteronomy* (NIVAC; Zondervan, 2012), on Deuteronomy 31:2
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), on Deuteronomy 31:2; 34:7', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', 'Deuteronomy', 7, 2, 1, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard harmonization (D. A. Carson, Craig Blomberg, Robert Stein, Darrell Bock) invokes the ancient legal-cultural principle *qui facit per alium facit per se* — "he who acts through another acts himself." The principle is genuinely attested: rabbinic law treats an agent (שָׁלִיחַ, *shaliach*) as the legal extension of the principal (m. Berakhot 5:5; b. Qiddushin 41b, "a man''s agent is as himself"), and Greco-Roman usage similarly credits a patron with what his emissaries do. On this reading Matthew telescopes the embassies, narrating as the centurion''s own act what was done through his representatives, while Luke preserves the mediating detail. Since both can be true of the same event, there is no formal contradiction at the level of what occurred.

The agency principle is real and the harmonization is not arbitrary — this is its strongest form, and at the bare level of "what happened" it can stand. Hence "partially harmonized." But it strains against Matthew''s narrative grammar. Matthew 8:7 has Jesus reply in direct discourse, "I will come and heal him," and 8:8 has the centurion answer in the first person, "I am not worthy that you should come under my roof." This is reciprocal dialogue, narrated with the centurion as the speaking party present to Jesus. Agency convention would more naturally yield mediated phrasing of the kind Luke actually supplies ("he sent... saying through them"); Matthew instead compresses the scene so thoroughly that the principal personally converses. The harmonization rescues the event but not the *narration*: Matthew''s text, read as it stands, places the centurion before Jesus.

The critical frame is the Synoptic Problem. The pericope is absent from Mark and shared by Matthew and Luke, marking it as Q (or a closely related sayings tradition) — one of the rare Q narratives. Joseph Fitzmyer, John Nolland, and Ulrich Luz argue the evangelists shaped a common tradition to different ends: Matthew streamlines to spotlight Jesus'' direct encounter with exemplary Gentile faith (climaxing in 8:10-12, "not even in Israel have I found such faith"), while Luke elaborates the double embassy to dramatize Jewish-Gentile mediation, a recurring Lukan theme (cf. the God-fearer Cornelius in Acts 10). The differences are redactional, not reportorial. Verdict: harmonizable at the level of event via the agency principle, but the divergence in *who approaches whom* is best explained as the two evangelists redacting a shared Q tradition for distinct theological purposes rather than as independent precise reportage.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 2 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1991), pp. 17-22, on Matthew 8:5-8
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 8-20* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2001), on Matthew 8:5-13
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke I-IX* (AB 28; Doubleday, 1981), pp. 648-653, on Luke 7:1-10
John Nolland, *Luke 1-9:20* (WBC 35A; Word, 1989), on Luke 7:1-10
Robert H. Stein, *Luke* (NAC 24; Broadman & Holman, 1992), on Luke 7:3-7
Craig L. Blomberg, *The Historical Reliability of the Gospels* (IVP Academic, 2nd ed. 2007), on Matthew 8:5-8
D. A. Carson, *Matthew*, in *The Expositor''s Bible Commentary*, Vol. 9, rev. ed., ed. Tremper Longman III and David E. Garland (Zondervan, 2010), on Matthew 8:5-8
John P. Meier, *A Marginal Jew*, Vol. 2: *Mentor, Message, and Miracles* (Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, 1994), on Matthew 8:5-13; Luke 7:1-10', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Luke', 10, 2, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The apologetic harmonization (D. A. Carson, Murray Harris) distinguishes *de jure* from *de facto* sovereignty: YHWH is the rightful, ontological king of the cosmos, while Satan is a usurper exercising delegated, temporary, permitted influence over the fallen "present evil age," a regency that the eschaton terminates. Greg Boyd''s *God at War* presses a thoroughgoing warfare worldview in which the cosmos is contested territory throughout Scripture.

This two-tier reading has genuine Pauline support and coheres internally within the New Testament. Paul''s apocalyptic framework treats "this present evil age" (Gal 1:4) as dominated by hostile powers under divine sufferance: the "rulers of this age" crucified the Lord of glory (1 Cor 2:8), and creation is subjected to bondage awaiting liberation (Rom 8:20-21, 38-39). The title "god of this age" in 2 Cor 4:4 is qualified temporally (τοῦ αἰῶνος τούτου) precisely to mark a bounded, penultimate dominion, not an ontological rivalry to the Creator. At the level of New Testament theology, the *de jure/de facto* distinction is a coherent and arguably the intended reading. To that degree the tension is partially harmonized.

What the harmonization cannot do is read this two-stage sovereignty back into the Hebrew Bible. When Joshua 2:11 or Isaiah 37:16 affirms YHWH as God of all the kingdoms, the Deuteronomistic and Isaianic authors are not reserving a category for "another power that presently administers the nations." The bifurcated cosmic regency is a development of Second Temple Judaism, not a feature of pre-exilic Israelite thought. The earlier Hebrew Bible in fact knows a different model — the divine council of Deuteronomy 32:8-9, where in the Qumran (4QDeut-j) and the earliest recoverable Septuagint reading the Most High apportions the nations among "the sons of God" while keeping Israel as his own portion. The text-critical point should be stated precisely: 4QDeut-j reads בני אלהים ("sons of God") and the primary, oldest recoverable LXX reading is υἱῶν θεοῦ ("sons of God"), both against the MT''s "sons of Israel" (בני ישראל), which is widely judged a theologically motivated revision; the reading ἀγγέλων θεοῦ ("angels of God") found in some Greek witnesses is a later, secondary harmonizing variant. Psalm 82 likewise has God judging the subordinate *elohim* of the nations. That council framework is closer to henotheistic administration than to a single satanic usurper, and it is itself distinct from the absolute monotheism of the Isaiah 37 / Psalm 47 affirmations, which signals development even within the Hebrew corpus. Mark S. Smith has reconstructed this trajectory from the Ugaritic divine-council background through Israel''s emergent monotheism, making clear that the council model and the later cosmic dualism are successive, not identical, stages.

John J. Collins, Paolo Sacchi, and Larry Hurtado have traced how apocalyptic literature — the Watcher myth of 1 Enoch, the dualism of the Qumran Community Rule (1QS 3-4, the Two Spirits), and Jubilees with its figure of Mastema — progressively assigned fallen angelic powers (Belial, Mastema, eventually ὁ Σατανᾶς) authority over the present age. Hurtado''s work is especially relevant because it shows that this elaboration of hostile intermediary powers coexisted with, rather than displaced, a robust Jewish monotheism: subordinate powers could be granted bounded dominion without compromising the Creator''s unique sovereignty. The New Testament inherits this framework and applies it Christologically, so that Satan''s defeat is the inauguration of the age to come. "Ruler of this world" is therefore not extractable from the Hebrew Bible; it is a later cosmological development retrofitted onto older monotheistic affirmations. Verdict: internally harmonizable within the New Testament''s de jure/de facto and two-ages scheme, but that scheme is a Second Temple development that does not exist in the Hebrew Bible texts cited, so the cross-testamental tension is real even where the NT-internal logic coheres.', 'John J. Collins, *The Apocalyptic Imagination: An Introduction to Jewish Apocalyptic Literature* (Eerdmans, 3rd ed. 2016), on 2 Corinthians 4:4
Paolo Sacchi, *The History of the Second Temple Period* (JSOTSup 285; Sheffield Academic Press, 2000), on John 12:31
Larry W. Hurtado, *One God, One Lord: Early Christian Devotion and Ancient Jewish Monotheism* (T&T Clark, 3rd ed. 2015), on Joshua 2:11
Greg A. Boyd, *God at War: The Bible and Spiritual Conflict* (IVP Academic, 1997), on Luke 4:5-7
D. A. Carson, *The Gospel According to John* (Pillar New Testament Commentary; Eerdmans, 1991), on John 12:31
