The consensus apologetic harmonization is the two-monuments reading, defended by Gleason Archer, Richard Hess, and David Howard: Joshua erected one set at Gilgal (the stones the tribal representatives carried, vv. 1-8, 20-24) and a second set in the riverbed as a separate memorial (v. 9). This reading has genuine grammatical footing. The Hebrew of v. 9, *wĕ''et-shtêm ''esrēh ''ăvānîm hēqîm yĕhôshua'' bĕtôk hayyardēn*, says simply that Joshua "set up" twelve stones without identifying them as the same stones the men carried; nothing in the consonantal text forces a single set. On this reading there is no contradiction — just two monuments and a compressed narrative. The reading is permissible, and it is why the entry is best classed as partially harmonized rather than a flat collision.

Where it strains is the texture of the passage. Verse 20 specifies "those twelve stones, *which they took out of Jordan*" (*''ăsher lāqĕchû min-hayyardēn*); the demonstrative and relative clause point back to the only set of stones the chapter has narrated lifting — those of vv. 3-8. If v. 9 introduces an entirely new second monument, it does so abruptly, with no transitional marker, mid-sequence. The Septuagint compounds the difficulty: LXX Joshua 4:9 reads that Joshua set up "other twelve stones" (*allous dōdeka lithous*), an explicit harmonizing plus that the Masoretic Text lacks. That the Greek tradition felt the need to add "other" is itself evidence that ancient readers already perceived the very ambiguity the two-monuments theory exploits — and that the smoother reading is secondary.

The critical-scholarly read goes to source and tradition history. The clause "unto this day" (*''ad hayyôm hazzeh*) in both v. 9 and the Gilgal etiology is a classic etiological formula: the narrator is explaining why visible stone monuments stood at named sites in the editor''s own day. Burke Long''s study of Old Testament etiological narrative treats exactly this kind of "and they are there to this day" tag as the signature of a tradition explaining an existing landmark. J. Alberto Soggin and Robert Boling argue that Joshua 4 weaves together two parallel etiologies about the crossing — one anchored to the cultic site of Gilgal, one to a stone feature at or near the ford — that a redactor combined into a single narrative without smoothing the seams. Hans Wilhelm Hertzberg likewise read the chapter as composite. On this model the doubling is not a deliberate pair of monuments but the residue of merging two stories about the same memorial event.

The verdict turns on parsimony. The two-monuments harmonization is grammatically possible and even has the LXX "other" to support it, so the contradiction is not airtight. But the demonstrative in v. 20, the abruptness of v. 9, the doubled etiological formula, and the secondary character of the Greek plus all point the same direction: the cleanest explanation of the textual oddities is the merging of two parallel traditions, not a tidy pair of intended monuments. Verdict: partially harmonizable, but the doublet hypothesis better explains the seams.', 'J. Alberto Soggin, *Joshua: A Commentary* (OTL; Westminster, 1972), pp. 62-66, on Joshua 4:9, 20
Robert G. Boling, *Joshua* (AB 6; Doubleday, 1982), pp. 169-180, on Joshua 4
Hans Wilhelm Hertzberg, *Die Bücher Josua, Richter, Ruth* (ATD 9; Vandenhoeck & Ruprecht, rev. ed. 1969), on Joshua 4
Burke O. Long, *The Problem of Etiological Narrative in the Old Testament* (BZAW 108; de Gruyter, 1968), on the etiological ''unto this day'' formula
Richard S. Hess, *Joshua* (TOTC; IVP, 1996), on Joshua 4:9, defending two monuments
David M. Howard Jr., *Joshua* (NAC 5; Broadman & Holman, 1998), on Joshua 4:9
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), on Joshua 4
Trent C. Butler, *Joshua 1-12* (WBC 7A; Zondervan, 2nd ed. 2014), on Joshua 4', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', 'Joshua', 7, 2, 1, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

Apologists harmonize along two lines. The rebuild line, associated with Kenneth Kitchen and many evangelical commentators, treats "forever" as hyperbole or as a delimited ruin-period and posits that Ai was resettled later. The wrong-Ai line notes that *''ay* is a common noun ("ruin") and proposes that the post-exilic Ai is simply a different site bearing the same generic name. John Bimson and, more prominently, Bryant Wood have pursued alternative identifications.

Both defenses run into trouble before archaeology even enters. The hyperbole reading empties *tēl ''ôlām* of force precisely where the narrator has built a name-pun on permanence; conquest rhetoric elsewhere that apologists read literally is here recast as figurative only because Nehemiah forces the issue. The two-Ai theory lacks textual support: Nehemiah pairs "Bethel and Ai" as adjacent settlements, the identical pairing found in Genesis 12:8, Genesis 13:3, and Joshua 7-8, where Ai lies east of Bethel near Beth-aven. Geographically and textually the post-exilic Ai is the conquest Ai.

The deeper problem is that neither claim survives the dig. The standard identification of Ai is et-Tell, excavated by Judith Marquet-Krause in the 1930s and Joseph Callaway in 1964-1972. Both found a substantial Early Bronze city destroyed around 2400 BCE, then an occupational gap, then a modest unfortified Iron I village (c. 1200-1050 BCE) — and crucially no Late Bronze Age city at all. At every plausible date for an Israelite conquest the site was either an ancient heap or a small unwalled hamlet, not the fortified royal city Joshua 8 storms. The town that needed conquering did not exist when the narrative sets it. Callaway, who began as a believer in the biblical account, concluded the archaeology could not be reconciled with Joshua 8.

William Dever and Israel Finkelstein fold this into the broader case that the conquest narrative is etiological folk-history: the name *hā''ay*, "the ruin," attached to a conspicuous ancient mound, and the story of Joshua "making" it a ruin retrojects an Israelite hero to explain ruins that already lay on the landscape. On that reading the conflict with Nehemiah resolves into a worse problem — Joshua 8 is not reportage at all, so its "forever" never described a destruction that historically occurred. The Iron I village at et-Tell, in turn, plausibly supplies the modest "Ai" of the Persian-period lists.

Bryant Wood''s relocation of Ai to Khirbet el-Maqatir, a smaller site north of et-Tell with some Late Bronze and Iron I material, is the most determined apologetic response; it seeks a site whose stratigraphy could fit a fifteenth- or thirteenth-century destruction. But mainstream archaeology has not accepted the identification: the site is too small to match the "city" and royal apparatus of Joshua 7-8, the dating is contested, and the move is driven by the need to rescue the text rather than by independent topographic argument. Verdict: genuine contradiction within the canon, compounded by an archaeological record that undercuts both the text and its harmonizations.', 'Joseph A. Callaway, "The 1968-1969 ''Ai (et-Tell) Excavations," *BASOR* 198 (1970): 7-31, on the stratigraphy of et-Tell
William G. Dever, *Who Were the Early Israelites and Where Did They Come From?* (Eerdmans, 2003), on Ai and the conquest tradition
Israel Finkelstein and Neil Asher Silberman, *The Bible Unearthed* (Free Press, 2001), pp. 81-82, on Ai
Trent C. Butler, *Joshua 1-12* (WBC 7A; Zondervan, 2nd ed. 2014), on Joshua 8:28
Kenneth A. Kitchen, *On the Reliability of the Old Testament* (Eerdmans, 2003), on the conquest sites
Bryant G. Wood, "The Search for Joshua''s Ai," in *Critical Issues in Early Israelite History*, ed. Richard S. Hess et al. (Eisenbrauns, 2008), proposing Khirbet el-Maqatir
Robert G. Boling, *Joshua* (AB 6; Doubleday, 1982), on Joshua 8', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', 'Joshua, Nehemiah', 7, 2, 1, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The simplest harmonization is lexical. Genesis 8:22''s verb is *lō'' yishbotû* — "shall not cease/Sabbath," i.e., shall not be permanently abolished. John Walton, John Sailhamer, and Kenneth Mathews read this as a standing ordinance guaranteeing the durability of the created order, not a moment-by-moment promise that the heavens will never be interrupted. A single anomalous day within an otherwise unbroken system does not negate a long-term covenant. At the level of textual logic this is sound: "shall not cease" and "was paused once" are not strict contradictories. Some apologists (Hugh Ross) press further, recasting Joshua 10 as a localized atmospheric phenomenon, prolonged refraction, or even a prayer for the sun''s heat to relent during the hailstorm of v. 11 — but these readings abandon the surface sense to buy modern plausibility and are not required by the text.

The more decisive consideration is genre, and here the text itself supplies the key. Joshua 10:13 states that the episode is "written in the book of Jashar" (*sēfer hayyāshār*), a lost anthology of Israelite verse also cited at 2 Samuel 1:18. Verses 12-13 are poetry — parallelistic, addressed to sun and moon as if commanding them — embedded in prose. Robert Boling, J. Alberto Soggin, and Richard Nelson all argue that the original was a fragment of war poetry, and that the literalizing clause "about a whole day" (*kĕyôm tāmîm*) is the prose narrator''s interpretation of a poetic image. Ancient battle poetry routinely magnifies a day; "the sun stood still" is the idiom of a day that felt decisive and unending, comparable to the cosmic imagery of Judges 5:20 ("the stars in their courses fought against Sisera"). On this reading the poem never asserted a halt in celestial mechanics; the prose frame did, secondarily.

Where the harmonization cannot fully rescue the literal reading is cosmology. Joshua 10:12 has Joshua command the sun to stand still *over Gibeon* and the moon *over the valley of Aijalon* — bodies that move across a fixed firmament and can be told to halt at map coordinates. The miracle is constructed inside a pre-Copernican cosmos; "the sun stood still" describes solar motion stopping, not terrestrial rotation. Read literally as a historical event, its physics are incoherent on any post-Galilean understanding, which is exactly why Ross-type defenses must abandon the plain sense. The genre reading avoids this by not requiring the text to be physics at all.

The net result favors partial harmonization. The pause-versus-cease distinction defuses the strict logical collision with Genesis 8:22, and the Book of Jashar citation shows the long-day text is quoted poetry rather than astronomical reportage. The contradiction emerges only when both passages are flattened to literal historical-scientific claims — precisely the move the genre-critical reading rejects. Verdict: largely harmonizable on genre and lexical grounds; the residual problem is cosmological, not covenantal.', 'Robert G. Boling, *Joshua* (AB 6; Doubleday, 1982), pp. 281-285, on the Book of Jashar fragment
Richard D. Nelson, *Joshua: A Commentary* (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 1997), on Joshua 10:12-13
J. Alberto Soggin, *Joshua: A Commentary* (OTL; Westminster, 1972), on Joshua 10:12-13
John H. Walton, *The Lost World of the Israelite Conquest* (IVP Academic, 2017), on the long day and ancient cosmology
Kenneth A. Mathews, *Genesis 1-11:26* (NAC 1A; Broadman & Holman, 1996), on Genesis 8:22
Trent C. Butler, *Joshua 1-12* (WBC 7A; Zondervan, 2nd ed. 2014), on Joshua 10:12-13', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', 'Genesis, Joshua', 7, 2, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The strongest harmonization, defended by Daniel Block, K. Lawson Younger, and Richard Hess, attends to the syntax of v. 33. The verse opens with Horam "coming up to help Lachish" — the city Joshua is besieging in v. 31-32, not Gezer. The object of "smote" is "him and his people," i.e., Horam and the relief force he led into the field. Joshua nowhere in chapter 10 narrates marching on, besieging, or capturing the city of Gezer itself; the verse records the destruction of an army that sortied to relieve Lachish, after which Israel moves on. On this reading the population of Gezer was never claimed annihilated, the city was never occupied by Israel, and Judges 1:29 simply records the later, lasting failure of Ephraim to dispossess it. This is exegetically careful and genuinely eases the Gezer-specific tension.

The defense holds for Gezer in isolation, which is why this entry is best classed as partially harmonized. But it cannot quiet the larger seam, and the canon itself keeps reopening it. Joshua 11:23 declares, "So Joshua took the whole land, according to all that the LORD said unto Moses... and the land rested from war," and Joshua 21:43-45 insists, "there failed not ought of any good thing which the LORD had spoken." Against these totalizing summaries, Judges 1 supplies a systematic catalog of failure: Israel did not drive out the inhabitants of Gezer, Beth-shean, Taanach, Megiddo, Aijalon, Dor, Ibleam, or the Philistine plain (Judges 1:27-35). The friction is not really about Gezer alone; it is between the idealized all-conquered land of Joshua and the patchwork, incomplete settlement of Judges 1. Gezer is one data point in a structural disagreement.

Archaeology and the rest of the canon both side with the Judges picture. Excavation at Tell Gezer shows continuous Canaanite occupation through the Late Bronze Age and into Iron I, with no destruction layer matching an Israelite conquest in the relevant window. And the biblical narrative itself concedes the point: 1 Kings 9:16 reports that Pharaoh "had gone up, and taken Gezer, and burnt it with fire, and slain the Canaanites that dwelt in the city," then gave it to Solomon as a dowry. A still-Canaanite Gezer in the tenth century, taken not by Israel but by Egypt, confirms that the city was never destroyed in Joshua''s day.

The critical-scholarly synthesis, following Martin Noth, Frank Moore Cross, and Richard Nelson, reads the Deuteronomistic Historian as having combined an idealized total-conquest source with a more piecemeal regional-settlement tradition, preserving both without reconciliation. Israel Finkelstein''s settlement archaeology supports the gradual, internal-emergence model of the Judges stratum over the blitz-conquest of the Joshua summaries. Verdict: the Gezer verse itself is plausibly harmonizable as a field-army defeat, but it sits inside a genuine, unresolved Joshua-versus-Judges contradiction about the completeness of the conquest.', 'Daniel I. Block, *Judges, Ruth* (NAC 6; Broadman & Holman, 1999), on Judges 1:29 and the conquest summaries
K. Lawson Younger Jr., *Judges and Ruth* (NIVAC; Zondervan, 2002), on the partial conquest
Richard S. Hess, *Joshua* (TOTC; IVP, 1996), on Joshua 10:33
