The leading harmonization, defended by Daniel Block, K. Lawson Younger, and Trent Butler, is the dynastic-name reading: "Jabin" (Hebrew *yāvîn*, plausibly connected to a root for "discernment") functioned as a recurring throne-name or dynastic title at Hazor, much as "Pharaoh" served Egypt, "Abimelech" the Philistine kings of Genesis, or "Ben-hadad" Aram-Damascus. On this view Joshua''s Jabin and Deborah''s Jabin are two historical kings of one Hazorite line, and the two destructions are two events separated by a rebuild. Hess and the Provan-Long-Longman history take this line, noting that ancient Near Eastern dynasties did reuse throne names and that destroyed cities were routinely reoccupied.

The reading is not unreasonable in principle, which is why this is partial rather than no harmonization. The difficulty is the density of shared detail. Joshua 11:10 stresses that Hazor was "the head of all those kingdoms"; Judges 4 presents Hazor again as the dominant regional power oppressing Israel — the same exalted status the conquest account claimed to have ended. Joshua''s account emphasizes total extermination and a burned city; Judges has Hazor fielding nine hundred iron chariots under Sisera not long after. A city annihilated to the last breath does not readily reconstitute itself as the head of all the kingdoms within a generation or two. Moreover, the actual antagonist of Judges 4-5 is Sisera, based at Harosheth-ha-goiim; Jabin is something of a frame figure, present in the prose (ch. 4) but absent from the older poem (the Song of Deborah, ch. 5, which knows only Sisera). That distribution strongly suggests Jabin was secondarily attached to the Sisera tradition by the prose editor, a point Boling develops at length.

The archaeological data favor a single major destruction. Yigael Yadin''s excavations identified a violent conflagration of the Late Bronze Canaanite city, and Amnon Ben-Tor''s later work refined the destruction to roughly the thirteenth century BCE, with no evidence of two distinct major Canaanite-city destructions of the kind two separate Jabin defeats would require. One great burning, not two, sits in the ground; the unburnt mudbrick collapse and the deliberately decapitated statuary Ben-Tor recovered point to a single decisive event rather than a sequence of comparable destructions.

The critical synthesis, following Boling, Soggin, and Nelson, treats Joshua 11 and Judges 4 as parallel literary developments of memory about a single major Israelite-Canaanite conflict in northern Galilee, which became attached to two leader cycles — the all-Israel Joshua cycle and the Deborah-Barak cycle — and were preserved side by side by the Deuteronomistic editor. Finkelstein''s regional history of the north is compatible with a single decisive event behind both. The dynastic-name harmonization remains formally available and cannot be disproven, but it must posit a second annihilation and a rapid reconstitution that neither the verbal texture of the texts nor the stratigraphy of Tell Hazor independently supports. Verdict: harmonizable in principle via a dynastic throne-name, but the doublet hypothesis is the more economical explanation of the shared detail and the single destruction layer.', 'Yigael Yadin, *Hazor: The Rediscovery of a Great Citadel of the Bible* (Random House, 1975), on the destruction of Canaanite Hazor
Amnon Ben-Tor, "The Fall of Canaanite Hazor — The ''Who'' and ''When'' Questions," in *Mediterranean Peoples in Transition*, ed. Seymour Gitin et al. (Israel Exploration Society, 1998)
