The standard harmonization, advanced by Richard Hess, David Howard, Younger, and Butler, is the corporate-leadership reading: Joshua 11:21 reports the conquest at the strategic level, crediting the commander-in-chief for a campaign carried out by the army, while Joshua 15 and Judges 1 zoom in on Caleb''s specific role within that operation. The convention is real — ancient Near Eastern royal inscriptions and biblical narrative alike credit kings and leaders with deeds executed by their forces (Joab takes Rabbah but summons David to claim the city, 2 Samuel 12:26-29). On this reading the texts are complementary, differing only in resolution, and the entry is best classed as partially harmonized.

The difficulty is twofold. First, the closure in Joshua 11:22 asserts completion: "there was none of the Anakim left in the land," naming only the three Philistine cities as exceptions. That is the language of a finished operation, not an interim report awaiting Caleb''s follow-up — and then Joshua 15:14 has Caleb still needing to drive out the three named sons of Anak afterward. Second, the doubling is not a single zoom-in but a threefold attestation distributed across two books with three slightly different formulations and even a fourth subject (Judah, Judges 1:10). A pattern that recurs three times in varied wording is harder to explain as one event narrated at different magnifications.

The critical-scholarly read attributes the unevenness to layered traditions. Martin Noth, Robert Boling, and J. Alberto Soggin treat Joshua 11:21-23 as a Deuteronomistic summary that idealizes a unified conquest under Joshua, while Joshua 15:13-19 preserves an older, originally independent Calebite tribal tradition about how the clan of Caleb took the Hebron hill country. Notably, Caleb is repeatedly identified as a Kenizzite (Joshua 14:6, 14) — an outsider clan attached to Judah — which fits a memory of a non-Israelite or proto-Judahite group settling the southern highlands and later absorbed into the all-Israel schema. Judges 1:1-21 preserves yet a further stratum, often read as a more realistic catalog of tribal settlement. Niels Peter Lemche and Israel Finkelstein argue the Calebite and Kenizzite material reflects independent southern hill-country traditions retrofitted into the conquest narrative.

The verdict is mixed. The corporate-leadership convention makes a literal contradiction avoidable: a commander may be credited with what a subordinate executes, and "Joshua destroyed them" need not exclude "Caleb drove them out." But the completion language of Joshua 11:22 and, above all, the threefold doubling across books are explained more economically by the merging of an older Calebite tribal tradition with a later Deuteronomistic all-Israel summary than by a single event described at varying resolutions. Verdict: harmonizable on the surface, but the redactional layering is the more probable account of the doublet.', 'Robert G. Boling, *Joshua* (AB 6; Doubleday, 1982), on Joshua 11:21 and 15:13-14
J. Alberto Soggin, *Joshua: A Commentary* (OTL; Westminster, 1972), on Joshua 11:21
Martin Noth, *The Deuteronomistic History* (JSOTSup 15; Sheffield Academic Press, 2nd ed. 1981), on the conquest summaries
