The harmonization, advanced by Richard Hess and David Howard, is the rebalancing-and-overlap reading, and it has a genuine textual hook. Joshua 19:9 states outright that "the part of the children of Judah was too much for them: therefore the children of Simeon had their inheritance within the inheritance of them," establishing that the editors knew Judah''s portion was oversized and was redistributed to other tribes. The Danite allotment (Joshua 19:40-48) sat on the western edge of Judah''s territory, and Eshtaol and Zoreah lay on the Judah-Dan frontier. On this view the towns were Judahite by the original inventory and Danite by a later apportionment, so both notices can be true at different moments or describe a shared border zone. This defuses a flat contradiction and warrants a partial-harmonization rating.

The weakness is that the text never narrates the transfer of these specific towns. Joshua 19:9 explicitly grounds Simeon''s enclave in Judah''s surplus, but says nothing of Eshtaol and Zoreah, and Joshua 15 and 19 each simply assigns them without acknowledging the other. The reconstruction is supplied by the reader to bridge the seam. Worse, the broader Danite story does not fit a tidy transfer. Judges 1:34 reports that "the Amorites forced the children of Dan into the mountain: for they would not suffer them to come down to the valley" — Dan failed to occupy its lowland inheritance at all. Judges 18 then narrates the entire tribe migrating north to conquer Laish, which they rename Dan, abandoning the southern territory. The southern Danite allotment, including Eshtaol and Zoreah, never functioned as effective Danite land in the way a clean reassignment would imply. Yet the Samson cycle anchors the hero in exactly this district as Danite territory: Judges 13:25 places the spirit of the LORD stirring him "between Zorah and Eshtaol," and Judges 16:31 buries him "between Zorah and Eshtaol in the buryingplace of Manoah his father."

The critical-scholarly read treats Joshua 13-21 as among the most heavily redacted material in the book — a compilation of tribal boundary descriptions and town lists drawn from different sources and periods. Yohanan Aharoni and Zecharia Kallai argued that these geographical lists freeze administrative realities from distinct eras (pre-monarchic, Davidic, post-Solomonic district systems) into a single text whose editors did not reconcile them. On that model the Judah inventory of Joshua 15 reflects a Judahite-administrative roster (in which border Shephelah towns counted as Judah''s), while the Dan list of Joshua 19 preserves an older or competing claim about Danite territory that the Samson traditions corroborate. Eshtaol and Zoreah appear in both because the two source documents overlapped without harmonization.

The verdict is mixed. Joshua 19:9 makes a rebalancing genuinely plausible, so this is not an airtight collision; the towns could have shifted from Judah to Dan. But the transfer of these specific towns is never stated, Dan''s actual history undercuts a working southern allotment, and the more economical explanation is that two administrative lists from different periods, both preserved by the redactor, simply disagreed. Verdict: partially harmonizable in principle, but most likely a redactional artifact of competing geographical sources.', 'Yohanan Aharoni, *The Land of the Bible: A Historical Geography* (Westminster, rev. ed. 1979), on the tribal town lists
Zecharia Kallai, *Historical Geography of the Bible: The Tribal Territories of Israel* (Magnes Press / Brill, 1986), on Dan and Judah
Robert G. Boling, *Joshua* (AB 6; Doubleday, 1982), on the allotment lists
