The underlying Balak narrative in Numbers 22-24 sides squarely with Jephthah. Balak, alarmed at Israel, does not muster an army or join battle; he hires the diviner Balaam to curse Israel, the cursing repeatedly fails and turns to blessing, and Balak sends Balaam home in frustration (Numbers 24:10-11). No combat between Moab and Israel is narrated anywhere in the Balak episode. Jacob Milgrom''s commentary on Numbers underscores that the Balak-Balaam confrontation is conducted entirely through attempted sorcery, never through arms.

The apologetic harmonization runs two ways. The broad-sense reading, favored by Daniel Block and K. Lawson Younger, takes *wayyillāḥem* in Joshua 24:9 to denote hostility short of pitched battle — diplomatic or magical "warfare," i.e., the attempt to destroy Israel by hiring Balaam to curse them. The immediate context supports reading the whole episode as a hostile project YHWH frustrated: Joshua 24:10 continues, "But I would not hearken unto Balaam; therefore he blessed you still: so I delivered you out of his hand." The verse summarizes the Balaam affair as an act of aggression that God overturned, so "warred" could be loose summary language for that aggression. The second, weaker line (Trent Butler) posits unrecorded skirmishes preserved in Joshua''s source but omitted from Numbers.

The broad-sense reading is the more defensible of the two and is widely adopted for Joshua 24 in isolation, since *lḥm* can occasionally carry an extended sense and the context does frame the Balaam episode as hostility. But it fails precisely where it must succeed — in Judges 11. Jephthah is constructing a legal-historical brief to the Ammonite king arguing that Israel never wronged Moab or seized Moabite land, and his proof is that Balak, Moab''s own king, never contested Israel by force: he neither "strove" (the *rîv* of a legal/territorial dispute) nor "fought" them. If hiring Balaam counted as "warring," Jephthah''s argument collapses on its face, because the Ammonite could reply that Balak did precisely that. Jephthah''s rhetorical point requires that Balak took no hostile action against Israel at all — which is the plain sense of Numbers and the opposite of Joshua 24:9. The same author-tradition cannot have both Balak waging war (Joshua 24) and Balak''s complete non-aggression functioning as a load-bearing premise of a legal argument (Judges 11) without contradiction; the broad-sense move that rescues one passage destroys the other.

The critical-scholarly read (Boling, Soggin) treats this as one of several internal inconsistencies in which the Deuteronomistic Historian preserved divergent summaries of older traditions without harmonizing them: Joshua 24''s recital schematizes the wilderness threats into acts of "war" YHWH defeated, while Judges 11''s Jephthah speech draws on a tradition (matching Numbers) in which Balak never fought at all. Verdict: genuine contradiction; the broad-sense harmonization saves Joshua 24 only by breaking Jephthah''s argument in Judges 11.', 'Robert G. Boling, *Judges* (AB 6A; Doubleday, 1975), on Judges 11:25
J. Alberto Soggin, *Judges: A Commentary* (OTL; Westminster, 1981), on Judges 11:25
Daniel I. Block, *Judges, Ruth* (NAC 6; Broadman & Holman, 1999), on Judges 11:25
Trent C. Butler, *Judges* (WBC 8; Thomas Nelson, 2009), on Judges 11:25
Trent C. Butler, *Joshua 13-24* (WBC 7B; Zondervan, 2nd ed. 2014), on Joshua 24:9
