The semantic spine matters. The Hebrew *nepeš* is not an immortal soul that survives the body intact; it denotes the living, breathing self, and at death the person becomes a *repa''im*, a diminished shade. Psalm 88:5 places the dead among those "whom thou rememberest no more," and Psalm 115:17 — "the dead praise not the LORD, neither any that go down into silence" — is a theological claim, not decorative verse: praise requires a living worshipper. Isaiah 38:18, Hezekiah''s psalm, says the same. None of this anticipates conscious afterlife.

Then Daniel 12:1-2 — almost universally dated by critical scholars to the Maccabean crisis around 165 BCE — produces the first unambiguous resurrection text in the Hebrew Bible: "many of them that sleep in the dust of the earth shall awake, some to everlasting life, and some to shame and everlasting contempt." John Collins, in the Hermeneia *Daniel*, ties this directly to the martyrdoms under Antiochus IV: resurrection enters Jewish thought as a theodicy, an answer to the scandal of the faithful being slaughtered with no earthly vindication. Isaiah 26:19 belongs to the so-called Isaiah Apocalypse (chs. 24-27), which Brevard Childs and others date to the late Persian or early Hellenistic period rather than to eighth-century Isaiah. Ezekiel 37''s valley of dry bones, by contrast, is a national-restoration metaphor (the bones are explicitly "the whole house of Israel"), not individual resurrection — which underscores that the imagery was repurposed over time. The trajectory continues outside the canon: 1 Enoch, 2 Maccabees 7 (the mother and seven sons who expect bodily restoration), and the Qumran texts show resurrection belief consolidating in the two centuries before Paul, even as the Sadducees still rejected it (Acts 23:8).

The apologetic move, from Gleason Archer forward, is to argue that the "no afterlife" texts describe only the visible decay of the body, or speak from a limited human vantage, or use "sleep" as a euphemism that already presupposes waking. This does not survive contact with the texts. Ecclesiastes 3:19 equates the fate of humans and beasts — "they have all one breath; so that a man hath no preeminence above a beast" — which is a theological position, not a description of rot. A more sophisticated version distinguishes the *intermediate state* (Sheol) from the *final resurrection*, arguing both can be true of one anthropology. But Qoheleth''s "the dead know nothing" denies precisely the conscious continuity that the intermediate-state harmonization needs, and the LXX''s rendering of these verses preserves the same finality rather than smoothing it toward later belief.

The most economical reading is doctrinal evolution. Kugel, Levenson, and Segal have each traced it: early Israelite religion had no robust afterlife; resurrection emerged in apocalyptic Judaism as a response to unjust suffering; the New Testament inherits the developed view and centers it on Christ''s own rising (1 Cor 15:16). All these texts sit in the canon, but they describe incompatible anthropologies. Verdict: genuine contradiction reflecting historical development, not a single harmonizable doctrine.', 'Philip S. Johnston, *Shades of Sheol: Death and Afterlife in the Old Testament* (IVP Academic, 2002), on Sheol and the West Semitic background of Hebrew death language
John J. Collins, *Daniel: A Commentary on the Book of Daniel* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1993), pp. 390-398, on Dan 12:1-3 and the origins of resurrection belief
