The conditional texts can likewise be aligned with one another. 2 Chronicles 7:14 makes forgiveness contingent on humbling, prayer, seeking God''s face, and turning from wickedness; 1 John 1:9 conditions it on confession; Matthew 6:15 — embedded in the Lord''s Prayer and its appended commentary (vv. 14-15) — conditions it on forgiving others. Davies and Allison note that the Matthean condition is not a meritorious transaction but a description of the disposition of the genuinely repentant: the unforgiving heart has not actually received grace. Read this way, the three conditional passages specify complementary facets of a single repentant posture, and even Joshua 24:19 can be folded in as "will not forgive the unrepentant." Hence partial harmonization.

What resists this synthesis is Jeremiah 31:34. The new-covenant oracle promises, "I will forgive their iniquity, and I will remember their sin no more," and the surrounding verses frame this as Yahweh''s unilateral act — the law written on the heart, the relationship established by divine initiative, not contingent on a prior human condition. Jack Lundbom''s Anchor Bible treatment stresses the eschatological, unconditional character of the promise: it is precisely what the old covenant could not deliver. To assimilate this to the conditional scheme one must read an implied "if they repent" into a text that pointedly omits it, which inverts the oracle''s rhetorical point. Ralph Klein''s Hermeneia treatment of 2 Chronicles 7 shows, conversely, how the Chronicler softened the harsher Deuteronomistic theology of irrevocable judgment — evidence that the biblical tradition itself was renegotiating the terms of forgiveness over time.

Walter Brueggemann frames exactly this kind of spread as "core testimony" versus "countertestimony": Israel confesses a God who forgives, and also a God who will by no means clear the guilty, and the canon holds both without resolving them. The fully harmonized reading requires three stacked moves — that "will not forgive" means "will not forgive without repentance," that conditional and unconditional language describe stages of one offer, and that Matthew''s condition is functionally identical to confession. The first two are defensible; the third strains; and Jeremiah''s unconditional promise remains the genuine outlier. Verdict: partially harmonizable — the conditional and rhetorical texts cohere, but Jeremiah 31:34''s unconditional grace reflects a distinct theological voice that the conditional scheme cannot fully absorb.', 'Trent C. Butler, *Joshua 13-24* (WBC 7B, 2nd ed.; Zondervan, 2014), pp. 287-296, on Josh 24:19-20
