The standard harmonization, in K. Lawson Younger''s NIVAC volume and earlier in Keil, runs: Judah captured and burned the lower city but did not garrison it; the Jebusites retained or recaptured the fortified citadel (the later Zion/Ophel stronghold); and Benjamin subsequently inherited a still-Jebusite Jerusalem. Daniel Block offers a milder form — the city was torched but not depopulated, and the Jebusites simply persisted. This is internally coherent and the lower-city/citadel distinction is archaeologically real. But it requires inserting an unnarrated sequence — a Jebusite recapture or survival of the citadel — between v. 8 and v. 21 to keep both verses literally true. The text reports a burning and then a failure to dispossess; it never reports the reversal the harmonization depends on.

The source-critical reading is more economical. Trent Butler in WBC and Robert Boling in the Anchor Bible Judges treat the chapter as stitched from distinct traditions; Butler regards v. 8 as a later Judahite, pro-monarchic notice celebrating a conquest the older negative list in v. 21 simply does not know. The two verses encode rival memories rather than sequential events. The external evidence reinforces this. There is no destruction layer at Jerusalem fitting a Late Bronze / early Iron Age conquest of the scale Judges 1:8 implies; Finkelstein and Silberman lay out the archaeological case that Jerusalem was a modest, continuously occupied highland settlement in this period. Decisively, 2 Samuel 5:6-9 has David capture Jerusalem from the Jebusites at his accession — generations after Judges 1:8 supposedly burned it — and the Jebusites are still in possession of the stronghold. The downstream narrative aligns with v. 21, not v. 8.

So the data converge: an internal contradiction within a single chapter, an archaeological absence where Judges 1:8 needs a destruction, and a later canonical narrative that presupposes a still-Jebusite Jerusalem. The harmonization preserves inerrancy only by supplying a Jebusite reconquest the text withholds. Verdict: genuine contradiction, best explained as the unsmoothed juxtaposition of competing conquest traditions.', 'Trent C. Butler, *Judges* (WBC 8; Thomas Nelson, 2009), pp. 13-30, on Judg 1:1-2:5
Robert G. Boling, *Judges: Introduction, Translation, and Commentary* (AB 6A; Doubleday, 1975), pp. 53-60
Daniel I. Block, *Judges, Ruth* (NAC 6; B&H, 1999), pp. 91-104
Israel Finkelstein and Neil Asher Silberman, *The Bible Unearthed* (Free Press, 2001), pp. 72-96, 132-145
K. Lawson Younger Jr., *Judges and Ruth* (NIV Application Commentary; Zondervan, 2002), pp. 53-72
C. F. Keil, *The Books of Joshua, Judges, and Ruth* (Biblical Commentary on the Old Testament, trans. James Martin; T&T Clark, 1865), pp. 251-260, on Judg 1:8, 21', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', 'Judges', 7, 2, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard harmonization, from Keil-Delitzsch through Daniel Block''s NAC, relocates the failure from Yahweh''s power to Judah''s faith: Yahweh could have driven out the valley-dwellers, but Judah lost nerve before their armored superiority. The grammatical hook is that Judah is the syntactic subject of "could not." This is the strongest form of the apologetic case and it is not frivolous. But the verse does not mention faith, fear, or disobedience; it gives a single, material reason — "because they had chariots of iron." The harmonization supplies a motive (faithlessness) the text withholds and suppresses the causal clause the text supplies. Judges elsewhere is perfectly willing to name Israelite failure as sin (2:1-3, 2:11-15); its silence here is conspicuous.

The critical reading takes the verse at its word as a fossil of older tradition. Susan Niditch in the OTL treats Judges 1 as a composite catalogue of conquest failures preserving folk memories that the Deuteronomistic editors could not fully assimilate to the high doctrine of divine omnipotence emerging in their period. Verse 19 is, in Brueggemann''s terms, countertestimony — an admission of Yahweh''s limitation against superior military technology that slipped past the redactor. Two further data points support this. First, the Septuagint tradition already registers discomfort: some Greek witnesses recast the verse so that the failure is *because* the valley-dwellers had chariots — i.e., the inability is treated as a real obstacle requiring explanation, and other witnesses adjust the subject, the very scribal fidgeting a theologically awkward verse provokes. Second, the iron-chariot motif is historically anachronistic for a wholesale Late Bronze conquest: militarily significant iron technology in the Levant belongs to the Iron Age, so the detail likely reflects the later setting of the tradents rather than the events narrated, reinforcing that this is a layered text.

The distinction between the older poetic source (Judg 5) and the prose framework also matters: the Song of Deborah is widely regarded as among the oldest Hebrew poetry, and it credits Yahweh directly, whereas the 1:19 notice belongs to a different tradition stream with a different theology of what Yahweh''s presence guarantees. Verdict: genuine contradiction within the book — an older admission of limitation against iron chariots stands unreconciled beside a later narrative of effortless divine victory over them, and the faith-deficit harmonization reads a cause into the text that the text declines to give.', 'Trent C. Butler, *Judges* (WBC 8; Thomas Nelson, 2009), pp. 27-29, on Judg 1:19
Susan Niditch, *Judges: A Commentary* (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 2008), pp. 39-44
