Judges 5:26-27 paints a different scene. After Jael strikes, the poem stacks three verbs in a tight tricolon — כָּרַע נָפַל שָׁכָב (kāraʿ nāfal shākāv), ''he bowed, he fell, he lay,'' repeated with incremental variation — ''between her feet (בֵּין רַגְלֶיהָ) he bowed, he fell; where he bowed, there he fell, despoiled.'' The vocabulary is vertical and dynamic: a figure sinking from a standing or kneeling posture down to the ground at Jael''s feet. Robert Alter, analyzing the passage in The Art of Biblical Poetry, reads the verb-chain as the deliberate slow-motion choreography of collapse, which only makes sense if Sisera had height to fall from.

The apologetic harmonization, represented by Daniel Block''s NAC volume and K. Lawson Younger''s NIVAC, reads the poem as describing death-throes: the prose tells us he was asleep when struck, the poetry depicts his convulsive final movements after the blow. The verbs can in isolation bear a ''writhing'' sense, and this is the strongest form of the reconciliation. But it founders on the prose''s own detail. Judges 4:21 explicitly says the peg fastened him ''to the ground,'' pinning the skull in place; a man spiked through the temple into the earth does not rise, kneel, and topple at his killer''s feet. The poem''s בֵּין רַגְלֶיהָ (''between/at her feet'') is positional, locating where Sisera ended up relative to a standing Jael — not the language of an immobilized corpse twitching where it lay.

A second line of criticism deepens the divergence. Yair Zakovitch, Susan Niditch, and others note that the poem''s idiom — Sisera collapsing ''between her feet,'' a phrase that elsewhere carries sexual overtones (cf. Deut 28:57; Ezek 16:25) — may preserve an older, less domesticated tradition in which an erotic encounter precedes the killing, and that the prose of chapter 4 has sanitized this by safely putting Sisera to sleep before the violence. Whether or not one accepts the seduction reading, it underscores that chapters 4 and 5 are not a single author''s complementary report but distinct compositions with different sensibilities, joined editorially.

This is precisely the kind of case the documentary and redaction critics point to: an editor placed a poem and its prose paraphrase adjacent without smoothing their physical details, confident that readers would harmonize by sequence (''first he slept, the poem just dramatizes'') rather than by mechanics. The death-throes harmonization requires assuming the peg did not immobilize him — which contradicts the prose''s explicit pinning — and reading positional language as convulsive language. The more economical conclusion is that two independent traditions about Jael and Sisera, one prose and one poetic, describe the moment of death in mutually exclusive postures: one supine and unconscious, the other upright and sinking. Verdict: genuine tension between two parallel traditions, left unreconciled by the redactor.', 'Susan Niditch, *Judges* (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 2008), pp. 65-85, on Judges 4-5.
Robert Alter, *The Art of Biblical Poetry* (rev. ed.; Basic Books, 2011), pp. 49-62, on Judges 5:26-27.
