Gideon''s fleece in Judges 6:36-40 then does exactly what Deuteronomy seems to forbid. Gideon proposes a conditional sign — ''if you will save Israel by my hand as you have said'' — lays out the wool, asks for dew on the fleece but not the ground, and when that is granted, asks God not to be angry (''let not your anger burn against me'') and requests the reverse sign for confirmation. God complies twice without rebuke. Malachi 3:10 sharpens the picture from the divine side: ''Prove me now herewith (בְּחָנוּנִי, beḥanuni, from בחן, bḥn, ''test, assay''), says the LORD of hosts.'' Here God invites the very examination Deuteronomy forbids, using a Hebrew testing-root.

The standard harmonization — represented by Block''s NAC Judges and by the IVP Bible Background Commentary — distinguishes illegitimate testing (a demand born of unbelief, as at Massah) from legitimate seeking of confirmation (a humble request for assurance, as with Gideon). This is a genuine and defensible theological distinction; it tracks something real about the difference between an ultimatum and a prayer. There is also a lexical case for it: Deuteronomy and the Massah narrative use נסה (nsh), Gideon''s narrative speaks of his wanting to ''know'' (ידע, ydʿ), and Malachi uses בחן (bḥn). One could argue the prohibition targets a specific attitude rather than the bare act of requesting a sign.

The difficulty is that the distinction must be supplied from outside the texts that are in tension. Deuteronomy 6:16 attaches no qualifier — it does not say ''do not test God from distrust,'' it says do not test God. The Gideon narrative itself never flags the fleece as a permitted category of testing; Gideon''s own anxious ''let not your anger burn'' suggests even he suspects he is treading near the line, and the narrator simply reports compliance without comment. Critically, the texts also straddle different theological streams. The Deuteronomic ideology prizes unconditioned obedience and is suspicious of sign-seeking; the older hero traditions in Judges preserve folkloric patterns in which divine confirmation through portents is routine and unproblematic (compare the signs sought by and given to Moses in Exodus 4, or Hezekiah in Isaiah 38); Malachi reflects a covenant-lawsuit setting in which God himself authorizes a test of tithing fidelity. These are not a single voice carefully calibrating an attitude; they are different authors with different theologies of how Israel may relate to divine signs.

The harmonizer''s attitude-based reading rescues coherence at the level of systematic theology by importing a category the conflicting passages do not articulate. As a constructive reading it is reasonable; as a claim about what the texts themselves assert it overreaches. The more economical description is that the canon contains both a categorical prohibition against testing God and narrative cases in which testing God is approved and even invited, and the editors never reconciled them. Verdict: partially harmonizable on a defensible but text-external attitude distinction; the surface tension between an absolute command and approved counter-examples remains genuine.', 'Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1996), pp. 79-81, on Deuteronomy 6:16.
Daniel I. Block, *Judges, Ruth* (NAC 6; B&H, 1999), pp. 270-281, on Judges 6:36-40, representative harmonization.
Andrew E. Hill, *Malachi* (AB 25D; Doubleday, 1998), pp. 311-317, on Malachi 3:10.
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew* (ICC, vol. 1; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 363-365, on Matthew 4:7.
Richard D. Nelson, *Deuteronomy: A Commentary* (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 2002), pp. 91-95, on Deuteronomy 6:16.
John H. Walton, Victor H. Matthews, and Mark W. Chavalas, *The IVP Bible Background Commentary: Old Testament* (InterVarsity Press, 2000), pp. 253-254, on Judges 6, representative harmonization.', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Deuteronomy, Judges, Matthew, Luke', 6, 2, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The apologetic harmonization is round-number rhetoric, and it is well grounded. Block''s NAC and the IVP Bible Background Commentary both observe that ''seventy sons'' is a stock figure for a complete dynastic body in the ancient Near East. The Ugaritic Baal cycle speaks of the ''seventy sons of Athirat (Asherah),'' the Panammuwa and related Aramaic inscriptions describe royal fratricide in formulaic terms, and Ahab is credited with seventy sons in Samaria (2 Kings 10:1-7), who are likewise slaughtered en masse. As a literary convention ''seventy'' signals dynastic completeness rather than a census figure, and on that reading 8:30 makes no precise numerical claim.

The convention handles one side of the problem but not the other. Even granting that ''seventy'' is symbolic for Gideon''s full complement of sons, Judges 9:5 narrates Jotham''s escape as a literal event embedded in a literal massacre: he hides, survives, and later delivers the fable of the trees from Mount Gerizim (9:7-21), and 9:56-57 treats the deaths as concrete crimes for which Abimelech and Shechem are punished. The narrative therefore wants the seventy to be real enough to be murdered and Jotham real enough to escape — which is exactly the level of literalism at which the count fails to add up. Trent Butler''s WBC treats this as a marker that the Abimelech material in chapter 9 was originally an independent tradition joined to the Gideon cycle of chapter 8 without numerical reconciliation. Susan Niditch reads the ''seventy slain, one survives'' pattern as a stock folkloric motif — compare Athaliah''s dynastic massacre and the infant Joash''s rescue in 2 Kings 11, and Jehu''s seventy heads in 2 Kings 10 — again pointing to traditional patterning rather than bookkeeping.

The deeper point is source-critical. The ''seventy sons'' of 8:30 and the ''seventy slain'' of 9:5 belong to two narrative units with different protagonists and concerns: chapter 8 closes the Gideon cycle, chapter 9 is the self-contained tragedy of Abimelech''s usurpation and downfall at Shechem. Each carries its own use of the conventional number, and the editor who joined them did not adjust the figures so that survivor plus victims plus slayer produced a coherent sum. The symbolic-seventy reading rightly defuses the charge that 8:30 is a failed headcount, but it cannot rescue the internal arithmetic once Jotham''s literal survival and Abimelech''s literal agency are placed against a literal seventy slain. Verdict: a genuine numerical seam produced by the joining of two traditions, softened but not eliminated by the conventional force of ''seventy.''', 'Trent C. Butler, *Judges* (WBC 8; Thomas Nelson, 2009), pp. 222-256, on Judges 8:29-9:57.
Susan Niditch, *Judges* (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 2008), pp. 109-122, on Judges 9.
Daniel I. Block, *Judges, Ruth* (NAC 6; B&H, 1999), pp. 302-336, on Judges 8:30 and 9:5.
Robert G. Boling, *Judges* (AB 6A; Doubleday, 1975), pp. 159-184, on Judges 8-9.
John H. Walton, Victor H. Matthews, and Mark W. Chavalas, *The IVP Bible Background Commentary: Old Testament* (InterVarsity Press, 2000), pp. 257-259, on the seventy-sons motif.', 0, NULL, 7, 'OT_internal', 'Judges', 1, 1, 1, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The critical reading takes the two works as products of a live Second Temple debate. On linguistic and ideological grounds many scholars — Campbell in the Anchor Bible Ruth, Eskenazi and Frymer-Kensky in the JPS commentary — date the composition of Ruth to the post-exilic period and read it as a pointed counter-narrative to the Ezra-Nehemiah purity program. The book is set ''in the days when the judges judged'' but speaks to the fifth-century question of ethnic boundaries: it answers the policy of expulsion with a story in which Israel''s greatest king owes his existence to a Moabite woman of exemplary loyalty. Whether or not one accepts a strict polemical intent, Ruth and Ezra-Nehemiah plainly represent opposing valuations of Moabite intermarriage, and the canon, by including both, declines to adjudicate between them — much as it preserves the welcoming universalism of Isaiah 56:3-7 alongside the exclusion of Deuteronomy 23.

The harmonizing tradition, represented by Block''s NAC and the IVP Bible Background Commentary, runs in two main directions. First, the gender argument: Deuteronomy 23:3 uses the masculine form מוֹאָבִי (moʾavi), so the bar applies only to Moabite men, not women — a reading the Mishnah develops explicitly (m. Yevamot 8:3, ''an Ammonite and a Moabite are forbidden … but their women are permitted'') precisely to legitimate David''s ancestry. Second, the conversion argument: Ruth''s confession, ''your people shall be my people, and your God my God'' (Ruth 1:16), incorporates her into Israel and dissolves her Moabite status. Both are coherent and were taken seriously within the tradition; the gender reading in particular has real grammatical purchase, since Hebrew gentilics are gendered.

Neither move, however, holds against the texts in tension. The masculine-only distinction is not stated in Deuteronomy 23; it is a rabbinic inference framed centuries later for the express purpose of protecting David''s line, and Ezra''s own program does not honor it — Ezra 9-10 targets the foreign wives, the women, specifically. The conversion argument fails on the narrative''s own insistence: Ruth is still ''Ruth the Moabitess'' in 2:2, 2:21, and even in 4:10, where Boaz publicly acquires ''Ruth the Moabitess … to be my wife'' before the elders. The book will not let her ethnic identity dissolve; that retention is part of the point. The most economical reading is that Israelite and early Jewish opinion on intermarriage with Moabites was genuinely divided across the biblical period — inclusive in Ruth and Isaiah 56, exclusionary in Deuteronomy 23 and Ezra-Nehemiah — and that the canon preserves both poles without harmonizing them. The later gender and conversion arguments are best understood not as the texts'' own resolution but as downstream attempts to reconcile a tension the tradition itself felt acutely. Verdict: a genuine, and very likely deliberate, intra-canonical contradiction.', 'Edward F. Campbell, *Ruth: A New Translation with Introduction, Notes, and Commentary* (AB 7; Doubleday, 1975), pp. 27-36, 159-173, on Ruth 4:13-17.
Tamara Cohn Eskenazi and Tikva Frymer-Kensky, *Ruth* (JPS Bible Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 2011), pp. xv-xliv.
H. G. M. Williamson, *Ezra, Nehemiah* (WBC 16; Word, 1985), pp. 127-159, on Ezra 9-10.
Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1996), pp. 209-212, on Deuteronomy 23:4-7.
Daniel I. Block, *Judges, Ruth* (NAC 6; B&H, 1999), pp. 605-733, on Ruth, representative harmonization.
John H. Walton, Victor H. Matthews, and Mark W. Chavalas, *The IVP Bible Background Commentary: Old Testament* (InterVarsity Press, 2000), pp. 277-287, on Ruth, representative harmonization.', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Ruth, Ezra', 6, 3, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

1 Chronicles 6 then incorporates this same Elkanah and Samuel into a Kohathite Levitical genealogy descending from Korah son of Izhar son of Kohath son of Levi (1 Chr 6:22-28; vv. 7-13 in the Hebrew numbering). The redactional purpose is transparent in the Chronicler''s wider program: by the post-exilic period, legitimate cultic service at the central sanctuary required demonstrable Levitical descent, and the Chronicler systematically anchors the temple personnel — singers, gatekeepers, priests — in elaborate Levitical pedigrees. A figure as cultically prominent as Samuel, who offers sacrifice and ministers before Yahweh, had to be assimilated to that framework. The same chapter makes the motive explicit: at 1 Chronicles 6:33 Heman the Levitical singer is descended from ''Joel, the son of Samuel,'' so the Chronicler''s Samuel is functionally the ancestor of a guild of temple Levites.

The traditional harmonization — articulated by Keil and Delitzsch in the nineteenth century and carried forward by Tsumura''s NICOT volume, Andrew Hill''s NIVAC Chronicles, and Walton''s IVP Background Commentary — argues that ephrati here is residential, not tribal: Levites were distributed among the other tribes through the Levitical-cities system of Numbers 35 and Joshua 21, so Elkanah could be a Levite by descent who happened to dwell in Ephraim. The case deserves a fair hearing, because it is not logically impossible: a Levite clan settled in Ephraimite hill country is exactly what the Levitical-cities legislation envisions, and 1 Samuel 1:1 does call the place har ephrayim. The harmonization therefore secures bare consistency.

The difficulty is lexical and methodological. The gentilic ephrati elsewhere carries ethnic-tribal force, not mere domicile. In Judges 12:5-6 the word distinguishes Ephraimites from Gileadites in a tribal conflict — it is an identity, not an address. In Ruth 1:2 the ''Ephrathites of Bethlehem-Judah'' denotes clan membership (the Ephrathah clan within Judah), again ethnic rather than residential. Reading 1 Samuel 1:1 as ''Ephraim-resident'' rather than ''Ephraimite'' therefore strains the term''s ordinary semantics to protect a later genealogy. More decisively, the source-critical picture, set out by Sara Japhet in her OTL commentary and from the Samuel side by P. Kyle McCarter Jr. in the Anchor Bible, is that the Chronicler is harmonizing — drawing an earlier, non-Levitical Samuel tradition into the genealogical system the Second Temple cult demanded. On this reading the Levitical pedigree is not an independent witness to Samuel''s ancestry but a secondary literary construction.

The parsimonious conclusion is layered. At the level of strict logical possibility, the two texts can be reconciled: a Levite by descent can reside in Ephraim. But the better explanation of the data is developmental — the older tradition of 1 Samuel 1 presents Samuel as an Ephraimite, and the later Chronicler reassigned him to Levi to legitimize his cultic role and to undergird the descent of the temple singers. Verdict: harmonizable at the level of bare consistency, but the evidence favors a later genealogical retrofit over a single coherent lineage.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *1 Samuel: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Bible 8; Doubleday, 1980), pp. 51-58, on 1 Sam 1:1.
Sara Japhet, *I & II Chronicles: A Commentary* (Old Testament Library; Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 154-167, on 1 Chr 6.
