John H. Walton, Victor H. Matthews, and Mark W. Chavalas, *The IVP Bible Background Commentary: Old Testament* (InterVarsity Press, 2000), pp. 285-286, on 1 Sam 1:1.', 0, NULL, 6, 'OT_internal', '1 Samuel, 1 Chronicles', 3, 3, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The cosmology reconstructed by Othmar Keel, John Walton, and Wayne Horowitz from the biblical texts and their Mesopotamian and Egyptian neighbors is a three-tiered universe: a flat, disk-shaped earth resting on pillars or foundations sunk into the cosmic waters (tehom), a solid dome — the raqia of Genesis 1:6-8 — stretched overhead to hold back the upper waters, with windows or floodgates (Gen 7:11), and the realm of the dead (Sheol) beneath. Genesis 1''s firmament, Job''s pillars, Psalm 104''s foundations, and the ''waters above'' all belong to this single conceptual world, which the biblical writers shared with their cultural milieu rather than transcended.

Against that backdrop, Job 26:7 is the outlier verse, and even there the concordist reading is overdrawn. blimah is a hapax legomenon — it occurs nowhere else in the Hebrew Bible — and its sense is debated; it is usually parsed as beli (''without'') plus mah (''anything''), hence ''nothing,'' but the term is too rare to bear a precise technical weight. Decisively, the immediate context is mythological, not astronomical. The same poem speaks of the ''pillars of heaven'' trembling at God''s rebuke (26:11), of God ''dividing the sea'' by his power (26:12), and of his hand piercing the ''fleeing serpent'' (26:13) — the chaoskampf imagery of Rahab and Leviathan. David Clines''s Word Biblical Commentary on Job reads chapter 26 as a hymn to divine power saturated with combat-myth motifs, not a set of observational claims about celestial mechanics. The ''nothing'' on which the earth hangs is one stroke in a rhetorical portrait of God''s overwhelming might, paralleling the suspension of the northern sky ''over the empty place'' (al-tohu) in the same verse.

The concordist move requires extracting a single clause from a mythologically loaded poem, disregarding the trembling heavenly pillars and the slain sea-dragon two verses later, ignoring every other cosmological text in the canon that assumes pillars, foundations, and a solid firmament, and crediting the poet with a heliocentric or Newtonian intuition for which there is no evidence. That is not exegesis but retrojection of modern science. Even granting that Job 26:7 reflects a somewhat more abstract image than the pillar texts, the canon as a whole does not speak with one voice on what the earth rests upon: most texts say pillars and foundations; one poetic line says ''nothing.'' The texts are not describing the same cosmology. Verdict: genuine reflection of a pre-modern ANE cosmology; the apologetic ''modern astronomy'' reading of Job 26:7 is overstated and the pillar/foundation texts are not harmonizable with it.', 'David J. A. Clines, *Job 21-37* (Word Biblical Commentary 18A; Thomas Nelson, 2006), pp. 614-641, on Job 26.
John H. Walton, *Ancient Near Eastern Thought and the Old Testament: Introducing the Conceptual World of the Hebrew Bible*, 2nd ed. (Baker Academic, 2018), pp. 165-198.
Othmar Keel, *The Symbolism of the Biblical World: Ancient Near Eastern Iconography and the Book of Psalms* (Eisenbrauns, 1997), pp. 15-60.
Wayne Horowitz, *Mesopotamian Cosmic Geography* (Mesopotamian Civilizations 8; Eisenbrauns, 1998).
C. L. Seow, *Job 1-21: Interpretation and Commentary* (Illuminations; Eerdmans, 2013).
Henry M. Morris, *The Biblical Basis for Modern Science* (Baker, 1984), pp. 240-249, on Job 26:7 (representative apologetic reading).', 0, NULL, 1, 'OT_internal', '1 Samuel, Job', 7, 2, 2, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

The Hebrew of 7:2 is, however, less determinate than the English suggests: vayehi miyom shevet ha-aron be-qiryat ye''arim vayirbu ha-yamim vayihyu esrim shanah, literally ''and it came to pass, from the day the ark dwelt in Kiriath-jearim — and the days multiplied and became twenty years — and all the house of Israel lamented after Yahweh.'' The clause vayirbu ha-yamim (''the days multiplied'') followed by the lamentation note attaches the twenty years to the period of national mourning that culminates in the Mizpah repentance and renewal of verse 3 (''return unto the LORD with all your hearts''). P. Kyle McCarter Jr. in the Anchor Bible notes precisely this ambiguity: the twenty years may mark the interval from the ark''s arrival to that revival, not the entire span of its residence.

This is the backbone of the standard harmonization, developed by Keil in the nineteenth century and carried forward in Tsumura''s NICOT volume: the verse times the period of lament and the Mizpah turning-point, while the ark itself simply remained at Abinadab''s house through all the intervening years until David moved it. On that reading the ark could have stayed roughly fifty to seventy years total, with no contradiction, since the text never claims twenty years was the full term. Grammatically the construction can bear this; the syntax of 7:2 is genuinely loose, and the harmonization is not a strained imposition.

What the harmonization costs is candor about the silence. Read naturally and in sequence, verse 2 looks like it states how long the ark was there, and verse 3 moves directly to the Mizpah renewal with no signal that decades of additional storage are unfolding off the page. The reconciliation rescues a consistent chronology by inserting an unrecorded multi-decade gap into the gap. Source-critically, Antony Campbell''s FOTL volume and McCarter treat 1 Samuel 7 as a Deuteronomistic compositional unit whose stylized ''twenty years'' belongs to the editorial framework — a round figure of the same order as the recurring forties and twenties of Judges — rather than a datum carefully synchronized with Saul''s regnal years, which the Deuteronomistic History never actually computes for the ark.

The parsimonious conclusion is layered. The number can be harmonized with the longer storyline by restricting it to the period of lament, and the Hebrew permits that reading. But the more economical historical explanation is that the chronology of this stretch of 1-2 Samuel was never tightly coordinated, and the twenty-year notice is a framework figure rather than a precise duration. Verdict: harmonizable through a defensible reading of the loose Hebrew syntax, but the tension reflects an uncoordinated editorial chronology more than a deliberately exact one.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *1 Samuel* (Anchor Bible 8; Doubleday, 1980), pp. 143-149, on 1 Sam 7:1-2.
David Toshio Tsumura, *The First Book of Samuel* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 2007), pp. 226-235, on 1 Sam 7 (representative harmonization).
Antony F. Campbell, *1 Samuel* (Forms of the Old Testament Literature 7; Eerdmans, 2003), pp. 84-92.
A. A. Anderson, *2 Samuel* (Word Biblical Commentary 11; Word, 1989), pp. 99-109, on 2 Sam 6.
Ralph W. Klein, *1 Samuel* (Word Biblical Commentary 10, 2nd ed.; Thomas Nelson, 2000), pp. 64-72.
C. F. Keil, *The Books of Samuel* (Biblical Commentary on the Old Testament, trans. James Martin; T&T Clark, 1866), pp. 68-71, on 1 Sam 7:1-2 (representative harmonization).', 0, NULL, 7, 'OT_internal', '1 Samuel, 2 Samuel', 1, 2, 1, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The difficulty is that ''Vashni'' is otherwise unattested as a personal name in the Hebrew Bible, while in this very verse ''Joel'' has dropped out — even though the rest of the Chronicler''s own genealogy presupposes Joel. A few verses later, at 1 Chronicles 6:33, Heman the temple singer is traced back as ''the son of Joel, the son of Samuel,'' which shows unambiguously that the Chronicler knew Joel as Samuel''s son. The internal evidence of Chronicles itself thus contradicts the MT of 6:28.

The versional evidence points the same direction. The Septuagint of 1 Chronicles 6 preserves both names — the firstborn Ioel (Joel) and the second Abia (Abijah) — and the Syriac Peshitta and the Arabic likewise read ''Joel'' here. The BHS critical apparatus notes these readings. The standard text-critical reconstruction, set out in Ralph Klein''s Hermeneia commentary and consistent with the kinds of scribal accidents catalogued by Emanuel Tov, is that the Hebrew originally read something like ha-bekhor yo''el ve-ha-sheni aviyyah (''the firstborn Joel and the second Abijah''). A copyist''s eye-skip (haplography) lost yo''el ve-ha-sheni, and the surviving or marginally re-supplied phrase we-ha-sheni (''and the second'') was then misread as a proper name. The crux is graphic: the consonants of we-ha-sheni / wsny closely resemble what a later vocalizer read as ''Vashni.'' In other words, the ''name'' Vashni is a fossilized fragment of the Hebrew word for ''the second,'' the very word that in 1 Samuel 8:2 introduces Abijah.

The older apologetic harmonization — represented by Gleason Archer, who proposed that ''Vashni'' is simply an alternative name for Joel otherwise unknown to us — is unnecessary and has no positive support; it multiplies an unattested second name to avoid conceding a copyist''s slip. The cleaner and now-standard solution is text-critical: the MT of 1 Chronicles 6:28 is corrupt, the Septuagint and the parallel at 6:33 preserve the original sense, and Joel is Samuel''s firstborn in both books once the better reading is restored. Modern critical translations (NRSV, NIV, ESV) emend the verse accordingly, typically printing ''the firstborn Joel, and the second Abijah.''

The one honest qualification is that the corruption sits in the canonical Masoretic Text and is inherited by translations that follow it woodenly, such as the King James Version; a reader comparing KJV 1 Samuel 8:2 with KJV 1 Chronicles 6:28 encounters a real disagreement that the Hebrew tradition itself has effectively flagged and that the versions repair. Verdict: scribal corruption in the MT of 1 Chronicles 6:28, fully harmonized once the Septuagint reading and the internal evidence of 1 Chronicles 6:33 are restored.', 'Ralph W. Klein, *1 Chronicles* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2006), pp. 206-211, on 1 Chr 6:13/6:28.
P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *1 Samuel* (Anchor Bible 8; Doubleday, 1980), pp. 156-157, on 1 Sam 8:2.
Gary N. Knoppers, *1 Chronicles 1-9* (Anchor Bible 12; Doubleday, 2004), pp. 415-432.
Sara Japhet, *I & II Chronicles* (Old Testament Library; Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 156-167.
Emanuel Tov, *Textual Criticism of the Hebrew Bible*, 3rd rev. ed. (Fortress, 2012), pp. 224-236.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 187-189, on the Vashni/Joel question (representative harmonization).', 0, NULL, 6, 'OT_internal', '1 Samuel, 1 Chronicles', 3, 2, 1, 5, 1, 'MT 1 Chr 6:28 (Heb. 6:13) reads the otherwise-unattested name ''Vashni'' where the original likely read yo''el ve-ha-sheni (''Joel, and the second''); haplography dropped ''Joel'' and the residual wsny was reabsorbed as a name, with LXX and Peshitta preserving ''Joel'' (confirmed by 1 Chr 6:33 [Heb. 6:18] naming Joel as Samuel''s son).', 'BHS apparatus at 1 Chronicles 6:13 (Eng. 6:28); Klein, 1 Chronicles (Hermeneia), ad loc.');

The strongest harmonization, articulated in evangelical Psalms scholarship such as Tremper Longman''s work, distinguishes the experiential from the ontological: ''God hides his face'' is the language of the sufferer''s perception during distress, not a claim that God has in fact withdrawn his help; God remains a ''very present help'' (Ps 46:1) even when, subjectively, he feels absent. For the lament psalms this reading is exegetically sound. The genre of complaint precisely voices the felt gap between covenant promise and present experience, and psalms like 22 and 88 typically move (88 is the notable exception) from protest toward renewed trust. Within that frame, presence and felt absence are two vantage points on the same reality, and the texts are compatible.

The harmonization does not, however, reach every text the question gathers. Habakkuk 1:2 is a prophet''s complaint — ''O LORD, how long shall I cry, and thou wilt not hear!'' — and the divine answer in 1:5-6 does not say that God has been quietly helping all along; it announces that Yahweh is raising up the Chaldeans (Babylonians) to invade Judah. The narrative resolves the complaint by confirming that God is bringing about the very catastrophe the prophet begged him to avert. Francis Andersen''s Anchor Bible Habakkuk treats this as a genuine theodicy crisis, not a misperception to be corrected. Isaiah 45:15 — ''Verily thou art a God that hidest thyself'' (el mistatter) — occurs in a passage celebrating God''s decisive action through Cyrus; the hiddenness is predicated of God''s mode of working in history, affirmed theologically rather than lamented as a subjective feeling. Ezekiel 20:3 has Yahweh flatly refusing to be consulted by the elders of Israel (''I will not be enquired of by you''), and 1 Samuel 8:18 predicts that the people who demand a king will cry out and ''the LORD will not hear you in that day.'' These are not descriptions of a sufferer''s mistaken impression; they are assertions that divine withholding is real.

Walter Brueggemann''s framework in The Message of the Psalms and Theology of the Old Testament names this directly: alongside Israel''s ''core testimony'' to Yahweh''s reliable fidelity stands a ''countertestimony'' of divine hiddenness, absence, and unresponsiveness, generated by the lived experience of defeat and exile. Jon Levenson''s Creation and the Persistence of Evil makes the complementary point that the seamless omnipotence assumed by later systematic theology was never airtight in ancient Israelite religion, where divine power could be portrayed as contested, delayed, or deliberately withheld. The canon preserves both voices because Israel''s history demanded theological language for absence as much as for presence.

The verdict is therefore split. Where the texts are lament psalms, the experiential/ontological distinction harmonizes them well, and Psalm 22 itself models the resolution. Where the texts affirm divine hiding and refusal as theological fact and the surrounding narrative confirms it — Habakkuk, Isaiah 45:15, Ezekiel 20:3, 1 Samuel 8:18 — the harmonization fails, and a real countertestimony stands within the canon. Verdict: partially harmonizable; genuine theological tension between the ''reliable help'' tradition and an affirmed countertestimony of divine hiddenness.', 'Walter Brueggemann, *The Message of the Psalms: A Theological Commentary* (Augsburg, 1984), pp. 51-78.
Walter Brueggemann, *Theology of the Old Testament: Testimony, Dispute, Advocacy* (Fortress, 1997), pp. 317-358, on countertestimony.
Jon D. Levenson, *Creation and the Persistence of Evil: The Jewish Drama of Divine Omnipotence* (Princeton University Press, 1994).
Francis I. Andersen, *Habakkuk* (Anchor Bible 25; Doubleday, 2001), pp. 100-141, on Hab 1:2-5.
John Goldingay, *Psalms* (Baker Commentary on the Old Testament Wisdom and Psalms, 3 vols.; Baker Academic, 2006-2008).
Tremper Longman III, *Psalms: An Introduction and Commentary* (Tyndale Old Testament Commentaries 15-16; InterVarsity Press, 2014) (representative harmonization).', 0, NULL, 2, 'OT_internal', '1 Samuel, Psalms, Isaiah, Ezekiel, Hosea, Micah, Nahum, Habakkuk', 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The traditional harmonizations, marshaled in Gleason Archer''s *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* and the IVP Bible Background Commentary, run along three lines. First, two different men named Ner — the Ner of 1 Chronicles being, say, a grandfather whose name recurred in the family (papponymy was common). Second, genealogical telescoping: "Ner begat Kish" as shorthand for "Ner''s line produced Kish," with a generation silently skipped. Third, textual corruption in one tradition or the other. The strongest single move combines the first two: a fuller reconstructed stemma in which Abiel (also called Jeiel, per 1 Chr 9:35) fathered Ner, and Ner in turn fathered both Kish and a second Ner, so that 1 Sam 14:51''s "Ner... son of Abiel" and 1 Chr 8:33''s "Ner begat Kish" describe two different Ners separated by a generation. McCarter notes this is the reconstruction needed to save both texts.

The weakness is that each move buys coherence at a price. Positing an unmentioned second Ner invents a person the text does not name. Telescoping is a genuine and well-attested feature of ancient Near Eastern genealogy, but 1 Chronicles 9:35-44 is the most detailed Saulide genealogy in the Hebrew Bible and is built precisely on explicit father-son links (*holid*, "begat"); telescoping there would defeat the document''s evident purpose. The third option — textual corruption — actually carries the most evidence. The Septuagint of 1 Samuel 14:51 diverges from the Masoretic Text in a way that some scholars read as harmonizing toward the Chronicles line, and Ralph Klein''s Hermeneia *1 Chronicles* treats the verse''s textual history at length. The instability of the witnesses is itself a datum: ancient copyists felt the friction.

A further structural clue is the doublet. 1 Chronicles 8:33 and 9:39 reproduce the same Ner-Kish-Saul formula twice within one book — once closing the Benjaminite list of chapter 8, once closing the post-exilic Jerusalem resettlement census of chapter 9, where 9:35-44 functions as a narrative bridge into Saul''s death in chapter 10. Klein, Knoppers, and Sara Japhet all note the duplication. The likeliest account is that the Chronicler drew on one source genealogy twice for two structural purposes, or that a later redactor recopied it to reattach the Saulide line to the resettlement frame. Either way, the "Ner begat Kish" tradition has two in-book witnesses inside Chronicles before it ever meets the Samuel tradition.

McCarter''s Anchor Bible *1 Samuel* reads the two genealogies as independent traditions and observes that the Samuel material is itself not fully consistent — 9:1 traces Kish through Abiel and a string of otherwise unknown names, while 14:51 makes Ner and Kish brother-sons of Abiel. Knoppers''s Anchor Bible *1 Chronicles 1-9* treats the Chronicler as working from distinct source material never coordinated against Samuel. The parsimonious conclusion is that two independent genealogical traditions about the house of Saul circulated and were both canonized without reconciliation. Harmonization is achievable through emendation or by inserting unnamed persons, but the text as transmitted assigns Kish two different fathers. Verdict: genuine tension, most plausibly rooted in independent source traditions and an unstable textual transmission.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *1 Samuel* (Anchor Bible 8; Doubleday, 1980), pp. 169-173, 254-258, on 1 Sam 9:1 and 14:50-51
Ralph W. Klein, *1 Chronicles* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2006), pp. 256-275, on 1 Chr 8:29-40 and 9:35-44
Gary N. Knoppers, *1 Chronicles 1-9* (Anchor Bible 12; Doubleday, 2004), pp. 484-510
Sara Japhet, *I & II Chronicles* (Old Testament Library; Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 197-208
David Toshio Tsumura, *The First Book of Samuel* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 2007), pp. 263-269, representative harmonization
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), on 1 Sam 9:1 and 1 Chr 8:33', 0, NULL, 6, 'OT_internal', '1 Samuel, 1 Chronicles', 3, 2, 1, 3, 1, 'The Septuagint of 1 Sam 14:51 preserves a reading of the Ner/Kish/Abiel relationship that differs from the MT and is discussed as bearing on whether Ner is Kish''s father or brother.', 'Klein, 1 Chronicles (Hermeneia), pp. 256-275; McCarter, 1 Samuel (AB 8), pp. 254-258 on the LXX of 1 Sam 14:51');

The standard harmonization, defended in Tsumura''s NICOT and earlier in Keil & Delitzsch, distinguishes two senses of the verse. "Subdued" describes the immediate, decisive defeat at Mizpah; "came no more into the territory of Israel" is read as referring to a bounded period of Philistine quiescence after that victory, not permanent neutralization; and "the hand of the LORD was against the Philistines all the days of Samuel" is taken as a general statement of divine disposition rather than a claim of unbroken Israelite military supremacy. On this reading, the Philistines could still wage war (chapter 13) while Yahweh''s hand remained fundamentally against them across Samuel''s career. This is the strongest form of the apologetic, and it is not absurd: Hebrew narrative summaries do at times state tendencies rather than exceptionless facts.

The difficulty is the explicit temporal totality of the clause. "They came no more into the territory of Israel... all the days of Samuel" is not naturally read as "for a while," and chapter 13 has the Philistines doing precisely what 7:13 says they would not do — penetrating deep into Israelite territory — while Samuel lives. The apologetic reading must convert "all the days of Samuel" into "during some unspecified stretch of Samuel''s days," which drains the temporal claim of its force.

The critical reading, following McCarter''s Anchor Bible *1 Samuel* and Antony Campbell''s form-critical work on the Deuteronomistic History, identifies 7:13 as a redactional closing formula characteristic of the Deuteronomistic editor. It functions exactly like the idealizing judge-summaries in Judges — "and the land had rest forty years" (Judg 3:11; 5:31; 8:28) — which round off a deliverance episode with a theological declaration of peace under a divinely raised leader. Such formulas express the editor''s schema of judgeship, not a chronicle. The Mizpah narrative itself is framed by the Ebenezer stone (7:12), a memorial of victory, and the summary completes that literary frame. Martin Noth''s classic analysis explains why the friction exists: the Deuteronomist stitched together pre-existing narrative blocks — the Samuel traditions, the rise of Saul, the Philistine wars — and laid his own summarizing formulas over them without harmonizing the seams. The result is that 7:13 idealizes Samuel''s judgeship as a period of Philistine subjugation while the source material in chapters 9-15 portrays continuing Philistine dominance.

The "limited subduing" harmonization can be made to work only by softening "all the days of Samuel" into a vague interval, which the Hebrew resists. The more economical explanation is that 7:13 is a Deuteronomistic theological closure that the editor did not reconcile against the narrative he subsequently included. Verdict: genuine internal tension, best explained as a redactional summary formula at odds with the source narrative.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *1 Samuel* (Anchor Bible 8; Doubleday, 1980), pp. 143-153, 225-236, on 1 Sam 7 and 1 Sam 13
Antony F. Campbell, *1 Samuel* (Forms of the Old Testament Literature 7; Eerdmans, 2003), pp. 84-145
Martin Noth, *The Deuteronomistic History* (JSOTSup 15; JSOT Press, 1981)
Ralph W. Klein, *1 Samuel* (Word Biblical Commentary 10, 2nd ed.; Thomas Nelson, 2000), pp. 64-72, 122-132
David Toshio Tsumura, *The First Book of Samuel* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 2007), pp. 230-235, representative harmonization
C. F. Keil, *The Books of Samuel* (Biblical Commentary on the Old Testament; trans. James Martin; T&T Clark, 1866), pp. 73-75, on 1 Sam 7:13', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', '1 Samuel', 7, 2, 1, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The crux is the Hebrew word *rishonah*. The clause reads *ha-makkah ha-rishonah ʾasher hikkah yonatan we-noseʾ kelaw* — "the first/initial blow which Jonathan and his armor-bearer struck." *Rishonah* can carry an absolute temporal sense ("the first ever") or a relative, episode-internal sense ("the opening/initial" stroke of this particular engagement). Hebrew uses *rishon/rishonah* in both ways elsewhere — for the literal first in a sequence and for the initial phase of an event. Nothing in the morphology forces the career-spanning reading; context governs, and the context here is the Michmash battle, not a survey of Jonathan''s life.

The harmonization, defended in Tsumura''s NICOT and earlier in Keil, takes the episode-internal sense: "that first slaughter" denotes the opening casualties of the Michmash rout — the roughly twenty men felled in the initial assault before the larger panic and Israelite counterattack (14:15-23) — not the first killing of Jonathan''s career. This is grammatically sound, and it is precisely the reading adopted by modern critical translations: the NRSV ("In that first slaughter"), the NIV ("In that first attack"), and the ESV ("In that first strike") all render *rishonah* as marking the opening phase of the engagement. On this construal the Geba and Michmash actions sit in untroubled sequence, and the "first slaughter" simply heads the Michmash episode.

The KJV''s wording is not arbitrary, however, and the friction was felt in antiquity. McCarter''s Anchor Bible *1 Samuel* notes that the verse is textually messy. The Septuagint preserves a divergent form that does not carry the "first slaughter" formula in the same way, which raises the possibility that the Masoretic clause reflects an interpretive expansion or gloss not original to the narrative. S. R. Driver''s classic *Notes on the Hebrew Text... of the Books of Samuel* flags the textual difficulties throughout 1 Samuel 13-14, including the awkwardness of this clause. The instability of the witnesses is itself the tell: scribes and translators were already negotiating the phrase. So there are two convergent routes to dissolving the tension. The translation route reads *rishonah* as "opening" rather than "first-ever" — a legitimate and now-standard rendering. The text-critical route observes that MT and LXX disagree on the clause''s shape, so the difficulty partly arises from an unstable transmission rather than from a coherent claim that Michmash preceded Geba.

Neither route requires special pleading, and they reinforce each other. The contradiction is real in the KJV''s English but does not reflect a substantive disagreement in the underlying narrative, which plainly places Geba first (chapter 13) and Michmash after (chapter 14). This is among the softer Samuel "contradictions" — a function of the King James translation tradition and a knotty verse rather than a doctrinal or historical conflict in the source. That the contradiction evaporates under the readings adopted by virtually every modern critical translation is the strongest evidence that it was a translation artifact to begin with. Verdict: overstated — a translation/text-critical artifact, harmonizable on standard philological grounds.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *1 Samuel* (Anchor Bible 8; Doubleday, 1980), pp. 233-245, on 1 Sam 13:3 and 14:14
S. R. Driver, *Notes on the Hebrew Text and the Topography of the Books of Samuel* (2nd ed.; Clarendon, 1913), pp. 105-115, on 1 Sam 14
David Toshio Tsumura, *The First Book of Samuel* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 2007), pp. 339-380, representative harmonization
C. F. Keil, *The Books of Samuel* (Biblical Commentary on the Old Testament; trans. James Martin; T&T Clark, 1866), pp. 138-141, on 1 Sam 14:14
Hans Wilhelm Hertzberg, *I & II Samuel: A Commentary* (Old Testament Library; Westminster, 1964), pp. 99-116', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', '1 Samuel', 7, 2, 1, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

This case has a comparatively clean resolution that critical scholarship broadly accepts, resting on a well-documented editorial phenomenon. The element *baʿal* ("lord, master") was an acceptable epithet — applied even to Yahweh — in the early monarchy, and personal names compounded with it were unremarkable. As *baʿal* became identified with the Canaanite storm-god and theologically odious, scribes systematically substituted *bosheth* ("shame") for *baʿal* in names. The clearest demonstration is Saul''s son Eshbaal ("man of Baal," 1 Chr 8:33) appearing as Ishbosheth ("man of shame," 2 Sam 2-4), and Jonathan''s son Merib-baal (1 Chr 8:34) appearing as Mephibosheth (2 Sam 4:4; 9). McCarter documents this *baʿal/bosheth* pattern in his Anchor Bible *2 Samuel*. The "Ishvi" of 1 Samuel 14:49 is most plausibly a corrupted or alternate short form of the same name — *ʾish-yo* or a defective *ʾish-baʿal* — so the three-name and four-name lists overlap more than the surface suggests: Jonathan, Malchishua, and Ishvi/Eshbaal are the same three men under varying forms.

That leaves Abinadab as the genuine discrepancy: he appears in 1 Chronicles 8:33 and, crucially, in 1 Samuel 31:2 as one of Saul''s sons killed at Gilboa alongside Jonathan and Malchishua. His historicity is therefore not in doubt even within Samuel; he is simply absent from the family summary at 1 Samuel 14:49. This is the residual problem the *baʿal/bosheth* explanation does not touch.

The Chronicles four-son list, moreover, is textually well-anchored because it appears twice. 1 Chronicles 8:33 and 9:39 are a near-verbatim doublet — "Saul begat Jonathan, and Malchishua, and Abinadab, and Eshbaal" — once closing the Benjaminite genealogy of chapter 8, once closing the post-exilic Jerusalem resettlement census of chapter 9 just before the narrative pivots to Saul''s death in chapter 10. Klein (Hermeneia), Knoppers (Anchor Bible), and Japhet (OTL) read this as the Chronicler reusing one source genealogy for two structural functions. The four-son tradition thus has two in-book witnesses inside Chronicles before it is even compared to Samuel, giving it a textual stability the brief 1 Samuel 14:49 summary lacks.

The standard apologetic move (Archer, Tsumura) is that 1 Samuel 14:49 deliberately abbreviates — listing only sons relevant to the immediate narrative, or omitting Abinadab if he died young or played no role in the succession. This is possible but ad hoc, since 1 Samuel 31:2 shows Abinadab alive into the Gilboa battle. The cleaner reading, defended by McCarter, is that 14:49 is a short, formulaic household notice that does not claim exhaustiveness, while Chronicles preserves a fuller and more stable genealogy; the apparent name-disagreement over Ishvi/Eshbaal dissolves into the *baʿal/bosheth* substitution and textual variation around a single individual.

So the contradiction is largely real at the level of the printed lists but mostly resolvable. The name divergence is a transmission and editorial artifact; the count divergence reduces to the single omission of Abinadab from a summary that is not presented as complete. That residual omission is a redactional question — why the summary truncates — rather than a flat assertion that Saul had a different set of sons. Verdict: largely harmonizable as a textual/editorial matter (name substitution plus a non-exhaustive summary), with a minor unresolved redactional wrinkle in 1 Samuel 14:49''s omission of Abinadab.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *1 Samuel* (Anchor Bible 8; Doubleday, 1980), pp. 254-258, on 1 Sam 14:49-51
P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *2 Samuel* (Anchor Bible 9; Doubleday, 1984), pp. 85-87, on the baʿal/bosheth substitution
Ralph W. Klein, *1 Chronicles* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2006), pp. 252-275, on 1 Chr 8:29-40 and 9:35-44
Gary N. Knoppers, *1 Chronicles 1-9* (Anchor Bible 12; Doubleday, 2004), pp. 484-510
Sara Japhet, *I & II Chronicles* (Old Testament Library; Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 197-208
David Toshio Tsumura, *The First Book of Samuel* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 2007), pp. 380-385, representative harmonization', 0, NULL, 6, 'OT_internal', '1 Samuel, 1 Chronicles', 3, 2, 1, 5, 1, 'The name ''Ishvi'' (1 Sam 14:49) reflects the documented later editorial substitution of boshet (''shame'') for ba''al in personal names, the same figure appearing as ''Eshbaal'' (1 Chr 8:33) and ''Ishbosheth'' (2 Sam 2-4).', 'McCarter, 2 Samuel (AB 9), pp. 85-87 on the ba''al/boshet substitution; McCarter, 1 Samuel (AB 8), pp. 254-258');

The apologetic harmonization, defended in Tsumura''s NICOT and most evangelical Samuel commentaries, reads the two as sequential and distinct in scope: chapter 13 rejects Saul''s *dynasty* (his line will not inherit the throne), while chapter 15 rejects Saul *personally* (Yahweh tears the kingship from Saul himself). The lexical data give this reading real purchase. In 13:14 Samuel says *loʾ taqum mamlakhtekha*, "your kingdom will not stand/continue" — a statement about the endurance of Saul''s reign or line. In 15:23 Samuel says *wayyimʾasakha mi-melekh*, "he has rejected you from being king" — a statement about Saul''s own status. The two declarations are not verbally identical, and the harmonist is right that they can be distinguished as dynastic versus personal repudiation. This is the strongest form of the reconciling case.

The difficulty is that the narrative does not treat chapter 13 as a prior, lesser rejection that chapter 15 completes. 1 Samuel 15:11 frames Yahweh''s regret — "It repenteth me that I have set up Saul to be king" — as the turning point, with no backward reference to a rejection already pronounced in chapter 13. After chapter 15 Samuel and Saul never meet again (15:35), and the consequence flows directly: in chapter 16 Samuel anoints David as the replacement, explicitly because Yahweh has rejected Saul (16:1). The dynastic transfer is narratively grounded in chapter 15, not chapter 13. If chapter 13 had already ended Saul''s dynasty, chapter 15''s rejection and the immediate move to anoint David would be redundant rather than climactic. There is also a chronological strain: 1 Samuel 13:1 opens with a regnal formula and 13:8-14 places the first rejection at the very start of Saul''s reign, yet Saul continues to rule and to be treated as legitimately king for years afterward, which fits awkwardly with a definitive dynastic termination in his first regnal acts.

McCarter''s Anchor Bible *1 Samuel* reads the doublet as the product of joining independent traditions about why the house of Saul failed; Campbell''s FOTL volume and Klein''s WBC concur, and Wellhausen''s *Prolegomena* gave the classic source-critical statement that early Israel preserved more than one explanation of Saul''s downfall — a cultic-offense tradition and an Amalekite-*ḥerem* tradition — which the editor incorporated without collapsing into a single sequence of escalating guilt. The harmonist''s dynasty-then-person scheme is grammatically available and captures a genuine difference in the two declarations, but it imposes a smoothing logic on what bears the marks of a redactional doublet: two complete rejection scenes, each presented as decisive, neither cross-referencing the other. Verdict: genuine doublet — the canon preserves two distinct rejection traditions, and the harmonizing sequence, while possible, is read into rather than out of the text.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *1 Samuel* (Anchor Bible 8; Doubleday, 1980), pp. 225-236, 257-271, on 1 Sam 13:8-15 and 1 Sam 15
Julius Wellhausen, *Prolegomena to the History of Israel* (trans. J. Sutherland Black and Allan Menzies; A. & C. Black, 1885), pp. 245-272
Antony F. Campbell, *1 Samuel* (Forms of the Old Testament Literature 7; Eerdmans, 2003), pp. 132-167
Ralph W. Klein, *1 Samuel* (Word Biblical Commentary 10, 2nd ed.; Thomas Nelson, 2000), pp. 122-159
David Toshio Tsumura, *The First Book of Samuel* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 2007), pp. 339-410, representative harmonization
Hans Wilhelm Hertzberg, *I & II Samuel: A Commentary* (Old Testament Library; Westminster, 1964), pp. 105-129', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', '1 Samuel', 7, 2, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The principal apologetic moves run in two directions. Gleason Archer argues that the *herem* was demonstrably incomplete: Amalekites reappear raiding in 1 Samuel 27 and 30, and an Amalekite remnant is destroyed by Simeonites only in Hezekiah''s day (1 Chronicles 4:43). On this reading "Agagite" need not mean a literal descendant of one executed king but simply "of Amalekite/Agagite stock," with Agag functioning either as a dynastic title (compare the recurring "Abimelech" among Philistine kings) or an ethnic label. The strongest form of this case notes that 1 Samuel itself contradicts its own totalizing report within a dozen chapters, so the survival of Amalekites is not a problem imported from Esther but one already internal to Samuel.

The second, and in critical scholarship the dominant, approach is literary. Jon Levenson, Adele Berlin, and Carol Bechtel read Esther as constructing a deliberate Saul-versus-Agag rematch. Mordecai is introduced in Esther 2:5 as a Benjaminite "son of Kish" — Kish being the father of Saul (1 Samuel 9:1) — and Haman as "the Agagite." The author is signaling that the Benjaminite house will at last finish the business Saul botched, a reading reinforced by the threefold refrain in Esther 9:10, 15, 16 that the Jews "laid not their hands on the spoil," the precise failure for which Saul was condemned (1 Samuel 15:9, 19). Frederic Bush notes that the gentilic functions typologically rather than genealogically: Esther is a diaspora novella working midrashically on the Saul cycle, not a chronicle preserving Amalekite family records.

The literary frame is the most economical solution, and it is the consensus. But it is worth seeing precisely what it costs the harmonizer who wants both texts as straight history. The frame works as literary echo only because both poles are stylized: the *herem* narrative is the Deuteronomistic Historian''s polemic against Saul''s disobedience (its interest is theological, not a casualty report — McCarter and Diana Edelman both read 1 Samuel 15 as a rejection-of-Saul tradition), and Esther''s "Agagite" is a typological tag, not a lineage. So either the Amalekites were not in fact annihilated, in which case the emphatic "utterly destroyed all" is false on its own terms, or "Agagite" is symbolic, in which case Esther is not reporting genealogy at all. Archer''s survival argument actually concedes the first horn: it rescues Esther only by impeaching Samuel''s own claim to completeness.

The surface friction therefore dissolves, but not in a way that vindicates a literalist reading of either text. Read 1 Samuel 15 as theological polemic and Esther as festival fiction, and there is no genealogical impossibility — there never was a living biological chain from Agag to Haman that the author intended as fact. The tension is real only for the interpreter who insists on reading both as sober history simultaneously. Verdict: harmonizable as literary typology, but only at the cost of the historicist reading both passages are usually pressed to support.', 'Jon D. Levenson, *Esther: A Commentary* (Old Testament Library; Westminster John Knox, 1997), pp. 67-69, on the Saul-vs-Agag literary frame in Esther.
Adele Berlin, *Esther: The Traditional Hebrew Text with the New JPS Translation* (JPS Bible Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 2001), pp. 33-35, on the typological Agagite tag.
Carol M. Bechtel, *Esther* (Interpretation; Westminster John Knox, 2002), pp. 31-35, on Esther''s reworking of the Saul tradition.
Frederic W. Bush, *Ruth, Esther* (Word Biblical Commentary 9; Word, 1996), pp. 383-385, on the spoil refrain echoing Saul''s failure.
P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *1 Samuel* (Anchor Bible 8; Doubleday, 1980), pp. 260-269, on the Deuteronomistic rejection-of-Saul narrative.
Diana Edelman, *King Saul in the Historiography of Judah* (JSOTSup 121; JSOT Press, 1991), pp. 71-93, on the polemical shaping of 1 Samuel 15.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 169-170 [apologetic foil], on Amalekite survival and the Agagite label.', 0, NULL, 6, 'OT_internal', '1 Samuel, Esther', 3, 2, 1, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

The traditional harmonization, as old as Keil and Delitzsch and repeated by Archer and Eugene Merrill, is that one of Jesse''s sons died young and childless, so the Chronicler — composing a genealogy, not a biography — simply dropped him. The strongest form of this argument is grounded in the Chronicler''s documented technique: he routinely compresses genealogies, telescopes generations, and omits figures who do not serve his Davidic-cultic program. A son who left no descendants would be genealogically invisible, and the Chronicler''s interest is descent, not completeness. Rabbinic tradition pressed harder, positing a son named Elihu; the slot finds a possible anchor in 1 Chronicles 27:18, which names "Elihu, of the brethren of David" as a tribal officer over Judah — read by some as a seventh non-David brother (the Septuagint and several Hebrew witnesses there read "Eliab," the known eldest, which complicates rather than resolves the matter).

The difficulty is that the Chronicler is not silently abbreviating; he is counting. "David the seventh" is an ordinal claim, a deliberate numbering, not a partial roster that happens to stop short. To salvage the dead-brother hypothesis one must suppose the Chronicler knew of an eighth son from his Samuel source, chose to drop him, and then renumbered David from eighth to seventh — which is not omission but active recalculation against the inherited tradition. Sara Japhet''s commentary flags precisely this: the Chronicler regularly reshapes received material to fit structural and numerical schemes, and a seven-son count for the house of David is plausibly such a scheme rather than an accident of pruning. Ralph Klein''s Hermeneia volume reaches the same conclusion, treating the discrepancy as the Chronicler conforming the Davidic family to a tidy seven-son ideal — seven being a number of completeness — rather than evidence of a deceased brother. Gary Knoppers notes the broader pattern of the Chronicler''s genealogical artistry in 1 Chronicles 1-9, where lists are theological constructions, not registry transcripts.

There is also a textual wrinkle. The Septuagint of 1 Samuel 17:12 belongs to the very block of MT "plus" material (17:12-31) that is absent from the shorter LXX-B recension, which means the eight-son figure of 17:12 sits in disputed, possibly secondary, text. That does not rescue the harmonization — 16:10-11 independently yields eight — but it shows the Samuel tradition itself is layered, and that the round numbers in these narratives are doing literary rather than archival work.

The parsimonious reading is that 1 Chronicles is a tendentious recasting of the Samuel material and the counts diverge because each author served a different design. The dead-brother solution is purely post hoc: no text records such a death, the Chronicler never alludes to one, and the harmonization must invent a corpse to preserve a number. Verdict: genuine numerical discrepancy, best explained as the Chronicler''s structural recasting rather than a brother who died young.', 'Sara Japhet, *I and II Chronicles: A Commentary* (Old Testament Library; Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 75-77, on the Chronicler''s structural recasting of Jesse''s sons.
Ralph W. Klein, *1 Chronicles* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2006), pp. 92-94, on conforming the Davidic family to a seven-son count.
Gary N. Knoppers, *1 Chronicles 1-9* (Anchor Bible 12; Doubleday, 2003), pp. 304-307, on the artistry of the Chronicler''s genealogies.
P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *1 Samuel* (Anchor Bible 8; Doubleday, 1980), pp. 273-279, on the Samuel anointing and 17:12 plus-material.
Hans Wilhelm Hertzberg, *I and II Samuel: A Commentary* (Old Testament Library; Westminster, 1964), pp. 138-141, on Jesse''s eight sons in 1 Samuel 16.
Carl Friedrich Keil and Franz Delitzsch, *Biblical Commentary on the Books of the Chronicles*, trans. Andrew Harper (T&T Clark, 1872), pp. 65-67 [apologetic harmonization], on a son dying young.
Eugene H. Merrill, *A Commentary on 1 & 2 Chronicles* (Kregel Exegetical Library; Kregel Academic, 2015), pp. 71-75 [apologetic harmonization], defending the omitted-son explanation.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 187-188 [apologetic foil], on the missing son of Jesse.', 0, NULL, 7, 'OT_internal', '1 Samuel, 1 Chronicles', 1, 1, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The harmonizing case has two strands. The first, taken by Archer and by Kaiser in Hard Sayings of the Bible, reads "man of war" as an admiring courtier''s hyperbole: David had killed a lion and a bear protecting the flock (17:34-36), enough to earn the epithet without battlefield experience, so there is no real contradiction in his being untested against an army. The second strand compresses and psychologizes the timeline: Saul''s mental deterioration explains his failure to recall a recently arrived musician, and the question "whose son is this youth?" (17:55) seeks David''s lineage — relevant because Saul had promised his daughter and tax exemption to the victor''s household (17:25) — not his personal identity.

These readings, however, do not engage the decisive evidence, which is text-critical. The Old Greek of 1 Samuel preserved in Codex Vaticanus (LXX-B) is markedly shorter here: it lacks 17:12-31 and 17:55-18:5 — precisely the material that generates the contradiction. In the shorter text David is introduced once, as Saul''s musician-armor-bearer, and the Goliath episode does not reintroduce him as an unknown. P. Kyle McCarter argues that LXX-B reflects an earlier, shorter Hebrew recension and that the Masoretic Text is a conflation of two originally independent David-introduction traditions, stitched together without harmonizing the inconsistency. Emanuel Tov reaches the same conclusion, treating the MT pluses as a secondary literary expansion rather than an Old Greek abridgment. The text-critical evidence is not unanimous: Dominique Barthelemy, in the OBO 73 volume that recorded a four-way scholarly debate, argued for the priority of the longer MT and read the LXX as a deliberate shortening, and Anneli Aejmelaeus has cautioned about reconstructing the Old Greek''s parent text with confidence. The Qumran witness 4QSam-a is regrettably fragmentary at exactly this passage and does not settle the direction.

Even granting the dispute over which recension is prior, the contradiction is not a misreading: the two portraits cannot both be smoothly true of one continuous narrative, which is why an ancient witness either lacks or telescoped the offending verses. The hyperbole defense is the weakest available response because it addresses only the epithet "man of war" and ignores the structurally harder data — Saul''s total non-recognition of his own armor-bearer in 17:55-58 and David''s never having "proved" armor (17:39). Those features are not explained by calling a lion-killer a warrior. The harmonizer who insists on the MT as a single seamless report must reject the shorter Greek as corrupt, which inverts the judgment of nearly every major textual critic of Samuel, who regard the difficilior shorter text as the more likely earlier form. Verdict: genuine contradiction arising from the conflation of two independent traditions, with manuscript evidence (LXX-B) preserving a stage before the seam was created.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *1 Samuel* (Anchor Bible 8; Doubleday, 1980), pp. 284-309, on the two conflated David traditions and the shorter LXX-B.
Emanuel Tov, *Textual Criticism of the Hebrew Bible*, 3rd ed. (Fortress, 2012), pp. 334-336, on the MT pluses in 1 Samuel 17.
Dominique Barthelemy et al., *The Story of David and Goliath: Textual and Literary Criticism* (OBO 73; Vandenhoeck & Ruprecht, 1986), pp. 1-114, recording the four-way debate over recension priority.
Anneli Aejmelaeus, "How to Reach the Old Greek in 1 Samuel and What to Do with It," in *Congress Volume Helsinki 2010*, ed. Martti Nissinen (VTSup 148; Brill, 2012), pp. 185-205, on Old Greek methodology in Samuel.
Hans Wilhelm Hertzberg, *I and II Samuel: A Commentary* (Old Testament Library; Westminster, 1964), pp. 147-152, on the David-introduction doublet.
Walter C. Kaiser Jr. et al., *Hard Sayings of the Bible* (InterVarsity Press, 1996), pp. 215-217 [apologetic foil], on the hyperbole reading of "man of war."
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 178-180 [apologetic foil], on David''s prior warrior status.', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', '1 Samuel', 7, 2, 2, 1, 1, 'LXX-B (Codex Vaticanus) lacks 1 Sam 17:12-31 and 17:55-18:5, the very passages that generate the warrior-vs-untested-youth tension, reflecting a shorter recension against the conflated MT.', 'Tov, Textual Criticism of the Hebrew Bible, 3rd ed. (Fortress, 2012), 334-336; cf. Barthelemy et al., The Story of David and Goliath, OBO 73.');

The standard harmonization, advanced by Archer, Kaiser, and David Tsumura in the NICOT volume, reads verse 50 as a proleptic summary of the whole encounter and verse 51 as the narrated detail: the stone felled and mortally wounded Goliath, and the sword stroke was the coup de grace and decapitation. Tsumura grounds this in a recognized feature of Hebrew narrative — the summary-then-resumptive-detail pattern, where a verse states the outcome and a following verse fills in the mechanics. On this reading the polel *waymotetehu* in v. 51 carries the nuance "finished him off," perfectly compatible with the stone having already done the decisive damage. As a description of Hebrew narrative syntax this is not implausible; the pattern genuinely exists.

The friction the harmonization must absorb is the function of the clause "there was no sword in the hand of David." That is not part of a neutral summary of stage one of a two-stage kill; it is a pointed theological aside, the same theology David voices in 17:47 — "the LORD saveth not with sword and spear." The narrator is asserting the manner of victory as the point: deliverance came without a sword. Verse 51 then puts a sword — Goliath''s own — into David''s hand and has him kill with it. The reader is asked to hold "no sword killed him" and "David killed him with the sword" within two verses.

Text-criticism again clarifies the mechanism. The block of MT material peculiar to the longer recension (relative to LXX-B) includes verse 50; the shorter Old Greek moves directly from the stone-strike to David running and dispatching Goliath with the sword (the substance of vv. 49, 51), without the intervening editorial summary of v. 50. McCarter and Tov treat v. 50 as part of the secondary expansion. If the shorter text is earlier, the contradiction is the by-product of a later editor''s theological gloss colliding with the inherited sword narrative; the original told a single, coherent account in which the sling fells Goliath and the sword finishes and beheads him.

The upshot is a genuine fork for the harmonizer. Either v. 50 is a redactional addition — in which case the canonical text is demonstrably composite and the "no sword" clause is a later editor''s theology, not the original storyline — or the summary-then-detail reading is correct and the narrator emphatically denied a sword in v. 50 only to narrate a sword-kill in v. 51, which strains the plain force of his own gloss. The summary-then-detail reading is grammatically viable for the *killing* verbs; what it cannot comfortably absorb is the deliberate "no sword" assertion. Verdict: harmonizable at the level of Hebrew narrative syntax (summary then detail), but the emphatic "no sword" clause is better explained as a secondary theological gloss preserved unevenly in the textual tradition — partially harmonizable, with the residual tension located in v. 50''s editorial comment.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *1 Samuel* (Anchor Bible 8; Doubleday, 1980), pp. 284-309, on the secondary status of 17:50 in the longer recension.
David Toshio Tsumura, *The First Book of Samuel* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 2007), pp. 462-466, on the summary-then-detail reading of vv. 50-51.
Emanuel Tov, *Textual Criticism of the Hebrew Bible*, 3rd ed. (Fortress, 2012), pp. 334-336, on the longer-recension pluses in 1 Samuel 17.
Antony F. Campbell, *1 Samuel* (Forms of the Old Testament Literature VII; Eerdmans, 2003), pp. 178-192, on the structure of the Goliath duel narrative.
Ronald F. Youngblood, *1, 2 Samuel* (Expositor''s Bible Commentary, rev. ed., vol. 3; Zondervan, 2009), pp. 222-225, on the coup-de-grace reading of v. 51.
Walter C. Kaiser Jr. et al., *Hard Sayings of the Bible* (InterVarsity Press, 1996), pp. 215-217 [apologetic harmonization], on the manner of Goliath''s death.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), p. 180 [apologetic foil], on how Goliath died.', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', '1 Samuel', 7, 2, 3, 1, 1, 'The shorter LXX-B text of the David-Goliath narrative lacks v.50 (the ''no sword'' summary), so the slingstone/sword tension is a feature of the longer MT recension.', 'Tov, Textual Criticism of the Hebrew Bible, 3rd ed. (Fortress, 2012), 334-336; McCarter, 1 Samuel, AB 8, 284-309.');

The apologetic response, taken by Archer, by the NIV Study Bible note, and by Tsumura in his NICOT volume on 2 Samuel, is that 2 Samuel 21:19 is scribally corrupt and 1 Chronicles 20:5 preserves the original: a copyist garbled "Elhanan slew Lahmi the brother of Goliath" into "Elhanan slew Goliath the Bethlehemite," generating a phantom clash with the David story. Stated at its strongest, this is a genuine text-critical argument — the consonantal overlaps are real, and corruption in proper names and patronymics is common in Samuel, a book whose Masoretic text is notoriously damaged.

The difficulty is that the proposed direction of change runs against the governing principle, *lectio difficilior potior*: the harder reading is more likely original because scribes harmonize toward coherence, not away from it. "Elhanan killed Goliath" is the theologically awkward reading — it competes with the most celebrated single combat in Israel''s tradition — and is exactly the sort of claim no later editor would invent. "Elhanan killed the brother of Goliath" is the convenient reading, precisely what a harmonizer protecting the David tradition would produce. McCarter, A. A. Anderson in the Word commentary, and Graeme Auld in the OTL volume all therefore judge 2 Samuel 21:19 the earlier text and 1 Chronicles 20:5 the deliberate smoothing; Ralph Klein and Sara Japhet read the Chronicles parallel the same way from the Chronicler''s side. The apologetic case has to suppose that the Chronicler — or his Vorlage — uniquely preserved a correct reading that every other witness lost, and that the loss happened to fall on the one verse whose original form would embarrass the national epic.

The wider data reinforce the critical reconstruction. Second Samuel 23:24 lists among David''s elite warriors "Elhanan the son of Dodo of Bethlehem" — an Elhanan from Bethlehem, the very town later attached to David — which several scholars (McCarter, McKenzie) take as a clue that an authentic Goliath-slaying by a Bethlehemite hero named Elhanan stood at the root of the tradition and was subsequently transferred to David as the David legend grew. On that reconstruction the David-and-Goliath narrative of 1 Samuel 17 is a secondary literary development that absorbed an older exploit, the Chronicler later detected the collision, and 1 Chronicles 20:5 is the editorial repair that betrays awareness of the problem it solves.

The "scribal corruption" defense is not unreasonable on its face, but it inverts the text-critical principle it invokes and requires a uniquely convenient transmission accident. The simpler, more powerful explanation is that Elhanan slew Goliath in the older tradition, the feat was reassigned to David, and the Chronicler harmonized. Verdict: genuine contradiction reflecting a transferred legend, with 1 Chronicles 20:5 a transparent later harmonization rather than a preserved original.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *II Samuel* (Anchor Bible 9; Doubleday, 1984), pp. 449-450, on the priority of 2 Samuel 21:19.
A. Graeme Auld, *I & II Samuel: A Commentary* (Old Testament Library; Westminster John Knox, 2011), pp. 568-571, on Elhanan as Goliath''s slayer.
A. A. Anderson, *2 Samuel* (Word Biblical Commentary 11; Word, 1989), pp. 254-255, on 2 Samuel 21:19 and its Chronicles parallel.
Ralph W. Klein, *1 Chronicles* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2006), pp. 408-409, on the Chronicler''s smoothing of the Elhanan tradition.
Sara Japhet, *I and II Chronicles: A Commentary* (Old Testament Library; Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 369-371, on the reworking of 2 Samuel 21:19.
Steven L. McKenzie, *King David: A Biography* (Oxford University Press, 2000), pp. 76-79, on the legendary transfer of Goliath''s defeat to David.
David Toshio Tsumura, *The Second Book of Samuel* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 2019), pp. 309-313 [apologetic harmonization], defending the scribal-corruption reading.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 178-179 [apologetic foil], on who killed Goliath.', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', '1 Samuel, 2 Samuel', 7, 1, 3, 1, 1, '2 Sam 21:19 (MT) credits Elhanan with killing Goliath; 1 Chron 20:5 reads ''Lahmi the brother of Goliath,'' a harmonizing reparse of the same consonants (beit-hallachmi / et-lachmi achi).', 'McCarter, II Samuel, AB 9 (Doubleday, 1984), 449-450; Klein, 1 Chronicles, Hermeneia, 408-409.');

Then 1 Samuel 17:55 shatters that. Watching David advance on Goliath, Saul turns to Abner and asks, "whose son is this youth?" The Hebrew, ben-mî zeh hanna''ar, is the idiom of personal identification, not genealogical record-keeping. Abner — śar-hatsāḇā'', commander of the entire army, the man under whom an armor-bearer would serve — answers under oath, "As thy soul liveth, O king, I cannot tell." After the fight Saul puts the question to David himself: "Whose son art thou, thou young man?" Three layers of non-recognition (Saul, Abner, the court) follow directly on a chapter that made David Saul''s intimate.

The apologetic harmonization comes in two flavors. The first (Archer, Kaiser) holds that Saul was not asking who David was but about his lineage, because the reward of 17:25 — the king''s daughter and tax-exemption for the victor''s father''s house — required knowing the family. The second (Tsumura, Youngblood) appeals to Saul''s documented mental deterioration: a man tormented by an evil spirit might fail to recognize his own retainer, especially after a gap in which a maturing David''s appearance changed.

Flavor one fights the grammar. If the question were about the household for a marriage-contract, Abner''s sworn confession of total ignorance is inexplicable; a general would know the family of the king''s own armor-bearer. "I cannot tell" answers "who is he?", not "what is his tax status?" Flavor two must extend Saul''s amnesia to Abner and to the whole court, none of whom suffer from an evil spirit, and it cannot explain why the narrator frames the scene as a genuine first identification.

The text-critical data decides the matter. The Old Greek of Codex Vaticanus (LXX-B) lacks a large block of the Hebrew here, including 17:12-31 and the crucial 17:55-18:5, producing a far smoother narrative in which David is already Saul''s musician and no double-introduction occurs. The Masoretic Text preserves the longer, conflated form. The dominant critical view (McCarter, Auld, Hertzberg, building on Wellhausen and Driver) is that 16:14-23 and chapter 17 began as two independent traditions of David''s arrival at court — one as court musician, one as giant-slayer plucked from obscurity — that a redactor set side by side. The shorter LXX-B reflects a Hebrew Vorlage from before the full conflation; the MT reflects the conflated stage. Tov treats the divergence as a paradigm case of literary editions developing within the textual transmission itself, not mere scribal slips.

That compositional account predicts exactly what we find: two incompatible "first encounters," each internally coherent, joined without harmonization. A single author writing continuous history would not have Saul personally recruit, love, and arm a young man and then, one chapter later, fail to recognize him while his own general swears ignorance. The non-recognition is not a psychological subtlety; it is the visible seam between two sources. Verdict: genuine contradiction, the residue of two stitched-together traditions.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *1 Samuel* (Anchor Bible 8; Garden City: Doubleday, 1980), pp. 295-309, on the David-introduction doublet.
