The apologetic harmonization is reasonable and McCarter himself grants it some force: David is invoking the contracted bride-price (Hebrew mōhar), the legally stipulated condition Saul named, not the bonus he threw in to humiliate the king in return. Legal claims to a wife would naturally cite the covenanted terms rather than the actual body-count. The diplomatic, quasi-juridical register of 2 Samuel 3:14 — a formal demand for restitution of a wife — supports this reading. On its own this resolves the tension between the demand and David''s recollection.

But there is a text-critical complication that reframes the whole question. The Septuagint and the Qumran scroll 4QSam-a read one hundred in 1 Samuel 18:27, not two hundred. On that tradition David delivers exactly what was asked, and 2 Samuel 3:14''s "hundred" matches precisely; there is no numerical clash at all. The MT''s two hundred is then most plausibly explained as an early scribal enhancement of David''s prowess. McCarter argues that one hundred is original and the doubled figure is a glorifying expansion, a pattern well attested in the Samuel tradition, where 4QSam-a repeatedly preserves shorter and arguably earlier readings against the MT. Ulrich''s study of the Qumran Samuel text and Josephus documents how often the scroll and the Old Greek agree against the MT, and Auld and Anderson lean toward the shorter number here.

This is therefore a soft contradiction whose resolution depends on a text-critical judgment. If the MT''s two hundred is primary, the numbers genuinely diverge and one needs the "covenanted price versus delivered amount" distinction to reconcile them — a workable but not airtight move. If the Qumran/LXX one hundred is primary, the figures agree and the apparent contradiction is simply an MT scribal variant. Either route concedes something. The position that insists simultaneously on the MT''s two hundred as the inerrant original and on a harmonized reading must do work on both fronts at once: defend the doubled figure as authentic and then explain the discrepancy through the legal-price distinction. Most critics find it cleaner to treat one hundred as original throughout. Verdict: harmonizable, but at the cost of either accepting a textual variant or invoking a legal-versus-actual distinction the bare numbers do not state.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *1 Samuel* (Anchor Bible 8; Garden City: Doubleday, 1980), pp. 316-318, on the 100/200 variant.
P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *II Samuel* (Anchor Bible 9; Garden City: Doubleday, 1984), pp. 113-115, on 2 Samuel 3:14.
