A. Graeme Auld, *I & II Samuel* (Old Testament Library; Louisville: Westminster John Knox, 2011), pp. 219-221, 379-381.
Eugene Ulrich, *The Qumran Text of Samuel and Josephus* (Harvard Semitic Monographs 19; Missoula: Scholars Press, 1978), pp. 75-78, on 4QSam-a and the Old Greek.
Hans Wilhelm Hertzberg, *I and II Samuel* (Old Testament Library; Philadelphia: Westminster, 1964), pp. 158-160.
Ronald F. Youngblood, *1, 2 Samuel* (Expositor''s Bible Commentary, rev. ed., vol. 3; Grand Rapids: Zondervan, 2009), pp. 232-234 [apologetic harmonization].
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Grand Rapids: Zondervan, 1982), p. 181 [apologetic foil].', 0, NULL, 7, 'OT_internal', '1 Samuel, 2 Samuel', 1, 1, 2, 3, 1, 'LXX and 4QSam-a read 100 (not 200) Philistines in 1 Sam 18:27, matching the 100 of 2 Sam 3:14 and eliminating the numerical clash in that textual tradition.', 'McCarter, 1 Samuel, AB 8, 316-318; Ulrich, The Qumran Text of Samuel and Josephus, HSM 19, 75-78.');

The apologetic response — argued by Tsumura and Youngblood — is the strongest harmonization in this cluster: the chapter is not naming two origins but tracing one escalation. Verse 9''s "eyed David" (Hebrew ''ōyēn, regarded with suspicion) is the seed; verses 12-16 develop it into fear; and verse 29''s "became David''s enemy continually" (lit. "all the days") is the terminal, summarizing endpoint of that arc, not a fresh ignition. On a plain literary reading this is persuasive. Narratives routinely state a development at its inception and again at its climax, and the adverb "continually" naturally reads as the settled state the suspicion finally reached.

Where the matter becomes genuinely interesting is the manuscript evidence. The Old Greek of Codex Vaticanus (LXX-B) is markedly shorter through 1 Samuel 18, lacking verses 10-11 (the first spear-throw), 17-19 (the Merab episode), and portions of 21, 26-27, and 29-30. The resulting Greek narrative is cleaner and single-tracked: the song, Saul''s suspicion, the Michal plot, the foreskins, full enmity. McCarter argues that LXX-B reflects an earlier, shorter Hebrew Vorlage and that the MT represents a later conflated text in which a second tradition (including the first spear-throw and Merab) was woven in. On that view the MT''s apparent multiplicity of turning-points is an artifact of conflation, while the shorter text shows the original clean escalation.

This yields a soft contradiction whose status depends on how the question is framed. If the claim is merely "the chapter contains two summary statements about the onset of enmity," the escalation reading dissolves it: both refer to one deteriorating relationship. If the claim is "the chapter is internally chaotic because it fuses sources," the critical account is the more economical, and the chaos is real — but chaos produced by conflation is not the same as a flat logical contradiction. The chapter is messy; "messy" is not "contradictory." The SAB framing presses a genuine source-seam into a binary either/or it was never structured to answer. Verdict: overstated — a real redactional seam, but the two statements harmonize as stages of a single escalation on any continuous reading of the chapter.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *1 Samuel* (Anchor Bible 8; Garden City: Doubleday, 1980), pp. 304-318, on the conflated MT of 1 Samuel 18.
A. Graeme Auld, *I & II Samuel* (Old Testament Library; Louisville: Westminster John Knox, 2011), pp. 218-225.
Hans Wilhelm Hertzberg, *I and II Samuel* (Old Testament Library; Philadelphia: Westminster, 1964), pp. 157-162.
Antony F. Campbell, *1 Samuel* (Forms of the Old Testament Literature VII; Grand Rapids: Eerdmans, 2003), pp. 195-207.
David Toshio Tsumura, *The First Book of Samuel* (NICOT; Grand Rapids: Eerdmans, 2007), pp. 481-498 [apologetic harmonization].
Ronald F. Youngblood, *1, 2 Samuel* (Expositor''s Bible Commentary, rev. ed., vol. 3; Grand Rapids: Zondervan, 2009), pp. 229-235 [apologetic harmonization].', 0, NULL, 8, 'OT_internal', '1 Samuel', 2, 2, 2, 3, 1, 'LXX-B is substantially shorter through 1 Sam 18, omitting vv. 10-11, 17-19 and parts of 21, 26-30, yielding a single-tracked escalation rather than multiple ignition points.', 'Tov, Textual Criticism of the Hebrew Bible, 3rd ed. (Fortress, 2012), 334-336; McCarter, 1 Samuel, AB 8, 304-318.');

In 1 Samuel 10 Saul is freshly anointed by Samuel, meets a band of prophets coming down from the high place, is seized by the spirit of God, and prophesies among them; the bystanders who knew him are astonished, and the saying captures public surprise that the new ruler shows a prophetic streak. The mood is legitimating and positive. In 1 Samuel 19 the situation is inverted: Saul, now David''s relentless pursuer, sends three sets of messengers to Naioth in Ramah, each of whom is overcome by prophetic ecstasy; finally Saul himself comes, strips off his clothes, and lies naked prophesying "all that day and all that night." Here the same saying marks Saul''s humiliation, his prophetic frenzy presented as pathetic and out of control.

The apologetic response (Archer, Kaiser) is that both episodes contributed to the proverb''s currency — it arose in chapter 10 and was reinforced in chapter 19. This is not absurd; proverbs do accrete occasions of use. But it does not square with the grammar. The ''al-kēn formula does not mean "this is one of several occasions on which people said"; it means "this is the reason people say." Deploying that origin-formula twice for one saying is precisely the redactional fingerprint critics identify as a source-doublet, comparable to the twin etiologies of Beersheba in Genesis 21 and 26 or the double naming of Bethel.

The critical reading, shared by McCarter, Hertzberg, and Auld, is that 1 Samuel preserves two independent traditions about Saul and ecstatic prophecy — one early and favorable, one late and degrading — and that the redactor incorporated both with their separate etiologies intact. Diana Edelman, in her study of the Saul historiography, reads the two scenes as serving opposite ideological ends within the larger pro-Davidic shaping of the material: chapter 10 establishes Saul''s charismatic legitimacy, while chapter 19 dismantles it, the once-empowering spirit now an instrument of his undoing in Samuel''s very presence. The Deuteronomistic compiler had both traditions in front of him and retained both, untroubled by the fact that an origin-formula can only literally apply once.

The "both events contributed" defense, examined closely, is itself a concession: it admits that at least one of the two narrators is not in fact reporting the origin, since a single saying can have only one origin. Either chapter 10 originated it and chapter 19 reapplies it anachronistically, or chapter 19 originated it and chapter 10 places it too early, or both narrators independently claim originating status and at least one is mistaken. The text supplies no mechanism for choosing, because none was intended — the duplication is the natural result of compiling parallel source material rather than composing a single unified account. Verdict: genuine doublet, two etiologies for one proverb, the signature of compiled tradition.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *1 Samuel* (Anchor Bible 8; Garden City: Doubleday, 1980), pp. 181-186, 327-333, on the parallel prophet-band etiologies.
Hans Wilhelm Hertzberg, *I and II Samuel* (Old Testament Library; Philadelphia: Westminster, 1964), pp. 84-88, 167-169.
A. Graeme Auld, *I & II Samuel* (Old Testament Library; Louisville: Westminster John Knox, 2011), pp. 112-118, 229-232.
