The apologetic responses cluster into three moves. (1) The grammatical move: *epi* with the genitive can be read as a literary reference marker, "in the passage about Abiathar the high priest," appealing to Mark 12:26, *epi tou batou*, "in the passage about the bush." This is the line favored by D. A. Carson and endorsed by Craig Blomberg. (2) The proleptic-title move: Abiathar was the famous high priest of David''s reign, so Mark designates the period by its better-known name, much as one might say "when Lincoln was a boy in Kentucky." (3) Archer''s two-priests move: because 2 Samuel 8:17 reverses the names (Ahimelech son of Abiathar), there may have been overlapping father-son pairs, so a reading that keeps both texts accurate is allegedly available.

The data cuts against each. On the grammar, Joel Marcus shows that *epi* plus a genitive proper name in this construction normally means "in the time of" or "in the lifetime of" — exactly the temporal sense it carries elsewhere in Luke-Acts (Luke 3:2, *epi archiereōs Hanna kai Kaiapha*, "in the high-priesthood of Annas and Caiaphas"). The supposed parallel in Mark 12:26 is not parallel: there the construction includes the definite article (*epi tou batou*) and the object is a known textual unit ("the bush"), whereas 2:26 has an anarthrous personal name in the genitive. The proleptic-title move concedes the historical point and merely relabels the error as deliberate imprecision. Archer''s two-pairs hypothesis is unfalsifiable special pleading that the narrative nowhere supports, and 2 Samuel 8:17 is itself widely regarded as a scribal corruption (see the parallel entry on Abiathar''s parentage).

The most telling evidence is text-internal and text-external. Externally, the phrase *epi Abiathar archiereōs* is simply absent from a significant block of witnesses — it is omitted by Codex Bezae (D), W, and the Old Latin and Old Syriac traditions, a deletion that the editors of NA28 explain as scribes excising a known difficulty. Internally, both later Synoptics that reproduce the saying — Matthew 12:3-4 and Luke 6:3-4 — repeat the showbread argument in detail yet drop the name Abiathar entirely. On the two-source hypothesis, where Matthew and Luke used Mark, this is precisely the editorial fingerprint one expects: two independent evangelists, working from Mark, recognized the chronological problem and silently removed it. Adela Yarbro Collins, Marcus, and Bart Ehrman all read the omission as corrective redaction rather than coincidence.

The most economical account is that Mark, or Mark''s pre-Markan source, or the tradition placed in Jesus'' mouth, erred on an Old Testament detail; later tradents noticed and cleaned it up, in the manuscripts and in the parallel Gospels alike. The harmonization industry depends on a sense of *epi* the Markan Greek does not naturally bear and on coincidences the manuscript record better explains as deliberate fixes. Verdict: genuine error preserved in Mark and silently corrected downstream.', 'Joel Marcus, *Mark 1-8: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Bible 27; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 239-241, on the temporal sense of *epi Abiathar archiereōs* in Mark 2:26.
Adela Yarbro Collins, *Mark: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 200-203, on the Markan redaction and Synoptic correction at Mark 2:25-26.
P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *I Samuel* (Anchor Bible 8; Doubleday, 1980), pp. 348-355, on Ahimelech as priest of Nob and Abiathar''s later flight to David.
Bart D. Ehrman, *Jesus, Interrupted* (HarperOne, 2009), pp. 7-9, on the Abiathar/Ahimelech error as a Markan mistake.
D. A. Carson, *Matthew* (Expositor''s Bible Commentary, rev. ed., vol. 9; Zondervan, 2010), pp. 327-328, offering the apologetic ''in the passage about Abiathar'' reading.
Craig L. Blomberg, *The Historical Reliability of the Gospels*, 2nd ed. (IVP Academic, 2007), pp. 178-180, endorsing the reference-marker reading [apologetic foil].
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 362-363, proposing the two-priests harmonization [apologetic foil].', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_vs_NT', '1 Samuel, Mark', 7, 2, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Jesus invokes the episode in all three Synoptics (Matthew 12:3-4; Mark 2:25-26; Luke 6:3-4) to defend his disciples'' plucking grain on the Sabbath, and in each he says David ate "and those who were with him" — Mark and Luke even adding that David "gave also to those who were with him." The argument requires multiple eaters: Jesus is drawing an a fortiori parallel between his hungry disciples and David''s hungry band, so the companions must be in the picture.

The strongest harmonization, advanced by Archer, Carson, and Blomberg, reads the two texts at different focal lengths. David''s men existed and were nearby; the five-loaves request and David''s own statement that the young men''s vessels were holy (21:5) presuppose a company to provision. Jesus, summarizing the episode, foregrounds the band David was acting for. On this reading the Synoptic phrase is not a claim that the men stood beside David at the altar but a loose, accurate-in-substance reference to David and his followers. The Hebrew text does not deny that David had men; it stages them offstage. This is a genuine softening: the contradiction is not a flat one, because "alone" in 21:1 describes the scene at Nob, while "those with him" in the Gospels describes the cause for which David acted.

Two considerations keep the tension from dissolving entirely. First, the existence of the companions in 1 Samuel 21 rests on David''s own deceptive speech; the narrator never confirms a band is actually waiting nearby. When David next appears, in 22:1-2, he flees alone to the cave of Adullam, and only there do his brothers, his father''s house, and "every one that was in distress" gather to him — roughly four hundred men. On the plain sequence of the Deuteronomistic narrative, David''s recognized band coalesces after Nob, not before, which is why some critics judge that the Synoptic Jesus has the men eating bread they were not yet present to eat. (The Greek text of 1 Samuel 21 in the LXX is somewhat fuller than the MT and complicates, but does not remove, this picture.) Second, the redactional pressure is visible. The showbread tradition is doing argumentative work in a Sabbath controversy, and Mark''s framing — David "and those with him" — is shaped to mirror the disciples. Marcus, Collins, and Luz all note that the evangelists are not reconstructing 1 Samuel with antiquarian precision; they are deploying it.

The honest assessment lands between the poles. As a bare contradiction the charge is overstated: a contextual reading of David provisioning his band makes Jesus'' phrasing defensible. But the harmonization buys its coherence by treating David''s cover-story as historical fact and by reading past the narrative''s insistence on his isolation at Nob. Verdict: partially harmonizable — the tension is real but turns on narrative framing rather than a flat numerical contradiction.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *I Samuel* (Anchor Bible 8; Doubleday, 1980), pp. 348-352, on David''s deception at Nob and the LXX longer text of 1 Samuel 21.
Joel Marcus, *Mark 1-8* (Anchor Bible 27; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 239-244, on the redactional shaping of the showbread tradition for the Sabbath dispute.
Adela Yarbro Collins, *Mark: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 200-203, on the argumentative function of the David example.
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 8-20: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2001), pp. 180-184, on Matthew''s handling of the David parallel.
Bart D. Ehrman, *Jesus, Interrupted* (HarperOne, 2009), pp. 6-9, on the Synoptic reuse of the showbread tradition.
Craig L. Blomberg, *The Historical Reliability of the Gospels*, 2nd ed. (IVP Academic, 2007), pp. 178-180, offering the contextual reading of David''s band [apologetic foil].
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 362-363, on the provisioning harmonization [apologetic foil].', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_vs_NT', '1 Samuel, Matthew, Mark, Luke', 10, 2, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

Against this stand three notices that invert the relationship. The court list in 2 Samuel 8:17 reads "Zadok the son of Ahitub, and Ahimelech the son of Abiathar, were the priests." The parallel in 1 Chronicles 18:16 has "Abimelech the son of Abiathar" (the MT here reads Abimelech, a recurring scribal slip for Ahimelech), and 1 Chronicles 24:6 again names "Ahimelech the son of Abiathar." So a single notice in Samuel and two in Chronicles reverse father and son.

The apologetic harmonization, run by Keil and Delitzsch and by Archer, posits two father-son pairs: Abiathar named a son Ahimelech after the boy''s grandfather, a common papponymic practice, so the Ahimelech of 2 Samuel 8:17 is Abiathar''s son serving alongside him. This is internally possible — papponymy is well documented — but the text never introduces a second Ahimelech, and the harmonization has to manufacture an entire priestly figure to rescue the consistency of the genealogy.

The text-critical solution is both older and simpler. Julius Wellhausen argued that 2 Samuel 8:17 suffered a transposition of names and that the original read, in line with all of 1 Samuel, "Abiathar son of Ahimelech son of Ahitub." The corruption then propagated into the Chronicler''s source, since Chronicles here depends on the Samuel court list. McCarter and A. A. Anderson both adopt the emendation, and it is supported by the suspiciously parallel "Zadok son of Ahitub" in the same verse: the likeliest scenario is that a scribe assimilated the second priest''s filiation to the first, swapping Abiathar and Ahimelech. Witnesses in the Lucianic (Antiochene) recension of the Greek tradition point toward the corrected order, and the variation between Ahimelech and Abimelech in 1 Chronicles 18:16 confirms that this verse was a locus of scribal instability.

What is striking is that the apologetic and critical solutions converge on the same historical result: the priest in question was Abiathar son of Ahimelech, exactly as 1 Samuel says four times over. They differ only on the status of the Masoretic Text. The two-pairs hypothesis preserves the inerrancy of the received text by inventing an unattested second priest; the scribal-error hypothesis accepts that the MT of 2 Samuel 8:17 and its Chronicles parallels carry a transmission mistake. The Chronicler''s own habits make the latter the more economical: as Japhet, Klein, and Knoppers show, the Chronicler frequently reproduced and occasionally garbled the genealogical lists he inherited, and the priestly courses of 1 Chronicles 24 are a heavily schematized, late composition in which such a transposition would easily survive uncorrected.

The upshot is a clean dilemma. On the surface the texts contradict one another about whether Abiathar fathered Ahimelech or vice versa. One cannot hold simultaneously that the Masoretic Text is errorless and that the genealogy is internally consistent: the harmonization that saves consistency does so by positing a person the narrative never names, while the reading that respects the received text concedes a contradiction. Verdict: harmonizable as to the underlying history (Abiathar son of Ahimelech) via a scribal transposition in 2 Samuel 8:17 — but the received text contradicts itself.', 'Julius Wellhausen, *Der Text der Bücher Samuelis* (Vandenhoeck & Ruprecht, 1871), pp. 176-178, the classic emendation of 2 Samuel 8:17.
P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *II Samuel* (Anchor Bible 9; Doubleday, 1984), pp. 253-256, adopting the transposition reading of 2 Samuel 8:17.
