The standard harmonization, defended by Archer, Eugene Merrill, and Andrew Hill, leans on the semantics of the Hebrew verb. The Chronicler uses *dāraš*, which can carry the freighted sense of "seek with covenant devotion," while 1 Samuel 28:6 uses *šāʾal*, "to ask, request." On this reading, Chronicles does not deny that Saul made the inquiry of 28:6; it denies that he sought Yahweh rightly, with a faithful and obedient heart — so the verses describe a defective inquiry (Samuel) and the absence of a true inquiry (Chronicles) without formal contradiction.

The lexical move is thinner than it looks. *Dāraš* and *šāʾal* are near-synonyms in the language of consulting the deity, and the Chronicler himself uses *dāraš* elsewhere for the plain act of inquiry, including inquiry that succeeds (e.g., the contrast he draws when faithful kings *dāraš* Yahweh). More decisively, the verse explicitly opposes Saul''s inquiry of the medium to his non-inquiry of Yahweh: "he asked counsel of a medium ... and inquired not of the LORD." The natural force is not "he inquired badly" but "he went to the medium instead of to Yahweh" — a flat statement that Yahweh was not consulted, which is exactly what 1 Samuel 28:6 contradicts. To make the harmonization work one must read *dāraš* as "seek devotedly" in Chronicles while reading the very act it allegedly describes, in Samuel, as a genuine inquiry that simply failed.

The critical explanation is straightforward redaction. The Chronicler''s treatment of Saul is notoriously tendentious: Saul is dispatched in a single chapter that functions wholly to clear the stage for David, and the death notice is a theological summary, not a transcript. As Japhet, Klein, and Knoppers document, the Chronicler systematically reshaped the Deuteronomistic History to deliver an unambiguous doctrine of retribution — faithfulness brings life, *maʿal* (unfaithfulness) brings death. The complicated picture in 1 Samuel 28, where Saul did the licit thing first and only then turned to necromancy, blunts that doctrine. So the Chronicler flattens it: Saul died because he was unfaithful and consulted a medium rather than Yahweh, full stop. Steven McKenzie reads 1 Chronicles 10 as a programmatic theological preface to David''s reign precisely on these grounds. The inconvenient detail of 28:6 — that Saul tried Yahweh and got nothing — is simply not preserved, because it would complicate the moral.

The harmonization that wants both texts to be exactly accurate must assign *dāraš* one meaning in Chronicles and treat *šāʾal* in Samuel as a different kind of act, an asymmetry the Hebrew does not warrant. The more economical reading is that the Chronicler rewrote his source to sharpen a theological point and in doing so reversed a narrative fact. Verdict: genuine contradiction arising from the Chronicler''s tendentious redaction.', 'Sara Japhet, *I and II Chronicles* (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 228-233, on the Chronicler''s retribution theology and reshaping of Saul.
Ralph W. Klein, *1 Chronicles* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2006), pp. 287-294, on the Saul death notice as theological summary.
