The decisive harmonizing distinction, developed at length by N. T. Wright and standard in the technical commentaries, is between resuscitation and resurrection. The earlier figures were resuscitated: restored to ordinary mortal life, subject again to death. Lazarus walked out of the tomb to die a second time; Jairus''s daughter grew up and died; the Shunammite''s son lived a normal lifespan. Resurrection in the sense Paul intends is something categorically different — the entry into an imperishable, transformed, deathless body that Paul spells out in the same chapter (1 Corinthians 15:42-54, *sōma pneumatikon*, raised "in incorruption"). Christ is the first to be raised in that sense, and the agricultural metaphor *aparchē* makes the point precisely: the firstfruits are the initial installment of a harvest of the same kind, guaranteeing the rest (15:23, "Christ the firstfruits, then those who belong to Christ at his coming"). The cognate language of Colossians 1:18 and Revelation 1:5 — Christ as *prōtotokos ek tōn nekrōn*, "firstborn from the dead" — confirms that the claim is about inaugurating a new order, not about chronological priority among all revivals.

This distinction is not special pleading imported to rescue the text; it is built into Paul''s own vocabulary and argument. The whole logic of 1 Corinthians 15 depends on Christ''s resurrection being the unrepeatable, eschatological first of a series that the dead in Christ will share — a status none of the resuscitated figures occupy, since all of them died again. Fitzmyer reads Acts 26:23 the same way, with *prōtos* marking Christ as the pioneer of eschatological resurrection rather than the first human ever to return from death. Thiselton and Fee both stress that *aparchē* is a representative-and-guarantee term, not a stopwatch.

The Samuel example, on which this entry leans, is in fact the weakest of the lot and cuts against the objection. The apparition at Endor is not a resuscitation to bodily life at all: Samuel is summoned as a shade "coming up out of the earth" (28:13), speaks, and is not described as restored to living embodiment — he is a denizen of Sheol briefly made to appear. By any definition Samuel neither resuscitated nor resurrected; he was, at most, a necromantic manifestation. As a counterexample to "first to rise from the dead" it fails on its own terms. The genuinely tight counter-cases are Lazarus and the others raised within the Gospel narratives — but those are precisely the cases the resuscitation/resurrection distinction was designed to handle, and handles cleanly: they returned to die again, whereas the risen Christ "dies no more; death no longer has dominion over him" (Romans 6:9).

One can still observe that the Hebrew writers and the Synoptic narrators did not operate with Paul''s developed two-age framework, so the harmony is a function of Christian theological systematization rather than a claim the earlier authors would have recognized. But that is a point about conceptual development, not a logical contradiction. Read in their own senses, the texts do not collide: Paul asserts Christ''s chronological-and-categorical primacy in eschatological, deathless resurrection, a claim untouched by temporary revivals to mortal life. Verdict: harmonizable — the texts use ''rise from the dead'' in two distinct senses, and Paul''s claim concerns resurrection-to-immortality, not revival.', 'N. T. Wright, *The Resurrection of the Son of God* (Fortress, 2003), pp. 209-219, 332-334, on the resuscitation/resurrection distinction [apologetic distinction].
Anthony C. Thiselton, *The First Epistle to the Corinthians* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 2000), pp. 1223-1230, on *aparchē* as a representative-and-guarantee term.
Gordon D. Fee, *The First Epistle to the Corinthians* (NICNT, rev. ed.; Eerdmans, 2014), pp. 829-836, on the firstfruits metaphor and the resurrection harvest.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Acts of the Apostles* (Anchor Bible 31; Doubleday, 1998), pp. 759-764, on *prōtos* in Acts 26:23 as pioneer of eschatological resurrection.
Richard B. Hays, *First Corinthians* (Interpretation; John Knox, 1997), pp. 263-268, on the eschatological framework of 1 Corinthians 15.
P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *I Samuel* (Anchor Bible 8; Doubleday, 1980), pp. 418-423, on the Endor apparition as a summoned shade from Sheol.
Dale C. Allison Jr., *Resurrecting Jesus: The Earliest Christian Tradition and Its Interpreters* (T&T Clark, 2005), pp. 199-219, on early Christian categories of resurrection versus revival.', 0, NULL, 3, 'OT_vs_NT', '1 Samuel, Acts, 1 Corinthians', 7, 3, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The same chapter punctures it. Verse 33: Samuel tells Agag, "as thy sword hath made women childless, so shall thy mother be childless among women." Agag''s mother is alive — "all the people" of verse 8 was not actually all the people. The problem compounds across the books. 1 Samuel 27:8-9 has David raiding the Amalekites among the "old inhabitants of the land." 1 Samuel 30:1 has Amalekites numerous enough to sack Ziklag and carry off captives, and David''s reprisal in 30:17 explicitly lets 400 escape on camels. Then 2 Samuel 1:8-10 has an Amalekite present at Saul''s death — the very nation Saul allegedly exterminated furnishes his executioner (or claimed one). Centuries later in Esther 3:1, Haman the "Agagite" — read by tradition as a descendant of Agag — is grand vizier of Persia. The line Saul was to extinguish keeps reappearing.

The strongest apologetic position is that "utterly destroyed all" is ancient Near Eastern war-rhetoric hyperbole, not a census claim. Paul Copan, Nicholas Wolterstorff, and Richard Hess argue this from comparative epigraphy. Egyptian, Assyrian, and Moabite royal inscriptions routinely claim total annihilation that was demonstrably non-literal. The Mesha Stele''s boast that Israel "perished forever" is the textbook case — Israel demonstrably persisted. Merneptah''s Stele claims "Israel is laid waste, his seed is not," again hyperbolically. The function of such language is to assert decisive, divinely-backed victory, not to log a body count. K. Lawson Stone defended a comparable reading for the Joshua conquest material, and Younger''s monograph documents the conquest-account genre in detail.

The hyperbole reading has real evidentiary backing, and a critical scholar should grant it. But it cuts both ways. If 1 Samuel 15:8 is rhetorical hyperbole, then the text reports as accomplished something that did not literally happen — and the same standard then has to apply to Joshua and Judges, where the conquest is similarly totalizing and similarly contradicted (Judges 1 has Canaanites everywhere Joshua said they were destroyed). More acutely, the herem texts cannot be simultaneously literal divine commands carrying real moral and theological weight and convention-bound rhetoric that nobody expected to be true. The apologist who wants Yahweh''s command in 15:3 to be a genuine moral act and the execution report in 15:8 to be non-literal convention has imported two incompatible reading-frames into a single chapter. The hyperbole defense resolves the internal numerical tension only by surrendering the historicity the same reader usually wants to preserve.

Most critical scholars (McCarter, Hertzberg, Klein, Niditch, Edelman) read the chapter as stylized theological narrative whose function is to explain Saul''s rejection, not to chronicle a literal genocide. On that reading the "all" of verse 8 is formulaic, the later Amalekite appearances reflect the ongoing reality the formula overwrote, and the tension is a byproduct of combining an idealized herem account with the realistic court history of David. Verdict: harmonizable as ANE hyperbole, but only at the cost of conceding the report is non-literal — which dissolves the contradiction and the literalist reading together.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *1 Samuel* (AB 8; Garden City: Doubleday, 1980), pp. 260-269, on 1 Samuel 15.
Hans Wilhelm Hertzberg, *I and II Samuel* (OTL; Philadelphia: Westminster, 1964), pp. 121-128, on 1 Samuel 15.
Ralph W. Klein, *1 Samuel* (WBC 10, 2nd ed.; Nashville: Thomas Nelson, 2000), pp. 145-155, on 1 Samuel 15.
Susan Niditch, *War in the Hebrew Bible: A Study in the Ethics of Violence* (New York: Oxford University Press, 1993), pp. 28-77.
Diana Edelman, *King Saul in the Historiography of Judah* (JSOTSup 121; Sheffield: JSOT Press, 1991), pp. 71-93.
Paul Copan, *Is God a Moral Monster? Making Sense of the Old Testament God* (Grand Rapids: Baker, 2011), pp. 158-178 [apologetic hyperbole reading].
Nicholas Wolterstorff, "Reading Joshua," in Michael Bergmann, Michael J. Murray, and Michael C. Rea, eds., *Divine Evil? The Moral Character of the God of Abraham* (Oxford: Oxford University Press, 2011), pp. 236-256 [apologetic non-literal reading].
K. Lawson Younger Jr., *Ancient Conquest Accounts: A Study in Ancient Near Eastern and Biblical History Writing* (JSOTSup 98; Sheffield: JSOT Press, 1990), pp. 227-266.
Lawson G. Stone, "Ethical and Apologetic Tendencies in the Redaction of the Book of Joshua," *Catholic Biblical Quarterly* 53.1 (1991): pp. 25-36.', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', '1 Samuel, 2 Samuel', 7, 2, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The traditional harmonization processes each in turn. Suicide is the literal physical cause (1 Samuel 31; 1 Chronicles 10:4). The Amalekite is lying in 2 Samuel 1 to ingratiate himself with David, expecting a reward for dispatching David''s rival — instead David executes him as a self-confessed regicide. The Philistines "slew Saul" in the sense that they inflicted the mortal wounds and created the situation; the Hebrew of 21:12 uses nakah, "struck/smote," which has a broad semantic range. And Yahweh "slew him" in 10:14 in the theological sense that every event is ultimately divine providence, here divine judgment for Saul''s covenant breach. Archer, Youngblood, and Walter Kaiser all run this stacked harmonization.

The Amalekite-was-lying move is the strongest, and it is genuinely defensible. The narrator of 2 Samuel 1 plausibly intends the reader to see through the Amalekite: his story conflicts with the account just given in 1 Samuel 31, and David''s response makes narrative sense only if the man is taken at his self-incriminating word, true or not. McCarter and Anderson both grant this reading. The Hebrew supports it too: 1 Samuel 31:4-5 uses forms of mut ("die") in a sequence that leaves no room for a third party finishing Saul off. So the suicide and the Amalekite accounts can be reconciled as fact versus fabrication.

The other two claims harmonize only by letting the verb "to kill" do progressively more metaphorical work. The Philistines did not literally kill Saul — they wounded him and he killed himself; "slew" in 21:12 is at best loose causation. Yahweh did not literally kill Saul — 10:14 is theological causation laid over a historical event. Each step is individually plausible, but stacking them means "killed" carries four different senses across the four texts: self-inflicted, third-party (alleged/false), proximate-causal, and ultimate-providential. With that much semantic flexibility one could harmonize almost any set of death reports.

The critical reading treats these as independent or redactionally-shaped traditions, each foregrounding a different cause for a different purpose. The Deuteronomistic account in 1 Samuel 31 wants Saul as the tragic suicide who falls on his sword rather than be taken. The court apology in 2 Samuel 1 needs a non-David agent of Saul''s death so the narrative can clear David of the king''s blood while still letting Saul die — and the Amalekite, conveniently a member of the cursed nation, serves perfectly (Halpern and McKenzie read the whole David narrative as an extended exoneration of a man suspiciously well-served by the deaths of his rivals). The Chronicler, working with Samuel as a source, reshapes the death into explicit divine retribution for consulting the medium of Endor and disobedience. They do not synthesize into a single forensic account; they reflect different theological agendas applied to a shared event. Verdict: partially harmonizable — suicide and the lying Amalekite genuinely cohere, but the Philistine and divine "killings" reconcile only by equivocating on the verb, and the spread of accounts is best explained as distinct traditions.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *II Samuel* (AB 9; Garden City: Doubleday, 1984), pp. 56-67, on 2 Samuel 1:8-10.
A. A. Anderson, *2 Samuel* (WBC 11; Dallas: Word, 1989), pp. 1-15, on 2 Samuel 1:8-10.
Hans Wilhelm Hertzberg, *I and II Samuel* (OTL; Philadelphia: Westminster, 1964), pp. 229-236, on 1 Samuel 31.
Sara Japhet, *I and II Chronicles* (OTL; Louisville: Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 225-233, on 1 Chronicles 10:14.
Ralph W. Klein, *1 Chronicles* (Hermeneia; Minneapolis: Fortress, 2006), pp. 287-294, on 1 Chronicles 10:14.
Steven L. McKenzie, *King David: A Biography* (Oxford: Oxford University Press, 2000), pp. 110-122.
Baruch Halpern, *David''s Secret Demons: Messiah, Murderer, Traitor, King* (Grand Rapids: Eerdmans, 2001), pp. 76-87.
Ronald F. Youngblood, *1, 2 Samuel* (Expositor''s Bible Commentary, rev. ed., vol. 3; Grand Rapids: Zondervan, 2009), pp. 444-450 [apologetic harmonization].
Walter C. Kaiser Jr., Peter H. Davids, F. F. Bruce, and Manfred T. Brauch, *Hard Sayings of the Bible* (Downers Grove: IVP, 1996), pp. 217-219 [apologetic harmonization].
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Grand Rapids: Zondervan, 1982), pp. 181-182 [apologetic foil].', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', '1 Samuel, 2 Samuel, 1 Chronicles', 7, 2, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The rest of Samuel — the very narrative the Chronicler is abridging — contradicts the globalized version. Abner takes Ishbosheth, Saul''s son, and makes him king over Israel in 2 Samuel 2:8-11; he reigns two years before being assassinated. Mephibosheth, Jonathan''s lame son and Saul''s grandson, survives and eats at David''s table (2 Samuel 9). Rizpah, Saul''s concubine, is alive in 2 Samuel 3:7 and again in 2 Samuel 21. And 2 Samuel 21:1-14 has David hand over seven male descendants of Saul to the Gibeonites for execution to end a three-year famine: Rizpah''s two sons Armoni and Mephibosheth, plus the five sons of Saul''s daughter (the MT reads Michal, but they are Merab''s sons by Adriel — itself a textual crux). That is at minimum nine living members of Saul''s house after Gilboa.

The apologetic harmonization is the Chronicler-summary defense. "All his house died together" means the household that was with Saul at the battle — his sons present at Gilboa and his fighting retinue — not every blood relative. Eugene Merrill and Andrew Hill read bayit here as the military household or the immediate fighting unit rather than the dynastic line, and Archer takes the same line. This has lexical support: Hebrew bayit is genuinely elastic, denoting a physical house, a dynasty ("the house of David"), an extended family, or a retinue, depending on context.

But the apologist''s lexical move does not fully cover the Chronicler''s editorial move. The point is not merely what bayit could mean but why the Chronicler changed "all his men" to "all his house" in the first place. The Chronicler compresses Saul''s entire reign and death into a single chapter whose function is to terminate the house of Saul cleanly so that David''s kingship has no genealogical competition; the very next material moves to David. Japhet, Klein, and Knoppers all argue the Chronicler deliberately heightens the totality of Saul''s end to underscore the theological discontinuity between the rejected house and the chosen one. The Chronicler unquestionably knew the Samuel traditions about Ishbosheth and the surviving Saulides — he was working from that scroll — and chose to omit the two-year rival reign because it complicated his clean transfer of "the kingdom unto David" (10:14). On this reading the wording is not an innocent summary but a tendentious one.

So the contradiction is real at the canonical surface — 1 Chronicles 10:6 says the whole house died at Gilboa; 2 Samuel narrates a house that conspicuously did not — but it is a product of deliberate Chronistic theology rather than a naive factual blunder. The apologetic harmonization survives only by reading bayit as "battle retinue," which is possible lexically but runs against the Chronicler''s evident purpose in making the statement total. Verdict: genuine surface contradiction arising from intentional Chronistic compression; the lexical harmonization is available but reads against the grain of the redaction.', 'Sara Japhet, *I and II Chronicles* (OTL; Louisville: Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 228-233, on 1 Chronicles 10:6.
Ralph W. Klein, *1 Chronicles* (Hermeneia; Minneapolis: Fortress, 2006), pp. 287-294, on 1 Chronicles 10:6.
Gary N. Knoppers, *1 Chronicles 10-29* (AB 12A; New York: Doubleday, 2004), pp. 521-529, on 1 Chronicles 10:6.
P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *II Samuel* (AB 9; Garden City: Doubleday, 1984), pp. 83-97, 437-449, on 2 Samuel 2 and 21.
Steven L. McKenzie, *1-2 Chronicles* (Abingdon Old Testament Commentaries; Nashville: Abingdon, 2004), pp. 122-126, on 1 Chronicles 10:6.
