Andrew E. Hill, *1 and 2 Chronicles* (NIV Application Commentary; Grand Rapids: Zondervan, 2003), pp. 154-159 [apologetic harmonization].
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Grand Rapids: Zondervan, 1982), pp. 192-193 [apologetic foil].', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', '2 Samuel, 1 Chronicles', 7, 2, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The apologetic harmonization is the dual-name hypothesis: this son bore two names, Chileab and Daniel, and each book preserved one. Archer, Keil-Delitzsch, and Youngblood take this line. Multiple naming is well attested in the Hebrew Bible — Jacob/Israel, Solomon/Jedidiah (2 Samuel 12:25), Gideon/Jerubbaal, Uzziah/Azariah — so dual naming is not implausible per se, and other Davidic sons in the parallel lists do show name-pairs across the textual tradition.

The textual-critical picture is more illuminating. The name was unstable in transmission. The Septuagint of 2 Samuel 3:3 does not read "Chileab": Codex Vaticanus reads Δαλουια (Daluia) and the Lucianic/Antiochene recension preserves still other forms, while the parallel Chronicles tradition supports Daniel. McCarter reconstructs the original name as Daniel ("God is my judge"), with the MT Samuel "Chileab" and the Greek "Daluia/Daluyah" reflecting downstream scribal confusion of an originally Daniel-like form. Anderson and Auld note the same instability; Japhet flags the variant tradition on the Chronicles side. The graphic and aural distance between dalet-nun-yod-aleph-lamed (Daniel) and kaf-lamed-aleph-bet (Chileab) is large, which is itself why some scholars suspect Chileab may be a secondary form or an alternate sobriquet that displaced the original in the Samuel line.

Weighing the two explanations: the dual-name hypothesis is possible but entirely undocumented — neither text says "who was also called," the standard biblical signal for a second name (contrast Solomon, where 2 Samuel 12:25 explicitly supplies "Jedidiah"). The textual-variation hypothesis is documented: surviving manuscripts actually carry divergent readings, which is precisely the footprint of scribal evolution. Critically, Chronicles is dependent on Samuel as a source — the Chronicler reproduced this Hebron list almost verbatim — so the divergence at this one name reflects either differing Vorlagen (the Chronicler''s Samuel scroll already read something nearer Daniel) or a deliberate substitution, not two independent witnesses to a dual name.

This is a minor contradiction; proper names are among the most corruption-prone elements in any manuscript tradition, and nothing theological hangs on it. But it is real on the canonical surface, and the harmonization that preserves the inerrancy of both names requires positing an unattested cultural fact (a second name) where the manuscript evidence already supplies a sufficient natural explanation. Verdict: minor genealogical discrepancy, most economically explained as textual corruption of a single name; the dual-name harmonization is possible but post-hoc and undocumented.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *II Samuel* (AB 9; Garden City: Doubleday, 1984), pp. 100-103, on 2 Samuel 3:3.
A. A. Anderson, *2 Samuel* (WBC 11; Dallas: Word, 1989), pp. 56-60, on 2 Samuel 3:3.
A. Graeme Auld, *I & II Samuel* (OTL; Louisville: Westminster John Knox, 2011), pp. 377-381, on 2 Samuel 3:3.
Sara Japhet, *I and II Chronicles* (OTL; Louisville: Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 92-98, on 1 Chronicles 3:1.
Gary N. Knoppers, *1 Chronicles 1-9* (AB 12; New York: Doubleday, 2003), pp. 314-322, on 1 Chronicles 3:1.
Ronald F. Youngblood, *1, 2 Samuel* (Expositor''s Bible Commentary, rev. ed., vol. 3; Grand Rapids: Zondervan, 2009), pp. 318-321 [apologetic harmonization].
Carl Friedrich Keil and Franz Delitzsch, *Biblical Commentary on the Books of Samuel* (Edinburgh: T&T Clark, 1866), pp. 290-293 [apologetic harmonization].
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Grand Rapids: Zondervan, 1982), p. 182 [apologetic foil].', 0, NULL, 6, 'OT_internal', '2 Samuel, 1 Chronicles', 3, 2, 1, 2, 1, 'David''s second Hebron son is Chileab in 2 Sam 3:3 and Daniel in 1 Chr 3:1, with further LXX variants (e.g. Daluyah) in 2 Sam 3:3 reflecting scribal evolution of the name.', 'McCarter, II Samuel, AB 9, pp. 100-103; cf. Anderson, 2 Samuel, WBC 11, pp. 56-60.');

The apologetic and critical options are the same as before. The dual-name hypothesis (Archer, Keil-Delitzsch) holds that the son bore both names; the textual-corruption hypothesis (McCarter, Anderson, Japhet, Klein, Knoppers) holds that a single name was destabilized in transmission. The Greek tradition again confirms the instability: the LXX of 2 Samuel 3:3 carries forms such as Δαλουια (Daluia) rather than either Chileab or Daniel, marking this name as a known crux in the manuscript history.

What the full-list view adds is decisive for adjudicating between the two explanations: the agreement on five of six names, in identical order, with identical maternal attributions, demonstrates that these are not independent traditions but a single list transmitted through two channels. The Chronicler used Samuel (or a shared Vorlage) as his source and reproduced the Hebron list; the genealogist of 1 Chronicles 3 was a copyist of an existing roster, not an independent informant. This bears directly on the harmonization. If the lists were independent attestations, a dual name would be a natural way to explain why two genuine witnesses preserved different names. But because they are a single list reproduced twice, the lone divergence is far more plausibly a transmission event — either the Chronicler''s Samuel scroll already read a Daniel-form at the second slot, or a scribe in one line substituted or corrupted the name — than a record of an otherwise-unmentioned dual name. A genealogist faithfully copying five names verbatim and silently swapping the sixth for an alternate sobriquet, with no "also called" marker, is the less economical scenario.

The dual-name reading therefore requires an unattested cultural detail (that David''s second son went by two names) precisely where the manuscript record already furnishes divergent readings that explain the data. The textual-corruption reading requires only what the apparatus shows: a proper name that varied across copies. Names are the single most corruption-prone class of data in scribal transmission, and nothing theological depends on the outcome. Verdict: a single-name transmission variant within a shared list; the contradiction is real on the canonical surface but trivial in substance, and the dual-name harmonization, while possible, is undocumented and reads against the evident copying relationship between the two texts.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *II Samuel* (AB 9; Garden City: Doubleday, 1984), pp. 100-103, on 2 Samuel 3:2-5.
