Hans Wilhelm Hertzberg, *I and II Samuel* (OTL; Philadelphia: Westminster, 1964), pp. 257-260, on 2 Samuel 3:2-5.
Carl Friedrich Keil and Franz Delitzsch, *Biblical Commentary on the Books of Samuel* (Edinburgh: T&T Clark, 1866), pp. 290-293 [apologetic harmonization].
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Grand Rapids: Zondervan, 1982), p. 182 [apologetic foil].', 0, NULL, 6, 'OT_internal', '2 Samuel, 1 Chronicles', 3, 2, 1, 2, 1, 'The name of David''s second son varies between Chileab (MT 2 Sam 3:3) and Daniel (MT 1 Chr 3:1), with the LXX of 2 Samuel preserving still other forms (Daluyah/Dalouia), indicating an unstable name in early transmission.', 'McCarter, II Samuel, Anchor Bible 9, 100-103 (discussing LXX readings at 2 Sam 3:3)');

2 Samuel 11-12 is a tightly controlled court narrative organized around David''s sin and its consequences. Nathan delivers the parable of the ewe lamb and the oracle of judgment; the child of the adultery sickens and dies; David, having fasted and mourned, rises and comforts Bathsheba, "and went in unto her, and lay with her: and she bare a son, and he called his name Solomon" (2 Sam 12:24). The narrative architecture is theodicy: the LORD takes one child and, in a gesture of restored favor, gives back another whom "the LORD loved" and named Jedidiah (12:25). The sequence reads as immediate replacement, with no intervening births mentioned. The story is not a birth register; it never claims completeness, but its rhetorical effect plainly presents Solomon as the next child after the dead infant — the second by Bathsheba.

1 Chronicles 3:5 offers a flat genealogical enumeration: "these were born unto him in Jerusalem; Shimea, and Shobab, and Nathan, and Solomon, four, of Bathshua the daughter of Ammiel." The Chronicler''s list is deliberate and ordered; genealogy is his governing literary mode, and he closes the entry with the explicit total "four." Two textual details deserve note. First, the name appears as Bathshua (bat-shua) rather than Bathsheba (bat-sheva), and the father is Ammiel rather than Eliam (2 Sam 11:3, where the consonants are transposed: Eliam/Ammiel). These are recognized variant forms of the same persons, not different individuals. Second, the Chronicler entirely excises the Uriah-adultery episode; in his idealized portrait of David there is no narrative pressure to compress the birth order around a death, so the four sons stand in their natural sequence. The presence of a son named Nathan here is also striking, since it produces an heir-line through Nathan in Luke''s genealogy (Luke 3:31), independent of Solomon.

The standard harmonization observes, correctly, that 2 Samuel 12 nowhere states that Solomon was the second-born; it simply narrates his birth without listing siblings. On this reading Shimea, Shobab, and Nathan are born silently in the interval, and 12:24 records only the theologically significant birth. The steel-manned form of the argument is genuinely available: ancient narrative routinely reports selectively, and a birth notice is not a denial that other children existed. Keil and Delitzsch and most evangelical commentators take this line, and it cannot be refuted on strictly logical grounds.

What the harmonization costs, however, is the plain rhetorical force of the Samuel text. The comfort-and-conception scene is built to read as consolation immediately answered — the contiguity of mourning and birth is the point. To insert three additional sons between verse 23 and verse 24 is to read against the grain of a passage engineered for the opposite effect. The harmonization is logically possible but narratively unnatural; it rescues consistency at the price of the very pathos the author constructed.

The more economical historical-critical account is that the two writers worked from different traditions for different purposes. The Deuteronomistic court history (Samuel) reshaped its material around David''s transgression and divine forgiveness; the post-exilic Chronicler recalculated the Davidic line from genealogical sources, smoothing the moral record and listing the sons in sequence. Neither author was coordinating with the other, and neither was wrong on his own terms — the discrepancy is a byproduct of independent composition. The contradiction is real at the level of impression but technically harmonizable on the silence of Samuel.

Verdict: partially harmonizable — the count reconciles only by reading Samuel''s replacement narrative as a selective notice that silently passes over three intervening sons.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., II Samuel (Anchor Bible 9; Doubleday, 1984), pp. 296-304, on the Bathsheba-Solomon birth narrative and its theological framing at 2 Samuel 12:24-25
Sara Japhet, I & II Chronicles: A Commentary (Old Testament Library; Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 95-99, on the Chronicler''s genealogical lists and selective handling of the Bathsheba material at 1 Chronicles 3:5
Gary N. Knoppers, 1 Chronicles 1-9 (Anchor Bible 12; Doubleday, 2003), pp. 322-326, on the four sons of Bathshua and the differences from Samuel at 1 Chronicles 3:5
Ralph W. Klein, 1 Chronicles (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2006), pp. 117-119, on 1 Chronicles 3:5
Steven L. McKenzie, 1-2 Chronicles (Abingdon Old Testament Commentaries; Abingdon, 2004), pp. 70-75, on the Chronicler''s reshaping of Davidic genealogy', 0, NULL, 6, 'OT_internal', '2 Samuel, 1 Chronicles', 3, 2, 1, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The original-language data is the heart of the matter. The Hebrew of Samuel reads goren nakon; the consonants nkwn can be vocalized as a proper name (Nachon) or read as the niphal participle nakon, "prepared/established" — possibly a descriptive phrase rather than a name. Chronicles reads goren kidon; kidon elsewhere means "javelin" (as in Goliath''s weapon, 1 Sam 17:6, 45) and could carry overtones of "destruction." The versional witnesses confirm that the name was unstable in antiquity: the Septuagint of 2 Samuel 6:6 has Nachor/Nodab-type forms, and manuscripts of the Greek and the Syriac diverge among themselves. The simplest inference is that one or both names are corruptions of a single lost original, the consonants having shifted in transmission.

This fits the broader textual picture of Samuel. The Qumran scroll 4QSam-a, though damaged at this point, repeatedly shows that the Masoretic text of Samuel has suffered significant corruption and that Chronicles and the Old Greek frequently preserve readings closer to a different, sometimes older, Hebrew Vorlage. McCarter''s Anchor Bible volume catalogues dozens of such cases. On this view the Chronicler did not invent Chidon; he found a divergent name in his source or reproduced a variant tradition, and the two canonical texts now preserve two stages or branches of a damaged transmission.

The classic harmonization, advanced by Keil and Delitzsch in the nineteenth century, proposes that the threshing-floor bore two names — owned by a man named Nachon but also known as Chidon — or that one term is the owner''s personal name and the other a local designation. As a logical possibility this cannot be excluded: ancient sites did sometimes carry alternate names (compare Bethel/Luz, Jebus/Jerusalem). The steel-manned version of the argument notes that dual toponyms are well attested, so a single floor with two designations is not implausible in principle.

The weakness is evidentiary, not logical. Neither narrator gives any hint of a second name — no "which is also called," no apposition, no gloss of the kind the biblical writers regularly supply when they do equate two place-names. Samuel simply says Nachon; Chronicles simply says Chidon. The dual-name solution must therefore postulate a tradition that neither text preserves in order to reconcile a difference that, on the manuscript evidence, looks like ordinary scribal divergence in a notoriously unstable book. The harmonization is possible but unmotivated; the corruption hypothesis is both motivated and economical.

Nothing of theological weight rides on the location — no doctrine depends on whether the floor was Nachon''s or Chidon''s. But the question is precisely the kind of mundane factual detail on which two faithful witnesses to the same event would be expected to agree, and the Hebrew Bible''s two reports do not. The honest conclusion is that this is a name-variant generated in transmission rather than a coordinated record.

Verdict: textual corruption / divergent tradition — most plausibly a single original name garbled in the transmission of an unstable Samuel text, not a contradiction either author intended.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., II Samuel (Anchor Bible 9; Doubleday, 1984), pp. 161-170, on 2 Samuel 6 and the textual problems with the threshing-floor name
Sara Japhet, I & II Chronicles: A Commentary (Old Testament Library; Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 277-281, on 1 Chronicles 13:9
Ralph W. Klein, 1 Chronicles (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2006), pp. 327-332, on 1 Chronicles 13:9
