Steven L. McKenzie, The Chronicler''s Use of the Deuteronomistic History (Harvard Semitic Monographs 33; Scholars Press, 1985), pp. 41-55
C. F. Keil and F. Delitzsch, Biblical Commentary on the Books of Samuel, trans. James Martin (T&T Clark, 1866), pp. 332-336, for the nineteenth-century dual-name harmonization at 2 Samuel 6:6', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', '2 Samuel, 1 Chronicles', 4, 2, 1, 2, 1, 'The threshing-floor name differs (Nachon in MT 2 Sam 6:6 vs. Chidon in MT 1 Chr 13:9), with the LXX of 2 Samuel reading Nodab/Nodan, reflecting scribal instability of the name.', 'McCarter, II Samuel, Anchor Bible 9, 161-170 (LXX variants at 2 Sam 6:6)');

The textual evidence points clearly toward a resolution. The Septuagint of 2 Samuel 8:4 reads seven thousand, agreeing with Chronicles against the Masoretic Hebrew. The Qumran Samuel scroll 4QSam-a, though fragmentary here, belongs to a textual family that repeatedly aligns with the Old Greek and with Chronicles against the MT of Samuel, and McCarter reconstructs the original as seven thousand. The most economical mechanism is the loss of the Hebrew word or numeral for "thousand" (elep) in the transmission of Samuel — Hebrew numerals were susceptible to haplography and to the dropping of an abbreviating letter — so that an original "seven thousand" became "seven hundred" in the MT. On this account Chronicles and the LXX preserve the older number, and the MT of Samuel carries a copyist''s error.

Here the critical and the apologetic analyses largely converge. Gleason Archer and most evangelical commentators reach the same conclusion: the figure in MT Samuel is a scribal corruption, and the autographic number was seven thousand. A second, weaker harmonization sometimes offered is that the 700 represent an elite subset (officers, chariot-cavalry) of the larger 7,000; but neither text describes the 700 as a subdivision — each simply gives a single total with the same descriptor (parashim, horsemen) — so this reading imports a distinction the texts do not make. The subset proposal is therefore inferior to the straightforward textual explanation, which both camps accept.

What the agreement exposes is a methodological point rather than a doctrinal scandal. Resolving the discrepancy requires conceding that the received Hebrew text of 2 Samuel — the text printed in standard Bibles and treated as authoritative in many traditions — contains a transmission error that misstates a military figure by a factor of ten. For historical-critical scholarship this is unremarkable: ancient numerals are among the least stable elements of any manuscript tradition, and Samuel is a particularly corrupted book. For a doctrine of inerrancy the implication is more delicate, since the harmonization works only by locating inerrancy in lost autographs rather than in any extant text. The contradiction between the canonical books is real; its resolution is textual, and it is one that confessional and critical readers can share, even as they draw different conclusions from it.

Verdict: scribal corruption in MT Samuel — the original reading was almost certainly 7,000, preserved by Chronicles and the LXX; the tension resolves at the level of text criticism, not by harmonizing two intended numbers.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., II Samuel (Anchor Bible 9; Doubleday, 1984), pp. 243-251, on 2 Samuel 8:4 and the LXX/4QSam-a evidence for 7,000
Ralph W. Klein, 1 Chronicles (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2006), pp. 397-401, on 1 Chronicles 18:4
Gary N. Knoppers, 1 Chronicles 10-29 (Anchor Bible 12A; Doubleday, 2004), pp. 691-702, on the Hadadezer campaign
Steven L. McKenzie, 1-2 Chronicles (Abingdon Old Testament Commentaries; Abingdon, 2004), pp. 156-160
Anneli Aejmelaeus, "How to Reach the Old Greek in 1 Samuel and What to Do with It," in Congress Volume Helsinki 2010, ed. Martti Nissinen (VTSup 148; Brill, 2012), pp. 185-205, on the methodological problem of using the LXX to reconstruct MT Samuel corruptions
Gleason L. Archer, Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 188-189, for the standard evangelical scribal-corruption argument on 2 Samuel 8:4 // 1 Chronicles 18:4', 0, NULL, 7, 'OT_internal', '2 Samuel, 1 Chronicles', 1, 1, 2, 4, 1, 'MT 2 Sam 8:4 reads 700 horsemen while the LXX of 2 Samuel and MT 1 Chr 18:4 read 7,000, widely explained as a haplography/omission of the ''thousand'' marker in MT Samuel.', 'McCarter, II Samuel, Anchor Bible 9, 243-251 (LXX and 4QSam-a evidence at 2 Sam 8:4)');

The first discrepancy mirrors the 2 Samuel 8 // 1 Chronicles 18 horsemen problem and yields to the same analysis. A tenfold gap in a number is the signature of numeral corruption in transmission, and the loss or addition of the Hebrew word for "thousand" (elep) is the most economical mechanism. As with the earlier passage, the textual instability of Samuel — confirmed by the LXX and the 4QSam-a tradition repeatedly siding with Chronicles — makes it likely that one figure (most plausibly the higher, 7,000) reflects the original and the other is a copyist''s error. This is the point at which Archer and other evangelical writers concede a scribal corruption, and on the textual merits they are right to.

The second discrepancy is harder and cannot be dismissed as a numeral slip, because it turns on different Hebrew words, not different numerals. Samuel''s 40,000 are parashim, "horsemen," while Chronicles'' 40,000 are raglim, "footmen." These are lexically distinct terms that do not arise from the confusion of similar letters or the dropping of an abbreviation; the swap is at the level of the noun, not the figure. Three explanations are available: the Chronicler drew on a different source tradition; he deliberately recast the troop type to suit his portrait of Davidic warfare; or a word-level corruption entered one of the manuscripts. None of these is a trivial transcription accident.

The harmonization advanced by Keil and Delitzsch proposes that the 40,000 were a mixed force, with Samuel counting the cavalry component and Chronicles the infantry component of the same army. Stated at its strongest, this appeals to the genuine reality that ancient armies combined arms and that two reporters might foreground different elements. The difficulty is that neither text describes the 40,000 as mixed or as a partial count. Each gives one round number attached to one descriptor — 40,000 horsemen, or 40,000 footmen — with no qualifier suggesting that the other arm is being omitted. The harmonization works only by inserting a composition for the force that neither narrator supplies, converting two flat assertions into two partial reports. That is possible but unmotivated by anything in the text.

The critical reconstruction treats these as parallel battle reports from two post-exilic compositions drawing on overlapping but non-identical source traditions, compounded by the numeral instability endemic to MT Samuel. The 700/7,000 element is recoverable as textual corruption; the horsemen/footmen element is more likely a genuine divergence in tradition or deliberate editorial reshaping. As before, the textual resolution of the numeral, accepted across the critical-confessional divide, still requires admitting that one canonical text misreports, which complicates any claim of an inerrant received text.

Verdict: partly textual corruption (700 vs. 7,000), partly genuine divergence (horsemen vs. footmen) — the number harmonizes text-critically, but the troop-type discrepancy resists harmonization without importing data the texts do not provide.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., II Samuel (Anchor Bible 9; Doubleday, 1984), pp. 268-275, on 2 Samuel 10:18
Ralph W. Klein, 1 Chronicles (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2006), pp. 410-415, on 1 Chronicles 19:18
Gary N. Knoppers, 1 Chronicles 10-29 (Anchor Bible 12A; Doubleday, 2004), pp. 706-721, on the Aramean campaigns
Sara Japhet, I & II Chronicles: A Commentary (Old Testament Library; Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 360-364, on 1 Chronicles 19:18
Steven L. McKenzie, 1-2 Chronicles (Abingdon Old Testament Commentaries; Abingdon, 2004), pp. 161-165
Gleason L. Archer, Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 184-189, on the numeral discrepancies in the Aramean campaign reports
C. F. Keil and F. Delitzsch, Biblical Commentary on the Books of Samuel, trans. James Martin (T&T Clark, 1866), pp. 380-385, for the mixed-forces harmonization at 2 Samuel 10:18', 0, NULL, 7, 'OT_internal', '2 Samuel, 1 Chronicles', 1, 1, 2, 2, 1, 'The 700/7,000 chariot-men difference is explicable as a thousand-marker corruption in MT Samuel, but the horsemen-vs-footmen swap (parashim vs. raglim) is a word-level divergence not reducible to a simple numeric variant.', 'McCarter, II Samuel, Anchor Bible 9, 268-275 (2 Sam 10)');

The textual evidence strongly suggests the figure in 15:7 is corrupt. The Septuagint (in major witnesses), the Syriac Peshitta, the Vulgate, and several Hebrew manuscripts read "after four years" rather than "forty." Josephus, in Antiquities 7.196, likewise knows the interval as four years. The most probable mechanism is scribal assimilation: forty (arbaim) is a stock round number in Samuel and the surrounding narratives — David''s reign, the years of various judges and oppressions — and a copyist plausibly expanded an original "four" (arba) to the familiar "forty." Most critical commentators, including McCarter, adopt "four years" as the original reading, counted most naturally from some fixed point such as Absalom''s return to Jerusalem or his reconciliation with David, both narrated shortly before in 2 Samuel 14.

On this passage the apologetic and critical analyses again converge: the standard conservative treatment also takes the MT''s "forty" as a transmission error and "four" as the autographic reading. The two camps agree on the diagnosis and differ only on the inference. The critic regards the corruption as a normal, expected feature of an ancient hand-copied text and as evidence that Scripture is a human document subject to scribal accident. The confessional reader locates inerrancy in the autographs and treats the recoverable "four" as vindicating the original while conceding error in the received MT. Either way, the contradiction within the canonical Hebrew text is genuine and is resolved only by judging one reading mistaken.

A second harmonization tries to preserve the MT''s "forty" by counting it not from David''s accession but from his anointing by Samuel (1 Sam 16) or from some other earlier benchmark, so that forty years from the anointing might fall within his reign. The arithmetic, however, does not cohere cleanly with the ages and regnal totals the text supplies, and — decisively — nothing in 15:7 signals such an alternative epoch. The verse gives a bare "after forty years" with no antecedent to anchor the count to an anointing rather than to the reign just enumerated in chapter 5. The proposal rescues the number only by supplying a reckoning scheme the text never states, and it is generally regarded as forced even by those sympathetic to harmonization.

Verdict: scribal corruption in MT Samuel — the original almost certainly read "four years" (so LXX, Peshitta, Vulgate, Josephus); the internal contradiction is real in the received text and dissolves once the textual error is recognized.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., II Samuel (Anchor Bible 9; Doubleday, 1984), pp. 353-362, on 2 Samuel 15:7 and the ''four years'' / ''forty years'' textual variant
A. Graeme Auld, I & II Samuel: A Commentary (Old Testament Library; Westminster John Knox, 2011), pp. 503-508, on 2 Samuel 15:7
Antony F. Campbell, 2 Samuel (Forms of the Old Testament Literature 8; Eerdmans, 2005), pp. 137-145, on the Absalom rebellion narrative
David Toshio Tsumura, The Second Book of Samuel (New International Commentary on the Old Testament; Eerdmans, 2019), pp. 219-228, on the textual problem at 2 Samuel 15:7
Josephus, Antiquities of the Jews 7.196, Loeb Classical Library, trans. H. St. J. Thackeray and Ralph Marcus (Harvard University Press, 1934), preserving the ''four years'' tradition at 2 Samuel 15:7', 0, NULL, 8, 'OT_internal', '2 Samuel', 2, 2, 1, 4, 1, 'MT 2 Sam 15:7 reads ''forty years'' while the LXX, Syriac, and some Hebrew MSS read ''four years,'' the latter taken by most critics (and Josephus, Ant. 7.196) as original, resolving the internal chronology.', 'McCarter, II Samuel, Anchor Bible 9, 353-362 (four/forty variant at 2 Sam 15:7)');

The ethnic designation is the substantive problem. 2 Samuel 17:25 in the Masoretic Text calls Ithra a yisre''eli, an "Israelite," whereas 1 Chronicles 2:17 calls Jether a yishme''eli, an "Ishmaelite." The two words differ by a single consonant and a shift in vowel pattern (yiśrĕ''ēlî vs. yišmĕ''ēlî), which makes a copying error in one direction or the other mechanically plausible: the medial mem and resh, together with the surrounding letters, lie within easy reach of scribal confusion, and the loss or substitution of a single consonant is among the commonest classes of error in the Hebrew tradition. The decisive question is which reading is original.

The versional evidence points away from the Masoretic "Israelite." The Septuagint of 2 Samuel 17:25 does not read "Israelite" at all: the major witnesses read Iezraēlitēs, "Jezreelite" (yizrĕ''ē''lî), and the Lucianic and other strands reflect "Ishmaelite," agreeing with Chronicles. The Vulgate likewise has Iezrahelites. That the Greek and Latin traditions diverge from MT here, and that one significant strand converges on Chronicles, tells against the Masoretic reading being primary. McCarter and most critical commentators reconstruct the original as "Ishmaelite," matching 1 Chronicles 2:17, on two grounds: first, that calling a man simply "an Israelite" within an Israelite narrative is informationally empty—the designation does no work, whereas "Ishmaelite" identifies a meaningful ethnic origin; and second, that Chronicles, despite its later date, frequently preserves an older or superior reading of the Samuel material against the Masoretic Samuel text, a phenomenon dramatically confirmed by the Qumran Samuel scrolls (4QSam-a), which repeatedly side with the Chronicler and the Septuagint against MT.

The harmonizing options are real but weak. One proposal is that yitra''/yeter was an Ishmaelite by descent who had taken up residence among Israelites, so that one text records his birth-ethnicity and the other his adopted civic identity. This is not impossible in principle—the concept of the resident alien (gēr) is well established—but it is a story neither text tells. Neither narrator says "born an Ishmaelite, naturalized as an Israelite"; each gives a single, flat designation. A second proposal treats the two terms as describing different things, ethnicity versus geography, which only works if one emends to "Jezreelite," and that emendation is itself an admission that the MT as it stands is not original.

The most economical account is that the Samuel text suffered a one-consonant corruption from yišmĕ''ēlî to yiśrĕ''ēlî and that Chronicles preserves the older reading. This yields a genealogically interesting result: Amasa, who became commander of David''s (and earlier Absalom''s) army, was half-Ishmaelite, a non-Israelite at the apex of the Davidic military hierarchy. The verse also sits inside a knotty genealogical problem—2 Samuel 17:25 makes Amasa''s mother Abigail the daughter of Nahash and sister of Zeruiah, while 1 Chronicles 2:16 makes Abigail and Zeruiah daughters of Jesse—so the pericope is independently known to be textually disturbed. Verdict: harmonizable as variant name-forms, but the ethnic discrepancy is best explained as a scribal corruption in MT Samuel, with Chronicles preserving the older "Ishmaelite."', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., II Samuel (Anchor Bible 9; Doubleday, 1984), pp. 392-394, on 2 Sam 17:25 and the yisre''eli/yishme''eli variant.
Ralph W. Klein, 1 Chronicles (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2006), pp. 91-95, on 1 Chr 2:17.
Gary N. Knoppers, 1 Chronicles 1-9 (Anchor Bible 12; Doubleday, 2003), pp. 296-300, on 1 Chr 2:16-17.
Sara Japhet, I & II Chronicles (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 79-82, on 1 Chr 2:17.
Emanuel Tov, Textual Criticism of the Hebrew Bible (3rd ed.; Fortress, 2012), on the agreement of 4QSam-a, the Septuagint, and Chronicles against MT Samuel.', 0, NULL, 6, 'OT_internal', '2 Samuel, 1 Chronicles', 3, 2, 1, 4, 1, 'MT 2 Sam 17:25 reads ''Israelite'' (yisre''eli) while 1 Chr 2:17 reads ''Ishmaelite'' (yishme''eli), differing by a single letter; critics reconstruct ''Ishmaelite'' as original, treating Samuel''s reading as scribal corruption.', 'McCarter, II Samuel, Anchor Bible 9, 392-394 (yisre''eli/yishme''eli variant at 2 Sam 17:25)');

The traditional harmonization is that Absalom''s three sons predeceased him in childhood, so that by the time of the pillar he had in fact been left without a surviving heir. Childhood mortality in the ancient Levant was severe, and the rabbinic tradition explicitly supplied this explanation. The reading deserves a fair hearing: it is demographically realistic, and the anonymity of the three sons in 14:27—they are counted but not named—is sometimes read as a literary hint that they did not survive to carry a name forward. It is also worth correcting a common misattribution: Josephus, Antiquities 7.243, does not assert that the sons died. He records only Absalom''s motive for the monument, that "if his children should perish, his name would remain by that pillar"—a conditional anxiety about the future, not a notice of deaths already suffered. Josephus is therefore a witness to the tension, not a solution to it.

The difficulty with the "sons died young" reading is that no text says it. 2 Samuel 14:27 attaches no death notice to the births; the verb is a straightforward narrative perfect, "there were born" (wayyiwwālĕdû), reporting completed fact, not prophecy. 2 Samuel 18:18 does not say "after his sons had died"; it gives Absalom''s reasoning as a standing condition. The harmonization works only by importing an event—three childhood deaths—that neither narrator records, and by compressing it into the relatively short span of Absalom''s adult life, which ends in his revolt and death while still a young man. A second harmonizing move, that 14:27 anticipates sons yet to be born, founders on the same perfect-tense verb: the Hebrew reports a past event, not a projection.

The critical reading attends to the composite character of the material. Much of 2 Samuel 13-20 belongs to the so-called Succession Narrative (or Court History), itself widely held to incorporate older traditions that a later editor stitched together. 2 Samuel 18:18 is an etiological notice: it explains a real monument, "Absalom''s Monument" (yad ''Abšālōm), that the original audience could see standing in the King''s Valley, and the explanation is anchored in his childlessness. Such etiologies are typically older and weight-bearing, because they are tied to a tangible landmark the community already knew. The three-sons notice of 14:27, by contrast, is a free-floating piece of family data that connects to nothing else in the narrative—no son ever appears, acts, or is mourned—and reads like material drawn from a different genealogical source. The contradiction most plausibly reflects an editor''s failure to reconcile a family notice from one tradition with a monument etiology from another. Notably, the Septuagint and the Lucianic text show variation precisely around 14:27, and some scholars suspect the verse is a secondary insertion. Verdict: a genuine internal contradiction, best explained by the composite, multi-source character of the Absalom material rather than by an unrecorded death of three children.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., II Samuel (Anchor Bible 9; Doubleday, 1984), pp. 340-349, 405-408, on 2 Sam 14:27 and the 18:18 monument tradition.
A. Graeme Auld, I & II Samuel (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 2011), pp. 489-495, 536-540.
David Toshio Tsumura, The Second Book of Samuel (NICOT; Eerdmans, 2019), pp. 191-198, 250-258.
Antony F. Campbell, 2 Samuel (FOTL 8; Eerdmans, 2005), pp. 121-128, on source layers in the Absalom narrative.
Josephus, Jewish Antiquities 7.243 (Loeb Classical Library; trans. H. St. J. Thackeray and Ralph Marcus; Harvard University Press, 1934), on Absalom''s monument.', 0, NULL, 7, 'OT_internal', '2 Samuel', 1, 1, 1, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

Here the textual evidence largely vindicates a harmonization, but in a manner that reframes the problem rather than dissolving it. A significant body of witnesses reads not "Michal" but "Merab" at 2 Samuel 21:8: two Hebrew manuscripts, the Lucianic recension of the Septuagint, the Syriac Peshitta, and the relevant strand of the targumic tradition. Merab was Michal''s older sister, and 1 Samuel 18:19 states explicitly that "Merab, Saul''s daughter, was given to Adriel the Meholathite as a wife." The convergence is exact: the five sons of Adriel the Meholathite belong to Merab, who married Adriel, not to Michal, who was given to Paltiel and then reclaimed by David. The Masoretic "Michal" at 21:8 is therefore widely judged a scribal substitution—most economically a copyist''s slip from the more prominent sister to the less prominent one, the name "Michal" being far more salient in the David narratives.

This is one of the relatively rare cases in which the conservative explanation (a copying error; the text originally read Merab) and the critical conclusion coincide. McCarter, Klein, Tsumura, and Auld all reconstruct "Merab" as original, and the modern critical editions and translations follow suit—the NRSV reads "Merab" in the text, the NIV notes the variant, and the apparatus of BHS flags the corruption. The contradiction was generated, and is perpetuated, by translations that follow the Masoretic "Michal" without emendation, the King James Version chief among them; this is why the verse recurs in contradiction catalogues compiled from the KJV.

A secondary harmonization tries to keep the Masoretic "Michal" intact by appeal to levirate or surrogate practice: Michal is said to have raised, or to have borne to Adriel on Merab''s behalf, the five sons after Merab''s death, so that they are reckoned hers. The Talmud (b. Sanhedrin 19b) preserves a version of this, distinguishing the one who bears from the one who rears. The move is ingenious, but it is special pleading on two counts: it must reconcile 21:8 not only with 6:23 but with 1 Samuel 18:19''s plain statement that Merab, not Michal, married Adriel, and it has to manufacture an adoption scenario the text never mentions while still leaving 6:23''s emphatic "no child unto the day of her death" in tension.

The upshot is that the discrepancy is real at the level of the received Hebrew text and dissolves only once textual corruption is conceded. That is a thoroughly defensible scholarly position—Emanuel Tov cites the verse as a textbook instance of an MT error correctable from the versions—but it is not consistent with strong claims for the inerrant preservation of the Masoretic Hebrew, since the inerrant statement requires emending the text we actually possess. Verdict: harmonizable, but only by acknowledging a scribal corruption of Merab to Michal in MT 2 Samuel 21:8.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., II Samuel (Anchor Bible 9; Doubleday, 1984), pp. 439-443, on 2 Sam 21:8 and the Michal/Merab variant.
Ralph W. Klein, 1 Samuel (WBC 10, 2nd ed.; Thomas Nelson, 2000), pp. 187-190, on 1 Sam 18:19.
David Toshio Tsumura, The Second Book of Samuel (NICOT; Eerdmans, 2019), pp. 290-296.
A. Graeme Auld, I & II Samuel (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 2011), pp. 564-570.
Emanuel Tov, Textual Criticism of the Hebrew Bible (3rd ed.; Fortress, 2012), pp. 9-11, on 2 Sam 21:8 as a classic example of MT corruption.', 0, NULL, 7, 'OT_internal', '2 Samuel', 1, 1, 2, 4, 1, 'MT 2 Sam 21:8 reads ''Michal'' but a range of Hebrew MSS, the Lucianic Greek, and the Peshitta read ''Merab'' (cf. 1 Sam 18:19, Merab married to Adriel), widely regarded as the original; Tov cites this as a classic MT corruption.', 'Tov, Textual Criticism of the Hebrew Bible, 3rd ed., 9-11; McCarter, II Samuel, AB 9, 439-443');

This collides head-on with 1 Samuel 17, where the youth David fells Goliath in the celebrated single combat in the Valley of Elah. Two different slayers, one named victim, both narratives offered as historical report. The verse sits within a short catalogue (2 Samuel 21:15-22) of four Philistine champions, descendants of "the giant" (hā-rāpâ) of Gath, each killed by one of David''s warriors—Ishbi-benob, Saph, Goliath, and an unnamed six-fingered giant. The list reads as an old, independent tradition about the heroic deeds of David''s men, and within it the killing of Goliath is simply one entry among several, attributed to a comparatively obscure Bethlehemite named Elhanan. The most economical historical reconstruction, defended by McCarter and many critical scholars, runs in this direction: the older tradition credited the Goliath kill to Elhanan, and the famous David-and-Goliath narrative of 1 Samuel 17 is a later, theologically charged literary development that transferred the feat to the rising king. The presence of a textually expanded and unstable 1 Samuel 17 (the Septuagint of that chapter is dramatically shorter than the Masoretic Text) reinforces the impression of a story that grew.

The Chronicler, with both Samuel narratives before him, evidently noticed the clash and resolved it. 1 Chronicles 20:5 reads: "Elhanan son of Jair struck down Lahmi the brother of Goliath the Gittite, the shaft of whose spear was like a weaver''s beam." A previously unattested brother named Lahmi is introduced to absorb Elhanan''s kill, leaving David the sole slayer of Goliath. The mechanics of the change are visible in the consonants. The phrase in 2 Samuel 21:19 that the MT reads as bêt hallaḥmî, "the Bethlehemite," appears in Chronicles reanalyzed: the element laḥmî has been detached and treated as a personal name, "Lahmi," and the syntax rearranged with ''aḥî ("brother of") supplied. In other words, the same string of consonants that designated Elhanan''s hometown in Samuel was reread in Chronicles as the name of Goliath''s brother. Whether the Chronicler made this move himself or inherited a tradition that had already done so, the result is a harmonizing rewrite, and it is the source from which the KJV''s italicized "the brother of" in 2 Samuel 21:19 was retrojected.

A further harmonizing proposal posits two separate giants both called Goliath, but this multiplies entities to save the appearances and ignores the shared "weaver''s beam" formula that ties 2 Samuel 21:19 to 1 Samuel 17:7. The critical consensus—McCarter, Klein, Knoppers, Tsumura, Auld, Japhet—is that 2 Samuel 21:19 preserves the older, embarrassing tradition, and that both the Chronicler''s "Lahmi the brother" and the KJV''s bracketed insertion are successive editorial rescues of David''s exclusive claim to the kill. Verdict: a genuine contradiction in the underlying tradition, with the harmonization built into the canonical text of Chronicles and the KJV by later hands.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., II Samuel (Anchor Bible 9; Doubleday, 1984), pp. 449-452, on 2 Sam 21:19 and the Elhanan-Goliath problem.
Ralph W. Klein, 1 Chronicles (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2006), pp. 408-410, on the Chronicler''s harmonization in 1 Chr 20:5.
Gary N. Knoppers, 1 Chronicles 10-29 (Anchor Bible 12A; Doubleday, 2004), pp. 731-735, on 1 Chr 20:5.
David Toshio Tsumura, The Second Book of Samuel (NICOT; Eerdmans, 2019), pp. 298-302.
A. Graeme Auld, I & II Samuel (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 2011), pp. 570-575.
Sara Japhet, I & II Chronicles (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 369-372, on the Chronicler''s apologetic move.', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', '1 Samuel, 2 Samuel, 1 Chronicles', 7, 1, 2, 1, 1, 'MT 2 Sam 21:19 has Elhanan kill ''Goliath the Gittite'' (no ''brother of''), while 1 Chr 20:5 reads Elhanan killed ''Lahmi the brother of Goliath,'' widely seen as a Chronistic/scribal harmonization derived from misreading beth-hallahmi (''the Bethlehemite'').', 'McCarter, II Samuel, Anchor Bible 9, 449-452 (2 Sam 21:19)');

The standard harmonization addresses the psalm by appeal to genre and occasion. 2 Samuel 22 is liturgical poetry, not a moral ledger, and its superscription and content tie it to David''s deliverance "from the hand of all his enemies and from the hand of Saul." On this reading the "righteousness" and "clean hands" are forensic and relative: David is innocent in the specific dispute with his persecutors, especially Saul, having repeatedly spared Saul''s life and refused to seize the throne by violence. There is genuine merit here. The covenant-lawsuit idiom of clean hands typically concerns a particular cause, and the psalm''s setting is vindication against enemies, not a comprehensive self-assessment. Where it strains is in the unqualified diction: "according to my righteousness... the cleanness of my hands... I was blameless... I kept myself from guilt." The poem supplies no "in this matter" or "compared with Saul"; the limiting clause is supplied by the harmonizer, not the text. The most one can say is that genre softens the force of the claim without erasing its universality.

1 Kings 15:5 is harder to absorb, because it is prose and explicit. The Deuteronomistic Historian constructs David as the benchmark against whom every later Judahite king is measured, and he permits exactly one blemish on that benchmark: the Uriah affair. He does not write "save in the matter of Uriah and the census." That the census is omitted suggests either that this strand of the tradition did not reckon the census a comparable sin, or that the historian''s source did not include it, or that the formula is deliberately schematic, isolating the one transgression with dynastic consequences (the sword that would not depart from David''s house, 2 Sam 12:10). There is also a genuine text-critical wrinkle: the limiting clause "save only in the matter of Uriah the Hittite" is absent from part of the Greek tradition (it is lacking in Codex Vaticanus and some other LXX witnesses of 3 Reigns 15:5), which is why a number of critics treat it as a secondary expansion rather than original. That cuts against the harmonizer, not for him—on the shorter text the verse is an unqualified commendation of David with no Uriah exception at all, which only sharpens the clash with the psalm and the census, rather than a tidy single-exception formula.

The census narratives compound the problem in their own right. 2 Samuel 24:1 reports that the LORD''s anger was kindled against Israel and "he incited David" to number them—the grammatical subject of the inciting is famously ambiguous (the LORD himself, or an unnamed instigator), which is part of why 1 Chronicles 21:1 recasts the inciter as "Satan" (or "an adversary," śāṭān), a theological revision in its own right. Either way both texts agree David sinned in doing it. So the very act 2 Samuel 22 would seem to exclude is confessed two chapters later in the same book.

The critical reading attributes the divergence to different hands and agendas: the psalm is inherited liturgy idealizing the king; 1 Kings 15:5 is deuteronomistic ideology fashioning a royal standard; the census account belongs to an appendix (2 Sam 21-24) of separate traditions. No editor coordinated them. (The catalogue''s stray reference "2 Samuel:22:21" with the extra colon is merely a scrape artifact; the verse and its wording are as cited.) Verdict: a genuine tension that genre eases but does not dissolve, reflecting independent sources with incompatible evaluations of David.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., II Samuel (Anchor Bible 9; Doubleday, 1984), pp. 461-475, on 2 Sam 22 and the David psalm.
Mordechai Cogan, 1 Kings (Anchor Bible 10; Doubleday, 2001), pp. 393-397, on 1 Kgs 15:5 and the Deuteronomist''s qualified evaluation.
Marvin A. Sweeney, I & II Kings (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 2007), pp. 188-192.
Ralph W. Klein, 1 Chronicles (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2006), pp. 419-435, on the census narrative.
David Toshio Tsumura, The Second Book of Samuel (NICOT; Eerdmans, 2019), pp. 310-330.
Antony F. Campbell, 2 Samuel (FOTL 8; Eerdmans, 2005), pp. 195-210.', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', '2 Samuel, 1 Kings, 1 Chronicles', 7, 2, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The strongest harmonization, advanced by traditional commentators and defensible on its own terms, turns on the semantics of the superscription. The phrase divre david ha-aharonim need not mean "the chronologically last words David ever spoke." Divre can denote "acts," "matters," or "sayings," and ha-aharonim, "the later/latter," frequently functions as a literary marker distinguishing one collection from an earlier one (compare "the former" and "the latter" acts of a king in the Chronicler''s source notices, e.g., 2 Chronicles 9:29; 16:11). On this reading 2 Samuel 23:1 introduces David''s last *poetic* or *inspired* utterance — a closing literary set-piece — not a deathbed scene at all, while 1 Kings 2 supplies the historical-narrative deathbed. The two are then different speech-events at different registers, and there is no strict chronological collision. This much is sound, and a critical reader should grant it: 2 Samuel 23 is patently a literary unit of inspired verse, not a bedside report.

What the chronological resolution does not dissolve is the deeper portraiture problem the canon leaves unsettled. The dissonance is not merely about which sentence came last; it is about which David the tradition wished to enshrine as the summation of his reign. The compiler responsible for 2 Samuel 21-24 — the so-called Samuel "appendix," a deliberately arranged anthology of narrative, list, and poem framing the David story at its close — placed an inspired meditation on righteous kingship as David''s crowning word. The Deuteronomistic Historian who shaped 1 Kings 1-2 (the "Succession Narrative" or its redactional continuation) preserved a starkly different finale: David securing Solomon''s throne by directing posthumous executions. McCarter treats 2 Samuel 23:1-7 as a late insertion into the Samuel scroll, an editorial addition designed to give David a final prophetic word; Cogan and Sweeney read 1 Kings 2 as the older, harder-edged deathbed tradition carrying the Deuteronomist''s interest in legitimating Solomonic succession. Auld similarly locates the appendix material as a secondary framing layer.

So the tension resolves at the level of strict chronology and persists at the level of characterization. The harmonization is correct as far as it goes — 2 Samuel 23 is "last words" in the sense of last inspired poem, 1 Kings 2 is the deathbed proper — but it purchases that reconciliation by conceding that the two passages are independent editorial creations with incompatible theological agendas about the same man. One tradition remembers the psalmist whose Spirit-borne finale celebrates the king who rules justly; the other remembers the political operator whose last instruction is a kill order. The canon preserves both side by side and declines to choose, which is itself the historically interesting datum: the editors valued each portrait enough to keep it. Verdict: harmonizable as to chronology (two distinct speech-events, one poetic and one narrative), but the characterizational tension is real and reflects two separately redacted traditions about David''s end.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *II Samuel* (Anchor Bible 9; Doubleday, 1984), pp. 477-486, on 2 Sam 23:1-7 as a late insertion.
Mordechai Cogan, *1 Kings* (Anchor Bible 10; Doubleday, 2001), pp. 169-180, on the deathbed scene and the Joab/Shimei material.
Marvin A. Sweeney, *I & II Kings* (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 2007), pp. 62-70, on the literary character of 1 Kgs 2 and its Deuteronomistic shaping.
A. Graeme Auld, *I & II Samuel* (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 2011), pp. 591-600, on the Samuel appendix framing.
David Toshio Tsumura, *The Second Book of Samuel* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 2019), pp. 330-345, on 2 Sam 23:1-7.', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', '2 Samuel, 1 Kings', 7, 2, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The near-universal explanation, shared by critical and conservative commentators alike, is scribal loss: one name fell out during transmission, so the original list of thirty-seven was reduced to thirty-six while the summary total was preserved intact. The internal evidence for textual damage in this chapter is unusually strong. Several names appear in corrupted or doublet forms — the leader of the three is "Josheb-basshebeth the Tahchemonite" in the MT (23:8), an almost certainly garbled reading that the parallel in 1 Chronicles 11:11 gives as "Jashobeam," and the MT''s "Adino the Eznite" looks like a misvocalized fragment of a verb phrase ("he wielded his spear"). Place-name gentilics are likewise unstable. McCarter''s Anchor Bible treatment reconstructs the likely point of loss and emends accordingly, and the consonantal text shows precisely the kind of haplography and homoioteleuton that drops a short name between two similar endings.

The Chronicler''s parallel (1 Chronicles 11:10-47) complicates any simple count: that list is considerably longer, appending sixteen additional warriors with a markedly northern and Transjordanian flavor (verses 41b-47) that have no counterpart in Samuel. But the Chronicler''s expansion is a separate compositional matter; it does not generate the Samuel arithmetic problem, which is wholly contained in 2 Samuel 23. Na''aman has argued that the list reflects historical layering — an original roster of David''s officers (the shalishim) that grew by accretion — which makes a stray name''s loss or gain entirely ordinary.

The critical point is what the harmonization concedes. To rescue the summary number, one must posit that the received Hebrew text is corrupt — that a name once stood in the list and has dropped out. That is almost certainly the correct diagnosis, and it is endorsed by McCarter, Tsumura, and Auld. But it is not a defense of the text''s inerrancy as received; it is an admission that the canonical MT of 2 Samuel 23 contains a counting error visible to anyone who tallies the names. The harmonizer and the textual critic agree on the mechanism (a lost name) and differ only on the conclusion they draw from it. This is a useful corrective to the assumption that all contradiction claims hinge on theological mismatch; some, like this one, hinge on the plain failure of a list to match the total the same verse supplies, and the resolution itself confirms that the numbers in the transmitted text are not perfectly preserved. Verdict: harmonizable as to original intent via scribal loss of one name, but the received Masoretic text is internally inconsistent — a transmission error, not a reliable count.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *II Samuel* (Anchor Bible 9; Doubleday, 1984), pp. 488-498, on the list of mighty men in 2 Sam 23:8-39 and its textual problems.
Ralph W. Klein, *1 Chronicles* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2006), pp. 296-320, on the parallel in 1 Chr 11:10-47.
Gary N. Knoppers, *1 Chronicles 10-29* (Anchor Bible 12A; Doubleday, 2004), pp. 539-560.
David Toshio Tsumura, *The Second Book of Samuel* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 2019), pp. 346-372.
A. Graeme Auld, *I & II Samuel* (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 2011), pp. 605-625.
Nadav Na''aman, "The List of David''s Officers (šālîšîm)," *Vetus Testamentum* 38 (1988), pp. 71-79, on the historical layering of the list.', 0, NULL, 7, 'OT_internal', '2 Samuel', 1, 1, 1, 4, 1, 'The named list in MT 2 Sam 23:8-39 tallies to 36 against the stated total of 37, attributed to loss of a name in transmission (the MT list shows multiple corrupted names).', 'McCarter, II Samuel, Anchor Bible 9, 488-498 (2 Sam 23:8-39)');

The classic harmonization posits differing bases of enumeration. Keil and Delitzsch propose that Samuel and Chronicles count different categories: perhaps Chronicles'' larger Israel figure (1,100,000) folds in standing army units that Samuel''s 800,000 excludes, while Samuel''s Judah figure (500,000) includes contingents — the standing 288,000 of the divisional system, plus tribal officers — that Chronicles (470,000) leaves out. The Chronicler himself flags an irregularity: 1 Chronicles 21:6 notes that Joab "did not count Levi and Benjamin," and 27:24 adds that the census was never completed and never entered "in the chronicles of King David." An apologetic reading leans on these notices: the two texts captured the count at different stages or under different inclusion rules, so the divergent totals reflect real procedural differences rather than error.

The difficulty is that every such reconstruction supplies a counting distinction the texts do not articulate for these specific figures. Neither verse says "this total excludes the standing army" or "this includes the reserve." The 21:6 note about Levi and Benjamin would, if anything, make Chronicles'' numbers *lower*, not explain why its Israel figure is 300,000 *higher*. The harmonizations are unfalsifiable in form: any pair of numbers can be reconciled by inventing a category that bridges the gap.

The historically decisive observation is that neither figure can be literal. Finkelstein and Silberman, drawing on settlement-pattern survey, estimate the total population of Iron Age Israel and Judah combined in the low hundreds of thousands — men, women, and children together. A muster of 1.3 million (Samuel) or 1.57 million (Chronicles) fighting men is demographically impossible for the tenth century BCE and would imply a total population many times larger than the archaeology permits. The numbers are therefore best read as literary-theological constructions: round, hyperbolic figures expressing the magnitude of David''s realm rather than census data. The Chronicler, working from a text close to Samuel as his source, recast the figures to suit his own emphases — his composition repeatedly magnifies the scale of David''s preparations, especially around the Temple and the military divisions of 1 Chronicles 27. Japhet, Klein, and Knoppers read the divergences as redactional adjustment.

That critical insight explains the discrepancy without removing it. Recognizing both numbers as non-historical does not make them agree; each text presents its count as the straightforward result of Joab''s tour, and the counts differ. The contradiction stands at the level of the narrative claim — two reports of one event give two answers — even though, at the level of historical reference, neither answer is true. Verdict: genuine contradiction between the two reports, best explained as Chronistic re-rendering of inflated, non-literal figures rather than as two compatible counting schemes.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *II Samuel* (Anchor Bible 9; Doubleday, 1984), pp. 502-516, on the census in 2 Sam 24.
Ralph W. Klein, *1 Chronicles* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2006), pp. 419-435, on 1 Chr 21.
Gary N. Knoppers, *1 Chronicles 10-29* (Anchor Bible 12A; Doubleday, 2004), pp. 743-766.
Sara Japhet, *I & II Chronicles* (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 372-381.
Israel Finkelstein and Neil Asher Silberman, *The Bible Unearthed* (Free Press, 2001), pp. 142-145, on actual Iron Age demographic capacity.
C. F. Keil and F. Delitzsch, *Biblical Commentary on the Books of Samuel*, trans. James Martin (T&T Clark, 1866), pp. 502-510, for the counting-distinction harmonization at 2 Sam 24:9.', 0, NULL, 7, 'OT_internal', '2 Samuel, 1 Chronicles', 1, 1, 3, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The text-critical evidence weighs heavily toward "three" as original. The Old Greek (Septuagint) of 2 Samuel 24:13 reads three years, not seven, aligning with MT 1 Chronicles 21:12 against the MT of Samuel. The reading "three" also yields a tidy rhetorical symmetry that the alternatives confirm: three years of famine, three months of flight, three days of pestilence — a three/three/three triad characteristic of patterned prophetic ultimatums. Most critical commentators (McCarter, Tsumura) therefore reconstruct "three" as the original reading and judge MT Samuel''s "seven" a secondary corruption. The likeliest mechanism is assimilation: a scribe''s eye or memory was drawn to the seven-year famine motif elsewhere in the tradition — most prominently the seven-year famine of Joseph in Genesis 41, but also the seven-year famine Elisha announces in 2 Kings 8:1 — and "seven" displaced "three."

The harmonizing alternative tries to keep both numbers literal by connecting the famine here to the three-year famine of 2 Samuel 21:1 (the Gibeonite episode). The reconstruction runs: three years of famine had already occurred, a year of recovery or sowing followed, and Gad now offers three more, totaling seven, so MT Samuel''s "seven" is a cumulative figure while Chronicles counts only the prospective three. The proposal is ingenious but strained. It links two famine narratives separated by several chapters and by no narrative cross-reference; it inserts an unmentioned recovery year purely to make the arithmetic close; and it requires a reader to perform a calculation the text never invites. Crucially, it also runs against the manuscript evidence — the LXX of the very verse in question reads three, which is hard to explain if the author meant a cumulative seven.

The simpler and better-attested account is straightforward: the original read "three" (preserved in LXX Samuel and MT Chronicles), and MT Samuel suffered a numerical corruption to "seven." This is one of the cleanest examples in the Samuel-Chronicles synopsis where the discrepancy is demonstrably a transmission error in one witness rather than a substantive disagreement between two authors, because an external versional witness (the Greek) preserves the uncorrupted figure. As with the mighty-men count, the apologist and the critic largely converge on the mechanism — a defective reading in one text — and diverge only on the implication for the reliability of the received Masoretic Text. Verdict: scribal corruption in MT 2 Samuel 24:13; the original "three" is preserved by the LXX of Samuel and by Chronicles, so the contradiction is between witnesses, not authors.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *II Samuel* (Anchor Bible 9; Doubleday, 1984), pp. 504-512, on 2 Sam 24:13 and the seven/three textual variant.
Ralph W. Klein, *1 Chronicles* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2006), pp. 422-430, on 1 Chr 21:11-12.
Gary N. Knoppers, *1 Chronicles 10-29* (Anchor Bible 12A; Doubleday, 2004), pp. 750-755.
Sara Japhet, *I & II Chronicles* (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 375-378.
David Toshio Tsumura, *The Second Book of Samuel* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 2019), pp. 376-385.', 0, NULL, 7, 'OT_internal', '2 Samuel, 1 Chronicles', 1, 1, 2, 4, 1, 'MT 2 Sam 24:13 reads ''seven years'' of famine while the LXX of 2 Samuel and MT 1 Chr 21:11-12 read ''three years,'' with ''three'' taken as original (matching the three-three structure of the options).', 'McCarter, II Samuel, Anchor Bible 9, 504-512 (seven/three variant at 2 Sam 24:13)');

The most carefully constructed harmonization distinguishes the objects of purchase. Keil and Delitzsch, followed by Gleason Archer and a line of evangelical commentators, argue that 2 Samuel describes the purchase of only the immediate threshing floor and the oxen for fifty silver shekels, while 1 Chronicles describes a separate, larger transaction — David buying "the place" (Hebrew ha-maqom), understood as the entire future Temple precinct on Mount Moriah — for six hundred gold shekels. On this reading there are two purchases: a small one for the equipment and floor needed for the immediate sacrifice, and a comprehensive one for the whole sacred site. Archer adds that the Chronicler, writing with the completed Temple in view, naturally records the grander acquisition. Stated at full strength, the argument has internal logic: ha-maqom does sometimes carry the technical sense of a sanctuary site, and the Chronicler does foreground Moriah as the Temple location (2 Chronicles 3:1).

The weakness is that neither text presents two transactions. Both narrate a single, continuous purchase culminating immediately in sacrifice on the spot: in 2 Samuel David buys, builds the altar, and offers burnt offerings in the same breath (24:24-25); in 1 Chronicles the structure is identical (21:25-26). Neither account hints that the small price covered the gear while a separate large price covered a wider plot, and the "two transactions" are nowhere distinguished within either narrative — the distinction must be imported from outside to make the figures cohere. The harmonization manufactures a second purchase the text does not describe.

The better explanation is Chronistic enhancement. Writing in the post-exilic period, when the Jerusalem Temple was the center of Judahite identity, the Chronicler had strong theological motive to magnify the foundational transaction: fifty shekels of silver is the price a smallholder pays for equipment, while six hundred shekels of gold befits the king founding the site of the holy of holies. The shift from silver to gold and the tenfold-plus increase in quantity align with the Chronicler''s broader pattern of elevating David''s role in Temple preparation (compare the vast quantities David is said to amass for the Temple in 1 Chronicles 22 and 29). Japhet, Klein, Knoppers, and McKenzie read the figure as a deliberate Chronistic upgrade rather than independent data about a larger plot. Some have also noted that "six hundred" may carry symbolic resonance (a round, complete figure spread across all the tribes), reinforcing its literary rather than ledger character.

Either way the two reports cannot both be the literal price of one purchase. The older tradition (50 silver in Samuel) and the theologically heightened tradition (600 gold in Chronicles) describe the same event with incompatible numbers. Verdict: genuine contradiction, best explained as the Chronicler''s intentional enhancement of the Temple-site purchase, not as two distinct transactions.', 'P. Kyle McCarter Jr., *II Samuel* (Anchor Bible 9; Doubleday, 1984), pp. 510-516, on 2 Sam 24:24.
Sara Japhet, *I & II Chronicles* (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 380-385, on the Chronicler''s enhancement in 1 Chr 21:25.
Ralph W. Klein, *1 Chronicles* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2006), pp. 430-435, on the Chronistic redaction.
Gary N. Knoppers, *1 Chronicles 10-29* (Anchor Bible 12A; Doubleday, 2004), pp. 759-766, on the price discrepancy as theological enhancement.
Steven L. McKenzie, *1-2 Chronicles* (AOTC; Abingdon, 2004), pp. 175-180.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 187-188, for the standard two-transactions harmonization.
C. F. Keil and F. Delitzsch, *Biblical Commentary on the Books of Samuel*, trans. James Martin (T&T Clark, 1866), pp. 511-515, for the two-parcels harmonization at 2 Sam 24:24.', 0, NULL, 7, 'OT_internal', '2 Samuel, 1 Chronicles', 1, 1, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard harmonization is the argument from silence: Kings does not assert that Solomon was alone; it simply concentrates the narrative lens on the king. This is defensible and should be granted its full weight. 1 Kings 3 is built entirely around Solomon''s individual encounter with YHWH in the dream at Gibeon (1 Kgs 3:5-15), and the surrounding Deuteronomistic narrative streamlines accordingly, mentioning only the protagonist. A royal procession to a major sanctuary with a thousand victims would in any case have required substantial personnel — priests, attendants, herdsmen — so the presence of others is not historically improbable. At the level of strict logic, then, the texts are not mutually exclusive: one omits what the other includes.

The divergence is nonetheless substantive, and it is characteristically Chronistic. The Chronicler''s term for the gathered body is qahal (rendered "congregation"/"assembly"), the same liturgical-political collective he deploys at David''s transfer of the ark, Solomon''s accession, and Hezekiah''s Passover. Japhet and Klein both observe that the Chronicler systematically transforms private royal initiatives into corporate acts of "all Israel," a feature so consistent that it functions as a redactional signature rather than as independent historical memory. The Gibeon scene is a textbook instance: where the Deuteronomistic Historian wrote of the king alone, the Chronicler supplies "all the assembly with him," along with "the commanders of thousands and of hundreds, the judges, and all the leaders" (2 Chr 1:2). The added detail of the bronze altar of Bezalel and the tent of meeting at Gibeon (2 Chr 1:5) is likewise a Chronistic supplement designed to legitimate worship at a high place by anchoring it to authorized Mosaic furniture.

That legitimating impulse exposes the deeper issue. Both writers are mildly embarrassed that Solomon sacrifices at a bamah, since Deuteronomistic theology condemns the high places (cf. 1 Kgs 3:2-3, which explicitly excuses the practice on the grounds that "no house had yet been built for the name of the LORD"). The Chronicler, writing later and committed to cultic centralization, faces an even sharper problem and resolves it by making Gibeon a fully constituted national sanctuary visited by the entire community under proper auspices. The congregational framing is therefore not incidental color but theological apologetic.

Textually the variants are minor; the Greek of 3 Kingdoms does not import the assembly into the Kings account, so the difference is genuinely compositional rather than scribal. The result is a partial harmonization. If the question is whether the texts are logically contradictory, they are not — silence in Kings can accommodate a crowd. If the question is whether they present the same event in the same way, they plainly do not: 1 Kings 3 knows nothing of the congregation, while 2 Chronicles 1 makes the congregation central. The contradiction the Skeptic''s Annotated Bible poses sits on that second axis, and on that axis it reflects a real difference in how two authors reconstructed Solomon''s inaugural sacrifice. Verdict: overstated as a strict contradiction, but a genuine and theologically motivated Chronistic reframing.', 'Mordechai Cogan, *1 Kings* (Anchor Bible 10; Doubleday, 2001), pp. 184-193, on 1 Kgs 3:4 and the high-place problem.
Marvin A. Sweeney, *I & II Kings* (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 2007), pp. 75-82, on the Gibeon dream narrative.
Sara Japhet, *I & II Chronicles* (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 521-528, on the Chronicler''s congregational reframing in 2 Chr 1.
Ralph W. Klein, *2 Chronicles* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2012), pp. 18-26, on the corporate all-Israel framing.
Gary N. Knoppers, "Prayer and Propaganda: The Dedication of Solomon''s Temple and the Deuteronomist''s Program," *Catholic Biblical Quarterly* 57 (1995), pp. 229-254, on the Deuteronomistic shaping of Solomon''s cult activity.', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', '1 Kings, 2 Chronicles', 7, 2, 1, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

Matthew 3:11 places John the Baptist comparing himself to the coming one: "he who comes after me is mightier (ischyroteros) than I." The axis here is power and eschatological authority, not wisdom or covenant status, and again no contradiction arises with the Solomon claim.

The genuinely difficult material is the saying preserved in Matthew 11:11 and Luke 7:28, almost certainly drawn from Q. It runs in two clauses: "among those born of women none greater (meizon) than John the Baptist has arisen; yet the least (ho mikroteros) in the kingdom of heaven is greater (meizon) than he." The same comparative adjective, meizon, governs both halves, with no signaled shift in meaning. The standard salvation-historical harmonization — found in Davies and Allison, Luz, and Nolland — reads the verse as placing John at the summit of the old order, the last and greatest of the prophets pointing to the kingdom, while the kingdom itself constitutes a new order on a different scale, so that even its least participant outranks the greatest figure of the age now passing. This is good theology and almost certainly the saying''s intended sense. What it does not do is make "greater" univocal: the verse achieves its rhetorical force precisely by using the same word across two incommensurable scales, which is to say it trades on a deliberate paradox rather than dissolving one. The tension is internal to the logion and is, on the critical reading, intentional.

Matthew 18:1-4 supplies yet a third framework. To the disciples'' question "Who then is greatest (meizon) in the kingdom of heaven?" Jesus answers that one must become like a child, and "whoever humbles himself like this child is the greatest." Here greatness is not a fixed rank assigned to a named individual but a disposition — humility — that any member of the community may embody. This reframes the entire category: the kingdom inverts ordinary status hierarchies, so that asking "who is the greatest?" is itself the wrong question, the very ambition the saying rebukes (cf. Matt 20:26-27; Mark 9:35).

The honest verdict is layered. The Solomon-versus-Jesus-versus-John material is not a real contradiction; it conflates wisdom, might, covenant status, and kingdom rank, which are different measures. That portion is overstated. The residue that does not cleanly dissolve is the single Q saying that both crowns John as greatest of the born-of-women and subordinates him to the kingdom''s least — a calculated paradox built into the logion, not a copyist''s error or a clash between independent sources. Verdict: largely overstated through equivocation on "great," with one genuine intra-saying paradox that resists tidy harmonization.', 'Mordechai Cogan, *1 Kings* (Anchor Bible 10; Doubleday, 2001), pp. 184-200, on the wisdom theme in 1 Kgs 3.
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison Jr., *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 2 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1991), pp. 247-255, on Matt 11:11 and the Baptist sayings.
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 8-20* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2001), pp. 137-148, on the salvation-historical reading of Matt 11:11.
John Nolland, *The Gospel of Matthew* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 2005), pp. 456-463, on the two clauses of Matt 11:11.
Joel B. Green, *The Gospel of Luke* (NICNT; Eerdmans, 1997), pp. 296-302, on Luke 7:28 and the Q form of the saying.
