The leading harmonization, defended by R. T. France and Craig Blomberg, observes that Matthew 23:8-10 is a tightly structured triad of religious honorifics — "rabbi" (v. 8), "father" (v. 9), and "instructor/teacher" (kathēgētēs, v. 10) — directed against the status-seeking of the scribes and Pharisees (23:5-7) who "love the place of honor" and being called "rabbi." The Aramaic abba and Hebrew av were indeed used as honorifics for revered teachers and sages; rabbinic literature preserves the title Abba applied to certain authorities, and the patriarchs of the schools were spoken of in quasi-paternal terms. On this reading, "father" in v. 9 belongs to the semantic field of religious authority, not biological parentage. France, in the NICNT, argues the passage targets pretensions of spiritual paternity. Davies and Allison, in the ICC, agree that an absolute reading is overstated and that the polemic is aimed at claimants to religious authority. This contextual harmonization is strong: the verse stands inside an explicit list of teacher-titles, and the contrast term is the unique fatherhood of God, a religious rather than familial category.

What keeps the tension from fully dissolving is the Matthean formulation itself. The text says "call no one your father on the earth" (epi tēs gēs), a universalizing phrase, rather than "call no teacher father." Ulrich Luz, in the Hermeneia volume, notes that Matthew is deliberately maximalist in his anti-hierarchical rhetoric: the sayings function to flatten ecclesial structure within the community of those who are all "brothers" (adelphoi, v. 8), not merely to proscribe one professional title. The harmonization therefore succeeds at the level of authorial intent and semantic domain but requires the reader to supply the restriction "as a religious honorific," which the surface grammar leaves open. The early reception confirms the ambiguity: the Didache and later writers wrestled with the scope of such sayings, and the church never applied the prohibition to domestic usage, reading it — rightly, on the contextual analysis — as a critique of religious pretension.

Critically assessed, then, the two texts do not in fact issue contradictory commands once their domains are distinguished. The Decalogue governs kinship duty; Matthew 23:9 governs the politics of religious title-claiming and the community''s relation to the one heavenly Father. They speak past each other rather than against each other. The residual difficulty is purely verbal — Matthew''s chosen wording is broader than his evident target — which is why the harmonization is best classed as partial rather than complete. Verdict: harmonizable on a contextual reading; the apparent contradiction is largely an artifact of the saying''s deliberately sweeping phrasing.', 'R. T. France, *The Gospel of Matthew* (New International Commentary on the New Testament; Eerdmans, 2007), pp. 862-864, on ''father'' as a religious honorific.
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. III (International Critical Commentary; T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 276-278.
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 21-28* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2005), pp. 105-107, on the anti-hierarchical thrust of the saying.
Craig L. Blomberg, *Matthew* (New American Commentary 22; Broadman & Holman, 1992), pp. 342-343.
Brevard S. Childs, *The Book of Exodus* (Old Testament Library; Westminster, 1974), pp. 417-420, on the fifth commandment.
Harold W. Hoehner, *Ephesians: An Exegetical Commentary* (Baker Academic, 2002), pp. 793-796, on Ephesians 6:2.', 0, NULL, 3, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Exodus, Deuteronomy, Matthew, Ephesians', 6, 2, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

Apologists such as Gleason Archer and Norman Geisler argue, persuasively, that no two of these texts are addressing the same question. Josiah''s tearing expresses genuine contrition and is praised as such. Qoheleth merely catalogues; he prescribes nothing. Elisha''s rebuke is not aimed at the gesture but at the king''s faithless despair — the king tears his clothes because he assumes catastrophe, when the remedy stands ready in Israel. And Joel does not abolish the practice. The Hebrew construction wĕʾal ... is the well-known "not X but (rather) Y" idiom that James Barr and other Hebraists have shown frequently functions comparatively rather than absolutely (cf. Hosea 6:6, "I desire mercy and not sacrifice," which Jesus himself reads as a matter of priority, not prohibition). Joel is demanding that the outward sign be matched by inward repentance, not banning the sign.

This defense substantially holds at the level of formal logic: there is no single command "tear your clothes" set against "do not tear your clothes" under identical conditions. What the harmonization concedes, however, is a real diachronic shift in theological emphasis. Pre-exilic Deuteronomistic piety valorizes the physical gesture as a token of sincerity. The prophetic and post-exilic strands increasingly interiorize cult — Joel 2:13, Isaiah 58''s critique of fasting, Psalm 51:16-17''s "a broken and contrite heart, O God, you will not despise," and Amos 5:21-24''s repudiation of ritual without justice all relocate the weight from external observance to inner disposition. Hans Walter Wolff and Marvin Sweeney trace exactly this prophetic critique of cult, and it is the engine behind Joel''s polemic. The canon thus preserves voices with genuinely different priorities about the value of outward ritual, even where they do not formally contradict.

The verdict, then, depends on the standard applied. As a matter of strict logic, the texts are reconcilable: differing intentions, situations, and rhetorical idioms account for the surface diversity, and Joel''s "not...but" is comparative. As a matter of theology, the apologetic reconciliation succeeds only by granting that Scripture contains an evolving, internally weighted conversation about the worth of ritual gesture — pre-exilic approval of the act, prophetic subordination of it to the heart. Verdict: overstated as a logical contradiction; genuine as evidence of competing canonical attitudes resolved by intent, idiom, and theological development.', 'Hans Walter Wolff, *Joel and Amos* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1977), pp. 49-52, on Joel 2:13.
Marvin A. Sweeney, *The Twelve Prophets*, Vol. 1 (Berit Olam; Liturgical Press, 2000), pp. 162-165.
James Barr, *The Semantics of Biblical Language* (Oxford University Press, 1961), pp. 263-296, on Hebrew idiom and comparative negation.
Mordechai Cogan and Hayim Tadmor, *II Kings* (Anchor Bible 11; Doubleday, 1988), pp. 65-67, 290-293.
Ralph W. Klein, *2 Chronicles* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2012), pp. 491-495, on 2 Chronicles 34:27.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 297-299.
Tremper Longman III, *The Book of Ecclesiastes* (New International Commentary on the Old Testament; Eerdmans, 1998), pp. 117-119, on Ecclesiastes 3:7.', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', '2 Kings, 2 Chronicles, Ecclesiastes, Joel', 6, 2, 1, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

Apologetic responses, from Gleason Archer to the contributors to John Walton''s backgrounds commentary, divide into two lines. The first is chronological: 2 Kings 8:1-6 need not follow chapter 5 in time. Mordechai Cogan and Hayim Tadmor stress in the Anchor Bible volume that 2 Kings 4-8 is a loose anthology of Elisha legends rather than a sequential chronicle, and the Gehazi-at-court scene reads naturally as a flashback — the king asking Gehazi to *recount* (the Hebrew sappĕrâ-nāʾ, with the following narration in 8:5 describing past deeds) deeds already done. On this reading the conversation antedates the curse, and no leper appears at court at all. The second line proposes that the curse may have been lifted off-screen, though the text offers no warrant for this and it remains pure conjecture.

The first defense is genuinely strong and largely dissolves the *logical* problem: if 8:1-6 is anterior to chapter 5, there is no contradiction in events, only in canonical arrangement. What it concedes, however, is precisely the critical point. Burke Long in his Forms of the Old Testament Literature volume and Marvin Sweeney in the Old Testament Library commentary describe 2 Kings as a redacted collage of prophetic legend cycles drawn from independent oral traditions. The Gehazi-leprosy story and the Gehazi-at-court story almost certainly stem from separate cycles, gathered by the Deuteronomistic compilers and set side by side without harmonizing their implications about Gehazi''s status. This is the same compositional phenomenon visible elsewhere in the books of Samuel and Kings — for instance, the multiple, mutually awkward introductions of David to Saul in 1 Samuel 16-17, long recognized as a seam between sources.

The upshot is a split verdict depending on the question asked. As a matter of event-sequence, the contradiction is reconcilable: the court scene can be read as prior to the curse, and the Hebrew supports a retrospective sense. As a matter of the canonical narrative read straight through, the text places a man cursed with perpetual, hereditary tsaraʿat in casual conversation with the king three chapters later, with no notice of healing — an inconsistency that points to source-critical layering rather than a deliberately constructed timeline. Verdict: harmonizable by reordering, but the reordering itself testifies to the composite, non-sequential character of the Elisha traditions.', 'Mordechai Cogan and Hayim Tadmor, *II Kings* (Anchor Bible 11; Doubleday, 1988), pp. 67-71, 88.
Burke O. Long, *2 Kings* (Forms of the Old Testament Literature 10; Eerdmans, 1991), pp. 71-78, 102-104.
Marvin A. Sweeney, *I and II Kings* (Old Testament Library; Westminster John Knox, 2007), pp. 297-302, 320.
Jacob Milgrom, *Leviticus 1-16* (Anchor Bible 3; Doubleday, 1991), pp. 816-826, on tsaraʿat and ritual quarantine.
Choon-Leong Seow, "The First and Second Books of Kings," in *The New Interpreter''s Bible*, Vol. 3, ed. Leander E. Keck (Abingdon, 1999), pp. 197-200.
John H. Walton (ed.), *Zondervan Illustrated Bible Backgrounds Commentary*, Vol. 3 (Zondervan, 2009), pp. 192-194.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 209-210.', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', '2 Kings', 7, 2, 1, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard harmonization, advanced by Gleason Archer and presupposed by Edwin Thiele''s chronological work, rests on a well-attested feature of Biblical Hebrew kinship vocabulary. The noun bat ("daughter"), like its masculine counterpart bēn ("son"), is genealogically elastic: it can denote a granddaughter or, more broadly, a female descendant or member of a dynastic house. The same flexibility lets the Bible call Jesus "son of David" or label the kings of the northern realm "sons of Omri" — indeed, Assyrian royal inscriptions long after Omri''s death still designate Israel "the house of Omri" (bīt-Ḫumri). On this reading, "daughter of Omri" identifies Athaliah as a scion of the Omride dynasty whose founder and eponym was Omri, while "daughter of Ahab" gives her immediate parentage. Both can be true at once, and the apparent contradiction dissolves on standard lexicography. This harmonization is genuinely sound: it invokes no special pleading, only the ordinary semantic range of bat, and it is the reading adopted by most critical commentators as well as conservatives.

Where genuine scholarly debate remains is not logical but historical — namely, what Athaliah''s actual parentage was. The majority view, reflected in the natural narrative flow (she is repeatedly tied to "the house of Ahab" and acts to perpetuate Ahab''s and Jezebel''s dynastic interests), is that she was Ahab''s biological daughter, very possibly by the Phoenician princess Jezebel, with "daughter of Omri" being the looser dynastic label. A minority reconstruction, canvassed by Cogan and Tadmor and explored in the essays of Lester Grabbe''s volume on the Omrides, takes "daughter of Omri" literally and proposes that Athaliah was Omri''s daughter and thus Ahab''s sister, married into the Davidic line during Jehoshaphat''s reign to seal the Omride-Judahite alliance; on this view the "daughter of Ahab" notices would be a secondary harmonizing or chronological adjustment. Steven McKenzie and Baruch Halpern have weighed similar possibilities. The evidence does not decisively settle which woman the historical Athaliah was.

The crucial distinction is therefore between the textual contradiction (which the elastic-bat reading genuinely resolves) and the historical question (which remains open). The two biblical designations are not logically incompatible once bat is read with its normal range; the canonical text is internally consistent. What is unresolved is merely a matter of historical reconstruction about which descent the writers had in mind. Verdict: harmonizable — the apparent contradiction is a translation/lexical artifact of treating bat as strictly ''biological daughter,'' while the underlying historical parentage stays legitimately disputed.', 'Mordechai Cogan and Hayim Tadmor, *II Kings* (Anchor Bible 11; Doubleday, 1988), pp. 96-98.
Mordechai Cogan, *I Kings* (Anchor Bible 10; Doubleday, 2001), pp. 418-422, on Omride dynastic designations.
Ralph W. Klein, *2 Chronicles* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2012), pp. 308-310.
Steven L. McKenzie, *1 Kings 16 - 2 Kings 16* (International Exegetical Commentary on the Old Testament; Kohlhammer, 2019), pp. 198-201.
Lester L. Grabbe, ed., *Ahab Agonistes: The Rise and Fall of the Omri Dynasty* (Library of Hebrew Bible/Old Testament Studies 421; T&T Clark, 2007), pp. 28-32, 175-180.
Edwin R. Thiele, *The Mysterious Numbers of the Hebrew Kings* (rev. ed.; Kregel, 1983), pp. 102-105.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 206-207.', 0, NULL, 6, 'OT_internal', '2 Kings, 2 Chronicles', 3, 2, 1, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The consensus diagnosis is scribal corruption. On the mechanism the cited commentators are more cautious than the older handbooks: numbers in the Masoretic Text and its known forebears are written out as words, and there is no manuscript evidence that the biblical text was ever transmitted using alphabetic numerals (the letter-value system — kaph = 20, mem = 40 — is attested on coins and in later usage, but as an explanation for these scribal slips it is a hypothesis, not a documented fact). The more defensible mechanism, and the one the critical commentaries actually argue, is corruption within the spelled-out number words themselves: "twenty" drifting to "forty" in copying. The versional evidence points to a figure in the low twenties as original. The Septuagint of 2 Chronicles 22:2 reads twenty (a minority of witnesses twenty-two), and the Syriac Peshitta likewise supports a figure in the low twenties; crucially, 2 Kings 8:26 reads twenty-two in both the MT and the LXX. The corruption is thus isolated to the Masoretic stream of Chronicles, exactly what a localized copying error predicts.

This is not an isolated phenomenon. The Hebrew text of Kings and Chronicles is riddled with parallel numerical divergences — the most famous being Jehoiachin''s age at accession, eighteen in 2 Kings 24:8 but eight in 2 Chronicles 36:9, again an isolated single-stream error. Anneli Aejmelaeus and the broader school working on the Old Greek of Samuel–Kings have catalogued dozens of such cases, demonstrating that the numerical data in these books was among the least stable elements in transmission. Edwin Thiele, working from the conservative chronological side, reached the same conclusion about this verse: the forty-two is a copyist''s slip.

Where the camps part ways is over implication. Gleason Archer, Keil and Delitzsch, and the Geisler–Howe handbook treat the correction as a vindication: the autograph said twenty-two, a scribe later wrote forty-two, and the "original" was therefore inerrant. Critical scholars — Ralph Klein in the Hermeneia Chronicles volume, Sara Japhet in the Old Testament Library, Raymond Dillard in the Word Biblical Commentary, Mordechai Cogan and Hayim Tadmor in the Anchor Bible Kings volume — accept the textual-critical reconstruction but note its cost. Inerrancy as classically formulated by the Chicago Statement applies only to the non-existent autographs, never to any text actually possessed. For Protestant traditions that treat the Masoretic Text as canonical, the received text of 2 Chronicles 22:2 simply contains a false statement. The harmonization succeeds precisely by conceding that the canonical text errs.

Verdict: a genuine contradiction in the received text, resolved by appeal to a recoverable original reading — a textual-critical harmonization that works only at the price of admitting transmission error in the canonical Masoretic Chronicles.', 'Ralph W. Klein, *2 Chronicles* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2012), pp. 308-311, on the corruption of the age figure at 2 Chronicles 22:2
Sara Japhet, *I & II Chronicles* (Old Testament Library; Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 822-825, on the variant ages at 2 Chronicles 22:2
Mordechai Cogan and Hayim Tadmor, *II Kings* (Anchor Bible 11; Doubleday, 1988), pp. 99-100, on 2 Kings 8:26
