Edwin R. Thiele, *The Mysterious Numbers of the Hebrew Kings*, rev. ed. (Kregel, 1983), pp. 64-67, conceding scribal error at 2 Chronicles 22:2
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 206-207, on the autograph reading of 2 Chronicles 22:2
Raymond B. Dillard, *2 Chronicles* (Word Biblical Commentary 15; Word, 1987), pp. 173-174, on 2 Chronicles 22:2', 0, NULL, 7, 'OT_internal', '2 Kings, 2 Chronicles', 1, 1, 2, 1, 1, '2 Chronicles 22:2 MT reads ''forty-two'' against 2 Kings 8:26 ''twenty-two,'' with the LXX (and some other witnesses) reading twenty/twenty-two, indicating a numeral corruption in the Chronicles transmission.', 'BHS apparatus at 2 Chronicles 22:2');

Understanding the problem requires understanding the framework. The Historian dated each Judahite king by the regnal year of the contemporary Israelite king and vice versa, producing a web of cross-synchronisms. These synchronisms were almost certainly drawn from archival regnal lists rather than computed by the editor, and they frequently fail to cohere — a well-documented feature of the Kings chronology, not a one-off.

The dominant conservative harmonization comes from Edwin Thiele, whose system has governed evangelical chronology since the 1950s and has been refined by Andrew Steinmann and Leslie McFall. Thiele''s key tools are two: (1) the distinction between accession-year reckoning, in which a king''s partial first calendar year is counted as the predecessor''s last year (so his "year one" begins the following New Year), and non-accession-year reckoning, in which the partial year counts as "year one"; and (2) differing calendar starting points (Nisan vs. Tishri) in Israel and Judah. Because Judah and Israel could use different conventions at different times, a single real date can be expressed by two different synchronistic numbers. On Thiele''s reconstruction the twelve/eleven divergence resolves into compatible counting systems rather than a true conflict. This is genuinely sophisticated work, and within its own terms it is internally consistent.

The critical objection, pressed by Mordechai Cogan and Hayim Tadmor in the Anchor Bible volume, Marvin Sweeney in the Old Testament Library, and Volkmar Fritz in the Continental Commentary, is methodological. The reckoning shifts and the calendar switches that Thiele invokes are nowhere signaled in the text; they are postulated wherever the arithmetic fails. A system that can absorb any discrepancy by selecting whichever convention dissolves it is unfalsifiable, and unfalsifiability is not the same as confirmation. More pointedly, Cogan and Tadmor argue that 2 Kings 9:29 bears the marks of a later redactional gloss: it is a chronological aside intruding into the Jehu narrative, repeating the synchronism already given at 8:25 but with a different number, and was never harmonized with it. Sweeney concurs. On this reading the two figures are the residue of editorial layering — the original framework notice at 8:25 and a secondary, slightly divergent notice at 9:29 inserted by a later hand working from a different source or a different reckoning, with no editor smoothing the seam.

The parsimonious explanation, then, is not that the text encodes an unstated dual-calendar scheme but that the Deuteronomistic compilers worked from imperfect lists and tolerated minor inconsistencies. The Septuagint tradition, which preserves numerous divergent synchronisms, reinforces the picture of an unstable numerical substratum.

Verdict: a contradiction in the received text, harmonizable only by importing an elaborate, textually unsignaled chronological scheme; most economically read as a redactional inconsistency between an original framework notice and a secondary gloss.', 'Mordechai Cogan and Hayim Tadmor, *II Kings* (Anchor Bible 11; Doubleday, 1988), pp. 99, 110-112, on 2 Kings 9:29 as a gloss
Marvin A. Sweeney, *I and II Kings* (Old Testament Library; Westminster John Knox, 2007), pp. 322-324, on the divergent synchronisms
Edwin R. Thiele, *The Mysterious Numbers of the Hebrew Kings*, rev. ed. (Kregel, 1983), pp. 99-104, on reckoning conventions
Andrew E. Steinmann, *From Abraham to Paul: A Biblical Chronology* (Concordia, 2011), pp. 145-150, refining Thiele''s synchronisms
John Bright, *A History of Israel*, 4th ed. (Westminster John Knox, 2000), pp. 252-255, on the chronology of the Omride period
Volkmar Fritz, *1 & 2 Kings: A Continental Commentary* (Fortress, 2003), pp. 286-289, on the redactional chronological notices', 0, NULL, 8, 'OT_internal', '2 Kings', 2, 2, 1, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Roughly a century later, Hosea 1:4 reverses the verdict. Hosea names his firstborn Jezreel and announces that Yahweh will "avenge the blood of Jezreel upon the house of Jehu, and will cause to cease the kingdom of the house of Israel." "The blood of Jezreel" points directly at the bloodbath of the coup — Jezreel was where Joram and Jezebel died — and the announced punishment fell on Zechariah, the fourth-generation heir whose murder ended the dynasty (2 Kings 15:10-12). The God who promised the dynasty as a reward for the killings now treats the killings as bloodguilt demanding the dynasty''s extinction.

The strongest harmonization, articulated cautiously by Hans Walter Wolff and more confidently by Duane Garrett in the New American Commentary, distinguishes the mandate from the manner. Jehu was authorized to remove Ahab''s house but exceeded the commission in scope and motive — slaughtering Ahaziah and his kin (a Judahite, outside the Israelite mandate), the Baal worshippers en masse, and acting from political ambition rather than covenant zeal. Hosea, on this reading, condemns the excess and the self-serving bloodlust, not the obedience. The argument has real textual purchase: Jehu plainly overreached, and 2 Kings 10:31 immediately notes that he himself did not keep the law or abandon the calf-cult, undercutting any portrait of him as a righteous agent.

What the harmonization cannot absorb is 2 Kings 10:30. The Deuteronomist does not say Jehu did the right thing badly; he says Jehu did "that which is right in mine eyes" and "according to all that was in mine heart," and rewards him for it. If the manner was the offense, the Historian betrays no awareness of it. The cleaner explanation, advanced by Wolff, Andersen and Freedman in the Anchor Bible Hosea, and Marvin Sweeney, is diachronic: two theological traditions evaluate one purge from opposite ends of its consequences. The Deuteronomistic source celebrated Jehu''s coup as covenant fidelity and Yahwistic house-cleaning; Hosea, witnessing the violence, instability, and Assyrian pressure that the dynasty''s century ultimately produced, read the founding massacre as a crime that Yahweh would requite. The Black Obelisk of Shalmaneser III, which depicts "Jehu son of Omri" (Iaua mar Humri) prostrate and paying tribute, situates the dynasty in its real geopolitical squeeze — and it is the secure epigraphic attestation here. The Tel Dan Stele, sometimes dragged into this discussion, does not name Jehu at all and its bearing on the coup is debated (it most plausibly has an Aramean king claiming the deaths of Joram and Ahaziah), so the Black Obelisk, not Tel Dan, is the load-bearing extra-biblical evidence.

Verdict: a genuine contradiction in canonical evaluation. The manner-versus-mandate harmonization founders on 2 Kings 10:30''s unqualified divine approval; the data are best explained as competing theological judgments from different periods preserved side by side in the canon.', 'Francis I. Andersen and David Noel Freedman, *Hosea* (Anchor Bible 24; Doubleday, 1980), pp. 174-180, on Hosea 1:4
Hans Walter Wolff, *Hosea* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1974), pp. 18-22, on the condemnation of Jezreel
Mordechai Cogan and Hayim Tadmor, *II Kings* (Anchor Bible 11; Doubleday, 1988), pp. 113-118, on 2 Kings 9-10
Marvin A. Sweeney, *The Twelve Prophets* (Berit Olam; Liturgical Press, 2000), vol. 1, pp. 19-25, on Hosea 1:4
Duane A. Garrett, *Hosea, Joel* (New American Commentary 19A; Broadman & Holman, 1997), pp. 56-60, on the condemnation of Jehu''s excess
Walter Brueggemann, *1 & 2 Kings* (Smyth & Helwys Bible Commentary; Smyth & Helwys, 2000), pp. 396-410, on the Jehu narrative', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', '2 Kings, Hosea', 7, 2, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The apologetic harmonization, defended by Eugene Merrill and Martin Selman, treats the two as compatible at different levels of specificity. Kings gives the general fact — burial in the city of David — while Chronicles supplies a further detail, that within that city he was not laid in the royal tombs proper. "With his fathers" need denote only ancestral burial within the Davidic city, not a specific sepulchre, so there is no logical contradiction: a man can be buried in the city of David and not in the kings'' sepulchres simultaneously. As a matter of bare logic this is correct, and it is why this entry is better classed as partially harmonized rather than a flat factual collision.

What the leveling harmonization obscures is the literary and theological character of the Chronicler''s change. Sara Japhet, Ralph Klein, Raymond Dillard, and Steven McKenzie all read the added clause as a deliberate redactional comment driven by the Chronicler''s pervasive theology of immediate retribution: faithful kings prosper and are honored in death, apostate kings die badly and are denied honor. Joash fits the schema precisely — he begins well under the priest Jehoiada, restores the temple, then after Jehoiada''s death leads the nation into idolatry, murders Jehoiada''s son Zechariah, suffers an Aramean invasion, and is assassinated by his own servants. The denial of royal burial is the narrative seal on this decline. The Chronicler deploys the identical device elsewhere: Jehoram is buried in the city of David "but not in the sepulchres of the kings" (2 Chronicles 21:20), Uzziah the leper is buried "with his fathers in the field of burial which belonged to the kings" but not in the tombs themselves (2 Chronicles 26:23), and Ahaz is brought into the city "but they brought him not into the sepulchres of the kings of Israel" (2 Chronicles 28:27). The recurrence shows a systematic editorial hand, not four independent archival memories.

Gary Knoppers and Klein accordingly classify the burial notices as Chronistic theology rather than recovered historical data. The Chronicler had no new information about Joash''s tomb; he was interpreting the Kings record through his retributive grid, converting an unremarkable royal burial into a posthumous demerit. So the texts do not collide over the physical location of a body — both place it in the city of David — but over the canonical verdict on Joash''s reign, which Kings leaves neutral and Chronicles marks as disgraced.

Verdict: harmonizable as a general-versus-specific report at the level of geography, but the divergence is a genuine, theologically motivated rewriting of Joash''s standing; the tension is real and resolves only by recognizing the Chronicler''s redactional program.', 'Sara Japhet, *I & II Chronicles* (Old Testament Library; Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 848-852, on 2 Chronicles 24:25
Ralph W. Klein, *2 Chronicles* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2012), pp. 339-344, on the Chronicler''s burial notices
