The standard conservative resolution is Edwin Thiele''s, elaborated by Leslie McFall and Andrew Steinmann: posit an overlap. On this view Jehoash was installed as co-regent alongside his ailing father Jehoahaz around Judah''s year 37, then began his sole reign near year 40; the two dates record two milestones — coregency and sole accession — of one reign. The framework is internally coherent and Thiele applies the same coregency mechanism across the many comparable seams in Kings. Coregencies were a genuine ancient Near Eastern institution (the Davidic succession of Solomon during David''s lifetime is a biblical instance), so the hypothesis is not impossible in principle.

The critical objection is that the coregency is invoked rather than attested. Nothing in 2 Kings 13 mentions Jehoash ruling beside Jehoahaz; the overlap is reconstructed precisely and only because the numbers fail to add up, and the same move can be deployed wherever a discrepancy appears, which makes it unfalsifiable. Mordechai Cogan and Hayim Tadmor in the Anchor Bible volume classify 13:10 among the synchronisms that resist Thielian reconciliation without ad hoc adjustment, and John Bright and Marvin Sweeney treat the regnal numbers of this period as approximate data drawn from imperfect archival lists rather than a seamless chronology. The textual evidence supports the latter picture: Anneli Aejmelaeus''s work on the Old Greek of Samuel–Kings documents that the Septuagint preserves numerous variant regnal and synchronistic figures, indicating that the Hebrew tradition itself was unstable on exactly these numbers. A Historian compiling from divergent king-lists, and not always reconciling them, is the most economical account.

Verdict: a numerical contradiction in the received text, derivable by summing the data of 13:1 against the explicit figure of 13:10. It is harmonizable only by positing an unattested coregency the text never mentions; the simpler reading is a redactional inconsistency arising from unreconciled source lists.', 'Mordechai Cogan and Hayim Tadmor, *II Kings* (Anchor Bible 11; Doubleday, 1988), pp. 142-145, 148-150, on 2 Kings 13:1, 10
Edwin R. Thiele, *The Mysterious Numbers of the Hebrew Kings*, rev. ed. (Kregel, 1983), pp. 109-116, on the Jehoash coregency
Leslie McFall, "A Translation Guide to the Chronological Data in Kings and Chronicles," *Bibliotheca Sacra* 148 (1991), pp. 3-45, on the regnal synchronisms
Andrew E. Steinmann, *From Abraham to Paul: A Biblical Chronology* (Concordia, 2011), pp. 153-157, on the Israelite chronology
Marvin A. Sweeney, *I and II Kings* (Old Testament Library; Westminster John Knox, 2007), pp. 351-356, on 2 Kings 13
Anneli Aejmelaeus, "How to Reach the Old Greek in 1 Samuel and What to Do with It," in *Congress Volume Helsinki 2010*, ed. Martti Nissinen (VTSup 148; Brill, 2012), pp. 185-205, on variant numerical readings
John Bright, *A History of Israel*, 4th ed. (Westminster John Knox, 2000), pp. 257-259, on the chronology of the period', 0, NULL, 8, 'OT_internal', '2 Kings', 2, 2, 1, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The lexical defense is genuine and must be granted at the outset. The Greek gennaō and its Hebrew counterpart yālad ("beget") are not restricted to immediate biological paternity in Semitic and Hellenistic-Jewish usage; they can designate descent across generations. Genesis 46:8-27 and Ezra 7:1-5 (where Ezra''s priestly line skips six generations attested in 1 Chronicles 6) furnish clear precedents for telescoped genealogies that use the same begetting language. W.D. Davies and Dale Allison, R.T. France, and Donald Hagner all treat Matthew''s omissions as a recognized convention of theological genealogy rather than an error of fact, and on the narrow lexical question they are correct: "begat" need not mean "was the father of."

The harder and more interesting question is why these particular three were dropped. A long-standing suggestion, discussed (though not endorsed) by Raymond Brown in The Birth of the Messiah, is that Ahaziah, Joash, and Amaziah were excised because they descended from the house of Ahab through Athaliah — Ahaziah''s mother was the daughter of Ahab and Jezebel (2 Kings 8:26) — and Exodus 20:5 threatens the iniquity of the fathers "unto the third and fourth generation." On this reading Matthew (or his source) deliberately blotted out the tainted Omride bloodline down to the fourth generation. The proposal is elegant but speculative: Matthew nowhere signals it, and he retains other morally compromised figures (Manasseh, for instance) without embarrassment. Brown himself reads the omission more soberly as numerological convenience for the 14-14-14 scheme, which doubles as a gematria on David''s name (D-W-D = 4+6+4 = 14).

The text-critical record shows that the difficulty was felt early. A handful of later witnesses expand Matthew 1:8 to insert the missing kings, harmonizing the Gospel toward Chronicles — a secondary "correction" that the critical editions (NA28) reject precisely because it betrays scribal discomfort with the shorter, original reading. That the tradition tried to fix it confirms that ancient readers recognized the gap.

Where the critical assessment parts company with the apologists is on what the harmonization concedes. To say gennaō is elastic is to grant that Matthew''s chain is not a biological pedigree and cannot be read as one; the genealogy is a theological construction shaped by numerology and Christological typology, not an archival record. Bart Ehrman presses the further point that Matthew likely drew on genealogical traditions independent of the Chronicler and was indifferent to exhaustive accuracy. Either way, the literal claim "Joram begat Ozias" is false as biological paternity and true only as remote ancestry.

Verdict: harmonizable — the tension dissolves once "begat" is read in its attested elastic sense as a telescoped genealogy, at the cost of conceding that Matthew''s list is a stylized theological construction rather than a literal pedigree.', 'W.D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. I (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 174-180, on the 14-14-14 scheme and telescoped begetting.
Raymond E. Brown, *The Birth of the Messiah*, updated ed. (Doubleday, 1993), pp. 81-84, on the Athaliah/Omride rationale and numerological reading of Matthew 1:8.
R.T. France, *The Gospel of Matthew* (NICNT; Eerdmans, 2007), pp. 32-37, on Matthew''s genealogy as convention rather than biological pedigree.
Donald A. Hagner, *Matthew 1-13* (WBC 33A; Word, 1993), pp. 9-11, on the elastic sense of gennaō.
Gary N. Knoppers, *1 Chronicles 1-9* (AB 12; Doubleday, 2003), pp. 318-322, on the Chronicler''s Davidic succession list at 1 Chronicles 3:11-12.
Bart D. Ehrman, *Jesus, Interrupted* (HarperOne, 2009), pp. 32-37, on Matthew''s use of genealogical traditions independent of the Chronicler.', 0, NULL, 6, 'OT_vs_NT', '1 Chronicles, Matthew', 3, 2, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The strongest apologetic harmonization, framed by Douglas Stuart and Duane Garrett, rests on the distinction between divine agency and divine moral endorsement of human agents. On this reading Yahweh may announce, decree, or permit a catastrophe as covenantal judgment on Israel without thereby approving the motives of the foreign army that executes it. The conceptual anchor is Isaiah 10:5-15, where Assyria is the "rod of mine anger," the instrument God wields against his people — yet God then promises to punish Assyria for the arrogance and cruelty with which it wielded that role. Transposed to Hosea and Amos: the Assyrians (the unnamed agents behind Samaria''s fall) carry out Yahweh''s sentence, while the Ammonites in Amos acted from naked territorial greed — "that they might enlarge their border" — with no divine commission, and so stand condemned for their motive. The act is the same; the moral situation, the apologist argues, is not.

The distinction has real exegetical purchase, and an honest reading should concede it. Hosea 13 does not say Yahweh commands anyone to disembowel pregnant women; it announces the atrocity as the consequence Samaria''s rebellion has earned. Amos 1 explicitly targets motive. That much survives critique.

But the harmonization runs into the grammar of Hosea 13 itself. The oracle in 13:7-16 is dominated by first-person divine speech: "I will be unto them as a lion" (13:7), "I will meet them as a bear bereaved of her whelps, and will rend the caul of their heart" (13:8), "I will tear them." The violence is predicated directly of Yahweh in the first person; there is no syntactic distancing onto an Assyrian instrument within the oracle, and the closing verse about ripped-open women flows from the same divine voice. Hans Walter Wolff and James Crenshaw both note that the speaker who performs the violence and the speaker who would morally evaluate it are, in this text, the same. Eric Seibert argues that the canon here preserves two incompatible prophetic theologies side by side — one in which Yahweh is the direct perpetrator of war atrocities against his own people, another in which he is the moral judge of nations that commit exactly those atrocities — without any redactional attempt to reconcile them.

The divine-permission move, then, softens the tension by relocating agency away from Yahweh in Hosea 13; but it must do so against the first-person grammar of the very passage it is harmonizing. The cleaner description is that the same act receives opposite moral valuations indexed to the identity of the perpetrator and the identity of the victim.

Verdict: partially harmonizable — the agency/endorsement distinction blunts the contradiction, but it strains against the first-person divine speech of Hosea 13 and ultimately reflects competing prophetic theologies of Yahweh''s role in war.', 'Francis I. Andersen and David Noel Freedman, *Hosea* (AB 24; Doubleday, 1980), pp. 638-643, on Hosea 13:16 and the bāqaʿ imagery.
Hans Walter Wolff, *Hosea* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1974), pp. 226-232, on the first-person divine speech in Hosea 13:7-16.
Shalom M. Paul, *Amos* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1991), pp. 64-68, on the Ammonite atrocity and motive in Amos 1:13.
Douglas Stuart, *Hosea-Jonah* (WBC 31; Word, 1987), pp. 209-212, 313-315, on the divine-permission distinction across Hosea 13:16 and Amos 1:13.
James L. Crenshaw, *Defending God: Biblical Responses to the Problem of Evil* (Oxford University Press, 2005), pp. 75-82, on Yahweh as direct agent of judgment violence.
Eric A. Seibert, *The Violence of Scripture: Overcoming the Old Testament''s Troubling Legacy* (Fortress, 2012), pp. 60-78, on competing prophetic theologies of divine violence.
Duane A. Garrett, *Hosea, Joel* (NAC 19A; Broadman & Holman, 1997), pp. 269-272, presenting the divine-permission reading of Hosea 13:16.', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Hosea, Amos', 6, 3, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The complication is genuinely entangled with the broader chronological knot of the period. Verse 30''s synchronism is anomalous in another way: Hoshea''s coup is elsewhere and more naturally dated to the reign of Ahaz, Jotham''s son and successor (cf. 2 Kings 17:1, which synchronizes Hoshea''s accession to Ahaz''s twelfth year). So "the twentieth year of Jotham" appears to count Jotham''s notional years past his death and into Ahaz''s reign — a reckoning the surrounding text never explains.

Edwin Thiele''s celebrated reconstruction resolves this, as it resolves most of Kings'' regnal snarls, by positing a coregency. On Thiele''s scheme Jotham was installed as co-regent over his leprous father Uzziah/Azariah (2 Kings 15:5 has Jotham "over the household, judging the people" while Uzziah was quarantined), and his "years" could be counted from that earlier point; a twentieth year measured from the coregency would fall after his sixteen-year sole reign and overlap Ahaz. Andrew Steinmann develops this in detail. The strength of the proposal is that 2 Kings 15:5 does explicitly describe Jotham governing during his father''s incapacity, so a Jotham coregency is not invented from nothing.

But the coregency cannot do the specific work required here. Verse 5 places a regency at the front end of Jotham''s career, between him and Uzziah; the twentieth-year datum requires extending Jotham''s count at the back end, past his death, into Ahaz''s reign — a different and unattested operation. The text nowhere authorizes counting "years of Jotham" beyond the sixteen it assigns him. Mordechai Cogan and Hayim Tadmor in the Anchor Bible accordingly read 15:30 as a secondary editorial synchronism, a late gloss inserted to coordinate Israelite and Judahite data, which the glossator failed to reconcile with the formal notice in 15:33. Marvin Sweeney concurs that the synchronistic chronology of this stretch of Kings shows the seams of editorial coordination layered onto older source figures, preserved without smoothing. The phrase may also reflect simple interference from Pekah''s own twenty-year reign reported in 15:27 — a plausible source for a stray "twentieth year."

The critical reading thus does not need to deny that Jotham had a coregency; it denies that any attested reckoning convention extends his regnal count to a twentieth year. The bare textual data are: sixteen years officially assigned, a twentieth year referenced in a synchronism that elsewhere belongs to Ahaz. The likeliest explanation is editorial, not chronological — a redactor''s arithmetic that the final text never harmonized.

Verdict: a genuine numerical inconsistency, most economically explained as a late synchronistic gloss; the coregency harmonization rescues it only by importing a back-extended reckoning of Jotham''s years that the text neither states nor implies.', 'Mordechai Cogan and Hayim Tadmor, *II Kings* (AB 11; Doubleday, 1988), pp. 175-178, reading 2 Kings 15:30 as a secondary editorial synchronism.
Marvin A. Sweeney, *I and II Kings* (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 2007), pp. 376-378, on editorial layering in the regnal synchronisms.
Edwin R. Thiele, *The Mysterious Numbers of the Hebrew Kings*, rev. ed. (Kregel, 1983), pp. 124-130, on the Jotham coregency reconstruction.
