Steven L. McKenzie, *1-2 Chronicles* (Abingdon Old Testament Commentaries; Abingdon, 2004), commentary on 2 Chronicles 28.', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', '2 Kings, 2 Chronicles', 7, 2, 1, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The apologetic harmonization, represented by Walter Kaiser and (cautiously) by Iain Provan, distinguishes the evaluative axes. Hezekiah''s incomparability clause attaches to trust — "he trusted in the LORD" — while Josiah''s attaches to Torah-fidelity, "according to all the law of Moses," expressed in the Deuteronomic triad of heart, soul, and might (Deuteronomy 6:5). On this reading the two kings are unsurpassed in different respects: Hezekiah in trust, Josiah in covenant obedience, and the superlatives do not collide because they measure different things. The reading has real textual footing, since the two verses are indeed differently scoped grammatically — each "none like him" clause is grammatically tethered to a particular virtue.

But the qualification is reader-supplied at the decisive point. The incomparability assertion itself — "none like him before or after" — is stated absolutely in both verses; the limitation to a single virtue is an inference imported to relieve the clash, not a restriction the text marks as exclusive. Marvin Sweeney and Choon-Leong Seow both observe that the formulae are too closely parallel to be comfortably partitioned this way, and Provan, ordinarily a conservative reader, concedes in his commentary that the parallel is awkward and likely reflects different hands.

The source-critical explanation accounts for the doubling more economically. Since Martin Noth''s foundational thesis of a single Deuteronomistic History, Frank Cross''s double-redaction hypothesis (developed by Richard Nelson) has held that the History was edited in two main stages: a pre-exilic, Josianic edition (Dtr1) that built toward Josiah as the climactic ideal king who fulfilled the Deuteronomic program, and an exilic revision (Dtr2) that had to reckon with the catastrophe Josiah''s reforms did not avert — Judah fell anyway. On this model the Josiah incomparability formula belongs to the Josianic edition''s celebration of its hero, while the Hezekiah formula reflects a redactional layer elevating the earlier reformer-king whose trust delivered Jerusalem from Sennacherib (2 Kings 19). The two unqualified superlatives are vestiges of two editorial programs with two ideal kings, preserved side by side in the final text without harmonization.

The upshot is that the formulae are not best read as carefully coordinated praise of two complementary virtues but as the residue of a composite history that could not decide between its two model monarchs.

Verdict: a genuine tension at the plain-reading level; the qualified-superlative defense is possible but text-unmarked, and the doubling is more economically explained by the double redaction of the Deuteronomistic History, which honored both Hezekiah and Josiah as the incomparable king.', 'Mordechai Cogan and Hayim Tadmor, *II Kings* (AB 11; Doubleday, 1988), pp. 217-218, 290-291, on the incomparability formulae for Hezekiah and Josiah.
Marvin A. Sweeney, *I and II Kings* (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 2007), pp. 405-407, 455-456, on the awkward parallel of the two incomparability formulae.
Frank Moore Cross, *Canaanite Myth and Hebrew Epic* (Harvard University Press, 1973), pp. 274-289, on the double redaction of the Deuteronomistic History.
Iain W. Provan, *1 & 2 Kings* (New International Biblical Commentary; Hendrickson, 1995), pp. 260-263, 277-279, conceding the awkward parallel of the two formulae.
Richard D. Nelson, *The Double Redaction of the Deuteronomistic History* (JSOTSup 18; Sheffield, 1981), pp. 76-98, on the Josianic edition''s ideal-king motif.
Choon-Leong Seow, "The First and Second Books of Kings," in *The New Interpreter''s Bible*, Vol. 3, ed. Leander E. Keck (Abingdon, 1999), pp. 257-260, 285-289, on the parallel incomparability formulae.', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', '2 Kings', 7, 2, 1, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The strongest harmonizing case, argued by John Oswalt in his NICOT volume and earlier by Edward J. Young, treats the two notices as complementary rather than contradictory: a single miracle can authenticate several promises simultaneously, so the sign confirmed healing, added years, and the deliverance of Jerusalem all at once, with each narrator foregrounding a different facet. This reading deserves a fair hearing, because the logic is sound — a prodigy is not exhausted by any one referent, and nothing prevents one sign from sealing a multi-clause oracle. To that extent the tension is partially harmonizable: there is no contradiction in saying the shadow confirmed both Hezekiah''s recovery and the city''s safety.

What the harmonization cannot absorb is the deliberate editorial reshaping that produced the difference. In 2 Kings the healing is reported (20:7) before Hezekiah asks for the sign (20:8), so the prodigy retroactively confirms an act already accomplished and looks forward to the temple visit. In Isaiah the promise precedes the sign and the sign precedes the healing, and the whole episode is reframed around the Assyrian threat — the very crisis that dominates Isaiah 36-37. Joseph Blenkinsopp in the Anchor Bible and Brevard Childs in the Old Testament Library both read this as editorial rearrangement serving a different theological agenda, not merely a difference of emphasis. Hans Wildberger argues that Isaiah 36-39 was incorporated into the Isaiah scroll as a historical appendix (closely paralleling 2 Kings 18-20), and that the placement makes the deliverance of Zion from Sennacherib the organizing concern; Christopher Seitz develops the same point, treating these chapters as a hinge constructed to bind the Assyrian material to the Babylonian horizon opened by chapter 39. On that reading the 2 Kings version preserves the older, simpler healing narrative, and the Isaianic editor re-anchored the sign to the deliverance promise because deliverance is what his scroll is about.

Two further data points sharpen the picture. First, the Great Isaiah Scroll (1QIsa-a) and the Masoretic Isaiah lack the equivalent of Hezekiah''s question about whether the shadow should go forward or back, which 2 Kings 20:9-10 supplies at length; the Isaiah account is leaner and more thesis-driven. Second, Cogan and Tadmor note that the figs-and-boil notice fits organically in Kings but reads as an intrusive coda in Isaiah, where it sits after the sign it was meant to occasion — a classic sign of redactional relocation. The astronomical mechanism (a shadow retreating ten ma''alot) is identical in both; what shifts is the literary purpose to which the prodigy is bent.

Verdict: harmonizable as to logic — one sign can confirm several promises — but the accounts genuinely differ in structure and primary referent through editorial reshaping of a shared source, so the tension is partially, not fully, dissolved.', 'Joseph Blenkinsopp, Isaiah 1-39, Anchor Bible 19 (Doubleday, 2000), pp. 477-484.
Hans Wildberger, Isaiah 28-39: A Continental Commentary (Fortress, 2002), pp. 437-460.
Brevard S. Childs, Isaiah, Old Testament Library (Westminster John Knox, 2001), pp. 277-285.
Mordechai Cogan and Hayim Tadmor, II Kings, Anchor Bible 11 (Doubleday, 1988), pp. 254-263.
John N. Oswalt, The Book of Isaiah, Chapters 1-39, NICOT (Eerdmans, 1986), pp. 671-685.
Christopher R. Seitz, Zion''s Final Destiny: The Development of the Book of Isaiah (Fortress, 1991), pp. 149-191.
Edward J. Young, The Book of Isaiah, Vol. II (Eerdmans, 1969), pp. 510-528.', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', '2 Kings, Isaiah', 7, 3, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard harmonization, running from Keil and Delitzsch through Gleason Archer and Iain Provan, redefines be-shalom not as ''non-violently'' but as ''before the disaster'' and ''at peace with God.'' On this reading the substantive content of the oracle is that Josiah would be spared the sight of Jerusalem''s fall — and that is true: Josiah died in 609 BCE, the city fell in 587 BCE, and he was buried in his own tomb (2 Kings 23:30) rather than dying in exile or with the dynasty in ruins. The lexical basis is real. Hebrew shalom has a far broader semantic field than English ''peace,'' encompassing wholeness, well-being, and covenant standing; the idiom ''go to one''s fathers in peace'' appears in Genesis 15:15 of Abraham, where it plainly means an honorable death at the end of a full life rather than a death free of all trouble. Marvin Sweeney in the Old Testament Library concedes the grammatical possibility, and a covenantal or dynastic reading of ''peace'' is not a strawman invention but a defensible construal of the Hebrew.

Yet the harmonization strains against the natural force of the promise. Huldah''s word is delivered as a reward for Josiah''s penitence (22:19), and its plain narrative function is reassurance: he will be spared the coming evil. A king cut down by Egyptian archers and carried home dying is, on any ordinary reading, not what such a promise leads a reader to expect. The decisive datum is that the Chronicler himself felt the friction and tried to relieve it. Where Kings simply reports the death, 2 Chronicles 35:21-22 inserts a fresh element: Necho claims to speak God''s word ordering Josiah to stand down, Josiah refuses, disguises himself, and goes to battle anyway ''and hearkened not unto the words of Necho from the mouth of God.'' The Chronicler thereby converts the death from an unexplained tragedy into a consequence of disobedience — implicitly conceding that the bare inheritance from Kings created a theological problem about Huldah''s promise.

Ralph Klein in Hermeneia and Sara Japhet in the Old Testament Library read this as characteristic Chronistic theology: the Chronicler systematically supplies retributive explanations for the deaths of otherwise good kings (compare his handling of Asa and Uzziah), and the Necho speech is a constructed apologia rather than independent tradition. Christine Mitchell''s CBQ study reads the Josiah death scene as deliberately ironic — Josiah, the reformer who heeded the book, dies for not heeding a word from God spoken through a foreign king, a reversal that the Chronicler exploits for theological effect. Whatever the literary artistry, the upshot is that the resolution is a later editorial patch, not something the Kings author built into the original oracle.

Verdict: partially harmonizable — be-shalom can bear the sense ''before the catastrophe, at peace with Yahweh,'' which is literally satisfied — but the reading runs against the natural promissory force of Huldah''s oracle, and the Chronicler''s own corrective is the clearest evidence that the ancient tradents perceived a genuine tension.', 'Mordechai Cogan and Hayim Tadmor, II Kings, Anchor Bible 11 (Doubleday, 1988), pp. 295-300.
Marvin A. Sweeney, I and II Kings, Old Testament Library (Westminster John Knox, 2007), pp. 444-446, 457-460.
Ralph W. Klein, 2 Chronicles, Hermeneia (Fortress, 2012), pp. 511-525.
Sara Japhet, I & II Chronicles, Old Testament Library (Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 1042-1056.
