Joel S. Kaminsky, *Corporate Responsibility in the Hebrew Bible* (JSOTSup 196; Sheffield Academic Press, 1995), pp. 139-178, on Ezekiel 18', 0, NULL, 2, 'OT_internal', 'Job, Ecclesiastes, Ezekiel', 5, 3, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Job 11:7 is a rhetorical question on Zophar''s lips: ''Can you find out (timṣāʾ) the deep things of God? Can you find out the limit (taklît) of the Almighty?'' The expected answer is no. The standard harmonization presses exactly the distinction the original languages support: Paul speaks of perceiving God''s existence and power from creation, while Zophar denies exhaustive comprehension of God''s inner depths and perfection. ''I can see that God exists and is powerful'' and ''I cannot fathom the totality of God'' are not contradictory; they are the ordinary theological pairing of natural knowledge with divine incomprehensibility. Walton''s NIVAC volume makes this case, and it has real force, because the Hebrew taklît (''limit, completeness'') points specifically to exhaustive measurement, not to mere existence. On the strict wording, the two verses can stand together.

The harmonization is therefore partial rather than complete, because the book of Job as a whole pushes harder than Zophar''s single line. Clines notes that Zophar''s question, though spoken by a friend whose theology is ultimately rebuked (Job 42:7), is echoed and amplified by Yahweh himself in the whirlwind speeches (Job 38-41), which mount an extended demonstration that human beings cannot grasp the design or governance of creation — ''Where were you when I laid the foundations of the earth?'' Job, who has searched throughout, concedes in 42:3 that he ''uttered what I did not understand, things too wonderful for me.'' Newsom''s literary reading shows the book''s epistemology is deliberately humbling: creation silences human protest but does not yield the theological mastery natural theology assumes. Crenshaw situates this within a broader wisdom skepticism about reason''s reach. So while Job 11:7 in isolation is reconcilable with Romans 1:20 on the existence-versus-comprehension distinction, the book''s overall stance toward what creation teaches the human inquirer is markedly more reserved than Paul''s confident ''clearly perceived.''

The net assessment: at the level of the two specific verses, the contradiction is largely defused by attending to the Hebrew and Greek — Paul affirms perceptible divine power, Zophar denies exhaustive divine comprehension, and these are different claims. At the level of theological tradition, however, the wisdom stream (Job, Qoheleth) and the Pauline stream represent genuinely divergent estimates of how far reason can travel toward God, and that divergence is real even if the proof-texts themselves do not strictly collide. Verdict: partially harmonizable — the verses are reconcilable on careful reading, but they sit within two traditions with materially different confidence in natural theology.', 'Robert Jewett, *Romans: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 152-167, on Romans 1:20
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *Romans* (Anchor Bible 33; Doubleday, 1993), pp. 269-282, on Romans 1:20
David J. A. Clines, *Job 1-20* (Word Biblical Commentary 17; Word Books, 1989), pp. 252-258, on Job 11:7
Carol A. Newsom, *The Book of Job: A Contest of Moral Imaginations* (Oxford University Press, 2003), pp. 90-129, on Job''s epistemology
James L. Crenshaw, *Old Testament Wisdom: An Introduction*, 3rd ed. (Westminster John Knox, 2010), pp. 90-130, on wisdom skepticism
John H. Walton, *Job* (NIV Application Commentary; Zondervan, 2012), pp. 173-180, on Job 11:7', 0, NULL, 2, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Job, Romans', 5, 3, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard harmonization is a temporal one: the wicked prosper now but will be desolate later, whether through eventual reversal in this life or final judgment. Kidner''s Tyndale commentary on Psalms argues this, and for some texts it is exactly right. Psalm 73 supplies its own long-term resolution: the psalmist''s vertigo lifts only when he enters the sanctuary and perceives the wicked''s ''end'' (ʾaḥărît) — set in slippery places, swept to ruin in a moment (73:17-20). Read whole, Psalm 73 is itself an argument that present prosperity is provisional, and it harmonizes cleanly with Psalm 34:21''s long-horizon claim. Brown''s reading of the Psalter and Dahood''s Anchor Bible commentary both note this internal movement from complaint to resolution. To this extent the harmonization is sound, and one should grant it.

It fails, however, for the texts that supply no such corrective. Job 12:6 is a bare empirical claim embedded in Job''s rebuttal of the friends, and the book of Job pointedly refuses to resolve the suffering problem by appeal to an afterlife — early Israelite theology had no developed doctrine of post-mortem judgment, Sheol being the common destination of righteous and wicked alike (cf. Job 3:17-19; Ecclesiastes 9:2-3). Clines stresses that Job''s complaint stands unanswered on its own terms. Jeremiah 12:1 is even more resistant: the prophet demands an accounting, and the divine reply in Jeremiah 12:5 (''If you have raced with foot-runners and they have wearied you, how will you compete with horses?'') does not promise the wicked''s downfall but warns Jeremiah that conditions will worsen. Lundbom''s Anchor Bible commentary observes that God simply declines to vindicate the retribution premise. There is no deferred-justice corrective in the passage to harmonize with Psalm 34:21.

The critical conclusion, consistent with Crenshaw''s survey of the wisdom crisis and Newsom''s study of Job, is that Israelite religion worked through a long confrontation with the empirical failure of strict retribution and produced several coexisting responses: some texts hold the doctrine (Psalm 34); some abandon it and concede the wicked prosper without remedy (Job 12; Jeremiah 12); and some defer the resolution to the end of life or the eschaton (Psalm 73, later apocalyptic). The temporal harmonization succeeds precisely where a text signals deferred justice and fails precisely where it does not. Verdict: partially harmonizable — genuinely reconciled for Psalm 73, but a real and unresolved tension for Job 12:6 and Jeremiah 12:1, reflecting a canon that preserves competing answers rather than one doctrine.', 'James L. Crenshaw, *Old Testament Wisdom: An Introduction*, 3rd ed. (Westminster John Knox, 2010), pp. 90-130, on the act-consequence crisis
David J. A. Clines, *Job 1-20* (Word Biblical Commentary 17; Word Books, 1989), pp. 287-294, on Job 12:6
Mitchell Dahood, *Psalms II: 51-100* (Anchor Bible 17; Doubleday, 1968), pp. 187-198, on Psalm 73
William P. Brown, *Psalms* (Interpreting Biblical Texts; Abingdon, 2010), pp. 99-114, on Psalm 73
Jack R. Lundbom, *Jeremiah 1-20* (Anchor Bible 21A; Doubleday, 1999), pp. 645-657, on Jeremiah 12:1
Derek Kidner, *Psalms 1-72* (Tyndale Old Testament Commentaries; InterVarsity Press, 1973), pp. 138-141, 259-263, on Psalms 34 and 73
Carol A. Newsom, *The Book of Job: A Contest of Moral Imaginations* (Oxford University Press, 2003), pp. 90-129, on Job''s stance', 0, NULL, 4, 'OT_internal', 'Job, Psalms, Jeremiah', 5, 2, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

Job 21 is a frontal assault on that whole framework. Job''s complaint in 21:7-13 is not abstract theodicy but empirical observation: the wicked grow old, wax mighty in power, see their offspring established, their houses safe from fear, and at the end "in a moment go down to Sheol" — they die peacefully in bed. Job 24:1-12 presses the same point about the oppressed poor whose cries God seems not to answer. The author of Job knows the Proverbs slogan and is deliberately holding it up against the world as it actually is. Psalm 73 stages the identical crisis from the inside, with the psalmist nearly losing his footing because he envied the prosperity of the wicked (73:2-3), resolving it only by relocating the reckoning to a sanctuary-vision of their "end" (73:17) — a move that concedes the problem in the present and defers the solution.

Ecclesiastes is the canon contradicting itself within a single book. Qohelet at 8:13 repeats the Proverbs line verbatim in spirit ("it shall not be well with the wicked, neither shall he prolong his days"), yet at 7:15 he has already declared, "there is a righteous man that perisheth in his righteousness, and there is a wicked man that prolongeth his life in his wickedness," and 8:14 names this a hevel, an absurdity. C. L. Seow reads these juxtapositions as deliberate, the rhetorical seams of a writer recording orthodox maxims only to undercut them.

The standard apologetic move — found in Gleason Archer''s Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties and Walter Kaiser''s Hard Sayings of the Bible — distinguishes literary modes: Proverbs offers general principles, Job and Ecclesiastes note exceptions, and the prophets describe long-term or eschatological outcomes. The lexical observation is fair, but it concedes the substantive point. If Proverbs 10:27 states a mere statistical tendency rather than a truth, then it is not true as written, and Job is not contradicting a divine guarantee but exposing a moralizing fiction. Relocating the claim to "eschatology" also imports a category the texts lack: Job, Qohelet, and the older Psalms have no developed afterlife in which the ledger is balanced — Sheol levels everyone (Job 21:26; Eccl 9:2-3). The deferral only works once apocalyptic resurrection enters Israelite thought late (Dan 12:2), and that is precisely the development these books predate.

The critical consensus, traceable through James Crenshaw and Roland Murphy and into Carol Newsom''s reading of Job as a deliberate destabilization of wisdom, is that Israelite literature preserves an unresolved debate between retribution theology and protest theology. Comparable ancient Near Eastern texts — the Babylonian Ludlul bēl nēmeqi and the Babylonian Theodicy — voice the same protest, showing this was a live regional question, not a uniquely Israelite slip. The canon never harmonized the two voices; it transmitted both. Verdict: genuine, intra-canonical contradiction — and in Job it is the literary engine, not a flaw.', 'Klaus Koch, "Is There a Doctrine of Retribution in the Old Testament?" in Theodicy in the Old Testament, ed. James L. Crenshaw (Fortress, 1983), pp. 57-87, on the act-consequence nexus behind Proverbs 10:27; 24:20
Carol A. Newsom, The Book of Job: A Contest of Moral Imaginations (Oxford University Press, 2003), pp. 130-168, on Job 21 as a destabilization of wisdom retribution
David J. A. Clines, Job 21-37 (WBC 18A; Thomas Nelson, 2006), pp. 511-538, on Job 21:7
James L. Crenshaw, Old Testament Wisdom: An Introduction, 3rd ed. (Westminster John Knox, 2010), pp. 89-115, 169-191, on the retribution-versus-protest debate
C. L. Seow, Ecclesiastes: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary (AB 18C; Doubleday, 1997), pp. 287-298, on Ecclesiastes 7:15; 8:13-14
