Gleason L. Archer, Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 235-238, on the proverbs-as-general-principle defense
Walter C. Kaiser Jr. et al., Hard Sayings of the Bible (InterVarsity Press, 1996), on the literary-mode harmonization', 0, NULL, 4, 'OT_internal', 'Job, Psalms, Proverbs, Ecclesiastes, Jeremiah, Nahum', 5, 2, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The figure in chapters 1-2 has to be read against the divine-council background. The Hebrew is ha-satan: the definite article plus a common noun meaning "the adversary" or "the accuser," a title, not a proper name — one cannot say "the John." Peggy Day''s study established that the satan here is a functionary of Yahweh''s heavenly court, something like a roving prosecutor, comparable to the lying spirit dispatched by Yahweh in 1 Kings 22:19-23, where the council deliberates and a spirit volunteers to do Yahweh''s bidding. Even within the prologue, Yahweh initiates the conversation, points Job out (1:8; 2:3), sets the terms, and authorizes each blow; the satan acts only on warrant. The text''s own theology of causation is that the buck stops with Yahweh, which is exactly what Isaiah 45:7 states programmatically: Yahweh forms light and creates darkness, makes peace and creates ra''ah.

The standard apologetic harmonization (Archer; Geisler and Howe) invokes secondary causation: Yahweh permitted while the satan caused, so both verses are true under a doctrine of divine permission. The argument is coherent as later systematic theology, but it is anachronistic as exegesis of Job. The Joban author has no developed category of an autonomous evil agent against whom the permission/causation distinction would do real work. Job himself never blames the satan — he cannot, because he is never told about the wager — and his entire lawsuit (e.g., 9:17; 16:11-14; 30:21) is directed at God as the author of his suffering. The divine speeches of chapters 38-41 answer Job by asserting Yahweh''s untrammeled sovereignty over creation; they never deflect responsibility onto a third party. To resolve 42:11 by importing secondary causation is to answer a question the book deliberately refuses to dodge.

There is also a redactional layer. Carol Newsom and David Clines note that the prose frame (chs. 1-2; 42:7-17), with its folktale cadence and its patient, pious Job, operates with a different theological vocabulary from the poetic dialogues, where Job is anything but patient. Marvin Pope''s Anchor Bible volume situates ha-satan as a council member rather than the cosmic Satan of 1 Enoch and the New Testament. The trajectory by which the title hardens into a proper name and a fallen-angel myth is visible across the canon itself: the older 2 Samuel 24:1 has Yahweh incite David to the census, while the later 1 Chronicles 21:1 rewrites this as "Satan" inciting David — the Chronicler performing precisely the deflection the apologists wish to read back into Job. Job 42:11 belongs to the earlier theology, in which Yahweh owns the calamity outright.

Verdict: the contradiction is internal to Job, and the permission-versus-causation harmonization succeeds only by anachronism — the narrator at 42:11 sides with the older, harder monotheism.', 'Carol A. Newsom, The Book of Job: A Contest of Moral Imaginations (Oxford University Press, 2003), pp. 53-71, on the prose frame and the satan figure
Marvin H. Pope, Job: Introduction, Translation, and Notes, 3rd ed. (AB 15; Doubleday, 1973), pp. 9-11, 348-350, on ha-satan and Job 42:11
