The lexical data confirm that the prooftexts on the other side name different categories. Psalm 5:5 (MT 5:6) does not use the general word for fool; it uses holelim, the participle of halal in the sense of being boastful or arrogantly mad — "the arrogant shall not stand before your eyes." That is moral arrogance, not the cognitive or status-foolishness Paul plays with. Ephesians 5:15 uses asophos, literally "un-wise," and urges believers to walk as sophoi — wise. So at the level of vocabulary the apparent contradiction largely dissolves: three distinct Greek and Hebrew concepts have been flattened into the single English word "fool."

The residual tension is theological and is sharpened, not relieved, by authorship questions. Most critical scholars — Ernest Best in the ICC and Margaret MacDonald in Sacra Pagina among them — judge Ephesians to be deutero-Pauline, written by a later admirer. The standard markers include its un-Pauline elongated sentences, its distinctive vocabulary, its realized eschatology (believers already raised and seated in the heavenly places, 2:6), and its dependence on Colossians. The author of Ephesians 5:15 has absorbed Pauline language but writes within a more settled, household-code, Greco-Roman moral framework in which sophia is simply a virtue — precisely the term the historical Paul had ironized at Corinth. So the move from 1 Corinthians to Ephesians tracks a real development: from Paul''s apocalyptic inversion of worldly status markers to the post-Pauline church''s accommodation to Hellenistic virtue ethics. Both sit alongside the Hebrew wisdom tradition (Prov 1:7, "fools despise wisdom and instruction"; Michael Fox on the semantics of folly in Proverbs), which is unambiguously pro-sophia.

The harmonization therefore holds on the surface and fails underneath. To read 1 Corinthians 3:18 and Proverbs 1:7 as merely two phrasings of one idea is to ignore what Paul is arguing: that the cross overturns the very evaluative scale on which conventional wisdom operates. That is not the same claim as Proverbs'' confident commendation of wisdom; it is closer to its dialectical opponent. Verdict: overstated as a verbal contradiction — different words, different referents — but underneath it preserves a genuine theological divergence between apocalyptic Paul and the moralizing wisdom tradition, a divergence the disputed authorship of Ephesians only sharpens.', 'Anthony C. Thiselton, The First Epistle to the Corinthians (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 2000), pp. 153-180, 314-321, on Paul''s reclamation of moria
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, First Corinthians (AYB 32; Yale University Press, 2008), pp. 154-166, 207-217, on 1 Corinthians 1-4
Dale B. Martin, The Corinthian Body (Yale University Press, 1995), pp. 47-68, on status and wisdom at Corinth
Margaret Y. MacDonald, Colossians and Ephesians (Sacra Pagina 17; Liturgical Press, 2000), pp. 320-326, on Ephesians 5:15
Ernest Best, A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on Ephesians (ICC; T&T Clark, 1998), pp. 502-510, on Ephesians 5:15
Michael V. Fox, Proverbs 1-9 (AB 18A; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 38-43, on the vocabulary of folly
D. A. Carson, The Cross and Christian Ministry (Baker, 1993), pp. 11-43, on the foolishness of the cross', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Psalms, 1 Corinthians, Ephesians', 6, 3, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

Against this stands a heaven-dwelling stream that becomes dominant in Deuteronomic and exilic theology. The Deuteronomists developed what scholars call Name-theology (shem-theology): Yahweh himself dwells in heaven, and only causes his name (shem) to dwell at the chosen place (Deut 12:5, 11). The classic articulation is Solomon''s prayer in 1 Kings 8:27-30: "will God indeed dwell on the earth? Behold, heaven and the heaven of heavens cannot contain you; how much less this house that I have built" — yet God''s eyes and his name are present toward the temple. Psalm 123:1 ("O thou that dwellest in the heavens") and the late Wisdom of Ecclesiastes 5:2 ("God is in heaven, and thou upon earth") simply assume the heavenly-residence model.

There is, in addition, a Priestly counter-current that neither fully localizes Yahweh at Zion nor banishes him to heaven: the kabod (glory) descends to dwell (shakan, the root behind later Shekinah) in the tabernacle (Exod 40:34-35). Israel Knohl distinguishes the Priestly Torah''s silent, immanent presence from the Holiness School''s adjustments, and Stephen Geller traces how these schools reconceived where and how the deity is accessible. The Psalms preserve fragments of more than one model, occasionally side by side.

The harmonizing reading offered by John Walton and the IVP background commentary — God dwells in heaven essentially but localizes his presence at Zion sacramentally — is not merely an apologetic imposition; it is performed within the text itself, exactly in 1 Kings 8. That is why this entry is best classed as partially harmonized: a coherent synthesis exists and is biblical. The qualification is that the synthesis is a reconciliation, achieved by the Deuteronomistic Historian, of traditions that did not originally agree. The Zion-residence psalms do not mean "God is really in heaven but symbolically here"; their moshav and menuhah language means residence. Michael Hundley''s comparative study of divine presence in ANE temples shows that the tension between a deity''s cosmic seat and its cult-statue or sanctuary localization was felt across the region and resolved differently by different traditions. The biblical canon transmits the competing resolutions rather than collapsing them.

Verdict: harmonizable, but only via a synthesis the Deuteronomist himself supplied to bridge originally distinct theologies — the surface contradiction is real and the reconciliation is a later, traceable theological move.', 'Mark S. Smith, The Origins of Biblical Monotheism (Oxford University Press, 2001), pp. 83-106, on the divine-mountain motif behind Zion
Jon D. Levenson, Sinai and Zion: An Entry into the Jewish Bible (Winston, 1985), pp. 89-178, on the Zion tradition
Frank Moore Cross, Canaanite Myth and Hebrew Epic (Harvard University Press, 1973), pp. 91-111, on the divine-mountain enthronement
Michael B. Hundley, Gods in Dwellings: Temples and Divine Presence in the Ancient Near East (WAW Sup 3; SBL Press, 2013), pp. 351-384, on cosmic versus localized divine presence
Israel Knohl, The Sanctuary of Silence: The Priestly Torah and the Holiness School (Fortress, 1995), pp. 124-148, on the Priestly theology of presence
Stephen A. Geller, Sacred Enigmas: Literary Religion in the Hebrew Bible (Routledge, 1996), pp. 62-86, on the schools of Israelite religion
Frank-Lothar Hossfeld and Erich Zenger, Psalms 3: A Commentary on Psalms 101-150 (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2011), pp. 454-468, on Psalm 132:13-14
Sandra L. Richter, The Deuteronomistic History and the Name Theology (BZAW 318; de Gruyter, 2002), pp. 41-96, on shem-theology and 1 Kings 8:27-30', 0, NULL, 2, 'OT_internal', 'Psalms, Ecclesiastes, Joel', 5, 3, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

That is why the apologetic response (Archer; Geisler and Howe) is correct on the SAB''s chosen examples. Hebrew prophetic and narrative literature deploys deliberately coarse language for theological ends, and coarseness is not impurity in the relevant sense. Ezekiel 23 is the extreme case: the allegory of Oholah and Oholibah describes the sisters'' lust in language whose explicitness Moshe Greenberg analyzes as calculated prophetic shock. Habakkuk 2:16 and Malachi 2:3 use scatological and shaming imagery as instruments of judgment oracle. None of this contradicts a claim about the reliability of God''s word; it only contradicts a claim no one in the texts is making, namely that scripture is genteel.

The 2 Kings 18:27 / Isaiah 36:12 case is especially instructive because the tradition itself flagged the language. The Rabshakeh''s taunt that the besieged will "eat their own dung and drink their own piss" preserves a Ketib/Qere variation: the written consonantal text (Ketib) has the blunt terms hare''ehem (their dung) and shenehem/meymey raglehem, while the Masoretes supplied euphemistic Qere readings to be vocalized aloud — tso''atam for the excrement and meymey raglehem (literally "water of their feet") for the urine. Cogan and Tadmor note the scribal discomfort. The text knows its own vocabulary is rough and softens it in reading; this is evidence of editorial sensibility, not of impure revelation.

The genuine tension the question gropes toward lies elsewhere. If "every word of God is pure" is pressed into a doctrine of verbal inerrancy and pristine transmission, critical scholarship offers far harder counter-evidence than vulgar diction. Emanuel Tov and Eugene Ulrich have documented the pluriformity of the text: the books circulated in variant editions. The Septuagint of Jeremiah is roughly one-eighth (about 2,700 words) shorter than the Masoretic Jeremiah and arranges the oracles differently, and Qumran preserved a short Hebrew Vorlage (4QJer-b) matching the LXX alongside a long edition matching the MT — two divergent "pure words" of the same prophet. The Pentateuchal sources disagree on narrative particulars. Confronted with this, the question "which pure word?" has no single answer, and the slogan strains under historical scrutiny.

So the verdict splits. As stated, the contradiction is overstated: the purity claim is about reliability, not register, and the dung-and-piss prooftexts trade on an equivocation the Hebrew does not support — the Masoretes themselves euphemized them. The residual difficulty is real but belongs to textual criticism rather than to vocabulary: a doctrine of perfectly preserved "pure words" sits uneasily with demonstrable textual pluriformity. Verdict: SAB prooftexts overstated/equivocal; the serious version of the problem is transmission, not diction.', 'Emanuel Tov, Textual Criticism of the Hebrew Bible, 3rd ed. (Fortress, 2012), pp. 51-71, 283-326, on textual pluriformity and Jeremiah
Eugene Ulrich, The Dead Sea Scrolls and the Developmental Composition of the Bible (VTSup 169; Brill, 2015), pp. 47-78, on variant editions of biblical books
