Norman L. Geisler and Thomas Howe, When Critics Ask (Victor Books, 1992), pp. 213-217, on the purity of God''s word', 0, NULL, 2, 'OT_internal', '2 Kings, Psalms, Proverbs, Isaiah, Ezekiel, Habakkuk, Malachi', 5, 3, 2, 5, 1, 'In 2 Kings 18:27 // Isaiah 36:12 the Masoretes preserved a euphemistic Qere reading-tradition (''their excrement / water of their feet'') against the cruder Ketiv, a matter of vocabulary decorum, not of whether God''s word is ''pure.''', 'Masoretic Qere notes at 2 Kings 18:27 and Isaiah 36:12; cf. BHS apparatus');

Davies and Allison, in the ICC, flag the internal friction directly and treat 5:22 as part of Matthew''s intensification of Torah rather than a literal forensic statute. The strongest harmonizations come in three forms. First, the lexical move: Gustaf Dalman, Matthew Black, and Joachim Jeremias floated readings in which the underlying term is not Greek mōros but Aramaic moreh/mara, "rebel" (cf. Numbers 20:10, where Moses calls Israel ha-morim), so that Jesus condemns a specific charge of apostasy, not the ordinary word "fool." Second, the genre move (Carson, France): the Sermon is hyperbolic ethical instruction whose target is murderous contempt of the heart, not a vocabulary ban, so 23:17 — a prophetic rebuke without hatred — falls outside its scope. Third, the Christological move: Jesus as divine judge may pronounce on character in a way disciples may not.

Each has weaknesses. The Aramaic-moreh proposal has no manuscript support; every Greek witness reads mōre, and Luz rejects the retroversion as speculative, noting that Matthew''s audience read Greek and would hear mōros. It also fails to explain why 5:22 pairs mōre with rhaka, an ordinary insult, suggesting Matthew intends ordinary insults. The genre argument is the most defensible — and it does real work, because the antitheses elsewhere use deliberate overstatement (gouging out an eye, cutting off a hand in 5:29-30) — but conceding hyperbole concedes that 5:22 does not function as a flat prohibition, which is precisely the harmonizer''s point and the skeptic''s: read literally it forbids what Jesus then does. The Christological exception, finally, is unfalsifiable; it can absolve any inconsistency and so explains nothing.

The critical reconstruction, developed by Betz in the Hermeneia Sermon on the Mount and by Luz, is that the two passages were shaped in different rhetorical registers. Matthew 5:22 belongs to the antitheses, where the evangelist radicalizes the sixth commandment by tracing murder back to contempt; Matthew 23 is a woe-discourse reflecting the bitter polemic of the Matthean community after its rupture with formative Judaism, where "fools and blind" is conventional prophetic invective. The redactor did not harmonize them because they served distinct functions. Set alongside the Psalms'' confident use of nabal as a theological label, the canon at face value prohibits the insult, deploys it, and builds doctrine on it.

Verdict: a real intra-Gospel contradiction, mitigated but not dissolved by genre. The harmonizations succeed only by rewriting the Greek, downgrading the Sermon to hyperbole, or pleading a divine exemption.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew, Vol. 1 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 511-516, on Matthew 5:22
Ulrich Luz, Matthew 1-7: A Commentary (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 232-237, on Matthew 5:22
