The positive pole is broad. John 5:29 sorts the resurrected into "those who have done good" (hoi ta agatha poiēsantes) and "those who have done evil," with eschatological consequences; 2 Corinthians 5:10 has each judged for deeds done in the body, "whether good or bad"; 3 John 11 distinguishes the doer of good (who is of God) from the doer of evil. Matthew 19:17 has Jesus tell the rich man, "if you want to enter life, keep the commandments" — presupposing the commandments can be kept. The sharpest internal datum is that Paul himself, one chapter before his catena, writes in Romans 2:6-13 that God "will render to each according to his works" and that "the doers of the law will be justified."

The apologetic harmonization (Moo, Schreiner, Carson) is genuinely strong and turns on a soteriological/moral distinction: Romans 3 denies that anyone achieves the saving righteousness that satisfies God''s holiness apart from Christ, whereas the judgment texts describe morally good works that flow from grace (and, in the Reformed reading, Romans 2 describes either a hypothetical standard no one meets or the Spirit-wrought obedience of the regenerate). This distinction is centuries old and internally coherent; the New Testament does plainly affirm both human moral incapacity-for-self-salvation and a real judgment by works.

The critical objection, advanced since E. P. Sanders, is that the harmonization works only if one reads Paul through Augustine and the Reformers. Sanders argued that Second Temple Judaism was not a religion of works-righteousness, so Paul''s target was not human moral incapacity in general but the boundary-marking function of the law for Gentile inclusion. Stendahl showed that the "introspective conscience of the West" projected Luther''s anguish onto Paul. Dunn''s New Perspective reads Romans 2 as Paul''s own affirmation that doers of the law will be justified — not a hypothetical foil. On these readings the Synoptic, Johannine, and Jacobean traditions never shared Paul''s total-depravity framework to begin with; James 2:14-26 explicitly insists works justify. The contradiction, then, is not one voice contradicting itself so much as the canon preserving distinct anthropologies that later systematics flattened.

Verdict: partially harmonizable — the soteriological/moral distinction relieves much of the tension, but the relief depends on an Augustinian-Reformation reading that critical scholarship contests, and Romans 2 vs Romans 3 leaves a genuine intra-Pauline strain.', 'Joseph A. Fitzmyer, Romans: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary (Anchor Bible 33; Doubleday, 1993), pp. 330-339, on Romans 3:10-18
E. P. Sanders, Paul and Palestinian Judaism: A Comparison of Patterns of Religion (Fortress, 1977), pp. 491-523, on the framework behind Romans 3:12
Krister Stendahl, Paul Among Jews and Gentiles, and Other Essays (Fortress, 1976), pp. 78-96, on Romans 3:12
James D. G. Dunn, Romans 1-8 (WBC 38A; Word Books, 1988), pp. 149-153, on Romans 2:6-13
Robert Jewett, Romans: A Commentary (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 254-264, on Romans 3:10-18
Douglas J. Moo, The Epistle to the Romans (NICNT; Eerdmans, 1996), pp. 200-210, on Romans 2:6-13 and 3:12', 0, NULL, 4, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Psalms, Ecclesiastes, Isaiah, John, Romans, 2 Corinthians, 3 John', 5, 2, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The problem is that the Gospel sayings are not so qualified. Matthew 7:7-8 and Luke 11:9-10 give a categorical promise: aiteite kai dothēsetai hymin — "ask and it will be given you" — capped by the universalizing pas gar ho aitōn lambanei, "for everyone who asks receives." There is no timing clause, no disposition clause. Two chapters after the Lukan form, Luke 13:24 has the same Jesus warn, in the image of the narrow door, that "many will seek to enter and will not be able" — and the following verses (13:25-27) describe latecomers knocking on a shut door and being told "I do not know you," the very scenario Matthew 7:8 seems to exclude. The Hebrew Bible''s lament texts make the tension experiential rather than hortatory. Lamentations 3:8 — "even when I call and cry for help, he shuts out my prayer" — and 3:44, "you have wrapped yourself with a cloud so that no prayer can pass through," are spoken by a sufferer who is seeking in good faith and being met with silence. Adele Berlin and Dobbs-Allsopp read Lamentations 3 as theodicy protest in the tradition of Job, not as a warning against ill-timed prayer. Psalm 18:41 likewise reports enemies who "cried, but there was none to save them; even to the LORD, but he answered them not" — divine non-response as a fact of the world.

The apologetic synthesis (Carson, Blomberg, Wenham) is the "implicit conditions" reading: the unconditional promise assumes good-faith, persistent, repentant petition within God''s will; Luke 13 addresses opportunists who seek too late; Lamentations describes a temporary hiddenness, not a denial. This is theologically coherent and finds support in James 4:3 ("you ask and do not receive, because you ask wrongly") and 1 John 5:14 ("if we ask anything according to his will"). But the move is precisely what every harmonization here performs: it supplies qualifiers the text omits. Matthew 7:8 says "everyone," not "everyone who asks rightly and persistently and in time." The qualifiers are imported from elsewhere in the canon to discipline a saying that, standing alone, overpromises.

That the canon itself contains the lament texts is the decisive datum. Lamentations and Psalm 18:41 show that the experience of unanswered prayer was fully known to Israel''s authors; the unqualified Gospel promise was already, within the corpus, in tension with that lived testimony. Verdict: a real contradiction at face value; harmonizable only by importing conditions the promise-texts do not state, and the lament literature stands as the corpus''s own counter-evidence.', 'Michael V. Fox, Proverbs 1-9: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary (Anchor Bible 18A; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 105-110, 277-279, on Proverbs 1:28 and 8:17
Adele Berlin, Lamentations: A Commentary (Old Testament Library; Westminster John Knox, 2002), pp. 84-95, on Lamentations 3:8 and 3:44
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew, Vol. 1 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 678-688, on Matthew 7:7-8
François Bovon, Luke 2: A Commentary on the Gospel of Luke 9:51-19:27 (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2013), pp. 98-111, 306-318, on Luke 11:9-10 and 13:24
F. W. Dobbs-Allsopp, Lamentations (Interpretation; Westminster John Knox Press, 2002), pp. 111-128, on Lamentations 3:8 and 3:44
Gordon J. Wenham, Psalms as Torah: Reading Biblical Song Ethically (Baker Academic, 2012), pp. 153-176, on Psalm 18:41', 0, NULL, 2, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Psalms, Proverbs, Lamentations, Matthew, Luke', 5, 3, 3, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

Hebrews 8:6-7 argues the opposite valuation. Jesus has obtained "a more excellent ministry" as mediator of a "better covenant" enacted on "better promises," and the author reasons: ei gar he prōtē ekeinē ēn amemptos — "for if that first one had been faultless, no place would have been sought for a second." The logic is comparative and the implication explicit: a second covenant was sought because the first was not amemptos, not faultless. Hebrews 8 then quotes Jeremiah 31:31-34 at length to ground the claim scripturally, reading the prophet''s "new covenant" as proof that the old was, by God''s own testimony, destined for obsolescence (8:13, "what is becoming obsolete and growing old is ready to vanish away"). Attridge and Koester read this as classic Christian supersessionist argumentation built midrashically on Jeremiah.

The apologetic harmonization (Lane, Bruce, Ellingworth) distinguishes the law as moral revelation from the covenant as a salvific-cultic administration. On this reading Psalm 19 praises Torah as a perfect disclosure of God''s will, while Hebrews 8 faults not the law''s moral content but the Sinaitic covenant''s capacity to perfect worshipers and deal finally with sin (cf. Heb 9:9, 10:4). The two could then refer to different things — the law''s intrinsic righteousness versus the covenant''s provisional efficacy — and the apologist can point out that Hebrews 8:7-8 actually locates the fault "with them" (the people) in some manuscripts'' construal, softening the charge against the law itself.

The harmonization founders on the harder texts in Hebrews. Hebrews 7:18-19 states that "there is a setting aside of the former commandment (athetēsis... entolēs) because of its weakness and uselessness (dia to autēs asthenes kai anōpheles), for the law made nothing perfect." Here the object graded as weak and useless is the entolē, the commandment, not merely an administrative arrangement. Hebrews 10:1 adds that "the law has but a shadow (skia) of the good things to come and not the true form (eikōn) of these realities," so it can never perfect those who draw near. The vocabulary — astheneia, anōpheles, skia — predicates deficiency of the law itself, colliding with temimah. Pamela Eisenbaum and James Dunn argue that Hebrews preserves the most aggressive supersessionism in the New Testament, sharper even than Paul (who in Romans 7:12 still calls the law "holy, righteous, and good"). That Hebrews diverges from Paul on the law''s standing underscores that the canon does not speak with one voice here.

Verdict: a genuine theological contradiction. The moral-revelation-versus-cultic-administration distinction is coherent and rescues Hebrews 8 in isolation, but Hebrews 7:18-19 and 10:1 grade the commandment itself as weak and useless, which cannot be reconciled at face value with Psalm 19:7''s temimah without blunting the Greek.', 'Harold W. Attridge, The Epistle to the Hebrews: A Commentary on the Epistle to the Hebrews (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1989), pp. 219-228, on Hebrews 8:6-7
Craig R. Koester, Hebrews: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary (Anchor Bible 36; Doubleday, 2001), pp. 379-392, on Hebrews 8:6-7
Pamela M. Eisenbaum, The Jewish Heroes of Christian History: Hebrews 11 in Literary Context (SBL Dissertation Series 156; Scholars Press, 1997), pp. 89-128, on Hebrews 8:6-7
James D. G. Dunn, The Partings of the Ways Between Christianity and Judaism and Their Significance for the Character of Christianity (2nd ed.; SCM Press, 2006), pp. 86-102, on Hebrews 7:18-19
Paul Ellingworth, The Epistle to the Hebrews: A Commentary on the Greek Text (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 1993), pp. 407-415, on Hebrews 8:6-7
John Goldingay, Psalms, Volume 1: Psalms 1-41 (Baker Commentary on the OT Wisdom and Psalms; Baker Academic, 2006), pp. 283-300, on Psalm 19:7
William L. Lane, Hebrews 1-8 (WBC 47A; Word Books, 1991), pp. 207-216, on Hebrews 8:6-7
F. F. Bruce, The Epistle to the Hebrews (rev. ed.; NICNT; Eerdmans, 1990), pp. 184-196, on Hebrews 8:6-7', 0, NULL, 2, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Psalms, Hebrews', 5, 3, 3, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The reversing pole belongs to the Synoptic-prophetic and Jacobean streams. Matthew 19:23-24 has Jesus declare it "hard" (dyskolōs) for a rich man to enter the kingdom and then deploy the hyperbole of a camel (kamēlos) passing through a needle''s eye — a saying judged authentic across the spectrum of critical reconstruction, including the Jesus Seminar, precisely because it cuts against later church interests. Luke radicalizes the theme into a structural program: the Magnificat already announces that God "has filled the hungry with good things, and the rich he has sent away empty" (Luke 1:53), and the Lukan beatitudes are matched by woes — "woe to you who are rich, for you have received your consolation" (Luke 6:24). Halvor Moxnes and Christopher Hays document this Lukan reversal theology as an eschatological inversion of social position, not a warning about wealth''s misuse. James 5:1-6 stands squarely in the prophetic line of Amos 8 and Micah 2, ordering the rich to "weep and howl" for coming miseries, indicting them as defrauders of laborers — wealth as a status of guilt.

The most sustained apologetic harmonization is Blomberg''s Neither Poverty nor Riches, which argues a canonical via media: possessions are a blessing when received with gratitude and deployed generously, and a peril when they become idolatrous or oppressive. This third position has genuine textual anchors — 1 Timothy 6:17-19 commands the rich "to be rich in good works" rather than to divest, and Abraham, Job, and the Lukan benefactors are wealthy and approved. As a constructive biblical theology it is defensible.

Its weakness is that it is a synthetic third position rather than a reading either pole would recognize. Proverbs 112 and 15:6 do not say wealth is dangerous-when-misused; they say wealth attends righteousness. Luke 6:24 does not say riches are acceptable-when-shared; it pronounces woe on the rich as such, with their wealth itself constituting their already-received consolation. The harmonization smooths the contradiction by adding a qualifier — "depending on how it is used" — that neither source supplies. Walter Brueggemann frames the deeper point: the canon preserves a sustained internal contest between a royal-establishment theology that sacralizes prosperity and a prophetic-apocalyptic theology that condemns accumulation, and that contest is constitutive of the literature rather than a skeptic''s misreading. The traditions answered the question differently because they arose in different social locations and theological moments.

Verdict: a genuine, face-value contradiction; harmonizable only by constructing a mediating ethic that neither the prosperity texts nor the woe texts actually state, and arguably the most consequential social-theological tension in the Bible.', 'Klaus Koch, "Gibt es ein Vergeltungsdogma im Alten Testament?", Zeitschrift für Theologie und Kirche 52, no. 1 (1955), pp. 1-42, on the act-consequence framework behind Psalm 112 and Proverbs 13:22
