Peter H. Davids, The Epistle of James (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 1982), pp. 174-183, on James 5:1
Craig L. Blomberg, Neither Poverty nor Riches: A Biblical Theology of Possessions (New Studies in Biblical Theology 7; InterVarsity, 1999), pp. 84-145, on Matthew 19:23-24 and Luke 6:24', 0, NULL, 4, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Psalms, Proverbs, Matthew, Luke, James', 5, 3, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The strongest defense of the imprecatory tradition is Erich Zenger''s, which reads these psalms as protest theology spoken from positions of powerlessness — cries that hand vengeance over to God rather than enacting it — not as ethical blueprints. Bonhoeffer, Wenham, and Daniel Estes develop a related harmonization: the imprecatory psalmist petitions God to act and reads 58:10 as eschatological vindication of justice rather than vindictive personal pleasure. Proverbs 24:17-18, by contrast, is wisdom paraenesis, and its motive clause is telling: "lest the LORD see it and be displeased, and turn away his anger from him" — the gloater risks redirecting Yahweh''s wrath onto himself. This forward-looks to Matthew 5:43-48 and to Romans 12:17-21, where Paul quotes Proverbs 25:21-22 ("heap coals of fire") and Deuteronomy 32:35 ("vengeance is mine") to ground the renunciation of personal retaliation. The genre distinction is real and does ease the surface clash: a prohibition against personal gloating and a prayer ceding judgment to God need not be flatly equivalent claims.

Yet the harmonization strains at the specific verse cited. Psalm 58:10 is not petition; it is anticipated participation. The righteous one''s feet are in the blood. Hossfeld and Zenger''s Hermeneia commentary concedes the imagery is difficult and notes the psalm assumes a worldview in which divine justice is visibly executed and shared in by the righteous community — a vantage Proverbs 24 explicitly forbids the individual to relish. Reading 58:10 as purely eschatological also imports a developed afterlife-vindication scheme the psalm does not supply; the joy is at concrete, witnessed slaughter (ki-chazah, "because he has seen"). Text-critically the verse is stable across the Masoretic tradition and the Septuagint (which renders the bathing image with niptō/plynō, washing in blood), so the difficulty cannot be dissolved as scribal corruption.

The critical verdict, articulated by Walter Brueggemann and David Firth, is that the imprecatory psalms preserve an ethic the rest of the canon — most sharply the New Testament — actively rejects. Michael Fox''s Anchor Yale commentary on Proverbs underscores that 24:17 reflects the sapiential conviction that schadenfreude is itself a moral failure, independent of the enemy''s guilt. What stands in tension is therefore not a single proposition stated two ways but two distinct ethical traditions lodged in one Scripture: a cultic-imprecatory voice that celebrates witnessed retribution and a wisdom voice that prohibits the very gladness the psalmist enacts. The genre move harmonizes them as different speech acts, but only by granting that they prescribe opposite affective responses. Verdict: real and theologically significant; partially harmonizable by genre, but the harmonization works only by softening Psalm 58:10 past its plain, participatory sense.', 'Erich Zenger, A God of Vengeance? Understanding the Psalms of Divine Wrath, trans. Linda M. Maloney (Westminster John Knox, 1996), pp. 13-66, on the imprecatory psalms as protest theology
Frank-Lothar Hossfeld and Erich Zenger, Psalms 2: A Commentary on Psalms 51-100 (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2005), pp. 77-83, on Psalm 58:10
David G. Firth, Surrendering Retribution in the Psalms: Responses to Violence in Individual Complaints (Paternoster Biblical Monographs; Paternoster, 2005), pp. 17-44
Walter Brueggemann, Praying the Psalms: Engaging Scripture and the Life of the Spirit, 2nd ed. (Cascade Books, 2007), pp. 51-72
Michael V. Fox, Proverbs 10-31: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary (Anchor Yale Bible 18B; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 745-748, on Proverbs 24:17
Dietrich Bonhoeffer, Psalms: The Prayer Book of the Bible, trans. James H. Burtness (Augsburg, 1970), pp. 56-60
Gordon J. Wenham, Psalms as Torah: Reading Biblical Song Ethically (Baker Academic, 2012), pp. 167-176
Daniel J. Estes, Handbook on the Wisdom Books and Psalms (Baker Academic, 2010), pp. 152-159', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Psalms, Proverbs', 6, 2, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The strongest harmonization is the convergence of New Perspective and confessional-Reformed exegesis (Dunn, Wright, Stendahl on one side; Schreiner, Moo, Carson, Blomberg on the other). On this reading Paul''s polemical target, erga nomou, is the law observed as Jewish covenant boundary markers — circumcision, food laws, Sabbath, the badges that marked covenant membership — which he denies as the ground of justification; James''s target is a barren intellectual assent (cf. 2:19, "even the demons believe") that produces no ethical fruit. Paul''s "faith," they note, is never inert: it "works through love" (Galatians 5:6), and Paul expects the "obedience of faith" (Romans 1:5; 16:26). James''s "works" are precisely the fruit of living faith, not meritorious law-keeping. Stated this way, the two writers address different questions — Paul: on what basis is one reckoned righteous? James: what kind of faith actually saves? — and the contradiction substantially dissolves at the conceptual level.

But the synthesis carries real costs. Stephen Westerholm has argued forcefully that erga nomou in Paul most naturally means works done in obedience to the law in general, not merely ethnic boundary markers, which narrows Paul''s denial more than the Greek warrants. James''s pistis monon must likewise be read as something other than what the words say. And the sharpest pressure comes from within Paul''s own letter: Romans 2:6 affirms God "will repay each according to his works" (kata ta erga autou), and Romans 2:13 states that "the doers of the law will be justified" (hoi po?tai nomou dikaiōthēsontai) — the same verb Paul negates of works in 3:28. Paul does not stop to reconcile these within Romans, and critics (Sanders, Räisänen) have read the juxtaposition as unresolved tension rather than a single coherent system. Beyond Paul, Matthew and Revelation operate within an apocalyptic judgment-by-works framework: the sheep and goats are sorted by what they did or failed to do (Matthew 25:31-46), and the dead are judged kata ta erga autōn, "according to their works" (Revelation 20:12-13). One textual note compounds the difficulty: Mark 16:16, cited on the faith-alone side, belongs to the longer ending of Mark, absent from Codex Sinaiticus and Vaticanus and widely regarded as a secondary second-century addition (so the apparatus of NA28 and Metzger''s Textual Commentary), so it carries little weight as Markan soteriology.

John Barclay''s Paul and the Gift reframes the debate decisively: Paul''s grace is "unconditioned" (given without regard to the recipient''s prior worth) but not "unconditional" (it expects a transformed, obedient life), which is why Paul can both deny justification by works and affirm judgment according to deeds without, in his own mind, contradiction. The critical conclusion since E. P. Sanders is that the New Testament preserves multiple soteriologies that do not flatten into Reformation faith-alone-ism without distortion. Verdict: the contradiction is real at the verbal level and partially harmonizable conceptually, but the harmonization succeeds only by importing categorical distinctions (boundary-markers vs. ethical works; unconditioned vs. unconditional grace) that the texts themselves never spell out.', 'E. P. Sanders, Paul and Palestinian Judaism: A Comparison of Patterns of Religion (Fortress, 1977), pp. 491-523
