Isaiah 57:1 inhabits an entirely different register: ha-tzaddiq avad v''ein ish sam al-lev, "the righteous one perishes, and no one takes it to heart," with v. 2 adding that he "enters into peace; they rest on their beds." The passage belongs to what scholars since Bernhard Duhm have called Third Isaiah (Isaiah 56-66), the post-exilic prophetic material wrestling with the gap between Second Isaiah''s soaring restoration promises and the disappointing reality of the returned community. Joseph Blenkinsopp''s Anchor Bible commentary reads 57:1-2 as a lament that the righteous die prematurely and unnoticed — possibly, on some readings (Goldingay), even a reflection on the death of the faithful amid communal corruption — a direct empirical refutation of the retribution principle Psalm 92 assumes. Where the psalmist sees the righteous as cedars, the prophet sees them perishing while the community shrugs.

The standard apologetic harmonization (Motyer, Oswalt) reads Isaiah 57:1-2 eschatologically: the righteous "enter into peace," so their perishing is in fact a deliverance and their true flourishing lies beyond death; Psalm 92 is then read as describing ultimate vindication rather than guaranteed earthly prosperity. This is precisely the synthesis later rabbinic and Christian traditions adopted, and Isaiah 57:2''s "they rest on their beds" lends it some footing. But the reading is anachronistic for Psalm 92. As Jon Levenson and James Crenshaw document, the Hebrew Bible has no developed doctrine of beatific personal afterlife reward until late texts — Daniel 12:2 being the first unambiguous resurrection-to-reward statement, datable to the Maccabean crisis of the 160s BCE. Psalm 92 offers no such horizon; its flourishing is the palm and cedar of this life, fruit-bearing in old age (v. 14), not a postmortem consolation. Importing eschatology to rescue the psalm reads later theology back into an earlier text.

Brueggemann''s threefold typology clarifies the structure of the disagreement: psalms of orientation (retribution confidence), disorientation (lament and protest), and new orientation (vindication through suffering). Psalm 92 sits squarely in orientation; Isaiah 57:1 in disorientation. Both are canonical, both reflect genuine Israelite theological positions, and they are not the same claim phrased differently. The genre/eschatology harmonization succeeds at the level of canonical synthesis — later readers could and did integrate them — but concedes that the two texts make incompatible empirical claims about the fate of the righteous in this world. Verdict: a real tension between retribution theology and post-exilic realist lament; partially harmonizable only by importing an eschatology Psalm 92 does not possess.', 'Joseph Blenkinsopp, Isaiah 56-66: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary (Anchor Bible 19B; Doubleday, 2003), pp. 153-160, on Isaiah 57:1-2
Frank-Lothar Hossfeld and Erich Zenger, Psalms 2: A Commentary on Psalms 51-100 (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2005), pp. 434-445, on Psalm 92
