The standard harmonization reads Psalm 44:23 as rhetorical and Psalm 121:4 as ontological: the lamenter knows perfectly well that God does not literally sleep and deploys provocative "wake up" language to spur divine intervention. This is correct and is the reading shared across the critical commentary tradition — Hossfeld and Zenger, Goldingay, Brueggemann, and Dahood all recognize the lament''s right to theologically aggressive address. The imperative urah ("awake!") is a conventional petition idiom (cf. Psalm 7:6; 35:23; 59:4-5), a way of demanding that God act as though roused, not a doctrinal assertion that he was asleep. The complaint genre licenses speech that the affirmation genre does not, and on that basis the surface contradiction genuinely dissolves: the two psalms perform different speech acts — confession versus protest — rather than asserting contradictory propositions.

There is, however, a real comparative-religion residue worth flagging. The verb yashen (sleep) negated of Yahweh in Psalm 121:4 is precisely the charge Elijah hurls at Baal in 1 Kings 18:27, where he taunts the prophets that their god may be musing, on a journey, or asleep (yashen) and must be awakened — a deliberate polemic against a deity who can be caught napping. In the Ugaritic Baal cycle the storm-god dies and is revived, and divine sleep/inactivity is a live mythological category; Israelite authors knew that a sleeping god was a contemptible god, which is exactly why Psalm 121 negates the predicate so emphatically. That Psalm 44:23 can nonetheless summon Yahweh to "wake up" suggests its worldview did not absolutize the never-sleeps doctrine in the way the later, doctrinally tidy Psalm 121 does. Mark Smith and Frank Cross have argued that earlier Israelite religion was more at ease with anthropomorphic divine limitation than later monotheizing systematization permitted, and the lament idiom may preserve a faint trace of that earlier comfort even as it is domesticated into rhetoric.

The contradiction is therefore real at the surface but soft, and it harmonizes cleanly through genre analysis: lament language assumes God can be experienced (or addressed) as if sleeping, while didactic language insists he never does. The canon preserves both because they answer different needs — reassurance in Psalm 121, expostulation in Psalm 44. The harmonization holds, but it works precisely by conceding that the two texts are doing different things with the verb "sleep," not asserting equivalent claims. Verdict: surface contradiction, genuinely harmonizable through genre and speech-act analysis, with a theological residue showing how an earlier Israelite tolerance for limited divine agency was smoothed over in the later doctrinal psalm.', 'Frank-Lothar Hossfeld and Erich Zenger, Psalms 3: A Commentary on Psalms 101-150 (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2011), pp. 315-331, on Psalm 121
John Goldingay, Psalms, Volume 2: Psalms 42-89 (Baker Commentary on the Old Testament Wisdom and Psalms; Baker Academic, 2007), pp. 41-55, on Psalm 44
Mark S. Smith, The Early History of God: Yahweh and the Other Deities in Ancient Israel, 2nd ed. (Eerdmans, 2002), pp. 73-77
Frank Moore Cross, Canaanite Myth and Hebrew Epic: Essays in the History of the Religion of Israel (Harvard University Press, 1973), pp. 145-194
Mordechai Cogan, I Kings: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary (Anchor Bible 10; Doubleday, 2001), pp. 442-443, on 1 Kings 18:27
Walter Brueggemann and William H. Bellinger Jr., Psalms (New Cambridge Bible Commentary; Cambridge University Press, 2014), pp. 213-219, 529-532, on Psalms 44 and 121', 0, NULL, 2, 'OT_internal', 'Psalms', 5, 2, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The strongest apologetic move (Wanamaker in the NIGTC, Bruce in the WBC) parses the genitive pseudous ("of falsehood") as the operative qualifier and reads the phrase as either counterfeit wonders that merely appear miraculous or genuine displays whose falseness lies in the message they serve. The first reading would neutralize the contradiction outright: if Satan''s wonders are fake, then only God performs real ones. But the syntax and the parallels resist that reading. The single genitive pseudous most naturally governs the whole triad and characterizes the wonders'' purpose — they are wonders in the service of the Lie — rather than denying that anything actually happens. The closest parallel, Revelation 13:13-14, says the beast "performs great signs (sēmeia megala), even making fire come down from heaven before people," and "deceives" by means of "the signs it was permitted to perform" — the deception works precisely because the signs are real. Abraham Malherbe''s Anchor Bible commentary and most critical interpreters accordingly take pseudous as modifying the deceptive function, not the ontological reality, of the wonders.

Decisively, the Hebrew Bible itself already destabilizes the levaddo claim before 2 Thessalonians enters the picture. In Exodus 7:11-12 and 7:22 and 8:7 (Hebrew 8:3) the chartummim, Pharaoh''s magicians, replicate Aaron''s serpent and the first two plagues b''lateihem, "by their secret arts" — the narrative presents these as actual counter-wonders, not illusions, even as Aaron''s staff swallows theirs. Deuteronomy 13:1-3 (Hebrew 13:2-4) legislates for the case of a prophet or dreamer who gives an ot o mofet — a "sign or wonder" — that actually comes to pass yet summons Israel to other gods; the law assumes such figures can perform genuine, fulfilled signs, and tests them by doctrine rather than by efficacy. The point of Psalm 136''s l''vaddo is thus doxological: it celebrates Yahweh''s incomparability and his role as sole author of the redemptive wonders of creation and exodus catalogued in the psalm, not a metaphysical decree that no created or hostile agent can produce supernatural phenomena. Hebrew praise routinely uses "alone" and "none besides" as confessional superlatives (cf. Isaiah 45:5-6), not analytic propositions.

The apologetic conclusion — that only God''s wonders are salvific, authentic, or ultimately attributable to him — is essentially sound as a theological reading, but it is itself a harmonization that supplies qualifiers the psalmist never states. Psalm 136 says "alone"; Exodus, Deuteronomy, Revelation, and 2 Thessalonians each present other agents performing real signs. The contradiction is therefore real at the level of unqualified diction and dissolves only once one grants that levaddo is praise-language rather than a claim about the distribution of supernatural causal power. Verdict: real surface contradiction, partially harmonizable by genre — and notably, the Hebrew Bible''s own narrative texts complicate the ''alone'' of Psalm 136 long before the New Testament does.', 'Abraham J. Malherbe, The Letters to the Thessalonians: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary (Anchor Bible 32B; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 421-426, on 2 Thessalonians 2:9
Charles A. Wanamaker, The Epistles to the Thessalonians: A Commentary on the Greek Text (New International Greek Testament Commentary; Eerdmans, 1990), pp. 256-260, on 2 Thessalonians 2:9
F. F. Bruce, 1 and 2 Thessalonians (Word Biblical Commentary 45; Word Books, 1982), pp. 169-173, on 2 Thessalonians 2:9
Frank-Lothar Hossfeld and Erich Zenger, Psalms 3: A Commentary on Psalms 101-150 (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2011), pp. 502-510, on Psalm 136
William H. C. Propp, Exodus 1-18: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary (Anchor Bible 2; Doubleday, 1999), pp. 322-330, on Exodus 7:11-12
Jeffrey H. Tigay, Deuteronomy (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1996), pp. 128-130, on Deuteronomy 13:1-3
David E. Aune, Revelation 6-16 (Word Biblical Commentary 52B; Thomas Nelson, 1998), pp. 762-766, on Revelation 13:13-14', 0, NULL, 2, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Psalms, 2 Thessalonians', 5, 3, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The psalmist''s claim is absolute and is meant to be. Psalm 143 is the last of the seven traditional penitential psalms, and verse 2 grounds a petition: do not bring your servant into judgment, because under strict adjudication no one stands. Hossfeld and Zenger read this as a categorical confession of human creaturely unrighteousness before the holy God, not a rhetorical exaggeration. Crucially, Paul seized on exactly this verse. Romans 3:20 — "by works of the law no flesh will be justified (ou dikaiōthēsetai pasa sarx) before him" — is a deliberate echo of LXX Psalm 142:2 (the Septuagint numbering), expanded by the insertion of "by works of the law." Paul thus reads the psalm as the scriptural proof of universal unrighteousness, the linchpin of his argument in Romans 1-3. So one canonical voice (Psalm/Paul) makes non-justification a universal anthropological fact.

The standard harmonization is the Reformation two-aspects move: Psalm 143:2 denies justification by intrinsic merit, while Matthew 12:37 describes a forensic verdict grounded either in Christ''s imputed righteousness or in repentance-evidenced faith. France, Davies-Allison, and Luz all locate Matthew 12:37 in an eschatological-judgment frame, where words function as evidence of the heart''s true condition. On this reading no one is righteous by works, but words proceeding from a regenerate heart are vindicated at the assize. Westerholm''s careful study of the Pauline justification debate supplies the systematic scaffolding for keeping forensic acquittal and intrinsic unrighteousness in the same frame.

The difficulty is that this scaffolding is Pauline, not Matthean, and Matthew 12:37 is doing something else entirely in its own context. Matthew 12:36-37 reads: "every careless word people speak — they will give account for it on the day of judgment, for by your words you will be justified and by your words you will be condemned." This is judgment-by-deeds language — specifically judgment-by-speech — structurally parallel to the sheep-and-goats scene of Matthew 25:31-46, where eschatological destiny tracks concrete acts of mercy, and to Matthew 7:21-23 and 16:27, where reward follows works. There is no imputation, no forensic transfer, no faith-versus-works dialectic anywhere in the pericope. Luz and Allison both argue that Matthean soteriology operates on a structure resembling the "covenantal nomism" E. P. Sanders identified in Second Temple Judaism — election by grace, retention by obedience — rather than on a Pauline forensic scheme. The first evangelist genuinely thinks some people will be justified, on the basis of what comes out of their mouths.

The tension is not merely cross-testamental; the Hebrew Bible already houses both positions. On the no-one-is-righteous side stand Job 9:2 ("how can a mortal be just before God?"), Ecclesiastes 7:20 ("there is no one on earth who does good and never sins"), 1 Kings 8:46, and Psalm 143:2 itself. On the some-are-righteous side stand Genesis 18:23-25, where Abraham bargains for the righteous of Sodom on the presupposition that such people exist; Ezekiel 18, where anyone who turns and does righteousness shall live; and the entire wisdom celebration of the tsaddiq as a real and attainable category. These are not the same theology with different emphases; they are competing assessments of the human standing before God.

The imputed-righteousness harmonization is coherent as systematic theology and may even be true as theology. But it is not exegesis of Matthew 12:37, which uses justification language in a works-evidentiary sense the harmonization must override. Verdict: a genuine contradiction between two biblical soteriological strands, resolvable only by importing a Pauline framework Matthew does not share.', 'Frank-Lothar Hossfeld and Erich Zenger, *Psalms 3: A Commentary on Psalms 101-150* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2011), pp. 569-579, on Psalm 143:2
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 8-20: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2001), pp. 207-212, on Matthew 12:37
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 2 (International Critical Commentary; T&T Clark, 1991), pp. 351-356, on Matthew 12:36-37
R. T. France, *The Gospel of Matthew* (New International Commentary on the New Testament; Eerdmans, 2007), pp. 482-485, on Matthew 12:37
E. P. Sanders, *Paul and Palestinian Judaism: A Comparison of Patterns of Religion* (Fortress, 1977), pp. 419-428, on covenantal nomism
Stephen Westerholm, *Justification Reconsidered: Rethinking a Pauline Theme* (Eerdmans, 2013), pp. 41-72, on Romans 3:20''s citation of Psalm 143:2
Dale C. Allison, *The New Moses: A Matthean Typology* (Fortress, 1993), pp. 192-208, on Matthew''s works-oriented eschatology', 0, NULL, 4, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Psalms, Matthew', 5, 3, 3, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The negative side is textually narrower than it first appears. Proverbs 3:5 — al-tisha''en el-binatekha, "do not lean on your own understanding" — sits two columns from Proverbs 2:2 in the same composition. Michael Fox argues the proverb does not forbid reasoning but contrasts self-sufficient autonomy with trust in Yahweh; the antithetic parallelism ("trust in the LORD with all your heart" / "do not lean on your own understanding") frames the issue as where one''s ultimate confidence rests, not whether one thinks at all. The verb sha''an connotes leaning one''s full weight on a support, so the prohibition is against making one''s own intellect the load-bearing foundation, not against using it. Likewise 2 Corinthians 10:5 is heavily context-bound: Paul is dismantling specific rhetorical and philosophical arguments raised against his gospel by Corinthian opponents. The "thoughts" he captures are logismoi — calculations, reasonings, polemical arguments — not human cognition as such. Thrall''s ICC and Furnish''s Anchor Bible volume both read the military metaphor (demolishing strongholds, taking captives) as aimed at the opposition''s discourse, not at the believer''s faculty of reason.

The harmonization is therefore partly sound and should be granted its due. Read in context, the wisdom texts commend inquiry conducted under the canopy of trust in Yahweh, and the Pauline submission texts target specific oppositional arguments rather than human thinking per se. An honest reader cannot claim Proverbs 3:5 forbids all reasoning, nor that 2 Corinthians 10:5 demands intellectual self-lobotomy.

Yet the harmonization papers over a real divergence in the canon''s deeper assumptions. The wisdom tradition assumes the human capacity for inquiry yields reliable knowledge: Qohelet investigates and reaches conclusions on his own authority, sometimes in tension with received orthodoxy, and the canon preserves his dissent. The later New Testament increasingly subordinates reasoning to revelation and apostolic authority. The Pastoral Epistles — held by most critical scholars to be deutero-Pauline, written in Paul''s name a generation or more after his death — push hardest in this direction: 1 Timothy 6:3-4 and 2 Timothy 4:3 turn on "sound doctrine" (hygiainousa didaskalia) versus those who "will not endure" it, reframing the intellectual task as adherence to a deposited body of teaching (the parathēkē of 1 Timothy 6:20) rather than open investigation. The trajectory runs from Qohelet''s autonomous searching toward institutional gatekeeping of orthodoxy. No single verse forbids thinking, but the canon''s center of gravity shifts measurably from inquiry to conformity.

Verdict: the surface contradiction is largely harmonizable through context and parallelism, but the underlying trajectory — from wisdom-tradition inquiry to apostolic-authority submission — is real, ideologically significant, and not dissolved by the contextual readings.', 'Michael V. Fox, *Proverbs 1-9: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Bible 18A; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 145-150, on Proverbs 3:5
C. L. Seow, *Ecclesiastes: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Bible 18C; Doubleday, 1997), pp. 263-272, on Ecclesiastes 7:25
Margaret E. Thrall, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Second Epistle to the Corinthians*, Vol. 2 (International Critical Commentary; T&T Clark, 2000), ad loc. 2 Corinthians 10:5
Victor Paul Furnish, *II Corinthians: Translated with Introduction, Notes, and Commentary* (Anchor Bible 32A; Doubleday, 1984), pp. 458-466, on 2 Corinthians 10:5
Abraham J. Malherbe, *The Letters to the Thessalonians: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Bible 32B; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 327-335, on 1 Thessalonians 5:21
James L. Crenshaw, *Old Testament Wisdom: An Introduction*, 3rd ed. (Westminster John Knox, 2010), pp. 117-138, on the wisdom tradition''s epistemics
Raymond E. Brown, *An Introduction to the New Testament* (Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, 1997), pp. 662-668, on the deutero-Pauline Pastorals', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Proverbs, Ecclesiastes, 2 Corinthians, 1 Thessalonians', 6, 2, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

Ecclesiastes is what results when a wisdom teacher applies the Proverbs method with rigor and arrives at the opposite finding. Qohelet''s opening experiment (1:12-18) is framed as empirical: he gave his heart to seek out by wisdom all that is done under heaven, and the verdict is that increased wisdom yields increased ka''as (vexation, grief) and mak''ov (pain, sorrow). The conclusion is governed by the book''s organizing term hevel — literally "vapor" or "breath," connoting the insubstantial, fleeting, and ungraspable; Fox in A Time to Tear Down argues it is best rendered "absurd," the gap between expectation and outcome. Seow''s Anchor Bible commentary reads 1:18 as Qohelet''s deliberate counter to Proverbs-style optimism: the very faculty Proverbs promises will make you happy is the faculty that exposes life''s futility, since the wise person sees clearly what the fool is spared.

The standard harmonization (Estes, Kidner) distinguishes two kinds of wisdom: a merely human or "under the sun" wisdom that breeds grief, versus the fear-of-Yahweh wisdom of Proverbs that yields true happiness. Some go further and treat the epilogue, Ecclesiastes 12:13-14 ("fear God and keep his commandments"), as the editorial frame that domesticates Qohelet''s pessimism back into Proverbs orthodoxy. This deserves a fair hearing: there genuinely are two voices in the book, and the epilogist (the "frame narrator" of 1:1-2 and 12:8-14) does speak about Qohelet in the third person, signaling an editorial layer. The structural observation is sound.

But the harmonization fails as a reading of the book''s argument. Longman''s NICOT commentary — itself an evangelical work — is candid that the epilogue does not overrule the body; at most it offers a pious editorial coda that frames Qohelet''s words without retracting them, and 12:13-14 endorses Qohelet ("the words of the wise are like goads") rather than correcting him. The "two kinds of wisdom" move is also anachronistic: Qohelet uses the same word, hokmah, that Proverbs uses, and his complaint is precisely that this wisdom — the real thing, not a counterfeit — fails to deliver the promised satisfaction. He is not contrasting good wisdom with bad; he is reporting that wisdom as such did not produce the Proverbs payoff. The contradiction is intentional. Qohelet is doing to Proverbs what Job does to the retribution theology of Job''s friends, and what the lament psalms do to the confident piety of the hymns: interrogating the tradition from inside it. The canon preserves both the thesis and the dissent without harmonizing them, which is itself a theologically significant editorial decision.

Verdict: a real and deliberate contradiction within the wisdom canon; attempts to subordinate Ecclesiastes to Proverbs force one book''s project onto the other against the texts'' own aims.', 'C. L. Seow, *Ecclesiastes: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Bible 18C; Doubleday, 1997), pp. 124-131, on Ecclesiastes 1:18
Michael V. Fox, *Proverbs 1-9: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Bible 18A; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 156-159, on Proverbs 3:13
