Anne Marie Kitz, "The Plural Form of ''Ûrîm and Tummîm," *Journal of Biblical Literature* 116, no. 3 (1997): 401-410, on the Urim and Thummim
John Barton, *Oracles of God: Perceptions of Ancient Prophecy in Israel after the Exile* (Oxford University Press, 1986), pp. 121-128, on Israelite divinatory practice', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Numbers, Joshua, Proverbs', 6, 3, 1, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

Habakkuk is the prophetic voice that refuses the generalization on the basis of lived experience. The book opens with a complaint (1:2-4) that the law is paralyzed and justice never prevails because the wicked surround the righteous, then sharpens in 1:13 into a direct challenge: "why do you look on the treacherous and stay silent when the wicked swallows the one more righteous than he?" Andersen''s Anchor Bible commentary and Roberts''s OTL volume both read the book as a sustained interrogation of theodicy, voiced from inside the experience of Babylonian-era injustice. Crucially, Habakkuk does not receive the Proverbs answer. The reply in 2:4 — "the righteous shall live by his faithfulness (be''emunato)" — is not a denial that harm comes to the righteous but a counsel of endurance and trust amid harm, an eschatological deferral rather than a guarantee of immunity. Crenshaw''s study of biblical theodicy situates Habakkuk among the texts that concede the retribution principle does not hold in observable experience.

The New Testament inherits both strands and visibly strains to hold them together. 1 Peter 3:13 asks rhetorically, "who is there to harm you if you are zealous for what is good?" — but the very next verse retreats: "but even if you should suffer for righteousness'' sake, you are blessed" (3:14). Achtemeier''s Hermeneia commentary observes that the author, writing to communities actually facing hostility, qualifies the optimistic rhetorical question almost as soon as he poses it; the rhetorical "no one" of 3:13 is immediately bracketed by the realistic "even if" of 3:14. Hebrews 12:6 takes the opposite tack and quotes Proverbs 3:11-12 (via the Septuagint) to affirm that the Lord disciplines (paideuei) and scourges every son he receives — that is, suffering does come to the righteous, and is reframed as fatherly correction rather than as the absence-of-harm Proverbs promised. Lane''s and Attridge''s commentaries note that Hebrews thereby transforms suffering from a sign of divine disfavor into a sign of sonship.

The harmonization apologists favor — using Hebrews 12 as the integrating lens (so Schreiner on 1 Peter, Lane on Hebrews) — is real and is in fact attempted by Hebrews itself. But it works by conceding that Proverbs 12:21 is false as stated. Proverbs said no harm befalls the righteous; Hebrews says harm does befall them but is repurposed as discipline. Those are not the same proposition, and the move from one to the other is a theological retreat, not a reconciliation. The same tension surfaces wherever the Hebrew Bible confronts experience honestly: Job dismantles the friends'' retribution theology, and Psalm 73 confesses that the psalmist nearly lost his footing watching the wicked prosper while the righteous suffered, resolving the crisis only by relocating justice beyond present experience. The canon does not so much harmonize these voices as preserve the argument among them.

Verdict: a genuine contradiction. The biblical authors disagree over whether the righteous are shielded from harm, and the New Testament partly concedes the point by recasting harm-to-the-righteous as divine discipline — which abandons the original Proverbs claim rather than reconciling it.', 'Michael V. Fox, *Proverbs 10-31: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Yale Bible 18B; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 558-559, 657-658, on Proverbs 12:21 and 19:23
