The New Testament ransom theology has nothing to do with either Proverbs verse. Mark 10:45 and its Matthean redaction (Matt 20:28) use lytron anti pollōn — "a ransom in place of many." Joel Marcus documents the Greco-Roman manumission background of lytron as the price paid to free a slave, and the standard apologetic step is to read the saying against Isaiah 53''s suffering servant who pours out his life for the many. On any reading, the ransom here is Jesus — neither the wicked nor riches — and it is paid by God-in-Christ rather than collected from sinners. The pastoral stratum universalizes the claim: 1 Timothy 2:6 reads antilytron hyper pantōn, "a ransom for all." The entry''s evidence cites 2 Timothy 2:6, which contains no ransom statement at all; this is a scrape-side miscite for 1 Timothy 2:6 and is worth correcting in the database. The substantive point survives the correction: the deutero-Pauline pastorals make the ransom universal in scope, paid by the deity, not a transaction among the morally sorted.

The fourth answer dismantles the premise of the question. Job 15:14, Isaiah 64:6, and the catena of Romans 3:10-18 deny that there are any righteous to be ransomed at all. Douglas Moo notes that Paul''s chain of citations in Romans 3 is precisely engineered to dissolve the wisdom tradition''s clean ṣaddîq/rāšāʿ binary — the rhetorical work of the passage is to leave no one standing in the "righteous" column, which is the foundation for justification by faith. If no one is righteous, Proverbs 21:18''s transaction has no beneficiary; the economy collapses for want of a payee.

The harmonizing reply appeals to "different senses of righteous" or "different covenantal dispensations." But the four texts do not merely use a word differently; they operate within four incompatible economies of guilt-transfer. Proverbs works a retributive deed-consequence frame; the Synoptics an apocalyptic-substitutionary frame indebted to the servant songs; Paul a forensic-imputation frame in Romans; and the no-one-is-righteous strand a frame in which desert is universally negated. Martin Hengel''s study of the atonement traces how the NT lytron language emerged from a fusion of Jewish martyrology and Hellenistic ransom-redemption vocabulary — a development with no organic connection to the Proverbial maxims. These are not complementary vantage points on one doctrine but four answers generated by four different theologies of how innocence and guilt are balanced. Any one is coherent; the set is not. Verdict: genuine contradiction (with a verse-reference error to fix).', 'Bruce K. Waltke, *The Book of Proverbs: Chapters 15-31* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 2005), pp. 180-184, on Proverbs 21:18.
Michael V. Fox, *Proverbs 10-31: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AYB 18B; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 564-567, 688-690, on Proverbs 13:8.
