What it cannot do is eliminate the residual tension, because the verse remains as ambiguous as it sounds, and the reception history shows that the "credulous" reading was the default for centuries. The Vulgate''s omnia credit and the KJV''s "believeth all things" were read devotionally as praise of trustfulness toward all. Hans Conzelmann notes the deliberate hyperbole of the stanza and the resulting friction with Paul''s own demand elsewhere for the discernment of spirits (1 Cor 12:10; 14:29). Even granting the encomiastic reading, the poem still commends maximal trust as love''s instinct, set against a prophetic-wisdom stream that treats maximal trust as the road to being defrauded.

The other pole is sharply worded. Proverbs 14:15 frames the issue in wisdom-tradition terms: the petî — the gullible, morally immature simpleton — "believes every word," while the prudent "considers his steps." Michael Fox reads the petî not as merely naive but as a recognized character type whose credulity is a moral deficiency, the opposite of the disciplined ʿārûm. Proverbs 26:25 sharpens it to active suspicion: "when he speaks graciously, do not believe him, for seven abominations are in his heart." The same ethic appears in Pauline-community discourse at 1 Thessalonians 5:21 — panta dokimazete, "test all things" — which Abraham Malherbe situates within the regulation of charismatic prophecy in the assembly. Most pointed of all, 1 John 4:1''s mē panti pneumati pisteuete — "do not believe every spirit, but test the spirits" — is verbatim the negation of the Corinthian clause. Raymond Brown reads it as a direct response to the secessionist prophets of the Johannine schism: community discipline enforced through institutionalized skepticism, written by a different hand a generation or more after Paul.

So the picture is not a flat self-contradiction but a real divergence of stance softened by genre. The discernment texts are concrete instruction about whom and what to trust; the Corinthian clause is poetry about love''s generosity. Where the harmonization runs out is that even properly construed, Paul''s hymn makes trust the default and the wisdom-prophetic stream makes testing the default — and 1 John 4:1, written into the same canon, explicitly forbids believing "every spirit." The New Testament does not finally speak with one voice on whether the believer''s first move is to trust or to verify. Verdict: overstated as a flat contradiction (the encomium is not an epistemology), but a genuine and unresolved difference of ethical stance remains.', 'Anthony C. Thiselton, *The First Epistle to the Corinthians: A Commentary on the Greek Text* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 2000), pp. 1054-1059, on 1 Corinthians 13:7.
Hans Conzelmann, *1 Corinthians: A Commentary on the First Epistle to the Corinthians*, trans. James W. Leitch (Hermeneia; Fortress Press, 1975), pp. 222-227, on 1 Corinthians 13:7.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *First Corinthians: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AYB 32; Yale University Press, 2008), pp. 494-501, on 1 Corinthians 13:7.
Michael V. Fox, *Proverbs 10-31: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AYB 18B; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 578-580, on Proverbs 14:15.
Abraham J. Malherbe, *The Letters to the Thessalonians: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AB 32B; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 332-337, on 1 Thessalonians 5:21.
