2 Timothy 3:12 belongs to a different theological universe. Its claim is comprehensive and grammatically emphatic: pantes... hoi thelontes eusebōs zēn en Christō Iēsou diōchthēsontai — "all who want to live godly in Christ Jesus will be persecuted." The future passive diōchthēsontai states an expectation, not a possibility, and the universal pantes leaves no exemption. I. Howard Marshall observes that the pastoral reads the suffering of the apostles as the normative template: persecution is not a deviation from godliness but its evidence. Luke Timothy Johnson situates the verse within the inherited Second-Temple martyrological pattern — the conviction, developed in texts like 2 and 4 Maccabees and the Wisdom of Solomon 2-5, that the righteous suffer at the hands of the wicked precisely because they are righteous. The two verses thus encode opposite expectations of what divine approval looks like in the world: social peace versus social persecution.

Two harmonizations are usually offered. The first claims that "enemies will be at peace" means God restrains or pacifies one''s foes providentially, not that the godly person escapes hostility altogether. The second relegates Proverbs to "general wisdom" and elevates 2 Timothy to "specific Christian-era apocalyptic," so the two govern different domains. Both succeed only by emptying Proverbs 16:7 of its plain surface claim, which is not a probabilistic tendency but a stated outcome of divine favor. The decisive observation is internal to the Hebrew canon itself: the retribution principle Proverbs 16:7 assumes is repeatedly contradicted from within the wisdom corpus. Job''s entire frame is the suffering of a man God himself calls blameless (Job 1:8), and Qohelet flatly observes righteous people perishing in their righteousness and wicked people prospering (Eccl 7:15; 8:14). Roland Murphy and James Crenshaw both stress that the Hebrew Bible knows perfectly well that the righteous get hammered; the optimistic deed-consequence theology of Proverbs is precisely the view Job and Qohelet were written to interrogate.

This reframes the contradiction. It is not an artifact of reading an Israelite proverb anachronistically against a Christian epistle; it is a node in an intra-biblical debate over whether divine favor manifests as worldly flourishing. Proverbs 16:7 sits at the optimistic pole of that debate; 2 Timothy 3:12 lands at the opposite pole, where the mark of God''s approval is the cross rather than the peace treaty. The apocalyptic-martyrological theology of the pastorals does not merely add nuance to the wisdom maxim — it inverts its core expectation, in agreement with Job and Qohelet against Proverbs. Verdict: genuine contradiction, and one the Hebrew wisdom tradition was already having with itself.', 'Bruce K. Waltke, *The Book of Proverbs: Chapters 15-31* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 2005), pp. 14-16, on Proverbs 16:7.
Michael V. Fox, *Proverbs 10-31: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AYB 18B; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 614-616, on Proverbs 16:7.
Roland E. Murphy, *Proverbs* (WBC 22; Thomas Nelson, 1998), pp. 119-121, on Proverbs 16:7.
I. Howard Marshall, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Pastoral Epistles* (ICC; T&T Clark, 1999), pp. 786-789, on 2 Timothy 3:12.
Luke Timothy Johnson, *The First and Second Letters to Timothy: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AB 35A; Doubleday, 2001), pp. 421-424, on 2 Timothy 3:12.
James L. Crenshaw, *Old Testament Wisdom: An Introduction* (3rd ed.; Westminster John Knox Press, 2010), pp. 71-82, on Proverbs 16:7.', 0, NULL, 2, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Proverbs, 2 Timothy', 5, 2, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

Proverbs 17:22 represents the tradition in its sunniest register: lēb śāmēaḥ yêṭib gēhâ — "a joyful heart is good medicine." Michael Fox reads the line as proto-psychosomatic observation, the kind of claim paralleled in Egyptian Instruction literature and later Greek medical writing: cheerfulness produces physical wellbeing. Proverbs, on this verse, simply likes happiness and treats it as health-giving.

Luke 6:25 operates in an apocalyptic-prophetic register that does not. Joseph Fitzmyer reads the woes paired with the beatitudes (Luke 6:20-26) as classic prophetic reversal language: ouai... hoi gelōntes nyn, hoti penthēsete kai klausete — "woe to you who laugh now, for you will mourn and weep." François Bovon frames the woe as a deliberate inversion of Hellenistic eudaimonia, an attack on the comfortable assumption that present contentment signals divine favor. The verse is not aesthetic counsel about moderation; it is eschatological reversal — present laughter is a debt that the coming kingdom will call in.

The standard harmonization distinguishes kinds of happiness: worldly mirth versus spiritual joy, frivolous laughter versus mature contentment. The lexical evidence resists the move. Qohelet uses the same root, śmḥ, for the merriment he commends in 8:15 and the laughter he disparages in 7:3-4 and 2:2 ("of laughter I said, it is mad"); Luke uses the same verb, gelaō, for the blessed laughter promised in 6:21 and the condemned laughter of 6:25. The vocabulary does not partition itself into a sanctioned and an unsanctioned species of joy; the same words carry the positive and negative valuations, so the distinction must be imported rather than read out. James Crenshaw''s analysis of Qohelet underscores that the book''s tensions are not resolved by such glosses but are constitutive of its theology of contingency.

What remains is three positions that do not reconcile: a wisdom tradition that prescribes happiness as medicine (Proverbs), a wisdom voice that both commends and indicts it (Qohelet), and a Jesus tradition that treats present happiness as a down payment on future woe (Luke). The contradiction is real, and in Ecclesiastes it is internal to a single book. Verdict: genuine contradiction, partly intra-textual.', 'Choon-Leong Seow, *Ecclesiastes: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AB 18C; Doubleday, 1997), pp. 162-165, 242-256, 295-300, on Ecclesiastes 8:15 and 7:3-4.
Tremper Longman III, *The Book of Ecclesiastes* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 1998), pp. 181-189, 217-224, on Ecclesiastes 7:3-4.
Michael V. Fox, *Proverbs 10-31: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AYB 18B; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 633-635, on Proverbs 17:22.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke (I-IX)* (AB 28; Doubleday, 1981), pp. 633-640, on Luke 6:25.
François Bovon, *Luke 1: A Commentary on the Gospel of Luke 1:1-9:50*, trans. Christine M. Thomas (Hermeneia; Fortress Press, 2002), pp. 220-229, on Luke 6:25.
James L. Crenshaw, *Ecclesiastes: A Commentary* (OTL; Westminster Press, 1987), pp. 132-137, on Ecclesiastes 7:3-4.', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Proverbs, Ecclesiastes, Luke', 6, 2, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

Second Isaiah says the opposite, and says it programmatically. *Hineni ʿoseh ḥadashah* — "behold, I am doing a new thing" (43:19) — is the announcement of an intervention so categorically novel that the old categories will not survive it. Joseph Blenkinsopp, in the Anchor Bible Isaiah 40-55, documents how the *ḥadash* vocabulary in Deutero-Isaiah is deliberately polemical against Babylonian and broader ancient Near Eastern cyclical cosmology: YHWH is the one who disrupts the cycle, who makes a way in the wilderness where there was none. Third Isaiah escalates the claim to cosmic scale in 65:17 — "new heavens and a new earth" so total that "the former things shall not be remembered." Blenkinsopp''s companion volume on Isaiah 56-66 reads this as proto-apocalyptic, the seedbed of the genre Revelation later inherits. The New Testament runs the same direction: Victor Paul Furnish reads 2 Cor 5:17''s *kainē ktisis* ("new creation") as Paul''s most condensed statement of inaugurated eschatology, and Richard Bauckham (on 2 Peter) and David Aune (on Revelation) trace the Isaianic new-heavens-new-earth language straight into the apocalyptic finale. The whole eschatological strand is built on the premise that newness is precisely what God specializes in. John Collins''s survey of the apocalyptic imagination underscores how alien this rupturalist metaphysic is to the sapiential outlook.

The standard harmonization — articulated in various forms by Longman and by apologetic commentators — restricts Qohelet''s scope: "under the sun" (*taḥat ha-shemesh*) marks a register, the sublunar natural order observed without revelation, so that Qohelet denies novelty in nature while the prophets announce supernatural new acts of God. This deserves a fair hearing, because "under the sun" is in fact Qohelet''s signature qualifier (it recurs roughly thirty times across the book, depending on how one counts), and one can read it as bracketing the divine vantage. But the harmonization does not survive contact with the text''s own phrasing. The claim of 1:9 is universal — *kol ḥadash*, "any new thing" — and the supporting examples in 1:5-7 are cosmic (sun, wind, sea), not narrowly terrestrial. Qohelet is not carving out a domain in which God might still innovate; he is denying eschatology as a category of expectation. The apocalyptic strand depends on exactly the category Qohelet rejects. The deeper point is that this is not a clumsy author contradicting himself but two competing Jewish theologies of time housed in one canon: a wisdom-Hellenistic outlook that is cyclical and a prophetic-apocalyptic outlook that is rupturalist. The redaction-critical history matters here — Qohelet is late (Persian period, late fifth to early fourth century BCE, on Seow''s dating; the third century on the majority view), and the new-creation eschatology of Trito-Isaiah and the apocalypses crystallizes alongside and after it, so the canon preserves a live intramural dispute rather than a settled doctrine. Verdict: genuine theological contradiction; the register-restriction reading softens but cannot dissolve a claim the text frames universally.', 'Choon-Leong Seow, *Ecclesiastes* (Anchor Bible 18C; Doubleday, 1997), pp. 105-115, on Ecclesiastes 1:9
Tremper Longman III, *The Book of Ecclesiastes* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 1998), pp. 65-76, on Ecclesiastes 1:9
Joseph Blenkinsopp, *Isaiah 40-55* (Anchor Bible 19A; Doubleday, 2002), pp. 228-232, on Isaiah 43:19
Joseph Blenkinsopp, *Isaiah 56-66* (Anchor Bible 19B; Doubleday, 2003), pp. 285-291, on Isaiah 65:17
Victor Paul Furnish, *II Corinthians* (Anchor Bible 32A; Doubleday, 1984), pp. 314-318, on 2 Corinthians 5:17
Richard J. Bauckham, *Jude, 2 Peter* (Word Biblical Commentary 50; Word Books, 1983), pp. 325-329, on 2 Peter 3:13
David E. Aune, *Revelation 17-22* (Word Biblical Commentary 52C; Thomas Nelson, 1998), pp. 1115-1119, on Revelation 21:1
John J. Collins, *The Apocalyptic Imagination: An Introduction to Jewish Apocalyptic Literature*, 3rd ed. (Eerdmans, 2016), pp. 1-42, on the apocalyptic theology of time', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Ecclesiastes, Isaiah, 2 Corinthians, 2 Peter, Revelation', 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Begin with the no-afterlife strand, since the apologetic playbook''s first move is to act as if it isn''t there. Job 7:9, Job 14:10-14, Ecclesiastes 3:19-21, Ecclesiastes 9:5-6, Psalm 6:5, Psalm 88:5, Psalm 115:17, and Isaiah 38:18 are not stray outliers. James Crenshaw (OTL Ecclesiastes) and Choon-Leong Seow (AB Ecclesiastes) both treat Qohelet''s denial of any post-mortem reward as the book''s foundational anthropology — the dead "know nothing," the same fate befalls human and beast. Jon Levenson, in *Resurrection and the Restoration of Israel*, documents how the pre-exilic and early post-exilic Hebrew Bible has no developed afterlife: *Sheol* is the undifferentiated grave-shadow, identical for righteous and wicked. Philip Johnston''s *Shades of Sheol* reaches the same conclusion through close lexical work. The decisive datum is chronological: John Collins, in the Hermeneia Daniel, establishes that Daniel 12:2 — "many who sleep in the dust shall awake, some to everlasting life, some to everlasting contempt" — is the first unambiguous biblical reference to differential post-mortem destinies, composed in the 160s BCE in direct response to the Maccabean martyrdoms, when the problem of righteous sufferers who died unvindicated forced the innovation. The likely catalysts were Persian (Zoroastrian dualistic eschatology) and Hellenistic, absorbed across the late Second Temple period.

The eternal-torment strand is equally real. Dale Allison (ICC Matthew) shows how Matthean apocalyptic deploys Gehenna imagery drawn from Isaiah 66:24 and 1 Enoch to portray unending punishment. Joel Marcus, on Mark 9:43-48, declines to soften the imagery: the worm that does not die and the fire that is not quenched are quoted from Isaiah precisely for their permanence. Aune reads Revelation''s lake-of-fire torment *eis tous aiōnas tōn aiōnōn* ("forever and ever") as as strong a duration claim as Greek can make. So the apocalyptic hell is not a medieval invention.

The annihilationist strand turns on the verbs the torment reading would prefer to soften. *Apollymi* (perish), *phtheirō*/*phthora* (destroy, corruption), and *olethros* (destruction) are destruction-words. Romans 6:23 sets the wage of sin as *thanatos* (death), not torment; Philippians 3:19 names the enemies'' *telos* as *apōleia*; 2 Thessalonians 1:9 has *olethron aiōnion*, which conditionalists construe as a destruction whose *result* is permanent, not a destroying that never finishes. Fudge''s *The Fire That Consumes* and Glenn Peoples make this case from within evangelical confession, not from skepticism. The universalist strand rests on texts like 1 Cor 15:22 — "as in Adam all die, so in Christ shall all be made alive" (*pantes zōopoiēthēsontai*) — 1 Tim 4:10 (God "the Savior of all"), and 1 John 2:2 (propitiation "for the whole world"). Origen and the Cappadocians argued *apokatastasis* from precisely these; Hart''s *That All Shall Be Saved* re-litigates the Greek, contending that the *aiōnios* word-group denotes the age to come rather than infinite duration, so that "eternal torment" is not even securely present in Paul.

The harmonizations all reduce to assigning different texts to different vocabularies enforced selectively: *Sheol* means the body''s grave but not the conscious soul; "perish" means loss of relationship, not loss of existence; the universalist verses describe potential scope, not actual outcome; *aiōnios* means "eternal" for heaven but "resultant" for destruction. Each move is defensible in isolation; the trouble is that a single coherent doctrine requires all four conventions at once, applied inconsistently to keep one preferred picture standing. The honest critical reading is developmental: the Hebrew Bible mostly has no afterlife; the late Second Temple period imports differential destinies from Persian and Hellenistic sources; early Christianity carries the apocalyptic version forward while the annihilationist and universalist strands survive as canonical minority reports. Verdict: genuine contradiction — the canon preserves at least four incompatible eschatologies rather than one.', 'Jon D. Levenson, *Resurrection and the Restoration of Israel: The Ultimate Victory of the God of Life* (Yale University Press, 2006), pp. 1-22, 181-200, on Sheol and the late rise of resurrection belief
