Ecclesiastes 10:19, however, is not talking about greed at all, and that is what makes the tension real rather than verbal. Qohelet writes *ha-kesef yaʿaneh ʾet ha-kol* — "silver/money answers everything." Choon-Leong Seow, in the Anchor Bible Ecclesiastes, reads this as Qohelet''s characteristic unsentimental realism: money is the universal solvent of human problems, stated without moral hedging. The verb *ʿanah* ("answer, respond, meet the need of") frames money as the thing that responds to every demand. Tremper Longman, in the NICOT, and James Crenshaw, in the OTL, both note the bite of this: it appears in a book that elsewhere denounces the hoarding of wealth in unsparing terms (5:10-17 — "he who loves money will not be satisfied with money"; riches kept by their owner to his hurt; the rich man''s full belly that will not let him sleep). So Qohelet himself holds the descriptive observation (money fixes things) and a warning against money-lust in tension within one book — which is precisely why 10:19 cannot be read as naïve endorsement, but neither can it be read as a moral caution. It is a flat observation about how wealth functions in the social world.

The usual harmonization runs through the descriptive/prescriptive distinction: Ecclesiastes describes how things are (money does, in fact, answer many problems), while 1 Timothy prescribes how one should be disposed (do not love it). This is a genuinely fair reading and it relieves much of the surface tension; the two verses are not strictly affirming and denying the same proposition, since one concerns money''s utility and the other concerns the affective orientation of greed. But it is worth naming what the harmonization concedes. Qohelet is teaching, as wisdom, that the real-world function of wealth is to answer all things — a claim the Christian moralist tradition is precisely warning the reader not to internalize, because to believe that money answers everything is the cognitive engine of *philargyria*. The descriptive observation and the prescriptive warning are not formal contradictories, but they pull in opposite directions and reflect two different evaluative postures toward wealth that the canon never reconciles: a wisdom-pragmatist realism that treats money as the universal answer, and a Hellenistic-Jewish moralism that treats love of it as the universal corrupter. Whether one labels this "contradiction" or "tension within a multi-voiced canon" depends on how strictly one defines the terms. Verdict: partially harmonizable — no formal logical contradiction once the descriptive/prescriptive and lexical distinctions are honored, but a genuine and unresolved clash of evaluative outlooks on wealth.', 'Luke Timothy Johnson, *The First and Second Letters to Timothy* (Anchor Bible 35A; Doubleday, 2001), pp. 295-302, on 1 Timothy 6:10
I. Howard Marshall, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Pastoral Epistles* (ICC; T&T Clark, 1999), pp. 654-661, on 1 Timothy 6:10
Abraham J. Malherbe, "Hellenistic Moralists and the New Testament," in *Aufstieg und Niedergang der römischen Welt* II.26.1, ed. Wolfgang Haase (De Gruyter, 1992), pp. 267-333
Choon-Leong Seow, *Ecclesiastes* (Anchor Bible 18C; Doubleday, 1997), pp. 326-335, on Ecclesiastes 10:19
