Where the question turns scholarly-interesting is the proliferation of Christological titles. Larry Hurtado in Lord Jesus Christ and Martin Hengel in Studies in Early Christology document how the earliest Jesus communities accumulated titles from multiple Jewish prophetic and apocalyptic streams: the Davidic Messiah (Son of David, Lion of Judah, Root of David), the apocalyptic Son of Man (Daniel 7), the Mosaic Prophet, the Suffering Servant of Isaiah 53, the pre-existent Wisdom-Logos of John 1, the throne-sharer of Psalm 110, and the divine warrior of Revelation. Each title carries distinct theological freight. Calling Jesus ''Son of David'' makes a claim about lineage and royal legitimacy; calling him ''the Word'' makes a claim about pre-existence and divine agency; ''Lamb that was slain'' and ''Lion of Judah'' pull in opposite directions tonally even within Revelation. Raymond Brown''s An Introduction to New Testament Christology maps these title by title and concludes the assemblage forms not a unified system but a set of overlapping vocabularies the early church found useful for different rhetorical purposes.

Revelation supplies the one place where the text is at war with itself rather than merely various. Revelation 19:12 — ''he had a name written that no one knew but he himself'' — sits one verse before 19:13 (''his name is called The Word of God'') and a few chapters before 22:16 (''the bright and morning star''). David Aune in the WBC Revelation 17-22 reads 19:12 against ancient Near Eastern and Greco-Roman name-magic, in which knowledge of a divinity''s secret name confers power over it; the hidden name is a deliberate apocalyptic motif of inviolable sovereignty. The friction is real: within a few verses the seer both withholds and publishes the name. That is an internal tension Revelation never resolves, and it is not dissolved by the category-error point.

Isaiah 9:6 adds a further wrinkle. Joseph Blenkinsopp in the AB Isaiah 1-39 reads ''Wonderful Counsellor, Mighty God, Everlasting Father, Prince of Peace'' as a royal enthronement oracle, the throne-names following the Egyptian convention of bestowing a string of grand titulary on a new king (the likeliest referent being Hezekiah). The Christian reading projects these honorifics onto Jesus retrospectively; the names originally belonged to an Iron Age Judean monarch and were transferred to a first-century Galilean by later interpretation. Verdict: the headline ''contradiction'' is overstated (titles are not names), but two genuine items remain — Revelation''s internal known-versus-unknown name tension, and the historically demonstrable fact that ''Jesus''s'' identity was assembled from a portfolio of pre-existing prophetic-royal titles drawn from incompatible streams.', 'John P. Meier, A Marginal Jew: Rethinking the Historical Jesus, Volume One: The Roots of the Problem and the Person (Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, 1991), pp. 205-219, on the historical name Yeshua.
Dale C. Allison, Constructing Jesus: Memory, Imagination, and History (Baker Academic, 2010), pp. 221-304.
