Isaiah 26:10 sits inside the ''Isaiah Apocalypse'' (chs. 24-27), a later block most critics date to the late Persian or early Hellenistic period. The verse reads yuchan rasha bal-lamad tsedeq ... bal-yireh ge''ut YHWH — ''though the wicked be shown favor, he does not learn righteousness ... he will not see the majesty of YHWH.'' Blenkinsopp in the AB Isaiah 1-39 and Otto Kaiser in the OTL Isaiah 13-39 both read this as classic apocalyptic differentiation: eschatological vision of the deity is sorted by moral category — the righteous see, the wicked are barred. The same scroll therefore contains an unrestricted universal theophany and a morally screened one.

The apologetic harmonization typically distinguishes modes of seeing: ''all flesh'' will see God''s glory in some general sense (e.g., in the eschatological vindication of Zion), while only the righteous will ''behold his majesty'' in the deeper sense of beneficial communion. The distinction is intelligible, and a sympathetic reader can construct it. But it is imposed rather than derived. The Hebrew of 40:5 admits no subdivision — kol basar yachdaw, ''all flesh together,'' is maximally inclusive and is rhetorically meant to be — and bal-yireh ge''ut YHWH in 26:10 is a flat negation, not a ''lesser kind of seeing.'' The harmonization works only by reading a qualifier into one verse that its author conspicuously withheld. The two passages are not nuancing a shared doctrine; they are advancing different theologies of revelation, one universalist, one apocalyptic-discriminating.

This is a textbook exhibit for why source criticism of Isaiah matters. Bernhard Duhm''s 1892 Das Buch Jesaia established the tripartite division into First (1-39, with later insertions like the Apocalypse), Second (40-55), and Third Isaiah (56-66), and a century of refinement by Brevard Childs, H. G. M. Williamson, and Blenkinsopp has confirmed that the book is a multi-century anthology redacted to look like a unity. Childs, who reads the final form theologically, nonetheless concedes the compositional plurality. Williamson''s The Book Called Isaiah argues Deutero-Isaiah deliberately edited and extended the earlier material, which only sharpens the point: the layers were juxtaposed, not harmonized, by the editors. Verdict: a genuine intra-Isaianic contradiction produced by two distinct authors with incompatible eschatologies of who gets to see God.', 'Joseph Blenkinsopp, Isaiah 40-55: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary (Anchor Bible 19A; Doubleday, 2002), pp. 178-186, on Isaiah 40:5.
Klaus Baltzer, Deutero-Isaiah: A Commentary on Isaiah 40-55, trans. Margaret Kohl (Hermeneia; Fortress Press, 2001), pp. 50-58, on Isaiah 40:5.
Joseph Blenkinsopp, Isaiah 1-39 (Anchor Bible 19; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 363-373, on Isaiah 26:10.
Otto Kaiser, Isaiah 13-39: A Commentary, trans. R. A. Wilson (Old Testament Library; Westminster Press, 1974), pp. 199-219, on Isaiah 26:10.
Brevard S. Childs, Isaiah (Old Testament Library; Westminster John Knox Press, 2001), pp. 181-198, 295-307.
H. G. M. Williamson, The Book Called Isaiah: Deutero-Isaiah''s Role in Composition and Redaction (Clarendon Press, 1994), pp. 1-29.
Bernhard Duhm, Das Buch Jesaia (Handkommentar zum Alten Testament; Vandenhoeck & Ruprecht, 1892), foundational for the tripartite division of Isaiah.', 0, NULL, 2, 'OT_internal', 'Isaiah', 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

It does not, however, fully defeat the SAB''s point. On the surface — and in essentially every English translation a lay reader meets — 27:4 reads ''Fury is not in me,'' with no asterisk. And the contradicting texts are unambiguous and use the very same vocabulary. Isaiah 34:2 pours out YHWH''s chemah (the identical noun) on all the armies of the nations: we-chemah al-kol-tseva''am. Jeremiah''s portrait is dense with chemah and aph (''anger,'' literally ''nostril/snorting''): Jer 7:20 (''mine anger and my fury shall be poured out''), 21:5 (''in anger, and in fury, and in great wrath''), 30:23 (''the whirlwind of the LORD goeth forth with fury''). Jack Lundbom in the AB Jeremiah documents this enraged-deity portrait at length. Micah 5:15 promises vengeance ''in anger and fury,'' and Nahum 1:2 is the poetic apex — ''YHWH is a jealous and avenging God; YHWH avenges and is full of wrath (chemah)'' — which J. J. M. Roberts in the OTL Nahum reads as a theophanic hymn of divine fury. Zechariah 8:2 has YHWH ''jealous for Zion with great fury.'' K.-D. Schunck''s TDOT article on chemah catalogues the noun''s roughly 120 occurrences and notes that the overwhelming majority depict actual divine rage.

So the contradiction operates at two levels. Intra-Isaianic: chemah ein li (27:4) versus we-chemah al-kol-tseva''am (34:2), separated by seven chapters and using the same word. Brevard Childs in the OTL Isaiah and Otto Kaiser in the OTL Isaiah 13-39 both register the friction and treat it as a deliberate inner-Isaianic theological tension between a wrathful and a salvific God held across the book''s layers. Inter-prophetic: Isaiah 27:4 versus the Jeremiah-Micah-Nahum-Zechariah cluster, where YHWH''s fury is not occasional but characteristic.

The contextual harmonization succeeds in showing that 27:4 was probably never intended as a universal denial of divine anger, which is why this is rated partially harmonized rather than a flat contradiction. But the deeper observation — and here critical scholarship adds what the apologetic frame cannot — is that the Hebrew Bible''s portrayal of YHWH''s emotional life is irreducibly multi-vocal. Abraham Heschel''s The Prophets argued that the prophetic God is no impassible metaphysical absolute but a covenant-bound figure whose affect tracks Israel''s behavior; Francis Andersen and David Noel Freedman''s Micah similarly stresses the covenantal logic of divine pathos. The juxtaposition of Isaiah 27:4 and Nahum 1:2 is less a puzzle to be solved than the prophetic tradition''s refusal to settle on one doctrine of divine emotion. Verdict: overstated as a global ''God is never furious'' contradiction (27:4 is contextually bounded), but a real tension in surface reading and in the canon''s multi-vocal theology of wrath.', 'Joseph Blenkinsopp, Isaiah 1-39 (Anchor Bible 19; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 373-378, on Isaiah 27:4.
Brevard S. Childs, Isaiah (Old Testament Library; Westminster John Knox Press, 2001), pp. 195-204.
Otto Kaiser, Isaiah 13-39: A Commentary, trans. R. A. Wilson (Old Testament Library; Westminster Press, 1974), pp. 223-232, on Isaiah 27:4.
