The pastoral instruction of 1 Timothy 2:1-4 reads as if Jeremiah did not exist: parakalō oun prōton pantōn poieisthai deēseis, proseuchas, enteuxeis, eucharistias hyper pantōn anthrōpōn — ''I urge that, first of all, supplications, prayers, intercessions, and thanksgivings be made for all people.'' The phrase hyper pantōn anthrōpōn recurs, reinforced in v. 4 by the claim that God ''desires all people (pantas anthrōpous) to be saved.'' Luke Timothy Johnson in the AB First and Second Letters to Timothy reads this as deliberate anti-sectarian universalism, pushing back against any community that would restrict its intercessory scope to insiders; I. Howard Marshall in the ICC Pastoral Epistles documents the rhetorical force of the repeated ''all'' and judges it programmatic rather than merely aspirational.

The apologetic harmonization is reasonable and worth stating in full: the Jeremianic prohibition is situational and time-bound, a particular divine verdict on pre-exilic Judah at a moment when judgment had become irreversible, whereas 1 Timothy lays down a general standing instruction for the churches. The two commands are therefore not addressed to the same situation, and Lundbom would grant the situational framing. On the level of individual commands, this largely works — which is why the entry is rated partially harmonized rather than a flat contradiction.

What the harmonization does not finish is the doctrinal residue. 1 Timothy 2:1, especially read with v. 4, establishes that there is no category of person for whom one should refrain from praying, because God''s salvific will extends to all. The Jeremianic texts presuppose exactly the opposite premise: there are people, and circumstances, for whom intercession is futile because God has resolved not to hear, and the faithful prophet is therefore instructed not to expend prayer on them. Samuel Balentine''s Prayer in the Hebrew Bible analyzes these prohibitions precisely as a crisis in the theology of intercession — a recognition that prayer is not unconditionally efficacious. So even granting the situational specificity of Jeremiah''s bans, the principle they encode (intercession can be pointless, even forbidden) is the principle 1 Timothy denies. The tension is not at the level of any single instruction but at the level of the underlying doctrine of prayer, and the reception history shows both strands functioning as live warrants — the Jeremianic line feeding reflection on reprobation and the limits of intercession, the Pauline line undergirding the practice of universal prayer. Verdict: harmonizable as situational commands, but a genuine contradiction in the theology of whether prayer for everyone is ever appropriate.', 'Jack R. Lundbom, Jeremiah 1-20 (Anchor Bible 21A; Doubleday, 1999), pp. 478-487, 624-635, 705-712, on Jeremiah 7:16; 11:14; 14:11.
William L. Holladay, Jeremiah 1: A Commentary on the Book of the Prophet Jeremiah, Chapters 1-25 (Hermeneia; Fortress Press, 1986), pp. 254-262, 354-360, 432-439, on Jeremiah 7:16; 11:14; 14:11.
Robert P. Carroll, Jeremiah: A Commentary (Old Testament Library; Westminster Press, 1986), pp. 213-220, 271-275, 310-316.
I. Howard Marshall, A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Pastoral Epistles (International Critical Commentary; T&T Clark, 1999), pp. 415-432, on 1 Timothy 2:1.
Luke Timothy Johnson, The First and Second Letters to Timothy (Anchor Bible 35A; Doubleday, 2001), pp. 187-200, on 1 Timothy 2:1-4.
Samuel E. Balentine, Prayer in the Hebrew Bible: The Drama of Divine-Human Dialogue (Overtures to Biblical Theology; Fortress Press, 1993), pp. 33-62.', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Jeremiah, 1 Timothy', 6, 2, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

Luke 21:11 occupies an entirely different cultural and literary moment. The Synoptic Apocalypse (Mark 13 // Matt 24 // Luke 21) draws on the deep reservoir of Jewish apocalyptic tradition in which eschatological cosmic disturbances — earthquakes, eclipses, falling stars, blood and fire — are read as legitimate revelatory data announcing the Day of YHWH. Joseph Fitzmyer, in the Anchor Bible Luke X-XXIV, traces the literary background through Isaiah 13:10, Joel 2:30-31, and 1 Enoch, where celestial phenomena are the standard furniture of the end. François Bovon, in the Hermeneia Luke commentary, reads the Lukan phrase ap'' ouranou sēmeia megala — ''great signs from heaven'' — as straightforward apocalyptic-tradition material treating such portents as authentic eschatological markers. John Collins, in The Apocalyptic Imagination, documents at length how Jewish apocalyptic literature (Daniel, 1 Enoch, the Qumran scrolls) is saturated with cosmological signs that the Jeremianic author would have catalogued as Babylonian astrology, and James VanderKam''s work on Qumran calendrical and astronomical texts shows how thoroughly Second Temple Judaism had absorbed the very celestial science Jeremiah''s polemic rejected. Francesca Rochberg''s study of Mesopotamian celestial divination demonstrates that the technical apparatus of reading the heavens as portents passed into the wider ancient world rather than being abolished.

The strongest harmonization, advanced for centuries by Christian commentators, distinguishes two acts: forbidden astral divination, in which one studies the heavens to forecast events and shape decisions (Jeremiah''s target), versus passive recognition of God-given signs that accompany the eschaton (Luke''s subject). On this reading Jeremiah condemns the diviner''s craft, while Luke merely describes phenomena God will send. The distinction is genuine and does dissolve part of the tension: Luke 21 does not counsel anyone to take up horoscopy, and Jeremiah 10 is not denying that God can act in the heavens. The genre gap — legal-paraenetic prohibition versus apocalyptic prediction — reinforces the partial reconciliation. Yet the distinction collapses at the crucial point. The eschaton-announcing signs of Luke 21 are functionally identical to the divinatory signs of Babylonian astrology: cosmic disturbances read as portents of regime change and divine intervention. The Lukan apocalypse has imported the interpretive technique — heavenly events as decodable messages about the fate of nations — that the Jeremianic polemic existed to suppress. The deepest tension is therefore not ''look at the sky'' versus ''don''t,'' but between an anti-divination strand of Israelite religion that defined faithfulness as refusing to read celestial portents, and an apocalyptic strand that wholeheartedly adopted the same celestial-omen semiotics because they had become useful for mapping the end. Verdict: partially harmonizable on genre and act, but the underlying clash between anti-astral monotheism and apocalyptic cosmic semiotics is real.', 'Robert P. Carroll, *Jeremiah: A Commentary* (Old Testament Library; Westminster Press, 1986), pp. 253-261, on Jeremiah 10:2
Jack R. Lundbom, *Jeremiah 1-20: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Bible 21A; Doubleday, 1999), pp. 580-592, on Jeremiah 10:2
William L. Holladay, *Jeremiah 1: A Commentary on the Book of the Prophet Jeremiah, Chapters 1-25* (Hermeneia; Fortress Press, 1986), pp. 324-336, on Jeremiah 10:2
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke (X-XXIV)* (Anchor Bible 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1331-1341, on Luke 21:11
François Bovon, *Luke 3: A Commentary on the Gospel of Luke 19:28-24:53*, trans. James E. Crouch (Hermeneia; Fortress Press, 2012), pp. 102-110, on Luke 21:11
John J. Collins, *The Apocalyptic Imagination: An Introduction to Jewish Apocalyptic Literature*, 3rd ed. (Eerdmans, 2016), pp. 43-83, 280-310, on apocalyptic celestial signs
James C. VanderKam, *Calendars in the Dead Sea Scrolls: Measuring Time* (Literature of the Dead Sea Scrolls; Routledge, 1998), pp. 1-26
Francesca Rochberg, *The Heavenly Writing: Divination, Horoscopy, and Astronomy in Mesopotamian Culture* (Cambridge University Press, 2004), pp. 44-97', 0, NULL, 3, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Jeremiah, Luke', 6, 3, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

Matthew 7:21 takes the same liturgical vocabulary and negates the inference: ou pas ho legōn moi ''Kyrie, Kyrie'' eiseleusetai eis tēn basileian tōn ouranōn — ''not everyone who says to me Lord, Lord, will enter the kingdom of heaven, but the one who does the will of my Father.'' W. D. Davies and Dale Allison, in the International Critical Commentary Matthew, read this as intra-Christian polemic: the Matthean community faced confessors-without-works, perhaps charismatic prophets (note 7:22''s prophesying and exorcising), and the saying denies that invocation of the Lord''s name is by itself sufficient. Ulrich Luz, in the Hermeneia Matthew, concurs, reading 7:21-23 as Matthew''s pointed rejection of cheap confession divorced from obedience.

The prophetic rejection-texts supply an older layer. Jeremiah 14:12 belongs to the intercession-prohibition oracles in which YHWH instructs the prophet not to pray for the people and refuses to hear their cry; Lundbom situates it in the drought-and-judgment sequence. Ezekiel 8:18 sits inside the great temple-abomination vision (Ezek 8-11) where YHWH''s glory abandons Jerusalem; Walther Zimmerli, in the Hermeneia Ezekiel, reads the closed divine ear as the theological architecture justifying the exile. Micah 3:4 is straight prophetic-judgment form: the corrupt rulers will cry out and YHWH will hide his face. In each, calling on the Lord is explicitly futile.

The strongest harmonization, developed at length by Walter Kaiser and others, distinguishes two senses of ''calling.'' Sincere calling joined to faith and repentance secures deliverance (Joel, Acts, Romans); merely formal or hypocritical calling, unaccompanied by covenant fidelity, is justly ignored (Matthew, Jeremiah, Ezekiel, Micah). This is a coherent systematic move, and it is exegetically grounded: Matthew 7:21 itself contrasts saying with doing, and Paul defines saving ''calling'' as confession-plus-belief. On that reading the texts address different acts under one verb, and the contradiction softens into complementarity. What the harmonization costs, however, is the rhetorical force of the Joel-Pauline citation. Joel 2:32 as deployed in Romans 10 does work that depends on its unqualified pas — everyone, no exceptions — to ground the gospel''s reach to Gentiles. If one must import a faith-and-repentance qualifier drawn from Matthew 7:21, one is no longer quoting Joel 2:32 but a synthesis of Joel-plus-Matthew. The critical reading is developmental: Joel''s late-prophetic universalism was taken up by early Christianity as a grace prooftext, while the Matthean and prophetic-judgment strands preserved an older conditional theology in which divine hearing was always contingent on covenant fidelity. Different canonical strata operate with different theologies of intercessory access. The systematic reconciliation works, but only by reading each passage through assumptions another passage supplies. Verdict: partially harmonizable as canonical synthesis; not a single unconditional promise.', 'James L. Crenshaw, *Joel: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Bible 24C; Doubleday, 1995), pp. 167-172, on Joel 2:32
Douglas J. Moo, *The Letter to the Romans*, 2nd ed. (New International Commentary on the New Testament; Eerdmans, 2018), pp. 670-686, on Romans 10:13
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Acts of the Apostles* (Anchor Bible 31; Doubleday, 1998), pp. 252-258, on Acts 2:21
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew, Volume I* (International Critical Commentary; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 711-720, on Matthew 7:21
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 1-7: A Commentary*, trans. James E. Crouch (Hermeneia; Fortress Press, 2007), pp. 380-388, on Matthew 7:21
Jack R. Lundbom, *Jeremiah 1-20: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Bible 21A; Doubleday, 1999), pp. 704-712, on Jeremiah 14:12
Walther Zimmerli, *Ezekiel 1: A Commentary on the Book of the Prophet Ezekiel, Chapters 1-24*, trans. Ronald E. Clements (Hermeneia; Fortress Press, 1979), pp. 240-247, on Ezekiel 8:18
Francis I. Andersen and David Noel Freedman, *Micah: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Bible 24E; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 343-352, on Micah 3:4
Walter C. Kaiser Jr. et al., *Hard Sayings of the Bible* (InterVarsity Press, 1996), on the distinction between sincere and formal calling', 0, NULL, 4, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Jeremiah, Ezekiel, Joel, Micah, Matthew, Acts, Romans', 5, 3, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The origin of the seventy is symbolic. Jack Lundbom, in the Anchor Bible Jeremiah 21-36, documents the resonance of seventy as a round number of completion in the Hebrew Bible — the seventy nations of Genesis 10, the seventy elders of Exodus 24, the seventy years of a human lifespan in Psalm 90:10. A Babylonian parallel reinforces the point: an inscription of Esarhaddon assigns Babylon itself a seventy-year period of divine abandonment, a near-formulaic span for a deity''s withdrawal. William Holladay, in the Hermeneia Jeremiah, reads the seventy-year figure as deliberate symbol rather than chronicle. That the actual exile ran roughly half a century while the prophet rounds it to seventy is not a historical embarrassment but a feature of schematic prophecy. Daniel 9 confirms the figure''s elasticity within the canon itself: it reinterprets Jeremiah''s seventy years as seventy weeks of years — 490 years — stretching the prophecy down to the Maccabean crisis. John Collins, in the Hermeneia Daniel, shows this to be a second-century BCE apocalyptic recalculation of an already-schematic number. 2 Chronicles 36:21 offers yet another rationale, tying the seventy years to the land''s missed sabbatical rests.

The Epistle of Jeremiah — transmitted in Greek manuscripts and appearing as Baruch 6 in the Vulgate''s numbering — gives the duration as ''seven generations.'' Carey Moore, in the Anchor Bible volume on the additions, dates the work to roughly the late fourth or third century BCE and identifies ''seven generations'' as itself a schematic figure rooted in genealogical perfection-theology, seven being the completeness-number. A biblical generation is conventionally reckoned at thirty to forty years, yielding a span of 210 to 280 years — manifestly not seventy.

The apologetic case runs along three paths. First, the Epistle may envision a longer cultural or religious exile rather than the strict political captivity, so the two are not measuring the same thing. Second, ''seven generations'' is loose figurative idiom for ''a long time,'' as the verse''s own framing (''many years, and for a long time'') suggests. Third, on the Protestant canon the Epistle of Jeremiah is non-canonical, so no intra-scriptural contradiction arises at all. Each path has genuine traction, and together they substantially defuse the charge: both numbers are best read as theological schemata, not arithmetic, and they were never intended as competing stopwatch readings. Yet the reconciliation is only partial. The deeper datum survives every harmonization: Second Temple Jewish literature preserved multiple, mutually incompatible schematizations of the exile''s length — seventy years (Jeremiah), seventy weeks of years (Daniel), seven generations (Epistle of Jeremiah) — none of which matches the historical span of roughly fifty years from the temple''s fall to the return. Grabbe''s bottom line stands: the duration of the captivity was a theological construct before it was a measurement, and the witnesses do not agree. Verdict: harmonizable as competing schematic symbols (and moot on the Protestant canon), but historically none of the figures is literal.', 'Lester L. Grabbe, *A History of the Jews and Judaism in the Second Temple Period, Volume 1: Yehud — A History of the Persian Province of Judah* (Library of Second Temple Studies 47; T&T Clark, 2004), pp. 271-308, on the duration of the captivity
Jack R. Lundbom, *Jeremiah 21-36: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Bible 21B; Doubleday, 2004), pp. 245-258, 350-358, on Jeremiah 25:11 and 29:10
William L. Holladay, *Jeremiah 1: A Commentary on the Book of the Prophet Jeremiah, Chapters 1-25* (Hermeneia; Fortress Press, 1986), pp. 667-674, on Jeremiah 25:11
John J. Collins, *Daniel: A Commentary on the Book of Daniel* (Hermeneia; Fortress Press, 1993), pp. 343-359, on the seventy weeks reinterpretation
Carey A. Moore, *Daniel, Esther, and Jeremiah: The Additions* (Anchor Bible 44; Doubleday, 1977), pp. 317-358, on Baruch 6:2 (Epistle of Jeremiah)
Rainer Albertz, *Israel in Exile: The History and Literature of the Sixth Century B.C.E.*, trans. David Green (SBL Studies in Biblical Literature 3; Society of Biblical Literature, 2003), pp. 1-44, on the duration of the exile', 0, NULL, 7, 'OT_internal', 'Jeremiah, Baruch', 1, 2, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

A textual wrinkle deserves note. The Masoretic Text of 11:5 begins with lo'' (''not''), but the standard LXX-influenced emendation (followed by RSV/NRSV and surveyed by Macintosh in the ICC) moves that negative particle back to the end of the immediately preceding verse, 11:4, re-pointing it as לוֹ (lô, ''to him'') — an object marker for the verb in 11:4 rather than a negation in 11:5. Strip the lo'' out of 11:5 and the verse reads ''he shall return to the land of Egypt,'' which now agrees with 8:13 and 9:3. The Septuagint reflects exactly this uncertainty. Adopt the emendation and 11:5 harmonizes with the earlier oracles — but it does so by textual surgery, not exegesis, and the MT as transmitted plainly contradicts them. Macintosh, in the International Critical Commentary Hosea, surveys the variants and the difficulty of the verse.

Critical commentators have taken three main approaches. First, redaction history: the verses derive from different compositional moments preserved in the edited book. Hans Walter Wolff, in the Hermeneia Hosea, reads 8:13 and 9:3 as earlier Hosean material from a period when flight to Egypt was a live political option (Israel did at times seek Egyptian alliance against Assyria, cf. Hos 7:11; 12:1), and 11:5 as reflecting the actual outcome — Assyrian deportation following the fall of Samaria in 722 BCE. Second, the symbolic reading: ''Egypt'' functions as a theological cipher for bondage and the reversal of the Exodus rather than a literal destination. Francis Andersen and David Noel Freedman, in the Anchor Bible Hosea, develop this, taking ''return to Egypt'' as shorthand for un-doing the Exodus, with Assyria the literal destination. Third, the subgroup reading: different segments of Ephraim meet different fates, some fleeing to Egypt and others deported to Assyria. James Mays, in the Old Testament Library Hosea, entertains this, and it has a historical analogue — Jeremiah 41-44 records Judean refugees fleeing to Egypt after Jerusalem''s fall, so a comparable northern flight is not implausible.

Each approach carries a cost. The redactional reading is the most historically defensible but explicitly concedes that the contradiction is real, locating it in the book''s composition rather than dissolving it. The symbolic reading requires construing 8:13 and 9:3 metaphorically while reading 11:5 literally — an asymmetry of interpretation the text gives no signal to license; if ''Egypt'' is figurative in 8:13, consistency would make it figurative in 11:5 too, and the contradiction returns at the level of whether the figure applies. The subgroup reading has the most surface plausibility but is not what the verses say: neither passage distinguishes refugees from deportees, and 11:5 flatly denies the Egypt-return that 9:3 just affirmed of the same ''Ephraim.'' The simplest critical account remains Wolff''s. The book of Hosea was assembled across decades spanning the lead-up to and aftermath of the Assyrian destruction of the northern kingdom, and oracles delivered under shifting political assumptions were preserved side by side without harmonization. The question ''will Ephraim return to Egypt?'' has no single answer in Hosea because the book is a compilation, not a unified prediction. The contradiction is genuine; the only harmonization that does not require allegorizing verses the prophet likely meant literally is to relocate the tension into the book''s redactional history — which is to grant the contradiction at the level of original composition. Verdict: genuine internal contradiction, best explained by the book''s compositional history.', 'Hans Walter Wolff, *Hosea: A Commentary on the Book of the Prophet Hosea*, trans. Gary Stansell (Hermeneia; Fortress Press, 1974), pp. 144-148, 152-159, 197-203, on Hosea 8:13; 9:3; 11:5
Francis I. Andersen and David Noel Freedman, *Hosea: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Bible 24; Doubleday, 1980), pp. 510-521, 537-553, 575-585, on Hosea 8:13; 9:3; 11:5
James Luther Mays, *Hosea: A Commentary* (Old Testament Library; Westminster Press, 1969), pp. 124-130, 145-156, on Hosea 9:3 and 11:5
A. A. Macintosh, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on Hosea* (International Critical Commentary; T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 326-331, 343-360, 442-452, on Hosea 11:5
Marvin A. Sweeney, *The Twelve Prophets, Volume One: Hosea, Joel, Amos, Obadiah, Jonah* (Berit Olam; Liturgical Press, 2000), pp. 84-95, 113-120, on Hosea 8:13 and 11:5', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', 'Hosea', 7, 3, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The Septuagint translators rendered dag gadol as kētei megalō. In Greek, kētos denotes any large sea-creature — it is the word Homer uses for the monsters of the deep, the term in the Andromeda-and-Perseus rescue myth, and a category Aristotle employs broadly in his zoology. Liddell-Scott-Jones glosses it as ''sea-monster, huge fish, whale.'' It is, in short, the lexically appropriate Greek equivalent for an unspecified great sea-creature, not a technical term for the order Cetacea. Matthew 12:40, citing the Jonah episode, picks up the Septuagint vocabulary directly: en tē koilia tou kētous — ''in the belly of the kētos.'' Matthew is quoting the Greek Jonah, not introducing a new zoological claim.

The famous ''whale'' is a translation decision, not a feature of the text. The King James Version of 1611 rendered kētos as ''whale,'' reflecting the dominant Renaissance understanding of the word, but this is narrower than the Greek warrants. Modern critical translations diverge accordingly: the NRSV and NASB render Matthew 12:40 as ''sea monster,'' the ESV and NIV as ''great fish'' or ''huge fish,'' while the NABRE retains the traditional ''whale.'' Davies and Allison, in the International Critical Commentary Matthew, note that the KJV ''whale'' is misleading and that the underlying Greek specifies no such thing. The strong form of the alleged contradiction — ''fish versus whale'' — therefore dissolves into a translation artifact manufactured by the KJV''s anachronistic choice; rendered with parallel vocabulary, Jonah 1:17 and Matthew 12:40 say the same thing: a great fish / sea-creature.

The apologist is entitled to claim this harmonization, but at a price worth naming, and a deeper problem the SAB framing entirely misses. The price: the resolution concedes that the KJV — the translation that shaped four centuries of Anglophone Christian understanding — got the word wrong. The deeper problem: Matthew 12:40 has Jesus predict he will be ''three days and three nights'' (treis hēmeras kai treis nyktas) in the heart of the earth, in explicit parallel to Jonah''s three days and three nights in the fish. Yet the Synoptic passion chronology places the burial late Friday and the resurrection early Sunday — at most two nights (Friday, Saturday) and one full day, not three of each. Dale Allison, in Resurrecting Jesus, treats this as one of the genuinely difficult Synoptic chronology problems; the standard appeal to Jewish inclusive reckoning (any part of a day counting as a whole) can stretch ''three days'' to cover Friday-Saturday-Sunday but cannot manufacture three nights from two. Proposed fixes — a Wednesday or Thursday crucifixion to recover the missing night — conflict with the Gospels'' own Passover-and-Sabbath sequence. The fish-versus-whale dispute is essentially a KJV-translation artifact and is rightly set aside; the three-days-and-three-nights tension is a real, unresolved problem the question does not even raise. Verdict: the fish/whale contradiction is harmonizable as a translation artifact; the underlying three-days-and-three-nights chronology is not.', 'Jack M. Sasson, *Jonah: A New Translation with Introduction, Commentary, and Interpretations* (Anchor Bible 24B; Doubleday, 1990), pp. 149-159, on Jonah 1:17
James Limburg, *Jonah: A Commentary* (Old Testament Library; Westminster John Knox Press, 1993), pp. 49-56, on Jonah 1:17
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew, Volume II* (International Critical Commentary; T&T Clark, 1991), pp. 354-358, on Matthew 12:40
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 8-20: A Commentary*, trans. James E. Crouch (Hermeneia; Fortress Press, 2001), pp. 211-216, on Matthew 12:40
Dale C. Allison, *Resurrecting Jesus: The Earliest Christian Tradition and Its Interpreters* (T&T Clark, 2005), pp. 233-237, on the three-days chronology
Henry George Liddell and Robert Scott, *A Greek-English Lexicon*, rev. Henry Stuart Jones, 9th ed. with revised supplement (Clarendon Press, 1996), s.v. "κῆτος"', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Jonah, Matthew', 7, 2, 3, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard harmonization is the dual-causation argument advanced by Archer, Geisler, and most conservative commentators: the sailors are the proximate (instrumental) cause, YHWH the ultimate (efficient) cause, so both statements are true at distinct levels of agency. Read as theology this is unobjectionable, and read as Hebrew idiom it is in fact strong. The crucial point the bare ''contradiction'' framing misses is the genre of chapter 2. Jonah 2:3-10 is a tightly conventional individual thanksgiving psalm, and one of its defining conventions is precisely that the supplicant attributes the ultimate causation of his ordeal to God. Psalm 88:6-7 is the closest parallel: ''You have put me in the depths of the Pit... your wrath lies heavy upon me, and you overwhelm me with all your waves'' — language Jonah 2:3 echoes almost verbatim (''all your breakers and your waves passed over me''). Within that idiom, ''you cast me into the deep'' is not a claim about the immediate mechanics of being thrown overboard; it is the psalmist''s theological framing of his predicament as God-sent. On this point Sasson''s form-critical analysis is decisive: the prayer behaves as a literary unit governed by lament conventions, not as a forensic report tracking chapter 1''s details.

Where the matter becomes genuinely disputed is the literary-critical question of how the psalm came to sit where it does. A significant body of scholarship — Wolff, Limburg, and others — argues that 2:3-10 is a pre-existing psalm secondarily inserted by the author or a later hand, on the grounds that it is a thanksgiving for deliverance *already accomplished* (the speaker thanks God for rescue) sitting awkwardly inside the fish *before* the rescue, and that its watery imagery describes drowning in the open sea rather than confinement in a fish. On this reading the seams show, and the divine-agency clause is simply borrowed boilerplate that was never coordinated with the narrator''s account of the sailors. Against this, Landes and Magonet have mounted a serious defense of the psalm''s integrity, arguing that its placement is deliberate — the fish is itself the means of deliverance, so a thanksgiving from inside it is coherent — and that its vocabulary is woven into the book''s wider design. If the psalm is original, the ''two agents'' problem dissolves entirely into a single author deploying narrative report and psalmic idiom side by side, exactly as the dual-causation defense supposes.

The upshot is that this is among the weaker entries in the corpus. Even on the source-critical view that the psalm is an insertion, the divergence is not a factual disagreement between two narrators about what happened; it is the difference between prose narration and the inherited conventions of Hebrew prayer, in which God is routinely named as the author of the suffering a worshipper has survived. No competent ancient reader would have heard 2:3 as denying that the sailors did the physical throwing, any more than Psalm 88''s speaker means that no human hand brought about his troubles. The tension is real only if one insists that a lament''s God-language be read as a causal affidavit.

Verdict: overstated — a genre and idiom distinction, not a genuine factual contradiction.', 'Jack M. Sasson, *Jonah: A New Translation with Introduction, Commentary, and Interpretation* (Anchor Bible 24B; Doubleday, 1990), pp. 133-138, 168-201, on the form-critical character of the psalm in Jonah 2.
Hans Walter Wolff, *Obadiah and Jonah: A Commentary*, trans. Margaret Kohl (Continental Commentary; Augsburg, 1986), pp. 128-141, arguing the psalm is a secondary insertion.
James Limburg, *Jonah: A Commentary* (Old Testament Library; Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 60-67, on the psalm''s relation to the narrative.
George M. Landes, "The Kerygma of the Book of Jonah: The Contextual Interpretation of the Jonah Psalm," *Interpretation* 21 (1967), pp. 3-31, defending the psalm''s deliberate placement.
Jonathan Magonet, *Form and Meaning: Studies in Literary Techniques in the Book of Jonah* (Bible and Literature Series; Almond Press, 1983), pp. 39-55, on the literary integration of the psalm.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 301-302, on the dual-causation harmonization.', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', 'Jonah', 7, 2, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

Genesis 1:14, by contrast, declares the luminaries set in the firmament *lĕʾōtōt ûlmôʿădîm ûlyāmîm wĕšānîm* — ''for signs and for seasons and for days and years.'' The KJV''s ''signs'' and ''seasons'' is what generates the apparent clash. But the lexical weight here pulls toward the calendrical. *Môʿădîm* denotes appointed times, overwhelmingly the cultic festival calendar (the same word names the ''appointed feasts'' of Leviticus 23). And *ʾôt*, while broad, is most naturally read in this hendiadys-like cluster of time-words as ''sign'' in the sense of a temporal marker — the heavenly bodies regulate the ritual and agricultural year. The apologetic reading run by Archer and the *Hard Sayings* volume — that *ʾôt* here means calendar markers, not predictive omens — is therefore not a strained dodge but the philologically favored sense in context.

The decisive consideration is the Ancient Near Eastern matrix. Genesis 1 is written in polemical conversation with Mesopotamian cosmology, where the celestial bodies were deities and the great omen series *Enūma Anu Enlil* codified the reading of the sky for royal and national decisions. Rochberg''s work on Babylonian celestial divination shows precisely how integral astral omen-reading was to that culture. Genesis 1 demotes the sun and moon — pointedly never named, called only ''the greater light'' and ''the lesser light'' — to created lamps appointed by a single God. The chapter''s quarrel is over *who* governs the heavens (YHWH, not Shamash or Sin), not over whether the heavens carry meaning. Jeremiah 10:2 makes the same move explicit: ''do not be dismayed at the signs of the heavens (*mēʾōtôt haššāmayim*), as the nations are dismayed'' — conceding that there are heavenly *ʾōtōt* while forbidding the pagan dread and divinatory use of them.

That is why the harmonization largely holds. The two bodies of text are not answering the same question. Genesis 1 affirms the heavenly bodies as divinely appointed temporal signs within a creation account asserting monotheistic sovereignty. The legal texts prohibit a specific class of mantic practitioners who read the sky (and entrails, and omens) to divine the future apart from YHWH. ''The stars mark the festivals'' and ''do not consult astrologers'' are not contradictories. The framing of the contradiction trades on the English ambiguity of ''signs'' and on conflating two distinct things: the existence of celestial signs by divine appointment, and the forbidden professional practice of astrological divination.

Where a residual tension survives is at the cultural boundary, which the canon itself could not police cleanly: Matthew 2 has *magoi* — Mesopotamian astrologers, the very class Deuteronomy proscribes — read a star and arrive at the messiah, treated by the narrator with approval. That shows the line between sanctioned celestial ''sign'' and condemned astral divination was always porous in practice. But the porousness is a tension within the tradition''s reception, not a flat self-contradiction between Genesis 1 and the Holiness/Deuteronomic codes.

Verdict: overstated — different referents (calendar signs vs. pagan divinatory practice) rather than a genuine contradiction.', 'Jacob Milgrom, *Leviticus 17-22: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Bible 3A; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 1685-1690, on *meʿônēn* and the polemic against divination.
Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1996), pp. 172-174, on the catalogue of mantic specialists.
Claus Westermann, *Genesis 1-11: A Commentary*, trans. John J. Scullion (Continental Commentary; Augsburg, 1984), pp. 129-132, on the demotion of the luminaries and ''signs and seasons.''
Francesca Rochberg, *The Heavenly Writing: Divination, Horoscopy, and Astronomy in Mesopotamian Culture* (Cambridge University Press, 2004), pp. 1-43, on Babylonian celestial omens and *Enūma Anu Enlil*.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 84-85, arguing *ʾôt* here means calendar markers.
Walter C. Kaiser, Peter H. Davids, F. F. Bruce, and Manfred T. Brauch, *Hard Sayings of the Bible* (InterVarsity Press, 1996), pp. 144-145, harmonizing ''signs'' as calendrical rather than omens.', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Genesis, Leviticus, Deuteronomy', 6, 3, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

Matthew alone produces two animals. Matthew 21:2 has Jesus send for ''a donkey tied, and a colt with her'' (*onon dedemenēn kai pōlon met'' autēs*); 21:5 quotes Zechariah with the Greek *kai* read as straightforwardly additive (''on a donkey, *and* on a colt'') rather than as parallelism; and 21:7 then reports that the disciples ''brought the donkey and the colt (*tēn onon kai ton pōlon*), and put their cloaks on them (*ep'' autōn*), and he sat on them (*epanō autōn*).'' The two-animal reading is built into the entire pericope, not a slip in one verse.

The apologetic harmonization runs along two paths, and both are defended in their strongest form by Blomberg and by Carson in the Expositor''s commentary. Path one: the final *autōn* (''on them'') refers to the garments, not the animals — Jesus sat on the cloaks, which had been spread on both beasts. This is grammatically possible: *autōn* is a bare genitive plural and Greek allows some latitude in antecedent. But it is awkward, because the nearest and most natural antecedent of *epanō autōn* is the immediately preceding pair of animals, and the *ep'' autōn* clause earlier in the same verse plainly does place the cloaks ''on them'' (the animals). Path two: the mother donkey was simply led alongside for the colt''s sake — an unbroken colt being calmer with its dam present — and Matthew, knowing two animals were involved, mentioned both while Jesus actually rode only the colt. This is the most plausible defense and cannot be strictly disproven; but it rescues Matthew only by importing Mark/Luke/John''s single rider back into Matthew and treating Matthew''s own *epanō autōn* as loose.

The decisive evidence is redaction-critical and lies in Matthew''s demonstrable compositional habit. Working from Mark (Markan priority being the consensus solution to the Synoptic Problem), Matthew repeatedly doubles single figures in his source: Mark''s one Gerasene demoniac becomes two (Matt 8:28 vs. Mark 5:2); Mark''s one blind man at Jericho, Bartimaeus, becomes two (Matt 20:30 vs. Mark 10:46). Whatever the reason for this pattern, it shows Matthew is capable of numerical adjustment for his own purposes. In the triumphal entry the adjustment is driven by the formula-citation: Matthew has the strongest interest among the evangelists in showing Jesus fulfilling Scripture down to the detail, and here that interest led him to read Zechariah''s parallel cola as two distinct animals and to stage the scene accordingly. Davies and Allison (ICC), Luz (Hermeneia), and Nolland (NIGTC) converge on this judgment.

This is therefore not a case of two narrators describing one event in mutually exclusive ways at the level of brute fact; it is a case of Matthew''s account requiring Jesus to be associated with two animals in a way the other three Gospels and the Hebrew of Zechariah do not, generated by a misconstrual of poetic parallelism. The harmonizations do not eliminate the difficulty so much as relocate it: either the syntax of *epanō autōn* must be strained, or Matthew must be conceded to be vague in precisely the way that conveniently reconciles him with his sources — which concedes the redactional point.

Verdict: genuine compositional discrepancy — Matthew''s two animals against the single colt of Mark, Luke, and John.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, vol. 3 (International Critical Commentary; T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 114-125, on Matthew''s literalizing of Zechariah''s parallelism.
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 21-28: A Commentary*, trans. James E. Crouch (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2005), pp. 5-14, on the redaction-critical reading of the two animals.
John Nolland, *The Gospel of Matthew* (New International Greek Testament Commentary; Eerdmans, 2005), pp. 838-845, on the Greek of Matthew 21:7.
Carol L. Meyers and Eric M. Meyers, *Zechariah 9-14: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Bible 25C; Doubleday, 1993), pp. 124-131, on Zechariah 9:9 as synonymous parallelism.
Craig L. Blomberg, *The Historical Reliability of the Gospels*, 2nd ed. (InterVarsity Press, 2007), pp. 175-178, on the harmonizing defense of Matthew''s two animals.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke, John', 10, 2, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Against this the catalogue of ''do not tempt God'' texts is real and uses the cognate vocabulary. Deuteronomy 6:16, *lōʾ tĕnassû ʾet-YHWH* (''you shall not test YHWH''), from the root *nsh*; Exodus 17:2 (Massah) uses the same root for Israel''s testing of God; the LXX renders these with *ekpeirazō/peirazō*. Jesus throws Deuteronomy 6:16 at the tempter (Matt 4:7; Luke 4:12), Peter charges the circumcision party with ''testing God'' (*peirazete ton theon*, Acts 15:10), and Judith 8:11-12 rebukes the elders for putting God to the test.

The harmonization defended by Moo and Davids is a lexical one, and it is sound. *peirazō* (like Hebrew *nsh*) bears two distinct senses, both registered in BDAG: (1) to *test/try/put to the proof*, and (2) to *tempt/entice toward sin*. The ''do not tempt God'' texts uniformly belong to sense (1) — humans presuming to put God on trial, demanding he prove himself, provoking him to act (Massah: ''Is the LORD among us or not?''). James 1:13 belongs to sense (2) — God being enticed *toward evil*, which James explicitly qualifies with *kakōn*. These are not the same act. To say ''God cannot be lured into sinning'' and ''humans wrongly put God to the proof'' is no more contradictory than to say a judge cannot be bribed while litigants nonetheless try his patience. The shared root masks two different relations.

The genuinely unresolved pressure point is Christological, and the entry is right to press it. Hebrews 4:15 states that the high priest ''has been tested in every respect as we are, yet without sin'' — *pepeirasmenon de kata panta kath'' homoiotēta chōris hamartias*. The verb is *peirazō*, and the temptation narratives (Matt 4:1; Luke 4:2) describe Satan''s solicitation of Jesus, which slides toward sense (2). If the incarnate Son is *homoousios* with the Father (Nicaea/Chalcedon), and the Son was ''tested in every respect,'' then on the surface the *apeirastos kakōn* of James and the *pepeirasmenon kata panta* of Hebrews sit in tension. The orthodox resolution is the two-natures distinction: the Son was genuinely tested according to his assumed human nature, while the divine nature remained impassible and unsusceptible to sin — and Hebrews'' own *chōris hamartias* (''without sin'') signals that the testing never issued in, and on the classical view never could issue in, sin. On that account James and Hebrews are describing the same person under different natures, and the *kata panta* is qualified by the immediately following *chōris hamartias*.

Whether one regards that as a clean resolution or a sophisticated patch depends on one''s prior commitments. As a matter of lexicon and immediate context, James 1:13 and the ''tempt not God'' texts are not in contradiction at all — they use *peirazō*/*nsh* in different senses, as Allison and Johnson grant even while noting James''s debt to Hellenistic-Jewish ideas of divine impassibility (cf. the Wisdom tradition and Philo). The Christological strand is where a critic can still find a seam, but it is a seam the two-natures doctrine is precisely engineered to close, and it requires importing later conciliar Christology to make the Son''s testing a problem for James in the first place.

Verdict: largely harmonizable on lexical grounds (two senses of *peirazō*); a residual Christological tension that orthodox two-natures theology absorbs but does not erase.', 'Dale C. Allison Jr., *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Epistle of James* (International Critical Commentary; Bloomsbury T&T Clark, 2013), pp. 245-258, on *apeirastos* and James''s debt to Hellenistic-Jewish impassibility.
Luke Timothy Johnson, *The Letter of James: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Bible 37A; Doubleday, 1995), pp. 192-196, on the meaning of *apeirastos kakōn*.
Douglas J. Moo, *The Letter of James* (Pillar New Testament Commentary; Eerdmans, 2000), pp. 71-76, on the two-senses harmonization of *peirazō*.
Peter H. Davids, *The Epistle of James: A Commentary on the Greek Text* (New International Greek Testament Commentary; Eerdmans, 1982), pp. 80-84, on the test vs. tempt distinction.
Harold W. Attridge, *The Epistle to the Hebrews* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1989), pp. 139-143, on Jesus tested *kata panta* in Hebrews 4:15.
Frederick W. Danker, ed., *A Greek-English Lexicon of the New Testament and Other Early Christian Literature*, 3rd ed. (BDAG; University of Chicago Press, 2000), s.v. πειράζω, giving both ''test'' and ''tempt to sin.''', 0, NULL, 2, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Exodus, Deuteronomy, Malachi, Judith, Matthew, Luke, Acts, Hebrews, James', 5, 3, 3, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

Two classical harmonizations dominate, and each should be stated at full strength. The first, the levirate theory, goes back to Julius Africanus (early third century), whose letter to Aristides is preserved by Eusebius (*Ecclesiastical History* 1.7) and was revived in the twentieth century by Machen. On this view one Gospel names Joseph''s biological father and the other his legal father, the two lines having crossed through a levirate marriage (Deut 25:5-10): a man died childless, his half-brother (of the other Davidic line) married the widow, and the son was reckoned legally to the deceased but biologically to the living father. Africanus specifically located the crossing at Heli and Jacob (Matthew''s Jacob and Luke''s Heli being half-brothers by the same mother). The theory is internally coherent and not impossible, but it is unfalsifiable and rests on a tradition Africanus says came from Jesus''s relatives (the *desposynoi*) for which there is no independent control; it requires the levirate union to fall at precisely the generation needed, and the two lines must then diverge all the way back to David rather than only at the last step, which a single levirate marriage does not explain.

The second, the Mary theory, was popularized by Annius of Viterbo and John Lightfoot and is standard in modern apologetics (Geisler and Howe): Matthew gives Joseph''s line, Luke gives Mary''s, so the two trace Jesus''s descent through both legal-paternal and biological-maternal channels. Its fatal difficulty is the Greek of Luke 3:23: *ōn huios, hōs enomizeto, Iōsēph tou Hēli* — ''being the son, as was supposed, of Joseph, the son of Heli.'' Luke names Joseph, not Mary, and Heli as Joseph''s father. To salvage the theory one must read ''of Heli'' as ''son-in-law of Heli,'' a sense the bare genitive does not bear and which no ancient reader would supply, especially as every other link in Luke''s chain is a literal father-son genitive. Marshall and Fitzmyer both note that the *hōs enomizeto* (''as was supposed'') already does the work the harmonizers want — it flags the *legal* and *putative* nature of Joseph''s fatherhood given the virginal conception — without any appeal to Mary.

The critical consensus (Brown, *Birth of the Messiah*; Fitzmyer, AB Luke; Bauckham, *Jude and the Relatives of Jesus*) is that the two genealogies are independent constructions, each assembled to establish Davidic descent but drawing on different traditions and serving different theological ends. Matthew''s Solomonic royal line presents Jesus as the legitimate heir to the throne of Judah, structured into his stylized 3×14 (the *gematria* of David, dwd = 14). Luke''s line through Nathan — David''s son but not a king — may deliberately route the messiah around the discredited royal house (Jeremiah''s ''curse'' on Jeconiah, Jer 22:30, falls on Matthew''s line) and toward a non-royal Davidic descent; some have linked it to the prominence of ''the house of Nathan'' in the messianic mourning of Zechariah 12:12. Bauckham further argues both lists incorporate genuine pre-Gospel family tradition, which would explain the Shealtiel-Zerubbabel overlap without making either list a complete biological record.

The decisive point is logical: two lineages passing through two different sons of David cannot both be Joseph''s biological ancestry, and the harmonizations each pay a price — the levirate theory invents an unattested marriage at a convenient point and still cannot explain divergence all the way back to David, while the Mary theory must override the plain Greek of Luke 3:23.

Verdict: genuine genealogical discrepancy; the two principal harmonizations require either an unattested levirate marriage or a forced reading of Luke''s Greek.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Birth of the Messiah: A Commentary on the Infancy Narratives in the Gospels of Matthew and Luke*, new ed. (Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, 1993), pp. 57-95, 587-596, on the two genealogies as independent theological constructions.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke I-IX: Introduction, Translation, and Notes* (Anchor Bible 28; Doubleday, 1981), pp. 488-505, on Luke 3:23 and the Mary theory.
Richard Bauckham, *Jude and the Relatives of Jesus in the Early Church* (T&T Clark, 1990), pp. 315-373, on pre-Gospel family tradition in the genealogies.
I. Howard Marshall, *The Gospel of Luke: A Commentary on the Greek Text* (New International Greek Testament Commentary; Eerdmans, 1978), pp. 157-161, on the Greek of Luke 3:23.
J. Gresham Machen, *The Virgin Birth of Christ* (Harper, 1930), pp. 202-209, reviving the Africanus levirate theory.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 316-317, on harmonizing the two genealogies.
Norman L. Geisler and Thomas Howe, *When Critics Ask: A Popular Handbook on Bible Difficulties* (Victor Books, 1992), pp. 385-386, on the Mary-genealogy harmonization.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Luke', 3, 3, 4, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Two harmonizations have dominated since antiquity, and each deserves its strongest form. The first, transmitted by Julius Africanus (in his *Letter to Aristides*, preserved in Eusebius, *Ecclesiastical History* 1.7), invokes levirate marriage (Deut 25:5-10): Heli died childless, his uterine brother Jacob took the widow, and the resulting son Joseph was Jacob''s biological child but Heli''s legal heir. Africanus claimed this on the authority of the *desposynoi*, Jesus''s surviving relatives, which gives the tradition a respectable pedigree. Against it: the scheme requires Jacob and Heli to share a mother but different fathers (since Matthew''s and Luke''s lines diverge far above this point), an elaborate stipulation with no independent attestation; and crucially, fixing only the final generation does nothing to explain why the entire lists above Joseph differ — Matthew runs through Solomon and the royal Judahite kings, Luke through Nathan and a parallel non-royal line. The levirate device patches one rung of a ladder that diverges along its whole length.

The second harmonization, that Luke gives Mary''s line and *tou Hēli* means "son-in-law of Heli," became popular in Protestant exegesis through Luther and especially Annius of Viterbo, and was systematized by John Lightfoot and later by Geisler. Its appeal is that it makes both genealogies literally true of distinct people. But the Greek resists it. *Tou Hēli* is the identical articular genitive construction Luke uses for all seventy-six links in the chain (*tou Maththat, tou Leui*, and so on); to read this one slot as "son-in-law" while reading every other as "son" is special pleading unsupported by any parallel in Greek genealogical writing, where a man is never silently entered under his wife''s father''s name. Luke 3:23 also frames the whole list as Joseph''s ("as was supposed, of Joseph"), not Mary''s; she is not named.

The parsimonious critical reading is that Matthew and Luke drew on independent traditional materials, both purporting to supply Joseph''s ancestry, and neither redactor attempted reconciliation. This fits a larger pattern: the two infancy narratives agree on remarkably little — Matthew has magi, a star, Herod''s massacre, and a flight to Egypt; Luke has shepherds, a Roman census, and a peaceful return to Nazareth. Brown''s *Birth of the Messiah* treats the genealogies as theological constructions serving each Evangelist''s distinct portrait (Matthew''s royal-Davidic, Luke''s universal-Adamic) rather than as competing transcriptions of a single archive. Davies and Allison and Fitzmyer concur that the divergence is irreducible on historical-critical grounds. Bauckham''s careful study of the *desposynoi* lends the Africanus tradition more credit than usual but still cannot close the gap above Joseph.

Verdict: genuine contradiction. Matthew names Jacob, Luke names Heli, and the harmonizations require either an unattested levirate arrangement that fixes only one rung or a grammatically forced "son-in-law" the Greek will not bear.', 'Eusebius of Caesarea, *Ecclesiastical History* 1.7 (LCL 153; Harvard University Press, 1926), preserving Julius Africanus''s *Letter to Aristides*, on the levirate harmonization of Matthew 1:16 and Luke 3:23
Raymond E. Brown, *The Birth of the Messiah: A Commentary on the Infancy Narratives in the Gospels of Matthew and Luke* (new ed.; Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, 1993), pp. 84-94, 503-504, on the divergent genealogies
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke I-IX* (AB 28; Doubleday, 1981), pp. 491-492, 499-500, on Luke 3:23
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, vol. 1 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 173-181, on Matthew 1:16
Richard Bauckham, *Jude and the Relatives of Jesus in the Early Church* (T&T Clark, 1990), pp. 315-373, on the desposynoi traditions
Norman L. Geisler and Thomas Howe, *When Critics Ask: A Popular Handbook on Bible Difficulties* (Victor Books, 1992), pp. 385-386, on the Mary''s-genealogy harmonization', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Luke', 3, 3, 5, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The organizing principle is not chronological but numerological. "Fourteen" is the gematria value of David''s name in Hebrew: D-V-D (dalet-waw-dalet) = 4 + 6 + 4 = 14. Matthew, writing a Davidic-messianic Gospel that opens *biblos geneseōs Iēsou Christou huiou Dauid* (1:1), structures the genealogy so that David''s number governs all three epochs of Israel''s story — patriarchs to king, king to exile, exile to Messiah. Davies-Allison, Luz, and Brown all regard the 3×14 as a deliberate mnemonic-theological device rather than a census, and most note that Matthew has pruned his source to hit the number: between Joram and Uzziah (1:8) he omits three reigning Judahite kings — Ahaziah, Joash, and Amaziah — who appear in the Chronicler''s line (1 Chr 3:11-12). That omission is the clearest evidence that the count is engineered.

The counting itself is genuinely defensible once one grasps the design. From Abraham to David is fourteen with David as the fourteenth. From David (or Solomon) to the exile is fourteen. From the exile to Jesus is fourteen if Jechoniah is counted in this third panel. The standard evangelical harmonization, articulated by Carson, makes David and Jechoniah "pivot" figures, each belonging to two epochs — David closes group one and opens group two; the deportation generation closes group two and Jechoniah opens group three. This is not an ad hoc rescue: Matthew''s own wording invites it, since each summary clause is bounded by an event (David, the deportation, Christ) and the boundary figures naturally sit on both sides. On this reading the arithmetic of the *clauses* is internally coherent.

What the harmonization concedes is the more interesting point. If David and Jechoniah are each counted twice to reach forty-two, then the number of distinct human generations from Abraham to Jesus is forty-one, not forty-two — a single person is not two generations. The text is best read as claiming forty-two named *positions* across an overlapping triadic scheme, not forty-two consecutive begettings. There is also a textual wrinkle: a number of witnesses and the surrounding tradition show confusion at 1:11 over whether Jehoiakim should stand between Josiah and Jechoniah, and some early scribes evidently tried to add a name to round the third group to a clean fourteen — evidence that ancient readers already felt the count was tight. Whether one calls Matthew''s procedure a "manipulation" or a "stylized structure" is largely a matter of register; what is clear is that the scheme is numerological and that the historical sequence has been compressed to serve it.

The fair verdict is that the apparent shortfall dissolves once the pivot-counting and the deliberate gematria design are recognized — the forty-two is reached by counting boundary figures in both adjacent panels, which Matthew''s structure both permits and, arguably, intends. But the resolution is purchased at the cost of historical completeness: three real kings are missing, and "forty-two generations" means forty-two scheme-positions, not forty-two distinct men. Verdict: overstated as a flat contradiction; the count is harmonizable through Matthew''s own triadic gematria, but only by admitting the genealogy is theological architecture, not arithmetic.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, vol. 1 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 161-165, 184-187, on the 3x14 scheme
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 1-7: A Commentary*, trans. James E. Crouch (rev. ed.; Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 81-90, on the numerological structure
Raymond E. Brown, *The Birth of the Messiah* (new ed.; Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, 1993), pp. 69-84, on Matthew''s genealogy
John Nolland, *The Gospel of Matthew* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 2005), pp. 76-89, on the genealogical count
D. A. Carson, *Matthew* (rev. ed.; Expositor''s Bible Commentary 8; Zondervan, 2010), pp. 92-95, on the pivot-figure harmonization', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew', 1, 3, 4, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The count runs: Jechoniah begot Salathiel (1), Salathiel Zerubbabel (2), Zerubbabel Abiud (3), Abiud Eliakim (4), Eliakim Azor (5), Azor Zadok (6), Zadok Achim (7), Achim Eliud (8), Eliud Eleazar (9), Eleazar Matthan (10), Matthan Jacob (11), Jacob Joseph (12), and then Jesus is born of Mary (13). Thirteen begettings after Jechoniah, or thirteen new generations following the exile.

The harmonization, standard in evangelical handbooks (Carson, Blomberg, the *Hard Sayings* volume), counts Jechoniah himself as the first member of the third group. He is the natural pivot: he is the king under whom the deportation occurred (2 Kgs 24:15), so he closes the second epoch ("from David to the deportation are fourteen") and opens the third. Counting Jechoniah (1), Salathiel (2), through to Jesus (14) yields the stated fourteen. As with the David pivot in the first transition, this is not arbitrary — Matthew''s epoch boundaries are events (the deportation, the Christ) on which a single named figure sits. So the clause-arithmetic is internally satisfiable.

The matter is complicated by a genuine textual and onomastic problem at 1:11. Matthew there has Josiah begetting "Jechonias and his brethren" at the time of the deportation, but the historical sequence (1 Chr 3:15-16) is Josiah → Jehoiakim → Jeconiah/Jehoiachin. Jehoiakim has dropped out, and "Jechonias" at 1:11 may originally have been Jehoiakim, with Jeconiah/Jehoiachin the figure at 1:12. Several minuscules and patristic citations (and the Old Latin tradition) show scribes adding *Iōakim* at 1:11 precisely to restore the missing king and, not incidentally, to repair the third group''s count to a full fourteen. That ancient corrective effort is itself evidence that early readers perceived the shortfall — and it suggests the most economical solution may be that a name was lost in transmission, with the original list distinguishing Jehoiakim (end of group two) from Jeconiah (start of group three).

Whatever the mechanism, the deeper feature is that the genealogy is numerological, not historical. "Fourteen" encodes David''s name by gematria (D-V-D = 14), and the three-times-fourteen architecture serves Matthew''s Davidic Christology. The implausible generation-spacing confirms this: the first panel spans roughly a millennium (Abraham to David) across fourteen names — about seventy-one years per generation; the second spans some four centuries across fourteen — about twenty-nine years each; the third spans roughly six centuries (ca. 597 BCE to ca. 4 BCE) across thirteen-to-fourteen names — about forty-six years each. No real lineage produces such erratic averages; the panels are leveled to the number, not the chronology. The Chronicler''s records show Matthew compressed and selected, and the third panel is where the source material for the post-exilic period was thinnest, which is exactly where a name could most easily be missing or absorbed.

Verdict: overstated as a flat contradiction. The fourteen is recoverable either by counting Jechoniah as the third panel''s first member (the pivot reading Matthew''s structure invites) or by recognizing a likely Jehoiakim/Jeconiah conflation at 1:11 — a textual rather than logical fault. What does not resolve is the historical character of the list: the panels are gematria-driven theological architecture, and "fourteen" describes scheme-positions, not a verifiable count of distinct post-exilic generations.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, vol. 1 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 184-187, on the third-panel count
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 1-7: A Commentary*, trans. James E. Crouch (rev. ed.; Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 87-90, on the count and gematria
Raymond E. Brown, *The Birth of the Messiah* (new ed.; Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, 1993), pp. 74-84, on the Jechoniah/Jehoiakim issue
John Nolland, *The Gospel of Matthew* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 2005), pp. 86-89, on the textual variants at Matthew 1:11
Walter C. Kaiser et al., *Hard Sayings of the Bible* (InterVarsity Press, 1996), pp. 372-374, on the pivot-counting harmonization', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew', 1, 3, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The Davidic claim is widespread and emphatic. Paul opens Romans with a formula most scholars judge to be pre-Pauline and creedal: Jesus *tou genomenou ek spermatos Dauid kata sarka* — "who came from the seed of David according to the flesh" (1:3). The phrase is not decorative. It stands in deliberate antithesis to *kata pneuma hagiōsynēs* in 1:4 ("according to the spirit of holiness"), the two-stage flesh/spirit contrast structuring the whole confession; *kata sarka* locates the Davidic descent in the ordinary human, fleshly sphere. Jewett and Fitzmyer both read it as genealogical descent through the male line, which is how "seed of David" functioned in Second Temple messianism: 4QFlorilegium reads the "shoot of David" of the Nathan oracle (2 Sam 7) as a coming royal deliverer, and *Psalms of Solomon* 17 prays for a Davidic king to be raised up by physical succession. Acts 2:30 ("of the fruit of his loins"), Acts 13:23, 2 Timothy 2:8, and Revelation 22:16 ("the root and offspring of David") echo the same biological-dynastic logic. Both Gospel genealogies establish that descent through Joseph — Matthew through the royal Solomonic line (1:6-16), Luke through Nathan (3:23-31) — not through Mary.

The virginal-conception narratives then sever Joseph from the biological chain. Matthew 1:18 says Mary was found *en gastri echousa ek pneumatos hagiou* — "with child of the Holy Spirit" — before she and Joseph came together, and 1:25 specifies that Joseph "knew her not" until after the birth. Luke 1:34-35 has Mary protest that she "knows no man," and the angel answer that the Holy Spirit will come upon her. If Joseph supplies no "seed," the *kata sarka* descent through Joseph has no physical mechanism.

The strongest harmonization is legal adoption. Under Jewish custom, paternal acknowledgment — Joseph naming the child (Matt 1:25; cf. Luke 2:21) — confers full sonship, including inheritance and lineage rights. Brown lays this out fairly (pp. 138-139): Jesus is Joseph''s legal son and therefore legally a son of David, and royal/messianic claims in antiquity were transmitted by legal standing, not genetics. This genuinely resolves the *dynastic* question. What it cannot resolve is the *fleshly* one. The creedal formula Paul cites does not say "of the house of David" or "reckoned to David" but "of the seed of David *according to the flesh*," set against "according to the spirit" — and adoption is precisely not a fleshly relation. The authors of Acts, Romans, Hebrews, and Revelation are not making an adoption-law claim; they are making a descent claim, and adoption supplies legal paternity, not flesh.

The second harmonization, that Mary herself was of Davidic descent, would supply the missing biology. But neither Matthew nor Luke says so; both give Joseph''s line and present Mary''s Davidic status nowhere. The claim is a post-canonical development: it surfaces in Justin Martyr (*Dialogue with Trypho* 43, 100) and Irenaeus (*Against Heresies* 3.21.5), who assert Mary''s Davidic descent precisely to close this gap, and the second-century *Protevangelium of James* makes her a daughter of the house of David. Lincoln''s monograph documents how the early church reached for Mary''s lineage exactly because the canonical texts left the *kata sarka* claim mechanically unsupported once Joseph was removed.

Verdict: genuine, canon-internal contradiction. Legal adoption rescues Jesus''s *dynastic* Davidic title but not the repeated, creedally fixed claim of descent "according to the flesh"; the patch that would rescue the flesh-claim — Mary''s own Davidic descent — is read into, not out of, the Gospels.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Birth of the Messiah* (new ed.; Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, 1993), pp. 133-143, 287-316, 517-524, on adoption and Davidic descent
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *Romans: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AB 33; Doubleday, 1993), pp. 233-237, on Romans 1:3
Robert Jewett, *Romans: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 103-108, on Romans 1:3-4
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, vol. 1 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 197-222, on Matthew 1:18-25
Andrew T. Lincoln, *Born of a Virgin? Reconceiving Jesus in the Bible, Tradition, and Theology* (Eerdmans, 2013), pp. 67-97, on the canonical tension
Justin Martyr, *Dialogue with Trypho* 43, 100 (ANF 1, 1885), on Mary''s Davidic descent', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Luke, Acts, Romans, 2 Timothy, Hebrews, Revelation', 5, 3, 4, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Matthew anchors the birth to the reign of Herod the Great. The magi consult Herod (2:1-8), Herod orders the slaughter of Bethlehem''s infants (2:16), and the family returns from Egypt only "when Herod was dead" (2:19-20). Herod''s death is firmly dated to 4 BCE: Josephus (*Antiquities* 17.167, 17.213; *War* 1.665) reports that a lunar eclipse preceded it and that he died before a Passover, and the eclipse of March 4 BCE fits the sequence. Matthew''s terminus ante quem is therefore 4 BCE; with the "two years and under" of 2:16, the birth falls around 6-4 BCE. Luke 1:5 independently agrees, dating the conception of John the Baptist — and so Jesus six months later (1:26, 1:36) — to "the days of Herod, king of Judaea."

Luke 2:1-2 then introduces a second, incompatible anchor: a worldwide enrollment decreed by Augustus, "this first registration (*hautē apographē prōtē*) made while Quirinius was governing Syria" (*hēgemoneuontos tēs Syrias Kyrēniou*). Publius Sulpicius Quirinius''s Syrian command and the associated provincial census are securely dated to 6 CE, when Judaea was annexed as a Roman province following Archelaus''s deposition. Josephus (*Antiquities* 18.1-2, 18.26) describes this census in detail and reports the revolt of Judas the Galilean that it provoked — the very figure Luke himself, in Acts 5:37, places "in the days of the census." Luke thus knew the 6 CE census as a singular, memorable event, which makes his linking of it to a birth under Herod the more striking. The interval between the two anchors is at least nine or ten years.

The harmonizations are well-rehearsed. (1) Render *prōtē* comparatively — "this enrollment was *before* Quirinius governed Syria" — a reading N. T. Wright and earlier scholars proposed. Grammatically it strains: *prōtē* followed by a genitive absolute is most naturally the superlative "first," and the comparative sense would more typically require *protera* or *pro tou*; the construction is unusual and the proposal is a minority expedient. (2) Postulate an earlier, otherwise unattested Quirinius governorship of Syria. Sherwin-White and the standard prosopography of Roman Syria leave no room: the legates of the relevant years are documented — Sentius Saturninus (ca. 9-6 BCE), Quinctilius Varus (ca. 6-4 BCE), and later Volusius Saturninus — with Quirinius''s known career placing his Syrian command in 6 CE. The *Lapis Tiburtinus* (the "Tibur inscription"), often cited for a double governorship, does not name its honorand and does not specify Syria; the *Lapis Venetus* attests Quirinius conducting a census at Apamea but is fully consistent with his 6 CE command. No inscription places him in Syria before 6 CE. (3) Some appeal to a Herodian-era "client-kingdom" enrollment; but Rome did not conduct provincial property censuses in an independent client kingdom, which is exactly why the 6 CE annexation triggered the first such registration and the revolt.

The critical consensus — Brown, Fitzmyer, Bovon, Schürer''s revisers, and many moderates — is that Luke, writing decades later and from a distance, conflated the well-known 6 CE census with a birth that actually occurred under Herod, because the census furnished the narrative device he needed: a Roman administrative summons that could move a Galilean couple from Nazareth to David''s Bethlehem and so fulfill Micah 5:2. The historical implausibilities compound the dating error — Roman censuses taxed property where one resided, not ancestral towns, and did not require pregnant women to travel.

Verdict: genuine contradiction. Matthew''s date is before 4 BCE; Luke''s census anchor is 6 CE; and the rescues require either an undocumented earlier Quirinius governorship that the Roman record excludes or a comparative reading of *prōtē* the Greek resists.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Birth of the Messiah* (new ed.; Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, 1993), pp. 547-556, 666-668, on the Quirinius census
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke I-IX* (AB 28; Doubleday, 1981), pp. 392-405, on Luke 2:1-4
François Bovon, *Luke 1: A Commentary on the Gospel of Luke 1:1-9:50*, trans. Christine M. Thomas (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2002), pp. 83-86, on the census
A. N. Sherwin-White, *Roman Society and Roman Law in the New Testament* (Clarendon, 1963), pp. 162-171, on the Quirinius governorship
Emil Schürer, *The History of the Jewish People in the Age of Jesus Christ*, rev. and ed. Geza Vermes, Fergus Millar, and Matthew Black, vol. 1 (T&T Clark, 1973), pp. 399-427, on the census and Syrian legates
Flavius Josephus, *Jewish Antiquities* 17.167-213; 18.1-2, 18.26 (LCL; Harvard University Press, 1965), on Herod''s death and the Quirinius census', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Luke', 2, 3, 5, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The translation of *katalyma* sharpens, rather than dissolves, the picture. The KJV''s "inn" is almost certainly wrong; Luke uses *pandocheion* for a commercial inn (Luke 10:34), whereas *katalyma* is his word for a guest room (the same term denotes the Upper Room in Luke 22:11). Bailey and others argue persuasively that the scene is not a rejection at a Bethlehem hotel but a crowded family dwelling whose guest space was full, so the newborn was laid in the animals'' feeding trough in the lower living area. If that is right, Luke''s "manger" and Matthew''s "house" are not even opposed in kind — both could describe one extended-family residence.

Where the harmonization frays is chronology, not architecture. Matthew calls the child a *paidion* (young child, Matt 2:11), not the *brephos* (infant/newborn) that Luke uses at 2:12 and 2:16. More decisively, Herod''s order to kill the boys *apo dietous kai katōterō* — "two years old and under" — was set "according to the time he had ascertained from the magi" (Matt 2:16), implying the star and the visit could be placed up to two years after the birth. Matthew''s narrative thus envisions the family settled in a Bethlehem house for a substantial period, perhaps as long as two years. Luke, by contrast, has the family complete the purification rites on the fortieth day (Lev 12:4) and return to Nazareth (Luke 2:39) — no protracted Bethlehem residence, no house, no magi.

So the manger-versus-house tension is reconcilable when quarantined, but the reconciliation cannot be quarantined. To keep the family in a Bethlehem *oikia* long enough for Matthew''s chronology, one must overturn Luke''s quick return to Nazareth; to keep Luke''s quick return, one must compress Matthew''s two-year horizon into days. Brown (Birth of the Messiah) and Fitzmyer treat the two infancy narratives as independent compositions that share only the bare facts of a Bethlehem birth and Nazareth upbringing, reasoning back to those points from incompatible starting assumptions. Davies and Allison make the same judgment from the Matthean side. The "house" detail is therefore best understood not as a contradiction of the manger but as a thread that, when pulled, unravels into the larger geographic incompatibility treated in entries 393 and 394.

Verdict: harmonizable in isolation (manger and house can be sequential, and *katalyma* may not even mean "inn"), but the reconciliation only relocates the problem into the incompatible infancy itineraries.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Birth of the Messiah: A Commentary on the Infancy Narratives in the Gospels of Matthew and Luke* (Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, new ed. 1993), pp. 36-37, 175-201, 668-672, on the independence of the two infancy narratives.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke I-IX: Introduction, Translation, and Notes* (Anchor Bible 28; Doubleday, 1981), pp. 406-410, on katalyma and the manger scene.
Kenneth E. Bailey, *Jesus Through Middle Eastern Eyes: Cultural Studies in the Gospels* (InterVarsity Press, 2008), pp. 25-37, arguing that katalyma denotes a family guest room rather than a commercial inn.
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 1 (International Critical Commentary; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 247-258, on the magi pericope and Herod''s chronology.
Craig L. Blomberg, *The Historical Reliability of the Gospels*, 2nd ed. (InterVarsity Press, 2007), pp. 39-43, harmonizing the infancy settings sequentially.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Luke', 10, 2, 4, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

Matthew supplies no pre-Bethlehem backstory whatsoever. Joseph and Mary first appear together already in Bethlehem (Matt 2:1), and the magi find them not in temporary census lodging but in an *oikia*, a house (Matt 2:11). Nazareth is never mentioned until the family relocates there after the Egyptian sojourn, and even then the language is telling: *anechōrēsen eis ta merē tēs Galilaias* … *katōkēsen eis polin legomenēn Nazaret* — "he withdrew into the regions of Galilee … and settled in a city called Nazareth" (Matt 2:22-23). The verb *katoikeō* ("settle, take up residence") denotes establishing a new dwelling, not returning home, and Matthew frames the move as the fulfillment of a prophecy ("He shall be called a Nazarene") rather than as a homecoming. A reader with only Matthew would naturally infer that Bethlehem was the family''s original home and Nazareth a later, providentially directed relocation.

The strongest harmonization — advanced by Blomberg and Witherington — interleaves the accounts: Luke narrates the Nazareth-to-Bethlehem census journey, Matthew narrates a later moment with the family still in Bethlehem, and Luke 2:39 is read as a compressed summary that silently passes over the Egypt detour before the eventual Nazareth settlement. The proposal is not absurd; ancient narrators routinely abbreviate. But it founders on Luke''s own grammar. *Epestrepsan* ("they returned") presents the move to Nazareth as continuous with the temple visit, and *eis polin heautōn* presupposes Nazareth was already theirs. To make the harmonization work one must hold that Luke knew the Egypt tradition and deliberately erased it while writing a sentence whose verb and possessive both deny any gap — a maneuver Brown and Fitzmyer both reject as reading into the text what is not there.

The more economical critical explanation is that Matthew and Luke inherited independent traditions about the family''s residence and reasoned backward to the fixed point both needed: a birth in Bethlehem to satisfy Micah 5:2''s prophecy of a ruler from Bethlehem, combined with the universally known fact that Jesus was "of Nazareth." Luz argues that Matthew, assuming Bethlehem was home, required a mechanism (Herod''s threat, the flight to Egypt, the prophetic "Nazarene" formula) to deposit the family in Galilee. Luke, assuming Nazareth was home, required a mechanism (the census) to relocate the birth to Bethlehem. Both narratives are theology-driven solutions to the same constraint, approached from opposite ends. Davies and Allison reach the same verdict from the Matthean side, and Bovon from the Lukan.

Verdict: genuine contradiction. Luke states that Nazareth was the couple''s home before the birth; Matthew implies Bethlehem was, with Nazareth a later prophetically motivated resettlement. The harmonization survives only by attributing to Luke a silent omission that his own *epestrepsan eis polin heautōn Nazaret* contradicts.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Birth of the Messiah* (Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, new ed. 1993), pp. 224-225, 668-672, rejecting the silent-omission harmonization of Luke 2:39.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke I-IX* (Anchor Bible 28; Doubleday, 1981), pp. 425-426, on Luke 2:39 as a continuous-action return to Nazareth.
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 1-7: A Commentary*, trans. James E. Crouch, rev. ed. (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 119-127, on the Nazarene fulfillment formula and Matthean redaction.
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 1 (International Critical Commentary; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 270-281, on the aims of Matthew''s infancy narrative.
François Bovon, *Luke 1: A Commentary on the Gospel of Luke 1:1-9:50*, trans. Christine M. Thomas (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2002), pp. 105-106, on Nazareth as the home presupposed by Luke.
Ben Witherington III, *The Gospel of Luke: A Socio-Rhetorical Commentary* (Eerdmans, 2008), representing the interleaving harmonization of the infancy itineraries.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Luke', 10, 2, 4, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Luke knows nothing of any of this. Luke 2:21-39 moves from circumcision on the eighth day, to the purification offering at the Jerusalem temple on roughly the fortieth day (Lev 12:2-4 prescribes the timing), and then: *hōs etelesan panta ta kata ton nomon kyriou, epestrepsan eis tēn Galilaian eis polin heautōn Nazaret* — "when they had completed everything according to the law of the Lord, they returned to Galilee, to their own city Nazareth" (Luke 2:39). No magi, no Herodian threat, no Egypt. The family proceeds from the temple to Nazareth. The two Gospels thus locate the family in two different countries at roughly the same point in the first year of Jesus''s life.

The apologetic harmonization (Carson, Blomberg, the IVP Hard Sayings tradition) again rests entirely on reading Luke 2:39 as a summary that silently elides the Egyptian detour, on the grounds that Egypt did not serve Luke''s Jerusalem-to-Galilee theological geography. The reading is not impossible, but two features of the data resist it. First, Luke''s Greek is sequential, not summarizing: *epestrepsan* ("they returned") joined to *eis polin heautōn* ("to their own city") narrates an immediate, completed return with no signaled temporal gap. Second, Matthew''s own structure requires the magi visit *before* the flight (Matt 2:13). To fit Matthew''s magi-and-Egypt material into the space between Luke''s day-forty temple presentation and Luke''s Nazareth return, one must reconstruct the family travelling Bethlehem → Jerusalem (temple) → back to Bethlehem → receiving the magi → fleeing to Egypt → returning from Egypt → finally Nazareth — three Bethlehem-Jerusalem legs plus an international detour, the entirety of which Luke''s single verb supposedly conceals. A reconstruction is "possible" only in the sense that any silence can be filled with whatever is needed.

The critical consensus (Brown, Fitzmyer, Bovon, Allison) is that the two infancy narratives are independent compositions built on incompatible traditions, each satisfying the shared constraints of a Bethlehem birth and a Nazareth upbringing by a different narrative route — and each route is theologically shaped. Matthew''s is Moses-typological, as Allison''s monograph documents in detail: a Pharaoh-like Herod, the slaughter of the male children echoing Exodus 1, the family''s descent into and call out of Egypt sealed by *ex Aigyptou ekalesa ton huion mou* ("out of Egypt I called my son," Matt 2:15, citing Hosea 11:1, a verse whose original referent is the nation Israel, reapplied christologically). Luke''s route is temple- and Jerusalem-centered, framing Jesus''s origins within Israel''s cult and law. The Egypt episode is intelligible as Matthean typology, not as suppressed Lukan history.

Verdict: genuine contradiction. Matthew sends the family to Egypt; Luke takes them straight from the Jerusalem temple to Nazareth. Both cannot be the actual itinerary, and the harmonization that inserts Egypt into the silence of Luke 2:39 is a post hoc rationalization the Greek will not bear.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Birth of the Messiah* (Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, new ed. 1993), pp. 202-230, 668-672, on the incompatible infancy itineraries.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke I-IX* (Anchor Bible 28; Doubleday, 1981), pp. 425-429, on Luke 2:39 leaving no room for an Egyptian sojourn.
Dale C. Allison Jr., *The New Moses: A Matthean Typology* (Fortress, 1993), pp. 140-165, on the Moses typology shaping Matthew''s Egypt narrative.
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 1 (International Critical Commentary; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 259-281, on the flight to Egypt and the Hosea 11:1 citation.
François Bovon, *Luke 1: A Commentary on the Gospel of Luke 1:1-9:50*, trans. Christine M. Thomas (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2002), pp. 104-106, on Luke''s temple-centered structure.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Luke', 10, 2, 4, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The contradiction as the entry states it is partly an artifact of the textual base. The KJV''s "Bethabara" at John 1:28 follows the Textus Receptus, but the earliest and best witnesses (P66, P75, Aleph*, B) read *Bēthania*, which NA28 prints. The variant *Bēthabara* was promoted by Origen in the third century: as Metzger explains, Origen could not locate any "Bethany" beyond the Jordan and, partly on that geographic ground and partly because *Bēthabara* could be glossed as "house of preparation," preferred the alternative reading. The variant is thus best understood as an early scholarly emendation rather than the original text. Once *Bēthania* is recognized as original, the question is simply where this otherwise unattested Transjordanian Bethany lay — clearly not the Bethany near Jerusalem associated with Lazarus (John 11).

The locating of the site remains genuinely uncertain. Murphy-O''Connor surveys the candidates, and excavations at Wadi al-Kharrar / Al-Maghtas on the Jordanian east bank have been proposed as the spot, though the identification is not secure. This residual uncertainty, however, is a problem in historical geography, not a contradiction between Gospels: the synoptics and John agree the baptizing happened at the Jordan; only John ventures a settlement name, and that name''s referent is debated.

The more interesting critical observation is source-critical. The Fourth Gospel displays a recurrent interest in naming specific places — Aenon near Salim "because there was much water there" (John 3:23), Sychar (John 4:5), the pool of Bethesda with its five porticoes (John 5:2) — sometimes for symbolic or apologetic effect, sometimes reflecting independent topographical tradition. That John supplies a place-name where the synoptics give only a generalized wilderness setting (Mark 1:4-5) points to John''s drawing on a distinct geographic source-stream rather than to a factual conflict. Marcus''s Mark commentary likewise treats the synoptic Jordan setting as schematic wilderness theology rather than precise cartography.

Verdict: harmonizable, and overstated as a contradiction. "In the Jordan" and "in Bethany beyond the Jordan" are compatible once one is read as the river and the other as a riverside settlement; the apparent sharpness of the "Bethabara" form is a Textus Receptus reading Origen introduced, and the live scholarly question is John''s geographic specificity, not a clash of facts.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (i-xii): Introduction, Translation, and Notes* (Anchor Bible 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 44-45, on Bethany beyond the Jordan and Johannine geography.
Bruce M. Metzger, *A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament*, 2nd ed. (Deutsche Bibelgesellschaft, 1994), pp. 171-172, on Origen''s preference for Bethabara over Bethania.
Craig S. Keener, *The Gospel of John: A Commentary*, Vol. 1 (Hendrickson, 2003), pp. 449-451, harmonizing the Jordan and the riverside settlement.
Jerome Murphy-O''Connor, "Sites associated with John the Baptist," *Revue Biblique* 112 (2005), pp. 253-266, surveying candidate locations for Bethany beyond the Jordan.
Joel Marcus, *Mark 1-8: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Bible 27; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 147-156, on the schematic wilderness setting of Mark''s baptism account.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, John', 4, 2, 2, 5, 1, 'John 1:28 reads ''Bethany beyond the Jordan'' in the earliest manuscripts (NA28); the reading ''Bethabara'' (followed by the KJV/Textus Receptus) was preferred and popularized by Origen on geographic grounds, though the reading predates his endorsement.', 'Metzger, A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament, 2nd ed., pp. 171-172');

The strongest harmonization, advanced by Carson and Blomberg, exploits precisely the ambiguity of Matthew''s phrase: Matthew reports only that Pharisees and Sadducees *came to the site* of the baptizing — perhaps to observe, evaluate, or oppose — without claiming they submitted to it, which leaves Luke''s denial intact. As a matter of grammar this is defensible; *erchomenous epi to baptisma autou* does not entail that any of them was immersed. So construed, Matthew describes presence at the scene and Luke denies actual baptism, and the two need not collide.

The difficulty is that Matthew''s rhetorical framing pulls against reading the Pharisees as mere bystanders. The Baptist immediately turns on them with the same demand he makes of those entering the water: he calls them *gennēmata echidnōn* ("brood of vipers"), warns them to flee "the coming wrath," and orders them to *poiēsate karpon axion tēs metanoias* ("produce fruit worthy of repentance"). That diatribe presupposes people presenting themselves for a baptism of repentance, not spectators on the bank; the rebuke loses much of its point if its targets had no intention of being baptized. Matthew''s pericope thus reads most naturally as casting the Pharisees among the baptismal candidates, even though its grammar stops just short of saying so.

The cleaner critical account is redactional. The two evangelists are pursuing different agendas regarding the Pharisees. Matthew is markedly anti-Pharisaic throughout (cf. the woes of ch. 23) and tends to portray the Pharisees as confronted and condemned at every turn, including by John. Luke, as Nolland and Fitzmyer note, is interested in social stratification: he contrasts "all the people and the tax collectors" who accepted John''s baptism (Luke 7:29) with the Pharisees and the legal experts who did not, marking the religious-legal elite off from the populace. Meier, treating John historically, judges that the broad tradition — that the Pharisaic establishment ultimately did not align itself with John — underlies both, and that each evangelist has shaped it to his own ends. The accounts are therefore in real tension at the level of what they imply, even if Matthew''s wording can be parsed to avoid formal self-contradiction with Luke.

Verdict: partially harmonizable but strained — Matthew''s grammar permits the Pharisees to be mere onlookers, but his rhetoric casts them as baptismal candidates, and the smoothest explanation of the difference is the evangelists'' divergent redactional aims rather than a single underlying fact.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 1 (International Critical Commentary; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 301-312, on Matthew 3:7-12.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke I-IX* (Anchor Bible 28; Doubleday, 1981), pp. 670-678, on Luke 7:29-30 and the Pharisees'' rejection of John''s baptism.
John Nolland, *Luke 1-9:20* (Word Biblical Commentary 35A; Word, 1989), pp. 339-343, on Luke''s social stratification of John''s audience.
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 1-7: A Commentary*, trans. James E. Crouch, rev. ed. (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 134-142, on Matthew''s anti-Pharisaic redaction.
John P. Meier, *A Marginal Jew: Rethinking the Historical Jesus*, Vol. 2: *Mentor, Message, and Miracles* (Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, 1994), pp. 19-99, on the historical John the Baptist.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Luke', 7, 2, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

John 3:22 reads: *ēlthen ho Iēsous kai hoi mathētai autou eis tēn Ioudaian gēn, kai ekei dietriben met'' autōn kai ebaptizen* — ''Jesus came, and his disciples, into the Judean land, and there he was staying with them and was baptizing.'' The verb *ebaptizen* is third-person singular imperfect, and its grammatical subject is Jesus. The most natural reading is that Jesus personally was conducting baptisms in Judea, in parallel and apparent competition with John the Baptist, who is described baptizing at Aenon near Salim a few verses later (3:23).

The surrounding material reinforces that this is Jesus himself. In 3:26 John''s disciples come to him complaining: ''the one who was with you beyond the Jordan, to whom you have testified — here he is baptizing, and all are going to him.'' The complaint only stings if Jesus, the figure John had identified at the Jordan, is personally baptizing and drawing crowds. The whole pericope (3:22-30) is structured as a Baptist-versus-Jesus comparison, climaxing in John''s confession that ''he must increase, but I must decrease.''

John 4:1-3 then narrates that the Pharisees heard ''that Jesus was making and baptizing more disciples than John,'' and the text interrupts itself with a parenthesis: *kaitoige Iēsous autos ouk ebaptizen all'' hoi mathētai autou* — ''although Jesus himself was not baptizing, but his disciples (were).'' This flatly negates the plain sense of 3:22.

The traditional harmonization — found in Augustine, Calvin, and reaffirmed by Carson and Köstenberger — reads 3:22 loosely: Jesus ''baptized'' in the sense that his disciples baptized under his authority, the way one might say a general ''took the city'' that his troops actually stormed. The representative-subject idiom is genuine Greek usage, and the harmonization is grammatically conceivable. But it strains against the immediate context. John 3:26''s witnesses identify the baptizer specifically as the man who had stood with John at the Jordan — that is Jesus as an individual, not an undifferentiated ''group.'' And if 3:22 were already understood as ''his disciples baptized,'' the corrective gloss in 4:2 would be redundant; its very presence signals that someone read 3:22 as the text most naturally reads it and felt compelled to qualify it.

The near-universal critical reading (Brown, Bultmann, Schnackenburg) is that 4:2 is a redactional gloss — an editorial parenthesis inserted during the gospel''s composition to correct a perceived problem in the earlier tradition. Three features support this. First, syntax: 4:2 disrupts the flow between 4:1 and 4:3, so that 4:3 (''he left Judea'') resumes the subject of 4:1 awkwardly across the insertion. Second, the particle *kaitoige* (''and yet,'' ''although''), rare in John, signals concession and correction. Third, the shift to the third person plural for the disciples sits uneasily with 4:1''s singular focus on Jesus. Brown labels 4:2 a ''parenthetical remark... added to correct a misimpression that might be gained from iii 22'' (Anchor Bible 29, p. 164).

Why correct it? Brown and Bultmann locate the motive in developing sacramental theology: as baptism came to be understood as incorporation *into* Christ, the picture of Jesus personally administering it became theologically awkward — one is baptized into Christ, not by him alongside John. The gloss protects that developing framework while leaving the older tradition embedded in 3:22.

Verdict: genuine internal tension, most economically explained as a later editorial gloss (4:2) correcting an earlier statement (3:22). The representative-subject harmonization is possible but forces the grammar; either way the canonical text asserts both that Jesus baptized and that he did not.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (i-xii): Introduction, Translation, and Notes* (Anchor Bible 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 150-156, 164, on John 4:2 as a corrective gloss.
Rudolf Bultmann, *The Gospel of John: A Commentary*, trans. G. R. Beasley-Murray et al. (Westminster, 1971), pp. 167-176, on 4:2 as redaction.
Rudolf Schnackenburg, *The Gospel According to St. John*, vol. 1 (HTKNT; Herder and Herder, 1968), pp. 410-415, on the Baptist-Jesus material.
Craig S. Keener, *The Gospel of John: A Commentary*, vol. 1 (Hendrickson, 2003), pp. 580-583, on the harmonization options.
Andrew T. Lincoln, *The Gospel According to Saint John* (Black''s New Testament Commentary; Continuum, 2005), pp. 160-167, on the seam at 4:1-3.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'John', 7, 2, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Mark 1:11, in the earliest gospel, reads *sy ei ho huios mou ho agapētos, en soi eudokēsa* — ''you are my beloved Son; in you I am well pleased.'' The pronoun *sy* and the second-person verb make this direct address: the voice speaks to Jesus. The framing reinforces privacy — in 1:10 it is Jesus who ''saw the heavens being torn apart'' (*schizomenous*) and the Spirit descending ''into him'' (*eis auton*); the vision and the voice are oriented toward Jesus as recipient. Mark''s scene functions as a commissioning theophany experienced by Jesus.

Luke 3:22 follows Mark almost verbatim: *sy ei ho huios mou ho agapētos, en soi eudokēsa* — second person, identical construction. (A famous Western variant, attested in Codex Bezae and several Fathers, has the voice quote Psalm 2:7 in full: ''You are my Son, today I have begotten you.'' That variant, whatever its origin, is still second-person direct address and does not affect the Matthew-versus-Mark/Luke contrast.)

Matthew 3:17 alters the form: *houtos estin ho huios mou ho agapētos, en hō eudokēsa* — ''this is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased.'' The demonstrative *houtos* and the third-person verb make this an announcement *about* Jesus, addressed to whoever is present. Matthew also generalizes the vision: the heavens ''were opened'' (3:16) without Mark''s violent tearing, and the declaration is broadcast publicly.

Two harmonizations are offered. The first: the voice said both — ''you are my Son'' to Jesus and ''this is my Son'' to the bystanders, two utterances compressed by different evangelists. This is conceivable in the abstract but textually unsupported; all three Synoptics narrate a single voice-event, and no account hints at a double pronouncement. The second, more sophisticated: that *sy ei* and *houtos estin* both render an underlying Aramaic original whose deixis was looser. This founders on the fact that each Greek form is unambiguous and that Matthew is demonstrably editing Mark''s Greek text rather than independently translating a Semitic source — the verbal agreement is too close for independent translation.

The critical consensus (Davies-Allison, Luz, Marcus, Fitzmyer) is that Matthew redactionally converted Mark''s second-person private revelation into a third-person public proclamation, consistent with Matthew''s broader presentation of the baptism as Jesus''s public identification (note the dialogue with John in 3:14-15, unique to Matthew, which frames the event for an audience). Mark''s scene is about Jesus''s own commissioning; Matthew''s is about Jesus''s public attestation as Son.

Luke''s retention of Mark''s second-person form is itself significant. On the Two-Source theory, Luke''s siding with Mark against Matthew''s redaction is one of many minor pieces of evidence for Markan priority and for Luke''s independence from Matthew — if Luke had known and followed Matthew, the third-person form would be hard to explain.

The contradiction is not whether a heavenly voice spoke, nor what it said about Jesus''s sonship; it is the addressee. Mark and Luke have God speak to Jesus; Matthew has God speak about Jesus to the witnesses. A single utterance cannot be both second-person and third-person.

Verdict: genuine compositional contradiction, best explained as Matthean redaction of Mark for theological emphasis; the ''both were spoken'' harmonization has no textual warrant.', 'Joel Marcus, *Mark 1-8: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Bible 27; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 158-167, on Mark''s baptism theophany.
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, vol. 1 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 328-339, on Matthew 3:17.
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 1-7: A Commentary*, rev. ed., trans. James E. Crouch (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 142-149, on Matthew''s redaction.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke I-IX* (Anchor Bible 28; Doubleday, 1981), pp. 479-487, on Luke 3:22 and the Psalm 2:7 variant.
John P. Meier, *A Marginal Jew*, vol. 2 (Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, 1994), pp. 100-116, on the baptism tradition.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke', 10, 2, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Matthew 4 runs bread → temple → kingdoms-from-a-mountain. The cycle climaxes on ''an exceedingly high mountain'' where Satan offers ''all the kingdoms of the world and their glory'' (*pasas tas basileias tou kosmou kai tēn doxan autōn*), and Jesus dismisses him: *hypage, satana* (''Begone, Satan''). Mountains are theophanic loci throughout Matthew — the Sermon on the Mount, the Transfiguration mountain, and the closing mountain of the Great Commission (28:16) — so a mountain climax to the temptation cycle coheres with Matthew''s redactional geography and his Mosaic typology for Jesus.

Luke 4 runs bread → kingdoms-from-a-height → temple. Luke''s cycle climaxes at ''Jerusalem,'' on the pinnacle of the temple. Luke''s Gospel is famously Jerusalem-oriented: the infancy narrative begins in the temple (1:8-23) and ends there (2:41-50), the travel narrative is a long march toward Jerusalem (9:51ff.), and the Gospel closes in the temple (24:53) with Acts opening there. Ending the temptation in Jerusalem fits this structural gravitation.

The ordering also differs in detail: Matthew''s transitions use ''then'' (*tote*) and ''again'' (*palin*) and have Satan physically transporting Jesus, while Luke softens the third scene''s verb (*ēgagen*, ''he led him'') and adds that Satan showed the kingdoms ''in a moment of time'' (4:5). These are the fingerprints of two independent redactions of shared material.

Two harmonizations recur. The first holds that one evangelist gives chronological order and the other thematic order. This concedes the key point — that at least one Gospel narrates the temptations non-chronologically inside a narrative that otherwise reads as sequential — and it cannot specify which order is the ''real'' one without circular appeal. The second posits two separate temptation cycles, one reported by each evangelist. This is *ad hoc*: both accounts share the forty-day frame, the same three temptations, the same scripted exchanges built on Deuteronomy citations (Deut 8:3; 6:16; 6:13), and the same conclusion with Satan''s departure. Two distinct yet verbally near-identical cycles is precisely the kind of unfalsifiable multiplication scholars treat as a harmonization of last resort.

The critical consensus (Fitzmyer, Davies-Allison, Bovon, Marshall, Nolland) is straightforward redactional reordering of shared Q tradition, each evangelist arranging the temptations to climax in his preferred locus — mountain for Matthew, temple/Jerusalem for Luke. The harder question is which order stood in Q. A majority (Fitzmyer, Kloppenborg) favors Luke''s order as more original, partly because Matthew elsewhere reorders Q more freely than Luke, and partly because Matthew''s *hypage, satana* makes a more natural climactic dismissal where Matthew has placed it, suggesting Matthew moved the kingdoms scene to the end to create that climax. A minority argues Matthew preserves Q''s order with its scriptural citations in canonical-Deuteronomy sequence. The debate over the direction of editing does not affect the fact of editing.

Verdict: genuine compositional contradiction in sequence; the most economical reading is that Matthew and Luke independently reordered a shared Q narrative for theological climax, with Luke probably closer to Q''s order. The two-cycles harmonization is unparsimonious, and the topical-order harmonization concedes non-chronological narration.', 'Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke I-IX* (Anchor Bible 28; Doubleday, 1981), pp. 506-520, on the temptation order and Q.
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, vol. 1 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 351-374, on Matthew 4:1-11.
François Bovon, *Luke 1: A Commentary on the Gospel of Luke 1:1-9:50* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2002), pp. 138-147, on the Jerusalem climax.
John Nolland, *Luke 1-9:20* (Word Biblical Commentary 35A; Word, 1989), pp. 175-188, on Luke''s redaction.
John S. Kloppenborg, *Q, the Earliest Gospel* (Westminster John Knox, 2008), pp. 84-92, on the Q temptation narrative.
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 1-7: A Commentary*, rev. ed. (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 149-160, on Matthew 4.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Luke', 10, 2, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Premise 1 (John 1:1, 14): *en archē ēn ho logos, kai ho logos ēn pros ton theon, kai theos ēn ho logos* — ''in the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God'' — and *ho logos sarx egeneto* (1:14), ''the Word became flesh.'' The anarthrous *theos* in 1:1c has been debated for centuries (predicate nominative, qualitative force, definitely not the Arian ''a god'' of the New World Translation in mainstream Greek grammar), but in its canonical and Nicene reception the Johannine prologue is the textual cornerstone of Logos Christology, alongside Colossians 1:15-20 and Hebrews 1:3.

Premise 2 (James 1:13): *ho theos... apeirastos estin kakōn* — ''God is untemptable by evils'' (the adjective *apeirastos* is a New Testament hapax). The statement is categorical and unqualified.

Premise 3 (Hebrews 4:15; cf. Matthew 4:1; Luke 4:1-2): Jesus was tempted *kata panta kath'' homoiotēta chōris hamartias* — ''in every respect... yet without sin.'' Again categorical: the temptation was comprehensive.

The inference is valid: if Jesus is God, and God cannot be tempted, then Jesus cannot be tempted; but Jesus was tempted. The premises cannot all be true as stated without qualification.

The orthodox harmonization, developed across the Christological controversies and codified at Chalcedon (451 CE), is the two-natures doctrine. Jesus is one person (*hypostasis*) in two natures (*physeis*), divine and human, united ''without confusion, without change, without division, without separation.'' Predicates proper to one nature can be attributed to the single person — the *communicatio idiomatum*. So one may truly say ''Jesus was tempted'' (true in respect of his human nature) and ''God cannot be tempted'' (true in respect of the divine nature) without formal contradiction, because the temptation is predicated of the person according to his humanity. The background assumption is divine impassibility and immutability — God, in classical theism, cannot suffer the internal pull of an inclination toward evil.

This is a coherent systematic solution, and on its own terms it dissolves the formal contradiction. But it is a theological construct read back into texts that do not themselves operate with the distinction. Neither James nor Hebrews carves out a ''tempted-only-according-to-the-flesh'' exception; James says God is untemptable, full stop, and Hebrews says Jesus was tempted in every respect, full stop. The two-natures grammar that reconciles them is the product of fourth- and fifth-century debate (Nicaea 325, Constantinople 381, Ephesus 431, Chalcedon 451) and the vocabulary it forged — *homoousios*, *hypostatic union* — postdates the texts by three to four centuries.

Bart Ehrman''s *How Jesus Became God* traces the historical development from a low Christology (Jesus as exalted human messiah in early Pauline and pre-Pauline material) to the high, pre-existence Christology of the Johannine prologue, and argues that the very need for conciliar machinery shows the raw textual data produced tensions of exactly this kind. Richard Bauckham and Larry Hurtado push the high Christology earlier and argue Jesus was included within the divine identity from a very early stage, which sharpens rather than softens the puzzle: the higher the Christology, the more acutely James 1:13 bites against Hebrews 4:15.

The non-Trinitarian harmonization simply denies premise 1 — Jesus is not God, the Logos language is functional or metaphorical — and the syllogism never forms. This is textually economical but heterodox (Adoptionist, later Socinian, modern Unitarian and Jehovah''s Witness positions).

Verdict: harmonizable within Chalcedonian two-natures Christology, but only by importing a post-biblical theological distinction; on the face of the texts as written, the premises genuinely conflict.', 'Bart D. Ehrman, *How Jesus Became God: The Exaltation of a Jewish Preacher from Galilee* (HarperOne, 2014), pp. 211-251, 269-281, on Christological development.
Richard Bauckham, *Jesus and the God of Israel* (Eerdmans, 2008), pp. 1-59, on divine-identity Christology.
Larry W. Hurtado, *Lord Jesus Christ: Devotion to Jesus in Earliest Christianity* (Eerdmans, 2003), pp. 27-78, on early high Christology.
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (i-xii)* (Anchor Bible 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 3-37, on the Johannine prologue.
Dale C. Allison Jr., *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Epistle of James* (ICC; Bloomsbury T&T Clark, 2013), pp. 245-258, on James 1:13.
Harold W. Attridge, *The Epistle to the Hebrews* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1989), pp. 139-143, on Hebrews 4:15.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'John, James', 5, 3, 5, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

Matthew 4:10 cites Deuteronomy 6:13 (LXX) with *kyrion ton theon sou proskynēseis kai autō monō latreuseis* — ''you shall worship the Lord your God and serve him only.'' The verb *latreuō* denotes cultic, religious service, the devotion owed to a deity. Matthew 23:10 adds *kathēgētēs* — religious teacher or guide — and reserves the title for the Christ. The household codes, by contrast, use *hypotassō* (''be subject'') and *hypakouō* (''obey'') toward *kyrioi* (''masters'') in the social-economic master-slave relationship. On the surface these are distinct conceptual fields: cultic worship of God versus social subordination within a household.

The apologetic case — Blomberg, Carson, the IVP *Hard Sayings* tradition — states it at full strength: Scripture distinguishes the worship/service owed to God alone (*latreia*) from the civil and domestic obedience owed within ordered human relationships (*hypakoē*, *hypotagē*), exactly as Jesus distinguishes Caesar''s due from God''s (Mark 12:17) and as Paul grounds civil obedience in Romans 13. On this reading ''serve God only'' governs the cultic sphere and never forbids ordinary obedience to parents, magistrates, or masters; there is no contradiction because the two verbs occupy two non-overlapping domains.

This distinction is genuine and holds for the bare commands to obey. Where it falters is the specific language the household codes (the *Haustafeln* of Eph 5:21-6:9; Col 3:18-4:1; 1 Tim 6:1-2; Titus 2:9-10; 1 Pet 2:18-25) actually use to motivate slave obedience, which repeatedly borrows the vocabulary of devotion to God. 1 Peter 2:18 commands slaves to submit *en panti phobō* — ''in all fear'' — and the same paragraph distinguishes the objects of *phobos*: ''fear God, honor the emperor'' (2:17, *ton theon phobeisthe*), so the ''fear'' directed at masters in 2:18 sits uncomfortably close to the reverence reserved for God. Colossians 3:22 tells slaves to obey *en haplotēti kardias phoboumenoi ton kyrion* — ''in singleness of heart, fearing the Lord'' — folding the master-relationship into service of *ho kyrios*, a term Colossians uses for Christ. Ephesians 6:5 has slaves obey their earthly masters ''with fear and trembling, in singleness of heart, as to Christ'' (*hōs tō Christō*), and 6:7 ''with goodwill doing service (*douleuontes*) as to the Lord and not to men.'' Here the obedience is explicitly routed through, and analogized to, devotion to Christ — precisely the *hōs tō kyriō* framing that the *latreia/hypakoē* distinction was supposed to keep apart.

The critical reading (Balch, Horrell, with Lincoln, Dunn, and Elliott on the individual letters) places the *Haustafeln* within the Greco-Roman literature on household management (Aristotle''s *Politics*, Stoic *peri oikonomias* discussions): these are first-century social-control texts that mapped the new movement''s exclusive-loyalty language onto the existing hierarchy of master and slave so that Christianity could survive respectably in a slaveholding society. In doing so they collapsed the very boundary the harmonization needs, theologizing obedience to a human master as service rendered ''to the Lord.''

Verdict: partially harmonizable — the lexical distinction between *latreuō* and *hypakouō/hypotassō* is real and defuses the simplest form of the charge, but the household codes'' own diction of *phobos*, *haplotēs kardias*, and obedience *hōs tō Christō* reintroduces the tension by routing slave-obedience through the language Deuteronomy reserved for God alone.', 'David L. Balch, *Let Wives Be Submissive: The Domestic Code in 1 Peter* (SBL Monograph Series 26; Scholars Press, 1981), pp. 81-121, on the household codes.
David G. Horrell, *Solidarity and Difference: A Contemporary Reading of Paul''s Ethics*, 2nd ed. (Bloomsbury T&T Clark, 2016), pp. 215-248, on the household codes.
Andrew T. Lincoln, *Ephesians* (Word Biblical Commentary 42; Word, 1990), pp. 415-423, on Ephesians 6:5.
James D. G. Dunn, *The Epistles to the Colossians and to Philemon* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 1996), pp. 251-258, on Colossians 3:22.
John H. Elliott, *1 Peter: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Bible 37B; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 514-530, on 1 Peter 2:18.
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, vol. 1 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 372-373, on Matthew 4:10.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Ephesians, Colossians, 1 Timothy, Titus, 1 Peter', 6, 2, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

Mark 1:14 establishes the entire Galilean-ministry timeline with a single Greek clause: *meta de to paradothēnai ton Iōannēn* — "after John was handed over." The aorist articular infinitive (*paradothēnai*) marks a completed event prior to Jesus''s Galilean preaching, and the verb *paradidōmi* ("hand over," later used of Jesus''s own betrayal) deliberately foreshadows the Baptist''s fate at Herod Antipas''s hands (Mark 6:17-29). Matthew 4:12 carries the same chronological anchor forward almost verbatim, substituting *akousas de hoti Iōannēs paredothē* — "having heard that John was handed over." In both Synoptics the lakeside call of Peter and Andrew (Mark 1:16-20; Matt 4:18-22) is the inaugural act of the post-arrest period. The narrative locks Peter''s call into the time after the Baptist''s imprisonment.

John 1:35-42 tells a different story in a different place. Andrew is already a disciple of the Baptist when he hears John identify Jesus as *ho amnos tou theou*, the Lamb of God; he follows Jesus, finds his brother Simon, and brings him, whereupon Jesus renames him Cephas. The scene unfolds at Bethany beyond the Jordan (John 1:28), not at the Sea of Galilee. John 3:22-24 then makes the framing explicit with a parenthetical aside: *oupō gar ēn beblēmenos eis tēn phylakēn ho Iōannēs* — "for John had not yet been thrown into prison." The pluperfect-equivalent periphrasis (*ēn beblēmenos*) signals background information the narrator supplies precisely because it matters chronologically. The Fourth Gospel presents a substantial period of overlapping ministry — Jesus and John baptizing simultaneously (John 3:22-26; 4:1-2) — that the Synoptics have eliminated entirely.

The strongest harmonization, advanced by D. A. Carson in the Pillar volume, Andreas Köstenberger in the BECNT, and Craig Blomberg in *The Historical Reliability of John''s Gospel*, runs a two-stage call. John 1 narrates an initial, informal meeting and provisional discipleship at the Jordan; Mark and Matthew narrate a later, formal summons by the Sea of Galilee after the men had returned to their nets. On this reading Jesus "called them again," and the Synoptic accounts simply omit the earlier Jordan encounter. This is not impossible. The grammar of Mark 1:16-17 does not state in so many words that this is first contact, and there is independent evidence that disciples could move between teachers.

But the case is weaker than it looks. The Markan and Matthean pericopes read as initial-call narratives: the brothers abandon their nets *euthys* — "immediately" (Mark 1:18) — with no narrative signal of prior acquaintance, and the summons "I will make you fishers of men" functions as a vocation-defining first encounter, not a renewal. More decisively, John''s *oupō ēn beblēmenos* in 3:24 is not incidental detail but a deliberate authorial correction. A narrator only flags that an event preceded the Baptist''s imprisonment if a competing chronology places it afterward — which is exactly what the Synoptics do. The remark presupposes a reader who might otherwise assume the Synoptic sequence, and it pointedly resets it.

The critical consensus (Brown, *Anchor Bible* 29; Schnackenburg; and earlier Bultmann) holds that John 1-3 preserves an independent tradition of a Judean, pre-Galilean phase of Jesus''s activity that overlaps the Baptist''s career — a phase the Synoptics suppressed when they compressed all of Jesus''s ministry into a single post-arrest Galilean campaign organized around one climactic Jerusalem visit. Davies and Allison note that Matthew''s geographical theology requires John offstage before Jesus enters "Galilee of the Gentiles" (Matt 4:15-16, citing Isa 9:1-2), so the arrest functions as a literary hinge, not necessarily a chronological datum. Marcus similarly reads Mark''s *paradothēnai* as a theological signal linking John, Jesus, and the disciples in a chain of "handings over." Both Synoptists thus had redactional reasons to position the call where they did, independent of historical sequence.

Verdict: genuine contradiction. Mark and Matthew place the call after John''s imprisonment; John explicitly places the disciples'' first following before it. The two-stage harmonization is possible only by importing into the Synoptic accounts a prior encounter they neither narrate nor imply.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (i-xii): Introduction, Translation, and Notes* (AB 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 73-79, 154-156, on the independent Johannine tradition of a pre-Galilean ministry overlapping the Baptist.
Joel Marcus, *Mark 1-8: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AB 27; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 178-186, on paradothēnai and the lakeside call.
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 1 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 374-381, 391-401, on the arrest of John as a literary hinge into Galilee.
Rudolf Schnackenburg, *The Gospel According to St John*, Vol. 1, trans. Kevin Smyth (Crossroad, 1980), pp. 308-318, on the overlapping ministries in John 3.
D. A. Carson, *The Gospel According to John* (PNTC; Eerdmans, 1991), pp. 154-159, advancing the two-stage call harmonization.
Andreas J. Köstenberger, *John* (BECNT; Baker Academic, 2004), pp. 78-86, on a provisional Jordan discipleship preceding the Galilean call.
Craig L. Blomberg, *The Historical Reliability of John''s Gospel* (InterVarsity Press, 2002), pp. 79-85, on reconciling the call accounts.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, John', 2, 2, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Luke 4:38-39 has Jesus leave the Capernaum synagogue and enter *tēn oikian Simōnos*, "the house of Simon," where he heals Simon''s mother-in-law of a fever (the language *epestas epanō autēs epetimēsen tō pyretō*, "standing over her he rebuked the fever," assimilates the healing to an exorcism). Crucially, this Simon has not yet been introduced in Luke''s narrative; the definite article presumes a figure the reader is expected to recognize. Only afterward, in Luke 5:1-11, does Luke narrate the miraculous catch of fish and the call, climaxing in Peter''s confession *exelthe ap'' emou, hoti anēr hamartōlos eimi, kyrie* — "depart from me, for I am a sinful man, Lord" — which reads as a first revelation of Jesus''s identity, not a recommitment. In Luke the healing precedes the call by a full chapter.

Matthew reverses the sequence. Matthew 4:18-22 places the lakeside call as the inaugural act of the Galilean ministry; the Sermon on the Mount (chs. 5-7) and a cycle of miracles intervene; then Matthew 8:14-15 has Jesus enter *tēn oikian Petrou*, "Peter''s house," and heal the mother-in-law. Call first, healing later. Mark 1:16-31 sits between them: Peter is called (1:16-18), and on the same Sabbath Jesus heals the mother-in-law (1:29-31). Mark agrees with Matthew on order but compresses the events into a single day, against Matthew''s multi-chapter gap.

The standard harmonization, defended by R. T. France (NICNT Matthew) and Darrell Bock (BECNT Luke), is that Luke arranges topically rather than chronologically. Luke gathers the Capernaum miracles of 4:31-44 into a thematic unit establishing Jesus''s authority before backing up to narrate Peter''s call in 5:1-11; the unintroduced "Simon" of 4:38 is then a literary anticipation, a name the reader will only fully meet a chapter later. This is the familiar and largely correct observation that Synoptic sequence is governed by theological and rhetorical design, not by the clock.

The difficulty is that the harmonization vindicates Luke''s literary freedom at the cost of the historical reliability of the ordering it is invoked to defend. If Luke deliberately rearranged the material, then Luke and Matthew do not jointly preserve a recoverable historical sequence; one or both have moved the pieces. Fitzmyer (*Anchor Bible* 28), Bovon (Hermeneia), and Marshall (NIGTC) hold that Luke knew Mark''s tight Sabbath cluster (call, then same-day healing) and rearranged it, pulling the Capernaum healings forward so that Peter has already witnessed Jesus''s power before the dramatic call by the lake — which also smooths the psychological oddity in Mark and Matthew of fishermen abandoning everything for a stranger. The unintroduced "Simon" of Luke 4:38 is itself a fingerprint of this redaction: Luke transposed a healing that in his source stood after the call, leaving a character referenced before he is named.

Matthew, for his part, dismantled Mark''s Sabbath unit from the other end, detaching the mother-in-law healing and relocating it into his thematically arranged miracle collection in chapters 8-9, several chapters after the call. So the divergence is not a single rearrangement but two independent redactions of Mark pulling in opposite directions. The Markan order — call immediately followed by same-day healing — is plausibly the earliest, but even it is shaped by Mark''s compressed "day in Capernaum" framework and cannot be taken as a neutral transcript.

Verdict: genuine contradiction in sequence. Luke places the healing before the call; Matthew (with Mark) places it after. The topical-arrangement harmonization correctly explains why the orders differ, but in doing so it concedes that the evangelists were not reporting a fixed chronology — which is precisely the point at issue.', 'Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke I-IX: Introduction, Translation, and Notes* (AB 28; Doubleday, 1981), pp. 549-553, 559-568, on Luke''s reordering of the Capernaum material.
François Bovon, *Luke 1: A Commentary on the Gospel of Luke 1:1-9:50*, trans. Christine M. Thomas (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2002), pp. 158-165, 166-172, on the redaction of Luke 4-5.
Joel Marcus, *Mark 1-8: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AB 27; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 192-202, on Mark''s ''day in Capernaum.''
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 2 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1991), pp. 30-39, on the placement of the mother-in-law healing.
Darrell L. Bock, *Luke 1:1-9:50* (BECNT; Baker, 1994), pp. 432-444, on Luke''s topical arrangement.
I. Howard Marshall, *The Gospel of Luke: A Commentary on the Greek Text* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 1978), pp. 192-204, on the ordering of healing and call.
R. T. France, *The Gospel of Matthew* (NICNT; Eerdmans, 2007), pp. 317-321, on non-chronological Synoptic arrangement.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Luke', 2, 2, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The Mark/Matthew account is spare: Jesus walks past the Sea of Galilee, sees the brothers casting nets, says "Follow me, and I will make you fishers of men," and they abandon the nets *euthys*, "immediately" (Mark 1:18; Matt 4:18-20). There is no prior acquaintance, no fish miracle, and no Baptist context. The brevity is itself the point: Mark presents an authoritative summons that needs no psychological preparation.

John 1:35-42 differs at every point. Andrew is already a disciple of the Baptist; he hears John designate Jesus *ho amnos tou theou*, the Lamb of God; he and an unnamed companion (traditionally the Beloved Disciple) spend the day with Jesus; then Andrew finds Simon, declares "we have found the Messiah," and brings him. Peter is not summoned by Jesus at all in this version — he is recruited by his brother — and Jesus responds by renaming him Cephas. The setting is the Jordan (John 1:28), not Galilee.

Luke 5:1-11 supplies a third version. Jesus teaches from Simon''s boat, then directs him into deep water; Simon — already addressing Jesus as *epistata*, "master" — objects that they caught nothing all night but obeys. The haul is so vast the nets tear and two boats begin to sink; Peter falls at Jesus''s knees confessing himself *anēr hamartōlos*, "a sinful man," and Jesus answers *mē phobou, apo tou nyn anthrōpous esē zōgrōn* — "do not fear; from now on you will be catching people." Andrew is not even named; the spotlight falls on Peter, with James and John as partners.

The harmonization proceeds in two layers. First, sequencing (Carson, Köstenberger, Blomberg): John 1 is an initial Jordan meeting; Luke 5 is a later, formal call at Galilee marked by the miraculous catch; Mark/Matthew abbreviate that same Galilean call. Each gospel thus preserves a different cross-section of one multi-stage process. Second, the theological gloss that discipleship was a progressive deepening of commitment rather than a single instantaneous event — a genuinely defensible model of ancient rabbinic-style attachment.

But the texts resist the layering at concrete points. Mark 1:18''s *euthys* and the absence of any back-reference present the lakeside call as first contact, not a third stage. The renaming to Cephas, which John places at the Jordan in 1:42, is treated by Mark 3:16 and Matthew 16:17-18 as occurring later and in entirely different settings (the appointment of the Twelve; Caesarea Philippi) — so the same naming event floats across incompatible contexts. Peter''s self-designation as "a sinful man" in Luke 5:8 reads as a first encounter with the numinous, not a renewed allegiance to a teacher he had already followed at the Jordan and again at the lakeside.

The critical reading (Brown, *Anchor Bible* 29; Fitzmyer, *Anchor Bible* 28; Marcus, *Anchor Bible* 27; Davies-Allison, ICC) treats these as three independent traditions. The earliest layer likely had Peter called as a fisherman at the lakeside (Mark). John recast the tradition with a Baptist framing that subordinates the Baptist to Jesus — part of the Fourth Gospel''s broader polemic against a continuing Baptist sect (cf. John 1:8, 20; 3:30). Luke composed or expanded the miraculous-catch tradition to furnish Peter with a dramatic vocational moment fitting his later prominence; tellingly, the same miraculous catch reappears in a post-resurrection setting in John 21:1-14, suggesting a floating tradition variously deployed. Bauckham, defending Johannine eyewitness derivation, accepts that the Synoptic and Johannine call traditions are formally distinct, though he reads the distinction as complementary testimony rather than contradiction.

Verdict: genuine contradiction. No parsimonious reading collapses three different mechanisms — direct lakeside summons, Baptist-mediated recruitment via Andrew, and a miraculous-catch call — into one event. The harmonization must either invent a multi-stage process the texts do not narrate or force three independent traditions into a single contrived chronology.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (i-xii): Introduction, Translation, and Notes* (AB 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 73-79, on the Johannine recasting of the call.
Joel Marcus, *Mark 1-8: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AB 27; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 178-187, on the Markan lakeside call.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke I-IX* (AB 28; Doubleday, 1981), pp. 559-568, on the Lukan miraculous-catch call.
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 1 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 391-401, on the lakeside call and the naming of Peter.
Richard Bauckham, *Jesus and the Eyewitnesses: The Gospels as Eyewitness Testimony* (Eerdmans, 2006), pp. 127-145, on Johannine and Synoptic testimony.
D. A. Carson, *The Gospel According to John* (PNTC; Eerdmans, 1991), pp. 154-159, on a multi-stage call.
Andreas J. Köstenberger, *John* (BECNT; Baker Academic, 2004), pp. 78-86, on progressive discipleship.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke, John', 10, 2, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Matthew 5:1 reads *idōn de tous ochlous anebē eis to oros* — "seeing the crowds he went up into the mountain" — and the mountain frame is closed at Matthew 8:1, *katabantos de autou apo tou orous*, "when he had come down from the mountain." The articular *to oros* ("the mountain") and the ascent-and-descent inclusio bracket the entire Sermon on the Mount (5:3-7:27) as a single elevated event. Luke 6:12 likewise sends Jesus *eis to oros* to pray and to choose the Twelve, but then 6:17 has him come down — *katabas met'' autōn* — and stand *epi topou pedinou*, "on a level place," where the beatitudes and woes follow.

The harmonization is ancient. Augustine, in *De consensu evangelistarum*, treated the two as distinct discourses; later harmonizers, including Carson (EBC) and France (NICNT), more commonly identify them and read Luke''s *topos pedinos* as a level shelf or plateau on the mountainside — Jesus descends from the higher summit where he prayed to a flat area lower down where the crowd can gather. Lexically this is sound: *pedinos* denotes flat or level ground without specifying low altitude, and mountainous terrain in Galilee does include level terraces. On this reading Matthew describes the same setting from the perspective of the elevated massif, Luke from the perspective of the level patch upon it. The geographical objection thus largely dissolves.

The deeper problem is that the harmonization secures the locale only by quietly conceding that the two sermons are differently composed. Matthew''s discourse runs some 107 verses; Luke''s Sermon on the Plain runs about 30 (6:20-49). They share a recognizable core — beatitudes, love of enemies, not judging, the two builders — but Matthew''s version is roughly three times longer and contains large blocks (the six antitheses "you have heard that it was said... but I say to you," the Lord''s Prayer, teaching on almsgiving, fasting, and anxiety) that Luke either omits here or distributes across his central section in chapters 11, 12, and 13. Conversely, Luke''s woes (6:24-26) have no Matthean counterpart. The audiences also differ: Matthew has Jesus teaching the disciples (5:1-2) until the crowds react at 7:28, whereas Luke explicitly places "a great multitude" alongside the disciples on the level place (6:17-20).

The critical consensus (Davies-Allison, ICC; Luz, Hermeneia; Fitzmyer, *Anchor Bible* 28; Bovon, Hermeneia; Betz, Hermeneia) holds that both evangelists drew on a common body of Q sermon material and arranged it according to their own theological architecture. Matthew gathered Jesus''s teaching into five great discourses (chs. 5-7; 10; 13; 18; 24-25), each closing with the formula *kai egeneto hote etelesen ho Iēsous*, "and it happened when Jesus had finished" — a deliberate structure that many read as casting Jesus as a new Moses delivering authoritative instruction from a new Sinai, which makes the mountain setting theologically loaded rather than merely topographical. Luke, who reserves mountains for prayer and revelation (6:12; 9:28), stages the teaching on level ground among the people, consonant with his emphasis on Jesus''s reach to crowds, the poor, and the marginal (note the concrete "blessed are the poor" against Matthew''s "poor in spirit"). Betz argues the two are independent pre-redactional compilations of sayings, not a single transcript variously abridged.

The upshot is that the setting can be reconciled but the discourses cannot easily be — and the two questions are linked. If Matthew and Luke are reworking shared sayings into divergent literary settings for divergent theological ends, then the mountain/plain difference is itself a redactional signature rather than two camera angles on one afternoon. The plateau harmonization is technically available but explanatorily idle, since it leaves untouched the more telling differences in length, content, audience, and theology.

Verdict: setting harmonizable, discourse not. The level-place-on-the-mountain reading buys the geography only by conceding that the sermons are differently composed editions of Q material.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 1 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 410-427, on the mountain and new-Moses typology.
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 1-7: A Commentary*, trans. James E. Crouch, rev. ed. (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 169-186, on the Sermon''s composition from Q.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke I-IX* (AB 28; Doubleday, 1981), pp. 626-633, on the level place and the Lukan sermon.
François Bovon, *Luke 1: A Commentary on the Gospel of Luke 1:1-9:50*, trans. Christine M. Thomas (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2002), pp. 212-214, 220-229, on the level place and the beatitudes.
Hans Dieter Betz, *The Sermon on the Mount: A Commentary on the Sermon on the Mount, Including the Sermon on the Plain* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1995), pp. 78-88, on independent compilations.
R. T. France, *The Gospel of Matthew* (NICNT; Eerdmans, 2007), pp. 154-159, on the mountain setting and Luke''s level place.
Augustine of Hippo, *De consensu evangelistarum* (The Harmony of the Gospels), Book II, ch. 19, on reconciling the mount and the plain.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Luke', 10, 2, 3, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The "peace" passages span distinct semantic registers. Luke 2:14''s *epi gēs eirēnē* is angelic-liturgical announcement language steeped in Isaianic shalom oracles (Isa 9:6-7; 52:7). John 14:27''s *eirēnēn aphiēmi hymin, eirēnēn tēn emēn didōmi hymin* — "peace I leave with you, my peace I give to you" — is explicitly inward and is set in deliberate contrast to the peace "the world gives"; 16:33 locates this peace "in me" amid tribulation. Acts 10:36, *euangelizomenos eirēnēn*, is Lukan missionary summary describing reconciliation between God and humanity. Matthew 5:9 enjoins peacemaking as discipleship ethic, and Matthew 26:52 ("all who take the sword will perish by the sword") rejects violent resistance. These texts move comfortably within a single coherent claim: Jesus brings reconciliation with God and an interior peace, and forbids his followers'' aggression.

The harmonization, defended by Blomberg (NAC Matthew) and Carson (EBC), distinguishes two referents of *eirēnē*: (1) the vertical, eschatological-spiritual peace between God and humanity that Jesus genuinely brings, and (2) the horizontal, social-political peace that his coming disrupts because exclusive allegiance to him fractures households. On this reading there is no contradiction — different senses of one word. For John 14:27 the distinction is not merely available but textually marked, since Jesus himself separates his peace from the world''s. So far the apologetic case holds.

Where it breaks down is Matthew 10:34. Jesus says *mē nomisēte hoti ēlthon balein eirēnēn epi tēn gēn; ouk ēlthon balein eirēnēn alla machairan* — "do not think that I came to bring peace on the earth; I came not to bring peace but a sword." The qualifier *epi tēn gēn*, "on the earth," is the very phrase the angels use in Luke 2:14 (*epi gēs eirēnē*). Jesus is not contrasting his peace with a different, worldly peace (as in John 14); he is flatly denying that earthly peace was his purpose, using the angels'' own terms. Luke 12:51 makes the denial unmistakable: *dokeite hoti eirēnēn paregenomēn dounai en tē gē? ouchi, legō hymin, all'' ē diamerismon* — "do you suppose I came to give peace on the earth? No, I tell you, but rather division" — followed by the catalogue of divided households drawn from Micah 7:6.

The critical reading (Davies-Allison, ICC; Luz, Hermeneia; Fitzmyer, *Anchor Bible* 28A; Allison, *The End of the Ages Has Come*) treats Matthew 10:34-36 and Luke 12:51-53 as a single Q saying preserving a genuinely apocalyptic edge: the in-breaking kingdom is a crisis that sunders families, demanding allegiance over kinship. It coheres with other hard Q sayings — hating father and mother (Luke 14:26), letting the dead bury the dead (Luke 9:60), and the household set against itself. Allison situates these within an eschatological scenario in which the messianic woes include intrafamilial strife. The Lukan birth announcement of universal *eirēnē* and the Johannine gift of interior peace represent a different, and arguably later, theological register in which the apocalyptic sword has been recast as spiritual repose. The redactional difference between Matthew''s *machaira* ("sword") and Luke''s *diamerismos* ("division") suggests each evangelist already softened or specified the metaphor in transmission.

The two-senses harmonization therefore succeeds for John 14 and Acts 10, where the texts themselves distinguish kinds of peace, and partly for the discipleship sayings. It does not neutralize the direct collision between Luke 2:14 and Matthew 10:34, which stand in the same canon, use the same Greek phrase *epi (tēn) gēn*, and assert opposite content — earthly peace announced versus earthly peace explicitly disclaimed.

Verdict: partially harmonizable. The vertical/horizontal distinction resolves the John and Acts material, but the angel-versus-Jesus contradiction over peace "on the earth" remains a genuine tension reflecting divergent theological strata.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 2 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1991), pp. 217-221, on the sword saying and Micah 7:6.
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 8-20: A Commentary*, trans. James E. Crouch (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2001), pp. 110-118, on the sword/division saying.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV: Introduction, Translation, and Notes* (AB 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 994-998, on the division saying.
Dale C. Allison Jr., *The End of the Ages Has Come: An Early Interpretation of the Passion and Resurrection of Jesus* (Fortress, 1985), pp. 84-90, on family division as eschatological tribulation.
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (xiii-xxi): Introduction, Translation, and Notes* (AB 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 651-655, on the Johannine gift of peace.
Craig L. Blomberg, *Matthew* (NAC 22; Broadman, 1992), pp. 178-180, distinguishing spiritual from social peace.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Luke, John, Acts, Revelation', 5, 2, 4, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Matthew 5:16 reads *houtōs lampsatō to phōs hymōn emprosthen tōn anthrōpōn, hopōs idōsin hymōn ta kala erga kai doxasōsin ton patera hymōn ton en tois ouranois* — ''let your light shine before others, so that they may see your good works and glorify your Father in heaven.'' The verb chain is decisive: the seeing (*idōsin*) of *kala erga* is subordinated to a further telos, *doxasōsin ton patera* — the glorification of God. Visibility is instrumentalized toward God''s honor, not the agent''s. 1 Peter 2:12 echoes this almost verbatim — *ek tōn kalōn ergōn epopteuontes doxasōsin ton theon* — ''observing your good works, they may glorify God'' — which is unsurprising given that 1 Peter shows broad acquaintance with synoptic-style paraenesis (Elliott catalogs the parallels). The Petrine verse is a missiological maxim: exemplary conduct among the Gentiles produces eventual praise to God ''in the day of visitation.''

Matthew 6:1 reverses the field: *prosechete [de] tēn dikaiosynēn hymōn mē poiein emprosthen tōn anthrōpōn pros to theathēnai autois* — ''beware of practicing your righteousness before others in order to be seen by them.'' Note the shared phrase *emprosthen tōn anthrōpōn*, which appears in both 5:16 and 6:1; Matthew is deliberately reusing the locution. The difference lies entirely in the purpose clause: in 5:16 the telos is God''s glory, in 6:1 it is human spectatorship (*pros to theathēnai autois*). Verses 2-4 then narrow *dikaiosynē* to *eleēmosynē* (almsgiving), and the reward logic appears: do it for human eyes and ''you have your reward'' (*apechousin ton misthon autōn*), forfeiting the Father''s.

The strongest apologetic reading — developed by Carson, Blomberg, France, and Hagner — is motivational rather than venue-based. Both texts permit visible good works; what 6:1 forbids is making the visibility the goal. On this reading 5:16 and 6:1 are perfectly compatible: identical external behavior, opposite internal motive. The Greek genuinely supports this for the 5:16/6:1 pairing, since the contrast really does lie in the *hopōs/pros* clauses. A defender can fairly say Matthew himself bracketed the two with *emprosthen tōn anthrōpōn* precisely to invite the comparison and to teach that the orientation of the heart, not the location of the act, is what matters.

Where the motivational defense strains is the continuation of the discourse. Matthew 6:3 instructs *mē gnōtō hē aristera sou ti poiei hē dexia sou* — ''do not let your left hand know what your right hand is doing.'' That is not ''give publicly with a pure heart''; it is hyperbolic counsel toward secrecy so total that the giver scarcely registers the act. Likewise 6:6 on prayer: *eiselthe eis to tameion sou kai kleisas tēn thyran sou proseuxai tō patri sou tō en tō kryptō* — ''go into your inner room, shut the door, and pray to your Father in secret.'' The recommended behavior here is invisibility, not visibility-with-good-motive. The harmonization that fits 6:1 does not straightforwardly fit 6:3-6.

The critical reading (Davies-Allison, Luz, Betz) treats 5:16 and 6:1-18 as distinct traditions joined by the Matthean redactor. The lamp-saying in 5:14-16 has a public, missiological orientation; the ''when you give/pray/fast'' triad of 6:1-18 derives from a Jewish-piety tradition prizing hidden righteousness as the only kind that earns divine reward. The redactor framed both with *emprosthen tōn anthrōpōn* but did not fully reconcile their practical instructions.

Verdict: harmonizable for 5:16 versus 6:1 narrowly construed, on a genuine motivational distinction; partially harmonized overall, since 6:3-6''s counsel toward secrecy resists it.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew, Vol. 1 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 472-475, 575-582, on the redactional framing of 5:16 and 6:1-18
Ulrich Luz, Matthew 1-7: A Commentary, trans. James E. Crouch, rev. ed. (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 209-211, 295-303, on the lamp-saying and the hidden-piety triad
Hans Dieter Betz, The Sermon on the Mount (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1995), pp. 158-161, 329-339, on the cultic-piety background of Matthew 6:1-18
Donald A. Hagner, Matthew 1-13 (WBC 33A; Word, 1993), pp. 100-102, 137-142, on the motivational distinction
R. T. France, The Gospel of Matthew (NICNT; Eerdmans, 2007), pp. 175-179, 231-236, on the motivational reading of 5:16 and 6:1
John H. Elliott, 1 Peter: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary (AB 37B; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 466-471, on the synoptic parallels in 1 Peter 2:12', 0, NULL, 5, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, 1 Peter', 6, 2, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

Deuteronomy 4:2 reads *lōʾ tōsipû ʿal-haddābār ʾăšer ʾānōkî mĕṣawweh ʾetkem wĕlōʾ tigrĕʿû mimmennû* — ''you shall not add to the word which I command you, nor subtract from it.'' The pairing of addition (*ysp*) and subtraction (*grʿ*) is a fixed legal idiom; Deuteronomy 13:1 (Heb; 12:32 Eng) repeats it almost verbatim, and the same ''canon formula'' appears across the ancient Near East (Weinfeld notes its parallels in Mesopotamian and Hittite treaty-curse traditions, where it protects the inviolability of a sworn document). Tigay underscores that in its Deuteronomic context the clause guards the completeness and sufficiency of the covenant stipulations as delivered.

Matthew 5:21-22 has Jesus cite the sixth commandment (*ou phoneuseis*, LXX Exod 20:13 / Deut 5:17) and extend liability to the angry man, the one who says *rhaka*, and the one who says *mōre*. The governing formula — *ēkousate hoti errethē tois archaiois ... egō de legō hymin* — is structurally contrastive. Whether the *de* is adversative (''but I say'') or merely additive (''and I say'') is debated, and the answer affects the charge: an additive reading supports ''deepening,'' an adversative reading supports ''overriding.'' Either way, content not present in the Mosaic text is being supplied on Jesus''s own authority.

The strongest apologetic case — Carson, Blomberg, France, with Wright supplying the theological frame — distinguishes adding to Torah from interpreting or intensifying it. Jesus, they argue, exposes the commandment''s deeper intent: anger is the root from which murder grows, so prohibiting anger is exegesis, not legislation. For the murder/anger antithesis this is genuinely plausible, since rabbinic tradition itself drew ethical inferences from the Decalogue, and Deuteronomy 4:2 was never read in Judaism as forbidding interpretive expansion (the entire halakhic enterprise presupposes it does not).

The defense weakens across the wider antithesis block. In 5:31-32 Jesus restricts what Deuteronomy 24:1-4 plainly permits — that is functionally *subtraction* (*grʿ*), which Deut 4:2 forbids in the same breath as addition. In 5:33-37 his *mē omosai holōs* — ''do not swear at all'' — overrides the oath-regulating provisions of Numbers 30 and Deuteronomy 23:22-24, which assume and govern vow-making. ''Interpretation, not addition'' cannot easily absorb a command that cancels standing Torah permissions and obligations.

The critical reading (Davies-Allison, Luz, and especially Allison''s *The New Moses*) is that Matthew deliberately stages Jesus as a new Moses: the ascent of the mountain in 5:1, the five great discourses echoing the Pentateuch, and the antitheses as a new lawgiving. On this view Matthew''s Christology requires Jesus to occupy the position Deut 4:2 reserves to God alone — the one whose word may neither be added to nor diminished. The antitheses are thus less a violation Matthew overlooked than a claim Matthew intended: Jesus speaks with the authority of the lawgiver, not merely an interpreter of the law. Read that way, the tension with Deut 4:2 is real and theological, not accidental.

Verdict: genuine tension. The ''interpretation not addition'' defense plausibly covers the murder/anger antithesis but breaks on the divorce and oath antitheses, where Jesus narrows or annuls explicit Mosaic legislation — precisely what Deuteronomy 4:2 prohibits.', 'Jeffrey H. Tigay, Deuteronomy (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1996), pp. 42-43, on the canon-protection clause
Moshe Weinfeld, Deuteronomy 1-11 (AB 5; Doubleday, 1991), pp. 199-201, on the ANE parallels to the addition/subtraction formula
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew, Vol. 1 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 504-516, on the antithesis formula
Ulrich Luz, Matthew 1-7: A Commentary, trans. James E. Crouch, rev. ed. (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 224-242, on the antitheses
Dale C. Allison Jr., The New Moses: A Matthean Typology (Fortress, 1993), pp. 172-194, on the new-Moses typology of the Sermon
Hans Dieter Betz, The Sermon on the Mount (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1995), pp. 200-219, on the antitheses', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Deuteronomy, Matthew', 6, 3, 3, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

Deuteronomy 24:1 grounds permission in *kî māṣāʾ bāh ʿerwat dābār* — ''because he has found in her some unseemly thing.'' The phrase *ʿerwat dābār* (literally ''nakedness of a matter'') is notoriously elastic, and Second Temple Judaism exploited the ambiguity. The Mishnah (*m. Gittin* 9:10) preserves the school dispute: the Shammaites confined the ground to sexual misconduct (*dĕbar ʿerwāh*), while the Hillelites permitted divorce ''even if she spoiled a dish,'' and Akiba ''even if he found another fairer than she.'' Legal divorce in the period was effectively at the husband''s discretion.

Mark 10:11-12 gives the most rigorous form: *hos an apolysē tēn gynaika autou kai gamēsē allēn moichatai ep'' autēn* — and, strikingly, the reciprocal clause for a woman divorcing her husband (10:12), which reflects either a Greco-Roman setting or Mark''s adaptation, since Jewish law gave wives no parallel right. No exception is offered. Luke 16:18 agrees in substance. Most reconstructions of the historical Jesus (Sanders, Meier) treat this absolute prohibition, grounded in the creation order of Genesis 1-2 rather than in Deuteronomy, as the earliest layer.

Matthew adds the famous qualifier — *parektos logou porneias* (5:32) and *mē epi porneia* (19:9). The lexical range of *porneia* drives much of the debate: it may denote adultery (the Shammaite reading), marriage within forbidden degrees of kinship (Fitzmyer''s reading, supported by the use of *zĕnût* in CD 4:20-5:2 at Qumran for incestuous unions), or sexual immorality broadly. Davies-Allison, Luz, and Hagner read the clauses as Matthean redactional softening for a community that needed a workable halakhic exception.

Paul adds a fourth position. In 1 Corinthians 7:10-16 he carefully distinguishes the dominical command (''not I but the Lord,'' 7:10) from his own ruling (''I, not the Lord,'' 7:12), and then in 7:15 permits separation when an unbelieving spouse departs (*ei de ho apistos chōrizetai, chōrizesthō*). Paul is transparent that he is extending the tradition to a situation Jesus did not address.

The most serious harmonizations — Heth and Wenham, Instone-Brewer, and the older work of John Murray — argue that Mark and Luke abbreviate a teaching that originally carried the Shammaite-style exception Matthew preserves, that Paul applies the same principle pastorally, and that all cohere under one marriage ethic. Instone-Brewer in particular reconstructs a first-century rabbinic debate Jesus is presumed to be entering, so that the *porneia* exception is original and the absolute forms are compressions.

The critical objection is twofold. First, the abbreviation thesis requires that Mark and Luke independently drop precisely the clause their communities would most want to retain — a counterintuitive direction of redaction, since the tendency in transmission is to soften hard sayings, not harden them. Marcus notes that the more rigorous Markan form is the *lectio difficilior*. Second, Paul''s explicit ''I, not the Lord'' shows the early church knew it was developing, not merely transmitting, the teaching. The four positions look like a tradition negotiated across communities and stages rather than a single ruling variously abridged.

Verdict: genuine multi-source divergence. The positions reconcile only by postulating redactional abbreviation against the grain of normal transmission tendencies; the most natural reading is a developing tradition that successively tightens Deuteronomy and then re-admits exceptions under pastoral pressure.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988/1997), Vol. 1 pp. 528-532, Vol. 3 pp. 9-21, on the Matthean exception clauses
Joel Marcus, Mark 8-16 (AYB 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 705-718, on the Markan absolute prohibition
