John P. Meier, A Marginal Jew, Vol. 4: Law and Love (AYBRL; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 74-181, on the historical Jesus on divorce
David Instone-Brewer, Divorce and Remarriage in the Bible: The Social and Literary Context (Eerdmans, 2002), pp. 133-188, on the rabbinic-debate reconstruction
William A. Heth and Gordon J. Wenham, Jesus and Divorce (Hodder & Stoughton, 1984), pp. 99-125, on the evangelical harmonization
Moshe Weinfeld, Deuteronomy and the Deuteronomic School (Clarendon, 1972), pp. 282-297, on Deuteronomic divorce law
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, First Corinthians (AYB 32; Yale University Press, 2008), pp. 290-301, on the Pauline desertion exception', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Deuteronomy, Matthew, Mark, Luke, 1 Corinthians', 6, 3, 4, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Isaiah 64:6 (MT 64:5) belongs to a communal lament. The surrounding verses plead with a God who has ''hidden his face'' (64:7) over a people in exile: *wattĕmaʿăṭēnû bĕyad-ʿăwōnēnû* — ''and you have melted us into the hand of our iniquity.'' The image *kĕbeged ʿiddîm* — ''like a menstruous/soiled garment'' — describes the collective state of a guilty nation in a specific historical moment, not a metaphysical claim that no individual can possess any virtue. Blenkinsopp and Westermann both read it as corporate confession within the Trito-Isaianic lament, and to make it a universal anthropology (the Augustinian and later Calvinist use) is to lift it from its genre. The same caution applies to Paul''s catena in Romans 3:10-12, which strings together Psalm 14:1-3 and other texts; Fitzmyer notes that Paul reapplies originally particular or hyperbolic statements to a universal forensic argument about Jew and Gentile alike standing ''under sin.'' The Hebrew Psalm targets a class of fools and evildoers, not literally every human.

Meanwhile the Hebrew Bible cheerfully calls specific people righteous: Noah is *ʾîš ṣaddîq tāmîm* (Gen 6:9), Job is declared *tām wĕyāšār* by God himself (Job 1:8), and Luke 1:6 calls Zechariah and Elizabeth *dikaioi ... enantion tou theou*. Luke 23:50 describes Joseph of Arimathea as *anēr agathos kai dikaios* — ''a good and righteous man.''

The sharper problem is internal to Luke. In Luke 18:19 (paralleling Mark 10:18) Jesus responds to the address ''good teacher'' with *ti me legeis agathon? oudeis agathos ei mē heis ho theos* — ''why do you call me good? No one is good except God alone.'' That is a flat denial of *agathos* to anyone but God. Yet the same author, five chapters later, applies *agathos* to Joseph of Arimathea. Marcus and Fitzmyer both note the theological awkwardness of the Markan saying — so much so that Matthew 19:17 rewrites it to blunt the implication (''Why do you ask me about what is good?''), a redactional tell that early scribes and evangelists already felt the tension.

The standard apologetic move — Bock, the IVP *Hard Sayings* tradition, and Carson — distinguishes absolute goodness (God alone) from relative or derivative goodness (humans being called good in a qualified sense). Theologically this is coherent: Joseph can be ''good'' as a creature without claiming the unqualified moral perfection that belongs to God. The difficulty is that the Greek text does not mark the distinction. *Agathos* in Luke 18:19 and *agathos* in Luke 23:50 are the same lexeme with no qualifier; the relative/absolute split is supplied by the interpreter, not by the syntax. Where Luke wanted to qualify, as in 18:19, he could and did (''except God alone''); in 23:50 he does not.

Verdict: overstated on the Old Testament side, where the lament and Psalm genres dissolve the apparent universal claim; partially harmonized overall, since the relative-vs-absolute distinction is theologically sound but is not encoded in the shared Greek vocabulary of Luke 18:19 and 23:50, leaving a residual lexical tension Matthew himself smoothed.', 'Joseph Blenkinsopp, Isaiah 56-66 (AB 19B; Doubleday, 2003), pp. 263-273, on Isaiah 64 as communal lament
Claus Westermann, Isaiah 40-66: A Commentary, trans. David M. G. Stalker (OTL; Westminster, 1969), pp. 391-396, on the lament genre
Joel Marcus, Mark 8-16 (AYB 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 718-723, on oudeis agathos and its redaction
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV (AB 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1525-1528, on Luke 23:50
Darrell L. Bock, Luke 9:51-24:53 (BECNT; Baker, 1996), pp. 1865-1869, on the goodness of Joseph of Arimathea
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, Romans (AB 33; Doubleday, 1993), pp. 333-339, on the Romans 3:10-12 catena', 0, NULL, 4, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Isaiah, Luke', 5, 2, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

Matthew 6:5-6 is the strict text. The hypocrites are condemned because *philousin en tais synagōgais kai en tais gōniais tōn plateiōn hestōtes proseuchesthai, hopōs phanōsin tois anthrōpois* — ''they love to pray standing in synagogues and on street corners so as to be visible to people.'' The prescribed alternative is concrete: *eiselthe eis to tameion sou kai kleisas tēn thyran sou proseuxai tō patri sou tō en tō kryptō* — ''go into your inner room, shut the door, and pray to your Father in secret.'' The *tameion* is the storeroom or innermost chamber of a Palestinian house, frequently the only room with a door that could be barred (Betz and Luz note that the detail signals deliberate concealment). The recommended behavior is privacy, not merely purity of motive.

John 11:41-42 cuts directly against this: Jesus prays aloud at Lazarus''s tomb before a crowd and states the reason — *dia ton ochlon ton periestōta eipon, hina pisteusōsin hoti sy me apesteilas* — ''because of the crowd standing around I said it, so that they may believe you sent me.'' Brown observes that this is an unusually self-conscious ''audience-directed'' prayer, characteristic of the Fourth Gospel''s revelatory style, in which Jesus'' words function as signs for onlookers. The visibility is not incidental; it is the stated purpose — formally the very *hopōs phanōsin tois anthrōpois* Matthew 6:5 censures, redeemed in John only by its evangelistic end. 1 Timothy 2:8 then directs *proseuchesthai tous andras en panti topō* — ''that men pray in every place,'' lifting holy hands, a posture that is public and demonstrative; Mounce reads it as legislating the public liturgical assembly. Acts reinforces the pattern with assembled prayer throughout (1:14, 24; 3:1; 4:24-31; 20:36; 21:5).

The apologetic harmonization — Carson, France, Mounce — is the motivational distinction: Matthew condemns ostentation aimed at human admiration, not corporate or genuinely public prayer offered for proper ends. There is real force here, and it likely captures the redactor''s intent within 6:5 itself, where the censure clause is *hopōs phanōsin*. The Lord''s Prayer that immediately follows (6:9-13) is plainly cast for communal use (''our Father,'' plural pronouns throughout), which shows Matthew did not intend to abolish shared prayer altogether.

The difficulty, as in entry 407, is the positive instruction in 6:6. Matthew does not say ''pray publicly with a pure heart''; he says go into the inner room and shut the door. The remedy he prescribes is spatial seclusion, not merely interior re-orientation. A defender must treat 6:6 as hyperbole — and it may well be — but then John 11:42''s openly purpose-driven public prayer and 1 Timothy 2:8''s ''in every place'' sit at the opposite pole from the hyperbole''s literal sense.

The critical reading (Davies-Allison, Luz, Betz) places 6:5-6 inside the hidden-piety triad of 6:1-18 (almsgiving, prayer, fasting), a unit drawn from a Jewish-piety tradition prizing covert religious acts. Matthew inherited it and set it beside the communal Lord''s Prayer and his community''s evidently corporate worship without fully smoothing the join. John and the Pastorals reflect later communities for whom public, demonstrative prayer was simply normal.

Verdict: partially harmonized. The motivational distinction reconciles most of the canon''s public prayers, but Matthew 6:6''s directive to pray in a shut inner room is most naturally a counsel toward private rather than public prayer, leaving a residual tension with John 11:41-42 and 1 Timothy 2:8.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew, Vol. 1 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 583-588, on Matthew 6:5-6
Ulrich Luz, Matthew 1-7: A Commentary, trans. James E. Crouch, rev. ed. (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 303-308, on the tameion
Hans Dieter Betz, The Sermon on the Mount (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1995), pp. 357-369, on prayer in the inner chamber
Raymond E. Brown, The Gospel According to John (i-xii) (AB 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 425-437, on the prayer at Lazarus''s tomb
William D. Mounce, Pastoral Epistles (WBC 46; Thomas Nelson, 2000), pp. 105-110, on 1 Timothy 2:8
R. T. France, The Gospel of Matthew (NICNT; Eerdmans, 2007), pp. 237-241, on the motivational reading of Matthew 6:5-6', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, John, 1 Timothy', 6, 2, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

Matthew 6:9-13, with its Lukan parallel at 11:2-4, is a model prayer given in response to the disciples'' request, preserved most explicitly in Luke 11:1: *kyrie, didaxon hēmas proseuchesthai* — ''Lord, teach us to pray.'' The Matthean introduction *houtōs oun proseuchesthe hymeis* — ''pray, then, in this way'' — frames what follows as a template, not a fixed liturgical formula to be recited verbatim (Luz). Its petitions — the hallowing of the Name, the coming of the kingdom, daily bread (*ton arton hēmōn ton epiousion*, a notoriously rare word), forgiveness of debts, and rescue from the test — are conventional in shape, drawing on Jewish prayer-patterns such as the Qaddish and the Amidah (Davies-Allison). The prayer teaches *what* to seek and *how* to order petition; it is catechesis in the structure of address to God.

Romans 8:26 is doing something different and at a different register. Paul writes *hōsautōs de kai to pneuma synantilambanetai tē astheneia hēmōn* — ''likewise the Spirit also helps us in our weakness'' — and grounds it in *to gar ti proseuxōmetha katho dei ouk oidamen* — ''for we do not know what to pray for as we ought.'' The setting is the chapter''s groaning creation, the *douleia tēs phthoras* (''slavery to corruption,'' 8:21), and the believer''s own inward groaning. The Spirit''s intercession is *stenagmois alalētois* — ''with wordless groanings'' (or ''sighs too deep for words'') — language that Käsemann and Jewett connect to charismatic, possibly glossolalic, prayer-experience rather than to the absence of any verbal prayer.

The harmonization advanced by Cranfield, Moo, and Schreiner is sound and probably reflects Paul''s intent: the two passages concern different aspects of prayer. The Lord''s Prayer furnishes form and content; Romans 8:26 addresses the existential depth of prayer under affliction, where even a believer equipped with a model does not know the right object to ask for in a given trial. Decisively, *katho dei* — ''as we ought'' — qualifies the ignorance. Paul does not say believers cannot pray; he says they lack the deep knowledge of what to request, and the Spirit supplies that lack. The model and the help work at different levels and do not compete.

The residue is this. Romans 8:26 says *ouk oidamen* — ''we do not know'' — flatly, without restricting it to acute suffering, and the frame of Romans 8 is the universal creaturely condition, not a subset of hard cases. If the Lord''s Prayer had fully equipped Christians to pray, the unqualified verb is at least rhetorically awkward; Paul writes as though the problem of prayer remains open after any catechesis. The most economical critical explanation (Jewett; Käsemann) is that Paul''s pneumatology occupies a different stream from the Synoptic teaching. Paul wrote in the 50s, before the Gospels were composed, and shows no demonstrable knowledge of the Lord''s Prayer tradition; nowhere in the undisputed letters does he cite or allude to it. The canon later sets a Jewish-Christian catechetical stream (Matthew/Luke) beside a Pauline pneumatological stream without reconciling them. They are complementary in practice but not derived from one another.

Verdict: harmonizable at the level of meaning — the ''different aspects of prayer'' reading holds, anchored by *katho dei* — with the qualification that the unqualified *ouk oidamen* reflects an independent Pauline theology of prayer rather than a deliberate gloss on Jesus''s model. Overstated as a contradiction.', 'C. E. B. Cranfield, A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Epistle to the Romans, Vol. 1 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1975), pp. 421-426, on Romans 8:26
Robert Jewett, Romans: A Commentary (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 521-525, on Romans 8:26
Ernst Käsemann, Commentary on Romans (Eerdmans, 1980), pp. 239-242, on Romans 8:26
Douglas J. Moo, The Epistle to the Romans (NICNT; Eerdmans, 1996), pp. 521-526, on Romans 8:26
Thomas R. Schreiner, Romans (BECNT; Baker Academic, 1998), pp. 444-450, on Romans 8:26
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew, Vol. 1 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 591-615, on Matthew 6:9-13
Ulrich Luz, Matthew 1-7: A Commentary, rev. ed., trans. James E. Crouch (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 308-326, on Matthew 6:9-13', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Romans', 6, 2, 2, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

Matthew 6:10''s petition *genēthētō to thelēma sou, hōs en ouranō kai epi gēs* — ''your will be done, as in heaven so on earth'' — treats heaven as the locus where God''s will is already perfectly executed and earth as the realm where it is not yet. The comparative clause presupposes that heaven currently exemplifies what earth currently lacks; heaven is the standard, earth the petition (Davies-Allison).

Revelation 12:7-9 stages something else: *egeneto polemos en tō ouranō, ho Michaēl kai hoi angeloi autou tou polemēsai meta tou drakontos* — ''there was war in heaven, Michael and his angels fighting against the dragon.'' The dragon and his angels are defeated and thrown down to the earth. This presupposes a heavenly realm in which a powerful adversary can operate and from which he must be removed by battle — not obviously a place where God''s will is already and frictionlessly enacted.

The harmonizing case (Beale, Mounce, Aune) runs along two paths. First, the *temporal* path: the aorist *egeneto* narrates a completed event — the primordial expulsion of Satan — so that heaven''s present condition is one of restored, uncontested obedience, and Matthew 6:10 describes that settled post-war state. Second, the *cosmographic* path: in Second Temple and apocalyptic literature heaven is multi-tiered (Paul speaks of being caught up to the *tritou ouranou*, ''third heaven,'' 2 Cor 12:2; cf. the seven heavens of 2 Enoch and 3 Baruch), so conflict may occur in a lower heaven while God''s will reigns absolutely in the highest. Both moves enjoy textual support, and either can dissolve the literal collision of the two verses.

The complication is that Revelation 12 resists being tidily filed under either move. Many commentators read the chapter as recapitulatory rather than strictly past: the dragon''s expulsion is precisely what intensifies persecution on earth (12:12, ''woe to the earth... the devil has come down to you in great wrath''), and the victory over him is won ''by the blood of the Lamb'' (12:11), tying the heavenly war to the cross and the church''s present experience. The war is staged as cosmically active, not safely sealed in a pre-creation past.

The deeper issue lies behind the verses. The Hebrew Bible does not present heaven as a place of monolithic, automatic obedience. In 1 Kings 22:19-22 Yahweh sits enthroned amid the *ṣĕbāʾ haššāmayim* (''host of heaven'') and the council deliberates, with a ''spirit'' volunteering to entice Ahab. Job 1-2 has *haśśāṭān* (''the accuser'') roaming the earth and arguing his case in the divine court. Genesis 6:1-4 has the *bĕnê hāʾĕlōhîm* (''sons of God'') transgress by taking human wives. Daniel 10:13 has the ''prince of the kingdom of Persia'' withstand Gabriel for twenty-one days until Michael intervenes. As Mark Smith and John Collins document, the inherited Israelite and Second Temple picture of heaven is a populated, sometimes contested council, not a realm of instantaneous compliance.

The critical reading (Aune; Collins; Smith) is that Matthew 6:10''s serenely obedient heaven reflects a later, more streamlined cosmology, while Revelation 12 preserves the older mythic pattern of heavenly conflict rooted in Canaanite and Near Eastern combat myth (the dragon, the woman, the cosmic battle). The two are different cosmological strata that the canon places side by side.

Verdict: harmonizable at the verse level by the primordial-fall or multi-tier-heaven readings; the broader Hebrew Bible and Second Temple picture of heaven as a contested divine council sits in real, unresolved tension with the Lord''s Prayer''s heaven-as-perfect-obedience. Thin locally, deeper canonically.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew, Vol. 1 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 605-606, on Matthew 6:10
G. K. Beale, The Book of Revelation (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 1999), pp. 650-668, on Revelation 12:7
Robert H. Mounce, The Book of Revelation, rev. ed. (NICNT; Eerdmans, 1997), pp. 233-244, on Revelation 12:7-12
David E. Aune, Revelation 6-16 (WBC 52B; Thomas Nelson, 1998), pp. 691-700, on Revelation 12:7-9
John J. Collins, The Apocalyptic Imagination: An Introduction to Jewish Apocalyptic Literature, 3rd ed. (Eerdmans, 2016), pp. 280-313, on Revelation 12:7
Mark S. Smith, The Origins of Biblical Monotheism: Israel''s Polytheistic Background and the Ugaritic Texts (Oxford University Press, 2001), pp. 38-66, on the divine council (1 Kings 22:19; Job 1:6)', 0, NULL, 2, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Revelation', 5, 3, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

Matthew 6:25-34 turns on *merimnaō* — to be anxious, to be inwardly torn, to worry. The imperative is *mē merimnāte tē psychē hymōn* — ''do not be anxious about your life'' — as to what you will eat, drink, or wear. The argument is providential and rhetorical: the birds *ou speirousin oude therizousin* (''neither sow nor reap'') yet the Father feeds them; the lilies *ou kopiōsin oude nēthousin* (''neither toil nor spin'') yet are clothed more gloriously than Solomon. The conclusion is *mē merimnēsēte eis tēn aurion* — ''do not be anxious about tomorrow.'' Disciples are to *zēteite de prōton tēn basileian* — ''seek first the kingdom.''

1 Timothy 5:8 uses a different verb: *pronoeō* — to think ahead, to provide, to take forethought — and a different object, *tōn idiōn kai malista oikeiōn* (''one''s own, and especially household members''). The failure to provide is so grave it amounts to having *tēn pistin ērnētai* (''denied the faith'') and being worse than *apistos* (''an unbeliever''). The context (5:3-16) is the concrete administration of the widow-roll, an institutional welfare problem in a settled congregation.

The harmonizing case (Marshall, Mounce, France) distinguishes anxiety from forethought. *Merimna* names the paralyzing, faith-corroding fear about basic survival; *pronoia* names the responsible planning a head of household owes dependents. The Greek terms genuinely differ, and the conceptual distinction is real — Stoic moral psychology drew comparable lines between destructive *pathos* and rational *prohairesis*. On this reading Jesus condemns distrustful worry, the Pastor commands prudent provision, and no contradiction arises at the lexical level. This is a credible and partially successful move.

What it understates is how demanding Matthew 6:31-34 actually is. The instruction is not ''plan prudently while refusing to fret''; it is ''consider the lilies — they neither toil nor spin.'' The illustrative logic explicitly contrasts disciples with creatures that do *not* labor, sow, or store, and asks them to trust accordingly. Taken at face value this is a radical ethic that presupposes the imminent in-breaking of the kingdom and the obsolescence of ordinary economic forethought (Luz; Allison). It coheres with the sending instructions to take no bag, no bread, no money (Mark 6:8; Luke 9:3; 10:4) — an itinerant pattern, not a household budget.

The critical reading (Theissen; Allison; Crossan) is that Matthew 6 preserves an early, pre-70 wandering-charismatic ethic. Jesus and his first followers were itinerant prophets who did not work, save, or maintain households, because they expected the end soon. The Pastoral Epistles, which the dominant critical consensus regards as post-Pauline pseudepigrapha from the late first or early second century (Marshall is a notable dissenter arguing for an ''allonymous'' Pauline circle), reflect a settled, institutional Christianity in which households persist over decades, congregations administer welfare rolls, and the eschatological clock has slowed. The shift Theissen labels the move from ''wandering charismatics'' to ''local communities'' is precisely the shift between these two texts. The *merimna*/*pronoia* distinction is real, but it papers over a development in social setting and eschatological expectation that the canon preserves without reconciling.

Verdict: the lexical distinction defensibly softens the surface clash. The underlying tension — Jesus''s itinerant-prophet ethic versus the Pastorals'' settled-household ethic — is genuine and is one of the standard developmental seams critical scholarship maps across the New Testament. Partially harmonizable.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew, Vol. 1 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 642-664, on Matthew 6:25-34
Ulrich Luz, Matthew 1-7: A Commentary, rev. ed., trans. James E. Crouch (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 339-352, on Matthew 6:31-34
I. Howard Marshall with Philip H. Towner, A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Pastoral Epistles (ICC; T&T Clark, 1999), pp. 590-594, on 1 Timothy 5:8
William D. Mounce, Pastoral Epistles (WBC 46; Thomas Nelson, 2000), pp. 282-285, on 1 Timothy 5:8
Gerd Theissen, The First Followers of Jesus: A Sociological Analysis of the Earliest Christianity, trans. John Bowden (SCM, 1978), pp. 8-23, on Matthew 6:31, 34
Dale C. Allison Jr., The End of the Ages Has Come: An Early Interpretation of the Passion and Resurrection of Jesus (Fortress, 1985), pp. 84-101, on Matthew 6:34', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, 1 Timothy', 6, 2, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

Mark 16:9-20, the ''Longer Ending'' that supplies the casting-out-demons sign in v. 17, is absent from the two oldest and best Greek uncials, Codex Sinaiticus (ℵ) and Codex Vaticanus (B), both fourth century, and from the Sinaitic Syriac, important Old Latin witnesses (notably Codex Bobbiensis, which has a different ''Shorter Ending''), and a substantial number of Armenian and Georgian manuscripts. Eusebius and Jerome report that the passage was missing from almost all the Greek copies known to them. Its vocabulary and style diverge markedly from authentic Mark, and the syntactic seam at 16:8 (the abrupt *ephobounto gar*) shows that vv. 9-20 were appended to round off a Gospel that early scribes found uncomfortably open-ended (Metzger; Marcus). The critical consensus, reflected in the NA28 apparatus and the bracketing or footnoting of the passage in the NRSV, NIV, and ESV, is that 16:9-20 is a second-century addition. Building a contradiction on Mark 16:17 means building it on a passage that almost certainly was not original to Mark.

The tension survives in attenuated form without that verse, but it reduces to nuance. Mark 9:38-40, paralleled at Luke 9:49-50, has the disciples report *eidomen tina en tō onomati sou ekballonta daimonia* — ''we saw someone casting out demons in your name'' — and forbid him *hoti ouk ēkolouthei hēmin* (''because he was not following us''). Jesus answers *mē kōlyete* (''do not stop him'') and *hos gar ouk estin kath'' hēmōn hyper hēmōn estin* — ''whoever is not against us is for us.'' (Notably the Matthean saying at 12:30 runs the other way — ''whoever is not with me is against me'' — a separate logion in a different polemical context; the two are not strictly contradictory because their reference points differ.) Exorcism in Jesus''s name is here treated as effective and tolerable even by a non-disciple.

Matthew 7:22-23, part of the Sermon on the Mount and without Markan parallel, has many plead *kyrie kyrie, ou tō sō onomati... daimonia exebalomen?* — ''Lord, Lord, did we not... cast out demons in your name?'' — only to hear *oudepote egnōn hymas; apochōreite ap'' emou hoi ergazomenoi tēn anomian* — ''I never knew you; depart from me, you workers of lawlessness.'' The point is not that exorcism is illegitimate but that it cannot by itself authenticate a saving relationship.

The harmonization (Carson; France; Davies-Allison; Hagner) distinguishes the social and theological functions of the act. The narrative of the sons of Sceva (Acts 19:13-16), where Jewish exorcists invoke ''the Jesus whom Paul proclaims'' and are overpowered, shows the early church''s own awareness that the powerful Name could be wielded — even disastrously — by those outside genuine discipleship. So: outsiders may perform exorcisms (Mark 9); believers characteristically do (Mark 16, if it were authentic); and performing them proves nothing about one''s standing before Jesus (Matthew 7). These are three statements about distinct relationships to the act, and they are logically consistent. The question, ''is exorcism a sign of a true Christian,'' is answered by the canon with a coherent ''it accompanies them but does not by itself prove them'' — not with three incompatible verdicts.

Verdict: the lead citation (Mark 16:17) is a textually secondary interpolation and should not anchor the claim; the remaining Mark 9 / Matthew 7 material is nuance, not contradiction, and is harmonizable. The framing overstates the conflict.', 'Bruce M. Metzger, A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament, 2nd ed. (Deutsche Bibelgesellschaft, 1994), pp. 102-106, on Mark 16:17
Joel Marcus, Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary (Anchor Yale Bible 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 1088-1096, on Mark 16:9-20
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew, Vol. 1 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 715-720, on Matthew 7:22-23
