The omniscience-affirming texts are largely Johannine and Pauline, and most are confessions rather than narrator-assertions. John 16:30 is the disciples'' acclamation, *nyn oidamen hoti oidas panta* — ''now we know that you know all things'' — prompted by Jesus anticipating their question; it reports their growing faith, not a metaphysical inventory. John 21:17 is Peter''s *kyrie, panta sy oidas* — ''Lord, you know all things'' — in a scene testing Peter''s love, where ''you know all things'' functions as ''you know my heart.'' Colossians 2:2-3, *en hō eisin pantes hoi thēsauroi tēs sophias kai gnōseōs apokryphoi* (''in whom are hidden all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge''), is a hymnic, cosmic-Christological claim about Christ as the locus of divine wisdom, not a statement that the earthly Jesus consciously held every fact (Dunn, Colossians).

The ignorance texts, by contrast, are direct narrator-claims or Jesus''s own words. Mark 5:30 has Jesus *epignous en heautō tēn ex autou dynamin exelthousan* (''perceiving in himself that power had gone out of him'') turn and ask *tis mou hēpsato tōn himatiōn?* — ''who touched my garments?'' The disciples find the question absurd in a crush of people (5:31), and the woman must come forward and confess; the narrative does not read as a staged or rhetorical question (Marcus). Luke 2:52, *kai Iēsous proekopten en tē sophia* (''and Jesus increased in wisdom''), presupposes a developing, non-omniscient baseline. The hardest text is Mark 13:32 / Matthew 24:36: *peri de tēs hēmeras ekeinēs ē tēs hōras oudeis oiden, oude hoi angeloi en ouranō oude ho huios, ei mē ho patēr* — ''about that day or hour no one knows, not the angels in heaven, nor the Son, but only the Father.'' Jesus explicitly excludes himself, *ho huios*, from a specific knowledge. Its difficulty is confirmed by the textual record: the clause ''nor the Son'' is omitted in many Matthean witnesses, a scribal softening that itself testifies to early discomfort.

The harmonization, formalized at Chalcedon (451) and defended by orthodox commentators since (Pannenberg, Macleod, Bauckham, Wright), is the two-natures doctrine, often joined to a kenotic reading of Philippians 2:7''s *heauton ekenōsen* (''he emptied himself''). Jesus possesses omniscience in his divine nature while experiencing genuine human limitation in his human nature; the ignorance texts speak of the latter, the omniscience texts of the former. This is internally consistent and is the strongest form of the apologetic case. If one grants Chalcedon, Mark 13:32 and John 16:30 can both stand.

What the harmonization cannot show is that the Gospel writers were operating with this framework. Mark simply narrates Jesus not knowing who touched him and adds no parenthesis (''he asked this according to his human nature''). Mark 13:32 places the denial in Jesus''s own mouth, unqualified. The two-natures epistemology is a later conceptual achievement, and reading it back into Mark is anachronistic. The critical reading (Brown; Marcus; Dunn, Christology in the Making) is that New Testament Christology is developmental: Mark''s Jesus is more humanly limited; John''s Jesus knows hearts and origins and ''all things''; the deutero-Pauline hymns (Colossians, Ephesians, Philippians 2) ascend higher still. The omniscience question is a snapshot of that trajectory rather than two faces of a single settled metaphysic. Crucially, pre-Chalcedonian Christians took the limitation texts at face value: Adoptionists and Arians built whole Christologies on Mark 13:32 and Luke 2:52, which is precisely why the orthodox party had to develop the two-natures formula to contain them.

Verdict: disputed. The kenotic/two-natures harmonization is coherent as dogmatic theology if Chalcedon is granted, but it does not describe what the evangelists themselves were doing; Mark 5:30 and the unqualified Mark 13:32 present a Jesus who does not know certain things, and the omniscience texts are largely confessional. The resolution is doctrinal, not exegetical.', 'Raymond E. Brown, The Gospel According to John (xiii-xxi): Introduction, Translation, and Notes (Anchor Bible 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 711-714, 1101-1105, on John 16:30; 21:17
Joel Marcus, Mark 1-8: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary (Anchor Bible 27; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 359-369, on Mark 5:30
Joel Marcus, Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary (Anchor Yale Bible 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 911-915, on Mark 13:32
