Richard Bauckham, God Crucified: Monotheism and Christology in the New Testament (Eerdmans, 1998), pp. 25-42, on Colossians 2:2-3
James D. G. Dunn, The Epistles to the Colossians and to Philemon (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 1996), pp. 130-138, on Colossians 2:2-3', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Mark, Luke, John, Colossians', 5, 3, 4, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Begin with Matthew 8:11-12. The saying caps the centurion pericope: many will come *apo anatolōn kai dysmōn* — from east and west — to recline with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, while *hoi huioi tēs basileias* are cast *eis to skotos to exōteron*, into the outer darkness. The SAB gloss reads ''sons of the kingdom'' as ''the Jews.'' But Matthew''s own usage governs the phrase. At 13:38, in the interpretation of the weeds, *hoi huioi tēs basileias* are the good seed, the righteous — explicitly contrasted with *hoi huioi tou ponērou*, the sons of the evil one. The phrase tracks covenantal-eschatological status, not ethnicity. In the 8:12 context, set against a Gentile centurion''s exemplary faith, ''sons of the kingdom'' denotes those who presumed on covenant membership yet failed to respond — unfaithful Israelites, not Israel as such. Davies and Allison''s ICC stresses precisely this contrast between presumption and response, not a wholesale ethnic verdict.

That said, the deeper tension survives the correction. Romans 9-11 is Paul''s sustained wrestling with the same crisis: if Jesus is messiah and the bulk of Israel has not believed, has the divine promise *ekpeptōken* — fallen, failed (Rom 9:6)? Paul''s resolution turns on the remnant (9:27, citing Isaiah), the olive-tree allegory in which natural branches are broken off so wild branches may be grafted in (11:17-24), and the climactic mystery: *kai houtōs pas Israēl sōthēsetai*, ''and so all Israel will be saved'' (11:26). The meaning of *pas Israēl* is fiercely contested. Cranfield''s ICC reads it as ethnic Israel restored at the eschaton; Wright reads it as ''the totality of the elect, Jew and Gentile,'' the redefined people of God; Jewett''s Hermeneia and Fitzmyer''s Anchor Bible favor a future restoration of ethnic Israel as a corporate body. *Houtōs* (''in this manner'') and the future tense point most naturally to an eschatological event, not a tally of present conversions.

Matthew 1:21 is the most local of the three: *autos gar sōsei ton laon autou apo tōn hamartiōn autōn*, ''he will save his people from their sins.'' In the immediate Matthean frame *ton laon autou* designates Jesus''s own people, Israel, redeemed from sin — a claim later widened implicitly through the magi (2:1-12) and explicitly through the Great Commission (28:19) to encompass the nations.

The strongest harmonization — Carson, Blomberg, Moo — reads a single coherent narrative: Jesus came first to the lost sheep of the house of Israel (15:24); a faithful remnant believed and was saved; the unfaithful faced judgment (8:12); and Paul anticipates a future, corporate, eschatological salvation of Israel (11:26). On the distinction between present individual judgment of unbelief and future corporate restoration, the texts cohere. This is not a strawman: the categories are textually grounded — the remnant in Paul, the response-criterion in Matthew.

Where the residue lies is historical and tonal. Matthew, composed after 70 CE for a Jewish-Christian community in bitter conflict with the synagogue, sharpens the anti-unbelief polemic to a degree Paul does not: the wicked tenants (21:33-46), the self-imprecation ''his blood be on us and on our children'' (27:25), the wedding-feast judgment (22:1-14). Sanders and Räisänen see Matthew and Paul preserving theologies of Israel that pull hard against each other — Paul''s open future versus Matthew''s near-closure. Wright and Bird counter that these are stages of one tradition working out the same problem and can be held together. The formal contradiction the SAB alleges does not survive scrutiny; the theological and historical fault line does, and it carried catastrophic consequences in the later Christian reading of these texts.

Verdict: overstated as framed — partially harmonizable once ethnicity and covenantal faithfulness are not conflated, but a genuine theological tension between Matthean polemic and Pauline restoration remains.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew, Vol. 2 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1991), pp. 27-32, on Matthew 8:11-12 and the response-criterion reading of ''sons of the kingdom''.
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew, Vol. 1 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 209-213, on Matthew 1:21.
Robert Jewett, Romans: A Commentary (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 696-704, on Romans 11:26.
