The homeless logion is Q material, preserved nearly verbatim in Matthew 8:20 and Luke 9:58: *hai alōpekes phōleous echousin kai ta peteina tou ouranou kataskēnōseis, ho de huios tou anthrōpou ouk echei pou tēn kephalēn klinē* — ''foxes have dens and the birds of the air have nests, but the Son of Man has nowhere to lay his head.'' In both Gospels the saying answers a would-be follower (a scribe in Matthew, an unnamed man in Luke) and functions as a discipleship-cost warning. Its register is the itinerant ministry: Jesus and his band travel without settled accommodation of their own. The claim is about lacking owned property and a fixed domicile, not about being barred from every roof.

The Markan ''home'' references require attention to idiom. Mark 2:1 reads *eiselthōn palin eis Kapharnaoum di'' hēmerōn ēkousthē hoti en oikō estin* — ''having entered again into Capernaum after some days, it was heard that he was *en oikō*.'' The anarthrous phrase *en oikō* is idiomatic for ''at home'' or ''indoors'' and does not assert ownership. In a Capernaum setting the natural referent is the house of Simon and Andrew, introduced at Mark 1:29 as the place Jesus enters immediately after the synagogue and where he heals Peter''s mother-in-law. That household plausibly served as Jesus''s Galilean base. The archaeological dossier is consistent with this: the so-called ''House of Peter'' at Capernaum, a first-century insula structure that by the late Roman and Byzantine periods was venerated as a *domus-ecclesia* (its walls plastered and inscribed with Christian graffiti, later enclosed by an octagonal church), has been excavated and discussed by Tzaferis and assessed in its Galilean socioeconomic context by Reed. None of this establishes that Jesus owned a house; it supports the picture of a borrowed base.

Mark 2:15 is the sharper test: *kai ginetai katakeisthai auton en tē oikia autou* — ''and it happens that he was reclining in his house.'' Whose house is *autou*? The pronoun is genuinely ambiguous in Greek, but the immediate antecedent is Levi, just called at 2:14, and the Lukan parallel removes all doubt: Luke 5:29 states *epoiēsen dochēn megalēn Leuis autō en tē oikia autou*, ''Levi made a great banquet for him in his (Levi''s) own house.'' Accordingly the NRSV and NIV render Mark 2:15 as the dinner in Levi''s house. The KJV''s flat ''his house'' is what generates the appearance of a Jesus-owned residence; the Greek and the synoptic parallel do not require it.

The strongest harmonization — Carson, France''s NICNT Mark, Marcus''s Anchor Bible Mark — distinguishes two true propositions: Jesus owned no property of his own (the Q saying), and Jesus used others'' homes, chiefly Peter''s, as bases and was a guest at meals such as Levi''s banquet (the Markan notices). These do not collide because one concerns ownership and itinerancy, the other concerns hospitality and presence. The reading is exegetically disciplined rather than special pleading: it follows the actual antecedents and the synoptic control in Luke.

The residue is small. Mark 2:1''s *ēkousthē hoti en oikō estin* — ''it was heard that he was at home'' — reads idiomatically like word spreading that he was back at a recognized base, which suggests Mark''s audience may have pictured a quasi-permanent Capernaum ''home'' (Peter''s house functioning as such). Against that backdrop the Q logion is rhetorical hyperbole tuned to the discipleship-call setting, dramatizing cost rather than denying access to any roof. The two are compatible.

Verdict: harmonizable — the contradiction is largely a KJV translation artifact; the Greek prepositions and the Lukan parallel show ownership and hospitality are different claims.', 'Joel Marcus, Mark 1-8: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary (AB 27; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 213-218, 224-227, on Mark 2:1 and 2:15.
R. T. France, The Gospel of Mark (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 2002), pp. 119-128, on Mark 1:29 and 2:1.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV (AB 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 833-836, on Luke 9:58.
Ulrich Luz, Matthew 8-20: A Commentary, trans. James E. Crouch (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2001), pp. 19-22, on Matthew 8:20.
