Mark 2:13-14 has Jesus, teaching by the sea, summon *Leuin ton tou Alphaiou kathēmenon epi to telōnion* — ''Levi the son of Alphaeus sitting at the tax booth'' — with the terse *akolouthei moi*, ''follow me.'' Luke 5:27-28 reproduces Mark almost word for word: a tax collector *onomati Leuin*, ''named Levi,'' is called and rises to follow. Matthew 9:9 retells the identical scene — the booth, the call formula, the immediate response — but substitutes a name: *eiden anthrōpon kathēmenon epi to telōnion, Maththaion legomenon*, ''he saw a man seated at the tax booth, called Matthew.'' One narrative, two names.

The strongest harmonization — Carson, France''s NICNT, Bauckham, Blomberg — is the double-name theory. Levi (from the tribal/patronymic stock) was the man''s given name; Matthew (*Maththaios*, from Hebrew *Mattityāhū*, ''gift of YHWH'') was a second name, perhaps conferred at his call as Simon became Peter and Saul is also called Paul. Multiple names were genuinely common in first-century Jewish Palestine, where a Semitic name often coexisted with a Greek or Latin one (John Mark; Joseph Barsabbas Justus; Simon Peter/Cephas). Since the First Gospel was traditionally associated with the apostle Matthew — listed as *Maththaios ho telōnēs*, ''Matthew the tax collector,'' among the Twelve at 10:3 — it would naturally narrate the call under the apostolic name. This is a serious argument and must be granted its full force: onomastic doubling is well documented, and Mark''s own juxtaposition of ''Levi son of Alphaeus'' (2:14) with ''James son of Alphaeus'' (3:18) even raises the question of a family of Alphaeus.

The difficulty is the silence where the harmonization most needs support. There is no independent first-century attestation — none — that any individual was known by both ''Levi'' and ''Matthew.'' Each cited analogy is weaker than it looks. Simon→Peter is a renaming the Gospels narrate explicitly, as a name Jesus bestows (Mark 3:16; John 1:42); no comparable bestowal is reported for Levi. Saul/Paul involves two name-types (a Hebrew name and a Roman *cognomen*) and is repeatedly fixed to one person across Acts; the two are never presented as a renaming and are attested together. For Levi-Matthew, by contrast, no text ever equates the two names. The identification is precisely the thing in need of evidence, supplied only by the harmonizing hypothesis itself.

The redaction-critical reading (Davies-Allison; Luz; Marcus) offers a more economical account and turns on a genuine textual puzzle: Levi son of Alphaeus, dramatically called in Mark 2:14, never appears in Mark''s own list of the Twelve at 3:16-19. A figure given a full call narrative who then vanishes from the apostolic roster is anomalous. Matthew''s editor resolves the anomaly by replacing ''Levi'' with ''Matthew'' — a name that does stand in the Twelve — thereby anchoring the apostle (and the Gospel''s namesake-tradition) in a call story that Mark had attached to a non-apostle. The substitution is the kind of purposeful editorial move Matthew makes elsewhere with his Markan source, aligning tradition with the community''s apostolic claims. Fitzmyer notes the same Levi-absence problem from the Lukan side, where Levi likewise does not reappear among the Twelve.

Nothing here proves the man was not also called Matthew; people did bear two names. But the harmonization succeeds only by assuming the very identification the texts never make, while the redactional explanation accounts for both the name-change and the otherwise puzzling disappearance of Levi from the apostle lists.

Verdict: not harmonized — the two-name theory is logically possible but historically unsupported; the parsimonious reading is a Matthean redactional substitution anchoring the apostle Matthew in Mark''s call narrative.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew, Vol. 2 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1991), pp. 98-101, on Matthew 9:9.
Joel Marcus, Mark 1-8 (AB 27; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 225-227, on Mark 2:14 and the apostle list.
Ulrich Luz, Matthew 8-20: A Commentary, trans. James E. Crouch (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2001), pp. 32-36, on Matthew 9:9.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, The Gospel According to Luke I-IX (AB 28; Doubleday, 1981), pp. 588-595, on Luke 5:27-28.
Richard Bauckham, Jesus and the Eyewitnesses: The Gospels as Eyewitness Testimony, 2nd ed. (Eerdmans, 2017), pp. 108-112, on the Levi/Matthew identification.
R. T. France, The Gospel of Matthew (NICNT; Eerdmans, 2007), pp. 351-353, on Matthew 9:9.
John Nolland, The Gospel of Matthew (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 2005), pp. 384-387, on Matthew 9:9.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke', 7, 1, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The outreach strand is among the best-attested elements of the Jesus tradition. Mark 2:15-16, Matthew 9:10-11, and Luke 5:30, 7:34, 15:1-2 repeatedly show Jesus eating and drinking with *telōnai kai hamartōloi*, tax collectors and sinners, drawing Pharisaic complaint and the hostile label ''a glutton and a drunkard, a friend of tax collectors and sinners'' (Matt 11:19; Luke 7:34). Critical scholarship treats this as historically secure precisely because it satisfies the criterion of embarrassment: an early church would not invent a slur against its founder. Sanders set table fellowship with sinners at the center of Jesus''s mission; Meier analyzed the *telōnai* as a despised collaborator class; Crossan read the shared meals as enacted, boundary-dissolving ''open commensality.'' The gesture is deliberate and inclusive — outsiders are drawn in, not pushed out.

Matthew 18:15-17 points the other way. In the church-order discourse, an unrepentant offender who will not hear a private reproof, then two or three witnesses, then the assembly (*ekklēsia*), is to be treated *hōsper ho ethnikos kai ho telōnēs* — ''as the Gentile and the tax collector.'' The procedure already echoes the two-or-three-witness rule of Deuteronomy 19:15 and reads as community discipline, not evangelistic outreach.

The strongest harmonization — Blomberg, Carson — argues that ''publican'' here is cultural shorthand: Jesus (or Matthew''s Jesus) speaks to a Jewish audience for whom ''Gentile and tax collector'' simply named the social category of the outsider, so the phrase describes how such people were commonly regarded rather than prescribing contempt. Some press further: even an outsider remains an object of mission, so ''treat him as a tax collector'' could ironically mean ''keep pursuing him as Jesus pursued tax collectors.'' Stated at full strength, the appeal to idiom is not frivolous — *ethnikos* and *telōnēs* do function as a stock pairing for those outside the covenant community, and Matthew elsewhere uses *ethnikoi* this way (5:47; 6:7).

But the comparative grammar resists the softer gloss. The construction makes the *ethnikos kai telōnēs* the standard against which the offender is to be measured — ''let him be to you as...'' — that is, the model of how to regard him, namely as one now outside the community''s fellowship. The ironic ''keep pursuing him'' reading runs against the plain force of an exclusion procedure that climaxes a graduated disciplinary sequence; the point of the escalation is removal from fellowship, not renewed table-sharing. Davies-Allison and Luz read 18:15-20 as Matthean redaction reflecting the church-discipline practice of a late first-century community, retrojected into Jesus''s mouth; Luz in particular situates the passage in the developing order of the Matthean *ekklēsia*. That source judgment explains the friction: the inclusive table tradition descends from the historical Jesus, while the disciplinary rule encodes the later community''s need to manage its own boundaries.

So the two are not a flat logical contradiction — they address different objects (outsiders to be won vs. an unrepentant insider) and operate in different registers (mission vs. internal order), which is why the status here is partial harmonization. Yet the harmonization does not dissolve the tension so much as locate it: the same Gospel holds together a Jesus who scandalously welcomed publicans and a community rule that makes ''publican'' the very figure of the one you cut off. Labeling 18:17 ''cultural'' and 9:10 ''ethical'' does not erase that the one term carries opposite valences within a single document.

Verdict: partially harmonizable as differing objects and registers, but a real intra-Matthean tension between Jesus''s inclusive practice and the community''s disciplinary rule remains.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew, Vol. 2 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1991), pp. 782-787, on Matthew 18:15-17.
Ulrich Luz, Matthew 8-20: A Commentary, trans. James E. Crouch (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2001), pp. 451-455, on Matthew 18:15-20.
E. P. Sanders, Jesus and Judaism (Fortress, 1985), pp. 174-211, on Jesus and ''sinners.''
John P. Meier, A Marginal Jew: Rethinking the Historical Jesus, Vol. 3: Companions and Competitors (ABRL; Doubleday, 2001), pp. 40-73, on tax collectors.
John Dominic Crossan, The Historical Jesus: The Life of a Mediterranean Jewish Peasant (HarperSanFrancisco, 1991), pp. 261-264, on table fellowship.
