D. A. Carson, ''Matthew,'' in The Expositor''s Bible Commentary, rev. ed., Vol. 9 (Zondervan, 2010), pp. 461-463, on Matthew 18:17.', 0, NULL, 5, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke', 6, 2, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

Matthew collapses the two stages into one. By the time the ruler — unnamed in Matthew, who omits Jairus — opens his mouth, the girl is already dead: "My daughter has just now died (arti eteleutēsen), but come and lay your hand on her, and she will live" (Matt 9:18). The contradiction sits squarely in the verb. Eteleutēsen is the aorist of teleutaō, "to die," and the adverb arti ("just now," "at this moment") pins the action to the immediate past. This is a report of a completed death, not a hypothetical or a fear. The ruler is not saying "she may be dead by now"; he is announcing that she has died.

The standard apologetic harmonization (Archer; Geisler and Howe) argues that Matthew is simply summarizing or "telescoping" the longer Markan narrative, and that the ruler''s words reflect his reasonable assumption that, given the time elapsed, his daughter must by now have died. This reading is grammatically untenable. Arti eteleutēsen does not express probability or anticipation; Greek had ample resources for a future or a hypothetical (a future indicative, a conditional construction, or the adverb tacha, "perhaps"), and Matthew used none of them. The aorist of immediate completion reports an accomplished fact. A second apologetic move — that the ruler in Matthew is a different official altogether — founders on the close verbal and structural parallels and the embedded hemorrhaging-woman story common to all three, which mark this as one and the same tradition.

Critical scholarship (Davies and Allison; Luz; Hagner) reads the variation as a deliberate Matthean abbreviation serving a clear narrative and Christological purpose. By compressing the two stages, Matthew (1) eliminates the messenger interruption that complicates Mark''s flow, and (2) magnifies the miracle: in Mark and Luke the petition is for a healing of one who is dying, and only later does it become a raising of one who has died; in Matthew it is, from the outset, a request to raise the dead. Matthew thereby front-loads the resurrection motif and heightens the demonstration of Jesus'' authority over death. This is consistent with Matthew''s broader tendency to abbreviate Markan miracle stories — his version of the whole pericope is roughly a third the length of Mark''s — while sharpening their theological point.

The disagreement is real but small, and its origin is well understood: it is the predictable byproduct of a redactor compressing a two-stage source into a single scene. That such editing produces a factual discrepancy about whether the girl was alive or dead at the moment of the request is precisely the kind of evidence source critics cite for free editorial reworking of Mark by Matthew. The harmonization that the ruler merely "assumed" her death is post hoc rationalization that the Greek tense does not permit. Verdict: genuine contradiction arising from Matthean redactional compression of Mark.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison Jr., *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 2 (Matt 8-18) (ICC; T&T Clark, 1991), pp. 124-133, on Matthew''s compression of the Markan two-stage structure.
Donald A. Hagner, *Matthew 1-13* (WBC 33A; Word, 1993), pp. 246-250, on arti eteleutēsen and Matthean abbreviation.
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 8-20: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2001), pp. 38-44, on the redactional purpose of the compression.
Joel Marcus, *Mark 1-8: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AB 27; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 354-373, on Mark''s two-stage narrative and intercalation.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 333-334, representative harmonization (telescoping).
Norman L. Geisler and Thomas Howe, *When Critics Ask: A Popular Handbook on Bible Difficulties* (Victor, 1992), pp. 332-333, representative harmonization.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke', 10, 2, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Matthew tells it differently. He omits the power-transfer entirely and reverses the order. Jesus turns, sees the woman, and says, "Daughter, be of good comfort; your faith has made you whole," and only then does the narrator report, "and the woman was made whole from that hour (apo tēs hōras ekeinēs)" (Matt 9:22). In Matthew the healing follows the word; the touch alone does not cure. Where Mark and Luke have a contact-cure operating through impersonal dynamis, Matthew has a word-cure operating through the personal authority of Jesus'' speech and the woman''s faith.

The apologetic response (Blomberg; Carson) typically runs along two lines. The first holds that Matthew is merely noting that the cure was "confirmed" or "publicly ratified" by Jesus'' word, while the actual healing still occurred at the moment of contact. This is special pleading: Matthew has deliberately removed the contact-cure language (he has no euthys at the touch and no power-transfer) and placed the healing notice after the speech precisely to make the word the operative cause. The second line argues that apo tēs hōras ekeinēs is a loose idiom that does not fix a precise instant. But this founders on Matthew''s own usage. The identical formula appears at Matt 15:28 (the Canaanite woman''s daughter healed "from that very hour") and Matt 17:18 (the epileptic boy cured "from that hour"), and in both the healing demonstrably follows and results from Jesus'' authoritative utterance. Matthew uses the phrase consistently to mark a cure effected by the spoken word, which is exactly the opposite of Mark''s pre-verbal contact-cure.

Mainstream critical scholarship (Davies and Allison; Luz; Hagner; Marcus; Fitzmyer) accordingly reads the divergence as a Matthean theological edit. Matthew systematically downplays the quasi-magical, automatic transfer of power that pervades Mark''s miracle stories and reframes the cures as acts of Jesus'' sovereign word eliciting faith. This pattern is visible across Matthew''s redaction of Markan healings, and the relocation of the cure-notice in 9:22 is a small but characteristic instance of it. The result is a genuine inconsistency at the level of when and how the woman was healed: Mark and Luke have her cured the instant she touched the hem, before Jesus said anything; Matthew has her cured only after — and because of — his word. The disagreement is minor in narrative consequence but it is real and it is grounded in the source-text grammar and sequence. Verdict: genuine contradiction produced by Matthean theological redaction of Mark.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison Jr., *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 2 (Matt 8-18) (ICC; T&T Clark, 1991), pp. 128-133, on Matthew''s reframing of the cure as word-effected.
Donald A. Hagner, *Matthew 1-13* (WBC 33A; Word, 1993), pp. 248-250, on apo tēs hōras ekeinēs and Matthean redaction.
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 8-20: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2001), pp. 41-43, on the de-emphasis of contact-magic in Matthew.
Joel Marcus, *Mark 1-8: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AB 27; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 366-368, on the power-transfer (dynamis) and euthys in Mark.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke (I-IX)* (AB 28; Doubleday, 1981), pp. 743-749, on Luke''s parachrēma and dependence on Mark.
Craig L. Blomberg, *Matthew* (NAC 22; Broadman, 1992), pp. 159-161, representative harmonization.
D. A. Carson, "Matthew," in *The Expositor''s Bible Commentary*, rev. ed., Vol. 9 (Zondervan, 2010), pp. 268-271, representative harmonization.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke', 10, 2, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The second divergence dissolves under philological scrutiny and should be conceded as harmonized. "Cananaean" has nothing to do with the land of Canaan or the town of Cana; it transliterates the Aramaic qanʾanaʾ, "the zealous one," which Luke — writing for a Greek readership — simply translated as zēlōtēs. This is a translation artifact, not a conflict of tradition, and critical and traditional scholars alike accept it (Fitzmyer; Davies and Allison). It is the kind of bilingual doubling that pervades the Gospels.

The first divergence is harder. The standard apologetic move (Archer; Geisler and Howe; Carson) is to argue that Thaddaeus and Judas son of James are one man with two names, on analogy with Simon Peter / Cephas / Bar-Jonah or Levi / Matthew. The analogy is not absurd: bilingual Jews of first-century Palestine frequently bore a Semitic name and a Greek or Greco-Roman cognomen, and a disciple named Judas might well have adopted an alternative to avoid confusion with Judas Iscariot. But the proposal has a concrete weakness that critical scholarship (Meier; Bauckham) consistently presses: there is no independent attestation, in any first-century source, that Thaddaeus was ever also called Judas, nor any explanation of the double name. It is a harmonizing inference required by the discrepancy itself, not evidence that resolves it.

The textual tradition makes the situation worse rather than better. At Matt 10:3 the manuscripts cannot agree on the name. The Alexandrian witnesses (Sinaiticus, Vaticanus) read Thaddaios; Codex Bezae (D) and the Old Latin read Lebbaios; and the Byzantine majority text presents the conflated "Lebbaeus who was surnamed Thaddaeus." Metzger''s Textual Commentary explains the variants as the work of scribes already trying to bring Matthew into line with Mark and with one another — the apparatus is, in effect, a record of ancient harmonizing activity. NA28 prints Thaddaios as the earliest recoverable reading and treats the others as secondary. The KJV''s "Lebbaeus, whose surname was Thaddaeus" reflects the late conflated text, not the earliest one.

Meier''s conclusion in A Marginal Jew (vol. 3) is the most economical reading of the whole picture: the precise membership of the Twelve had already become somewhat fluid by the time the Synoptic tradition was written down, and the evangelists were preserving variant local lists. The symbolic number twelve — evoking the twelve tribes of restored Israel — was firm and historically rooted in Jesus'' ministry; the exact roster of the lesser-known members was not uniformly transmitted. John, notably, never gives a list at all, though he presupposes the Twelve (John 6:67-71). Eusebius already wrestled with the discrepancy, identifying Thaddaeus with the Thaddaeus of the Abgar legend, which only underscores how early the harmonizing impulse arose.

Verdict: partially harmonizable — the Cananaean/Zealot pair is a secure translation equivalence, but the Thaddaeus / Judas-son-of-James substitution rests on an unattested double name and is better explained as variant tradition; the textual apparatus at Matt 10:3 is the receipt of ancient harmonization in progress.', 'John P. Meier, *A Marginal Jew: Rethinking the Historical Jesus*, Vol. 3: *Companions and Competitors* (ABRL; Doubleday, 2001), pp. 130-147, 198-208, on the fluid membership of the Twelve.
Richard Bauckham, *Jesus and the Eyewitnesses: The Gospels as Eyewitness Testimony*, 2nd ed. (Eerdmans, 2017), pp. 93-113, on the lists of the Twelve and naming.
Bruce M. Metzger, *A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament*, 2nd ed. (Deutsche Bibelgesellschaft, 1994), pp. 21-22, on the Thaddaeus/Lebbaeus variants at Matt 10:3.
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison Jr., *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 2 (Matt 8-18) (ICC; T&T Clark, 1991), pp. 154-160, on the lists and the Cananaean etymology.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke (I-IX)* (AB 28; Doubleday, 1981), pp. 615-620, on zēlōtēs translating qanʾanaʾ.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 336-337, representative harmonization (one man, two names).
Norman L. Geisler and Thomas Howe, *When Critics Ask: A Popular Handbook on Bible Difficulties* (Victor, 1992), pp. 333-334, representative harmonization.
D. A. Carson, "Matthew," in *The Expositor''s Bible Commentary*, rev. ed., Vol. 9 (Zondervan, 2010), pp. 281-283, representative harmonization.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke, John, Acts', 10, 2, 3, 3, 1, 'Matt 10:3 varies among ''Thaddaeus'' (Alexandrian), ''Lebbaeus'' (Codex Bezae/Old Latin), and the conflate ''Lebbaeus called Thaddaeus'' (Byzantine), reflecting early attempts to harmonize the apostle lists.', 'Metzger, A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament, 2nd ed., at Matt 10:3 (NA28 apparatus)');

The strongest harmonization is the salvation-historical or "phased-mission" argument (Blomberg; Carson; Köstenberger): Jesus restricted the pre-resurrection mission to Israel because God''s redemptive plan unfolded in sequence — to Israel first, then, after the resurrection, to the nations — but always intended the eventual Gentile mission, foreshadowed even in Matthew (the magi of ch. 2, the centurion of 8:5-13, the Canaanite woman herself, who in the end is helped). This is a coherent theological scheme; it matches Paul''s own "to the Jew first, and also to the Greek" (Rom 1:16), and on its own terms it renders the restrictive and universal sayings compatible as two stages of one design rather than flat contradictions. To that extent the tension is genuinely, if partially, harmonizable: a single program can have a temporally bounded first phase.

Two problems keep the harmonization from being complete. First, Matt 15:24 is not phrased as a temporary measure. Jesus tells a Gentile petitioner to her face that he was "not sent" except to Israel, with no qualifier about phases or sequence; the restriction is stated as the scope of his commission, and the woman prevails only by accepting the position of a "dog" who may eat the crumbs. The saying preserves an older, sharply particularist Jewish-Christian tradition that Matthew has not smoothed over. Second, one of the universalizing prooftexts is textually compromised. Mark 16:15 belongs to the Longer Ending of Mark (16:9-20), which is absent from Codex Sinaiticus and Codex Vaticanus, unattested in the Sinaitic Syriac and the oldest Armenian and Georgian witnesses, marked with obeli or noted as doubtful in numerous later manuscripts, and unknown to Eusebius and Jerome as part of many copies. The consensus of textual criticism (Metzger; the Alands; Ehrman) is that it is a second-century scribal addition; the original Mark almost certainly ended at 16:8. Citing Mark 16:15 as Jesus'' own command is citing a verse the earliest Gospel did not contain.

The critical reconstruction (Meier; Sanders; Fredriksen) frames the whole picture historically: the Jesus movement began as a Jewish renewal movement oriented to the restoration of Israel — which is why the restrictive sayings survived — and only became a programmatic Gentile mission in the wake of Paul and the post-70 reconfiguration of the church. The evangelists then retrojected the universal mandate onto the risen Jesus to authorize a Gentile mission already underway, which is why the universalizing material clusters at the post-resurrection seams (Matt 28; the secondary Markan ending). Matthew thereby preserves both layers side by side: an older restrictive tradition embedded in the ministry, and a redactional universalism appended at the close.

Verdict: partially harmonizable as a salvation-historical sequence, but the harmonization is strained by the unqualified exclusivity of Matt 15:24 and undercut by the secondary, text-critically spurious status of Mark 16:15.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison Jr., *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 2 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1991), pp. 165-168, 549-552, and Vol. 3 (1997), pp. 676-691, on the restrictive sayings and the Great Commission.
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 21-28: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2005), pp. 622-637, on the redactional character of the Great Commission.
John P. Meier, "Salvation-History in Matthew: In Search of a Starting Point," *CBQ* 37 (1975): 203-215, on the salvation-historical structure of Matthew''s mission.
E. P. Sanders, *Jesus and Judaism* (Fortress, 1985), pp. 220-224, on Jesus'' mission as Israel-restoration.
Paula Fredriksen, *From Jesus to Christ: The Origins of the New Testament Images of Jesus*, 2nd ed. (Yale University Press, 2000), pp. 96-104, on the Gentile turn after Paul.
Bruce M. Metzger, *A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament*, 2nd ed. (Deutsche Bibelgesellschaft, 1994), pp. 102-106, on the secondary status of Mark 16:9-20.
Bart D. Ehrman, *The Orthodox Corruption of Scripture*, 2nd ed. (Oxford University Press, 2011), pp. 269-272, on the Longer Ending of Mark.
Craig L. Blomberg, *Matthew* (NAC 22; Broadman, 1992), pp. 173, 431-433, representative phased-mission harmonization.
D. A. Carson, "Matthew," in *The Expositor''s Bible Commentary*, rev. ed., Vol. 9 (Zondervan, 2010), pp. 297-299, 660-668, representative salvation-historical harmonization.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Acts, Galatians', 6, 3, 3, 1, 1, 'Mark 16:15 belongs to the Longer Ending (Mark 16:9-20), absent from Codex Sinaiticus and Vaticanus and the earliest Syriac/Armenian witnesses, and judged a secondary addition.', 'Metzger, A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament, 2nd ed., pp. 102-106 (Mark 16:9-20)');

The directionality is significant. On the two-source hypothesis, where Matthew and Luke independently agree against Mark, source critics look for a shared cause. Here that cause is most plausibly the Q mission discourse, which transmitted a stricter, more radical form of the charge reflecting the wandering-charismatic ideal of voluntary destitution current in the communities behind Q. Matthew and Luke conflated Mark''s instruction with the Q version and produced the more rigorous prohibition.

The apologetic harmonization, catalogued in Geisler and Howe and in Archer, leans on a lexical distinction in the verbs. Mark uses airō ("to take up, carry along") of the staff, whereas Matthew''s list is governed by ktaomai ("to acquire, procure") — the prohibition opens at Matthew 10:9 with mē ktēsēsthe ("do not acquire"), and the staff, sandals, and the rest in 10:10 hang off that verb as its objects. So, the argument runs, Mark permits carrying a staff one already owns while Matthew forbids procuring a new one. Give the harmonizer his due, and give him the whole list: under the ktaomai reading the ban covers the sandals exactly as it covers the staff, since hypodēmata in 10:10 is just another thing not to procure. The move is internally consistent for Matthew on its own terms — it does not split apart on the sandals the way one might assume. The strongest form of the harmonization rescues Matthew across the board.

The trouble is that it cannot touch Luke or Mark, and that is where the contradiction actually lives. Luke 9:3 does not use ktaomai at all — it uses airete (present imperative of airō, "take"), the same carry-it-along verb Mark uses, and still forbids the staff outright (mēte rhabdon). There is no "acquire versus carry" wiggle room in Luke; the disciples are told to take nothing, and Luke 10:4 adds "no shoes" to the seventy. And Mark is not describing a future acquisition either. Mark 6:9 has the disciples already shod as they leave — hypodedemenous sandalia, a perfect middle participle of hypodeō (hypodeomai) describing their settled state of being bound into their sandals at the moment of departure. Set Mark beside Luke and the verb-distinction evaporates: Mark sends them out wearing sandals and carrying a staff; Luke sends them out (airō) forbidden to take either. That is a flat contradiction in departure conditions, and no "having versus acquiring" distinction reaches it, because both Mark and Luke are talking about what the missionaries leave with, not what they procure on the road.

Critical scholarship (Davies and Allison; Marcus; Fitzmyer; Hagner; Luz) reads the divergence as redactional intensification. Mark''s relatively practical provision — a staff for the road and sandals for the feet, but no money, bread, or spare tunic — is sharpened by Matthew and Luke into a more austere image of the missionary, stripped even of the traveler''s basic gear and wholly dependent on his hosts. The radicalization fits the prophetic-charismatic self-understanding of the communities that preserved the Q form of the charge. The airō/ktaomai distinction may save Matthew on its own terms, but that internal rescue buys the harmonizer nothing, because the contradiction is between Gospels, not inside one. Verdict: genuine contradiction arising from redactional intensification of Mark via Q — locatable squarely in the Mark–Luke clash, which the verb-distinction cannot reach.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison Jr., *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 2 (Matt 8-18) (ICC; T&T Clark, 1991), pp. 168-180, on the mission charge and the staff/sandal variation.
Joel Marcus, *Mark 1-8: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AB 27; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 388-391, on hypodedemenous sandalia and Mark''s provision.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke (I-IX)* (AB 28; Doubleday, 1981), pp. 751-755, on Luke''s mission charge and the Q form.
Donald A. Hagner, *Matthew 1-13* (WBC 33A; Word, 1993), pp. 269-273, on Matthew''s intensification of the charge.
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 8-20: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2001), pp. 73-78, on redactional radicalization of the mission discourse.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 327-328, representative harmonization (airō vs ktaomai).
Norman L. Geisler and Thomas Howe, *When Critics Ask: A Popular Handbook on Bible Difficulties* (Victor, 1992), pp. 350-351, representative harmonization.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke', 10, 2, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard apologetic harmonization (Carson, Blomberg, Grudem) argues that the contradiction dissolves once the blasphemy is understood as a settled, lifelong refusal to recognize the Spirit''s work: it is unforgivable not because forgiveness is intrinsically withheld but because the blasphemer, by hardened disposition, never repents and so never seeks it. This reading does have textual traction in Mark''s qualifier, *enochos estin aiōniou hamartēmatos* — "is guilty of an eternal sin" — where the adjective *aiōnios* modifying *hamartēma* can be read as describing an enduring state or condition rather than a single discrete act. On this view the epistolary promises (forgiveness for "whosoever believeth," Acts 10:43; cleansing from "all unrighteousness," 1 John 1:9) remain open to anyone who in fact turns, and the unforgivable sinner is simply the one who definitionally never does.

The difficulty is that this requires reading the saying against its own grammar, particularly in Matthew. Matthew 12:32 frames the matter as a categorical denial of forgiveness across both eschatological ages: blasphemy against the Spirit "shall not be forgiven him, neither in this world, neither in the world to come" (*oute en toutō tō aiōni oute en tō mellonti*). The two-age formula (*hā-ʿōlām ha-zeh / hā-ʿōlām ha-bāʾ* in its Hebrew equivalent) is precisely the apocalyptic idiom for "never, under any circumstances, at any point in salvation history." It denies forgiveness in the present age and in the age to come — that is, it forecloses the very avenue the harmonization needs to leave open. Mark''s *ouk echei aphesin eis ton aiōna* ("never has forgiveness") and the threat of liability to "eternal" judgment point the same direction: a sin permanently outside the reach of pardon, not merely a sin its perpetrator happens never to repent of.

The more economical conclusion is that the Synoptic Jesus operated within a first-century Jewish apocalyptic framework in which certain sins were genuinely irremediable. This is not an isolated stance. The Qumran Community Rule (1QS 7:22-25) prescribes permanent expulsion without return for one who betrays the community''s truth, and *m. Sanhedrin* 10:1 lists categories of Israelites who "have no share in the world to come." Against that backdrop, an unforgivable sin is unsurprising; it reflects the period''s category of the apostate or the one who curses the divine power itself. The later epistolary tradition — Acts'' Pauline speeches, Colossians, 1 John — developed a fully universal forgiveness-through-faith soteriology in which Christ''s death covers "all trespasses" without remainder. Both theologies are canonical and both are coherent within their own horizons; they simply do not describe the same economy of forgiveness. The functional/never-repents harmonization smooths the surface but cannot absorb the two-age formula, which is the crux of the saying. Verdict: genuine theological tension between an apocalyptic judgment-saying and a later universalizing soteriology; only partially harmonizable, and not at the level of Matthew''s explicit grammar.', 'Joel Marcus, *Mark 1-8: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AB 27; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 282-285, on Mark 3:28-29 and the ''eternal sin'' clause
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison Jr., *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 2 (Matt 8-18) (ICC; T&T Clark, 1991), pp. 344-348, on Matthew 12:31-32
M. Eugene Boring, *The Continuing Voice of Jesus: Christian Prophecy and the Gospel Tradition* (Westminster John Knox, 1991), pp. 165-167, on Luke 12:10
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke (X-XXIV)* (AB 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 962-965, on Luke 12:10
Darrell L. Bock, *Blasphemy and Exaltation in Judaism: The Charge against Jesus in Mark 14:53-65* (WUNT 2/106; Mohr Siebeck, 1998), on first-century blasphemy categories relevant to Mark 3:29
D. A. Carson, "Matthew," in *The Expositor''s Bible Commentary*, rev. ed., Vol. 9 (Zondervan, 2010), pp. 343-347, on Matthew 12:31-32 (representative harmonization)
Craig L. Blomberg, *Matthew* (NAC 22; Broadman, 1992), pp. 202-205, on Matthew 12:31-32
Wayne Grudem, *Systematic Theology: An Introduction to Biblical Doctrine* (Zondervan, 1994), pp. 506-509, on the unpardonable sin', 0, NULL, 4, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke, Acts, Colossians, Titus, 1 John', 5, 3, 3, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The contrast with the Synoptic prison-question is sharp. The Matthew/Luke episode derives from Q (the wording is closely parallel), which makes it an early and independently attested tradition. The question is direct: *sy ei ho erchomenos ē heteron prosdokōmen* — "Are you the coming one, or are we to expect another?" The phrase "the coming one" (*ho erchomenos*) is itself an eschatological title, so John is asking precisely whether Jesus is the awaited figure — the very identification the Johannine Baptist has already made categorically. Historical-Jesus scholarship (Meier, Webb) treats the prison-question as strong material under the criterion of embarrassment: the early church would have had no motive to invent a story in which its herald-prophet doubts Jesus, which argues for its authenticity and against the Johannine portrait being historical.

The apologetic harmonization (Carson, Köstenberger, Blomberg) runs through two principal moves. First, that John in prison was discouraged rather than ignorant — he knew who Jesus was but, facing execution under Herod Antipas and puzzled that Jesus'' ministry did not match the fiery, axe-to-the-root judgment he had preached (Matt 3:10-12), sought reassurance. Second, that the question was posed for the benefit of John''s own disciples, to redirect them to Jesus, rather than reflecting John''s own doubt. The discouragement reading has real psychological plausibility and is not impossible; prophets elsewhere falter (Elijah under the broom tree, 1 Kings 19). But both moves require importing motivations the text does not supply. Matthew explicitly attributes the question to John himself — *pempsas dia tōn mathētōn autou eipen autō* ("sending word by his disciples he said to him") — and frames it as triggered by John hearing "the works of Christ," i.e., by the content of Jesus'' ministry, not by a pedagogical concern for his followers. The question is not phrased as a check-in or a discipleship exercise; it is a request for identification.

The more economical reading, and the one Brown''s commentary settles on, is that the Synoptic and Johannine traditions preserve incompatible portraits. The Synoptic Baptist is historically plausible: a prophet who recognized Jesus'' importance, baptized him, and later — from prison, with the kingdom not unfolding as he had announced — wondered whether Jesus was after all the eschatological figure. The Johannine Baptist is a theological construction whose only function is to identify the Son of God, with every trace of human ambiguity edited out for christological purposes. This is a redaction-critical conclusion, but it accounts for what the texts actually say better than welding the unwavering Johannine witness onto the doubting Synoptic prophet. Verdict: genuine contradiction rooted in the Fourth Evangelist''s redactional reshaping of the Baptist.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (I-XII)* (AB 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 55-71, on John 1:29-34
John P. Meier, *A Marginal Jew: Rethinking the Historical Jesus*, Vol. 2: *Mentor, Message, and Miracles* (ABRL; Doubleday, 1994), pp. 130-137, on Matthew 11:2-3
