The Johannine denial points the other way. In John 1:21 the Jerusalem delegation asks the Baptist directly, "Art thou Elijah?" and he answers flatly, *ouk eimi* — "I am not." He then denies being "the prophet" and identifies himself only as the voice of Isaiah 40:3. In the Fourth Gospel John exists exclusively to bear witness to Jesus; an independent identification of John as the eschatological Elijah would give him a salvation-historical role of his own and compete with the single-witness christology the Evangelist is constructing.

The standard apologetic harmonization (Carson, Köstenberger, Blomberg, Morris) is the "two senses" move. John is Elijah in a *functional* sense — the prophet who fulfills the Elijah-role of forerunner, coming "in the spirit and power of Elijah" (Luke 1:17) — but not in a *literal* sense; he is not the prophet Elijah personally returned. On this reading the Jerusalem delegation asks the literal question (are you the historical Elijah come back?) and John truthfully answers no, while Jesus speaks of the functional fulfillment. This is the cleanest available harmonization, and it has genuine support: Luke 1:17 is explicitly functional language, and Second Temple Judaism did not generally conceive of Elijah''s return as reincarnation but as the literal reappearance of the prophet who had been taken up alive (2 Kings 2:11) and was expected to come back bodily. So a Baptist who denied being the bodily-returned Elijah would not thereby deny being his typological fulfillment.

The harmonization succeeds at the level of authorial intent but does not dissolve the surface contradiction, because the Synoptic Jesus does not in fact restrict himself to functional language. In Matthew 11:14 he says *autos estin Ēlias ho mellōn erchesthai* — "he is Elijah who is about to come" — which deploys exactly the eschatological-return phrasing of Malachi''s prophecy (the participle *ho erchomenos / mellōn erchesthai* is the technical idiom for the expected coming figure). The Johannine delegation asks within the same eschatological category Jesus is affirming. They are operating with the same question, and they receive opposite answers: Jesus says John is the Elijah-to-come; John says he is not. The mainstream critical reading (Brown, Meier, Davies-Allison, and at length Öhler) is that the Synoptic tradition preserves an early Christian identification of John as Elijah-returned, while the Fourth Gospel actively suppresses it because by its time the church needed John subordinated to Jesus rather than standing as a parallel eschatological prophet. This is a redaction-critical conclusion, but it accounts for the divergence more economically than the functional/literal split, which has to assume the two parties are answering different questions when the wording shows they are answering the same one. Verdict: genuine contradiction; the functional/literal harmonization captures the writers'' intentions but cannot absorb Matthew''s eschatological-return language matching the Johannine question.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (I-XII)* (AB 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 46-50, on John 1:21
John P. Meier, *A Marginal Jew: Rethinking the Historical Jesus*, Vol. 2: *Mentor, Message, and Miracles* (ABRL; Doubleday, 1994), pp. 134-137, on Matthew 11:14
