The harmonization runs into trouble at three points. First, Mark''s wording is categorical. Mark 8:12 reads *ei dothēsetai tē genea tautē sēmeion* — literally "if a sign shall be given to this generation" — which is a Semitic oath-formula (the protasis of a self-imprecatory oath, "may I be cursed if…") functioning as an emphatic negative: absolutely no sign will be given. There is no exception clause in Mark at all. Second, Matthew (12:39; 16:4) and Luke (11:29) add the Jonah exception, which most critical scholars (Marcus, Davies-Allison, Boring) read as editorial softening: Matthew, uncomfortable with his source''s flat refusal, supplies a typological qualifier (the sign of Jonah, interpreted in Matt 12:40 as the resurrection-after-three-days). The very existence of three positions in the canon — many signs, no sign, one sign — is itself the fingerprint of redactional development rather than a single coherent teaching.

Third and most seriously, the Fourth Gospel does not merely report miracles; it builds its entire structure around *sēmeia* as the primary mode of authentication. John organizes Jesus'' public ministry around a series of signs (the "Book of Signs," John 1-12), beginning at Cana, which John 2:11 calls the first of his signs "and manifested his glory; and his disciples believed on him." John 20:30-31 states the purpose explicitly: "many other signs truly did Jesus… but these are written, that ye might believe." John 12:37 laments that "though he had done so many signs (*tosauta sēmeia*) before them, yet they believed not." For John, public signs are precisely the means by which Jesus authenticates his mission and elicits faith — the opposite of the Markan refusal to give a sign to validate himself. The two traditions are operating with incompatible theologies of sign and self-attestation.

The distinction between *dynameis* and a heavenly *sēmeion* genuinely defuses the surface tension between Mark''s refusal and the bare fact that Jesus worked miracles. What it cannot defuse is the conceptual clash between the Synoptic Jesus, who refuses signs as legitimating proof, and the Johannine Jesus, whose signs are the legitimating proof. The economical reading on which critical scholarship converges is developmental: Mark preserves an older tradition of a Jesus who refused signs-on-demand; Matthew softens the refusal with the Jonah qualifier; John constructs a counter-tradition in which signs are the central authenticating mode. Verdict: partly harmonizable at the level of vocabulary (heavenly sign vs. mighty work), but a genuine theological tension remains between the Synoptic refusal and the Johannine sign-christology.', 'Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AYB 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 504-509, on Mark 8:12
