Matthew softens the offense in two ways. First, he expands the singular Markan "mystery" into the plural "mysteries (μυστήρια) of the kingdom of heaven" (Matt 13:11). Second, and more significantly, where Mark writes the purpose conjunction ἵνα ("in order that"), Matthew substitutes ὅτι ("because"): "I speak to them in parables because (ὅτι) seeing they do not see" (Matt 13:13). The grammatical move converts Mark''s causal/telic statement — parables cause non-comprehension — into a descriptive one: parables respond to a non-comprehension that already exists. Matthew then quotes Isaiah in full as a fulfillment formula (13:14-15), tracking the LXX more closely. Luke compresses the whole thing (8:10), keeping ἵνα but dropping the explicit "lest they be forgiven." Davies and Allison document this softening trajectory in detail. Yet beneath the editorial smoothing, all three Synoptics retain the same two-tier structure: an esoteric circle that receives private explanation and an outer crowd that receives only riddling speech. Mark even notes that Jesus "explained everything privately to his own disciples" (4:34), making concealed instruction a structural feature of the ministry.

The Johannine Jesus says the opposite. Interrogated by Annas "about his disciples and his teaching" (περὶ τῆς διδαχῆς αὐτοῦ), Jesus answers: "I have spoken openly (παρρησίᾳ) to the world … and in secret I have said nothing" (ἐν κρυπτῷ ἐλάλησα οὐδέν, John 18:20). The strongest harmonization — advanced by Carson, Köstenberger, and Blomberg — observes that this is a legal deposition. The high priest''s question probes whether Jesus headed a clandestine, conspiratorial movement; Jesus'' reply, on this reading, denies *seditious secrecy*, not pedagogical selectivity. There is real contextual traction: παρρησίᾳ in forensic settings connotes the candor of one with nothing to hide from authorities, and the antithesis "synagogue and temple" names public venues, not the content of every saying. So construed, the verse rebuts a charge of conspiracy and does not address the question of graded instruction.

The difficulty is the sweep of Jesus'' own words. ἐν κρυπτῷ ἐλάλησα οὐδέν is a universal negative — "nothing have I spoken in secret" — not the narrower "I led no secret cabal." The Synoptic Jesus, by contrast, explicitly reserves the interpretation of the mysteries for the Twelve and frames parables as veiling devices for outsiders. Critical scholars (Brown, Marcus, Räisänen) therefore read John 18:20 as a Johannine theological construction: in the Fourth Gospel the incarnate Logos is, by definition, self-disclosure, so a hidden or esoteric Jesus is christologically impossible. Notably, John contains no parables and no "messianic secret" — the very motif Mark builds his Gospel around. The later reception confirms the stakes: the Gospel of Thomas (logion 62) and other gnostic texts cite the secret-teaching tradition as warrant for hidden gospels, precisely the trajectory John resists. The two portraits — Mark''s concealing Jesus and John''s wholly transparent Jesus — reflect divergent theologies that resist synthesis without privileging one text''s voice over the other.

Verdict: genuine contradiction, rooted in divergent redactional theologies; the legal-context harmonization narrows but does not dissolve the universal force of John 18:20.', 'Joel Marcus, *Mark 1-8: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AB 27; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 296-308, on the parable-theory and the Targumic background of the Isaiah 6 citation in Mark 4:10-12
