The contents of the Gospels — Matthew''s own contents above all — flatly resist this. Matthew is structured around five great discourses, and most of their material is not parabolic. The Sermon on the Mount (Matt 5-7), the missionary charge (Matt 10), the woes against the scribes and Pharisees (Matt 23), and the eschatological discourse (Matt 24-25) are delivered to crowds and consist overwhelmingly of beatitudes, commands, prophetic denunciations, and apocalyptic prediction. Matthew thus appears to contradict Matthew within the span of a few chapters.

The strongest harmonization — argued by France, and followed by Hagner, Carson, and Blomberg — restricts the absolute claim to its immediate literary frame. On this reading Mark 4:33-34 is a closing rubric for the parable-discourse: it describes how Jesus taught *on that occasion* and during the parable-intensive Galilean phase, not across his whole ministry. France notes that summary statements in Mark routinely function as section boundaries (cf. 1:39, 3:7-12), so a localized reading is grammatically and structurally plausible. The qualifier "to them" (αὐτοῖς), referring to the crowds rather than to all hearers, further narrows the scope: private and disciple-directed teaching is explicitly distinguished in the same verse ("privately he explained everything"). So construed, the summary distinguishes Jesus'' public, veiled mode from his private, plain mode, which is exactly the distinction the parable-theory of 4:11-12 sets up.

This harmonization carries real weight for the Galilean crowds, and it is the most defensible reading of Mark in isolation. Its limits are twofold. First, the imperfect ἐλάλει resists collapse into a single day''s activity; it most naturally describes Jesus'' customary manner with the crowds, which is why Matthew felt free to universalize it with οὐδέν and a prophetic proof-text. Critical commentators (Marcus, Davies-Allison, Hooker) read 4:33-34 as a redactional generalization that overstates the case to round off the collected-parable chapter — a literary flourish, not an ethnographic report of Jesus'' speech habits. Second, and decisively, the absolute claim collapses entirely once the canon is read whole: across twenty-one chapters the Johannine Jesus tells not a single parable in the Synoptic sense. John offers extended monologues, the seven "I am" sayings, and figurative discourses (the vine, the shepherd, John 10 and 15), which John himself labels παροιμίαι ("figures of speech," John 16:25, 29) and explicitly contrasts with plain speech — but none is a Synoptic-type narrative parable. If "without a parable he did not speak" were a universal claim about Jesus, John''s Gospel would be inexplicable.

The net judgment: the saying is best understood as a redactional summary keyed to the parable chapter and to Jesus'' public Galilean teaching, where it is broadly accurate. Pressed into an absolute claim — as Matthew''s wording invites and as a flat reading takes it — it is contradicted by the non-parabolic discourses elsewhere in the Synoptics and shattered by the parable-free Gospel of John.

Verdict: overstated as an absolute; harmonizable within Mark''s immediate context, but the Matthean universalizing and the Johannine evidence expose it as a redactional generalization rather than a literal description.', 'R. T. France, *The Gospel of Mark* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 2002), pp. 220-222, on Mark 4:33-34 as a section-closing summary
