The standard harmonization (Bock most fully, with Marshall and Blomberg) posits two distinct rejections: an early one (Luke 4) and a later one (Mark 6 / Matt 13). Jesus, a son of Nazareth, plausibly returned home more than once, and the differing details — a scroll-reading and a cliff in Luke, a Sabbath teaching and amazement at his "mighty works" in Mark — could mark two separate occasions. Bock notes verbal differences (Luke''s ἐν τῇ πατρίδι phrasing, the absence of the carpenter saying in Luke) as evidence against simple identity. This is textually possible and cannot be ruled out a priori.

Three considerations weigh against it. First, parsimony: positing two near-identical hometown rejections to preserve all three sequences multiplies events without independent warrant, the classic move that historical method treats with suspicion. The core data — same town, same astonishment at his ordinary origins, same proverb-like response ("no prophet is accepted in his hometown," which both Luke 4:24 and Mark 6:4 preserve in nearly identical wording) — point to one tradition, not two. The shared proverb is especially telling: it is unlikely that the identical aphorism attached to two separate, independently transmitted rejections.

Second, Luke''s composition shows unmistakable signs of editorial design. Critical scholarship (Fitzmyer, Bovon, Meier) reads Luke 4:16-30 as a deliberately constructed overture — a "Nazareth manifesto" — that announces the themes of the whole Gospel: Spirit-anointing, good news to the poor, fulfillment "today," and rejection by Israel issuing in a turn to outsiders. The Elijah-Elisha citation (1 Kgs 17, 2 Kgs 5), in which the prophets bless a Sidonian widow and a Syrian leper rather than Israelites, is a signature Lukan motif of Gentile inclusion, planted here as a preview of Acts. This front-loading is consistent with Luke''s documented technique elsewhere — the programmatic infancy canticles (Magnificat, Benedictus, Nunc Dimittis) and the thematically loaded opening of Acts all show the same compositional instinct.

Third, the very details that make Luke distinctive are the ones most marked as Lukan theology, which undercuts treating them as a neutral second event. The most economical explanation is that Luke took the single Markan Nazareth pericope, relocated it to the threshold of the ministry, and expanded it into a thematic prologue. The harmonization survives only if one disregards the redactional fingerprints throughout Luke 4 and accepts a narratively redundant doublet.

Verdict: genuine chronological contradiction best explained by Lukan relocation and expansion for programmatic purposes; the two-rejections harmonization is logically possible but improbable and unparsimonious.', 'Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke (I-IX)* (AB 28; Doubleday, 1981), pp. 526-540, on the Nazareth manifesto in Luke 4:16-30
François Bovon, *Luke 1: A Commentary on the Gospel of Luke 1:1-9:50* (Hermeneia; trans. Christine M. Thomas; Fortress, 2002), pp. 148-157, on Luke 4:16-30
