Luke 9:7-9 rewrites the scene. Luke retains the same menu of popular theories but changes Herod''s own posture: "Herod was perplexed (διηπόρει), because it was said by some that John had been raised … And Herod said, ''John I beheaded; but who is this about whom I hear such things?'' And he sought to see him." Luke''s Herod does not adopt the John-redivivus theory; he reports it as something "said by some" and then distances himself from it with a question. The verb διαπορέω denotes genuine bewilderment, not settled conviction. Where Mark and Matthew give a haunted certainty, Luke gives open-ended curiosity.

The harmonization (Bock, Marshall, Blomberg) reads the difference as psychological development: Herod first wavered among competing theories — exactly the perplexity Luke records — and only later, after further reflection, settled on the Baptist identification recorded in Mark and Matthew. On this reading the two reports capture different moments of the same evolving deliberation, and the texts are sequential rather than contradictory. This is coherent at the level of human psychology, and it cannot be excluded.

The difficulty is that all three reports are tied to the same narrative trigger — Herod''s reaction on first hearing of Jesus'' miracles — and so describe the same moment, not two stages of reflection. Mark and Matthew present the John identification as Herod''s conclusion at that point; Luke presents an explicit refusal to conclude at the same point. Critical scholarship (Marcus, Fitzmyer, Bovon) reads this as characteristic Lukan editing. Luke has a specific narrative investment in a curious-but-uncommitted Herod: only Luke includes the saying that "Herod … sought to see" Jesus (9:9), and only Luke supplies the Passion-week scene in which Antipas, having long wished to see Jesus and hoping for a sign, examines and mocks him before returning him to Pilate (Luke 23:6-12). The perplexed, inquisitive Herod of 9:9 is set up precisely to pay off in chapter 23; a Herod already convinced that Jesus was the resurrected Baptist would not fit that later portrait. Luke thus softens Mark''s guilt-ridden certainty into curiosity to serve his distinctive Herod material.

Historically, Harold Hoehner''s study of Antipas notes that the tetrarch''s interest in popular prophetic figures and his political wariness are plausible against the Herodian background, but the historical data do not adjudicate which evangelist''s portrait of his inner conclusion is accurate — they only show that either is conceivable for a ruler in his position. The contradiction operates at the level of "what Herod concluded" rather than "what Herod knew": all three agree the John-redivivus rumor reached him; they disagree on whether he embraced it. Mark/Matthew say yes; Luke says he withheld judgment. That is a real, if modest, contradiction, and it is most economically explained as Lukan redaction shaping Herod for the trial scene to come.

Verdict: genuine but minor contradiction at the level of Herod''s conclusion, best explained by Lukan redaction; the developmental harmonization is psychologically possible but strained against the shared narrative trigger.', 'Joel Marcus, *Mark 1-8: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AB 27; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 391-401, on Mark 6:14-16
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke (I-IX)* (AB 28; Doubleday, 1981), pp. 757-761, on Luke 9:7-9
