The redaction-critical background sharpens the point. Matthew 16:17-19 is unique to Matthew; the underlying confession derives from Mark 8:29, where Peter simply says "Thou art the Christ" and Jesus, far from blessing him, immediately enjoins silence and shortly after rebukes him as "Satan" (Mark 8:30, 8:33). Matthew has expanded the bare Markan confession into a beatitude grounded in special revelation, adding both the "Son of the living God" predicate and the flesh-and-blood/heavenly-Father antithesis. The Matthean expansion is therefore the very material that generates the collision with John, and it is precisely the layer most plausibly attributed to Matthean theology of Peter''s primacy (cf. Davies-Allison).

The standard harmonization (Carson, Köstenberger, Blomberg) runs through two complementary moves. The first is the two-stage reading: Andrew gave Peter a preliminary, second-hand identification in John 1, whereas Matthew 16 records a later, fuller, personally-mediated revelation that surpasses what Andrew could supply — note the added "Son of the living God," which goes well beyond Andrew''s "Messias." The second is the distinct-occasions reading: John records Peter''s first encounter, the Synoptics a separate climactic moment. Both moves deserve a fair hearing. It is genuinely true that the Matthean confession adds a christological predicate Andrew never voiced, and it is uncontroversial that John 1 and Matthew 16 narrate temporally distinct scenes.

The difficulty is that neither move engages what Matthew 16:17 actually asserts. Jesus does not say "the *fullness* of this confession was revealed by my Father, even though Andrew told you the basics." He says the revelation as such did not come from flesh and blood. If Peter had first learned the messianic identification from his brother, then a human being — Andrew — did in fact reveal to Peter that Jesus is the Messiah, and Jesus'' antithesis becomes, at minimum, misleading. The two-stage reading rescues the predicate "Son of the living God" but cannot rescue the core term *Christos*, which is exactly the word on Andrew''s lips. The distinct-occasions reading fares no better, since the contradiction is not about *when* Peter recognized Jesus but about *how* he first came to the messianic identification at all.

Critical scholarship (Brown, Meier, Davies-Allison) accordingly treats this as a structural divergence between two incompatible models of disciple-recognition. John front-loads full christological insight into chapter 1 — Andrew, Philip, and Nathanael all confess Jesus'' identity within hours of meeting him — as part of the Fourth Gospel''s realized, revelatory program in which Jesus'' glory is manifest from the outset. The Synoptics, by contrast, distribute recognition gradually across the ministry and stage its climax at Caesarea Philippi, a structure tied to the Markan messianic-secret motif. Brown notes that these two architectures cannot be reconciled at the historical level because they serve opposing theological agendas. The point is not that John and Matthew disagree about a date; it is that Matthew''s deliberate exclusion of human informants directly negates John''s report of one. Verdict: genuine contradiction.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (I-XII)* (AB 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 77-79, on Johannine front-loading of christological recognition in John 1:40-41
John P. Meier, *A Marginal Jew: Rethinking the Historical Jesus*, Vol. 3: *Companions and Competitors* (ABRL; Doubleday, 2001), pp. 221-245, on the historical Peter and the recognition traditions
