R. T. France, *The Gospel of Mark* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 2002), pp. 328-331, on the disciples'' incomprehension', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke, John', 7, 2, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The harmonization (Marshall, Bock, Carson, Geisler-Howe) rests on two solid philological observations. First, the qualifier *hōsei* ("about, approximately") explicitly flags Luke''s figure as a round estimate rather than a precise interval. An approximation of "about eight" is not in arithmetic conflict with a precise "six" if the counting bases differ. Second, and decisively, Greco-Roman authors frequently counted inclusively, reckoning both terminal days within a span. On an inclusive count, one brackets the day of the saying and the day of the transfiguration around the six full days that fall between them: 1 (the saying) + 6 (intervening) + 1 (the event) yields the "about eight" Luke gives, while Matthew and Mark count only the six intervening days exclusively. Luke elsewhere shows a tendency toward inclusive reckoning — most familiarly in the "third day" resurrection schema, where a Friday-evening burial to a Sunday-morning rising is counted as three days by including both endpoints. The same author who counts Friday-to-Sunday as "three days" can readily count a six-day gap as "about eight."

A second possibility, raised by Fitzmyer, is that Luke rounded toward the number eight for theological reasons: the eighth day carried resonances in early Christianity as the day of new creation and resurrection (cf. the later patristic ogdoad symbolism, and Luke''s own interest in eight-day intervals, e.g. the circumcision and naming sequences in Luke 1-2). On this reading Luke is not contradicting the six-day figure so much as gently restating it within a numerically suggestive frame, with *hōsei* signaling that precision is not the point. Bovon canvasses both the inclusive-counting and the theological-rounding explanations without finding them mutually exclusive.

Critical scholarship is therefore not exercised by this case. Fitzmyer, Bovon, and Marcus treat the divergence as a function of counting convention and Lukan stylistic preference rather than as a substantive disagreement about chronology. There is no competing tradition claiming the transfiguration happened on some materially different occasion; all three Gospels place it at the same point in the narrative, the same number of days after the same saying, with only the counting base differing. This is the kind of item that an honest critical reading should classify as a defensible harmonization rather than a genuine contradiction, since the inclusive/exclusive distinction is a real and well-documented feature of Hellenistic Greek and Luke demonstrably employs it elsewhere. The surface arithmetic differs; the underlying claim does not. Verdict: harmonizable — a counting-convention artifact, not a contradiction.', 'Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke (I-IX)* (AB 28; Doubleday, 1981), pp. 797-805, on Luke 9:28 and the eighth-day rounding
François Bovon, *Luke 1: A Commentary on the Gospel of Luke 1:1-9:50* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2002), pp. 369-381, on Luke 9:28
Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AYB 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 625-642, on Mark 9:2
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison Jr., *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 2 (Matt 8-18) (ICC; T&T Clark, 1991), pp. 685-706, on Matthew 17:1
I. Howard Marshall, *The Gospel of Luke: A Commentary on the Greek Text* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 1978), pp. 382-384, on inclusive counting in Luke 9:28
Darrell L. Bock, *Luke 1:1-9:50* (BECNT 3A; Baker, 1994), pp. 866-868, on the transfiguration day count
Norman L. Geisler and Thomas Howe, *When Critics Ask: A Popular Handbook on Bible Difficulties* (Victor, 1992), pp. 358-359, on the six/eight day count
D. A. Carson, "Matthew," in *The Expositor''s Bible Commentary*, rev. ed., Vol. 9 (Zondervan, 2010), pp. 423-425, on Matthew 17:1 and Luke 9:28', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke', 2, 2, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The lexical evidence makes the conflict difficult to soften. Mark uses *ēgnooun to rhēma* (imperfect of *agnoeō*), "they kept failing to know the saying." *Agnoeō* denotes genuine ignorance or non-comprehension, not partial or hesitant grasp. Luke reinforces this with *ēn parakekalymmenon ap'' autōn* — the saying "had been veiled from them," using the perfect passive participle of *parakalyptō*, a strong image of something deliberately covered over — and adds the purpose clause *hina mē aisthōntai auto*, "so that they should not perceive it." Matthew''s *elypēthēsan sphodra* points in precisely the opposite direction: the disciples received the message clearly enough to mourn.

The harmonization (Carson, Blomberg, Hagner) proposes a partial-comprehension state: the disciples understood enough to grieve (the prediction of death) but not enough to dispel their confusion (its meaning and the resurrection sequel), so that Matthew reports the affective surface and Mark/Luke the cognitive deficit. As psychology this is coherent — people regularly grieve a frightening message they do not fully understand — and it deserves a fair hearing, since the three texts are describing one event and a composite human reaction is not implausible in the abstract. The weakness is that the harmonization must override what the Greek of Mark and Luke actually says. *Agnoeō* and *parakekalymmenon* do not describe partial understanding shadowed by grief; they describe a saying that did not register at all, deliberately veiled. Matthew''s text, conversely, contains no trace of incomprehension to balance the grief. The composite picture exists only when one imports each evangelist''s distinctive note into the others'' silence — which is exactly the move redaction criticism warns against.

The better-attested explanation is directional editorial change. Matthew systematically rehabilitates the disciples relative to his Markan source: where Mark presents them as obtuse, frightened, and hard-hearted, Matthew softens or removes the censure, presenting the Twelve as a comprehending and responsive inner circle who address Jesus as *kyrie* and even worship him. Davies-Allison document this tendency across the Gospel, and it is precisely what one sees here — the Markan and Lukan incomprehension scrubbed and replaced with sober grief. Joseph Tyson''s classic study established the blindness/incomprehension of the disciples as a deliberate Markan redactional theme, not incidental reportage; Matthew, finding that theme theologically uncongenial, edits it out. Luke moves in the opposite direction, retaining and even heightening the Markan veiling, possibly to set up the later "opening" of the disciples'' understanding after the resurrection (Luke 24:45).

The partial-comprehension harmonization thus papers over a surface conflict while ignoring the documented vector of the editorial work. Matthew and Mark/Luke do not preserve complementary facets of one observation; Matthew has altered the source in a direction consistent with his broader portrait of the Twelve. Verdict: genuine contradiction, produced by Matthean redaction of Mark.', 'Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AYB 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 661-672, on Mark 9:30-32 and the disciples'' incomprehension
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison Jr., *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 2 (Matt 8-18) (ICC; T&T Clark, 1991), pp. 738-744, on Matthew 17:22-23 and the disciple-competence pattern
