Craig L. Blomberg, *Matthew* (NAC 22; Broadman, 1992), pp. 269-270, on Matthew 17:22-23
Joseph B. Tyson, "The Blindness of the Disciples in Mark," *JBL* 80 (1961): 261-268, on disciple-incomprehension as a Markan redactional theme', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke', 10, 2, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

Paul''s target is different. In 1 Cor 13:11 he uses *nēpios* — "infant," properly one not yet able to speak (*nē-* + *epos*) — as a developmental stage left behind: "when I was a child (*nēpios*) I spake as a child... but when I became a man (*anēr*), I put away childish things." In 1 Cor 14:20 he sharpens it with deliberate precision: "be not children (*paidia*) in understanding (*tais phresin*)... in understanding be men (*teleioi*)," while granting that in *malice* one should be infantile (*nēpiazete*). Ephesians 4:14 extends the same figure: mature believers should be "no more children (*nēpioi*), tossed to and fro... with every wind of doctrine." Conzelmann treats the *nēpios* of 1 Cor 13:11 as straightforwardly negative — the figure of incomplete knowledge set against adult *teleios* completeness — and Thiselton and Lincoln read the Pauline texts the same way.

The harmonization, then, is not a strained dodge but a recognition that the two authors metaphorize childhood for opposite features: Jesus for the child''s dependent lowliness, Paul for the child''s cognitive immaturity. A single literal entity (a small child) supplies two metaphorical tenors, and there is no contradiction in commending one feature while deprecating another. Crucially, 1 Cor 14:20 shows Paul himself making exactly this differentiated judgment in a single breath — childlike in malice, adult in understanding — which demonstrates that the distinction is native to the texts and not imposed by harmonists.

The one genuine pressure point, which the harmonization must address rather than dodge, is Matt 11:25 (par. Luke 10:21), where Jesus thanks the Father for having "hid these things from the wise and prudent, and revealed them unto babes" — and the word is *nēpioi*, the very term Paul deprecates. Here the Synoptic tradition does valorize a kind of cognitive infancy: revelation goes to the unlearned rather than the clever. Bertram''s TDNT entry traces this positive religious use of *nēpios* (dependence on God, receptivity) alongside its negative developmental use. So the lexical overlap the contradiction alleges is real at one node — but even there the referents differ: Matt 11:25 praises receptivity to divine revelation over self-sufficient worldly wisdom, whereas Paul deprecates doctrinal instability and gullibility within the church. These are compatible: one can be humbly receptive to God (Jesus'' *nēpioi*) while being doctrinally mature and stable (Paul''s adults).

The whole material is paraenesis — practical exhortation working through metaphor — not systematic anthropology, and metaphors are selected for the feature the speaker needs. Reading them as competing literal verdicts on "whether being a child is good" mistakes the genre. The tension is real at the level of vocabulary and is worth noting, but it dissolves once the distinct metaphorical referents are specified. Verdict: largely overstated; an equivocation on "childish" resolved by the texts'' own differentiation between childlike dependence and childish ignorance.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison Jr., *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 2 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1991), pp. 755-761, on Matthew 18:3
Hans Conzelmann, *1 Corinthians* (Hermeneia; trans. James W. Leitch; Fortress, 1975), pp. 226-227, on nēpios in 1 Corinthians 13:11
