The lexical point matters but does not dissolve the problem. The shepherd saying uses kalos (noble, fitting, beautiful), while the Synoptic exchange uses agathos (morally good), so one might argue the two words are not strictly the same claim. Yet the conceptual issue stands: in the Synoptics Jesus distinguishes himself from the one who alone is good, namely God, whereas in John he assumes a divine self-designation — the shepherd of Israel is YHWH''s own role in Ezekiel 34 and Psalm 23, and John 10:30 immediately glosses the discourse with ''I and the Father are one.''

The strongest apologetic harmonization, advanced by D. A. Carson in the Expositor''s Bible Commentary and by Blomberg, reads Mark 10:18 not as a denial but as a Socratic probe: Jesus is inviting the ruler to follow the logic of his own address — if you rightly call me good, recognize what that implies about my identity. On this reading Jesus never disclaims goodness; he presses the man toward a Christological inference. This is exegetically possible and theologically coherent, and it should not be dismissed as a strawman.

The difficulty is that it must read Mark through John. Mark''s plain wording distinguishes Jesus from God, and the most economical reading of ''there is none good but one'' is precisely a distinction, not a riddle. The textual history reinforces this. Matthew 19:17 in the earliest and best manuscripts (Vaticanus, Sinaiticus) reads ''Why do you ask me about the good?'' (ti me erōtas peri tou agathou) — Matthew has rewritten the question so that goodness attaches to the commandments rather than to a contrast between Jesus and God. The KJV''s ''Why callest thou me good?'' at Matthew reflects later scribal harmonization back toward Mark and Luke (the variant is well documented in NA28''s apparatus). Bart Ehrman, in The Orthodox Corruption of Scripture, treats Matthew''s rewording as a deliberate softening of a saying that, in Mark''s form, could embarrass a higher Christology — exactly the kind of dogmatically motivated alteration that recurs in the transmission of Christologically sensitive texts.

Placed on a developmental axis, the data line up: Mark (earliest, lower Christology) preserves a saying that separates Jesus from God; Matthew (later) edits the wording to remove the separation; John (latest, highest Christology) has Jesus openly predicating kalos of himself and claiming functional unity with the Father. Raymond Brown''s Anchor Bible John reads the shepherd discourse as a mature theological reflection, not a transcript of Galilean teaching. The harmonization succeeds only by importing John''s Christology into Mark''s mouth; critical scholarship instead reads the four Gospels as successive stages in the church''s articulation of who Jesus was. Verdict: harmonizable at the level of systematic theology, but the texts as written reflect a developmental difference in Christology rather than a single self-consistent claim.', 'Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Yale Bible 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 721-724, on Mark 10:18
Bart D. Ehrman, *The Orthodox Corruption of Scripture: The Effect of Early Christological Controversies on the Text of the New Testament*, 2nd ed. (Oxford University Press, 2011), pp. 95-99, on Matt 19:17
