The Greek of Mark 6:5 is the sharpest datum. Mark writes ouk edynato ekei poiēsai oudemian dynamin — ''he was not able to do any mighty work there.'' This is a statement of incapacity, not of choice. That Mark intends genuine inability is confirmed by Matthew''s reaction: editing his source, Matthew changes it to ouk epoiēsen ekei dynameis pollas — ''he did not do many mighty works there'' (Matt 13:58). The shift from ''could not'' to ''did not'' is precisely the dogmatic softening a later evangelist with a higher Christology would make. Joel Marcus and R. T. France both flag this as one of the clearest cases of Matthean theological correction of Mark.

The classical apologetic harmonization is kenotic, drawn from Philippians 2:6-8 (Christ ''emptied himself,'' ekenōsen) and systematized by writers from Charles Hodge to Wayne Grudem: in the incarnation the Son voluntarily refrained from exercising the full prerogatives of deity, exercising power only as the Father directed, and reclaimed their full public exercise at the resurrection (hence Matt 28:18''s ''is given,'' edothē, as an exaltation formula). On this reading Mark 6:5 describes a self-imposed restraint conditioned by unbelief, and Matt 20:23 reflects the Son''s submission within the economic Trinity. This is a coherent and venerable framework, and it should be presented at full strength.

Two problems blunt it. First, Mark''s ouk edynato resists the kenotic gloss: ''could not'' is not the natural vocabulary of voluntary restraint, which is exactly why Matthew rewrote it. Kenosis explains Matt 13:58 better than it explains Mark 6:5, and the direction of editing shows the early church found Mark''s wording problematic. Second, Matt 20:23 concerns an eschatological, heavenly prerogative — the allocation of thrones in the kingdom — not a limitation of Jesus'' earthly human nature, which is what kenosis is built to explain. If, as John 10:30 has it, the Son and the Father are functionally one, the deferral of so heavenly a decision to the Father sits awkwardly with the unitive Christology of the Fourth Gospel. Bart Ehrman''s How Jesus Became God lays out the developmental trajectory in which Jesus'' authority is progressively exalted across the sources, while Larry Hurtado, arguing for early high devotion, shows the diversity of the data the harmonization must integrate. The most economical reading is that Mark, Matthew, and John operate with different Christologies, and the canon preserves all three without flattening them into a single doctrine of power. Verdict: harmonizable by kenotic theology at the systematic level, but Mark''s ''was not able'' and the heavenly nature of the Matt 20:23 prerogative show the texts themselves reflect divergent, developing Christologies.', 'Joel Marcus, *Mark 1-8: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Bible 27; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 374-380, on Mark 6:5
R. T. France, *The Gospel of Mark* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 2002), pp. 240-244, on Mark 6:5
Bart D. Ehrman, *How Jesus Became God: The Exaltation of a Jewish Preacher from Galilee* (HarperOne, 2014), pp. 235-269, on Matt 28:18
Larry W. Hurtado, *Lord Jesus Christ: Devotion to Jesus in Earliest Christianity* (Eerdmans, 2003), pp. 259-347
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 3 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 92-94, on Matt 20:23
Donald A. Hagner, *Matthew 14-28* (WBC 33B; Word Books, 1995), pp. 580-583, 884-886, on Matt 20:23 and 28:18
Wayne Grudem, *Systematic Theology: An Introduction to Biblical Doctrine* (Zondervan, 1994), pp. 549-552, on kenosis', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, John', 5, 3, 3, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The Synoptic logion is steeped in Isaiah 53. The Suffering Servant ''bare the sin of many'' (rabbim, Isa 53:12) and ''shall... justify many'' (53:11), and Mark 10:45''s combination of vicarious death and ''the many'' is widely read as a deliberate echo. This supplies the strongest harmonizing argument, classically advanced by Joachim Jeremias in The Eucharistic Words of Jesus: in Hebrew and Aramaic ha-rabbim (''the many'') functions inclusively, denoting ''the great multitude,'' i.e. all, rather than ''many as opposed to all'' in the exclusive Greek sense. On this reading ''ransom for many'' and ''ransom for all'' are not two different scopes but the same scope expressed in Semitic and Hellenistic idiom. The Qumran community even used ha-rabbim as a self-designation for the whole congregation, supporting the inclusive force. This is the steel-man for harmony, and it is a serious philological case.

The theological stakes are why it matters. Reformed exegetes such as John Owen in The Death of Death in the Death of Christ press Mark 10:45''s pollōn to support a definite, limited atonement, reading the Pastoral pantōn as ''all kinds'' or ''all the elect.'' Arminian and Wesleyan readers run the inverse, taking the Synoptic ''many'' as the inclusive Semitism Jeremias described. The same two words are thus marshaled in opposite directions.

The harmonization carries real weight but does not fully dissolve the tension. Even granting Jeremias the Semitic inclusive sense of the underlying tradition, the Greek of Mark and Matthew reads pollōn, the ordinary Greek word that can imply ''many but not all,'' while the author of 1 Timothy chose antilytron hyper pantōn — adding the intensive prefix anti- and substituting the unambiguous pantōn. That substitution is itself evidence that an early Christian writer perceived the Synoptic formulation as susceptible to a restrictive reading and moved to forestall it by stating universality outright. The direction of development runs toward explicit universalism. On the mainstream critical view 1 Timothy is deutero-Pauline, composed late in the first century or early in the second (so Raymond Brown''s Introduction to the New Testament, with the Pastorals'' distinctive vocabulary, ecclesiology, and church order); on that view the rewording reflects a later theological clarification. Even if one holds to Pauline authorship, the choice of pantōn over pollōn remains a deliberate authorial decision.

So the underlying soteriology can plausibly be harmonized — both texts may intend a universally sufficient ransom — but the surface wording diverges in a way that has been theologically consequential. The contradiction is real at the level of the Greek text even where it softens at the level of intended meaning. Verdict: largely harmonizable via the Isaianic rabbim background, but the lexical shift from pollōn to pantōn is a genuine and theologically loaded difference, not a mere stylistic variant.', 'Joachim Jeremias, *The Eucharistic Words of Jesus*, trans. Norman Perrin (SCM, 1966), pp. 178-184, 226-231, on pollōn as inclusive Semitism, with reference to Mark 10:45
Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Yale Bible 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 749-758, on Mark 10:45
