The controlling datum is Markan priority, the near-consensus position that Mark was the earliest written Gospel and that Matthew and Luke independently used him as a source. On that model the three pericopes are one tradition redacted three ways, and the editorial fingerprints are visible. Matthew''s pluralization is not isolated. He doubles characters elsewhere: two Gadarene demoniacs (Matt 8:28) against Mark''s single man (Mark 5:2); two blind men at Matt 9:27, a pericope many regard as a Matthean doublet of this very Jericho story; and two animals at the triumphal entry (Matt 21:7) against Mark''s one (Mark 11:7). Davies and Allison treat this as a settled Matthean stylistic habit rather than independent eyewitness data — a recurring tendency to render Markan singulars as pairs, possibly to satisfy the Deuteronomic two-witness rule of Deut 19:15. The simplest account of Matthew''s ''two'' is therefore redactional, not historical.

Luke''s reversal of direction is the harder editorial move to explain, and Fitzmyer offers the standard solution: Luke needed Jericho itself as narrative real estate for the Zacchaeus episode (Luke 19:1-10), which occurs *in* the city, so the roadside healing was shifted to the approach to clear the stage. This is a plausible compositional motive and shows that the directional discrepancy is not a copying error but a structural choice.

The classical harmonization descends from Augustine''s *De Consensu Evangelistarum* II.65, which posited two distinct healings — one on the way in (Luke), one on the way out (Mark/Matthew) — with Matthew''s pair explained either as a conflation of both encounters or as Bartimaeus plus an unnamed companion whom Mark and Luke declined to mention. Archer and Blomberg defend versions of this. Steel-manned, the argument is not absurd: an itinerant healer who attracted crowds would plausibly have encountered blind beggars more than once, and Jericho sat on a major pilgrim route where the sick congregated at the gates. The case has internal logic.

It nonetheless founders on parsimony. The harmonization must invent healings the texts never narrate and treat the verbal overlap — the same Davidic appeal (''Son of David, have mercy''), the same crowd rebuke, the same restoration of sight, the same setting — as the chance recurrence of a near-identical script. To preserve inerrancy the reading multiplies miracles, postulating two or three almost indistinguishable incidents converging on one town with the same dialogue. Marcus and Fitzmyer both read the material instead as a single underlying tradition diversely redacted, which explains the shared diction *and* the differences with one mechanism rather than three coincidences. The ''companion'' variant is weaker still: it explains Matthew''s two but not why Mark and Luke would suppress a second healed man in a story whose whole point is the restoration of sight. The directional contradiction is also untouched by the companion theory, since it concerns Luke''s placement, not the count. Verdict: a single tradition redacted three ways — genuine contradiction in count and direction, not plausibly harmonized.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew* (ICC, vol. 3; T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 102-108, on Matthew''s doubling pattern
Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AYB 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 759-772, on the Bartimaeus tradition
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (AB 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1213-1217, on the directional reversal and the Zacchaeus motive
