Raymond E. Brown, *An Introduction to the New Testament* (ABRL; Doubleday, 1997), pp. 109-125, on the Synoptic problem', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke', 1, 1, 3, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard apologetic resolution multiplies the event: Jesus cleansed the temple twice, once early (John) and once late (Synoptics). Carson, Morris, and Köstenberger all defend this, and on paper it dissolves the chronology cleanly. Its strongest support is that John''s account carries vivid, independent detail absent from the Synoptics — the φραγέλλιον ἐκ σχοινίων, the ''whip of cords,'' and the explicit driving out of the oxen and sheep, plus the dialogue about destroying and raising ''this temple in three days'' (John 2:19). This argues that John drew on a tradition not derived from Mark, which is itself widely accepted.

But independence of source proves only that John had his own tradition, not that the event recurred. The decisive objection is the trigger function the action serves in the Synoptic plot. Mark 11:18 states that immediately after the cleansing ''the scribes and chief priests heard it and sought how they might destroy him''; the temple act is the proximate cause of the arrest. E. P. Sanders made this the historical lynchpin of his reconstruction: a public, symbolic assault on the temple economy — which Sanders reads as a prophetic enactment of the temple''s coming destruction rather than a ''cleansing'' of corruption — is precisely the provocation that brought the authorities down on Jesus within days. If Jesus had staged the identical demonstration two or three years earlier, the question is why it produced no consequence the first time and a death sentence the second. The two-cleansings hypothesis must hold that the most inflammatory act of Jesus'' public life was performed twice but only proved fatal once, which strains the very causal logic the Synoptics build.

The critical consensus is that John relocated a single event for theological purposes. Placing it at the start lets the Fourth Evangelist front-load the resurrection saying (''in three days I will raise it up,'' which the narrator glosses as ''the temple of his body,'' John 2:21) and announce his programmatic theme: Jesus supersedes the temple as the locus of God''s presence — a theme developed in the Samaritan-woman discourse, where worship is no longer tied to Jerusalem or Gerizim but is ''in spirit and in truth'' (John 4:21-24). Brown lays out the chronological problem and the Johannine motive; Schneiders and Thompson read the placement as redaction rather than a historical doublet. The fourth-Gospel author characteristically reorders material for theological architecture rather than annal-keeping. Verdict: one event placed differently — genuine chronological contradiction, with John''s relocation the best explanation, not plausibly harmonized by doubling the incident.', 'E. P. Sanders, *Jesus and Judaism* (Fortress, 1985), pp. 61-76, 301-305, on the temple action as the cause of the arrest
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (I-XII)* (AB 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 114-125, on John 2:13-22 and the chronological problem
Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AYB 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 778-790, on the Markan temple cleansing
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (AB 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1259-1265, on Luke 19:45-46
Sandra M. Schneiders, *Written That You May Believe: Encountering Jesus in the Fourth Gospel*, rev. ed. (Crossroad, 2003), pp. 130-146, on the Johannine relocation
Marianne Meye Thompson, *John: A Commentary* (NTL; Westminster John Knox, 2015), pp. 71-79, on the temple action as Johannine redaction
Andreas J. Köstenberger, *John* (BECNT; Baker Academic, 2004), pp. 105-114, two-cleansings view
D. A. Carson, *The Gospel According to John* (PNTC; Eerdmans, 1991), pp. 176-180, two-cleansings defense
Leon Morris, *The Gospel According to John*, rev. ed. (NICNT; Eerdmans, 1995), pp. 167-179, two-cleansings defense', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke, John', 2, 2, 4, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Matthew alone supplies the explicit fulfillment citation of Zechariah 9:9 (Matt 21:4-5): ''Tell ye the daughter of Sion, Behold, thy King cometh unto thee, meek, and sitting upon an ass, and a colt the foal of an ass.'' The Hebrew of Zech 9:9 reads ʿal-ḥămôr wĕʿal-ʿayir ben-ʾătōnôt — ''on a donkey, on a colt, the foal of a she-donkey.'' This is textbook synonymous parallelism: the second line restates the first, naming one animal in two ways, exactly as Hebrew poetry routinely doubles a single referent (cf. the waw of explication, ''even a colt''). Zechariah envisions one donkey. The LXX preserves the two nouns (ἐπὶ ὑποζύγιον καὶ πῶλον) without flagging the parallelism, and Matthew — citing a form close to the LXX/MT — appears to have read the two terms as two beasts and reshaped his narrative to match, producing a mother and her colt and the awkward plural ''on them.'' Davies and Allison state it flatly: Matthew has misunderstood the Hebrew parallelism. Marcus calls it over-literal exegesis. This is consistent with Matthew''s broader fulfillment-citation technique, where narrative details are at times generated to satisfy the cited prophecy.

The strongest harmonizing rescue is R. T. France''s: the mother was led along to settle the unbroken colt — a genuine animal-handling reality, since a colt ''whereon never man sat'' (Mark 11:2) would be calmer beside its dam. France, sympathetic to Matthew''s reliability, concedes the parallelism point but argues the second donkey could be historical even if Matthew''s prophetic framing is over-literal. The note is plausible, but Matthew never says this; the text presents the second animal as prophecy-fulfillment, not herd management, and Matthew''s ''sat upon them'' implies the evangelist pictures both as mounts, not one as an escort. The alternative apologetic line — that Mark and Luke ''simplified'' two animals to one — collapses on chronology: under Markan priority Mark and Luke are *earlier*, so Matthew is not being simplified by them; he is complicating their single colt.

The most economical reading therefore credits the Synoptic tradition with one animal, locates the doubling in Matthean redaction driven by the Zechariah citation, and treats John as a separate strand that bypasses the disciple-dispatch tradition entirely. On the historical question — how many animals did Jesus ride — the tension largely dissolves: three of four Gospels say one, and Matthew''s second beast is the residue of a citation technique, not independent testimony. What does not dissolve is the discrepancy at the level of the *narrated* text: Matthew''s two against the others'' one is a real difference, even if its cause is exegetical rather than evidentiary. Verdict: harmonizable at the level of the event (one donkey), with Matthew''s narrative count an artifact of over-literal prophecy fulfillment.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew* (ICC, vol. 3; T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 112-125, on the misread Hebrew parallelism
Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AYB 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 772-778, on over-literal exegesis
R. T. France, *The Gospel of Matthew* (NICNT; Eerdmans, 2007), pp. 775-780, mother-donkey rescue attempt
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (AB 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1242-1252, on Luke''s single-colt account
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (I-XII)* (AB 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 459-463, on John''s entry tradition
Donald A. Hagner, *Matthew 14-28* (WBC 33B; Word Books, 1995), pp. 594-599, on the two animals and the Zechariah citation
Craig L. Blomberg, *Matthew* (NAC 22; Broadman, 1992), pp. 311-313, harmonizing treatment', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke, John', 10, 2, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The Markan arrangement is a deliberate literary device — intercalation, or the ''Markan sandwich,'' in which one episode is split to frame another so the two interpret each other. Marcus and France both treat this as one of the clearest examples in the Gospel: the cursed, leafy-but-fruitless fig tree (A1) frames the temple cleansing (B), completed by the withered tree (A2), so that the temple is read as the barren tree under judgment for bearing leaves but no fruit. This is why Mark adds the otherwise puzzling note ''for the time of figs was not yet'' (Mark 11:13). Taken as botany the remark makes Jesus'' curse seem capricious; taken as symbol it is the point — the temple, like the tree, is out of season and fruitless, and judgment falls regardless. The seemingly contradictory detail is load-bearing for Mark''s frame, and Telford''s monograph established the cursing-and-cleansing complex as an enacted parable of the temple''s destruction.

Matthew, working from Mark under the standard two-source model, does not reproduce the intercalation. He collapses Mark''s two-day, three-scene structure into a single continuous fig-tree episode placed *after* the temple action, with the withering instantaneous. Davies and Allison read this as Matthean simplification: the evangelist trades Mark''s literary architecture for narrative economy and dramatic immediacy.

The apologetic harmonization, advanced by Carson and Blomberg, is that Matthew narrates thematically — grouping the fig-tree material into one block — while Mark narrates chronologically, so the divergence is one of arrangement, not error. As a description of ancient compositional convention this is correct and even illuminating: Greco-Roman and Jewish writers routinely arranged material topically, and Matthew''s grouping habit is well attested. But the defense concedes precisely what the question asks. If Matthew arranges thematically and Mark chronologically, then the two accounts do not report the same temporal sequence, and at most one preserves the order in which the events actually occurred. The harmonization rescues the authors'' integrity as ancient writers while surrendering the claim that both texts are chronological transcripts. Steel-manned to its strongest form — that thematic rearrangement was an honored technique requiring no apology — the argument still lands on the same conclusion: the Gospels shape chronology for purpose. Verdict: harmonizable as a difference of literary arrangement, not factual error — but only by granting that Matthew and Mark do not both report the historical sequence.', 'Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AYB 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 778-790, on the Markan intercalation
R. T. France, *The Gospel of Mark* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 2002), pp. 436-452, on the fig tree framing the temple
