Albert Schweitzer made 10:23 the pivot of his reconstruction in *The Quest of the Historical Jesus*: Jesus expected the Parousia imminently, within the very mission he was launching, and the prediction failed. This ''consistent eschatology'' remains the dominant reading in critical scholarship. Dale Allison''s *Jesus of Nazareth: Millenarian Prophet* and E. P. Sanders'' *The Historical Figure of Jesus* both regard 10:23 as authentic dominical material with a falsified time horizon, and they invoke the criterion of embarrassment: the church would not have manufactured a deadline it then watched lapse. Bart Ehrman''s *Jesus: Apocalyptic Prophet of the New Millennium* presents the same case, treating 10:23 as a flagship instance of Jesus'' apocalyptic miscalculation. Matt 24:14, meanwhile, reflects a later horizon. A mission to ''all the nations'' presupposes the Gentile outreach that opened only after Easter and the Pauline expansion, and the phrase coheres with the universalizing Great Commission of 28:19. Davies and Allison accordingly suggest the two verses preserve distinct traditions Matthew has set side by side: an older Israel-mission saying in 10:23 and a post-resurrection universal-mission saying in 24:14.

The apologetic harmonizations fall along two main lines. The first, reaching back to Calvin and revived by D. A. Carson in the Expositor''s Bible Commentary, takes ''the coming of the Son of Man'' in 10:23 to denote not the Parousia but the judgment of Jerusalem in 70 CE: Jesus ''came'' in the destruction of the temple, an event well within the disciples'' lifetimes. This typological reading is internally consistent and draws on Danielic ''coming'' imagery (Dan 7:13), but it must import a referent the immediate context never supplies; in chapter 10 the ''coming of the Son of Man'' is mentioned without any allusion to the temple, and Matthew elsewhere reserves the full apocalyptic vision of the Son of Man''s coming on the clouds (24:30) for a cosmic event, not a localized siege. The second line is the partial-preterist move defended in a measured form by R. T. France: 10:23 has been partly fulfilled and remains in progress. But this stumbles on the syntax. The verse does not predict an ongoing mission; it sets a terminus, asserting the disciples will *not have finished* the towns of Israel before the coming. That is a deadline, not a trajectory, and ''partial fulfillment'' cannot accommodate a sentence whose force lies in its claim of incompletion.

Attempts to read 24:14 down to fit 10:23 fare no better, since ''all the nations'' resists compression into a single generation''s circuit of Jewish towns. The cleanest account is the source-critical one: Matthew has juxtaposed an early imminent-mission logion with a later global-mission logion, and the two assume different timetables for the same end. Either Jesus expected the imminent end and the prediction failed, or ''the coming of the Son of Man'' silently changes referent between the two passages in a way the text never signals. Verdict: genuine contradiction reflecting layered eschatological tradition.', 'Albert Schweitzer, *The Quest of the Historical Jesus*, ed. John Bowden (Fortress, 2001), pp. 327-336, on Matt 10:23
Dale C. Allison, *Jesus of Nazareth: Millenarian Prophet* (Fortress, 1998), pp. 95-171
E. P. Sanders, *The Historical Figure of Jesus* (Penguin, 1993), pp. 169-188
Bart D. Ehrman, *Jesus: Apocalyptic Prophet of the New Millennium* (Oxford University Press, 1999), pp. 125-139
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew* (ICC, vols. 2-3; T&T Clark, 1991-1997), vol. 2 pp. 186-192 (on Matt 10:23), vol. 3 pp. 343-348 (on Matt 24:14)
R. T. France, *The Gospel of Matthew* (NICNT; Eerdmans, 2007), pp. 393-398, 911-914
