Critical scholarship reads the friction as the residue of the gap between the historical Jesus'' authentic apocalyptic preaching, which presupposed imminent divine intervention, and the early church''s *Naherwartung* crisis once the first generation began to die without the Parousia. The crisis surfaces explicitly in 2 Pet 3:4, where scoffers ask, ''Where is the promise of his coming? For since the fathers fell asleep, all things continue as they were,'' a question that would have no force unless an imminent return had in fact been expected and disappointed. Dale Allison and E. P. Sanders treat the generation sayings as among the most secure dominical material precisely because of their embarrassment value: a community would not invent predictions whose deadlines it then watched expire. C. H. Dodd''s ''realized eschatology'' attempted to dissolve the temporal problem by reading the kingdom sayings as announcements of a present, non-temporal reality already breaking in; but most critical scholars after Allison have returned to a consistent-eschatology framework in which the future, datable expectation is irreducible.

The apologetic harmonizations parallel those advanced for the previous contradiction. Carson and France reinterpret the ''coming of the Son of Man'' in 10:23 as the judgment of Jerusalem in 70 CE, an event within the generation. The most sophisticated form of the move is N. T. Wright''s in *Jesus and the Victory of God*, and it deserves a careful hearing. Wright argues that ''the coming of the Son of Man'' draws on Daniel 7, where the ''one like a son of man'' *comes* to the Ancient of Days, not to earth; the imagery is one of vindication and enthronement, not of a descent through the clouds. On this reading Jesus predicted his own vindication and the collapse of the temple-based order within a generation, and that prediction was fulfilled in 70 CE; the ''coming'' was metaphorical-political, the establishment of God''s reign through the events of the first century. This is the strongest available reconciliation, and it correctly observes that Daniel''s ''coming'' is directional toward God''s throne.

Yet Wright''s reading struggles with the specific wording of the generation sayings. Matt 16:28 promises that ''some standing here will not taste death'' until they see the kingdom''s arrival, a phrasing that singles out individual survivors and so implies a discrete, observable event within their lifespans rather than a diffuse metaphorical vindication. Matt 24:30, moreover, describes the Son of Man ''coming on the clouds of heaven with power and great glory'' while ''all the tribes of the earth mourn,'' language that reads far more naturally as a visible advent than as a siege of Jerusalem. And the metaphorical-vindication reading cannot easily absorb 24:14''s explicit precondition of a completed global mission, which presupposes precisely the extended interval the generation sayings exclude. The cleanest account remains that the historical Jesus expected an imminent, datable consummation and that the surviving sayings preserve that expectation, while later strata such as 24:14 accommodate the delay. Either the timetable was imminent and disappointed, or it was re-symbolized in a manner the early church, judging by 2 Peter, never recognized. Verdict: genuine contradiction rooted in the delay of the Parousia.', 'Dale C. Allison, *Jesus of Nazareth: Millenarian Prophet* (Fortress, 1998), pp. 95-171
E. P. Sanders, *The Historical Figure of Jesus* (Penguin, 1993), pp. 169-188
Bart D. Ehrman, *Jesus: Apocalyptic Prophet of the New Millennium* (Oxford University Press, 1999), pp. 125-162
N. T. Wright, *Jesus and the Victory of God* (Fortress, 1996), pp. 339-368, 510-519, on the vindication reading
