The Synoptic and apocalyptic side of the ledger is strikingly uniform. All three Synoptic discourses describe the Son of Man ''coming on/in the clouds with power and great glory,'' echoing Daniel 7:13, and Matthew adds that ''all the tribes of the earth shall mourn.'' Revelation 1:7 fuses Daniel 7:13 with Zechariah 12:10 (''they shall look on him whom they pierced'') to declare ''every eye shall see him.'' David Aune''s WBC treatment of Rev 1:7 documents this composite citation and its emphasis on universal visibility. Joel Marcus''s AYB commentary on Mark 13:26 situates the cloud-coming firmly in the apocalyptic register of public, cosmic disclosure. There is no internal tension within this stream; the difficulty, if any, lies between it and the Fourth Gospel.

That deeper tension is real and worth naming, even though this particular verse does not carry it. John''s Gospel is far less invested in a universally visible Parousia than the Synoptics. Its realized eschatology is pronounced: ''the hour is coming, and now is'' (John 5:25), the judgment is already enacted in the response to Jesus (3:18), and the ''coming'' of John 14:18 (''I will not leave you orphans; I will come to you'') and 14:23 (the Father and Son will ''make our home'' with the believer) push the eschaton inward and into the present. Rudolf Bultmann argued that John so thoroughly internalized eschatology that the future cosmic Parousia became essentially redundant, and he attributed the surviving future-oriented passages (5:28-29; 6:39-40) to an ''ecclesiastical redactor.'' C. H. Dodd, in *The Interpretation of the Fourth Gospel*, likewise emphasized the dominance of present realization, while James Dunn and most subsequent scholars have moderated Bultmann by insisting that genuine future-eschatological elements remain integral to John rather than secondary intrusions. The scholarly consensus is thus that John holds present and future in tension, with the center of gravity tilted toward present realization, whereas Mark 13 and its parallels foreground the future visible advent.

The apologetic harmonization of the cited verses is therefore not a strained rescue but the plain reading: John 14:19 speaks of the post-crucifixion interim and never touches the Parousia, so it cannot contradict texts about the Parousia. The framing of this contradiction pivots on a verse that critical and conservative commentators alike read as referring to the interim, and the stronger comparison would set Johannine realized eschatology (e.g., John 14:18-23) against Synoptic apocalyptic (e.g., Mark 13:26). On the proof-texts as cited, the tension dissolves. Verdict: harmonizable as framed; the underlying Johannine-vs-Synoptic eschatological tension is real but is not what this verse pairing establishes.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (XIII-XXI)* (AB 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 645-652, on John 14:19
Andreas J. Köstenberger, *John* (BECNT; Baker Academic, 2004), pp. 437-440, on John 14:19
Rudolf Bultmann, *The Gospel of John: A Commentary*, trans. G. R. Beasley-Murray et al. (Westminster, 1971), pp. 257-262, 617-623
C. H. Dodd, *The Interpretation of the Fourth Gospel* (Cambridge University Press, 1953), pp. 395-423
James D. G. Dunn, *Christianity in the Making, Volume 2: Beginning from Jerusalem* (Eerdmans, 2009), pp. 169-179
David E. Aune, *Revelation 1-5* (WBC 52A; Word Books, 1997), pp. 50-58, on Rev 1:7
Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AYB 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 906-912, on Mark 13:26', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke, John, Revelation', 10, 3, 3, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

On Markan priority, the consensus solution to the Synoptic Problem, Mark is the source Matthew and Luke are using, so the natural inference is editorial. Faced with Mark''s distinctive double-crow detail and the matching narrative payoff, Matthew and Luke smoothed the prediction to the simpler single-crow form, eliminating an awkward specificity. Joel Marcus in the AYB Mark and R. T. France in the NIGTC both read the divergence as Matthean and Lukan simplification of a more cumbersome Markan tradition; John, independent of the Synoptics here, carries the same single-crow form that circulated more broadly. Raymond Brown''s *The Death of the Messiah* surveys the whole complex and reaches the same judgment.

There is also a text-critical layer. The word ''twice'' (dis) in Mark 14:30 and ''a second time'' in 14:72, along with the first crow at 14:68, are unstable in the manuscript tradition: the original hand of Codex Sinaiticus and several other witnesses omit elements of the double-crow scheme, and Codex Vaticanus is among those that lack the first crow at 14:68. Bart Ehrman, in *The Orthodox Corruption of Scripture* and related text-critical work, argues that this instability is best explained as scribal harmonization, copyists assimilating Mark to the simpler form found in the other three Gospels by deleting the second crow. The editors of NA28 nonetheless retain ''twice'' and ''a second time'' as the most probable original Markan readings, treating the omissions as secondary harmonizations rather than the reverse. If that judgment is correct, Mark wrote ''twice,'' and the tendency of the tradition was to erase the doubling, not to add it, which strengthens the case that the single-crow form is the simplified one.

The standard apologetic harmonization, advanced by Gleason Archer and by Geisler and Howe, is that Jesus said both: Mark preserves the fuller, more precise saying, and the other three give a true but abbreviated version, since a denial completed before the second crow is also, trivially, completed before ''the cock crows.'' This is logically valid; the single-crow statements are not false on a both-utterances scenario, merely less detailed. It deserves to be taken seriously as a reconciliation of the numbers. The difficulty is that it inverts the most likely direction of literary dependence. The parallel structure and verbal overlap among the accounts indicate a copying relationship, and on Markan priority Matthew and Luke had Mark''s ''twice'' in front of them and did not reproduce it, which is far more economically described as omission of a known detail than as independent recourse to a separate, shorter original. A milder steel-man holds that the saying circulated in variable oral forms, with and without the doubling, so that each evangelist used whatever version reached him; this is plausible for John, who is plausibly independent, but less so for Matthew and Luke, whose dependence on Mark is well established. The numerical contradiction is thus reconcilable at the level of what Jesus might have said, but at the level of how the Gospels were composed it is most simply explained as redactional simplification of Mark. Verdict: harmonizable as to fact, but most economically explained as editorial smoothing of Mark on Markan priority.', 'Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AYB 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 977-983, 1029-1035, on Mark 14:30 and 14:72
R. T. France, *The Gospel of Mark* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 2002), pp. 575-578, 616-621
Bart D. Ehrman, *The Orthodox Corruption of Scripture*, 2nd ed. (Oxford University Press, 2011), pp. 246-249, on the Markan cock-crow variants
