Norman L. Geisler and Thomas Howe, *When Critics Ask* (Victor, 1992), pp. 372-373', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke, John', 10, 2, 3, 2, 1, 'The word ''twice'' (dis) in Mark 14:30/14:72 and the first cockcrow in 14:68 are omitted by some witnesses (e.g. Sinaiticus, Vaticanus partially), a likely scribal harmonization to the other gospels; NA28 retains ''twice'' as original.', 'Metzger, A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament, 2nd ed., on Mark 14:30 and 14:72');

John has no Gethsemane scene whatsoever. The entire ''troubled soul'' moment is compressed into John 12:27, set not in a garden on the night of arrest but in public during the final week: ''Now is my soul troubled (*tetaraktai*); and what shall I say? Father, save me from this hour: but for this cause came I unto this hour.'' The petition for deliverance is raised only to be discarded in the same breath. Raymond Brown''s *Death of the Messiah* offers the definitive analysis: John has deliberately reworked the agony tradition because Johannine Christology cannot tolerate a Jesus who genuinely seeks escape. The Fourth Gospel''s Jesus lays down his life of his own authority — ''no man taketh it from me, but I lay it down of myself'' (John 10:18) — and in the arrest scene he, not his captors, controls events. C. H. Dodd traced the way John transmuted the inherited agony material into a moment of sovereign resolve; Bultmann and Käsemann read the omission as theological editing, Käsemann famously arguing that John''s Jesus ''strides over the earth as a god.''

The strongest harmonizing case, defended by Carson and Köstenberger, treats John 12:27 and Gethsemane as two distinct, both-historical episodes: an earlier moment of inner conflict that Jesus resolved days before, and a later garden prayer in which he wrestled afresh. As narrative reconstruction this is not impossible — the Gospels are demonstrably selective, and John omits much the Synoptics include. But it founders on the emotional and theological *content*, not merely the chronology. The Synoptic Gethsemane Jesus genuinely entertains the petition — ''if it be possible, let this cup pass'' is a live request held open until submission overrides it. The Johannine Jesus poses ''shall I say, Father, save me?'' as a rhetorical non-starter, answered before it is finished: ''but for this cause came I unto this hour.'' One Jesus asks and waits; the other refuses to ask. These are not two stages of a single psychology resolving over time; they are two different Christological postures toward the passion. Ehrman in *How Jesus Became God* uses precisely this contrast as a model of the Synoptic-to-Johannine developmental arc: as Christology climbs, the marks of human frailty are progressively effaced. The choice is between two prayer-events with incompatible emotional logic or one tradition reshaped by Johannine theology — and the latter is the more economical reading. Verdict: genuine contradiction reflecting divergent Christologies.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Death of the Messiah*, vol. 1 (ABRL; Doubleday, 1994), pp. 146-234, on Gethsemane vs. John 12:27
C. H. Dodd, *The Interpretation of the Fourth Gospel* (Cambridge University Press, 1953), pp. 369-379, on the Johannine reworking of the agony tradition
