John 18:3-5 narrates the same arrest with no kiss at all. Instead Jesus, ''knowing all things that should come upon him,'' goes forth to meet the cohort, asks ''Whom seek ye?'', and identifies himself with the absolute *egō eimi* (''I am [he]''), at which the soldiers ''went backward, and fell to the ground'' (18:6). The phrase carries the same divine-name resonance John exploits throughout (8:58; 8:24; 8:28), and the prostration of the arresting party reads as a momentary theophany. Raymond Brown''s *Death of the Messiah* argues that John deliberately reworked the tradition: the Fourth Gospel''s sovereign Jesus must not be passively fingered by a traitor, so the kiss is removed and replaced by a self-revelation in which Jesus initiates and controls his own arrest. Ehrman observes the same editorial tendency across John — the consistent suppression of moments where Jesus appears passive or weak.

The strongest harmonizing case, advanced by Carson and Köstenberger, is sequential: Judas did kiss Jesus (as the Synoptics report), and Jesus then stepped forward and declared himself (as John reports), each evangelist preserving the portion that served his purpose. This is not impossible. Ancient narratives are selective, and a kiss followed moments later by a self-identification could in principle both have happened. Klassen''s study of Judas even reminds us that the kiss was a conventional disciple''s greeting, not necessarily a dramatic ''signal'' in the way later tradition imagined. But the harmonization is at best partial, because the two traditions assign incompatible *functions* to the act of identification. The Synoptic kiss exists because the party could not otherwise identify Jesus — Mark 14:44 states this as the operative reason. John''s self-identification exists because Jesus must not be identified by anyone but himself; the whole point of 18:4-6 is that he volunteers what no traitor''s sign was needed to supply. If Judas''s kiss had already marked Jesus, John''s ''Whom seek ye?'' and the soldiers'' need to be told ''I am he'' lose their narrative force; conversely, if Jesus announced himself unprompted, the prearranged signal was superfluous. The critical reading is that the Synoptic kiss is likely historical — it satisfies the criterion of embarrassment, since the early church had no motive to invent a betrayal by intimate gesture — while the Johannine *egō eimi* prostration is a theological composition. Verdict: harmonizable as a bare sequence of events, but the underlying traditions are in genuine tension over how, and by whom, Jesus was identified.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Death of the Messiah*, vol. 1 (ABRL; Doubleday, 1994), pp. 244-310, on the arrest narrative
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (XIII-XXI)* (AB 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 805-818, on John 18:3-6
Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AYB 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 998-1009, on Mark 14:43-45
Bart D. Ehrman, *How Jesus Became God* (HarperOne, 2014), pp. 254-269, on John''s editorial tendency
William Klassen, *Judas: Betrayer or Friend of Jesus?* (Fortress, 1996), pp. 78-92, on the kiss as a disciple''s greeting
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, vol. 3 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 506-512, on Matthew 26:47-49
