Matthew 26:57 names Caiaphas outright; Mark 14:53 and Luke 22:54 say only ''the high priest,'' which in 30-36 CE means Caiaphas, the sitting holder. None of the three mentions a preliminary stop at Annas''s house. The strongest harmonizing case, run by Köstenberger, Carson, and Bock, is that John simply preserves a detail the Synoptics omit: an informal preliminary hearing before the powerful elder statesman Annas, after which Jesus was sent on to Caiaphas for the formal proceeding. Given what we know of Annas''s standing — and Helen Bond''s study of Caiaphas supports the picture of a tightly interlocked priestly family — this reconstruction is entirely plausible historically. The title ''high priest'' applied loosely to Annas is no contradiction at all.

What does not resolve so neatly is the architecture of the trial. The Synoptics narrate a single nighttime gathering before Caiaphas and the assembled Sanhedrin, with the witnesses, the high priest''s adjuration, and the blasphemy verdict (Mark 14:55-64); John has no such formal Sanhedrin trial, only the Annas interrogation and a bare notice that Jesus was sent to Caiaphas, with the substantive judicial action deferred to Pilate. Several critical scholars, including Bultmann and Käsemann, read the Annas hearing as a Johannine literary doublet — the evangelist multiplying the interrogation to develop the theme of Jesus standing in sovereign command before his accusers — rather than as an independent historical datum. Brown, Marcus, and Fitzmyer take a more cautious line: an Annas hearing is credible, but John''s overall trial chronology is hard to square with the Synoptic Sanhedrin scene, and the two accounts cannot simply be laid end to end. So the tension is real, but it lies in the number and shape of the hearings, not in the question of who held the title. Verdict: the titulature problem is overstated; the how-many-hearings divergence is a genuine but partly harmonizable tension.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Death of the Messiah*, vol. 1 (ABRL; Doubleday, 1994), pp. 404-560, on the high-priestly hearings
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (XIII-XXI)* (AB 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 820-829, on John 18:13-24
Flavius Josephus, *Jewish Antiquities*, Books XVIII-XX, trans. Louis H. Feldman (LCL 433; Harvard University Press, 1965), 18.26-35; 20.197-203, on Annas and the high-priestly family
Helen K. Bond, *Caiaphas: Friend of Rome and Judge of Jesus?* (Westminster John Knox, 2004), pp. 27-50, on Caiaphas and the priestly family
Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AYB 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 1008-1023, on Mark 14:53
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (AB 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1452-1465, on Luke 22:54
Rudolf Bultmann, *The Gospel of John: A Commentary*, trans. G. R. Beasley-Murray, R. W. N. Hoare, and J. K. Riches (Westminster, 1971), pp. 642-647, on the Annas hearing as a doublet
Andreas J. Köstenberger, *John* (BECNT; Baker Academic, 2004), pp. 513-518, on the preliminary Annas hearing
