Darrell L. Bock, *Luke 9:51-24:53*, vol. 2 (BECNT; Baker Books, 1996), pp. 1782-1791, on Luke 22:54', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke, John', 10, 2, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The critical consensus, developed at length in Brown''s *Death of the Messiah* and grounded in E. P. Sanders''s *Jesus and Judaism*, is that Jesus very probably did say something about the temple''s destruction. The saying scores high on the standard criteria of authenticity. It is multiply attested across independent streams: Mark 14:58 and Matthew 26:61 (the trial), John 2:19 (the temple cleansing), the taunt at the cross in Mark 15:29, the charge against Stephen in Acts 6:14, and a related logion in Gospel of Thomas 71. It satisfies the criterion of embarrassment, since a prediction of the temple''s ruin sat uneasily with a movement that still revered the temple in its earliest years, and the ''rebuild in three days'' clause was an awkward inheritance. And it coheres with Jesus'' demonstrative action in the temple, read by Sanders as a prophetic sign of destruction in the Jeremianic mode (cf. Jeremiah 7). Theissen and Merz and Crossan likewise treat a temple-saying as historically secure even while differing on its precise wording and intent.

The Synoptic-Johannine divergence is thus about transmission and framing. Mark, writing around or just after the temple''s actual destruction in 70 CE, faced a delicate situation: Jesus'' prediction had become literally true, yet the resurrection application — raising the sanctuary in three days — strained credulity and invited the mockery the passion narrative itself records. Mark distances Jesus from the bald wording by placing it in the mouths of false, mutually contradictory witnesses, even as the saying continues to do its narrative and ironic work (the temple does fall; Jesus does rise). Matthew follows Mark, trimming ''made with hands / made without hands.'' John, by contrast, with a developed temple-replacement Christology (the risen body of Jesus as the new locus of God''s presence, cf. John 1:14; 4:21-24), claims the saying outright as a public utterance and supplies the editorial gloss ''he spake of the temple of his body'' (2:21).

The strongest harmonizing case, run by Carson, Köstenberger, and Blomberg, fits the data well: Jesus did utter such a saying (John), and the trial witnesses misquoted or garbled it (Mark/Matthew), which is precisely why their testimony was branded false and inadmissible. The Synoptic complaint is not that the saying was fabricated ex nihilo but that the witnesses ''agreed not together,'' consistent with a real logion distorted in transmission. Brown himself endorses a version of this: an authentic saying refracted into a courtroom accusation. To that extent the factual tension genuinely eases. What does not fully reconcile is the evangelists'' evaluative posture. The Synoptic narrators frame the saying as untrustworthy testimony advanced by men seeking Jesus'' death; John frames the identical saying as authentic prophecy with an inspired interpretation attached. The Gospels agree, on the most plausible historical reconstruction, that Jesus said it; they disagree sharply on whether the saying as reported is to be trusted. Verdict: harmonizable at the level of the underlying historical logion, but the narrative framing remains in genuine theological tension.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Death of the Messiah*, vol. 1 (ABRL; Doubleday, 1994), pp. 434-460, on the temple saying at the trial
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (I-XII)* (AB 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 114-125, on John 2:19-21
