Andreas J. Köstenberger, *John* (BECNT; Baker Academic, 2004), pp. 108-113, on John 2:19-21
Craig L. Blomberg, *Matthew* (NAC 22; Broadman, 1992), pp. 395-397, on Matthew 26:59-61', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, John', 7, 2, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The weight of Mark''s phrasing is hard to overstate. *Egō eimi* is the bald form of the divine self-designation that stands behind the Septuagint''s rendering of the Tetragrammaton in Exodus 3:14 (*egō eimi ho ōn*) and the recurring ''I am he'' (*''ănî hû''* / *egō eimi*) of Deutero-Isaiah (Isa 41:4; 43:10; 46:4). Joel Marcus argues in the Anchor Yale Bible that Mark intends the reader to hear this theophanic resonance: this is the climactic moment at which Jesus openly avows divine identity, and it is precisely why the high priest tears his garments and the council convicts him of blasphemy (Mark 14:63-64). The directness of the answer is load-bearing for Mark''s narrative logic.

Matthew softens the declaration into the circumlocution *sy eipas* and then pivots the emphasis to the Son of Man saying with the adversative ''nevertheless (*plēn*) I say unto you, Hereafter shall ye see the Son of man sitting on the right hand of power.'' Luke goes further, dividing the single Markan question into two (the Christ question in 22:67 and the Son of God question in 22:70) and giving each a deflecting answer — ''If I tell you, ye will not believe'' and ''Ye say that I am'' — so that the affirmation is routed back onto the interrogators. The apologetic harmonization, defended by Blomberg and Carson, holds that *sy eipas* is an idiomatic Semitic affirmation, a ''yes'' with a measure of deliberate reserve, so that all three evangelists report substantially the same affirmative answer. The grammatical observation has merit: ''thou hast said'' can indeed function as a guarded affirmation in Semitic idiom, and Bock''s detailed study of the blasphemy charge shows the answer was heard as affirmative enough to ground a conviction.

But the harmonization does not erase the difference it needs to erase. *Sy eipas* and *hymeis legete* place the assertion in the questioner''s mouth; *egō eimi* places it unreservedly in Jesus''. The evangelists chose distinct Greek formulations, and the choice runs against the grain of the usual assumption that later tradition heightens Christology. Here Matthew and Luke appear to be dialing back Mark''s most direct claim — a pattern Ehrman documents in *The Orthodox Corruption of Scripture*, where he notes the tendency of some witnesses and redactors to soften statements that could sound either presumptuous or theologically hazardous. The plausible motive is twofold: the unfiltered *egō eimi* could read as self-aggrandizing in a way that embarrassed later readers, and it made the blasphemy verdict land almost too neatly, as though Jesus had volunteered his own condemnation. Whatever the motive, the data are clear. One can grant that *sy eipas* affirms and still observe that it is not what Mark wrote. Verdict: genuine compositional contradiction, the product of Matthean and Lukan redaction of Mark''s *egō eimi*.', 'Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AYB 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 1002-1015, on Mark 14:61-62
Raymond E. Brown, *The Death of the Messiah*, vol. 1 (ABRL; Doubleday, 1994), pp. 483-515, on the Sanhedrin trial sayings
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, vol. 3 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 525-532, on Matthew 26:63-64
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (AB 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1463-1469, on Luke 22:67-70
Bart D. Ehrman, *The Orthodox Corruption of Scripture*, 2nd ed. (Oxford University Press, 2011), pp. 224-227, on the softening of Christological statements
Darrell L. Bock, *Blasphemy and Exaltation in Judaism and the Final Examination of Jesus* (WUNT 2/106; Mohr Siebeck, 1998), pp. 197-237, on the blasphemy charge
Donald A. Hagner, *Matthew 14-28* (WBC 33B; Word Books, 1995), pp. 798-803, on Matthew 26:63-64
Craig L. Blomberg, *Matthew* (NAC 22; Broadman, 1992), pp. 401-402, on sy eipas as affirmation
D. A. Carson, "Matthew," in *The Expositor''s Bible Commentary*, rev. ed., vol. 9, ed. Tremper Longman III and David E. Garland (Zondervan, 2010), pp. 624-628, on sy eipas', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke', 10, 2, 3, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard apologetic response, developed by Gleason Archer and by John Wenham in Easter Enigma and endorsed in Craig Blomberg''s Historical Reliability of the Gospels, is that Peter denied Jesus more than three times in a chaotic courtyard and each evangelist sampled three of the encounters. The steel-man version is genuinely plausible at the level of physical scene-setting: a man warming himself at a fire surrounded by servants and guards could be challenged by several voices in rapid succession, and oral memory of such a scene would naturally crystallize around the round number three. The decisive objection is internal: the gospels do not narrate an open-ended series. Each installs the prophecy of exactly three denials before cockcrow and then reports exactly three, treating the third as completion. Mark sharpens this by predicting two cockcrows (14:30, ''before the cock crow twice'') against three denials, a detail Matthew, Luke, and John all smooth to a single cockcrow — a textual and redactional tell that the later evangelists were already editing Mark''s framework. (The two-crow reading in Mark 14:30, 68, 72 is itself textually contested; the words ''twice'' and the first crow at 14:68 are absent from Sinaiticus and several witnesses, which is why some manuscripts harmonize Mark toward the others.) One cannot multiply the denials past three without breaking the prophetic structure each author deliberately builds.

The more economical reading, set out in Raymond Brown''s Death of the Messiah and reflected in the major critical commentaries (Marcus on Mark, Davies-Allison on Matthew, Fitzmyer on Luke), is redactional. Mark establishes the shape: girl, same girl, bystanders. Matthew finds the repeat-girl awkward and substitutes ''another maid,'' tidying the implausibility of one servant interrogating Peter twice. Luke, whose Passion foregrounds formal accusation and witness, recasts the second and third challengers as men who make assertive declarations rather than questions. John, having already introduced Malchus and the ear-cutting at the arrest (18:10), supplies narrative payoff by making the third accuser Malchus''s kinsman, and uses the doorkeeper detail to motivate how Peter got inside at all (the unnamed disciple ''known to the high priest'' admits him). Each variation serves the evangelist''s narrative and theological aims; none is a neutral transcript.

This leaves the apologist with a fork that cannot be evaded. If the four accounts each describe the same three historical encounters, they contradict one another on who issued the challenges. If they sample different encounters from a larger set, they contradict their own emphatic ''three denials before cockcrow'' framing. The harmonizing thesis can only purchase consistency on the second horn at the price of the prophetic structure every evangelist treats as fulfilled. Verdict: genuine contradiction in the details, generated by independent redaction of a fixed three-denial tradition.', 'Brown, Raymond E. *The Death of the Messiah: From Gethsemane to the Grave*. ABRL. Vol. 1. New York: Doubleday, 1994, pp. 587-626 (on the Peter denial accounts). Marcus, Joel. *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary*. AYB 27A. New Haven: Yale University Press, 2009, pp. 1019-1031 (on Mark 14:66-72). Davies, W. D., and Dale C. Allison. *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*. ICC. Vol. 3. Edinburgh: T&T Clark, 1997, pp. 542-552. Fitzmyer, Joseph A. *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV*. AB 28A. Garden City, NY: Doubleday, 1985, pp. 1455-1463. Brown, Raymond E. *The Gospel According to John (XIII-XXI)*. AB 29A. Garden City, NY: Doubleday, 1970, pp. 822-842. Wenham, John. *Easter Enigma: Are the Resurrection Accounts in Conflict?* 2nd ed. Grand Rapids: Baker, 1992, pp. 78-92. Archer, Gleason L. *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties*. Grand Rapids: Zondervan, 1982, pp. 343-345. Blomberg, Craig L. *The Historical Reliability of the Gospels*. 2nd ed. Downers Grove, IL: InterVarsity Press, 2007, pp. 178-185.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke, John', 10, 2, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The classic harmonization, defended by Gleason Archer and by Norman Geisler and Thomas Howe in When Critics Ask, combines the two: Judas hanged himself, the rope or branch later gave way, and the bloated corpse fell and split open on the rocks below. The medical plausibility is real — a body left hanging in a warm climate undergoes putrefaction and gas accumulation within days, and a fall onto rough ground could rupture the distended abdominal wall. The steel-man here is that the two reports are partial, each preserving a phase of a single event. The objection is textual rather than physiological: neither author writes the composite. Matthew narrates a completed suicide and moves on to the priests; nothing in his text anticipates a later fall. Acts narrates a fall and rupture as the death itself and gives no hint of a prior hanging. To run the harmonization one must import the rope into Acts and the fall into Matthew, supplying from each text precisely the element the other omits. The Greek prēnēs genomenos (''falling headlong / face-forward,'' or on a minority lexical reading ''swelling up'') reads as the cause of death, not as the post-mortem sequel to a hanging.

The accounts also diverge on the agent of the field''s purchase and on why it is called the field of blood — a related contradiction treated separately — which signals that these are not two halves of one report but two independent traditions, each an etiology for the Jerusalem place name Hakeldama. Raymond Brown''s Death of the Messiah sets the traditions side by side and concludes they developed independently to explain a known field and to satisfy the theological expectation that the betrayer meet a fitting end. The two obituaries are theologically shaped in different directions. Matthew models Judas on the archetypal traitor Ahithophel, David''s counselor who betrayed him and then hanged himself (2 Sam 17:23), reinforcing the typology of treachery within the inner circle. Acts deploys the genre of the gruesome divine-judgment death, whose closest scriptural and intertestamental analogues are the bowel-bursting ends of Antiochus IV in 2 Maccabees 9:7-12 and of Herod Agrippa I in Acts 12:23 (eaten of worms) — the wicked persecutor undone from within. A third early tradition, preserved by Papias (in Apollinaris''s quotation), has Judas swell to monstrous size and die crushed in his own bulk, neither cleanly hanged nor cleanly fallen, confirming that the early church carried multiple incompatible accounts.

The fork is therefore stark. The harmonizer must graft details across two texts that each present a self-sufficient and different mode of death, against the grain of the Greek of Acts 1:18; or one accepts that primitive Christianity had no single settled account of how Judas died, only the shared conviction that he died badly and that a Jerusalem field bore the name ''of blood.'' Verdict: genuine contradiction reflecting two independent etiological traditions.', 'Brown, Raymond E. *The Death of the Messiah: From Gethsemane to the Grave*. ABRL. Vol. 2. New York: Doubleday, 1994, pp. 1404-1418 (on the death-of-Judas traditions). Davies, W. D., and Dale C. Allison. *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*. ICC. Vol. 3. Edinburgh: T&T Clark, 1997, pp. 558-568 (on Matt 27:3-10). Fitzmyer, Joseph A. *The Acts of the Apostles*. AB 31. New York: Doubleday, 1998, pp. 219-226 (on Acts 1:18-19). Klauck, Hans-Josef. *Judas: Ein Jünger des Herrn*. QD 111. Freiburg: Herder, 1987, pp. 92-129. Meier, John P. *A Marginal Jew: Rethinking the Historical Jesus*. Vol. 3. New York: Doubleday, 2001, pp. 208-211 (on death of Judas traditions). Archer, Gleason L. *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties*. Grand Rapids: Zondervan, 1982, pp. 344-345. Geisler, Norman L., and Thomas Howe. *When Critics Ask: A Popular Handbook on Bible Difficulties*. Wheaton, IL: Victor Books, 1992, pp. 360-361. Keener, Craig S. *Acts: An Exegetical Commentary*. Vol. 1. Grand Rapids: Baker Academic, 2012, pp. 757-768.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Acts', 7, 2, 5, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The apologetic harmonization, advanced by Archer and by Geisler and Howe, reads Acts in an indirect or causal sense: Judas acquired the field in that his money paid for it, even though the priests executed the deed — a ''he did it because his money did it'' construction. As a principle of agency this is unremarkable; people are routinely said to build what their funds built. The grammatical and contextual evidence, however, resists it here. Acts uses ektēsato chōrion, the aorist middle of ktaomai with Judas as explicit subject, the ordinary verb for personally acquiring property; the clause ''with the reward of iniquity'' (ek tou misthou tēs adikias) presents Judas as the one holding and spending the money. Luke had idiomatic means to express purchase-on-behalf-of and did not use them. More tellingly, the two texts also disagree on why the field is called the field of blood (Hakeldama). Matthew anchors the name to the source of the funds — it was bought with blood money. Acts anchors it to the manner of Judas''s death — his bowels burst out there, staining the ground. These are two distinct etiologies for one place name, which is the signature of independent traditions rather than complementary reports. Raymond Brown''s Death of the Messiah and Joseph Fitzmyer''s AB Acts both classify this as a doublet etiology: both authors knew of a field near Jerusalem called Hakeldama and connected it to Judas, but each supplied a different purchaser and a different reason for the name.

A further crack in the Matthean account compounds the difficulty. Matthew 27:9-10 introduces the field-purchase as the fulfillment of ''that which was spoken by Jeremy the prophet,'' yet the quoted material — thirty pieces of silver, the potter, the price of valuation — corresponds to Zechariah 11:12-13, not to Jeremiah, with at most a loose echo of the potter''s field and field-purchase motifs in Jeremiah 18-19 and 32. The misattribution is a recognized textual and exegetical crux; Menken''s study of Matthew''s Old Testament text argues the evangelist worked from a conflated testimonium, and the variant manuscript tradition that drops ''Jeremiah'' or substitutes ''Zechariah'' shows ancient scribes already troubled by it. This does not bear on the who-bought-the-field question directly, but it underscores that Matthew''s potter''s-field pericope is a theologically constructed unit built around a prophecy citation rather than a neutral chronicle.

The steel-manned defense — read ektēsato loosely — remains logically available, but it requires overriding the active middle voice and the surrounding narrative of Acts, and it does nothing to reconcile the two divergent reasons for the field''s name. Neither evangelist tells the composite story; each tells his own. Verdict: genuine contradiction; two independent Hakeldama etiologies disagreeing on both the purchaser and the origin of the name.', 'Brown, Raymond E. *The Death of the Messiah: From Gethsemane to the Grave*. ABRL. Vol. 2. New York: Doubleday, 1994, pp. 1404-1418 (on the *Hakeldama* etiologies). Davies, W. D., and Dale C. Allison. *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*. ICC. Vol. 3. Edinburgh: T&T Clark, 1997, pp. 568-577 (on Matt 27:6-10 and the Zechariah/Jeremiah citation). Fitzmyer, Joseph A. *The Acts of the Apostles*. AB 31. New York: Doubleday, 1998, pp. 219-226 (on Acts 1:18-19). Menken, Maarten J. J. *Matthew''s Bible: The Old Testament Text of the Evangelist*. BETL 173. Leuven: Peeters, 2004, pp. 171-186 (on Matt 27:9-10 prophecy citation). Archer, Gleason L. *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties*. Grand Rapids: Zondervan, 1982, pp. 344-345. Geisler, Norman L., and Thomas Howe. *When Critics Ask: A Popular Handbook on Bible Difficulties*. Wheaton, IL: Victor Books, 1992, pp. 360-361. Keener, Craig S. *Acts: An Exegetical Commentary*. Vol. 1. Grand Rapids: Baker Academic, 2012, pp. 759-768. Hagner, Donald A. *Matthew 14-28*. WBC 33B. Dallas: Word Books, 1995, pp. 813-818.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Acts', 7, 2, 4, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The ablest harmonization, set out by D. A. Carson in his John commentary and by Andreas Köstenberger, distinguishes phases of one trial. All four gospels preserve the opening question ''Art thou the King of the Jews?'' and a short affirmative reply (''thou sayest,'' su legeis, in the Synoptics; the expanded equivalent in John). On this reading Jesus answers the initial christological-political question and then falls silent specifically before the priests'' barrage of further accusations — which is what Matthew and Mark report — while John simply expands the opening Pilate exchange the Synoptics compress. This is a fair reading of the first verses and genuinely dissolves part of the tension: the initial question-and-answer is common ground, and an author can legitimately abbreviate or amplify it. To that extent the accounts are harmonizable, and the difference is one of selection and emphasis.

What the phased-trial proposal cannot absorb is the character and theological weight of the Synoptic silence. Mark, followed by Matthew, does not merely report brevity; he constructs a scene of pointed, prophecy-shaped muteness echoing Isaiah 53:7 — ''as a sheep before her shearers is dumb, so he openeth not his mouth'' — making Jesus the silent Suffering Servant whose refusal to answer astonishes the Roman governor. The silence is the theological climax of the scene, not an incidental gap into which a Johannine dialogue can be inserted. John runs a contrary program throughout the Passion. As Raymond Brown''s Death of the Messiah and his AB John demonstrate, and as Bart Ehrman emphasizes in How Jesus Became God, the Fourth Gospel presents Jesus as the incarnate Logos who remains sovereign over every exchange. John has no Gethsemane prayer of anguish, no cry of dereliction from the cross, and no astonished-Pilate silence; in its place stands a Jesus who interrogates his interrogators and announces his own purpose. The contrast is systematic, not local: the Synoptic Servant who is led silently to slaughter and the Johannine Word who lays down his life of his own accord are two coherent but distinct christological portraits, and the silence-versus-dialogue divergence before Pilate is one of the clearest windows onto that difference.

The steel-manned composite — Jesus speaks at first, then goes silent — is not impossible to assemble, but no evangelist narrates it, and the assembly works only by treating Mark''s deliberate Isaianic silence as a mere temporal slot rather than the theological statement it is. The opening exchange harmonizes; the overall portraits do not. Verdict: partially harmonizable at the level of the initial question, but the Synoptic suffering-servant silence and the Johannine sovereign-Logos dialogue reflect two irreducibly different Passion theologies.', 'Brown, Raymond E. *The Death of the Messiah: From Gethsemane to the Grave*. ABRL. Vol. 1. New York: Doubleday, 1994, pp. 723-859 (on Pilate trial scenes across the four gospels). Brown, Raymond E. *The Gospel According to John (XIII-XXI)*. AB 29A. Garden City, NY: Doubleday, 1970, pp. 843-872 (on John 18:28-19:16). Marcus, Joel. *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary*. AYB 27A. New Haven: Yale University Press, 2009, pp. 1031-1037 (on Mark 15:1-5). Davies, W. D., and Dale C. Allison. *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*. ICC. Vol. 3. Edinburgh: T&T Clark, 1997, pp. 581-585. Keener, Craig S. *The Gospel of John: A Commentary*. Vol. 2. Peabody, MA: Hendrickson, 2003, pp. 1098-1116. Ehrman, Bart D. *How Jesus Became God: The Exaltation of a Jewish Preacher from Galilee*. New York: HarperOne, 2014, pp. 270-291. Carson, D. A. *The Gospel According to John*. PNTC. Grand Rapids: Eerdmans, 1991, pp. 588-600. Köstenberger, Andreas J. *John*. BECNT. Grand Rapids: Baker Academic, 2004, pp. 525-540.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, John', 10, 2, 3, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The usual harmonization, defended by John Wenham in Easter Enigma and by Craig Blomberg, is that Jesus was robed twice — first by Herod''s retinue, then by Pilate''s. Logically this is unobjectionable; two mockeries by two authorities are physically possible, and the color difference (gorgeous/lampran in Luke, scarlet or purple in the others) is sometimes pressed as evidence of two distinct garments. The difficulty lies in the narrative function of the robe in each account. In Matthew, Mark, and John the robing is one element of a structured mock-coronation: stripping, robe, crown of thorns, a reed for a scepter (Matthew), and the salute ''Hail, King of the Jews.'' The garment is integral to a ritual investiture, not a passing wardrobe note. Luke''s Herod scene contains none of this furniture — no crown, no scepter, no royal salute — only the single robe, after which Herod returns Jesus to Pilate still wearing it. The harmonization must therefore posit two visually similar but ceremonially different mockings that no single evangelist ever combines, with each writer omitting the other occasion entirely.

The critical explanation is redactional. The Herod-Antipas examination is found only in Luke and is widely regarded, following Joseph Fitzmyer''s AB commentary, Raymond Brown''s Death of the Messiah, and François Bovon''s Hermeneia volume, as a Lukan composition. Luke''s Passion is structured to display Jesus examined by every relevant Palestinian authority — Sanhedrin, Pilate, and Herod — and the Herod scene fulfills the program announced in Acts 4:25-27, where Herod and Pontius Pilate are said to have gathered together against the Lord''s anointed in fulfillment of Psalm 2. The robe appears in Luke''s Herod scene because Luke needs Jesus visibly marked as a mock ''king'' for the dramatic irony of the ruler who wanted to see a sign; but Luke relocates the visual mockery away from the praetorium that Mark had established as its setting. This is editorial reshaping, not the report of a second historical event. Notably, Luke has no praetorium mock-coronation at all — having moved the robing to Herod, he does not duplicate it under Pilate — which is precisely the pattern one expects if a single tradition has been transposed rather than supplemented.

The steel-manned two-robes reading remains available to anyone committed to it, but it requires that all four evangelists each suppressed an entire robing episode their tradition contained, and that none found it worth noting that Jesus was mocked with a royal garment twice. The more economical account is that Luke rewrote where, by whom, and to what theological end the mock-coronation occurred. Verdict: not harmonized; Luke''s Herod robing is best understood as a redactional transposition of the single mock-coronation the other gospels locate in Pilate''s praetorium.', 'Brown, Raymond E. *The Death of the Messiah: From Gethsemane to the Grave*. ABRL. Vol. 1. New York: Doubleday, 1994, pp. 760-786 (on the Herod scene as Lukan composition) and pp. 858-877 (on the mocking by soldiers). Fitzmyer, Joseph A. *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV*. AB 28A. Garden City, NY: Doubleday, 1985, pp. 1474-1485 (on Luke 23:6-12). Marcus, Joel. *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary*. AYB 27A. New Haven: Yale University Press, 2009, pp. 1043-1051 (on Mark 15:16-20). Davies, W. D., and Dale C. Allison. *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*. ICC. Vol. 3. Edinburgh: T&T Clark, 1997, pp. 599-606. Bovon, François. *Luke 3: A Commentary on the Gospel of Luke 19:28-24:53*. Hermeneia. Translated by James Crouch. Minneapolis: Fortress, 2012, pp. 260-272 (on Luke 23:6-12 Jesus before Herod). Wenham, John. *Easter Enigma: Are the Resurrection Accounts in Conflict?* 2nd ed. Grand Rapids: Baker, 1992. Blomberg, Craig L. *The Historical Reliability of the Gospels*. 2nd ed. Downers Grove, IL: InterVarsity Press, 2007, pp. 185-192.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke, John', 10, 2, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The strongest harmonization, advanced by Gleason Archer and by Geisler and Howe, rests on the genuine fuzziness of ancient color terminology. Greek *porphyra* did not denote ''purple'' in the narrow modern sense. It covered a broad band of the red-violet spectrum, and the prestige dye it named — Tyrian purple, extracted from the murex sea-snail — produced a deep, dark red-violet that ancient observers could describe as crimson or scarlet depending on light and batch. Meyer Reinhold''s study of purple as a status symbol in antiquity documents how elastic and status-laden the vocabulary was. On these grounds the difference is plausibly a description of one reddish-purple cloth from two angles, and the harmonization earns partial credit: the underlying garment is very likely the same.

Where the harmonization runs out is the literary relationship between the texts. On the dominant solution to the Synoptic Problem, Matthew is using Mark as a written source, and his substitution of *chlamyda kokkinēn* for Mark''s *porphyran* is a deliberate redactional change, not an independent eyewitness''s color memory. Davies and Allison argue in the ICC that Matthew''s choice increases the scene''s realism: Roman soldiers actually owned and wore the *chlamys* / *paludamentum*, a red military cloak, so dressing the mock-king in one fits what soldiers had to hand far better than draping him in costly imperial Tyrian purple. Joel Marcus, by contrast, reads Mark''s *porphyra* as chosen precisely for its royal-imperial resonance — the soldiers parody Jesus as a purple-clad emperor. Two evangelists are thus pursuing two different aims: Mark presses the regal symbolism; Matthew nudges the detail toward plausible military costume. John''s *porphyran* lines up with Mark, consistent with John''s drawing on a tradition that shares Markan features at several points in the Passion.

The lexical distinction matters here. *Kokkinos* (from *kokkos*, the kermes insect dye) and *porphyra* (the murex dye) named different dye sources and different price tiers, even if their hues bled into one another at the edges. So while the colors overlap enough that no one need posit two separate cloaks, the specific word each writer selected is distinct enough to read as an editorial decision rather than a mere perception ambiguity. Raymond Brown, in both *The Death of the Messiah* and his John commentary, treats the variation as part of the broader pattern of each evangelist shaping the mockery scene to his own ends, and notes that the color of royalty is the theological point common to all three regardless of the lexeme.

It is worth registering the limit of the critical reading: assigning *intent* to Matthew''s swap is an inference about redactional motive, and a defender can reasonably insist the change is trivial precisely because the hues overlap. That concession is fair. But the parsimonious account of why three texts of the same scene carry two distinct, well-attested dye terms is that the words were chosen, not merely remembered. The physical contradiction nearly dissolves; the editorial divergence does not.

Verdict: largely harmonizable at the level of the garment (overlapping ancient color terms), but the lexical split reflects a real redactional choice.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew* (ICC, vol. 3; Edinburgh: T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 600-606, on Matt 27:28 and the chlamys kokkinē.
