Donald A. Hagner, *Matthew 14-28* (WBC 33B; Dallas: Word Books, 1995), pp. 829-832, on Matt 27:28.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Grand Rapids: Zondervan, 1982), pp. 345-346, on the scarlet/purple robe.
Norman L. Geisler and Thomas Howe, *When Critics Ask: A Popular Handbook on Bible Difficulties* (Wheaton, IL: Victor Books, 1992), pp. 361-362.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, John', 10, 1, 3, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The most robust harmonization, presented by D. A. Carson in the Pillar commentary and Craig Blomberg in *The Historical Reliability of the Gospels*, is sequential: Jesus shouldered the crossbeam (*patibulum*) leaving the praetorium, but the scourging left him too weakened to continue, so the soldiers seized Simon partway along the route. Crucifixion victims typically carried only the horizontal beam, weighing perhaps thirty to forty kilograms, and a man flogged with the *flagrum* could plausibly collapse under it. The reconstruction is mechanically sound, it harmonizes the explicit statements without forcing any of them to mean its opposite, and it should be granted as possible. On this basis the entries are partially reconcilable: nothing in John denies a later substitution, and nothing in the Synoptics denies an initial stretch carried by Jesus.

The deeper problem is that the composite story belongs to no evangelist. Each Gospel narrates a complete and internally coherent scene, and the divergence tracks each writer''s theology of the Passion. The Synoptics, dependent on Mark, present a suffering, depleted Jesus led to death like the servant of Isaiah 53 — silent, scourged, and in Mark crying out the dereliction of Psalm 22, ''My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me.'' Within that portrait Simon is structurally apt: the brutalized Jesus needs another to bear his beam, and Mark even names Simon''s sons, Alexander and Rufus, as if they were known to his community, a mark of the tradition''s concreteness that Joel Marcus discusses. John''s Passion runs on the opposite program. There Jesus is sovereign throughout — he goes to the cross as to a throne, the crucifixion is his ''lifting up'' and glorification (John 3:14; 12:32), and he remains in command to the point of declaring ''It is finished'' as an accomplished work. The emphatic *heautō*, ''by himself,'' fits that christology: the Johannine Jesus does not require, and is not permitted, a substitute to carry the instrument of his enthronement. Raymond Brown in *The Death of the Messiah* and Rudolf Bultmann in his Johannine commentary both read the omission of Simon as a deliberate Johannine choice rather than ignorance of the tradition; the silence is theological. Some interpreters have additionally suspected an anti-docetic edge — John may stress that Jesus genuinely bore his own cross against teachers who denied the reality of his suffering — though Brown is cautious about pressing that motive.

Steel-manning the harmonizers once more: the two-stage route is the kind of detail an ancient narrator could compress, and abbreviation is not falsification. But neither John nor the Synoptics shows any seam where the handoff would occur. John reports Jesus carrying the cross with no hint of a later transfer; the Synoptics introduce Simon at the outset of the route with no hint that Jesus had carried it first (Luke''s ''that he might bear it after Jesus'' even pictures Simon walking behind a Jesus no longer under the beam). The historical kernel — a Cyrenian was pressed into service — is widely judged authentic precisely because the early church would not invent a stranger carrying the Messiah''s cross. The cleanest account of the data is that John, knowing the tradition, suppressed Simon to preserve his portrait of the self-possessed, self-offering Christ.

Verdict: mechanically harmonizable, but the divergence is better explained as Johannine redactional theology than as a single remembered sequence.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Death of the Messiah: From Gethsemane to the Grave* (ABRL, vol. 2; New York: Doubleday, 1994), pp. 913-917, on the cross-carrying and Simon of Cyrene.
