The strongest harmonization, run by John Wenham and Craig Blomberg, identifies Matthew''s ''vinegar mingled with gall'' (27:34) with Mark''s ''wine mingled with myrrh'' as two descriptions of the same pre-crucifixion sedative, and keeps the later sponge as a separate event attested across the Gospels. That move dissolves most of the apparent conflict, and it deserves real credit: the two-offer structure is exegetically sound and widely accepted, including by critical commentators such as Raymond Brown.

What the harmonization cannot absorb is Matthew''s specific substitution of *cholē*, gall, for myrrh. Davies and Allison, with Brown, argue this is a deliberate edit assimilating the narrative to Psalm 69:21 in the Septuagint: ''they gave me gall (*cholē*) for my food, and for my thirst they gave me vinegar (*oxos*) to drink.'' Matthew has conformed the historical detail to the prophetic text so that the scripture is fulfilled verbatim — the same technique that produces several of his formula citations. Mark''s ''wine and myrrh'' is the historically plausible reading, congruent with the anesthetic tradition; Matthew''s ''vinegar and gall'' is shaped by the psalm. So the divergence is not a disagreement about what was physically in the cup but a difference in how far each evangelist let scripture rewrite the detail. Notably, the actual gall offer in Matthew would be a bitter, undrinkable substance Jesus tastes and rejects, which fits the psalm better than it fits a merciful sedative — another sign of the literary pressure of Psalm 69.

John adds the singular detail of hyssop (*hyssōpō*) as the means of raising the sponge — botanically awkward, since hyssop is a small, flimsy plant ill-suited to bearing a soaked sponge to a man on a cross (a textual variant, *hyssō*, ''on a javelin,'' attested in a few witnesses, looks like an attempt to ease exactly this difficulty). Brown argues the choice is theological: hyssop is the plant used to daub the Passover lambs'' blood on the doorposts in Exodus 12:22, and John''s Passion deliberately aligns Jesus'' death with the slaughter of the Passover lambs (John 19:14, 36). The botanically improbable hyssop is retained because it carries the Passover typology John wants.

Steel-manning the apologetic case in full: the two-offer reconstruction is correct, and the gall/myrrh equivalence is *possible* if one supposes a bitter additive could be loosely called either. But the cleaner explanation of why Matthew alone writes ''gall'' is that he is targeting Psalm 69 — a redactional, scripture-driven choice that bends the historical detail. The drinks contradiction is therefore mostly an artifact of conflating two moments; what remains is Matthew''s prophecy-shaped wording.

Verdict: largely harmonizable via the two distinct offers, with Matthew''s ''gall'' a genuine redactional reshaping toward Psalm 69:21.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Death of the Messiah: From Gethsemane to the Grave* (ABRL, vol. 2; New York: Doubleday, 1994), pp. 940-952, on the drink offers and Psalm 69:21.
Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AYB 27A; New Haven: Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 1042-1043, 1060-1062, on Mark 15:23 and 15:36.
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew* (ICC, vol. 3; Edinburgh: T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 612-614, 624-625, on Matt 27:34.
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (XIII-XXI)* (AB 29A; Garden City, NY: Doubleday, 1970), pp. 909-913, 929-930, on John 19:29.
François Bovon, *Luke 3: A Commentary on the Gospel of Luke 19:28-24:53*, Hermeneia, trans. James Crouch (Minneapolis: Fortress, 2012), pp. 290-317, on Luke 23:36.
John Wenham, *Easter Enigma: Are the Resurrection Accounts in Conflict?*, 2nd ed. (Grand Rapids: Baker, 1992).
Craig L. Blomberg, *The Historical Reliability of the Gospels*, 2nd ed. (Downers Grove, IL: InterVarsity Press, 2007), pp. 191-194.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Grand Rapids: Zondervan, 1982), pp. 346-347.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke, John', 10, 2, 3, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The most common harmonization, found in Archer and in Geisler and Howe, leans on John''s notice (19:20) that the sign was written in three languages — Hebrew (Aramaic), Latin, and Greek. If each evangelist rendered a different language version, the argument goes, differences in word order and phrasing are exactly what one would expect; the Gospels are simply quoting different lines of a trilingual board. There is genuine substance here. Trilingual public notices were standard Roman administrative practice, the renderings across languages would not be word-for-word identical, and all four agree on the substantive charge. On that basis the entries are partially reconcilable: there is one placard, one accusation, and predictable surface variation.

The difficulty is that the differences are not the kind translation produces. Translation reorders and re-inflects the *same* content; it does not add and subtract *elements*. Yet that is precisely what the Gospels do. Mark has no name and no demonstrative; Luke adds ''this is''; Matthew adds the personal name ''Jesus''; John adds the qualifier ''of Nazareth.'' No single language would yield four different rosters of words. Raymond Brown, working through the data in *The Death of the Messiah*, judges that the original inscription was most likely close to Mark''s terse form and that each later evangelist expanded it for his own narrative aims. Matthew''s insertion of ''Jesus'' sharpens the irony his Gospel cultivates — the official Roman placard unwittingly proclaims the name of the Messiah. Luke''s ''this is'' smooths the Greek into a fuller declarative. John''s ''of Nazareth'' coheres with his Gospel''s sustained interest in Jesus'' origin and identity (cf. ''Jesus of Nazareth'' at the arrest, John 18:5, 7).

The Johannine form, moreover, is load-bearing for the very next verses. In John 19:21-22 the chief priests object to Pilate, ''Write not, The King of the Jews; but that he said, I am King of the Jews,'' and Pilate replies, ''What I have written I have written.'' That exchange — a small Johannine drama about who controls the meaning of Jesus'' kingship — presupposes a fixed, formal inscription that Pilate composed and refuses to alter, and it functions best on the elaborated Johannine wording. The titulus in John is thus not merely reported but deployed.

Steel-manning the harmonizers fully: the trilingual sign is a real datum, the shared charge is constant, and abbreviation of an official notice is unobjectionable in ancient historiography. A defender can reasonably say none of the four claims to give the complete text, so the shorter forms simply quote part of it. But that concession itself grants the point — the evangelists are selecting and shaping, and at least the additions of ''Jesus'' and ''of Nazareth'' are content, not translation. The economical explanation of four contents is redactional expansion of a short original, not three languages.

Verdict: substantially harmonizable on the shared charge and the trilingual notice, but the differing contents reflect each evangelist''s redactional shaping rather than mere translation.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Death of the Messiah: From Gethsemane to the Grave* (ABRL, vol. 2; New York: Doubleday, 1994), pp. 962-968, on the titulus variations.
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (XIII-XXI)* (AB 29A; Garden City, NY: Doubleday, 1970), pp. 901-902, 919-920, on John 19:19-22.
Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AYB 27A; New Haven: Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 1054-1056, on Mark 15:26.
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew* (ICC, vol. 3; Edinburgh: T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 614-616, on Matt 27:37.
François Bovon, *Luke 3: A Commentary on the Gospel of Luke 19:28-24:53*, Hermeneia, trans. James Crouch (Minneapolis: Fortress, 2012), pp. 309-313, on Luke 23:38.
Darrell L. Bock, *Luke 9:51-24:53* (BECNT, vol. 2; Grand Rapids: Baker, 1996), pp. 1858-1863, on Luke 23:38.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Grand Rapids: Zondervan, 1982), pp. 346-347, on the titulus.
Norman L. Geisler and Thomas Howe, *When Critics Ask: A Popular Handbook on Bible Difficulties* (Wheaton, IL: Victor Books, 1992), pp. 363-364.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke, John', 10, 1, 4, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard harmonization, argued by Gleason Archer, John Wenham, and Craig Blomberg, is chronological: both criminals mocked Jesus at first (the moment Mark and Matthew preserve), and one of them later underwent a change of heart and became the penitent thief Luke records. Crucifixion was a slow death lasting hours, and the proposal that a dying man, watching Jesus'' bearing, came to repentance over time is psychologically plausible and internally consistent. A second, weaker supporting move notes that Greek (like English) can use a plural loosely for a group when strictly only part of the group acts — ''they reviled him'' could be a generalizing plural. These observations earn the harmonization a hearing.

But the harmonization founders on the wider evidence of Luke''s redactional program, which the conversion theory does not merely supplement but contradicts in spirit. Luke systematically reshapes Mark''s crucifixion. He removes the cry of dereliction — Mark''s ''My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me'' (Mark 15:34) is absent from Luke — and replaces the Markan portrait of an anguished, forsaken Jesus with a serene, sovereign one who prays ''Father, forgive them; for they know not what they do'' (23:34) and dies committing his spirit calmly to the Father (23:46). Within this thoroughgoing recasting, the penitent thief is not an isolated extra fact but an integral element: he is the climactic instance of Luke''s signature theme of last-minute repentance and the embrace of the outcast, of a piece with the prodigal son (Luke 15), the tax collector justified over the Pharisee (18:9-14), and Zacchaeus (19:1-10). Joseph Fitzmyer in the Anchor Bible Luke reads the scene as Lukan composition serving these theological ends, and Bart Ehrman in *Jesus, Interrupted* uses it as a paradigm case of how Luke transforms Mark''s dying Jesus into a model of forgiveness. The good thief, on this reading, is a figure Luke''s theology requires, not a memory Luke alone retained.

The decisive point against the conversion harmonization is narrative: no Gospel actually tells the two-stage story. Mark and Matthew give no hint of any subsequent softening — for them the mockery from both sides is part of the total abandonment of Jesus, joined by passersby and chief priests. Luke gives no hint that his penitent thief ever mocked; from his first words the man fears God and defends Jesus. The reconstruction in which both first revile and then one repents is a composite reverse-engineered to reconcile the texts, narrated by none of them. The generic-plural defense is also strained: Mark''s and Matthew''s plural is most naturally read as genuinely dual, paralleling the dual mockery from rulers, soldiers, and crowd that frames the scene, and Matthew has independently chosen to retain and even underscore Mark''s ''both.''

The most economical explanation is redaction: Luke has rewritten the shared Markan datum of dual mockery to create the penitent thief, in service of his distinctive theology of forgiveness. The differing accounts cannot both be straightforwardly historical without importing a sequence neither narrates.

Verdict: genuine contradiction at the narrative level; best explained as Lukan redaction rather than a single harmonizable sequence.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Death of the Messiah: From Gethsemane to the Grave* (ABRL, vol. 2; New York: Doubleday, 1994), pp. 968-1011, on the crucifixion scenes.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (AB 28A; Garden City, NY: Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1507-1511, on Luke 23:39-43.
Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AYB 27A; New Haven: Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 1054-1059, on Mark 15:32.
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew* (ICC, vol. 3; Edinburgh: T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 617-618, on Matt 27:44.
François Bovon, *Luke 3: A Commentary on the Gospel of Luke 19:28-24:53*, Hermeneia, trans. James Crouch (Minneapolis: Fortress, 2012), pp. 313-317, on Luke 23:39-43.
Bart D. Ehrman, *Jesus, Interrupted: Revealing the Hidden Contradictions in the Bible (and Why We Don''t Know About Them)* (New York: HarperOne, 2009), pp. 64-69.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Grand Rapids: Zondervan, 1982), p. 347, on the two thieves.
Craig L. Blomberg, *The Historical Reliability of the Gospels*, 2nd ed. (Downers Grove, IL: InterVarsity Press, 2007), pp. 193-195.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke', 10, 2, 3, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The history of harmonization is long. Augustine in *De Consensu Evangelistarum* III.13 already felt the difficulty and offered two solutions: that the Jews'' clamor at the third hour secured the moral guilt while the soldiers'' physical act came at the sixth, and a *parasceue*-based reckoning. The dominant modern apologetic move is the dual-reckoning hypothesis, articulated by B. F. Westcott and carried forward by D. A. Carson and Andreas Köstenberger: John, writing in Asia Minor for a Greco-Roman audience, supposedly used Roman civil time counted from midnight, so his "sixth hour" is 6 a.m.; the trial therefore ends at dawn and the crucifixion follows three hours later, dissolving the conflict.

The hypothesis is elegant but it founders on John''s own usage. Every other temporal marker in the Fourth Gospel reads naturally as standard Jewish/Greek daylight reckoning counted from sunrise. John 1:39 has the two disciples remain with Jesus because "it was about the tenth hour" — clearly late afternoon, the natural reason to stay the night. John 4:6 sets Jesus wearily at Jacob''s well "about the sixth hour," i.e. the noon heat that motivates the request for water. John 4:52 has the fever leave the official''s son "at the seventh hour," 1 p.m. There is no instance anywhere in John where Roman civil reckoning is demonstrably in play; the dual-reckoning theory exists solely to neutralize 19:14, which makes it ad hoc by construction. Raymond Brown, in both his Anchor Bible John and *The Death of the Messiah*, walks through the proposal and rejects it precisely on this internal-consistency ground.

There is also a textual wrinkle. Because ancient scribes recognized the tension, some manuscripts of John 19:14 read "third hour" (gamma, the numeral for 3) rather than "sixth" (the numeral for 6) — a variant noted by the early commentators and explicable as deliberate assimilation to Mark, since the two Greek numerals were graphically confusable. The harder reading "sixth" is firmly attested and almost certainly original; the variant is itself evidence that copyists felt the contradiction.

The more economical explanation is redactional. John''s Passion is built around Passover typology. Jesus is introduced as "the Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world" (John 1:29), and John times the death to coincide with the slaughter of the Passover lambs in the temple, which began around noon on 14 Nisan. The deeper divergence follows: in the Synoptics the Last Supper *is* the Passover meal (Mark 14:12–16), so Jesus dies on 15 Nisan; in John the supper precedes Passover (John 13:1; 18:28, where the authorities avoid defilement so they can still eat the Passover), so Jesus dies as the lambs are killed on 14 Nisan. John P. Meier''s *A Marginal Jew* surveys the date-of-the-supper debate and concludes the two schemes cannot be merged without special pleading. Bart Ehrman frames the point sharply: the hour-of-the-day discrepancy is the visible tip of a whole-day disagreement about whether the Last Supper was even a Passover meal.

Steel-manning the apologist: dual reckoning is theoretically possible, and one cannot prove John never deviated from his own convention. But the burden runs the wrong way — the theory is invoked at exactly one verse against a uniform pattern, while Johannine paschal theology explains both the hour and the day at once. Verdict: genuine contradiction, best explained by John''s deliberate paschal chronology.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Death of the Messiah: From Gethsemane to the Grave* (ABRL; Doubleday, 1994), vol. 2, pp. 957-959, on the chronological discrepancy and Johannine paschal symbolism.
