The harmonizing reading, run by Gleason Archer and by John Wenham in *Easter Enigma*, is sequential: Jesus said all three (plus the other Lukan and Johannine sayings), and each evangelist preserved the one most significant to him. Mechanically this is possible; nothing forbids a dying man several utterances. But it dissolves under redactional analysis, because each evangelist does not merely *select* a saying — he constructs the entire death scene around it, and the resulting theologies are incompatible.

Mark''s Jesus dies in desolation. His only word from the cross is the cry of dereliction, screamed *phōnē megalē*, and Mark surrounds it with mockery, darkness, and a torn temple veil. Joel Marcus argues that Mark wants the reader to feel the scandal of a crucified messiah experiencing genuine divine abandonment — an apocalyptic sense of God''s withdrawal that the resurrection will reverse. (A noteworthy textual datum: the transliterated cry mixes Hebrew *Eli* with Aramaic *sabachthani*, and the manuscripts vary — Mark''s witnesses lean toward Aramaic *Elōi*, Matthew''s toward Hebrew *Ēli*, the latter making the bystanders'' confusion with "Elias/Elijah" more intelligible. The variation shows the tradition itself was unstable.)

Luke found the cry of dereliction theologically intolerable and systematically rewrote the scene. Joseph Fitzmyer, in the Anchor Bible Luke, documents the changes: the dereliction cry is removed and replaced with the calm Psalm 31:5 commendation; the daughters of Jerusalem are addressed with compassion on the way; the penitent thief is promised paradise; and the centurion calls Jesus "a righteous man" rather than "the Son of God." Luke''s Jesus dies as the *dikaios paschōn*, the suffering righteous one of Wisdom of Solomon 2–5 and the Maccabean martyrs — composed, forgiving, vindicated, in command of his own spirit. This is not Mark''s abandoned sufferer; it is its deliberate correction. François Bovon reads Luke 23:46 as the calm death of a martyr-witness modeling how the persecuted disciple should die (cf. Stephen''s near-identical commendation in Acts 7:59).

John pushes furthest. Throughout the Fourth Gospel Jesus is sovereign over the Passion — he lays down his life of his own accord (John 10:18), he is not seized but gives himself up (18:4–8), and he goes to the cross to complete the work the Father gave him (17:4). *Tetelestai* is therefore the verdict on a finished mission, the seal on the Lamb''s offering, not a cry of pain. Raymond Brown''s John commentary stresses that the word echoes 19:28 ("that the scripture might be fulfilled") and signals accomplishment, not collapse.

Bart Ehrman uses these three death scenes as his clearest illustration that the evangelists are not transcribing one event but proclaiming three distinct theologies of the cross — abandonment, righteous martyrdom, and sovereign completion. Steel-manning the harmonizers fully: three sayings in sequence cannot be disproved, and an oral tradition might well have remembered several utterances. But the sequential composite is told by no one ancient or modern in a coherent narrative form, and it erases the very thing each evangelist labored to construct. Verdict: genuine contradiction at the level of which words were last and what the death meant; the divergence is redactional, not reportorial.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Death of the Messiah: From Gethsemane to the Grave* (ABRL; Doubleday, 1994), vol. 2, pp. 1043-1088, on the death cries across the gospels.
Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AYB 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 1062-1068, on Mark 15:34 and Psalm 22.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (AB 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1515-1520, on Luke 23:46.
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (XIII-XXI)* (AB 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 929-931, on John 19:30 *tetelestai*.
