In Mark the confession is the structural climax of the whole book. Mark opens at 1:1 with "the beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God," and across the narrative no human character correctly applies that title — the demons know it, the voice from heaven declares it, but the disciples never grasp it and Jesus silences those who approach it (the messianic secret). The first human being to confess Jesus as Son of God is a pagan executioner, and he does so at the moment of death, watching *how* Jesus expired. Joel Marcus reads this as the deliberate breaking of the secret: revelation comes precisely at the cross, not in glory. Matthew preserves Mark''s wording almost verbatim, adding the apocalyptic signs (earthquake, opened tombs) that prompt the soldiers'' fear.

Luke, working from Mark, changes the confession to *dikaios*. Joseph Fitzmyer and Robert Karris both argue this is no stylistic accident but the keystone of Luke''s reframing of the crucifixion as a forensic vindication. Across Luke 23, Pilate three times pronounces Jesus innocent (23:4, 14, 22), Herod finds no capital fault (23:15), the penitent thief affirms "this man hath done nothing amiss" (23:41), and finally the centurion certifies him *dikaios*. Karris''s influential JBL article shows that *dikaios* carries the full Lukan sense of the innocent, righteous one whose unjust death is vindicated by God — the *dikaios* of the Wisdom of Solomon and the martyr tradition, consistent with Luke''s portrait of Jesus elsewhere (cf. Acts 3:14, 7:52, 22:14, where Jesus is "the Righteous One"). Luke''s centurion delivers a verdict; Mark''s centurion delivers a creed.

There is a textual subtlety worth noting. In Mark 15:39 the phrase lacks the definite article (*huios theou*, anarthrous), which has fueled a grammatical debate over whether the centurion meant "the Son of God" in the full Christian sense or "a son of a god" in a pagan Roman sense — possibly ironic on the soldier''s lips but theologically loaded for Mark''s reader. Either way the content is a divine-sonship claim, categorically distinct from Luke''s innocence verdict. Some Western witnesses also show minor variation, but the *huios theou / dikaios* divergence between the Gospels is firm in the manuscript tradition and cannot be attributed to scribal corruption; it is authorial.

The harmonization, offered by Archer, is that the centurion said both things in the course of the scene — there is no rule limiting him to one remark. This is not impossible. But it misses the point of the data: each evangelist presents his version as *the* climactic Gentile testimony and shapes the surrounding narrative to land on it. No source narrates the centurion saying both, and the redactional pattern — Luke systematically lowering Mark''s high-christological moments in favor of righteous-martyr framing, as Ehrman observes across the passion — explains the divergence far more economically than a lost composite speech. Verdict: genuine contradiction at the level of wording, best explained as Lukan redaction of Mark''s confession.', 'Robert J. Karris, "Luke 23:47 and the Lucan View of Jesus'' Death," *Journal of Biblical Literature* 105, no. 1 (1986): pp. 65-74, on Luke 23:47 and *dikaios*.
Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AYB 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 1063-1068, on Mark 15:39.
