The standard harmonization, run by D. A. Carson and John Wenham, observes that crucifixion was a protracted ordeal of some six hours, ample time for the women to move from near the cross to a distance (or vice versa). Physically this is unobjectionable, and for that reason the spatial data are not logically airtight contradictions: "near at one moment, far at another" is conceivable. This is why the tension is best classed as only partially resolved rather than a flat impossibility.

What the harmonization cannot supply is any textual warrant for the movement. Neither John nor the Synoptics describe the women relocating; the harmonized itinerary is constructed precisely to reconcile, and the more telling fact is *why* each evangelist fixes the women where he does. The Synoptic *apo makrothen* is not an idle geographic note. It echoes Psalm 38:11 LXX — "my friends and my neighbors drew near and stood against me, and my kinsmen stood afar off" — and the related lament motif of Psalm 88:8. Joel Marcus catches the allusion: the women at a distance complete the portrait of the righteous sufferer deserted by those nearest to him, the same psalmic substratum (Pss 22, 38, 69) that shapes the whole Markan Passion. The Synoptics *need* the women far away to sustain that image of abandonment.

John needs them near for the opposite reason. John 19:25–27 is unique to the Fourth Gospel: from the cross Jesus entrusts his mother to the Beloved Disciple — "Woman, behold thy son... Behold thy mother" — a scene with deep symbolic freight in Johannine theology (the formation of the new community at the foot of the cross). That pericope is unintelligible unless the women and the Beloved Disciple are within earshot. And the Beloved Disciple himself is conspicuously absent from all three Synoptic crucifixion scenes; the Synoptics know of no male disciple at the cross at all, which is part of why the bequest of Mary is found in John alone. Raymond Brown, in both his John commentary and *The Death of the Messiah*, treats this as a case where the Johannine and Synoptic crucifixion tableaux cannot be straightforwardly merged: the dramatic configurations serve different ends.

A related layer of divergence reinforces the point: the named women themselves differ across the Gospels. Mark lists Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James the younger and Joses, and Salome; Matthew has Mary Magdalene, the other Mary, and the mother of the sons of Zebedee; John has Jesus'' mother, her sister, Mary of Clopas, and Mary Magdalene — and uniquely places Jesus'' own mother there. Richard Bauckham''s *Gospel Women* analyzes these lists and the difficulty of correlating them, which shows the tradition about who stood where was not uniform.

Steel-manning the harmonizers fully: a change of position over six hours is genuinely possible, and the contradiction is not as hard as the death-cry or the hour-of-crucifixion problems. But the reconciliation rests on a movement no source reports, invented to smooth a divergence that arises because the Synoptics frame abandonment (psalmic distance) while John frames the founding of the new family (intimate proximity). Verdict: harmonizable in principle but redactionally driven; the "they moved" reconciliation is an inference the texts neither state nor require.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Death of the Messiah: From Gethsemane to the Grave* (ABRL; Doubleday, 1994), vol. 2, pp. 1013-1026, on the women at the crucifixion.
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (XIII-XXI)* (AB 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 904-906, 922-927, on John 19:25-27.
