François Bovon, *Luke 3: A Commentary on the Gospel of Luke 19:28-24:53*, trans. James Crouch (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2012), pp. 335-338, on Luke 23:49.
Richard Bauckham, *Gospel Women: Studies of the Named Women in the Gospels* (Eerdmans, 2002), pp. 203-256.
D. A. Carson, *The Gospel According to John* (PNTC; Eerdmans, 1991), pp. 615-619.
John Wenham, *Easter Enigma: Are the Resurrection Accounts in Conflict?*, 2nd ed. (Baker, 1992), pp. 64-77.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke, John', 10, 2, 3, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

On the first: Matthew 27:57–60, Mark 15:43–46, and Luke 23:50–53 all credit Joseph of Arimathea with requesting the body from Pilate, taking it down, wrapping it in linen, and laying it in a tomb. John 19:38–42 adds Nicodemus, who supplies "about an hundred pound weight" of myrrh and aloes. Crucially, none of the Synoptics states that Joseph acted *alone*; they simply do not mention a second person. John''s addition is therefore supplementary, not contradictory — the standard harmonization (Joseph as principal, Nicodemus assisting) is sound and is exactly the sort of elaboration John regularly supplies. This part genuinely resolves. (One may note redactional coloring even here: John''s lavish hundred-pound spice quantity gives Jesus a royal burial fit for a king, consistent with the Fourth Gospel''s high Christology, but a difference of theological emphasis is not a contradiction of fact.)

The second is harder. Acts 13:27–29, in Paul''s Pisidian Antioch sermon, attributes the disposal of the body to "they that dwell at Jerusalem, and their rulers" — the same subject that "desired Pilate that he should be slain." The Greek keeps the subject continuous: the *rulers* who condemned him are the ones who, "when they had fulfilled all that was written of him, took him down from the tree, and laid him in a sepulchre." Grammatically the burying party is the hostile Jewish leadership, not a devoted disciple. This sits awkwardly against both the Synoptic and Johannine traditions, in which Joseph is "an honourable counsellor, which also waited for the kingdom of God" (Mark) and explicitly "Jesus'' disciple" (Matthew, John).

The apologetic response, advanced by Darrell Bock and Ben Witherington, reads Acts 13:27–29 as compressed sermonic rhetoric: Paul is summarizing the Passion in broad strokes, and "they" denotes the Jerusalem establishment as the collective agent that *brought about* the sequence of events, the burial included, without specifying who physically handled the corpse. Joseph, on this reading, was himself a member of the Sanhedrin — "a counsellor" — so a loose reference to "the rulers" need not exclude him. This is possible. But it is strained against the wording: "they took him down from the tree, and laid him in a sepulchre" is a concrete, agent-specific statement, and the natural antecedent of "they" is the hostile party of v. 27–28, not a lone sympathizer.

The critical scholarship reads the divergence as evidence of developing burial traditions. John Dominic Crossan and Bart Ehrman argue that standard Roman crucifixion practice was to leave bodies on the cross or consign them to a common grave, and that the dignified entombment by a named, wealthy, sympathetic councilor is most plausibly a later Christian development designed to secure an honorable burial and a locatable, verifiable tomb — a development that grows more elaborate across the tradition (anonymous in the earliest layers, then Joseph, then Joseph plus Nicodemus, with ever more spices and ever more explicit discipleship). On this view Acts 13:27–29 may preserve an *earlier*, undeveloped memory in which Jesus'' opponents simply disposed of the body. The counter-argument from apologists is that an embarrassing burial by a Sanhedrist (a member of the condemning body) is unlikely to be invented and may be historical; Jodi Magness, surveying the archaeology of first-century Jerusalem rock-cut tombs, considers a hasty burial of Jesus by a Jewish figure consistent with Jewish law''s demand to bury the executed before nightfall (Deut 21:22–23), which complicates the flat "no burial ever happened" position.

Steel-manning both sides: the Synoptic–John difference dissolves, and "they" in Acts could be a rhetorical generalization. But the Acts attribution to the condemning rulers is not naturally read that way, and the most economical explanation is that distinct communities remembered and developed the burial differently. Verdict: Synoptic/John tension harmonizable; Acts 13:27–29 a genuine, unresolved divergence reflecting tradition development.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Death of the Messiah: From Gethsemane to the Grave* (ABRL; Doubleday, 1994), vol. 2, pp. 1201-1313, on the burial traditions.
Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (AYB 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 1069-1075, on Mark 15:42-47.
