The interesting question is why each evangelist chose his precise wording, because the choices are not random — they are shaped by each gospel''s theology and by the redactional relationships among the texts. Mark''s *anateilantos tou hēliou*, ''when the sun had risen,'' is the single most precise solar marker in any resurrection account. There is a long-noted internal awkwardness in Mark 16:2 itself: the women come *lian prōi*, ''very early,'' yet *anateilantos tou hēliou*, ''after sunrise'' — a near-redundancy that some manuscripts and ancient readers tried to smooth, and that Joel Marcus in his Anchor Yale Mark discusses as possibly preserving two layers of tradition. Mark''s emphasis on full daylight removes any suggestion of dim-light error: the empty tomb is discovered in plain sight. Matthew, who follows Mark as his primary source on the Two-Document Hypothesis, softens this to *epiphōskousēi*, less interested in the hour than in the angelic earthquake and descending theophany that he alone adds (Matt 28:2-4). John''s *skotias eti ousēs*, ''while it was still dark,'' is the most theologically loaded of the four. John has reduced the witnesses to Mary Magdalene alone, and her solo predawn approach fits the Fourth Gospel''s pervasive light/darkness symbolism (John 1:5; 3:19; 9:4; 13:30, where Judas goes out and ''it was night''). Raymond Brown''s Anchor Bible commentary treats the Johannine ''still dark'' as a deliberate symbolic detail: Mary comes in darkness and the resurrection dawns. The descriptions are thus not four reporters'' clock-readings but four authorial framings of one event.

The critical point, made by Dale Allison in *Resurrecting Jesus* and by Gerd Lüdemann, is that the four accounts draw on partially independent traditions each evangelist has shaped, not on coordinated eyewitness depositions. That said, harmonization on the bare question of timing does not require implausible assumptions. The honest verdict separates this entry from the harder ones around it: the divergence in hour is real but minor, and a group arriving over a few predawn minutes covers it. The contradiction sharpens only when the timing is bundled with the genuinely irreconcilable data — how many women came, whom they saw, and what they did next — which are treated in the neighboring entries. Verdict: overstated in isolation; the timing is harmonizable, and the real tensions lie elsewhere in the resurrection cycle.', 'Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Yale Bible 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 1081-1093, on Mark 16:1-2 and the lian prōi / anateilantos tou hēliou awkwardness
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (XIII-XXI)* (Anchor Bible 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 980-1004, on the Johannine ''still dark'' as light/darkness symbolism at John 20:1
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, vol. 3 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 663-672, on Matt 28:1
Dale C. Allison, *Resurrecting Jesus: The Earliest Christian Tradition and Its Interpreters* (T&T Clark, 2005), pp. 198-375, on the empty tomb traditions
John Wenham, *Easter Enigma: Are the Resurrection Accounts in Conflict?*, 2nd ed. (Baker, 1992), pp. 78-92
Craig L. Blomberg, *The Historical Reliability of the Gospels*, 2nd ed. (InterVarsity Press, 2007), pp. 195-202
Gerd Lüdemann, *The Resurrection of Jesus: History, Experience, Theology*, trans. John Bowden (Fortress, 1994), pp. 109-134', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, John', 2, 2, 4, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The difficulty is that Matthew and Mark do not merely under-report; they describe scenes structured around a single actor with no narrative room for a companion. Matthew''s angel is the only character with verbs: he descends from heaven, rolls back the stone, sits upon it, and addresses the women, while the guards become ''like dead men'' (Matt 28:2-4) — a tightly closed tableau. Mark''s young man sits alone *en tois dexiois*, ''on the right side,'' and speaks; there is no second figure anywhere in the frame. So the harmonization must claim not just selective mention but that two evangelists independently chose to narrate a two-figure scene as though only one were present, which is precisely the kind of coincidence redaction criticism explains more economically.

The deeper problem is identity, not number. Mark calls the figure a *neaniskos* and pointedly never uses *angelos*. This word choice has generated its own scholarly debate: the same rare term describes the young man who flees naked at Jesus'' arrest (Mark 14:51-52), and many critics — including Robin Scroggs and others discussed by Joel Marcus and Adela Yarbro Collins — read the two *neaniskos* references as a deliberate Markan inclusio, framing the passion with a figure of failed-then-restored discipleship or an angelic interpreter left studiedly ambiguous. Matthew resolves the ambiguity by making the figure an explicit *angelos kyriou* and adding the descending-from-heaven theophany absent in Mark. Luke pluralizes to *andres dyo* in *esthēti astraptousēi*, ''dazzling clothing,'' and John makes them *dyo angelous en leukois*, ''two angels in white,'' positioned at head and feet like the cherubim over the mercy seat (Exod 25:18-20). Raymond Brown and Dale Allison both read this as a developmental trajectory: Mark''s ambiguous young man is progressively angelified and the count escalates from one to two. The doubling is itself a recognizable Lukan-Johannine convention — Acts 1:10 places ''two men in white'' at the ascension, and the Transfiguration sets two figures, Moses and Elijah, beside Jesus (Mark 9:4). Textually, none of the four readings is in doubt; the variation is authorial, not scribal, so the contradiction cannot be dissolved by appeal to the manuscript tradition.

The strongest form of the apologetic case — that focusing on the speaking figure is genuine ancient narrative practice — deserves its hearing, and it is true as far as it goes. But it cannot account for the *qualitative* shift from ''young man'' to ''angel,'' which is not a matter of how many were mentioned but of what kind of being is described, nor for the patterned escalation from one to two across the chronological order of composition. Bart Ehrman frames this as a textbook case of reading the gospels ''horizontally'' versus ''vertically'': harmonized into a composite, the cast list multiplies beyond what any single account allows. Verdict: genuine contradiction; the number can be finessed, but the identity and the redactional escalation cannot.', 'Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Yale Bible 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 1081-1093, on the neaniskos of Mark 16:5
Adela Yarbro Collins, *Mark: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 794-799, on the young man at Mark 16:5
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (XIII-XXI)* (Anchor Bible 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 980-1004, on John 20:12
Dale C. Allison, *Resurrecting Jesus: The Earliest Christian Tradition and Its Interpreters* (T&T Clark, 2005), pp. 198-299
