The harmonization attempted by John Wenham reconstructs a staggered arrival: an advance party enters and finds the tomb empty, the angels then appear, and later-arriving women see the angel before they see inside. Logically this can be made to fit, and it is the strongest form of the case. But it depends on a substructure none of the four narratives contains — women splitting into subgroups arriving at different moments and, implausibly, not relaying to each other what they had just witnessed. Each gospel narrates a unified group (or, in John, a single woman) moving through one continuous scene.

The more economical reading, advanced by Raymond Brown and Dale Allison, is that the difference in order is editorial and theological rather than chronological. Mark and Matthew frame the encounter around the women''s mistaken expectation: they come to anoint a corpse (Mark 16:1 specifies the spices), and the angel''s role is to correct that assumption — the dramatic reversal requires the angel to deliver the news, so emptiness is disclosed through proclamation. Luke and John instead foreground the women''s autonomous discovery because each needs it to launch what follows: John uses Mary''s premature theft-assumption to set up the footrace of Peter and the Beloved Disciple to the tomb (John 20:3-10) and her subsequent recognition scene, while Luke uses the women''s puzzlement and the men''s verification to build toward the Emmaus road and the motif of disciples ''slow to believe'' (Luke 24:25). The ordering is a function of each narrative''s design, not of competing stopwatch reports of one event. On the Two-Document Hypothesis, Matthew is reworking Mark directly here, while Luke and John draw on partially independent empty-tomb traditions, which is why the Markan proclamation-first structure survives into Matthew but is restructured in the other two.

Unlike the cast-list problem of the adjacent entry, this discrepancy is more amenable to a charitable reading: the events reported (empty tomb, angelic encounter, message of resurrection) are the same, and only their sequence differs, which a sequenced reconstruction can in principle accommodate. The cost is inventing narrative machinery the texts do not supply. Verdict: partially harmonizable; the order is a redactional and theological variable rather than a flat factual contradiction, but the apologetic reconstruction that smooths it requires assumptions absent from every gospel.', 'Dale C. Allison, *Resurrecting Jesus: The Earliest Christian Tradition and Its Interpreters* (T&T Clark, 2005), pp. 198-299
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (XIII-XXI)* (Anchor Bible 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 980-1004, on John 20:1-2
François Bovon, *Luke 3: A Commentary on the Gospel of Luke 19:28-24:53* (Hermeneia; trans. James Crouch; Fortress, 2012), pp. 341-360, on Luke 24:2-4
Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Yale Bible 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 1081-1093, on Mark 16:5-6
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, vol. 3 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 663-675, on Matt 28:5-6
John Wenham, *Easter Enigma: Are the Resurrection Accounts in Conflict?*, 2nd ed. (Baker, 1992)
Bart D. Ehrman, *Jesus, Interrupted: Revealing the Hidden Contradictions in the Bible (and Why We Don''t Know About Them)* (HarperOne, 2009), pp. 47-60', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke, John', 2, 2, 4, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The textual evidence for Mark''s silence-ending is among the strongest in New Testament criticism. Mark 16:9-20, the ''Longer Ending,'' is absent from the two great fourth-century uncials, Codex Sinaiticus (ℵ) and Codex Vaticanus (B), and from the Sinaitic Syriac and important Armenian and Georgian witnesses. Eusebius (Ad Marinum) and Jerome both report that the most accurate Greek copies known to them ended at 16:8. Beyond the Longer Ending there exists a separate ''Shorter Ending'' and a ''Freer Logion'' interpolated within the Longer Ending in Codex Washingtonianus — a proliferation of competing conclusions that is itself the clearest evidence the church found 16:8 unbearable and repeatedly tried to mend it. Bruce Metzger''s *Textual Commentary* and the NA28 apparatus print 16:9-20 as secondary, and the judgment runs unbroken from Westcott and Hort through to the present editors. The vocabulary and style of 16:9-20 also diverge markedly from Markan usage, reinforcing the verdict.

With the secondary endings set aside, Mark closes on dread. The apologetic harmonization, run by John Wenham and given qualified room by D. A. Carson, reads *oudeni* as restricted: the women said nothing *to anyone they met on the road*, while still eventually delivering the message to the eleven. The strongest form of this case appeals to Mark''s habit of recording commands to silence (the ''messianic secret,'' e.g. Mark 1:44; 7:36) and argues the silence is momentary and en route. But the Greek resists it. The construction *oudeni ouden eipan* is an emphatic double negative — ''they said nothing to no one,'' i.e. absolutely nothing to anyone — and the clause is closed by *ephobounto gar*, an explanatory ''for they were afraid'' that grounds the whole silence in fear, not a temporary travel-restriction. Adela Yarbro Collins in the Hermeneia Mark and Joel Marcus in the Anchor Yale Mark both reject the restricted reading: grammatically the silence is unqualified, and the sentence is built to end the book on it. The much-debated fact that the gospel terminates on the postpositive conjunction *gar* — rare but not unparalleled in Greek — only underscores the abruptness.

Why would Mark end this way? The leading critical explanations, developed by Norman Petersen and John Dominic Crossan, read the silence as a deliberate literary and theological device: the narrative breaks off mid-action, throwing the burden of proclamation onto the reader and implicating even the last faithful witnesses in the failure that has dogged the disciples throughout the gospel. Matthew and Luke, writing later and using Mark as a source, found this catechetically intolerable and rewrote the women''s response into joyful obedient testimony. Bart Ehrman and Metzger present Mark 16:8 as a textbook case of later evangelists smoothing a theologically awkward Markan ending, and the church''s own manufacture of multiple substitute endings shows the ancient readers understood 16:8 exactly as the critics do — as an ending that needed fixing. Verdict: genuine contradiction, anchored in a secure text-critical result; the restricted-silence harmonization is grammatically untenable.', 'Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16: A New Translation with Introduction and Commentary* (Anchor Yale Bible 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 1081-1096, on Mark 16:8
Adela Yarbro Collins, *Mark: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 779-818, on Mark 16:8
Bruce M. Metzger, *A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament*, 2nd ed. (Deutsche Bibelgesellschaft, 1994), pp. 102-106, on Mark 16:9-20
Bart D. Ehrman, *The Orthodox Corruption of Scripture: The Effect of Early Christological Controversies on the Text of the New Testament*, 2nd ed. (Oxford University Press, 2011), pp. 264-265, on Mark 16:8
Norman R. Petersen, "When Is the End Not the End? Literary Reflections on the Ending of Mark''s Narrative," *Interpretation* 34, no. 2 (1980): pp. 151-166, on Mark 16:8
John Dominic Crossan, *The Birth of Christianity: Discovering What Happened in the Years Immediately After the Execution of Jesus* (HarperSanFrancisco, 1998), pp. 545-573, on Mark 16:8
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, vol. 3 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 659-675, on Matt 28:8
D. A. Carson, "Matthew," in *The Expositor''s Bible Commentary*, rev. ed., vol. 9, ed. Tremper Longman III and David E. Garland (Zondervan, 2010), pp. 661-670, on Mark 16:8
John Wenham, *Easter Enigma: Are the Resurrection Accounts in Conflict?*, 2nd ed. (Baker, 1992)', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke', 10, 2, 4, 1, 1, 'The longer endings of Mark (16:9-20, plus the shorter ending) are textually secondary—absent from Codex Sinaiticus and Vaticanus—so Mark originally ends at 16:8 with the women''s silence, which Matthew and Luke contradict with immediate proclamation.', 'Metzger, Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament, 2nd ed. (1994), pp. 102-106; NA28 apparatus at Mark 16:8-9');

The apologetic harmonization, advanced by Gleason Archer and by Norman Geisler and Thomas Howe, reads these as sequential moments: Jesus first appears to Mary while withholding recognition (John), then later appears to the group with recognition granted (Matthew). The strongest form notes that John 20 does narrate a delayed recognition resolved when Jesus says ''Mary'' (John 20:16), so non-recognition followed by recognition is internal to John and not implausible in principle. But stacking John''s scene under Matthew''s requires rewriting both. Matthew has the women, plural, meet Jesus on the road back from the tomb immediately after the angel; John has Mary, singular, encountering him only after she has run to fetch Peter and the Beloved Disciple and returned a second time (John 20:2, 11). The casts, the locations relative to the errand, and the choreography differ. There is a further unresolved snag the two-stage reading creates: in Matthew the women clasp his feet, whereas John''s Jesus tells Mary ''do not cling to me'' (*mē mou haptou*, John 20:17) — the Matthean foot-holding and the Johannine prohibition pull against each other if they are the same woman in adjacent moments.

Luke is the harder problem for any sequential reconstruction. Luke 24:22-24 does not merely omit a tomb appearance; it positively reports that the women saw angels rather than Jesus, and that those who checked the tomb ''did not see him.'' Luke reserves the first dominical appearances for the Emmaus road and the upper room (Luke 24:13-49), a deliberate editorial structure that builds his theme of disciples ''slow of heart to believe'' (24:25). To harmonize, one must treat Luke''s silence as mere omission, but Luke 24:24''s ''him they did not see'' is an assertion, not a gap.

The critical reading, from Raymond Brown forward, is that each evangelist shapes the appearance to his own theology. Matthew''s high Christology produces immediate *proskynēsis*, worship, consistent with his framing of the angel-theophany earlier in the chapter. John''s recognition-by-voice enacts the Good Shepherd motif of John 10:3-4, ''the sheep hear his voice … he calls his own by name'' — Mary knows him not by sight but when he names her, a theological point requiring the initial non-recognition. Luke foregrounds angelic testimony and the disciples'' incredulity, deferring recognition to the breaking of bread at Emmaus (24:30-31), itself a eucharistic motif. Dale Allison catalogues these as variant traditions each evangelist has developed rather than complementary slices of one sequence, and Bart Ehrman uses precisely this cluster to illustrate that the differences are the data, not gaps for the harmonizer to bridge. Verdict: genuine contradiction; the two-stage appearance can be stipulated only by overriding Matthew''s plural witnesses, John''s choreography and *mē mou haptou*, and Luke''s explicit denial that anyone saw Jesus at the tomb.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (XIII-XXI)* (Anchor Bible 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 1000-1004, on John 20:14-17
Dale C. Allison, *Resurrecting Jesus: The Earliest Christian Tradition and Its Interpreters* (T&T Clark, 2005), pp. 233-242
Bart D. Ehrman, *Jesus, Interrupted: Revealing the Hidden Contradictions in the Bible (and Why We Don''t Know About Them)* (HarperOne, 2009), pp. 47-55
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (Anchor Bible 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1539-1554, on Luke 24:22-24
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 347-349
Norman L. Geisler and Thomas Howe, *When Critics Ask: A Popular Handbook on Bible Difficulties* (Victor Books, 1992), pp. 348-352', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Luke, John', 10, 2, 4, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The strongest harmonization, advanced by Craig Blomberg and at book length by John Wenham, runs the appearances as a single itinerary: the women''s report sends the disciples to Galilee for the Matthean mountaintop appearance and the lakeside scene of John 21, after which they return to Jerusalem in time for the Luke 24 commission and Pentecost. Steel-manned, the argument leans on the fact that the resurrection window in Acts 1:3 stretches across forty days, ample time for a round trip, and on the observation that Luke is demonstrably selective elsewhere. The case is not frivolous.

It founders, however, on what Luke actually wrote. Luke 24 compresses the entire post-resurrection sequence — the Emmaus road, the return to Jerusalem, the appearance to the eleven, the ascension — into what reads as a single day ("that same day," 24:13; "the same hour," 24:33). Joseph Fitzmyer notes that Luke has deliberately telescoped the chronology and excised Galilee from the resurrection geography altogether; even the angelic words at the tomb are redacted, so that Mark''s "he goeth before you into Galilee" (Mark 16:7) becomes in Luke 24:6 a backward-looking reminder of what Jesus said "when he was yet in Galilee." Hans Conzelmann demonstrated that this is programmatic: for Luke, Jerusalem is the theological pivot, the place where salvation is accomplished and from which the Gentile mission radiates outward (Luke 24:47; Acts 1:8). Galilee is closed off precisely so that the narrative arc can run Jerusalem-to-Rome. Inserting a Galilean round trip into Luke 24 is not filling a gap Luke left open; it is overwriting a structure Luke built on purpose, and it must override the explicit prohibition of Acts 1:4 to do so.

Source-critically, the consensus diagnosis — from Rudolf Bultmann through Raymond Brown and Dale Allison — is that two independent and incompatible resurrection traditions stand behind the canonical Gospels: a Galilean-appearance strand (Mark, Matthew) and a Jerusalem-appearance strand (Luke, John 20). The Galilean strand is plausibly the earlier and more historical, since Galilee is where the movement originated and where the disciples would naturally have returned after the crisis in Jerusalem; the Jerusalem strand serves the theological need to anchor the church''s birth at the cultic and prophetic center. The harmonizing itinerary requires the reader to supply a journey that one evangelist shows no awareness of and another explicitly forbids. That is reconstruction in service of a conclusion, not exegesis of the text as given. Verdict: genuine contradiction, reflecting two divergent traditions.', 'Craig L. Blomberg, *The Historical Reliability of the Gospels* (2nd ed.; IVP Academic, 2007), pp. 145-149, on the itinerary harmonization of the Galilee and Jerusalem commands
John Wenham, *Easter Enigma* (2nd ed.; Paternoster, 1992), pp. 95-117, on the single-itinerary reconstruction
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke (X-XXIV)* (Anchor Bible 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1575-1577, on Luke''s redaction of the resurrection geography
Hans Conzelmann, *The Theology of St. Luke* (trans. Geoffrey Buswell; Harper & Row, 1961), pp. 93-94, on Jerusalem as Luke''s theological pivot
Rudolf Bultmann, *The History of the Synoptic Tradition* (trans. John Marsh; Harper & Row, 1963), pp. 284-291, on the Galilean vs. Jerusalem appearance traditions
Dale C. Allison, *Resurrecting Jesus: The Earliest Christian Tradition and Its Interpreters* (T&T Clark, 2005), pp. 260-269, on the divergent appearance traditions
Raymond E. Brown, *An Introduction to the New Testament* (Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, 1997), pp. 116-117, on the two resurrection traditions', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke, Acts', 10, 2, 4, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The usual harmonization, defended in detail by Gary Habermas, stacks the appearances chronologically — Magdalene first, then the two Marys, then Peter, then Emmaus — and treats Paul''s silence on the women as a culturally motivated omission. Steel-manned, this is coherent on its own terms: the Gospels nowhere claim to give an exhaustive list, and a creed designed for public proclamation might reasonably foreground the recognized leaders. But three obstacles remain.

First, the conflict is internal, not merely a matter of selective emphasis. Mark 16:9 states that Jesus "appeared first (πρῶτον) to Mary Magdalene" — an explicit ordinal that directly contradicts both Matthew''s two-woman scene and Paul''s Cephas-first creed. These are not complementary silences; they are competing claims about who was first.

Second, that decisive Markan verse is text-critically insecure. Mark 16:9-20, the "longer ending," is absent from the two oldest and best Greek manuscripts, Codex Sinaiticus (ℵ) and Codex Vaticanus (B), and Eusebius and Jerome report that the most accurate copies known to them ended at 16:8. Bruce Metzger''s committee rated the omission a near-certainty and judged the longer ending a second-century supplement. The vocabulary and style diverge sharply from the rest of Mark. So an apologist who invokes Mark 16:9 to fix the chronology is leaning on a later addition to harmonize the first-century material.

Third, and most weighty, is Paul. 1 Corinthians 15:3-7 is by near-universal consensus the earliest written resurrection tradition, and the language is formal: Paul says he "received" (παρέλαβον) and "delivered" (παρέδωκα) it — the technical pair for transmitting fixed tradition. Most scholars, including Gerd Lüdemann and Dale Allison, date the underlying creed to within a few years of the crucifixion. It names Cephas first, then the twelve, then five hundred, then James, then all the apostles, then Paul himself. No women appear. The standard reply — that women''s testimony was legally worthless, so the creed dropped them — fails on two counts. As Bart Ehrman observes, women''s testimony was in fact admissible in numerous first-century Jewish settings, and in any case Paul is reciting a confession for the believing community, not deposing witnesses before a Roman magistrate. The legal-inadmissibility argument is also double-edged: scholars frequently cite the very prominence of women in the tomb narratives as a mark of authenticity precisely because it would have been embarrassing to invent — which cuts against the claim that early tradents felt free to delete them.

The more economical reading is that the empty-tomb-and-women tradition and the appearance-to-Cephas tradition arose independently in different communities, and each evangelist chose which to foreground; Mark, Matthew, Luke, and John then narrativized the women''s role in mutually incompatible ways. Harmonizing the four requires both a textually doubtful verse and a strained account of Paul''s silence. Verdict: genuine contradiction, compounded by a scribal expansion in Mark.', 'Bart D. Ehrman, *How Jesus Became God* (HarperOne, 2014), pp. 138-144, on the divergent first-witness traditions and women''s testimony
Gary R. Habermas, *The Risen Jesus and Future Hope* (Rowman & Littlefield, 2003), pp. 16-25, on harmonizing the appearance sequence
Bruce M. Metzger, *A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament* (2nd ed.; United Bible Societies, 1994), pp. 102-106, on the secondary status of Mark 16:9-20
Gerd Lüdemann, *The Resurrection of Jesus: History, Experience, Theology* (trans. John Bowden; Fortress, 1994), pp. 109-117, on the early creed naming Cephas first
Dale C. Allison, *Resurrecting Jesus: The Earliest Christian Tradition and Its Interpreters* (T&T Clark, 2005), pp. 233-269, on the independent appearance traditions', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke, John, 1 Corinthians', 10, 2, 5, 1, 1, 'Mark 16:9 (''he appeared first to Mary Magdalene'') belongs to the longer ending of Mark (16:9-20), absent from the earliest witnesses (Sinaiticus, Vaticanus) and regarded as a second-century addition.', 'Metzger, A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament, 2nd ed. (UBS, 1994), pp. 102-106');

The "twelve" in Paul is the most tractable of the three, and the harmonization here partly succeeds. Anthony Thiselton and Gordon Fee argue, persuasively, that οἱ δώδεκα had become a fixed title for the apostolic body — the college, not a literal tally — much as one might speak of "the Twelve" institutionally even with a seat vacant. Paul uses such group designations loosely, and the title was evidently coined before the membership fluctuations of the passion week. There is also a real manuscript variant: a handful of witnesses (including some Western and later readings) substitute "the eleven" at 15:5. But the critical text of NA28 retains "the twelve," and on the principle of lectio difficilior the harder reading is to be preferred: scribes had every motive to soften "twelve" into "eleven" to match the Gospels, and almost none to create a numerical embarrassment. So the variant itself testifies that "the twelve" is original and titular. To that extent the count of twelve and the count of eleven are reconcilable.

What the titular reading cannot reach is John''s datum. John does not merely give a round number; he makes a specific narrative claim — Thomas was not in the room, and the count was therefore ten, with a second appearance "after eight days" (John 20:26) staged precisely to bring Thomas in. Paul shows no awareness of any of this: no Thomas, no locked room, no eight-day gap, no women, no geography. As Raymond Brown observes, the Johannine Thomas episode is a literarily developed scene serving the Gospel''s faith-without-seeing theme (20:29), not a datum embedded in the early kerygma. The economical conclusion, drawn by Hans Conzelmann and Dale Allison, is that Paul''s creed antedates the narrative elaborations and simply did not know them; the Gospel numbers reflect a later stage in which Judas''s death and Thomas''s absence had been woven into the story.

The apologetic case, made by Blomberg and by Geisler and Howe, leans entirely on the titular sense of "the twelve." That move is legitimate and does dissolve the twelve-versus-eleven friction. It does nothing, however, for the ten-versus-eleven friction internal to the Gospels-plus-John, nor for the larger fact that Paul''s appearance list and John''s narrative are independent traditions that cannot be merged without supplying connective tissue neither author provides. So the entry contains two distinct issues with two distinct outcomes: the title "twelve" is harmonizable; Paul''s ignorance of the Thomas tradition, and John''s count of ten against the Synoptic eleven, are not neatly so. Verdict: partly harmonizable (the title) over a residual genuine divergence (the narrative count and Paul''s independent tradition).', 'Anthony C. Thiselton, *The First Epistle to the Corinthians* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 2000), pp. 1204-1209, on the titular sense of ''the twelve'' and the textual variant
Gordon D. Fee, *The First Epistle to the Corinthians* (NICNT; rev. ed.; Eerdmans, 2014), pp. 803-806, on ''the twelve'' as the apostolic college
Hans Conzelmann, *1 Corinthians* (Hermeneia; trans. James W. Leitch; Fortress, 1975), pp. 257-258, on the creedal list and ''the twelve''
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (XIII-XXI)* (Anchor Bible 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 1024-1026, on the Thomas appearance
Dale C. Allison, *Resurrecting Jesus* (T&T Clark, 2005), pp. 233-242, on the creed predating the Gospel narratives
Craig L. Blomberg, *The Historical Reliability of the Gospels* (2nd ed.; IVP Academic, 2007), pp. 145-149, on the titular harmonization
Norman L. Geisler and Thomas Howe, *When Critics Ask: A Popular Handbook on Bible Difficulties* (Victor Books, 1992), pp. 459-461, on the number of disciples present', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke, John, 1 Corinthians', 10, 2, 4, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The strongest harmonization, set out by John Wenham and Craig Blomberg, proposes that Matthew simply omits the Jerusalem appearances and leaps to a later Galilean one — that Matthew 28:16 narrates an event days or weeks after John 20:19, not in competition with it. Steel-manned, this draws on the genuine selectivity of ancient biography and on the fact that John 21 does record a later Galilean appearance, proving the disciples did eventually travel north. So the existence of a Galilean appearance is not in dispute.

What is in dispute is whether Matthew''s mountaintop scene can be that later appearance. The Matthean text resists it. Matthew 28:16 reads as the disciples'' direct obedience to the command of 28:7 and 28:10 — they "went away into Galilee" to the mountain Jesus had "appointed," and the scene functions as the narrative''s climax and conclusion, delivering the Great Commission and ending the Gospel. Davies and Allison note that the unnamed mountain is a deliberate Matthean motif (cf. the Sermon on the Mount, the Transfiguration), a theological staging area, and that this single appearance bears the full weight of Matthew''s resurrection theology; there is no textual seam suggesting it is a sequel to unreported Jerusalem events. Matthew shows no awareness of a Jerusalem appearance at all — no locked room, no Thomas, no ascension from Bethany, no Pentecost. To make 28:16 a late episode, the harmonizer must read into Matthew an entire Jerusalem phase the evangelist never mentions, and must treat "the eleven" of 28:16 as having already seen the risen Christ in Judea despite 28:17''s note that "some doubted" — odd if they had already encountered him a week or more earlier.

The source-critical consensus, from Bultmann through Brown, reads this as the surface meeting of two independent traditions. The Galilean-appearance tradition surfaces in Mark 16:7 and is realized in Matthew 28 and John 21; the Jerusalem-appearance tradition governs Luke 24 and John 20. John, revealingly, contains both — Jerusalem in chapter 20, Galilee in chapter 21 — and chapter 21 is widely regarded as a later appendix precisely because 20:30-31 already reads as the Gospel''s conclusion, suggesting the two traditions were stitched together at the redactional level. The harmonizing timeline is possible only by supplying connective material that the texts withhold. Verdict: genuine contradiction between an originally Galilean and an originally Jerusalemite first-appearance tradition.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew, Vol. 3* (ICC; T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 676-689, on the Galilean mountaintop appearance
Raymond E. Brown, *An Introduction to the New Testament* (Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, 1997), pp. 116-117, on the two appearance traditions
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke (X-XXIV)* (Anchor Bible 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1573-1578, on the Jerusalem appearance the same day
John Wenham, *Easter Enigma* (2nd ed.; Paternoster, 1992), pp. 95-117, on the itinerary harmonization
Rudolf Bultmann, *The History of the Synoptic Tradition* (trans. John Marsh; Harper & Row, 1963), pp. 284-291, on the Galilean and Jerusalem traditions
Dale C. Allison, *Resurrecting Jesus* (T&T Clark, 2005), pp. 260-269, on the location of the first group appearance
Craig L. Blomberg, *The Historical Reliability of the Gospels* (2nd ed.; IVP Academic, 2007), pp. 145-149, on harmonizing the appearance locations', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke, John', 10, 2, 4, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The strongest harmonization, found in Leon Morris and D. A. Carson, places the forty-day temptation before John 1:35: John, on this reading, narrates events after the wilderness episode is already complete, so the baptism itself occurred off-page before John 1:19 and the temptation in the unrecorded interval. Steel-manned, the proposal exploits a genuine feature of the Fourth Gospel — John never directly narrates the baptism at all, but only the Baptist''s retrospective testimony to it (1:32-34), which leaves room to argue that time has elapsed. John also omits much that the Synoptics include, so omission of the temptation would be unremarkable in itself.

The difficulty is the texture of John 1 itself. The Baptist''s words in 1:32-34 ("I saw the Spirit descending... and I knew him not") read as fresh testimony to a recent event, and the "next day" of 1:35 follows immediately upon that testimony, not upon some forty-day absence. To make room for the temptation, the harmonizer must posit that between the Spirit''s descent and the Baptist''s report of it, Jesus had already vanished into the desert for forty days and returned — yet John 1:29-36 presents the Baptist pointing to Jesus as present and walking nearby ("Behold the Lamb of God," 1:29, 36), not as someone six weeks gone. The forty-day gap is not found in the text; it is imported to protect the Synoptic timeline.

The critical reading, represented by Raymond Brown, Joel Marcus, and Bart Ehrman, is that John preserves an independent tradition of Jesus'' early activity that simply does not include the wilderness temptation. The reason is theological as much as historical. Mark''s wilderness scene is freighted with Israel-typology — Jesus driven into the desert, tested forty days as Israel was tested forty years, ministered to by angels — and Matthew and Luke expand it into a structured three-fold dialogue with Satan keyed to Deuteronomy. John''s Christology has no place for such testing: his Jesus is the pre-existent Logos (1:1-14) who possesses the Spirit "without measure" (3:34) and is never shown in doubt, struggle, or temptation; even Gethsemane is recast. As Marcus notes of Mark and Brown of John, the two evangelists are pursuing different portraits, and the wilderness episode belongs to one program and is alien to the other. The result is two genuinely incompatible itineraries for the same window of time, each shaped by its author''s theology. Verdict: genuine contradiction reflecting independent traditions and divergent Christologies.', 'Joel Marcus, *Mark 1-8* (Anchor Bible 27; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 167-171, on the wilderness temptation and its typology
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (I-XII)* (Anchor Bible 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 65-67, 105-107, on the Johannine day-sequence after the baptism
Craig S. Keener, *The Gospel of John: A Commentary, Vol. 1* (Hendrickson, 2003), pp. 463-466, on the sequence of days in John 1
D. A. Carson, *The Gospel According to John* (PNTC; Eerdmans, 1991), pp. 146-149, on fitting the temptation before John 1:35
Leon Morris, *The Gospel According to John* (NICNT; rev. ed.; Eerdmans, 1995), pp. 132-138, on the post-baptism sequence
Bart D. Ehrman, *Jesus, Interrupted* (HarperOne, 2009), pp. 39-43, on the divergent post-baptism accounts', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Mark, John', 10, 2, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The Synoptic exorcism tradition, however, has demons making precisely this confession, and in language that meets or exceeds the Johannine bar. The Capernaum demoniac calls Jesus "the Holy One of God" (Mark 1:24; Luke 4:34); the unclean spirits cry "You are the Son of God" (Mark 3:11); the Gerasene Legion addresses him as "Jesus, Son of the Most High God" (Mark 5:7); and Luke 4:41 has demons coming out "shouting, ''You are the Son of God!''" with the editorial gloss that "they knew that he was the Christ." Joel Marcus notes that these confessions function in Mark as the supernatural recognition motif — the spirit world perceives Jesus'' identity before the human characters do. The point for our purposes is that the confessional *content* is identical to what 1 John makes salvific.

The historic harmonization, articulated by Calvin and standard since, distinguishes notitia/assensus (bare knowledge and assent) from fiducia (trusting faith). James 2:19 is the strongest internal warrant: "You believe that God is one; you do well. Even the demons believe — and shudder." James himself concedes that demons hold orthodox propositional belief and explicitly denies it is saving. That is a real distinction, drawn by the New Testament itself, and it earns the entry a partial harmonization: the canon does not naively equate confession with salvation.

But the move only partly works, and for textual reasons. The author of 1 John does *not* condition his test on the confessor''s inner disposition. The language is deliberately universal — "every spirit," "whoever" — precisely because it was meant as a usable diagnostic the community could apply to itinerant prophets without reading hearts. Painter stresses that the test is pneumatological and behavioral, not a window into fiducia. Read on its own terms, 4:2 says the confession itself marks the Spirit of God; it supplies no clause excluding insincere or demonic speakers. The Acts 19:13–16 episode of the sons of Sceva confirms that the early church recognized non-believers could invoke Jesus'' name with effect, so the boundary 1 John draws was not airtight in lived practice.

The most economical explanation is that 1 John and the Synoptics belong to different theological streams that never coordinated their formulas. The Johannine epistolary criterion was a polemical instrument against docetism; its author was not weighing whether Markan demons would pass it. James supplies the conceptual repair after the fact, but it is a repair across documents, not a unified doctrine the texts jointly intend. Verdict: partially harmonizable by the assent/faith distinction, but the unqualified Johannine wording leaves a genuine residual tension.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Epistles of John* (Anchor Bible 30; Doubleday, 1982), pp. 492-498, 564-566, on the docetic polemic behind 1 John 4:2, 4:15, 5:1
Joel Marcus, *Mark 1-8* (Anchor Bible 27; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 187-190, on demonic recognition of Jesus'' identity
John Painter, *1, 2, and 3 John* (Sacra Pagina 18; Liturgical Press, 2002), pp. 256-263, on the diagnostic function of the confession test
John Calvin, *Commentaries on the Catholic Epistles*, trans. John Owen (Calvin Translation Society, 1855), pp. 228-243, on the assent/faith distinction at James 2:19
Graham H. Twelftree, *Jesus the Exorcist* (WUNT 2.54; Mohr Siebeck, 1993), pp. 71-87, on demonic acclamations of Jesus', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Mark, Luke, James, 1 John', 5, 3, 3, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard harmonization, defended by D. A. Carson and Leon Morris, distinguishes town of origin from town of residence: Bethsaida was the brothers'' ancestral or birth town, Capernaum their adopted operational base. The exegetical hinge is the Greek polis in John 1:44. The word need not mean "current residence"; in koine usage it readily designates the town one is "of" — one''s home town or family town — much as English "he''s from" can name a birthplace independent of where someone now lives. On that reading there is no contradiction at all: a fisherman relocating a short distance along the same shoreline to a more advantageous harbor town is unremarkable, and the two notices simply answer different questions. This is a genuinely strong harmonization, not a strained one, and it carries the entry.

Two further considerations support rather than undercut it. First, John''s own narrative treats Bethsaida as a recognized identity marker, grouping "Philip of Bethsaida" with Andrew (John 12:21) as a recognizable provincial unit — consistent with an origin reference rather than a residence claim. Second, the archaeology of Bethsaida itself is unsettled in a way that loosens the geography: the long-standing identification with et-Tell, pressed by the excavator and disputed by R. Steven Notley (who argues et-Tell does not fit a first-century lakeside fishing village and favors el-Araj), shows that the very location of "Bethsaida" was fluid in antiquity and remains contested today. Jonathan Reed''s survey work on Galilean settlement makes clear how closely clustered and economically interconnected these lakeshore towns were, so movement between them was a normal feature of the region''s fishing economy.

That said, source criticism leaves a residue worth naming, even if it does not amount to a contradiction. Mark consistently treats Bethsaida as a destination distinct from Capernaum (Mark 6:45; 8:22), while the Fourth Gospel associates the earliest disciples with Bethsaida and the Baptist''s nearby activity at "Bethany beyond the Jordan" (John 1:28). The simplest description is that two tradition streams foreground two different towns in the disciples'' biographies — Mark the base of operations, John the place of origin — without either asserting the other to be false. The data are not in logical conflict; they are differently weighted.

The critical scholar should be candid that this is one of the easier entries in the gospel cycle. The relocation hypothesis is not an ad hoc rescue; it is the natural reading of polis and is consistent with ordinary first-century mobility. Verdict: harmonizable — the texts describe origin and residence, not two contradictory residences.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (I-XII)* (Anchor Bible 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 81-82, on John 1:44
Joel Marcus, *Mark 1-8* (Anchor Bible 27; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 196-200, on Mark 1:21, 29
D. A. Carson, *The Gospel According to John* (PNTC; Eerdmans, 1991), pp. 158-159, on the origin-vs-residence reading of John 1:44
Leon Morris, *The Gospel According to John* (NICNT, rev. ed.; Eerdmans, 1995), pp. 145-146, on John 1:44
Jonathan L. Reed, *Archaeology and the Galilean Jesus* (Trinity Press International, 2000), pp. 139-169, on Galilean lakeshore settlement
R. Steven Notley, "Et-Tell is Not Bethsaida," *Near Eastern Archaeology* 70.4 (2007), pp. 220-230, on the contested location of Bethsaida', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Mark, John', 4, 2, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard harmonizing reading, represented by I. Howard Marshall, treats the two pleas as complementary: the demons asked both not to be driven from the region and not to be sent to the abyss, and each evangelist preserved one facet. As far as logic goes, the two requests are not contradictory — both express the demons'' resistance to expulsion, and one could in principle entail the other. That keeps the tension genuinely mild and earns a partial harmonization: nothing in Mark is *falsified* by Luke.

But the "both happened" reflex is exactly the move Synoptic source criticism cautions against. The mainstream account, given Markan priority, is that Luke is editing Mark, and abyssos is a Lukan substitution that fits his more developed demonology rather than an extra detail recovered independently. Joseph Fitzmyer identifies the term as a Lukan touch reflecting his theological framework; François Bovon similarly reads Luke''s redaction as heightening the cosmic stakes of the confrontation. This is not an isolated case but part of a consistent pattern in which Luke theologizes Markan source material — sharpening the apocalyptic register, clarifying motives, smoothing geography. Graham Twelftree''s study of the exorcism narratives situates the Gerasene episode within precisely this redactional development of early Christian demonology.

The payoff is less about the plea itself than about what the difference reveals. If Luke and Mark were independent eyewitness depositions, the divergence between a geographic plea and a cosmological one would be a puzzle. On the dominant two-source model it is exactly what we expect: Luke had Mark in front of him and reworked it. The verse is therefore a clean, low-stakes specimen of Lukan redaction. The contradiction in the demons'' words is real but minor; its significance is evidentiary — it tells against the claim that the Synoptics are three independent reports. Verdict: partially harmonizable as complementary on the surface, but best explained as Luke theologically editing Mark.', 'Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke (I-IX)* (Anchor Bible 28; Doubleday, 1981), pp. 736-741, on abyssos as a Lukan addition at Luke 8:31
I. Howard Marshall, *The Gospel of Luke* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 1978), pp. 337-339, on the abyss request at Luke 8:31
Joel Marcus, *Mark 1-8* (Anchor Bible 27; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 342-348, on Mark 5:9-10
François Bovon, *Luke 1: A Commentary on the Gospel of Luke 1:1-9:50* (Hermeneia, trans. Christine M. Thomas; Fortress, 2002), pp. 326-329, on Luke''s redaction of the abyss plea
Graham H. Twelftree, *Jesus the Exorcist* (WUNT 2.54; Mohr Siebeck, 1993), pp. 75-87, on demonology in the Gerasene exorcism', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Mark, Luke', 4, 2, 2, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

This is not a case of two separate miracles being conflated. It is one pericope, told three times, with the same Davidic acclamation and the same instantaneous cure, in which the shared frame and the divergent internals cannot both be flat reportage.

The first apologetic move, found in Geisler and Howe, posits two healings — one entering, one leaving — with Matthew bundling both, which is why he counts two men. The difficulty is that the text supplies no warrant for two cures. The reconstruction requires Jesus to heal one blind man on the way in and a second on the way out, both using the identical "Son of David" formula, with each evangelist preserving a different fragment and omitting the other event entirely. That does not harmonize the three accounts; it manufactures a fourth that none of them records.

The second move, associated with Gleason Archer, invokes two Jerichos — the ruined Old Testament tell and the Herodian new city nearby — so that Jesus could be leaving one while approaching the other. The proposal is ingenious but ad hoc: none of the evangelists mentions two towns, and the hypothesis is generated solely to neutralize Luke''s "drew near" against Mark''s "went out." It is reasoning built backward from the desired conclusion.

On the number, the usual observation — that Mark and Luke spotlight the memorable, named Bartimaeus without denying a companion — reflects a real redactional tendency and is fair as far as it goes. But it leaves the location problem, which is the load-bearing contradiction, untouched.

The parsimonious explanation, and the consensus under Markan priority, is that Matthew and Luke each edited Mark''s single Bartimaeus narrative. Davies and Allison and Donald Hagner note Matthew''s documented habit of doubling figures — compare the two Gadarene demoniacs in Matthew 8:28 against Mark''s single man, and the two blind men of Matthew 9:27–31. John Nolland and Joseph Fitzmyer explain Luke''s relocation of the setting to the approach to Jericho as a narrative accommodation: Luke needs Jesus *inside* Jericho immediately afterward for the Zacchaeus episode (Luke 19:1–10), which is unique to his Gospel, so the healing is shifted to the entry to keep the sequence coherent. The change is editorially motivated, not a competing memory.

The choice is therefore stark. One may posit two separate inerrant miracles plus two unmentioned towns, or accept that Matthew and Luke redacted Mark and were not concerned to preserve his geography. The evidence — Matthew''s attested doubling, Luke''s structural need for the Zacchaeus pericope, and the otherwise verbatim shared frame — points decisively to the latter. Verdict: genuine contradiction at the level of flat reportage, fully explained by redaction of Mark.', 'Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16* (Anchor Yale Bible 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 759-767, on Mark 10:46-52
John Nolland, *Luke 18:35-24:53* (Word Biblical Commentary 35C; Word Books, 1993), on Luke 18:35-43 and the Zacchaeus sequence
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel according to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 3 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1997), on Matthew 20:29-34
Donald A. Hagner, *Matthew 14-28* (Word Biblical Commentary 33B; Word Books, 1995), on Matthew 20:29-34 and Matthean doubling
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (Anchor Bible 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1212-1218, on Luke 18:35-43
