Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), on the two-Jerichos harmonization', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke', 10, 2, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The historic harmonization, articulated by William Lane and Craig Blomberg, distinguishes saving belief from bare invocation. Mark 16:17, on this reading, promises the sign to true believers, while Matthew 7 and Mark 9 portray outsiders wielding a power-laden name without the inner faith that saves. That distinction is theologically coherent and earns a partial harmonization, because the texts can be read as describing two different categories of person — the believing community and effective-but-faithless invokers.

Two problems keep the resolution partial. The first is textual, and decisive in its own register. Mark 16:17 is part of the "longer ending" of Mark (16:9–20), which is absent from the two oldest and best complete manuscripts, Codex Sinaiticus and Codex Vaticanus, as well as from important versional and patristic witnesses; Eusebius and Jerome both report that the most accurate copies known to them ended at 16:8. Bruce Metzger''s *Textual Commentary* assigns the shorter text the highest confidence, and the longer ending is near-universally regarded as a second-century scribal supplement composed in a non-Markan style and vocabulary. The believers-only formulation, in other words, is not part of the earliest Gospel of Mark; it is a later harmonizing summary appended by a church that wished to tie the charismatic signs more tightly to authentic faith. The tension is thus partly between a late textual layer and the earlier canonical tradition.

The second problem is historical. Early Christians evidently regarded the name of Jesus as an efficacious formula whose power did not depend on the wielder''s spiritual standing. The sons of Sceva in Acts 19:13–16 — itinerant Jewish exorcists with no allegiance to Jesus — attempt the formula "I adjure you by the Jesus whom Paul proclaims," and the episode assumes the name *could* be wielded by outsiders, even as it backfires on these particular pretenders. Graham Twelftree''s study of early Christian exorcism documents this widespread conviction that the name itself carried power. That picture sits more naturally with Matthew 7 and Mark 9, where outsiders exorcise effectively, than with the longer ending''s tighter linkage of the sign to belief.

So the belief/invocation distinction does real work and prevents this from being a flat, irresolvable contradiction. But it does not dissolve the tension cleanly: the sharpest "believers-only" statement is a secondary addition, and the surrounding canonical and Acts material assumes the name''s power is portable beyond the community. Verdict: partially harmonizable by the belief/invocation distinction, but the key proof-text is a late, text-critically secondary ending.', 'Bruce M. Metzger, *A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament*, 2nd ed. (United Bible Societies, 1994), pp. 102-106, on the longer ending of Mark
Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16* (Anchor Yale Bible 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 1088-1096, on the longer ending of Mark
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 1 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 715-720, on Matthew 7:21-23
Graham H. Twelftree, *In the Name of Jesus: Exorcism Among Early Christians* (Baker Academic, 2007), pp. 113-128, on the efficacy of Jesus'' name
William L. Lane, *The Gospel According to Mark* (NICNT; Eerdmans, 1974), pp. 601-611, on the belief/invocation distinction
Craig L. Blomberg, *The Historical Reliability of the Gospels*, 2nd ed. (IVP Academic, 2007), pp. 159-162, on harmonizing the exorcism sayings', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke', 7, 2, 2, 1, 1, 'Mark 16:17 belongs to the longer ending of Mark (16:9-20), absent from Sinaiticus and Vaticanus and judged a second-century addition.', 'Metzger, A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament, 2nd ed. (UBS, 1994), pp. 102-106');

The tension is plainest against Luke''s infancy material. Luke deliberately saturates chapters 1-2 with Spirit-language: John the Baptist is filled with the Holy Spirit ek koilias mētros autou, "from his mother''s womb" (Luke 1:15); Elizabeth is filled with the Spirit (1:41); Zacharias is filled and prophesies (1:67); and the Spirit "was upon" Simeon (2:25), who is moved by the Spirit into the temple (2:27). Mark 12:36 and Acts 1:16 add the standard Second Temple conviction that David spoke the Psalms "by the Holy Spirit." Across the canon the Spirit is the agent of prophecy from Genesis onward, and Luke''s birth narrative is a self-conscious revival of that prophetic-Spirit motif.

The leading harmonization, articulated by D. A. Carson (PNTC, 1991, pp. 328-329) and earlier by B. F. Westcott (1882, pp. 123-124), distinguishes the Spirit''s episodic Old-Covenant empowerings from the permanent, indwelling, corporate gift of the New Covenant inaugurated at Pentecost. As systematic theology this distinction is coherent and has deep roots — James D. G. Dunn''s classic study (1970, pp. 173-182) maps precisely such a transition in the Lukan-Johannine material. The difficulty is that John 7:39 does not draw the distinction Carson and Westcott need; it makes an unqualified temporal claim. John does not write "the Spirit had not yet been given in its new-covenant corporate mode"; the narrator simply states that there was not yet Spirit, because Jesus was not yet glorified. The harmonization reads a later dogmatic taxonomy back into a sentence that lacks it. John R. Levison (2009, pp. 226-242, 366-371) shows that first-century Judaism had no single "two-stage" pneumatology onto which 7:39 maps cleanly; the verse instead reflects John''s distinctive linkage of the Spirit to the exalted Christ, a high-Christological framework standing in tension with the broader prophetic pneumatology Luke and Mark assume.

A second, narrative contradiction operates independently of the Lukan problem. John 20:22 has the risen Jesus breathe on the assembled disciples on Easter evening with the words "Receive the Holy Spirit" (labete pneuma hagion) — an act unmistakably presented as the bestowal of the Spirit John 7:39 had promised. Acts 2:1-4, however, has the same group receive the Spirit fifty days later at Pentecost, amid wind and tongues of fire. Raymond Brown (AB 29A, 1970, pp. 1037-1039) catalogs the long history of attempts to reconcile these — the Johannine breathing as a "partial" or "proleptic" giving, a symbolic anticipation, a separate empowerment for mission versus a later empowerment for witness. Each requires importing a category the texts do not supply; John presents 20:22 as the fulfillment, not a down payment. The two Spirit-givings belong to two distinct theological accounts of the same community''s origin.

Verdict: genuine contradiction. The harmonization rescues systematic coherence only by qualifying an unqualified verse, and the Easter-versus-Pentecost giving compounds the problem within the canon''s own narratives.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (I-XII)* (Anchor Bible 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 322-324, on John 7:39.
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (XIII-XXI)* (Anchor Bible 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 1037-1039, on John 20:22 and Acts 2.
James D. G. Dunn, *Baptism in the Holy Spirit* (SCM Press, 1970), pp. 173-182, on the transition in Lukan-Johannine pneumatology.
John R. Levison, *Filled with the Spirit* (Eerdmans, 2009), pp. 226-242, 366-371, on first-century Jewish pneumatology.
D. A. Carson, *The Gospel According to John* (PNTC; Eerdmans, 1991), pp. 328-329, on John 7:39.
Brooke Foss Westcott, *The Gospel According to St. John* (John Murray, 1882), pp. 123-124, on John 7:39.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Mark, Luke, John, Acts', 2, 3, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Mark 14:12 dates the disciples'' preparation to "the first day of unleavened bread, when they killed the passover," and the meal Jesus eats that night is unambiguously the Passover seder (Mark 14:14-16; cf. Luke 22:15, "with desire I have desired to eat this passover with you"). The crucifixion then follows the next morning — Mark 15:25 places it at the third hour — so for the Synoptics Jesus dies on Nisan 15, the festival day itself. John reverses this. In John 18:28 the priests avoid Pilate''s praetorium "lest they should be defiled; but that they might eat the passover," meaning the seder is still future on the morning of Jesus'' trial. John 19:14 then dates the sentencing to "the preparation of the passover" (paraskeuē tou pascha), around the sixth hour — the very time the lambs were being killed. For John, Jesus dies on the afternoon of Nisan 14, before the meal.

The Johannine motive is overt and theological. John alone frames Jesus from the outset as "the Lamb of God" (John 1:29, 36), notes that no bone of him was broken (19:36, echoing the Passover-lamb regulation of Exodus 12:46), and times the death to coincide with the slaughter of the lambs. Joachim Jeremias (1966, pp. 15-88) mounted the major defense of the Synoptic chronology as historically primary, arguing the Last Supper was a genuine seder and that John reshaped the timing theologically. Most subsequent critical scholars — Raymond Brown (1994, pp. 1350-1378), John Meier (1991, pp. 386-401), E. P. Sanders (1993, pp. 71-76) — accept that the divergence is real, and several lean toward John''s chronology as closer to history. Two considerations favor John: a capital trial and execution conducted on the night and day of Passover would violate the festival rest and is difficult to square with later rabbinic procedure (m. Sanhedrin 4:1 forbids capital trials on the eve of a festival), and the Synoptic seder-framing serves the institution of the Eucharist, giving the Synoptists their own theological reason to set the meal as Passover.

Two harmonizations recur. Annie Jaubert (ET 1965) proposed that Jesus followed a sectarian solar calendar, attested at Qumran, under which his Passover fell earlier in the week than the temple establishment''s lunar reckoning, so both chronologies could be "correct" on different calendars. The theory is ingenious but has won no majority following: it requires Jesus to keep a calendar the Gospels never mention, and it cannot account for the Synoptic insistence that the lambs were being killed (Mark 14:12) on the day of his meal. The second move is lexical — that "eat the passover" in John 18:28 refers not to the lamb but to the week-long festival offerings, especially the chagigah sacrifice eaten on Nisan 15. But John''s own dating in 19:14 ("the preparation of the passover") and 19:31 (the coming sabbath being a "high day") fixes the death on Nisan 14, undercutting the broader reading. Brown''s detailed survey (1994, pp. 1350-1378) concludes the two schemes cannot be reconciled without distorting one of them.

Verdict: genuine contradiction, with John''s chronology shaped by Passover-lamb typology and the Synoptics'' by Eucharistic theology.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Death of the Messiah*, Vol. 2 (Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, 1994), pp. 1350-1378, on the date of the crucifixion.
John P. Meier, *A Marginal Jew*, Vol. 1 (Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, 1991), pp. 386-401, on the date of the Last Supper.
