E. P. Sanders, *The Historical Figure of Jesus* (Penguin, 1993), pp. 71-76, on the crucifixion date.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Mark, John', 2, 3, 4, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

At the fulfillment level the picture is the same. Matthew 26:74, Luke 22:60, and John 18:27 record a single cock-crow after the third denial. Mark 14:68 (in the Majority Text and the Textus Receptus) reports a first crow after the initial denial, and Mark 14:72 a second crow after the third, matching the doubled prediction. Mark''s account is internally consistent; the other three are internally consistent with their own single-crow predictions. The problem is cross-Gospel: the traditions diverge on how Jesus'' words ran and how they were fulfilled.

The textual evidence complicates Mark itself. The clause kai alektōr ephōnēsen ("and the cock crew") at the end of Mark 14:68 is absent from Sinaiticus (a), B (Vaticanus), L, W, and other significant witnesses, and the word dis ("twice") in 14:72 and 14:30 is likewise unstable across the tradition. Bruce Metzger''s textual commentary (1994, pp. 95-97) judges that the first-crow notice in 14:68 and the "twice" wording are likely secondary scribal harmonizations designed to make the narration line up with the prediction — meaning even Mark may originally have had only a single crow, with the doubled version a later intrusion. Joel Marcus (2009, pp. 998-1015) and the editors of NA28 weigh the same data; the matter is genuinely disputed at the textual level, but on every reconstruction the predictions across the four Gospels do not agree.

The standard harmonization, advanced by Gleason Archer (1982, pp. 343-345) and John Wenham (1992, pp. 73-78), is that Mark simply preserves a fuller account: the others mention only the second, decisive crow without denying the first, so no formal contradiction obtains in the narration. As far as the fulfillment goes, this is a coherent more-detail/less-detail argument and would carry weight if the only difference were the number of crows narrated. But it cannot reach the prediction. "Before the cock crow twice" and "before the cock crow" are mutually exclusive accounts of what Jesus actually said, and the harmonization offers no mechanism to make both true. Davies and Allison (1997, pp. 540-551) and Raymond Brown (1994, pp. 605-626) treat the divergence as a clear instance of Matthew and Luke editing their Markan source — flattening the distinctive double-crow into the single crow that fit the proverbial "cockcrow" as a marker of dawn.

Verdict: genuine contradiction at the level of Jesus'' prediction, compounded by an unstable Markan text; the more-detail harmonization addresses only the narration, not the differing prophecies.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Death of the Messiah*, Vol. 1 (Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, 1994), pp. 605-626, on Peter''s denials.
Bruce M. Metzger, *A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament*, 2nd ed. (United Bible Societies, 1994), pp. 95-97, on Mark 14:68, 72.
Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16* (Anchor Yale Bible 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 998-1015, on Mark 14:67-72.
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 3 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 540-551, on Matthew 26.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 343-345, harmonizing the cock-crow accounts.
John Wenham, *Easter Enigma: Are the Resurrection Accounts in Conflict?*, 2nd ed. (Paternoster, 1992), pp. 73-78, on Peter''s denials.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke, John', 10, 2, 3, 2, 1, 'The first cockcrow clause at Mark 14:68 (''and the cock crew'') is omitted by Sinaiticus, B, L, W and others, and the ''twice'' (dis) at Mark 14:30/14:72 has its own variant pattern, bearing on whether Mark originally narrated one or two crowings.', 'Metzger, A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament, 2nd ed. (1994), on Mark 14:68 and 14:72');

The most defensible harmonization, set out by Craig Blomberg (2007, pp. 155-156), appeals to a recognized convention of ancient historiography: narratives frequently arrange material thematically or topically rather than in tight temporal sequence — what scholars sometimes call dischronologized narration. On this view neither evangelist is making a chronological assertion about the precise instant the veil tore relative to the last breath; each is grouping the crucifixion''s accompanying signs for narrative effect. This is a genuinely strong defense at the level of factual reconciliation, because the difference is one of arrangement, not of asserted event: all three agree the sun darkened, the veil tore, and Jesus died within the same brief window. There is no claim in any Gospel that the veil tore at a moment the others deny.

What the harmonization concedes, and what critical scholars emphasize, is that the ordering is editorial and theological rather than reportorial. Joseph Fitzmyer (1985, pp. 1517-1520) shows that Luke has deliberately rearranged his Markan source: by moving the veil-tearing forward into the cosmic darkness, Luke groups the ominous signs (the three hours of darkness, the rending of the veil) into a single prelude, leaving Jesus'' death as a serene, sovereign act — "Father, into your hands I commend my spirit" (Luke 23:46) replaces Mark''s wordless or cry-of-dereliction death. Mark''s placement, where the veil tears as God''s reaction to the death, coheres with Mark''s theology of the cross as the climactic moment of revelation, immediately followed by the centurion''s confession (Mark 15:39). Raymond Brown (1994, pp. 1098-1118), Joel Marcus (2009, pp. 1056-1068), and Davies and Allison (1997, pp. 627-634) all read the veil-tearing as a redactionally placed symbol — of judgment on the temple, of the opening of access to God, or of the apocalyptic unveiling at Jesus'' death — whose position serves each evangelist''s distinct message.

Thus the tension dissolves as a factual contradiction but only at a cost the conventional inerrancy claim cannot easily pay: it requires granting that at least one evangelist (Luke, on the standard view) consciously reordered the sequence for theological effect rather than preserving the order of events. The harmonization succeeds as history-of-genre and fails as a defense of strict chronological reportage.

Verdict: harmonizable as a difference of thematic arrangement, but only by conceding deliberate editorial reordering — not a factual contradiction, yet not strict chronological agreement either.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Death of the Messiah*, Vol. 2 (Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, 1994), pp. 1098-1118, on the temple veil.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke (X-XXIV)* (Anchor Bible 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1517-1520, on Luke 23:45-46.
Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16* (Anchor Yale Bible 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 1056-1068, on Mark 15:37-38.
