The difficulty lies in the details the harmonization must absorb. John''s Gospel narrates a solo Magdalene scene, yet in John 20:2 Mary reports to Peter and the Beloved Disciple, "they have taken away the Lord out of the sepulchre, and we know not (ouk oidamen) where they have laid him." The first-person plural "we know not" is anomalous in an otherwise single-woman narrative, and most critical commentators — Raymond Brown (1970, pp. 980-1004) and Andrew Lincoln (2005, pp. 488-491) — read it as a fossil betraying an earlier tradition in which Mary was one of several women, which John has redacted down to a Mary-focused scene for his own dramatic and theological purposes (the intimate recognition scene of 20:11-18). If that reading is right, John has not merely declined to mention others; he has reshaped a multi-woman tradition into a solo one, and the "we" is the seam.

The accounts also diverge on why the women came, and these purposes are not all compatible. Mark 16:1 and Luke 24:1 have the women bring spices to anoint the body — yet John 19:39-40 has already reported that Joseph and Nicodemus anointed Jesus with a hundred pounds of myrrh and aloes at burial, leaving no occasion for a later anointing. Matthew 28:1 gives a different purpose entirely: the women came simply "to see the sepulchre," with no spices, consistent with Matthew''s having sealed and guarded tomb (27:62-66) that would make anointing impossible. John 20:1 supplies no stated reason at all. These differences in personnel, timing ("when it was yet dark" in John 20:1 versus "as it began to dawn" in Matthew 28:1 and "very early" in Luke 24:1), and purpose indicate that the evangelists drew on independent and partly divergent resurrection traditions rather than excerpting a single shared report. Dale Allison''s detailed study (2005, pp. 233-269) catalogs these discrepancies and concludes that the empty-tomb narratives reflect a fluid oral tradition that each evangelist shaped, not a stenographic record.

Thus the numerical question harmonizes, but the underlying texts do not fully cohere. Calling all four accounts strictly compatible requires reading "and others were also present, for reasons compatible with my version" into each silence — and it has to set aside John''s anomalous plural and the conflicting purposes of the visit.

Verdict: the head-count harmonizes as partial lists, but the divergent purposes and John''s redactional seam mark genuinely independent, not fully reconcilable, traditions.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John (XIII-XXI)* (Anchor Bible 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 980-1004, on John 20:1-2.
Dale C. Allison, *Resurrecting Jesus* (T&T Clark, 2005), pp. 233-269, on the empty-tomb narratives.
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 3 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 661-666, on Matthew 28:1.
Andrew T. Lincoln, *The Gospel According to Saint John* (BNTC; Hendrickson, 2005), pp. 488-491, on John 20:1-2.
John Wenham, *Easter Enigma*, 2nd ed. (Paternoster, 1992), pp. 79-94, on the women at the tomb.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 347-349, on the number of women at the tomb.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke, John', 1, 1, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The decisive question is the tense and aspect of the Greek verbs. Matthew uses a series of aorist indicatives — egeneto ("there was/happened"), katabas ("having come down"), apekulisen ("rolled back"), ekathēto ("was sitting"). The aorists are the default narrative tense for sequenced action, and Matthew strings them in forward order: earthquake, descent, stone-rolling, the guards becoming "as dead men" (28:4), then the angel addressing the women (28:5). The natural reading places the descent and the stone-rolling within the women''s field of view, contemporaneous with their arrival. There is no pluperfect (which Greek possessed and Matthew could have used) to mark the action as prior and completed before the women showed up. The introductory kai idou ("and behold") is an attention-marker Matthew uses dozens of times to introduce a new development in the foreground of the narrative (e.g., 3:16, 9:2, 17:5), not a signal for analepsis.

The strongest harmonization — argued by John Wenham in Easter Enigma and William Lane Craig in his historicity study — reads 28:2 as a flashback (analepsis): Matthew is informing the reader how the stone came to be rolled away at some earlier moment, and the women did not witness it. On this reading the four accounts converge on an already-open tomb. The move is grammatically possible: Greek aorists can on occasion carry pluperfect force, and a narrator can supply background without an explicit temporal marker. R. T. France''s NICNT commentary adopts the flashback reading, but candidly grounds it in theological and narrative coherence rather than in any syntactic signal in the text itself; he concedes Matthew has not cued the shift the way a careful narrator might.

The critical objection is twofold. First, Matthew''s syntax runs forward without interruption, and the guards'' terror (28:4) is plainly simultaneous with the descent — yet the guards are still a live concern in Matthew''s narrative when the women are present, which keeps the events bundled together temporally. Second, and more importantly, the flashback reading is reverse-engineered to dissolve a conflict the reader already knows exists from the other Gospels; nothing in Matthew alone prompts it. Davies and Allison, in the ICC, treat the earthquake, the descending angel, and the fainting guards as Matthean redactional additions belonging to the evangelist''s penchant for apocalyptic stage-machinery — the same hand that adds an earthquake and rising saints at the crucifixion (27:51-53). These elements are not in Mark, Matthew''s source; they are Matthew''s dramatization of the resurrection as a cosmic theophany. Once that is recognized, the contradiction is not a chronological puzzle to be solved but a redactional fingerprint: Matthew has supplied a vivid opening scene the other evangelists lack, and in doing so has positioned events at the tomb that the others do not report. The flashback rescue is harmonization by syntactic gymnastics — possible to assert, but unsupported by the Greek and unmotivated by the text. Verdict: genuine contradiction, rooted in Matthean redaction.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew, Vol. 3 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 663-666, on Matthew 28:2 as Matthean apocalyptic redaction
R. T. France, The Gospel of Matthew (NICNT; Eerdmans, 2007), pp. 1098-1102, defending the flashback reading of Matthew 28:2
Dale C. Allison, Resurrecting Jesus (T&T Clark, 2005), pp. 299-310, on the divergent empty-tomb narratives
Raymond E. Brown, The Death of the Messiah, Vol. 2 (Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, 1994), pp. 1300-1306, on the tomb traditions
John Wenham, Easter Enigma, 2nd ed. (Paternoster, 1992), pp. 79-94, the flashback harmonization of Matthew 28:2
William Lane Craig, Assessing the New Testament Evidence for the Historicity of the Resurrection of Jesus (Edwin Mellen, 1989), pp. 207-220, on the harmonized tomb sequence', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke, John', 10, 2, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Luke''s verb is the crux. Epestēsan is the aorist of ephistēmi, "to come upon, stand by, present oneself suddenly." In Luke-Acts it is a stock term for the abrupt arrival of a divine or angelic presence (compare Luke 2:9, where the angel of the Lord "came upon" the shepherds, and Acts 12:7, where an angel "stood by" Peter). It does not mean anestēsan, "they stood up," which would describe rising from a seated posture. The verb places the figures already standing — indeed appearing — when the women perceive them, not transitioning from sitting to standing.

The standard apologetic reconstruction, developed by Wenham and endorsed by Blomberg, is sequential: the messengers were seated when the women first entered (Mark, John), then stood during the encounter, which is the moment Luke captures. This is internally tidy but founders on the lexical point: Luke''s epestēsan is an arrival verb, not a rising verb, so it does not narrate figures getting up — it narrates figures appearing in standing posture. The harmonization quietly substitutes anestēsan for what Luke actually wrote.

The deeper contradiction, however, is not posture but number and identity. Mark has one young man (neaniskon), not explicitly called an angel. Matthew 28:2-5 has one angel. Luke has two men. John has two angels. The traditional defense — "the mention of one does not exclude two" — has force only where a text is summarizing; it fails where a text presents a complete and self-contained encounter, which is how Mark and Matthew read. Mark''s neaniskos delivers the full kerygmatic message and dismissal on his own; nothing in the Markan scene leaves room for an unmentioned companion. Joel Marcus notes that Mark''s "young man" may even deliberately echo the neaniskos who fled naked at the arrest (Mark 14:51-52), a literary inclusio that would be undone by positing a second figure. Fitzmyer treats Luke''s two men as Lukan adaptation, paralleling the two men at the ascension (Acts 1:10). Brown lays the four accounts side by side and concludes they reflect independent developments of an empty-tomb tradition rather than four reports of one event.

Taken in isolation, the sitting/standing detail is minor and arguably harmonizable as two compatible vantage points within a fluid scene. But it cannot be detached from the number, location, and choreography differences that travel with it. The cumulative pattern — one figure or two, inside or outside, sitting or standing, named angel or unnamed young man — is precisely what independent oral traditions about a remembered scene produce, and precisely what a single eyewitness deposition would not. Verdict: mild and arguably harmonizable on posture alone, but a genuine contradiction once the number of messengers is included.', 'Joseph A. Fitzmyer, The Gospel According to Luke (X-XXIV) (Anchor Bible 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1543-1552, on Luke 24:4 and the two-men motif
Joel Marcus, Mark 8-16 (Anchor Yale Bible 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 1078-1084, on the young man at the tomb in Mark 16:5
Raymond E. Brown, The Death of the Messiah, Vol. 2 (Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, 1994), pp. 1300-1313, on the independent empty-tomb traditions
Andrew T. Lincoln, The Gospel According to Saint John (BNTC; Hendrickson, 2005), pp. 488-494, on the two angels in John 20:11-12
Dale C. Allison, Resurrecting Jesus (T&T Clark, 2005), pp. 233-310, on the cumulative empty-tomb divergences
John Wenham, Easter Enigma, 2nd ed. (Paternoster, 1992), pp. 79-94, the sequential posture harmonization
Craig L. Blomberg, The Historical Reliability of the Gospels, 2nd ed. (IVP Academic, 2007), pp. 152-156, defending the sequential posture harmonization', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Mark, Luke, John', 10, 2, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard apologetic move is the composite-narrative reconstruction that runs through all of these resurrection harmonizations: there were two angelic encounters. The women first met the angel sitting outside on the stone (Matthew), then went inside and met an angel or angels there (Mark, Luke, John). Matthew telescoped the inner encounter; the others telescoped the outer one; both are compatible if two distinct meetings occurred. Wenham builds his reconstruction on exactly this assumption.

The decisive objection is that the speeches are the same speech. Matthew''s outside angel says, "He is not here: for he is risen, as he said" (28:6), and instructs the women, "go quickly, and tell his disciples that he is risen from the dead; and, behold, he goeth before you into Galilee" (28:7). Mark''s inside young man says, "He is not here: behold the place where they laid him" (16:6), and instructs them, "tell his disciples and Peter that he goeth before you into Galilee" (16:7). These are not two messengers delivering two messages; they are one messenger delivering one message — the identical announcement and the identical Galilee commission — located in two different places. Once the speeches are recognized as a single tradition, the two-encounter reconstruction loses its footing: it requires the same words to be spoken twice, by two different angels, in two different positions, which no text states.

The redaction-critical explanation is simpler and better evidenced. Matthew''s source for the empty-tomb scene is Mark, where the messenger sits inside. Matthew has added his characteristic apocalyptic machinery — the earthquake, the descending angel of the Lord, the fainting guards (28:2-4), none of which appears in Mark — and in staging that descent he has moved the messenger out of the tomb and onto the stone, the natural perch for a figure who has just rolled it away. Davies and Allison and R. T. France both recognize the Matthean additions as redactional embellishment of the Markan Vorlage. The relocation of the angel is a byproduct of Matthew''s dramatization, not evidence of a second visitor.

The composite-encounter harmonization is a textbook case of reconciliation by invention: the second angelic meeting Wenham needs to make the geography cohere is not narrated in any of the four Gospels. It must be supplied by the harmonizer to keep the accounts from colliding. A reconstruction that can only survive by adding scenes none of the witnesses report has crossed from harmonization into composition. Verdict: genuine contradiction, the product of Matthean redaction of Mark.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew, Vol. 3 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 663-672, on Matthew''s redaction of Mark 16
R. T. France, The Gospel of Matthew (NICNT; Eerdmans, 2007), pp. 1098-1110, on the Matthean additions to the empty-tomb narrative
Raymond E. Brown, The Death of the Messiah, Vol. 2 (Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, 1994), pp. 1300-1313, on the divergent tomb-encounter traditions
Dale C. Allison, Resurrecting Jesus (T&T Clark, 2005), pp. 299-322, on the empty-tomb traditions
John Wenham, Easter Enigma, 2nd ed. (Paternoster, 1992), pp. 79-94, the two-encounter composite reconstruction', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke, John', 10, 2, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

That appendix appears to be summarizing Luke. Mark 16:12-13 — "he appeared in another form unto two of them, as they walked, and went into the country. And they went and told it unto the residue: neither believed they them" — is a compressed paraphrase of Luke''s Emmaus narrative (Luke 24:13-35). James Kelhoffer has argued at length that the author of the Longer Ending knew and epitomized Luke and the other Gospels, drawing his resurrection appearances from them. But in compressing the Emmaus story, the Markan epitomizer reverses its outcome. Luke 24:33-34 is explicit and structural: when the two return to Jerusalem, they "found the eleven gathered together, and them that were with them, saying, The Lord is risen indeed, and hath appeared to Simon." In Luke the eleven are already proclaiming the resurrection — on the basis of a prior appearance to Simon Peter (the appearance Paul also attests, 1 Corinthians 15:5) — at the very moment the Emmaus pair walks in. The Markan Ending instead has the eleven disbelieve the two travelers'' report.

The only available harmonization is to posit distinct reception stages: an initial disbelief (Mark) followed by belief once the Simon appearance was confirmed (Luke). The texts will not bear it. Luke does not narrate a disbelief-then-belief sequence around the Emmaus report at all; the belief is already in place, anchored in the Simon appearance, before the Emmaus disciples speak. There is no temporal slot in Luke for the Markan disbelief, because in Luke the cause of the eleven''s belief (the appearance to Simon) precedes the Emmaus pair''s arrival, whereas in Mark the disbelief follows the travelers'' report. The two accounts assign opposite responses to the same moment.

The cleanest reading is text-critical and tradition-historical: the Longer Ending is a later attempt to give the abrupt Markan ending (which stops at 16:8 with the women''s silence and fear) a fuller resurrection conclusion, stitched together from the other Gospels. In summarizing Luke''s Emmaus episode the epitomizer either followed a divergent tradition or simply rendered the scene carelessly, and the result contradicts the source he was condensing. Fitzmyer and Marcus both treat the Longer Ending as secondary and note its imperfect fit with the material it draws upon. Verdict: genuine contradiction, and one that dissolves into a textual-critical problem — Mark 16:12-13 is not original to Mark, and where it summarizes Luke it gets the reception backwards.', 'Bruce M. Metzger, A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament, 2nd ed. (United Bible Societies, 1994), pp. 102-106, on the secondary status of Mark 16:9-20
James A. Kelhoffer, Miracle and Mission: The Authentication of Missionaries and Their Message in the Longer Ending of Mark (WUNT 2.112; Mohr Siebeck, 2000), pp. 65-122, on the Longer Ending as an epitome of the other Gospels
Joel Marcus, Mark 8-16 (Anchor Yale Bible 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 1088-1096, on the Longer Ending of Mark
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, The Gospel According to Luke (X-XXIV) (Anchor Bible 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1559-1572, on Luke 24:33-34 and the Emmaus episode
Dale C. Allison, Resurrecting Jesus (T&T Clark, 2005), pp. 260-269, on the appearance traditions including the appearance to Peter', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Mark, Luke', 10, 2, 2, 1, 1, 'Mark 16:12-13 belongs to the longer ending of Mark (16:9-20), absent from Sinaiticus and Vaticanus and judged a second-century addition that appears to summarize Luke''s Emmaus account while reversing its reception.', 'Metzger, A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament, 2nd ed. (UBS, 1994), pp. 102-106');

The second stream grounds judgment in belief. Mark 16:16 — "he that believeth and is baptized shall be saved; but he that believeth not shall be damned" — and the Johannine cluster at 3:3, 3:18, and 3:36 make faith and the new birth decisive. John 3:18 condemns the unbeliever "already," with deeds never entering the calculus, and 3:36 sets "the wrath of God" abiding on the one who does not believe the Son.

The standard harmonization runs through James 2:17 ("faith without works is dead") and through the Reformed distinction that good works are the necessary evidence and fruit of saving faith rather than its ground. Blomberg makes this move; it is the mainstream evangelical reconciliation. Stated at its strongest, the claim is that the two criteria are one criterion viewed from two angles: genuine faith inevitably produces obedient deeds, so to judge by deeds and to judge by faith yield the same verdict on the same people.

The difficulty is that the deed-criterion passages do not present works as evidence of an underlying faith; they present works as the operative ground. Luke 10:28 attaches no belief-condition whatsoever — the lawyer is told that doing the love commands is itself the path to life. Matthew 25 separates the nations with no reference to belief in Jesus at all; the sheep are surprised to learn whom they served. Conversely, John 3:18 condemns purely on unbelief, with no audit of deeds. Each text presents its criterion as sufficient on its own terms within its own frame. The harmonization works only by importing the absent criterion into passages that pointedly omit it.

Three further considerations sharpen the tension. First, Mark 16:16 — one of the strongest "belief alone" prooftexts — lies inside the Longer Ending (16:9-20), absent from Sinaiticus and Vaticanus and judged by Metzger and the UBS committee to be a second-century addition; the verse was probably not in Mark''s original Gospel. Second, the Johannine emphasis on belief is itself the product of a distinct theological trajectory; Raymond Brown traces the Fourth Gospel''s realized eschatology and faith-centered soteriology to a community development quite separate from the Synoptic strands. Third, E. P. Sanders showed that Second Temple Judaism, the matrix of the deed-language in Matthew and Luke, operated within a covenantal nomism in which obedience and divine grace were not opposed — so the works-language need not be read as crude merit-theology, but neither can it be collapsed into Johannine faith-alone. James D. G. Dunn documents how the faith/works axis was contested across the earliest churches, including within the Pauline mission. The canon preserves competing soteriological streams it never fully reconciled. Verdict: genuine theological contradiction, only superficially dissolved by the faith-produces-works harmonization.', 'Bruce M. Metzger, A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament, 2nd ed. (United Bible Societies, 1994), pp. 102-107, on the secondary status of Mark 16:9-20
Joel Marcus, Mark 8-16 (Anchor Yale Bible 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 1088-1096, on the Longer Ending of Mark
Raymond E. Brown, The Gospel According to John I-XII (Anchor Bible 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 130-141, on John 3 and Johannine soteriology
James D. G. Dunn, The Theology of Paul the Apostle (Eerdmans, 1998), pp. 334-389, on faith and works in early Christianity
E. P. Sanders, Paul and Palestinian Judaism (Fortress, 1977), pp. 511-515, on covenantal nomism and works-language
Craig L. Blomberg, The Historical Reliability of the Gospels, 2nd ed. (IVP Academic, 2007), pp. 156-160, the faith-produces-works harmonization', 0, NULL, 4, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke, John', 5, 3, 3, 3, 1, 'Mark 16:16 sits inside the Longer Ending of Mark (16:9-20), absent from Codex Sinaiticus and Vaticanus and judged a secondary second-century addition.', 'Metzger, A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament, 2nd ed. (1994), pp. 102-107');

Romans 2:13 states the principle plainly: "not the hearers of the law are just (dikaioi) before God, but the doers of the law shall be justified (dikaiōthēsontai)." In context (Romans 2:1-16) Paul is establishing divine impartiality — God judges Jew and Gentile by the same standard of deeds before he reaches the verdict of 3:9-20 that "all are under sin" and 3:23 that "all have sinned." The dominant Reformed harmonization, argued at length by Douglas Moo, reads 2:13 as a true-but-unrealized principle: the Law genuinely justifies its perfect doer, but since no one perfectly does it, the clause functions to drive every reader toward grace. This is exegetically defensible — the future passive dikaiōthēsontai sits inside a courtroom argument whose punch line is universal condemnation. On this reading Romans 2:13 and Galatians 2:21 do not contradict, because both deny that anyone is in fact justified by works of Torah. Galatians 2:21''s logic — "if righteousness comes through the Law, then Christ died for nothing" — is the same coin from the other side: the cross presupposes that the Law could not deliver righteousness. So far the intra-Pauline material coheres, and the entry should concede this.

What does not dissolve is Luke 1:6. Luke describes Zacharias and Elisabeth as "righteous (dikaioi) before God, walking in all the commandments and ordinances of the Lord blameless (amemptoi)." This is not hypothetical and not aspirational. Luke names two specific people and asserts, in the indicative, that they achieved blameless Torah-observance and were righteous before God on that basis. The vocabulary is deliberately Septuagintal — it echoes the description of Noah (Genesis 6:9) and Job (Job 1:1, amemptos) — and situates the couple squarely within the piety of Second Temple Judaism. Joseph Fitzmyer notes that Luke''s infancy narrative is steeped in this Jewish-Christian idiom of covenant faithfulness. Luke simply does not share Paul''s anxiety that Torah-keeping competes with the cross; for Luke, observant Jews can be and are righteous.

The deeper historical point comes from E. P. Sanders and the New Perspective. Sanders argued that Second Temple Judaism was not a religion of works-righteousness but of "covenantal nomism" — one entered the covenant by grace (election) and kept the commandments to remain within it. On this view Romans 2:13 and Luke 1:6 reflect the native Jewish framework, in which doing the Law and being righteous belong together. Paul''s break, as James Dunn and N. T. Wright develop it, was not a critique of Jewish legalism but a Christological inference: because God acted decisively in Christ, the Torah could no longer be the boundary-marker of the people of God. Galatians 2:21 is the conclusion of that inference, not a premise derived from prior reflection on human moral incapacity. Hans Dieter Betz reads 2:21 precisely as the rhetorical climax of Paul''s defense of his law-free gospel.

The upshot: the canon preserves two theological streams. The mature Paul forecloses Torah as a path to righteousness for Christological reasons; Luke, writing within a stream still comfortable with Jewish piety, affirms Torah-righteousness as a present reality. The intra-Pauline statements can be reconciled; the Lukan affirmation and the Pauline foreclosure cannot be fully integrated without flattening one author into the other. Verdict: partially harmonizable — the Pauline material coheres, but Luke and Paul represent unintegrated streams.', 'E. P. Sanders, Paul and Palestinian Judaism (Philadelphia: Fortress, 1977), pp. 442-447, 511-515, on covenantal nomism.
James D. G. Dunn, The Theology of Paul the Apostle (Grand Rapids: Eerdmans, 1998), pp. 334-389, on Galatians 2:21.
N. T. Wright, Paul and the Faithfulness of God (Minneapolis: Fortress, 2013), pp. 925-966.
Douglas J. Moo, The Epistle to the Romans (NICNT; Grand Rapids: Eerdmans, 1996), pp. 139-157, on Romans 2:13 (hypothetical reading).
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, The Gospel According to Luke I-IX (AB 28; Garden City: Doubleday, 1981), pp. 322-324, on Luke 1:6.
Hans Dieter Betz, Galatians (Hermeneia; Philadelphia: Fortress, 1979), pp. 124-126, on Galatians 2:21.', 0, NULL, 4, 'NT_internal', 'Luke, Romans, Galatians', 5, 3, 4, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The oldest harmonization is the levirate hypothesis preserved by Eusebius from Julius Africanus (early third century, Ecclesiastical History 1.7). On this view Joseph had a biological father and a legal father because of a levirate marriage (Deuteronomy 25:5-10): one of Matthan/Matthat died childless, his brother raised up seed in his name, so Joseph could legitimately be reckoned son of both Jacob and Heli. Stated in its strongest form, Africanus has Estha married successively to two half-brothers, Matthan (Solomonic line) and Melchi (Nathanic line), producing Jacob and Heli as uterine half-brothers; Heli dies childless, Jacob performs the levirate duty, and Joseph is Jacob''s biological but Heli''s legal son. The fatal weakness is that this device reconciles only the single generation immediately above Joseph. It does nothing for the forty-odd generations back to David, where the two lists run on entirely separate tracks. Levirate marriage requires the two husbands to share a father, so the lines should converge at that point; Matthew and Luke never converge between David and Joseph. To salvage the scheme one must posit a stacked sequence of levirate marriages down the whole genealogy — the textbook shape of an ad hoc rationalization.

The second classic move, revived by Gleason Archer and many popular apologists, is that Luke gives Mary''s line: Heli was Mary''s father, and Luke names Joseph only as the conventional male head. The Greek does not bear this. Luke 3:23 reads tou Iōsēph tou Ēli — "of Joseph, of Heli" — with the same genitive construction (tou + name) running unbroken through the entire list. To make Heli Mary''s father one must read the construction differently for this one link than for all the others, and supply a "son-in-law" sense the text never states. Luke has already framed the list around Joseph ("as was supposed the son of Joseph"), and Luke elsewhere shows no interest in tracing Mary''s ancestry. The Marian reading is not attested before the Renaissance and arose precisely to solve this problem.

The critical consensus, set out by Raymond Brown in The Birth of the Messiah and supported by Davies-Allison and Fitzmyer, is that the two genealogies are independent theological constructions rather than competing archival records. No one in first-century Galilee possessed documented descent from David spanning a millennium for an artisan''s family; such records did not exist, and post-exilic genealogical data is fragmentary even for priestly lines. Matthew builds a royal, Solomonic, throne-of-David genealogy to present Jesus as legitimate Davidic king; Luke runs the line back past David through Adam to God to present Jesus as the universal son of God and savior of all humanity. Each list serves its evangelist''s Christology and was never designed to dovetail with the other. Verdict: genuine contradiction.', 'Raymond E. Brown, The Birth of the Messiah, updated ed. (ABRL; New York: Doubleday, 1993), pp. 57-95, with the levirate-marriage discussion at pp. 503-504, on Matthew 1:16 and Luke 3:23.
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew, Vol. 1 (ICC; Edinburgh: T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 165-188, on Matthew 1:1-17.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, The Gospel According to Luke I-IX (AB 28; Garden City: Doubleday, 1981), pp. 488-498, on Luke 3:23-38.
Marcus J. Borg and John Dominic Crossan, The First Christmas (New York: HarperOne, 2007), pp. 95-108.
Gleason L. Archer, Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties (Grand Rapids: Zondervan, 1982), pp. 316-318, on the Marian-genealogy harmonization.
Eusebius, Ecclesiastical History 1.7, preserving the letter of Julius Africanus on the levirate harmonization of the two genealogies.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Luke', 3, 3, 4, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The Matthean fourteen-fourteen-fourteen scheme is demonstrably an artificial construction rather than a transcript of records. To reach fourteen in the second tetradecad, Matthew omits three Judahite kings the Old Testament records — Ahaziah, Joash (Jehoash), and Amaziah — between Joram and Uzziah/Azariah (compare 1 Chronicles 3:11-12; 2 Kings 8-14). He also leans on counting Jeconiah at the close of the second set and the head of the third to make the numbers balance, which is why the third group, strictly counted, falls one short. As Brown and Davies-Allison observe, the organizing number fourteen is almost certainly the gematria of David''s name: in Hebrew DWD (dalet-waw-dalet) sums to 4+6+4 = 14, so the entire genealogy is a numerical hymn to the son of David. Marshall Johnson''s study of the function of biblical genealogies shows that such lists were routinely shaped for theological and mnemonic ends, not preserved as neutral demographic data.

The standard apologetic response invokes telescoping: ancient Semitic genealogies freely skipped generations, so "begat" can mean "was the ancestor of," and a twenty-seven-name list and a forty-two-name list can both be "true" at different levels of compression. The principle is sound in general — Matthew''s own omission of three kings proves genealogies were abridged, and Ezra 7:1-5 famously telescopes the priestly line. But telescoping cannot reconcile these two lists, because telescoping is selective preservation: a compressed list keeps a subset of the same names. Matthew and Luke do not share a subset; between David and Joseph they share virtually no names at all (the apparent Shealtiel-Zerubbabel overlap aside, and even there the neighboring names differ). One cannot be a compressed version of the other when the underlying name-sets are disjoint. They are two distinct genealogical traditions running through two distinct sons of David, so the count gap reflects different routes, not different degrees of abbreviation.

The economical explanation is the same as for entry 503. Neither evangelist had access to a continuous thousand-year pedigree for a Galilean artisan''s family, because no such archive existed. Each constructed a genealogy to carry his Christology — Matthew the royal-Davidic schema numerically keyed to David''s name, Luke the universal-Adamic descent reaching back to God — and the divergent totals are the predictable result of two independent constructions that were never meant to be summed against each other. Verdict: genuine contradiction, rooted in two independently composed and theologically driven genealogies.', 'Raymond E. Brown, The Birth of the Messiah, updated ed. (ABRL; New York: Doubleday, 1993), pp. 69-84, on Matthew''s numerological structure.
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew, Vol. 1 (ICC; Edinburgh: T&T Clark, 1988), pp. 161-165, 184-188, on Matthew 1:1-17.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, The Gospel According to Luke I-IX (AB 28; Garden City: Doubleday, 1981), pp. 488-505, on Luke 3:23-38.
Marshall D. Johnson, The Purpose of the Biblical Genealogies, 2nd ed. (SNTSMS 8; Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1988), pp. 139-228.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Luke', 3, 3, 4, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The third SAB answer — "Or was it Mary Magdalene?" — invokes a conflation that is historically traceable and textually baseless. It stems from Pope Gregory the Great''s Homily 33 (591 CE), which fused the sinful woman of Luke 7, Mary of Bethany, and Mary Magdalene into a single penitent prostitute. Nothing in any of the four anointing pericopes links the woman to Mary Magdalene. Luke in fact introduces Mary Magdalene afresh in the very next pericope (Luke 8:2) as one "out of whom went seven devils," with no hint that she is the woman of 7:36-50; the juxtaposition tells against, not for, the identification. Jane Schaberg traces how the composite "Magdalene" image was built in Western tradition and dismantled by modern scholarship (the Catholic calendar itself dropped the identification in 1969).

The strongest apologetic harmonization, defended by Craig Blomberg, distinguishes the events: Luke 7 is one anointing (Galilee, early, a sinful woman), and Mark/Matthew/John report another (Bethany, late, Mary). On this view the shared motif of wiping the feet with the hair is simply a repeated act, and there is no contradiction because different women are described. The distinction between Luke 7 and the Bethany event is genuinely defensible and resolves the bare question. What remains strained is the duplication of so distinctive a gesture — a woman loosening her hair (itself a scandalous act in that culture) to wipe Jesus'' feet, with costly ointment — occurring independently on two occasions, with one of those women being the same Mary of Bethany John ties to the Lazarus story.

The critical reconstruction, set out by Raymond Brown and Fitzmyer, is that Luke and John draw on a common anointing tradition that each evangelist has redacted. The hair-and-feet detail, absent from Mark/Matthew, appears in both Luke 7 and John 12 precisely because they share a source distinct from the Markan head-anointing. Luke relocated the episode early and recast the woman as a penitent sinner to serve his recurring Sondergut theme of Jesus and the marginalized; John kept the Bethany setting of Mark but moved the anointing to the feet and named Mary to bind it to the Lazarus narrative and to anticipate the foot-washing of John 13. The residue is two accounts that agree on a vivid gesture but disagree on the woman, the place, and the time. Verdict: the bare question is harmonizable by distinguishing events, but the Magdalene identification is a later conflation and the underlying data points to one tradition reworked.', 'Raymond E. Brown, The Gospel According to John I-XII (AB 29; Garden City: Doubleday, 1966), pp. 449-454, on the anointing traditions.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, The Gospel According to Luke I-IX (AB 28; Garden City: Doubleday, 1981), pp. 684-694, on Luke 7:36-50.
Joel Marcus, Mark 8-16 (AYB 27A; New Haven: Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 928-937, on Mark 14:3-9.
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew, Vol. 3 (ICC; Edinburgh: T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 442-450, on Matthew 26:6-13.
Jane Schaberg, The Resurrection of Mary Magdalene (New York: Continuum, 2002), pp. 65-94, on the Magdalene conflation.
Craig L. Blomberg, The Historical Reliability of the Gospels, 2nd ed. (Downers Grove: IVP Academic, 2007), pp. 184-187 (two-events reading).
Gregory the Great, Homily 33 (Forty Gospel Homilies, 591 CE), the source of the Western conflation of the sinful woman, Mary of Bethany, and Mary Magdalene.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Luke, John', 10, 2, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The residue that this does not settle is that each scene is more than an isolated datum — it is a representative window onto how its Gospel construes the Samaritan question, and the two constructions pull in opposite directions. Luke''s overall portrait is strikingly pro-Samaritan: the parable of the Good Samaritan (Luke 10:25-37) makes a Samaritan the moral exemplar, and the cleansed Samaritan leper alone returns to give thanks (Luke 17:11-19). Yet Luke opens the journey to Jerusalem (the great central section, 9:51-19:27) precisely with Samaritan rejection, and Jesus rebukes James and John for wishing to call down fire (9:54-55). Fitzmyer reads this as Lukan staging: the rejection sets the journey''s tone and is immediately countered by Jesus'' refusal to retaliate, framing the mission''s eventual reach to Samaria. John 4 is even more emphatic in the other direction. Jesus crosses ethnic and religious boundaries to hold an extended theological dialogue with a Samaritan woman, discloses his messianic identity to her ("I that speak unto thee am he," John 4:26), and the townspeople conclude that he is "the Saviour of the world" (John 4:42) — a universalizing confession Brown identifies as a Johannine theological climax.

The historical background, documented by Reinhard Pummer, makes Luke''s hostile village wholly plausible. Samaritans rejected the centrality of the Jerusalem temple and revered Mount Gerizim as the true sanctuary (the very dispute the Samaritan woman raises in John 4:20); Jewish pilgrims traveling through Samaria toward Jerusalem were natural targets of friction, and Josephus records violent clashes along that pilgrim route (Antiquities 20.118-136). So Luke 9:52-53 draws on a realistic tradition of Samaritan resistance to Jerusalem-bound Jews. John 4, by contrast, draws on a tradition reflecting the early Christian mission to Samaria — the same mission narrated in Acts 8:4-25, where Philip evangelizes a Samaritan city with great success. Craig Keener situates the Johannine Sychar episode within that missionary memory.

In sum, the literal contradiction dissolves once one notes the different localities, but the deeper tension is real at the level of theological framing: Luke and John remember and deploy the Samaritan encounter from different communal vantage points and for different rhetorical ends, and those portraits are not simply additive. Verdict: harmonizable as discrete events, but the divergent framings reflect distinct redactional agendas.', 'Raymond E. Brown, The Gospel According to John I-XII (AB 29; Garden City: Doubleday, 1966), pp. 166-185, on John 4.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, The Gospel According to Luke I-IX (AB 28; Garden City: Doubleday, 1981), pp. 825-829, on Luke 9:51-56.
Reinhard Pummer, The Samaritans: A Profile (Grand Rapids: Eerdmans, 2016), pp. 1-25, 153-178.
Craig S. Keener, The Gospel of John: A Commentary, Vol. 1 (Peabody: Hendrickson, 2003), pp. 584-635, on John 4.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Luke, John', 7, 2, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

Everything depends on menounge. The particle is not a flat negation; it is corrective-affirmative, used to intensify or redirect rather than to deny (compare Romans 9:20, 10:18, Philippians 3:8, where it carries "indeed/rather"). Jesus does not say Mary is not blessed; he relocates the basis of blessing away from physical kinship toward a quality of discipleship available to anyone. The contrast is also lexical: the woman uses the verbal makaria-language of bodily relation; Jesus answers with the plural makarioi defined by hearing and keeping. Luke composed both passages, which is strong internal evidence that he perceived no contradiction. Indeed Luke has already armed the reader to harmonize: in 1:38 Mary answers "be it unto me according to thy word," and in 1:45 Elizabeth blesses her precisely "for [she] believed" — Mary is presented as the model hearer-and-keeper. On Luke''s own terms Mary is blessed on both counts, womb and faith.

The strongest harmonization is therefore not apologetic special pleading but Lukan exegesis itself, and the Catholic and Orthodox traditions have developed it rigorously: Mary is the supreme disciple, blessed in the manner Jesus commends. Augustine''s much-cited formulation — that Mary was more blessed in conceiving Christ''s faith than in conceiving Christ''s flesh — captures the move exactly. Brown, Donfried, Fitzmyer, and Reumann, in the ecumenical volume *Mary in the New Testament*, conclude that Luke 11:27-28 does not disparage Mary but subordinates the physical to the spiritual, with Mary herself the bridge between the two.

What remains, and what the entry rightly flags, is a redactional seam. Luke inherits from the wider tradition a recurrent saying that de-centers biological family: Luke 8:19-21 ("My mother and my brethren are these which hear the word of God, and do it"), paralleling the sharper Markan 3:31-35, where Jesus'' kin stand "without" and are implicitly contrasted with the circle seated around him. The sayings tradition consistently relativizes kinship in favor of obedience-based belonging to the new family of God. Luke''s infancy material pulls the other way, exalting Mary''s maternal role. Luke reconciles the two by making Mary exemplify the obedience the sayings demand — but the fact that the reconciliation requires effort, and that Jesus'' words in 11:28 explicitly shift the ground from "the womb" to discipleship, shows the two strata were not originally cut from the same cloth. The redaction holds; the seam is visible. Verdict: harmonizable within Luke''s own theology, with a real underlying redactional tension between infancy and sayings traditions.', 'Raymond E. Brown, Karl P. Donfried, Joseph A. Fitzmyer, and John Reumann, eds., *Mary in the New Testament* (Fortress / Paulist, 1978), pp. 105-177, on Luke''s treatment of Mary.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (Anchor Bible 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 925-928, on Luke 11:27-28.
Raymond E. Brown, *The Birth of the Messiah*, updated ed. (Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, 1993), pp. 316-329, on Luke 1:26-38.
John Nolland, *Luke 9:21-18:34* (Word Biblical Commentary 35B; Word, 1993), pp. 654-657, on Luke 11:27-28.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Luke', 7, 2, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

James 3:15-17 operates on an entirely different axis. James is not asking who is the better strategist; he is distinguishing two kinds of sophia. "Earthly, sensual (psychikē), devilish" wisdom is marked by "bitter envying and strife" and produces "confusion and every evil work"; the wisdom "from above" is "first pure, then peaceable, gentle, and easy to be intreated, full of mercy and good fruits." This is a moral-spiritual taxonomy of character, squarely within the Hellenistic-Jewish wisdom tradition that James everywhere channels — the catalogue of virtues recalls Sirach and the Wisdom of Solomon, and the false-versus-true-wisdom contrast is the standard wisdom-versus-folly distinction. James pointedly does not concede that earthly wisdom is genuinely wise; he says it merely passes for wisdom while being, at root, folly.

The two texts therefore use the wisdom-vocabulary in different senses — instrumental cleverness (phronēsis) in Luke, moral-spiritual character (sophia) in James — and a charge of contradiction here is a textbook equivocation. Fitzmyer''s Anchor Bible treatment of Luke 16:1-13 makes the rhetorical-not-categorical reading explicit, stressing that Jesus commends prudence, not the steward''s ethics. Luke Timothy Johnson and Dale Allison, on James 3, situate the passage in the wisdom tradition and underscore that James is contrasting types, not strategists. There is no plausible reading on which Luke''s Jesus claims worldly people possess superior moral or salvific wisdom, nor on which James claims the godly are better at securing worldly advantage. The populist appeal of the contradiction is real — a surface reading of the King James does pit "wiser" against "wiser" — but the appeal evaporates once the two senses are distinguished. Verdict: overstated; a lexical equivocation, not a contradiction.', 'Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (Anchor Bible 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1094-1111, on Luke 16:1-13.
John Nolland, *Luke 9:21-18:34* (Word Biblical Commentary 35B; Word, 1993), pp. 793-810, on Luke 16:1-13.
Luke Timothy Johnson, *The Letter of James* (Anchor Bible 37A; Doubleday, 1995), pp. 268-275, on James 3:13-18.
Dale C. Allison, *The Epistle of James* (International Critical Commentary; Bloomsbury T&T Clark, 2013), pp. 568-589, on James 3:13-18.', 0, NULL, 5, 'NT_internal', 'Luke, James', 6, 2, 1, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

The two leading harmonizations are ingenious but ad hoc. Gleason Archer proposes three blind men in two separate encounters — one as Jesus approached (Luke), two as he left (Matthew/Mark) — with each evangelist reporting a partial slice. The difficulty is that all three narrate what reads as the same single episode, with the same cry ("thou son of David, have mercy"), the same roadside setting, and the same healing, and none signals a separate occasion. Craig Blomberg''s alternative invokes archaeology: Herodian Jericho lay roughly a mile from the old Jewish/Tell es-Sultan site, so Jesus could be "leaving" one Jericho while "approaching" the other, with the healing in between. This is geographically real — there were indeed two adjacent Jerichos — but the gospels show no awareness of a two-city distinction, and the move still leaves the number discrepancy (one vs. two) untouched.

The redactional account is far more economical and explains both divergences at once. First, the number: Matthew demonstrably has a habit of doubling Markan figures. He turns Mark''s single Gerasene demoniac (Mark 5:2) into two (Matthew 8:28), and in the very next pericope after this one he reads Zechariah 9:9 to produce two animals at the triumphal entry (Matthew 21:2-7) where Mark has one colt (Mark 11:2). The doubling of one blind man into two fits this established Matthean tendency. Second, the geography: Luke relocates the healing because of what he places immediately after it. Luke 19:1-10 narrates Zacchaeus *inside* Jericho; for that scene to follow, Jesus must still be entering the city when the blind man is healed, so Luke shifts the cure to the approach. Luz''s Hermeneia commentary documents the Matthean doubling pattern, and Marcus''s Anchor Yale commentary establishes Markan priority and Mark''s place as the source both later evangelists redacted.

The harmonizations require either an unlikely three-beggar coincidence or a twin-Jericho shell game that still cannot account for the doubled number. The redactional explanation accounts for every difference — number and direction — from known compositional habits of Matthew and Luke working on a shared Markan source. Verdict: genuine contradiction at the narrative level, best explained as Synoptic redaction of a single Markan story.', 'Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16* (Anchor Yale Bible 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 757-770, on Mark 10:46-52.
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 8-20* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2001), pp. 549-555, on Matthew 20:29-34.
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 3 (International Critical Commentary; T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 102-110, on Matthew 20:29-34.
John Nolland, *Luke 18:35-24:53* (Word Biblical Commentary 35C; Word, 1993), pp. 894-901, on Luke 18:35-43.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 333-334, on the three-blind-men harmonization.
Craig L. Blomberg, *The Historical Reliability of the Gospels*, 2nd ed. (IVP Academic, 2007), pp. 181-183, on the twin-Jerichos harmonization.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, Luke', 10, 2, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The most serious harmonization, with deep patristic roots and adopted by modern apologists such as Norman Geisler, distinguishes the destinations. "Paradise" in this scheme is an intermediate abode of the righteous dead — frequently identified with the "Abraham''s bosom" of Luke 16:22-23 and connected to the realm where 1 Peter 3:19 has Christ preach "to the spirits in prison" — distinct from the heavenly throne room of the Father to which the ascension (John 20:17; Acts 1:9-11) leads. On this reading Jesus could be in the intermediate paradise Friday and still not have ascended to the Father by Sunday. The view has internal coherence and real second-Temple precedent: paradeisos (a Persian loanword for a walled garden, used in the LXX for Eden) does denote an abode of the blessed in texts like 1 Enoch and the Testament of Levi, and the geography of the afterlife in early Judaism was genuinely layered. This is why the entry is best rated disputed rather than flatly unresolved: the distinction is not invented out of nothing.

But it is not in Luke or John either. Luke does not present paradise as a way-station preceding ascension; Acts 1 simply has Jesus ascend after the resurrection appearances, with no intervening cosmology mapped. The harmonization reads a multi-tiered itinerary into texts that do not develop one, and it must coordinate Luke, John, 1 Peter, and intertestamental sources into a synthetic scheme none of the authors states.

A second, weaker apologetic move repunctuates Luke 23:43 — since the earliest manuscripts lack punctuation — to read "Verily I say unto thee today, shalt thou be with me in paradise," making "today" modify the act of speaking rather than the arrival. Grammatically the comma can fall either way, but the move is stylistically alien to Luke: his fixed introductory formula is amēn legō soi/hymin ("verily I say unto thee/you"), which appears repeatedly across the Gospel as a self-contained unit and never elsewhere takes an appended "today." Inserting sēmeron into the formula is unparalleled in Lukan usage, whereas "today" attaches naturally and emphatically to the promise (Luke is fond of sēmeron — see 2:11, 4:21, 19:9). The repositioned comma solves the problem only by rewriting Luke''s idiom.

Fitzmyer reads Luke 23:43 at face value — the criminal will be with Jesus in paradise that very day — and Brown concurs in *The Death of the Messiah*. Brown''s John commentary, meanwhile, treats John 20:17''s "not yet ascended" as integral to John''s distinctive theology of glorification, in which cross, resurrection, and ascension form a unified movement to the Father not yet complete on Easter morning. The two evangelists are working out different post-mortem itineraries, and reconciling them requires a harmonizing cosmology external to both. Verdict: a real tension; harmonizable only by importing an intermediate-state scheme the texts do not articulate — hence disputed.', 'Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (Anchor Bible 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1507-1511, on Luke 23:39-43.
Raymond E. Brown, *The Death of the Messiah*, Vol. 2 (Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, 1994), pp. 1000-1013, on the criminals at the cross.
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John XIII-XXI* (Anchor Bible 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 992-994, 1011-1017, on John 20:17.
Joel B. Green, *The Gospel of Luke* (New International Commentary on the New Testament; Eerdmans, 1997), pp. 820-823, on Luke 23:39-43.
Norman L. Geisler, *When Critics Ask* (Victor, 1992), pp. 415-416, on the intermediate-state harmonization.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Luke, John', 5, 4, 3, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard harmonization, advanced by Gleason Archer among others, posits two distinct satanic entries: an initial entry that planted the betrayal scheme (Luke 22:3) and a second, sealing entry at the Supper (John 13:27). The case for it leans on John 13:2, which notes that before the meal "the devil [had] already put into the heart of Judas Iscariot... to betray him" — suggesting a diabolical influence preceding the climactic entry of 13:27. That is the strongest form of the argument, and it deserves to be stated fairly: John himself appears to distinguish an earlier prompting (13:2) from the entry proper (13:27). But the harmonization runs into the texts'' own language. Luke 22:3 narrates a single, complete entry with no hint that a further entry is to come; John 13:27 narrates a single entry with no "again." And crucially, the move requires coordinating Luke''s pre-Supper entry with John''s pre-Supper prompting and then John''s at-Supper entry into a three-beat sequence that neither evangelist actually narrates. Luke knows nothing of a second entry; John''s 13:2 is a prompting of "the devil," not a narrated "entry," and is its own clause distinct from the entry in 13:27.

The more economical reading, set out in Brown''s *Death of the Messiah* and Fitzmyer''s Anchor commentary on Luke, is that Luke and John independently deployed the same theological tool to solve the same problem. Early Christianity faced an acute difficulty: how could one of the Twelve, chosen by Jesus, betray him? Attributing the act to satanic agency removed the scandal from Jesus'' judgment and from human freedom alike, and both evangelists reached for it. Luke front-loads the possession so that Judas''s entire trajectory toward betrayal is demonically driven from the outset, integrating it with Luke''s framing of the passion as a cosmic contest (note Luke 22:53, "this is your hour, and the power of darkness"). John locates it at the Supper because John builds the meal into the dramatic hinge of the narrative: the morsel marks Judas, Satan enters, Judas exits "into the night," and the hour of glorification begins (13:31). Each placement serves the evangelist''s distinct theology, which is precisely why they differ.

The two-entries harmonization is the characteristic apologetic move of multiplying events to dissolve a discrepancy, and it founders on the singular, definitive way each text narrates the entry. Verdict: genuine contradiction in placement, best explained as independent redactional use of a shared Judas-and-Satan tradition.', 'Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (Anchor Bible 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1373-1376, on Luke 22:3-6.
Raymond E. Brown, *The Death of the Messiah* (Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, 1994), Vol. 1, pp. 734-761, and Vol. 2, pp. 1394-1418, on the Judas traditions.
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John XIII-XXI* (Anchor Bible 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 571-580, on John 13:21-30.
John Nolland, *Luke 18:35-24:53* (Word Biblical Commentary 35C; Word, 1993), pp. 1029-1033, on Luke 22:3-7.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 343-344, on the two-entries harmonization.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Luke, John', 2, 2, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

1 Corinthians 6:9-11 is a vice-catalogue, a recognizable Greco-Roman and Hellenistic-Jewish rhetorical form (compare 1 Cor 5:10-11, Gal 5:19-21, Rom 1:29-31, and the Stoic and Philonic lists Conzelmann documents). Such catalogues function paraenetically — they warn a community against backsliding by naming the conduct that marks those outside the kingdom. Paul''s grammar is the hinge. In 6:11 he writes kai tauta tines ete — ''and such things WERE some of you'' — using the imperfect, then three aorist verbs in the passive: apelousasthe, hegiasthete, edikaiothete (''you were washed, you were sanctified, you were justified''). The aorists mark a decisive past event (baptismal conversion) that severs the Corinthians from their former identity. Paul''s whole point is that people who once belonged to those categories now do not. He is not excluding the repentant; he is excluding the unrepentant who continue in the conduct.

The repentant criminal of Luke 23 is the paradigm case of exactly what 6:11 describes, telescoped into a single deathbed moment. Luke''s Gospel makes repentance and last-minute reversal a structural theme — the prodigal (15:11-32), Zacchaeus (19:1-10), the tax collector justified over the Pharisee (18:9-14). Fitzmyer reads the crucified criminal as the climactic Lukan example of salvation extended to the marginal at the eleventh hour. The man confesses Jesus'' innocence, acknowledges his own guilt, and appeals to Jesus'' coming reign; Jesus answers with the only use of paradeisos in the Gospels. This is conversion, not an exception to Paul''s rule.

Steel-manned, the skeptical case rests entirely on reading 6:9-10 in isolation from 6:11. Verses 9-10 do read as a flat categorical exclusion — ''neither... thieves... shall inherit the kingdom of God'' — and a reader who stops there has a surface contradiction with Luke 23. But the exclusion clause and the rehabilitation clause are a single sentence-unit in Paul''s argument; they cannot be severed without distorting the syntax. Thiselton and Fitzmyer both treat 6:9-11 as one indivisible movement: warning, then assurance. Critical scholarship accordingly does not regard this as a genuine theological contradiction. The two authors share, rather than dispute, the premise that the kingdom is closed to the unrighteous and open to the converted. Luke dramatizes the conversion; Paul states the principle. The only ''contradiction'' is the artifact of quoting verse 10 and not verse 11.

It is worth noting the residual editorial point: Paul and Luke write from different theological idioms — Paul in forensic-baptismal categories, Luke in narrative-soteriological ones — but they are mutually intelligible and non-contradictory. Verdict: overstated; harmonizable on the plain reading of one additional verse.', 'Anthony C. Thiselton, *The First Epistle to the Corinthians* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 2000), pp. 436-453, on 1 Corinthians 6:9-11.
Hans Conzelmann, *1 Corinthians* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1975), pp. 105-108, on 1 Corinthians 6:9-11.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *First Corinthians* (Anchor Yale Bible 32; Yale University Press, 2008), pp. 256-265, on 1 Corinthians 6:9-11.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (Anchor Bible 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1500-1511, on Luke 23:32-43.', 0, NULL, 4, 'NT_internal', 'Mark, Luke, 1 Corinthians', 5, 3, 3, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

The first offer comes before the crucifixion. Mark 15:23 reports oinos esmyrnismenos, ''wine mingled with myrrh''; Matthew 27:34 alters this to oxos meta choles, ''vinegar/sour wine mixed with gall.'' Mark''s myrrhed wine fits a known custom of providing an analgesic to the condemned; the Babylonian Talmud (b. Sanhedrin 43a) preserves a tradition that a stupefying drink was given to those led out to execution. Jesus refuses it in both Gospels — in Mark flatly (''he received it not''), in Matthew after tasting (''he would not drink''). Matthew''s substitution of ''gall'' is almost certainly an assimilation to Psalm 69:21 LXX (''they gave me gall for my food''), a redactional move Davies and Allison document. The refusal carries theological weight: Jesus declines to dull his suffering.

The second offer comes near the moment of death. Mark 15:36 and Matthew 27:48 describe a bystander filling a sponge with oxos — the cheap sour wine (posca) of Roman soldiers, not the same drink as the first offer — and lifting it on a reed to Jesus'' lips. Crucially, the Synoptics do not say whether Jesus drank this. John 19:28-30 narrates only this second offer, prefaced by Jesus'' cry ''I thirst'' (dipso), and states that Jesus ''received'' (elaben) the sour wine before declaring ''It is finished.''

The two-offer harmonization, defended by Carson, Morris, and Blomberg, is textually sound: the Synoptic refusal applies to the analgesic, the Johannine acceptance to the soldiers'' posca, and there is no formal contradiction. Where the harmonization thins is the precise SAB question. Only John makes the positive assertion that Jesus drank; the Synoptics are simply silent on whether he consumed the second offering. So the cleanest statement is that the Synoptics record a refused first drink and an unresolved second drink, while John records an accepted second drink. The conflict arises only if one collapses both Synoptic offers into a single ''no.''

The more significant critical observation, developed at length in Brown''s Death of the Messiah, is redactional rather than chronological. John has visibly reshaped the second offer to fulfill Psalm 69:21 explicitly. He adds the ''I thirst'' saying (dipso), which has no Synoptic parallel, and he introduces a botanical oddity — the sour wine is raised on hyssop (hyssopos) rather than the reed (kalamos) of Mark and Matthew. Hyssop is a small, floppy plant ill-suited to lifting a sponge to a crucified man''s mouth; many scholars suspect either a deliberate Passover allusion (hyssop applied the Passover blood in Exod 12:22, and John frames Jesus as the Passover lamb) or a textual corruption of hysso (''on a javelin''), a conjecture noted in the apparatus. Either way, John''s scene is theologically elaborated to present Jesus as consciously fulfilling Scripture: ''that the scripture might be fulfilled, [he] saith, I thirst'' (19:28).

Steel-manned, the apologetic case is strong on the surface contradiction and weak on the redactional one: it correctly shows that no verse states both that Jesus drank and that he refused the *same* offer, but it does not explain John''s distinctive ''I thirst,'' the hyssop, and the explicit fulfillment formula, which mark John''s account as a deliberate theological composition rather than independent corroboration. Verdict: harmonizable as to the bare facts (two offers); the residual issue is Johannine theological elaboration, not factual contradiction.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Death of the Messiah*, Vol. 2 (Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, 1994), pp. 940-952, 1075-1080, on the wine/vinegar offers.
Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16* (Anchor Yale Bible 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 1043-1046, 1062-1065, on Mark 15:23, 15:36.
