Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John XIII-XXI* (Anchor Bible 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 908-910, 928-931, on John 19:28-30.
D. A. Carson, *The Gospel According to John* (PNTC; Eerdmans, 1991), pp. 619-621, on the two-offers harmonization.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark, John', 10, 2, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

Once those passages carry the weight, the destinations are narrower and stranger than ''hell.'' The Greek of 1 Peter 3:19 does not use hades or geenna; it says the spirits are en phylake — ''in prison.'' The standard critical identification, following the trajectory of 1 Enoch and shared by Jude 6 and 2 Peter 2:4, is that these ''spirits in prison'' are the disobedient angels (the ''sons of God'' of Genesis 6:1-4, the Watchers) who were bound for their transgression before the Flood — note that 3:20 specifies they were disobedient ''in the days of Noah.'' Jesus'' proclamation to them is thus set in a specifically Jewish-apocalyptic cosmology of imprisoned rebel spirits, not the punitive hell of medieval Christianity. Achtemeier and Elliott both read 3:19 against this Enochic background; whether the ''preaching'' is a proclamation of triumph or an offer of salvation is debated, but the locale is the Watchers'' prison.

Luke''s destination is equally technical. Paradeisos is a Persian loanword (pairidaeza, ''enclosed garden'') that the LXX uses for Eden and that Second Temple literature applies to the heavenly dwelling of the righteous dead — compare 2 Corinthians 12:4 (Paul caught up ''into paradise''), Revelation 2:7, and the locations described in 1 Enoch and 2 Enoch. So Luke 23:43 places Jesus (and the criminal) in the garden-abode of the righteous.

The harmonizing tradition is venerable and internally coherent: Augustine and Aquinas worked out itineraries in which the souls of the dead inhabited distinct compartments (the ''limbo of the fathers,'' the place of the righteous, the prison of the damned), so that Christ between death and resurrection could visit more than one locale — preaching liberation to some, proclaiming judgment to others. On that scheme Luke''s paradeisos and 1 Peter''s phylake are simply two stops on one post-mortem journey, and there is no contradiction. The weakness is that this synthesis is a later theological construction; neither Luke nor 1 Peter shows any awareness of the other''s geography or of a multi-stage itinerary. The texts are independent traditions, each using a different apocalyptic motif for a different purpose: Luke deploys paradise to dramatize individual salvation through the cross, while 1 Peter deploys the descent-to-the-Watchers to assert the cosmic scope of Christ''s victory over hostile powers.

Steel-manned, the harmonization is logically possible and was historically influential, which is why this is rated partially rather than not harmonized. But it succeeds only by superimposing a synthetic later schema on two authors who never coordinated their claims; and it cannot rehabilitate the SAB''s ''hell,'' since the Petrine destination is the phylake of the Watchers, not a place of human damnation. Verdict: the surface ''heaven vs. hell'' contradiction is overstated/misframed; what remains is two independent apocalyptic traditions, reconcilable only via a later harmonizing scheme.', 'Paul J. Achtemeier, *1 Peter* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1996), pp. 252-262, 286-291, on 1 Peter 3:18-20 and 4:6.
John H. Elliott, *1 Peter* (Anchor Bible 37B; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 638-710, on 1 Peter 3:18-20.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (Anchor Bible 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1507-1511, on Luke 23:43.
Justo L. Gonzalez, *A History of Christian Thought*, Vol. 1 (Abingdon, 1987), pp. 173-176, on the descent tradition.
Alan E. Bernstein, *The Formation of Hell* (Cornell University Press, 1993), pp. 233-275.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Luke, 1 Peter', 5, 4, 3, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The principal harmonization, argued in detail by F. F. Bruce, is that Luke 24:50-51 is a compressed narrative summary that contains no explicit time-marker fixing the ascension to Easter day; the gap between 24:43 and 24:50 can silently accommodate the forty days that Acts 1:3 supplies. This is textually possible — Luke does telescope — but it requires the reader to import the Acts chronology into a Gospel narrative whose connective tissue (''that same day,'' ''the same hour'') runs the other way. The compression reading rescues the timeline only by overriding Luke''s own continuity markers.

John 20:26 is a harder obstacle. It places a post-resurrection appearance ''after eight days,'' when Jesus shows Thomas his wounds — still in the pre-ascension, bodily-appearing state more than a week after Easter. Any reading that compresses the ascension into resurrection day collapses against this verse. For that reason most apologists abandon the single-day reading of Luke 24 and adopt Acts 1:3''s forty days as the master schema, treating Luke 24, John 20, and Acts 13:31 (''seen many days'') as compatible with a forty-day window. That move resolves John 20:26 but concedes that Luke 24 as written does not say what the harmonized chronology needs it to say.

The Mark 16:19 datum is textually compromised at the source. It belongs to the Longer Ending of Mark (16:9-20), which Metzger and the critical-text consensus judge a second-century addition: it is absent from the two oldest complete uncials, Codex Sinaiticus and Codex Vaticanus, is marked or omitted in other witnesses, and contains non-Markan vocabulary. One of the four ascension timelines therefore derives from a secondary, non-original text and carries no weight as independent Markan testimony.

The critical-scholarly explanation, developed by Mikeal Parsons in The Departure of Jesus in Luke-Acts, is redactional. Luke faced a compositional problem: he wanted the ascension to serve as the climactic closure of his Gospel (Luke 24) and also as the hinge opening Acts and leading to Pentecost (Acts 1). He narrated it twice, shaped to the rhetorical needs of each volume — terse and conclusive at the end of the Gospel, expanded with the forty-day instruction period at the start of Acts. The two accounts do not represent two independent memories of when the event occurred; they represent one author deploying the same event differently. The ''forty days'' is most plausibly a theological number: forty evokes Moses'' forty days on Sinai (Exod 24:18; 34:28) and Elijah''s forty days to Horeb (1 Kings 19:8), framing the resurrection appearances as a period of authoritative instruction rather than supplying a chronological datum.

Steel-manned, the apologetic case is at its strongest in noting that Luke 24 contains no sentence explicitly dating the ascension to Easter day, so the texts are not strictly self-contradicting word-for-word. But that defense survives only by reading Luke 24 against its own narrative flow and by privileging Acts 1:3 as the controlling text. The most economical account of the data is that the timelines diverge because Luke wrote the ascension twice for two different purposes, and that the Markan witness is a later interpolation. Verdict: genuine contradiction, rooted in Lukan compositional priorities and compounded by a secondary Markan ending.', 'Mikeal C. Parsons, *The Departure of Jesus in Luke-Acts: The Ascension Narratives in Context* (JSNTSup 21; JSOT Press, 1987), pp. 113-200.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Acts of the Apostles* (Anchor Bible 31; Doubleday, 1998), pp. 199-213, on Acts 1:1-11.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (Anchor Bible 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1586-1593, on Luke 24:50-53.
Bruce M. Metzger, *A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament*, 2nd ed. (Deutsche Bibelgesellschaft / United Bible Societies, 1994), pp. 102-107, on Mark 16:9-20.
Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16* (Anchor Yale Bible 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 1088-1096.
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John XIII-XXI* (Anchor Bible 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 1019-1049, on John 20:19-29.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Mark, Luke, John, Acts', 2, 3, 4, 1, 1, 'Mark 16:19 is part of the Longer Ending of Mark (16:9-20), absent from the earliest manuscripts (Sinaiticus, Vaticanus) and regarded as a secondary addition.', 'Metzger, A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament, 2nd ed. (1994), pp. 102-107');

The standard apologetic harmonization, advanced by Norman Geisler among others, is sequential: the disciples were first ''terrified and affrighted, and supposed that they had seen a spirit'' (Luke 24:37), and then, after Jesus calmed them, showed his hands and feet, and offered proof of his corporeality, they became ''glad'' (John 20:20). On this reading the two emotions are not contradictory but consecutive — stages in a single arc from fear to joy. The reading has genuine textual support within Luke itself: Luke 24:41 says the disciples ''yet believed not for joy, and wondered,'' a remarkably precise notice of mingled disbelief and emerging joy that captures exactly the transition the harmonization posits. So at the level of ''could both be true of the same evening,'' the answer is yes, and that is why this entry is rated partially rather than not harmonized.

The limitation is in John''s telling. John 20:19-20 has Jesus appear through shut doors, say ''Peace be unto you,'' show his hands and side, and then — ''then were the disciples glad, when they saw the Lord.'' The gladness reads as the immediate response to seeing him, not as a delayed recovery from prior terror. John narrates no fear phase whatsoever; the Lukan terror, which Luke makes structurally central, is simply absent. The sequencing solution therefore works as a logical possibility but does not account for why John would suppress an element so prominent in the parallel tradition.

The critical-scholarly explanation is that Luke and John draw on distinct Easter-appearance traditions, each shaped to a different theological program. Luke''s overriding concern in 24:36-43 is the physical reality of the risen Jesus against the suspicion that the disciples have seen a pneuma (a ghost): Jesus invites them to handle him (''a spirit hath not flesh and bones''), shows his hands and feet, and eats a piece of broiled fish. The initial terror is functional — it sets up the demonstration of corporeality. John''s concern in 20:19-23 is different: the risen Jesus bestows peace (''Peace be unto you,'' repeated), breathes the Spirit (''Receive ye the Holy Ghost,'' the Johannine ''Pentecost''), and commissions the disciples (''as my Father hath sent me, even so send I you''). In that frame gladness, not terror, is the fitting response, and John has no reason to dwell on fright. Fitzmyer treats the Lukan scene as anti-docetic apologetic; Brown reads the Johannine scene as a unified commissioning event. Dale Allison and C. F. Evans both argue that the appearance narratives are independently shaped traditions rather than a single harmonizable transcript.

Steel-manned, the apologetic position is correct that nothing in the two verses logically forbids a fear-then-joy sequence, and Luke 24:41 even supplies an internal bridge. What it cannot supply is a reason for John''s complete omission of the terror that Luke foregrounds; the cleanest account of that omission is editorial, not chronological — each evangelist selected and shaped the emotional response to fit the theological work his scene was doing. Verdict: partially harmonizable as a logical sequence, but best explained as two redactionally shaped traditions of the same appearance.', 'Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (Anchor Bible 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1572-1583, on Luke 24:36-49.
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John XIII-XXI* (Anchor Bible 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 1019-1048, on John 20:19-23.
C. F. Evans, *Resurrection and the New Testament* (SBT Second Series 12; SCM, 1970), pp. 89-127.
Dale C. Allison Jr., *Resurrecting Jesus* (T&T Clark, 2005), pp. 269-299, on the appearance traditions.
Norman L. Geisler, *When Critics Ask* (Victor, 1992), pp. 421-422, on the sequencing harmonization.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Luke, John', 10, 2, 3, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The strongest harmonization runs through the Greek verb. John 20:17 reads mē mou haptou — a present imperative middle of haptomai. Greek present imperatives with the negative mē characteristically command the cessation of an action already in progress rather than prohibiting an action not yet begun. So the most natural force is "stop touching/clinging to me," not "do not begin to touch me." Raymond Brown in the Anchor commentary renders it "do not cling to me," reconstructing a scene in which Mary has already grasped the risen Jesus and will not let go; he tells her to release him because his return to the Father and the sending of the Spirit are not yet complete (cf. 20:22). On that reading 20:17 forbids clinging, not contact, and is fully consistent with Thomas being invited to touch a week later. C. K. Barrett and Craig Keener accept essentially this grammatical point. The harmonization is therefore strong for the Johannine internal tension, and it should be granted plainly.

What it does not resolve is Matthew 28:9. There the women, meeting Jesus on the road, "took hold of his feet and worshipped him" — ekratēsan autou tous podas, from krateō, "to grasp/hold firmly." That is precisely the clinging behavior John 20:17 is read as forbidding, and in Matthew it draws no rebuke whatsoever; Jesus simply tells them not to be afraid and to carry his message. So even granting the "stop clinging" reading of John, the two narratives frame the identical physical act in opposite ways: in John clinging must stop because the ascent is incomplete; in Matthew clinging in worship is the appropriate response and is left undisturbed. Luke 24:39 ("handle me and see") and the Thomas pericope both invite tactile verification of corporeality, which is yet a third emphasis.

The older apologetic tradition, represented by Matthew Henry and others, tried to harmonize Matthew against John by sequencing: John 20:17 occurred before some technical ascension and Matthew 28:9 after it, so the women could touch Jesus once he had "ascended" in the sense 20:17 had in view. This requires positing two distinct ascensions — an intermediate ascent to the Father on Easter morning and the final ascent of Acts 1 forty days later — a scheme the Gospels never develop and which Luke''s own forty-day timetable (Acts 1:3) cuts against. The construct is built to relieve the tension rather than read out of the text.

The critical reading, in Brown and in Davies-Allison''s ICC Matthew, is that Matthew and John preserve independent resurrection-appearance traditions with different theological centers of gravity. Matthew''s tradition emphasizes embodied recognition, prostration, and worship at Jesus'' feet; John''s emphasizes a transition — recognition that gives way to mission, with touch deferred until after the resurrection-and-glorification cluster is conceptually closed. Both traditions narrate a Magdalene-centered first encounter (Matthew with the women, John with Mary alone), and the divergence over touch is one symptom of their separate development. The contradiction is real but milder than the unqualified "touch me not" framing suggests, because John 20:17 is plausibly about clinging rather than contact in general.

Verdict: harmonizable within John (a present-imperative idiom), but a residual genuine tension with Matthew 28:9 that the grammar does not erase.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John XIII-XXI* (AB 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 992-994, 1011-1017, on the ''do not cling to me'' reading of John 20:17
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 3 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 668-676, on Matthew 28:9
Craig S. Keener, *The Gospel of John: A Commentary*, Vol. 2 (Hendrickson, 2003), pp. 1192-1196, on John 20:17
C. K. Barrett, *The Gospel According to St. John*, 2nd ed. (Westminster, 1978), pp. 565-566, on John 20:17
Dale C. Allison Jr., *Resurrecting Jesus* (T&T Clark, 2005), pp. 233-269, on the independent appearance traditions
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (AB 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1572-1583, on Luke 24:36-43', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Luke, John', 5, 4, 3, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The strongest harmonization, articulated in D. A. Carson''s Pillar commentary and supported by Keener, distinguishes Jesus'' two missions. In his first coming the purpose is salvific — "I came not to judge the world but to save the world" (12:47). At his eschatological return he exercises the judicial authority the Father has delegated (5:27, and note that 5:28-29 explicitly looks to the resurrection of the dead). On this reading the present-tense denials describe the purpose and tenor of the incarnate ministry, while the affirmations describe the appointed eschatological role. The framing is theologically coherent, it matches the future orientation of Acts, 2 Corinthians, and Revelation, and it tracks the early church''s articulation of the first and second advents. As a way of reconciling John with the rest of the New Testament, it is genuinely strong.

Brown, in the Anchor commentary, makes a subtler and more strictly Johannine move that does not even require the two-comings scheme. The Johannine noun krisis is bivalent: it denotes both a process of separation and a verdict. In 3:18-19 and 9:39 Jesus'' very presence functions as krisis — "this is the krisis, that the light has come into the world" — independently of any active pronouncement of sentence. The light arrives; people are sorted by how they respond to it. On this realized-eschatology reading, Jesus "judges no one" in the sense of not actively pronouncing condemnation against those who come to him, while simultaneously being the locus at which judgment occurs by virtue of his presence. The man born blind episode in chapter 9 makes the point structurally: Jesus heals and reveals, and judgment falls out as a by-product of how the religious authorities react. Bultmann''s influential reading pressed this realized dimension hard, treating future-judgment language in John as secondary; most scholars today, with Brown and Barrett, hold that John keeps both a present and a future judgment in tension rather than collapsing one into the other.

The verse-level contradiction is nonetheless real. "All judgment" given to the Son (5:22) set against "I judge no one" (8:15) is a flat opposition on the surface, and the SAB framing is not wrong to register it. What dissolves the opposition is not the surface text but the conceptual apparatus: a disambiguation of krino/krisis between active sentencing and presence-as-sorting, plus a temporal distribution of judgment across the two advents. Both moves are well grounded in John''s own vocabulary and structure, and once granted the statements cohere. A reader who refuses to import those frameworks and reads each verse as an isolated propositional claim will find a contradiction; a reader who attends to Johannine usage will find a deliberately layered theology of judgment.

Verdict: harmonizable, but only by attending to the bivalence of Johannine krisis and the present/future distribution of judgment — not at the flat surface of the verses.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John I-XII* (AB 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 219-221, 345-347, 376-377, 489-493, on Johannine krisis
C. K. Barrett, *The Gospel According to St. John*, 2nd ed. (Westminster, 1978), pp. 259-263, 339-342, 433-435, on Johannine judgment
Craig S. Keener, *The Gospel of John: A Commentary*, Vol. 2 (Hendrickson, 2003), pp. 648-655, 738-740, on Johannine judgment
D. A. Carson, *The Gospel According to John* (PNTC; Eerdmans, 1991), pp. 250-258, 451-453, on the two-comings harmonization
Rudolf Bultmann, *The Gospel of John: A Commentary* (Westminster, 1971), pp. 154-157, 345-346, on realized eschatology in John', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'John, Acts, 2 Corinthians, Revelation', 5, 3, 3, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The controlling background for both passages is Deuteronomy 19:15, the Torah rule that a matter is established only "at the mouth of two or three witnesses." Self-testimony, by that standard, is legally worthless: a litigant cannot be his own corroborating witness. The strongest harmonization, traceable to patristic exegetes such as Chrysostom and Augustine and developed by Carson, Keener, and Barrett, reads John 5:31 as a rhetorical concession to that legal standard rather than a categorical denial. Jesus grants, for argument''s sake, that if his testimony stood alone it would not meet the Mosaic evidentiary threshold — and then immediately produces the required corroboration: John the Baptist (5:33), his own works (5:36), the Father (5:37), and the scriptures (5:39). So 5:31 is hypothetical and concessive, the opening move of a forensic argument, not a confession that his witness is false.

In John 8:13 the Pharisees turn the same Deuteronomy 19:15 rule against Jesus: "You bear witness of yourself; your witness is not true" — they are quoting his own concession back at him. Jesus'' response in 8:14 shifts the frame. He claims that his self-witness is in fact valid because he possesses unique epistemic access — he knows "whence I came and whither I go" — that no ordinary human witness has; his testimony is not the self-serving claim of an interested party but the report of one who alone knows the relevant facts. Then in 8:17-18 he nonetheless satisfies the two-witness rule on its own terms: "In your law it is written that the testimony of two persons is true. I am one that bears witness of myself, and the Father who sent me bears witness of me." The legal standard is met, twice over.

The harmonization therefore runs: 5:31 concedes the two-witness rule as the baseline; 8:14 challenges the adequacy of that rule given Jesus'' unique status; and both passages converge on the Father as the corroborating second witness. Brown reads the sequence essentially this way in the Anchor commentary, and Andrew Lincoln''s monograph on the lawsuit motif shows that the entire Fourth Gospel is structured as a cosmic trial (lawsuit, witnesses, testimony, verdict), so the forensic argumentation is not ad hoc but the gospel''s organizing literary form. Jesus is simultaneously defendant and witness in a trial that John frames from the prologue to the passion.

What the SAB framing captures correctly is that a flat, propositional reading of either verse in isolation yields a contradiction: 5:31 says self-witness is not true, 8:14 says it is true. The contradiction dissolves only once the reader grants the legal-rhetorical frame — that 5:31 speaks within the hypothetical of self-witness standing alone, while 8:14 speaks from Jesus'' actual position with the Father as co-witness. That frame is well rooted in John''s own text and is the consensus reading; it is not an imposition. But it is a frame, not a surface harmony.

Verdict: harmonizable through John''s forensic two-witness argument; a genuine contradiction only at the level of isolated proof-texting.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John I-XII* (AB 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 222-227, 340-345, on John 5:31-47 and 8:13-20
C. K. Barrett, *The Gospel According to St. John*, 2nd ed. (Westminster, 1978), pp. 260-272, 337-342, on John 5:31 and 8:13-18
Craig S. Keener, *The Gospel of John: A Commentary*, Vol. 1 (Hendrickson, 2003), pp. 654-664, 738-745, on John 5:31 and 8:14
Andrew T. Lincoln, *Truth on Trial: The Lawsuit Motif in the Fourth Gospel* (Hendrickson, 2000), pp. 1-138, on the lawsuit motif', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'John', 7, 3, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The controlling background is Deuteronomy 19:15, the Torah requirement that a matter be established by two or three witnesses. By that standard a person''s testimony about himself is, on its own, legally inadmissible. The standard harmonization — patristic in origin and developed by Carson, Keener, Barrett, and Lincoln — reads John 5:31 not as a categorical statement that Jesus'' self-witness is false, but as a concession to that evidentiary rule: if his testimony stood alone, it would not meet the Mosaic threshold. Jesus then supplies the corroboration the law demands — John the Baptist (5:33), his works (5:36), the Father (5:37), the scriptures (5:39). So the "not true" of 5:31 means "not legally sufficient unsupported," not "false."

John 8:14 then operates from a different vantage. When the Pharisees throw the two-witness rule back at Jesus (8:13), he answers that his self-witness is in fact true because he, uniquely, knows "whence I came and whither I go" — he is not an ordinary interested party whose self-report is suspect, but the one witness with direct knowledge of the matter at issue. He then proceeds in 8:17-18 to satisfy the rule on its own terms by naming the Father as the second witness. The resolution thus turns on recognizing two distinct frames: 5:31 reasons inside the hypothetical "if I alone testify," while 8:14 reasons from Jesus'' actual epistemic and ontological position with the Father as co-witness. Brown, Barrett, Keener, and Lincoln all read the sequence this way, and Lincoln shows that the whole Fourth Gospel is built as a cosmic lawsuit — witnesses, testimony, cross-examination, verdict — so this forensic give-and-take is the gospel''s native idiom, not an apologetic contrivance.

Where the SAB framing remains legitimate is at the surface: read flatly and in isolation, 5:31 says the self-witness is not true and 8:14 says it is true, and that is a direct propositional opposition. A literalist who declines to supply the legal frame will see a contradiction; a reader who attends to the Deuteronomic background and John''s trial structure will see two coordinated moves in a single argument. The tension is genuine only at the level of decontextualized proof-texting.

Verdict: harmonizable on the same basis as id=519 — a forensic concession-then-corroboration argument grounded in the two-witness rule; the entry is also a near-duplicate of 519 and a candidate for consolidation.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John I-XII* (AB 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 222-227, 340-345, on John 5:31 and 8:13-20
C. K. Barrett, *The Gospel According to St. John*, 2nd ed. (Westminster, 1978), pp. 260-272, 337-342, on John 5:31 and 8:14
Craig S. Keener, *The Gospel of John: A Commentary*, Vol. 1 (Hendrickson, 2003), pp. 654-664, 738-745, on John 8:14
Andrew T. Lincoln, *Truth on Trial: The Lawsuit Motif in the Fourth Gospel* (Hendrickson, 2000), pp. 1-138, on the lawsuit motif', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'John', 7, 3, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

By the time of Mark the causal universe has been repopulated. Mark 9:25 names the spirit directly — to alalon kai kōphon pneuma, "the mute and deaf spirit" — and Jesus commands it to come out and enter the boy no more. The disability is not merely afflicted by the spirit; in Mark''s idiom the spirit is characterized by, and produces, the muteness and deafness, and removing the spirit removes the condition. This is characteristic Second Temple demonology, a development largely absent from the older strata of the Hebrew Bible and well attested in the literature of the intertestamental period: the Watcher myth and origin of evil spirits in 1 Enoch 15-16, the demonology of Jubilees, and the exorcistic and apotropaic texts from Qumran (e.g., 4Q560, 11Q11). Archie Wright traces how Genesis 6:1-4 was reread to generate a population of malevolent spirits responsible for human affliction — a conceptual shift that gives the Synoptic exorcism narratives their causal backdrop. Joel Marcus, in the Anchor Yale Mark, situates the chapter 9 episode squarely within this framework.

The harmonizing response is to treat these as different cases: God authors some conditions, demons cause others, and Exodus 4:11 and Mark 9 simply describe distinct instances with no logical conflict. Stated fairly, this is not unreasonable on its own — the New Testament does not deny divine sovereignty, and one could hold that God permits or even ordains what demons execute (the Joban model of mediated affliction). The difficulty is that Exodus 4:11 is not phrased as a case-by-case attribution. It is a sweeping rhetorical claim of authorship — "Is it not I, Yahweh?" — deployed precisely to assert that no other agent stands behind speech or the senses. To then introduce an independent demonic etiology for the same class of conditions is to qualify a claim the Exodus text frames as unqualified. The two texts reflect different theological worlds, not two halves of one coherent doctrine of disability.

John 9:1-3 supplies a third etiology that resists assimilation to either of the first two. The disciples assume a Deuteronomic retributive model — someone sinned, the man or his parents — and Jesus rejects it outright: "Neither this man nor his parents sinned, but that the works of God might be revealed in him." That is neither the direct-divine-authorship claim of Exodus (the man''s blindness is not presented as Yahweh''s punitive or creative act) nor the demonological frame of Mark (no spirit is named); it is a teleological reframing in which a congenital condition exists to become the occasion of a revelatory sign. Raymond Brown notes that John here explicitly sets aside the retributive theology of suffering. Three texts, three different accounts of why a person cannot see or hear: sovereign divine making, demonic affliction, and providential staging of a sign.

Verdict: genuine theological development across the corpus, not a single coherent doctrine; the case-by-case harmonization founders on the universal phrasing of Exodus 4:11.', 'John J. Collins, *Introduction to the Hebrew Bible*, 3rd ed. (Fortress, 2018), pp. 119-122, on divine sole agency in Exodus
William H. C. Propp, *Exodus 1-18* (AB 2; Doubleday, 1999), pp. 213-215, on Exodus 4:11
Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16* (AYB 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 652-661, on Mark 9:14-29
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John I-XII* (AB 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 371-376, on John 9:1-3
Archie T. Wright, *The Origin of Evil Spirits: The Reception of Genesis 6:1-4 in Early Jewish Literature* (WUNT 2/198; Mohr Siebeck, 2005), on demonic causation in Second Temple Judaism', 0, NULL, 3, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Exodus, Mark, John', 5, 3, 2, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

The more substantive issue is literary and source-critical, and here the tension is real. Mark 8:22-26 is the single most plausible synoptic candidate for what John 9 has reworked. Joel Marcus catalogs the shared elements: the use of saliva, the laying on of hands, a process that unfolds in stages, and a movement from blindness to confessed sight. Mark''s pericope is also conspicuous for being one of only two Markan miracles (with the Decapolis deaf-mute of Mark 7:31-37, which also uses saliva) that both Matthew and Luke omit entirely. The standard redaction-critical explanation is that the two-stage failure — Jesus asking ''Do you see anything?'' and getting the famous reply that men look ''like trees, walking,'' requiring a second touch — was theologically awkward. A Messiah who needs a second attempt does not fit the later evangelists'' Christology, so they cut it. Mark himself almost certainly intended the two-stage cure symbolically, framing it between the disciples'' dull-sighted misunderstanding (8:14-21) and Peter''s still-partial confession at Caesarea Philippi (8:27-30): the disciples, like the man, see but not yet clearly.

John 9 keeps the saliva — a detail apparently too embedded in the tradition to discard, and one paralleled in Hellenistic healing lore (Tacitus, Histories 4.81, and Suetonius, Vespasian 7, both report Vespasian healing a blind man with spittle, indicating saliva was a recognized cultural sign of healing power) — but replaces the messy, repeated touch with a single competent act mediated through washing in the Pool of Siloam. John then supplies an interpretive gloss found nowhere in Mark: Siloam means ''Sent'' (apestalmenos), tying the cure to Johannine sending Christology. Raymond Brown treats John 9 as drawing on traditional miracle material that John has theologically elevated, and the cumulative parallels make a developmental relationship more economical than pure coincidence.

The apologetic rejoinder — these are simply two distinct events — must then explain why Jesus employed an unusual saliva-and-clay technique that appears nowhere else in his recorded ministry, in two different cities, producing two structurally parallel narratives. That is possible but less parsimonious than the redactional account, in which one underlying tradition generated both. John Meier''s analysis of the saliva traditions notes their relative rarity and their tendency to attract embarrassment-based omission, which is why their preservation in Mark is a mark of antiquity.

Net assessment: if the question is ''were these the same factual event,'' the harmonization holds and the contradiction is overstated. If the question is ''how does the Gospel tradition portray Jesus curing blindness,'' the texts give two non-overlapping methods, and the most economical explanation for the John 9 portrayal is a Johannine reworking of the Markan tradition rather than an independent second miracle. Verdict: harmonizable as distinct events; better explained as Johannine redaction of the Markan tradition.', 'Joel Marcus, *Mark 8-16* (Anchor Yale Bible 27A; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 590-598, on Mark 8:22-26 and its parallels to John 9
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John I-XII* (Anchor Bible 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 369-382, on John 9 and its possible Markan source
John P. Meier, *A Marginal Jew: Rethinking the Historical Jesus*, Vol. 2 (Doubleday, 1994), pp. 690-694, on the saliva-healing traditions
Craig L. Blomberg, *The Historical Reliability of the Gospels*, 2nd ed. (IVP Academic, 2007), pp. 198-202, on the two-events apologetic position
Tacitus, *Histories* 4.81, on Vespasian''s healing of a blind man with spittle', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Mark, John', 10, 2, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The Reformed harmonization, defended by Thomas Schreiner, Ardel Caneday, and Wayne Grudem, argues that the apostates of Hebrews and 2 Peter were never genuinely regenerate; they shared in the community''s experiences without saving faith, ''tasting'' without consuming. The warning passages then function as the very means God uses to preserve the elect. This is coherent, but it strains the Hebrews vocabulary. As Harold Attridge documents, photisthentas (''enlightened'') is, in early Christian usage attested by Justin Martyr (First Apology 61), effectively technical baptismal language, and ''partakers of the Holy Spirit'' is precisely how Hebrews elsewhere describes genuine believers (Heb 3:1, 3:14, where the same metochoi root appears for those who ''share in Christ''). The author piles up insider language and then says these people can fall irrevocably; reading them as never-saved imports a category the writer does not flag.

The Arminian harmonization, represented by Ben Witherington, accepts that apostasy is real and reads the security texts as addressing external threats rather than voluntary internal defection. This works well for Romans 8:38-39, where, as Joseph Fitzmyer notes, every item in the list is an external agent or cosmic power — nothing in the list is the believer''s own will. But it strains John 10:28, whose ''they shall never perish'' is grammatically unconditional, and where, as Raymond Brown observes, the emphasis falls on the Father''s protective power, not on the contingency of the sheep''s perseverance.

The source- and community-critical reading treats these as distinct theological traditions that the canon never reconciled. Hebrews and 2 Peter emerge from communities preoccupied with maintaining commitment under pressure — Hebrews'' audience faced social ostracism and the temptation to revert (Heb 10:32-34), and its warnings are pastoral instruments against drift. The Johannine and Pauline assurance texts emerge from contexts emphasizing the believer''s security in Christ and God''s elective faithfulness. Richard Bauckham situates 2 Peter''s polemic against false teachers who promised freedom while themselves apostatizing, which sharpens its insistence that abandonment is catastrophic. J. Louis Martyn reads Galatians 5:4 within Paul''s apocalyptic framework, where ''falling from grace'' means defecting from the realm of the Spirit back under the Law.

Both streams are canonical; neither is dominant; the systematic frameworks (eternal security versus conditional perseverance) are post-biblical resolutions imposed to relieve a tension the texts themselves leave standing. Verdict: a genuine theological tension between distinct New Testament traditions, harmonizable only by importing a qualifier the opposing texts do not supply — hence disputed rather than resolved.', 'Harold W. Attridge, *The Epistle to the Hebrews* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1989), pp. 167-175, on Hebrews 6:4-6 and the impossibility of restoration
J. Louis Martyn, *Galatians* (Anchor Bible 33A; Doubleday, 1997), pp. 470-474, on Gal 5:4 and falling from grace
Richard Bauckham, *Jude, 2 Peter* (Word Biblical Commentary 50; Word, 1983), pp. 275-279, on 2 Pet 2:20-21
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John I-XII* (Anchor Bible 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 397-407, on John 10 and the security of the sheep
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *Romans* (Anchor Bible 33; Doubleday, 1993), pp. 535-540, on Romans 8:38-39
Thomas R. Schreiner and Ardel B. Caneday, *The Race Set Before Us: A Biblical Theology of Perseverance and Assurance* (IVP, 2001), representing the Reformed harmonization
Wayne Grudem, "Perseverance of the Saints: A Case Study from the Warning Passages in Hebrews," in *The Grace of God, the Bondage of the Will*, Vol. 1, ed. Thomas R. Schreiner and Bruce A. Ware (Baker, 1995), pp. 133-182, on the Hebrews warning passages
Ben Witherington III, *Letters and Homilies for Hellenized Christians*, Vol. 1 (IVP, 2007), pp. 217-225, on the Arminian reading of Hebrews 6
Justin Martyr, *First Apology* 61, on ''enlightenment'' as a designation for baptism', 0, NULL, 4, 'NT_internal', 'John, Romans, Galatians, Hebrews, 2 Peter', 5, 3, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The orthodox harmonization is Trinitarian and coherent on its own terms. Because the persons of the Trinity act inseparably toward the created order — the principle later formalized as opera trinitatis ad extra indivisa sunt — the Father can be named as the ultimate source (fons divinitatis) and the Son as the mediating agent of the same single mission of the Spirit. ''The Father sends'' and ''I send from the Father'' are then two vantage points on one indivisible act, not two competing causes. On this reading there is no contradiction, only the complementary roles intrinsic to the divine processions. The difficulty is chronological: this is a fully developed fourth- and fifth-century synthesis, articulated against the background of the Nicene and Cappadocian settlements, applied to a first-century text whose pneumatological agency is genuinely unsystematic. Raymond Brown — a Catholic priest and no anti-Trinitarian — acknowledged in the Anchor Bible that John''s Spirit-language is fluid about agency, and that the Paraclete sayings may reflect the discourse''s compositional history, with chapters 15-17 plausibly a later expansion of an originally shorter discourse centered on chapter 14. Gary Burge''s study of Johannine pneumatology likewise treats the variation in sender as reflecting the evangelist''s layered Christological emphasis rather than a worked-out doctrine of procession.

The downstream history shows how live the ambiguity was. The question of whether the Spirit proceeds from the Father alone (the natural reading of 14:26) or from the Father and the Son (drawn from 15:26) is precisely the Filioque dispute. The Latin West interpolated ''and the Son'' (Filioque) into the Nicene-Constantinopolitan Creed; the Greek East rejected the addition as both unauthorized and theologically erroneous. A. Edward Siecienski traces how patristic exegetes on both sides built their cases on these very verses — Western theologians leaning on John 15:26''s ''whom I will send,'' Eastern theologians on John 14:26 and 15:26''s ''from the Father.'' The clause became a formal cause of the 1054 schism between Rome and Constantinople and remains church-dividing. That an entire half of Christendom anchored its identity in one reading of these verses and the other half in the alternative is itself evidence that the Johannine text does not unambiguously specify the agency.

Craig Keener''s commentary defends the substantive harmony — the variation reflects the unity of Father and Son in John''s theology (cf. 10:30, ''I and the Father are one'') rather than rival claims — and on the level of the finished Gospel''s theology this is the most natural reading. The honest assessment is therefore twofold. Within the theological world of the finished Fourth Gospel, the shift of sender is intelligible as complementary, since for John the works of the Son are the works of the Father. But as a flat reading of who performs the act of sending, the grammatical subject genuinely changes, and the centuries-long Filioque controversy demonstrates that the resolution required doctrinal machinery the text alone does not supply. Verdict: harmonizable on Trinitarian premises and within Johannine theology; the surface tension is real and historically consequential.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John XIII-XXI* (Anchor Bible 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 638-650, 689-693, 707-714, on the Paraclete passages and possible compositional layers
Craig S. Keener, *The Gospel of John: A Commentary*, Vol. 2 (Hendrickson, 2003), pp. 967-981, on the John 14-15 Paraclete sayings
Gary M. Burge, *The Anointed Community: The Holy Spirit in the Johannine Tradition* (Eerdmans, 1987), pp. 110-149, on Johannine pneumatology
A. Edward Siecienski, *The Filioque: History of a Doctrinal Controversy* (Oxford University Press, 2010), pp. 27-50, on the patristic exegesis of these verses', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'John', 5, 3, 3, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The traditional harmonization, defended by D. A. Carson and Leon Morris, leans on the present tense of erota in 16:5 — ''none of you is asking me now.'' On this reading, the earlier questions of Peter and Thomas were superficial or self-concerned (Peter immediately pivots to whether he can follow and his readiness to die; Thomas focuses on not knowing the way), and by chapter 16 the disciples have lapsed into sorrowful silence (16:6, ''because I have said these things sorrow has filled your hearts''), no longer pressing the inquiry. Jesus, on this account, laments not that they never asked but that they are no longer engaging with the meaning of his departure. The reading is grammatically possible — erota is indeed present and can bear a ''right now'' force — but contextually strained. The complaint that ''none of you asks'' lands awkwardly when two disciples demonstrably did ask, and the harmonization must supply a shift in the disciples'' mood between chapters that the text of 16:5 itself does not mark.

The more economical explanation, defended across a broad range of Johannine scholarship, is compositional. Raymond Brown and Rudolf Bultmann both concluded that the Farewell Discourse is not a single seamless composition. The most cited aporia is at 14:31, where Jesus says egeiresthe, agomen enteuthen — ''Rise, let us go from here'' — after which, instead of leaving, he continues speaking for three more chapters and does not actually depart until 18:1. The natural inference is that the original discourse concluded at 14:31 and that chapters 15-17 are a later supplement, drawn from Johannine community tradition and inserted before the closing words could take effect. Chapter 16 appears to have been composed without 13:36 and 14:5 in view, which is exactly why 16:5 can have Jesus say no one has asked the question those verses record. Bultmann''s solution was rearrangement (textual displacement); Brown''s and John Ashton''s was redactional layering; either way the data point in the same direction — distinct compositional strata stitched together.

Craig Keener registers the harmonizing reading and the present-tense argument fairly, but the cumulative weight of the 14:31 problem, the doubled discourse material, and the verbatim contradiction at 16:5 makes the seams hypothesis the better account. It explains the contradiction without requiring Jesus to misremember a conversation from minutes earlier and without importing an unstated change in the disciples'' demeanor. The harmonization rescues consistency only by reading into 16:5 a temporal qualifier and a psychological backstory the verse does not state. Verdict: a genuine internal contradiction, best explained as a seam between compositional layers in the Farewell Discourse.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John XIII-XXI* (Anchor Bible 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 581-604, 710, on the composite Farewell Discourse and the 16:5 problem
Rudolf Bultmann, *The Gospel of John: A Commentary*, trans. G. R. Beasley-Murray et al. (Westminster, 1971), pp. 459-461, 558-560, on the displacement and composite structure of the discourse
Craig S. Keener, *The Gospel of John: A Commentary*, Vol. 2 (Hendrickson, 2003), pp. 1027-1029, on the harmonizing reading of 16:5
D. A. Carson, *The Gospel According to John* (Pillar New Testament Commentary; Eerdmans, 1991), pp. 532-533, representing the apologetic harmonization
Leon Morris, *The Gospel According to John*, rev. ed. (New International Commentary on the New Testament; Eerdmans, 1995), pp. 615-617, on the harmonizing reading of John 16:5
John Ashton, *Understanding the Fourth Gospel*, 2nd ed. (Oxford University Press, 2007), pp. 408-435, on Johannine composition', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'John', 7, 2, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard harmonization, represented by D. A. Carson and Andreas Köstenberger, restricts the scope of panta in 15:15. On this reading, ''all things'' is comprehensive only with respect to what Jesus has revealed about the Father''s purposes, his own identity and mission, and the disciples'' standing as ''friends'' rather than ''servants'' — the immediate point of the verse, which contrasts the servant who does not know his master''s business with the friend who does. The ''many things'' of 16:12 then refer to fuller understanding that will come after the resurrection through the Paraclete, who ''will guide you into all truth'' (16:13, hodegesei hymas en te aletheia pase). Read sequentially, 15:15 describes the completeness of Jesus'' self-disclosure during his earthly ministry and 16:12 the further unfolding reserved for the Spirit; the two are then complementary stages, not contradictory claims. This is a coherent reading and the most natural one within the finished Gospel''s theology, since John repeatedly notes that the disciples did not understand at the time but would later (cf. 2:22, 12:16, 14:26). To that extent the tension genuinely softens.

The weakness is lexical and methodological. The plain force of panta ha ekousa — ''all things that I heard'' — resists easy restriction; the harmonization converts ''all things'' into ''all things in the relevant category,'' supplying the category from outside the verse. The text does not say ''all things necessary for now'' or ''all things about my mission''; it says all things heard from the Father. C. K. Barrett and Raymond Brown both treat the discrepancy as a marker of the discourse''s compositional layering, with chapters 15-17 as a secondary expansion. On this account, 16:12-13 belongs to a stratum focused on the Paraclete''s ongoing teaching role — a Johannine community theologumenon that legitimated the community''s later doctrinal development as Spirit-mediated continuation of Jesus'' teaching. The function of 16:12 in its own setting is not to retract 15:15 but to ground the community''s continuing revelation in Jesus'' own promise; the contradiction with 15:15 is a byproduct of joining two layers that were not composed in view of each other.

Both accounts can be true at different levels. As finished-text theology, the sequence (full disclosure in the ministry, further unfolding through the Spirit) coheres, and John''s repeated ''they understood later'' motif supports it. As a flat reading of the two assertions, ''I have made known all things'' and ''I have many things still to say'' stand in contradiction unless panta is silently narrowed, and the most economical explanation for why the discourse contains both is that it was assembled from distinct layers — the same hypothesis that accounts for the 14:31 aporia and the 16:5 problem in adjacent material. Verdict: partially harmonizable as sequential stages of revelation, with the residual tension best explained by the discourse''s composite construction.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John XIII-XXI* (Anchor Bible 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 681-682, 706-716, on John 15:15, 16:12 and Paraclete revelation
C. K. Barrett, *The Gospel According to St. John*, 2nd ed. (Westminster, 1978), pp. 477-478, 488-489, on John 15:15 and 16:12
Craig S. Keener, *The Gospel of John: A Commentary*, Vol. 2 (Hendrickson, 2003), pp. 1011-1013, 1031-1032, on John 15:15 and 16:12
D. A. Carson, *The Gospel According to John* (Pillar New Testament Commentary; Eerdmans, 1991), pp. 522-523, 540-541, representing the standard harmonization
Andreas J. Köstenberger, *John* (Baker Exegetical Commentary on the New Testament; Baker Academic, 2004), pp. 462-465, 472-474, on John 15:15 panta and 16:12 in tandem
John Ashton, *Understanding the Fourth Gospel*, 2nd ed. (Oxford University Press, 2007), pp. 420-432, on compositional layers in the Farewell Discourse', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'John', 7, 2, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

Matthew has the women depart the tomb "with fear and great joy" (*meta phobou kai charas megalēs*), meet Jesus on the road, seize his feet (*ekratēsan autou tous podas*), and worship him. The recognition is instantaneous; there is no doubt, no delay, no failure of identification. John, by contrast, has Mary alone at the tomb, weeping (*klaiousa*), bent over peering inside, interrogated by two angels, and then turning to see Jesus standing behind her — whom she takes for the gardener (*dokousa hoti ho kēpouros estin*). She recognizes him only when he speaks her name, *Mariam* (John 20:16).

The standard maximalist harmonization (Wenham, *Easter Enigma*; echoed in part by Licona) is a sequential reconstruction: Mary came to the tomb, ran to fetch the disciples (John 20:1-2), and returned for the weeping encounter (John 20:11-18), while the other women — separated from her — had the joyful Matthew 28 meeting. On this reading the two scenes are simply different moments involving different subsets of women. The reconstruction is not impossible, but it survives only by importing connective scaffolding that none of the Gospels supply: the splitting of the party, the second trip, the precise interleaving of encounters. Matthew names "the two Marys" together throughout 28:1-9 and gives no hint that Mary Magdalene peeled off for a separate, contrary experience.

The emotional discrepancy alone is the weakest part of the case; moods can shift, and a person can move from grief to joy within an hour. The harder problem is the recognition failure, because in John it is not incidental detail but load-bearing theology. John 10 develops the motif of the shepherd who knows his sheep and calls them by name (10:3-4, 14); Mary''s failure to recognize Jesus by sight and her instant recognition at the sound of her name (*Mariam*) is the narrative payoff of that motif. The non-recognition is constructed, not reported. Matthew needs no such moment because Matthew is pursuing a different theological aim — the vindication of Jesus'' authority through the women''s immediate worship and the subsequent Great Commission (28:16-20).

Source-critically, the resurrection appearances are precisely the zone of greatest divergence across the four Gospels, as Davies and Allison observe in the third volume of their ICC Matthew. The earliest stratum, Mark''s original ending (16:8), has the women flee in terror and "say nothing to anyone" — no appearance of Jesus at all. Matthew then supplies an appearance to the women on the road; John supplies an elaborate independent scene with angels, weeping, the gardener confusion, and the *noli me tangere* exchange (20:17). These are not interlocking fragments of a single itinerary but competing narrative theologies that developed as the resurrection traditions diversified in the first generation. Raymond Brown''s Anchor Bible treatment of John 20 reaches a similar conclusion: the Johannine scene is a self-contained literary and theological unit, not a supplement designed to dovetail with the Synoptics.

Ehrman frames the broader pattern bluntly: the resurrection narratives are the clearest case of independent traditions that the evangelists did not, and perhaps could not, harmonize. The honest critical read is that Matthew and John preserve rival memories of Easter morning, each shaped to its author''s purposes, and that the differences in mood, place, and recognition are artifacts of that divergence rather than complementary camera angles on one event. Verdict: genuine contradiction.', 'John Wenham, *Easter Enigma*, 2nd ed. (Baker, 1992), representing the maximalist harmonization of the resurrection-morning accounts.
Michael R. Licona, *Why Are There Differences in the Gospels?* (Oxford University Press, 2017), pp. 177-184, on harmonization attempts for resurrection narratives.
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 3 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 659-675, on Matthew 28.
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John XIII-XXI* (Anchor Bible 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 980-1017, on John 20 resurrection appearances.
Bart D. Ehrman, *How Jesus Became God* (HarperOne, 2014), pp. 132-169, on the diversity of resurrection traditions.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, John', 10, 2, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The "yes" side is overwhelming and consistent. Hebrews 11:1 defines faith (*pistis*) as "the substance of things hoped for, the evidence of things not seen" (*pragmatōn elenchos ou blepomenōn*). 2 Corinthians 4:18 directs attention to "the things which are not seen"; 5:7 declares "we walk by faith, not by sight" (*dia pisteōs gar peripatoumen, ou dia eidous*); Romans 8:24 grounds salvation in an unseen hope; and John 20:29 has the risen Jesus pronounce blessed "they that have not seen, and yet have believed." The whole stream is univocal: the unseen is the proper object of faith.

The sole counterweight is Colossians 2:18, and here the apparent contradiction is a creature of the King James text. The contested clause is *ha heoraken embateuōn* — literally "entering into [the] things which he has seen." A large body of later manuscripts inserts the negative *mē* ("things which he has NOT seen"), and the KJV follows that reading, producing a verse that seems to condemn belief in unseen things. But the shorter reading, without the negative, is earlier and better attested; Metzger''s *Textual Commentary* and the apparatus of the critical editions favor it, and the negative is widely judged a secondary scribal expansion. On the critical text, Colossians 2:18 is not condemning faith in the invisible at all. It is criticizing opponents in the Colossian community who boast about visionary experiences they claim to have had — "intruding into the things he has seen, vainly puffed up by his fleshly mind." Fred Francis''s classic study reconstructs these opponents against a background of ascetic-visionary mysticism and angelic worship; Dunn''s NIGTC commentary works through the same textual and exegetical terrain. So the contradiction the SAB detects is, in one direction, a KJV artifact: correct the text and the verse no longer addresses the same subject as Hebrews 11.

That said, a deeper seam remains, and it is worth naming rather than papering over. The New Testament simultaneously elevates faith without sight as the higher virtue (John 20:29; Hebrews 11:1, 11:6) and grounds apostolic authority precisely in eyewitness sight: 1 John 1:1 ("that which we have seen with our eyes... have handled"), Luke 1:2 ("eyewitnesses... of the word"), 1 Corinthians 15:5-8 (the catalogue of those to whom the risen Christ appeared, ending with Paul himself), and 2 Peter 1:16 ("we were eyewitnesses of his majesty"). Ehrman has highlighted this as a structural irony of early Christianity: sight is normative and authority-conferring for the first generation, while faith without sight becomes normative for everyone afterward. The Thomas pericope in John 20 is in fact the literary hinge that manages the transition — Thomas sees and believes, and the narrator pivots to bless those who will believe without seeing, a verse Brown reads as addressed directly to John''s later audience.

This residual tension is not the contradiction the SAB pairing actually catches, and it is only a contradiction at all if one reads the eyewitness texts and the faith-without-sight texts as competing universal epistemological claims. They are better read as marking two stages: the foundational generation that saw, and the believing generations that did not. The texts distribute the two epistemologies across time rather than asserting both of the same people at once. Verdict: the surface contradiction is overstated and rests on a rejected textual reading; a genuine but milder developmental tension lies underneath, which the narrow SAB pairing does not quite capture.', 'Harold W. Attridge, *The Epistle to the Hebrews* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1989), pp. 305-314, on Hebrews 11:1 and the definition of faith.
Bruce M. Metzger, *A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament*, 2nd ed. (Deutsche Bibelgesellschaft, 1994), p. 555, on the Colossians 2:18 textual variants.
James D. G. Dunn, *The Epistles to the Colossians and to Philemon* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 1996), pp. 178-188, on the textual problem in Colossians 2:18.
Fred O. Francis, "Humility and Angelic Worship in Col 2:18," *Studia Theologica* 16 (1962): 109-134, on the visionary background of the Colossian opponents.
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John XIII-XXI* (Anchor Bible 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 1023-1049, on John 20:29 and the Thomas pericope.
Bart D. Ehrman, *Jesus, Interrupted: Revealing the Hidden Contradictions in the Bible* (HarperOne, 2009), pp. 102-114, on the eyewitness/faith-without-sight tension in early Christian epistemology.', 0, NULL, 4, 'NT_internal', 'John, Romans, 1 Corinthians, 2 Corinthians, Colossians, Hebrews', 5, 3, 2, 5, 1, 'Col 2:18: the KJV reads ''intruding into those things which he hath not seen,'' reflecting manuscripts with the negative mē, whereas the earlier/better-attested reading omits the negative (''things which he has seen,'' i.e. visionary claims), reversing the sense.', 'Metzger, A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament, 2nd ed., p. 555 (Col 2:18)');

Acts 1:1 opens: *ton men prōton logon epoiēsamēn peri pantōn, ō Theophile, hōn ērxato ho Iēsous poiein te kai didaskein* — "The former treatise I made, O Theophilus, of all that Jesus began both to do and to teach." Two features defuse the apparent totality claim. First, the controlling verb is *ērxato*, "began." Luke is not asserting that volume one recorded everything Jesus ever did; he is framing the Gospel as the account of what Jesus *began* to do and teach, with the Acts narrative continuing that work through the Spirit and the apostles. The "began/continued" structure is itself a programmatic statement about the relationship between the two volumes. Second, *peri pantōn* ("concerning all") functions here as a conventional summarizing flourish typical of the secondary preface that opens a multi-volume Hellenistic work. Joseph Fitzmyer, in the Anchor Bible Acts, reads 1:1 against precisely this convention, comparing the recapitulatory prefaces of writers such as Josephus and Lucian; Loveday Alexander''s monograph on the Lukan preface situates both Luke 1:1-4 and Acts 1:1 within the scientific- and technical-prose preface tradition, where such phrases mark transition and scope rather than literal completeness. Craig Keener''s commentary reaches the same judgment about the recapitulatory function of the verse.

John 21:25 belongs to an entirely different genre register. "And there are also many other things which Jesus did, the which, if they should be written every one, I suppose that even the world itself could not contain the books that should be written." This is a peroration — a deliberate closing hyperbole. Raymond Brown documents the trope in Hellenistic and Jewish literature: the "the world could not contain it" figure appears in comparable forms in Philo and in rabbinic hyperbole about the inexhaustibility of Torah. The statement signals literary closure and the inexhaustible significance of its subject; it is not a quantitative claim competing with a preface in another book by another author.

The alleged contradiction therefore requires reading Acts 1:1''s *peri pantōn* at maximal strength while reading John 21:25 with wooden literalism, and then treating the two as assertions about the same proposition. Read in their native registers, they are not even the same kind of utterance: one is a volume-scope preface using *ērxato*, the other is a closing hyperbole using a recognized inexhaustibility topos. Neither author is making a catalogue claim about the exhaustive completeness of any single book.

It is worth conceding that the harmonization here is a matter of genre judgment rather than mechanical proof, and a reader determined to press *peri pantōn* literally can keep the tension alive. But the weight of Acts and Johannine scholarship — Fitzmyer, Keener, Alexander, Brown — converges on reading both phrases as literary rather than as inventory statements. On that reading the two verses are doing different jobs and never collide. Verdict: harmonizable; the contradiction is largely a translation-and-register artifact rather than a substantive collision.', 'Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Acts of the Apostles* (Anchor Bible 31; Doubleday, 1998), pp. 195-201, on the Acts 1:1 preface and its Hellenistic conventions.
Loveday Alexander, *The Preface to Luke''s Gospel* (SNTSMS 78; Cambridge University Press, 1993), on Hellenistic preface conventions.
Craig S. Keener, *Acts: An Exegetical Commentary*, Vol. 1 (Baker, 2012), pp. 645-660, on the Acts preface.
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John XIII-XXI* (Anchor Bible 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 1129-1130, on John 21:25 as Hellenistic closure topos.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'John, Acts', 7, 2, 2, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

Acts 1:15 reports that in the days after the ascension "the number of names together were about an hundred and twenty" (*ēn te ochlos onomatōn epi to auto hōsei hekaton eikosi*). The setting is explicit: believers gathered *epi to auto*, in one place, in Jerusalem, in the interval before Pentecost. Paul, writing in the mid-50s and quoting a tradition older still, says the risen Christ "was seen of above five hundred brethren at once; of whom the greater part remain unto this present, but some are fallen asleep" (1 Corinthians 15:6). The clause *ephapax* ("at once") makes this a single mass appearance, and Paul''s appeal to surviving witnesses is an argument for verifiability addressed to the Corinthians.

The standard harmonization (F. F. Bruce, Bock) is geographic and demographic. The 120 of Acts 1:15 are the specific company assembled in one Jerusalem room shortly after the ascension; the 500-plus of 1 Corinthians 15:6 were witnesses of an appearance that most likely occurred in Galilee — the region to which the risen Jesus directs the disciples in Matthew 28:16-20 and Mark 16:7 — after which they dispersed to their homes. On this reading the two numbers are snapshots of different places and moments, and there is no arithmetic to reconcile. Fitzmyer, in both his Acts and his 1 Corinthians commentaries, treats the figures as belonging to distinct settings, which is enough to dissolve a strict numerical collision.

The difficulty is the cost of the reconstruction. If more than 500 people personally witnessed the risen Jesus — by any measure the most extraordinary event of their lives — and fewer than 120 then constituted the founding Jerusalem community, the harmonization implies roughly a 75% attrition among resurrection eyewitnesses at the very moment the movement was forming. Acts itself shows no awareness of a large pool of additional witnesses scattered through Galilee; the narrative frames the 120 as the core community out of which Pentecost erupts (Acts 2), not as one local fragment of a much larger body of people who had seen the same thing.

The sharper critical observation, pressed by Lüdemann and Ehrman, is the silence. The appearance to "more than five hundred at once" is found nowhere in the four Gospels or in Acts. It surfaces only in Paul''s pre-Pauline creedal citation. If 500 people had collectively seen the risen Jesus, the absence of any echo of so striking an event in the Gospel-Acts tradition is hard to explain on the harmonization view. Lüdemann reads the datum as a reference to a mass ecstatic experience that Paul received as tradition and could not independently verify; on this account the 500-witness claim circulated in Pauline circles but did not propagate into the narrative Gospel stream.

The upshot is a partial resolution. The geographic reading genuinely removes the appearance of a head-count contradiction, so this is not a flat numerical error. But it leaves two real problems unanswered — the implausible attrition rate and the total narrative silence about the 500 — which is why the resolution is only partial rather than complete. Verdict: partially harmonizable; the strict numerical collision dissolves on a geographic reading, but the silence of the Gospel-Acts tradition about the 500 remains a genuine difficulty.', 'Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Acts of the Apostles* (Anchor Bible 31; Doubleday, 1998), pp. 222-225, on Acts 1:15 and the 120.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *First Corinthians* (Anchor Yale Bible 32; Yale University Press, 2008), pp. 549-554, on 1 Corinthians 15:6 and the 500.
Gerd Lüdemann, *The Resurrection of Jesus: History, Experience, Theology* (Fortress, 1994), pp. 100-108, on the historicity of the 500-witness tradition.
Bart D. Ehrman, *How Jesus Became God* (HarperOne, 2014), pp. 144-169, on the diversity and growth of resurrection appearance traditions.
Darrell L. Bock, *Acts* (BECNT; Baker, 2007), pp. 85-89, representing the geographic harmonization.
F. F. Bruce, *The Book of the Acts*, rev. ed. (NICNT; Eerdmans, 1988), pp. 43-46, on the size of the Jerusalem community at Acts 1:15.', 0, NULL, 7, 'NT_internal', 'Acts, 1 Corinthians', 1, 2, 3, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

Matthew 27:3-10 narrates remorse: Judas "repented himself," returned the thirty pieces of silver to the chief priests, threw the coins down in the temple (*rhipsas ta argyria eis ton naon*), departed, and hanged himself (*apēnxato*). The priests, unwilling to put blood money into the treasury, used it to buy (*ēgorasan*) the potter''s field as a burial ground for strangers, which is why it was called the Field of Blood — it was purchased with blood money. Acts 1:18-19 tells a different story: "Now this man purchased a field (*ektēsato chōrion*) with the reward of iniquity; and falling headlong, he burst asunder in the midst (*prēnēs genomenos elakēsen mesos*), and all his bowels gushed out," and the field was called Akeldama, "the field of blood," because Judas''s blood was spilled there.

The classic apologetic fusion (Archer; Geisler and Howe) tries to weld the two: Judas bought the field indirectly, through the priests acting as his agents; he then hanged himself, and afterward the rope broke or the corpse decomposed and the swollen body burst open when it fell. On this reconstruction both narratives are partly true and merely emphasize different stages. The harmonization fails at each joint. First, Matthew states that the priests bought the field (*ēgorasan*) with money Judas had thrown away in disgust; there is no language of agency or proxy, and the whole point of Matthew''s scene is that Judas rid himself of the money. Acts, by contrast, has Judas himself acquire the field with the "reward of iniquity," which presupposes he kept it. Second, the manner of death is not merely supplemented but replaced: Matthew has hanging; Acts has a headlong fall and bodily rupture, with no mention of a rope. The burst-after-hanging scenario is invented to bridge texts; neither author describes it. Third, even the etiology of the field''s name diverges: in Matthew it is the Field of Blood because of the blood money, in Acts because of Judas''s spilled blood. The same Aramaic name, Hakeldama, is given two incompatible explanations.

Critically, both narratives are better understood as independent legendary developments of an early tradition associating Judas''s death with a plot of land near Jerusalem. Matthew''s account is constructed around scriptural fulfillment: the thirty pieces of silver and the potter echo Zechariah 11:12-13, with the potter''s-field motif drawing on Jeremiah 18-19, which is why Matthew 27:9 attributes the quotation to Jeremiah though it is largely from Zechariah — a telling sign of composite, scripturally driven shaping. Acts builds its portrait typologically: the wicked man whose body ruptures recalls the death of the impious in Wisdom 4:19 and the conventions of the gruesome deaths of God''s enemies, while the replacement of Judas is justified by Psalm 69:25 and Psalm 109:8, both cited at Acts 1:20. Davies and Allison, Fitzmyer, Raymond Brown in *The Death of the Messiah*, and Hans-Josef Klauck''s monograph on Judas all read the two passages as parallel, theologically motivated etiologies rather than overlapping eyewitness reports.

The apologetic reconstruction collapses because it treats two competing midrashic narratives as if they were two camera angles on a single event. They are not the same genre of claim. One may judge which death-tradition is more primitive or more compelling, but the texts cannot both be received as straightforward history. Verdict: genuine contradiction arising from two independent legendary traditions.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 3 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 558-572, on Matthew 27:3-10.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Acts of the Apostles* (Anchor Bible 31; Doubleday, 1998), pp. 220-228, on Acts 1:15-22 and the death of Judas.
Raymond E. Brown, *The Death of the Messiah*, Vol. 2 (Doubleday, 1994), pp. 1394-1418, on the Judas death traditions and their typological framing.
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 343-345, representing the standard apologetic harmonization.
Norman L. Geisler and Thomas Howe, *When Critics Ask: A Popular Handbook on Bible Difficulties* (Victor Books, 1992), pp. 363-364, on the Matthew/Acts Judas-death harmonization.
Hans-Josef Klauck, *Judas: A Disciple of Jesus* (Liturgical Press, 2006), pp. 87-102, on the legendary development of the Judas-death traditions.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Acts', 7, 1, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The grammatical data is the place to begin. The dominant formula across the New Testament is built on the verb *egeirō*, "to raise," almost always in the form *ho theos ēgeiren auton ek nekrōn* ("God raised him from the dead") — Acts 2:24, 2:32, 4:10, 13:30, plus Gal 1:1, Col 2:12, 1 Thess 1:10, Rom 4:24, 10:9, 1 Cor 6:14, 15:15. Here Jesus is the grammatical object (the patient) and God the Father is the subject (the agent). A parallel set of texts uses the so-called "divine passive" — *ēgerthē*, "he was raised" (e.g., 1 Cor 15:4, Rom 6:4) — where the unstated agent is understood to be God. In neither construction does Jesus act on himself. This fits the early Christological pattern Bart Ehrman terms "exaltation Christology": Jesus is the one whom God vindicated, raised, and exalted to lordship (cf. the pre-Pauline formula embedded in Rom 1:3-4 and Phil 2:9, where God is the one who "highly exalted" him).

The John 2:19-21 saying breaks this pattern. There Jesus speaks in the active/future — "in three days I will raise it up" (*en trisin hēmerais egerō auton*) — making himself the agent of his own resurrection. This is distinctively Johannine and coheres with the Fourth Gospel''s high Logos Christology and the explicit claim of John 10:18, "I have authority to lay [my life] down, and I have authority to take it up again." Tellingly, the Johannine narrator must gloss the saying: "But he was speaking of the temple of his body" (2:21). This redactional clarification is necessary precisely because the saying''s traditional form did not carry a self-resurrection meaning. The Synoptic parallels preserve a temple-destruction saying placed on the lips of false witnesses (Mark 14:58; Matt 26:61) and the mocking taunt at the cross (Mark 15:29), all without any self-raising element — strong evidence that the original logion concerned the temple and was secondarily Christianized in John toward bodily resurrection.

Romans 8:11 supplies a third agent: "the Spirit of him who raised Jesus." Read closely the verse still makes God the one "who raised Jesus," but assigns the Spirit the role of agent in raising believers'' mortal bodies, and the syntax allows the Spirit a role in Christ''s resurrection as well. This pneumatological emphasis is rarer and looks like a Pauline development rather than a fixed formula.

The parsimonious reading is developmental. The earliest and most heavily attested formula is "God raised Jesus," rooted in the resurrection''s function as divine vindication. The Pauline Spirit-language is a side variant tied to Paul''s anthropology of the indwelling Spirit. The Johannine self-raising formula is the latest refinement, possible only once the pre-existent-Logos Christology was in place — which is why it surfaces in the Gospel most distant from the Synoptic tradition and requires an editorial gloss to land. One may retroject a Trinitarian harmony onto this data, and confessionally it holds; but the same evidence reads equally as three stages of Christological development. The data is constant; the interpretive frame is what changes. Verdict: harmonizable at the dogmatic level, but the underlying datum is genuine Christological development across strata.', 'Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Acts of the Apostles* (Anchor Bible 31; Doubleday, 1998), pp. 256-263, on the resurrection formulas in the Acts speeches
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *Romans* (Anchor Bible 33; Doubleday, 1993), pp. 489-491, on Rom 8:11
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John I-XII* (Anchor Bible 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 114-125, on John 2:19-21 and the temple-body saying
Bart D. Ehrman, *How Jesus Became God* (HarperOne, 2014), pp. 211-251, on exaltation versus incarnation Christology
Larry W. Hurtado, *Lord Jesus Christ: Devotion to Jesus in Earliest Christianity* (Eerdmans, 2003), pp. 175-185, on early resurrection formulas', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'John, Acts, Romans, Galatians, Colossians, 1 Thessalonians', 5, 3, 4, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

Acts 5:29, by contrast, has the apostles openly defying a legitimate authority: the Sanhedrin held recognized jurisdiction over Jewish religious life, and Peter flatly refuses to comply — *peitharchein dei theō mallon ē anthrōpois*, "we must obey God rather than men." If Romans 13 is the rule, Acts 5:29 is the exception that Romans 13 nowhere acknowledges.

The cleanest reading treats these as distinct theological streams addressing distinct situations through distinct genres. Romans, written c. 56-57 CE to believers in the imperial capital, urges political quietism as both ethic and survival strategy in the years before the Neronian persecution; some scholars connect 13:6-7 to concrete unrest over taxation under Nero. 1 Peter, addressing dispersed and pressured Christians in Asia Minor (1 Pet 1:1), counsels submission "for the Lord''s sake" to "every human institution" (*pasē anthrōpinē ktisei*, 2:13) as a way to disarm slander against the community (2:15). Both are hortatory paraenesis aimed at minimizing friction with Rome. Acts 5:29 belongs to a different genre entirely: Lukan narrative theology, where the apostles are cast as exemplary figures of conscience who defy corrupt power. Each text performs different rhetorical work for a different audience, which is why no single resolution rule emerges from the canon itself.

The downstream history confirms the unresolved character of the tension. Romans 13 has anchored the political theology of nearly every authoritarian Christian regime — invoked by defenders of the divine right of kings, by the pro-apartheid Dutch Reformed establishment in South Africa, and, more recently, by U.S. officials defending immigration enforcement policy (the 2018 appeal by Attorney General Jeff Sessions is the textbook case). Acts 5:29, conversely, is the foundational proof-text for Christian civil disobedience, from the Anabaptist refusal of the magisterial sword to Martin Luther King Jr.''s "Letter from Birmingham Jail." The same New Testament licenses both unconditional submission and principled resistance, and it supplies no internal mechanism for adjudicating between them. The harmonizing rule is reasonable and ancient, but it is a theological synthesis laid over the texts rather than a reading drawn out of them. Verdict: harmonizable in systematic theology, but the canon itself leaves the resolution to the reader.', 'Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *Romans* (Anchor Bible 33; Doubleday, 1993), pp. 661-673, on Romans 13:1-7
John H. Elliott, *1 Peter* (Anchor Bible 37B; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 484-499, on 1 Pet 2:13-17
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Acts of the Apostles* (Anchor Bible 31; Doubleday, 1998), pp. 336-340, on Acts 5:29
Richard A. Horsley, ed., *Paul and Empire: Religion and Power in Roman Imperial Society* (Trinity Press International, 1997), pp. 184-204, on Pauline political theology
John Howard Yoder, *The Politics of Jesus*, 2nd ed. (Eerdmans, 1994), pp. 193-211, on the Romans 13 problem and the Anabaptist reading', 0, NULL, 5, 'NT_internal', 'Acts, Romans, 1 Peter', 6, 2, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard apologetic resolution, going back to Augustine and defended by Gleason Archer, treats Genesis 11:26 as a summary notice that does not make Abram the firstborn. The argument: the verse lists three sons, so the begetting age of 70 need only mark when Terah began fathering children, not when Abram specifically was born. Abram is named first for theological prominence, not birth order — so he could have been born when Terah was 130, making him 75 at Terah''s death at 205. The same move (named-first ≠ born-first) is applied in the genealogies of Genesis 5 and 10.

The maneuver is grammatically strained. The begetting notices of Genesis 11 follow a rigid, repeated formula — "X lived Y years and begat Z" — and in every single other instance in the chapter the stated age is the age at which the *named* child was born (11:10, 11:12, 11:14, 11:16, 11:18, 11:20, 11:22, 11:24). The harmonization must make 11:26 uniquely a summary statement while treating every parallel notice as direct. That is special pleading driven by the need to reconcile, not by anything in the Hebrew syntax, which is identical across the formulae.

The textual evidence sharpens, rather than dissolves, the problem. At Genesis 11:32 the Samaritan Pentateuch reads Terah''s age at death as 145, against the 205 of the Masoretic Text (and the Septuagint) — and 145 is precisely the figure (70 + 75) that makes Abram''s departure at 75 fall after his father''s death, harmonizing 11:26, 11:32, 12:4, and Acts 7:4 in one stroke. The critical consensus, reflected in Emanuel Tov''s standard treatment, is that this is a secondary, harmonizing emendation, exactly the kind of chronological ''correction'' the Samaritan tradition is known to make, not the original reading. That matters for the argument: the Masoretic 205 is the harder and earlier reading, and the Samaritan 145 is the fingerprint of an ancient scribe confronting the same contradiction the modern apologist confronts — and ''fixing'' the numbers rather than reading 11:26 loosely. The variant does not rescue the inerrantist; it documents that the inconsistency was felt as early as the witnesses themselves.

Acts 7:4 is the deeper issue. Stephen''s speech reflects a Second Temple Jewish tradition — paralleled in Philo (*De Migratione Abrahami* 177) — that explicitly placed Abraham''s departure from Haran after Terah''s death, reading the sequence theologically as a clean break from the father''s household. Stephen (or Luke''s source) is following that interpretive tradition, which does not align with the plain arithmetic of Genesis 12:4. The critical consensus, from Wellhausen through Joel Baden, is that the Abraham chronology was assembled from the Priestly source''s genealogical framework (the precise ages of Genesis 11) and the Yahwistic narrative (the departure at 75 in Genesis 12), and the seam between the sources generated the inconsistency. Acts 7 then preserves a separate Hellenistic-Jewish reading that fit one possible reconstruction but not Genesis 12:4 as transmitted. Three traditions, no clean fit, and an ancient variant that proves the scribes saw the problem too. Verdict: genuine contradiction, with the Samaritan variant confirming early recognition of it.', 'Claus Westermann, *Genesis 12-36: A Commentary* (Augsburg, 1985), pp. 144-156, on the Abrahamic chronology problem
E. A. Speiser, *Genesis* (Anchor Bible 1; Doubleday, 1964), pp. 77-79, 86-87, on Genesis 11-12 sources
Joel S. Baden, *The Composition of the Pentateuch* (Yale University Press, 2012), pp. 230-237, on the P/J source layers in the Abraham cycle
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Acts of the Apostles* (Anchor Bible 31; Doubleday, 1998), pp. 369-371, on Stephen''s speech and its Hellenistic Jewish chronology
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 380-381, representing the standard apologetic harmonization
Victor P. Hamilton, *The Book of Genesis, Chapters 1-17* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 1990), pp. 365-369, representing the conservative position
Emanuel Tov, *Textual Criticism of the Hebrew Bible*, 3rd ed. (Fortress Press, 2012), on the Samaritan Pentateuch''s harmonizing chronological corrections', 0, NULL, 7, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Genesis, Acts', 1, 3, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Acts 9:26 places Saul''s arrival in Jerusalem immediately after his escape from Damascus over the wall in a basket (9:25), within a narrative sequence that knows nothing of any Arabian sojourn. The Lukan order runs: conversion → preaching in Damascus → plot and escape → Jerusalem, where Barnabas introduces him to the apostles (9:27). There is no three-year gap, no Arabia, and Paul meets the apostolic body, not just Cephas and James.

The traditional harmonization (F. F. Bruce, Ben Witherington) inserts the Arabian period into the temporal vagueness of Acts 9:23 — "when many days were fulfilled" (*hōs de eplērounto hēmerai hikanai*) — arguing that Luke''s "many days" can elastically accommodate three years including the Arabian trip. As a smoothing exercise this is possible, but it requires reading 9:23-26 as a radically compressed summary that silently swallows a multi-year ministry in Nabataea and a return to Damascus, with no narrative hint that any such compression has occurred. The phrase "many days" is doing an enormous and unsignaled amount of work.

The critical consensus, from Ernst Haenchen forward and methodologically formalized by John Knox and Gerd Lüdemann, holds that the primary first-person source must be weighted above the secondary third-person narrative. Luke either did not know of the Arabian period or omitted it because it cut against his theological program of presenting Paul as rapidly and harmoniously integrated into the apostolic community of Jerusalem. Paul''s entire rhetorical purpose is the opposite: to establish his independence from that community. The two accounts are driven by incompatible agendas and produce incompatible itineraries — most starkly on the number of apostles Paul met (Acts: the apostolic circle; Galatians: Cephas and James only) and on whether the Jerusalem visit was immediate or three years removed. The methodological default of critical scholarship, weighting the eyewitness letter over the later narrative, yields Paul. Verdict: genuine contradiction; Paul''s first-person testimony is the stronger evidence.', 'Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Acts of the Apostles* (Anchor Bible 31; Doubleday, 1998), pp. 433-441, on Acts 9 and the Galatians problem
J. Louis Martyn, *Galatians* (Anchor Bible 33A; Doubleday, 1997), pp. 168-186, on Gal 1:15-24
Ernst Haenchen, *The Acts of the Apostles: A Commentary* (Westminster, 1971), pp. 318-336, classic statement of the discrepancy
John Knox, *Chapters in a Life of Paul* (Abingdon, 1950), foundational critical reconstruction prioritizing the letters
Ben Witherington III, *The Acts of the Apostles: A Socio-Rhetorical Commentary* (Eerdmans, 1998), pp. 320-328, representing the conservative harmonization
F. F. Bruce, *Paul: Apostle of the Heart Set Free* (Eerdmans, 1977), pp. 75-86, on the Damascus-to-Jerusalem chronology', 0, NULL, 8, 'NT_internal', 'Acts, Galatians', 2, 3, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

In 1 Corinthians 8:4-8 Paul argues that "an idol is nothing in the world" (*ouden eidōlon en kosmō*) and that, in consequence, meat sacrificed to it — *eidōlothyton* — is theologically neutral: "food will not commend us to God; we are no worse off if we do not eat, and no better off if we do." The only operative constraint is pastoral: do not wound the conscience of the "weak" brother for whom the association with idolatry is still real (8:9-13), an argument Paul extends in 1 Cor 10:23-33 and parallels in Romans 14. The question is thus, for Paul, a matter of Christian liberty governed by love, not by law.

Acts 15:28-29 has the Jerusalem Council, under James, decree that Gentile believers must "abstain from things sacrificed to idols, from blood, from things strangled, and from sexual immorality." Crucially, these are framed as binding — *epanankes*, "necessary things" — and authorized by the Holy Spirit ("it seemed good to the Holy Spirit and to us"). On idol-meat specifically, Acts states a categorical prohibition where Paul states liberty.

The traditional harmonization (F. F. Bruce, Bruce Metzger on the textual side) reads the Decree as a localized, ad hoc accommodation for the mixed congregations of Antioch, Syria, and Cilicia — note the address in Acts 15:23 — aimed at preserving table fellowship between Jewish and Gentile believers, not as a universal and permanent code. On this reading Paul could honor the Decree''s spirit in those regions while teaching liberty on the underlying theology elsewhere. This is genuinely plausible as far as it goes, and the categories of "things strangled" and "blood" point toward Jewish dietary scruples about table fellowship rather than abstract doctrine. The textual history reinforces the fluidity: the Western text (Codex Bezae and allies) drops "things strangled" and adds a negative form of the Golden Rule, transforming the Decree from a food code into a moral one — evidence that even early scribes were uncertain what the prohibitions were for.

The harmonization founders on Paul''s silence. The Decree, if Paul knew of it and considered himself bound, would have settled the Corinthian dispute in a sentence — yet across the extended treatment of 1 Cor 8-10 Paul never cites it, never alludes to a Jerusalem ruling, and grounds his answer entirely in his own theology of the one God and the weak conscience. The same silence holds in Galatians 2, where Paul recounts the Jerusalem meeting and reports only that the pillars added nothing to him (Gal 2:6) — the opposite of an apostolic decree imposing four requirements. The critical assessment of Gerd Lüdemann is that Luke composed the Decree as a retrojected ecclesial compromise; James D. G. Dunn, more moderately, treats it as a historical accord whose terms Paul either did not accept as binding or understood differently than Luke presents. Either way, the historical Paul taught liberty on idol-meat without the categorical conditions Acts 15 attaches. Verdict: partially harmonizable as a local accommodation, but Paul''s structural silence leaves a real Pauline-vs-Lukan tension.', 'Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *First Corinthians* (Anchor Yale Bible 32; Yale University Press, 2008), pp. 333-352, on 1 Cor 8 and idol-meat
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Acts of the Apostles* (Anchor Bible 31; Doubleday, 1998), pp. 555-568, on the Apostolic Decree
James D. G. Dunn, *Beginning from Jerusalem* (Christianity in the Making, Vol. 2; Eerdmans, 2009), pp. 459-470, on the historical reliability of the Acts 15 decree
Gerd Lüdemann, *The Acts of the Apostles: What Really Happened in the Earliest Days of the Church* (Prometheus, 2005), pp. 173-185, on Lukan composition of the Apostolic Decree
F. F. Bruce, *The Book of the Acts*, rev. ed. (NICNT; Eerdmans, 1988), pp. 294-302, representing the conservative harmonization', 0, NULL, 5, 'NT_internal', 'Acts, 1 Corinthians', 6, 3, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The strongest harmonization, advanced by Fee and Garland, turns on the fact that Paul is not using one word but two. *Hagiazō* in 7:14 denotes relational or cultic holiness — the unbelieving spouse and the children are drawn into the sphere of covenant holiness, rendering the marriage legitimate within the community and the children *hagia* rather than *akatharta* ("unclean"). This is a status conferred by association, not a verdict on the unbeliever''s eternal destiny. *Sōzō* in 7:16, by contrast, denotes eschatological salvation, which for Paul always runs through personal faith (Romans 10:9-10). On this reading the two verses predicate different things of the unbeliever and there is no contradiction at all — 7:14 secures the integrity of the mixed marriage so the believer need not divorce, while 7:16 holds out conversion only as a hope. This distinction is exegetically sound and the entry rightly grants it: within 1 Corinthians 7 the tension largely dissolves.

Two qualifications keep it from dissolving completely. First, Paul''s own usage of the *hagi-* word-family elsewhere is soteriologically loaded — believers are *hēgiasmenoi en Christō Iēsō* and *klētoi hagioi* in 1 Corinthians 1:2, where sanctification and election are scarcely separable. The line Fee draws between cultic and saving holiness is real but porous in Pauline thought. Second, and more seriously for harmonization, 7:14 extends the conferred holiness to the children — *epei ara ta tekna hymōn akatharta estin, nyn de hagia estin* — which presses uncomfortably close to a corporate, household conception of grace.

That is precisely where Acts 16:31 reopens the problem. Luke has Paul and Silas tell the Philippian jailer *pisteuson epi ton kyrion Iēsoun, kai sōthēsē sy kai ho oikos sou* — "believe on the Lord Jesus, and you will be saved, you and your household." The verb here is *sōzō*, the salvation word, not the sanctification word, and the promise is grammatically tied to the jailer''s single act of belief. This reflects the *oikos* (household) pattern of Greco-Roman religion documented by Malherbe, in which the paterfamilias''s allegiance carried the whole house — slaves, dependents, kin — into a new cult. The Lukan baptisms of whole households (Lydia in 16:15, the jailer in 16:33, Cornelius in Acts 10) presuppose this corporate logic. Fitzmyer notes that the Western text even expands 16:31 to underscore the household''s inclusion. The Reformed harmonization reads *ho oikos sou* distributively — "each member of your house who likewise believes" — but that supplies a qualifier the Greek does not contain; the natural force of *sōthēsē sy kai ho oikos sou* is that the house is saved on the head''s faith.

The net result is a partial harmonization. The intra-chapter "contradiction" between 7:14 and 7:16 is more apparent than real once *hagiazō* and *sōzō* are distinguished. But the deeper theological divergence — Paul''s individualized, contingent model of who gets saved versus Luke''s corporate *oikos* promise — is not closed by that lexical move. Verdict: the two-verse tension in 1 Corinthians 7 is harmonizable; the Acts-versus-Paul tension on household salvation is genuine and unresolved.', 'Fitzmyer, Joseph A. *First Corinthians*. Anchor Yale Bible 32. New Haven: Yale University Press, 2008, pp. 298-308 (on 1 Cor 7:12-16).

Fee, Gordon D. *The First Epistle to the Corinthians*. New International Commentary on the New Testament. Grand Rapids: Eerdmans, 1987, pp. 297-307 (representing the sanctification/salvation distinction).

Garland, David E. *1 Corinthians*. Baker Exegetical Commentary on the New Testament. Grand Rapids: Baker, 2003, pp. 283-300.

Fitzmyer, Joseph A. *The Acts of the Apostles*. Anchor Bible 31. New York: Doubleday, 1998, pp. 588-592 (on Acts 16:31 and household salvation).

Malherbe, Abraham J. *Social Aspects of Early Christianity*. Philadelphia: Fortress, 1983, pp. 64-91 (on household conversion in the Greco-Roman context).', 0, NULL, 4, 'NT_internal', 'Acts, 1 Corinthians', 5, 3, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The critical resolution operates on two fronts. First, the Pastoral Epistles are widely judged pseudepigraphic. Since Holtzmann''s nineteenth-century work and through Brown, Bauckham, and Ehrman, the case rests on a vocabulary statistically divergent from the undisputed Paulines, a developed church order (overseers, deacons, enrolled-widow lists), a polemic against "myths and endless genealogies" suiting a second-century setting, and a domesticated ethic remote from the apocalyptic urgency of 1 Corinthians 7. If 1 Timothy is a Pauline-school product of c. 100-130 CE, then 2:11-12 is not Paul restricting women but a later author constraining a tradition that had been more open — and even grounding the restriction in a reading of Genesis (Eve deceived first) that Paul nowhere else deploys.

Second, 1 Corinthians 14:34-35 carries its own text-critical cloud. The verses float: in the Western tradition (Codex Claromontanus, Codex Fuldensis marginalia, and the minuscules of family D) they appear after 14:40 rather than after 14:33. Fee and Conzelmann argue that this floating, combined with the direct collision with 1 Corinthians 11:5 — where Paul regulates rather than forbids women praying and prophesying in the assembly — marks 14:34-35 as a post-Pauline interpolation, possibly imported from the same milieu that produced 1 Timothy 2. Fitzmyer is more cautious, retaining the verses while conceding the gravity of the displacement evidence. Either way, the silence command sits awkwardly four chapters after Paul presupposes women speaking.

The complementarian harmonization, represented by Schreiner and Köstenberger, is not a strawman and deserves its strongest form: they argue the texts address distinct functions and so do not contradict. Priscilla''s instruction of Apollos was private (in their home, with her husband), not authoritative public teaching over the gathered church; Phoebe''s *diakonos* role was service, not governance; and the prohibitions in 1 Corinthians 14 and 1 Timothy 2 target the authoritative teaching office and the public evaluation of prophecy. On this reading the canon is consistent: women minister broadly but do not hold the specific office of authoritative teacher-elder. The reading is internally coherent and takes the texts as a unified Pauline whole.

It founders on two assumptions the critical reading rejects. It must treat all the texts as genuinely Pauline and roughly contemporaneous, which the pseudepigraphy of the Pastorals undercuts; and it must read the restrictive texts as the interpretive key that domesticates the permissive ones, when the historical and text-critical evidence points the other way — toward an earlier egalitarian practice (Galatians 3:28, Junia, Phoebe, Priscilla) that was progressively narrowed, as MacDonald traces, by interpolation and pseudepigraphic walk-back. The disagreement is therefore not merely exegetical but turns on authorship, dating, and textual integrity, which is why the status here is best labeled disputed rather than cleanly harmonized or not. Verdict: a genuine canonical tension whose resolution depends entirely on contested judgments about Pauline authorship — the historical Paul looks far more like the colleague of Priscilla and Junia than the silencer of 1 Timothy.', 'Ehrman, Bart D. *Forged: Writing in the Name of God*. New York: HarperOne, 2011, pp. 93-114 (on the Pastoral Epistles as pseudepigrapha).

Brown, Raymond E. *An Introduction to the New Testament*. Anchor Bible Reference Library. New York: Doubleday, 1997, pp. 638-680 (on the Pastoral Epistles and the case for pseudepigraphy).

Fee, Gordon D. *The First Epistle to the Corinthians*. New International Commentary on the New Testament. Grand Rapids: Eerdmans, 1987, pp. 699-708 (arguing 1 Cor 14:34-35 is an interpolation).

Conzelmann, Hans. *1 Corinthians*. Hermeneia. Philadelphia: Fortress, 1975, pp. 246-248 (on the interpolation hypothesis).

Fitzmyer, Joseph A. *First Corinthians*. Anchor Yale Bible 32. New Haven: Yale University Press, 2008, pp. 529-533 (on the textual problem in 1 Cor 14:34-35).

MacDonald, Margaret Y. *The Pauline Churches*. SNTSMS 60. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1988 (on the trajectory from egalitarianism to restriction in the Pauline tradition).

Bauckham, Richard. *Gospel Women: Studies of the Named Women in the Gospels*. Grand Rapids: Eerdmans, 2002 (on the prominence of women in early Christian sources).

Schreiner, Thomas R., and Andreas J. Köstenberger, eds. *Women in the Church*, 3rd ed. Wheaton: Crossway, 2016 (representing the complementarian harmonization).', 0, NULL, 5, 'NT_internal', 'Acts, 1 Corinthians, 1 Timothy', 6, 3, 4, 1, 1, 'In the Western text tradition (D F G, and some Old Latin/Vulgate witnesses) 1 Cor 14:34-35 is transposed to follow v. 40; many scholars (Fee, Conzelmann) take this dislocation as evidence the verses are a non-Pauline interpolation.', 'Metzger, A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament, 2nd ed., on 1 Cor 14:34-35; NA28 apparatus at 1 Cor 14:34-35');

The text-critical wrinkle is genuine but must be stated carefully. Eusebius of Caesarea, writing before Nicaea, repeatedly cites Matthew 28:19 in a short form — "Go and make disciples of all nations in my name" (*en tō onomati mou*) — omitting the triadic clause. Conybeare catalogued these citations in 1901 and argued the triadic formula was a post-Nicene liturgical insertion. The difficulty is that no extant Greek manuscript, and no version, lacks the triadic clause; the entire documentary tradition, including the early papyri and the major uncials, reads the full formula. Eusebius''s short citations are most plausibly explained as loose paraphrase keyed to the missionary thrust of the verse rather than as testimony to a different Vorlage, and Ferguson and the Davies-Allison commentary treat the interpolation thesis as unproven. So the strong form of the textual argument — that the triadic clause is demonstrably a forgery — overreaches.

The more durable critical point is developmental. Even granting the triadic formula as original to Matthew, Matthew was composed c. 80-90 CE, and its wording reflects the catechetical and liturgical practice of the Matthean community, not necessarily the earliest baptismal rite. The *Didache* preserves both: it prescribes triadic baptism (7.1, 7.3) yet in 9.5 restricts the eucharist to those "baptized into the name of the Lord," suggesting the two formulas coexisted and that the christological form was older. Hurtado and Dunn both treat Jesus-name baptism as the primitive practice, with the triadic formula crystallizing as trinitarian devotion matured. On this reading Acts is the more reliable witness to first-generation practice and Matthew 28:19 retrojects a later liturgical idiom onto the risen Jesus.

The traditional harmonization, given its best form by Carson, holds that "in the name of" denotes coming under the authority and ownership of the one named, so that baptism into Jesus is implicitly baptism into the triune God of whom Jesus is the Son — the formulas differ in wording but not in referent, and Acts may simply use shorthand for the fuller rite. This is theologically coherent and the phrase *eis to onoma* does carry the sense of transfer of allegiance. Its weakness is the importation of developed trinitarian theology into pre-Nicene narrative: it assumes precisely the equivalence between Jesus-name and triadic baptism that the historical sequence calls into question, and it cannot explain why Luke, who knew the Great Commission tradition, never once depicts the apostles using the triadic words.

The result is a partial harmonization. The two formulas can be reconciled as successive stages of a single developing tradition, and "in the name of" can bear an allegiance sense in both — but only on the critical concession that Matthew 28:19''s triadic wording is the church''s liturgical voice rather than a verbatim dominical command. Verdict: harmonizable as a development, not as a single uniform apostolic practice — the surface contradiction is real and resolves only by dating the triadic formula late.', 'Conybeare, F. C. "The Eusebian Form of the Text Matth. 28:19." *Zeitschrift für die Neutestamentliche Wissenschaft* 2 (1901): 275-288 (foundational text-critical argument).

Davies, W. D., and Dale C. Allison. *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 3. International Critical Commentary. Edinburgh: T&T Clark, 1997, pp. 682-688 (on Matt 28:19 and the baptismal formula).

Fitzmyer, Joseph A. *The Acts of the Apostles*. Anchor Bible 31. New York: Doubleday, 1998, pp. 263-267 (on Acts 2:38 and Jesus-name baptism).

Hurtado, Larry W. *Lord Jesus Christ: Devotion to Jesus in Earliest Christianity*. Grand Rapids: Eerdmans, 2003, pp. 198-202 (on the development of baptismal practice).

Dunn, James D. G. *Baptism in the Holy Spirit*. London: SCM, 1970, pp. 117-131 (on early baptismal formulas).

Ferguson, Everett. *Baptism in the Early Church*. Grand Rapids: Eerdmans, 2009, pp. 134-145 (on the textual and liturgical history of Matt 28:19).

Carson, D. A. "Matthew." In *The Expositor''s Bible Commentary*, rev. ed., Vol. 9, edited by Tremper Longman III and David E. Garland, 23-670 (esp. 666-668 on Matt 28:19). Grand Rapids: Zondervan, 2010 (representing the Trinitarian harmonization).', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Acts', 7, 2, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The classic harmonization, traceable to Augustine and made famous in modern apologetics by Gleason Archer, exploits a case difference. In 9:7 *phōnēs* is genitive (*akouō* + genitive), in 22:9 *phōnēn* is accusative (*akouō* + accusative). Archer argues that *akouō* with the genitive means merely to perceive a sound, while with the accusative it means to hear with comprehension — so the companions heard the sound (9:7) but did not understand the words (22:9). Stated at its strongest, the case-distinction would let both verses stand: the men registered an acoustic event without grasping its content.

The construction does not survive examination. The genitive/accusative alternation with *akouō* is not a stable semantic marker of comprehension in Koine. Wallace''s *Greek Grammar Beyond the Basics* cautions explicitly that the case distinction "is not a hard and fast rule" and that counterexamples run both directions; the same caution appears in BDF. Indeed John 5:25 and 5:28 use the genitive of clearly intelligible speech (the dead hearing the voice of the Son of God), and Acts itself uses both cases without the alleged consistency. F. F. Bruce, no skeptic, conceded that the grammatical solution cannot be pressed. The distinction was retrofitted to relieve the tension rather than derived from independent usage.

The harmonization also addresses only one of several discrepancies, leaving the others untouched. The visual reports diverge: 9:7 has the companions *mēdena theōrountes* — seeing no one — while 22:9 has them *to men phōs etheasanto* — seeing the light. And the postural reports diverge across the third retelling: Acts 26:14 has Paul tell Agrippa *pantōn katapesontōn hēmōn eis tēn gēn* — "when we had all fallen to the ground" — against 9:7''s *heistēkeisan*, "they stood." Three narrations of one event yield three configurations of who saw, who heard, who stood, and who fell. No single grammatical maneuver reconciles the set.

The critical explanation, from Haenchen forward and developed by Fitzmyer, is that Luke composed the three accounts independently for their distinct rhetorical settings — third-person narrative in chapter 9, defense before a hostile Jewish crowd in chapter 22, forensic apology before Agrippa and Festus in chapter 26 — and felt no obligation to align the incidental details. Ancient historiography permitted exactly this freedom; speeches were composed in character (as Thucydides 1.22 frankly admits of the genre). The variations are the expected fingerprint of a theological storyteller, not of a stenographer harmonizing a deposition. The companions'' experience is precisely the kind of background detail an author shifts to serve the moment''s emphasis — corroborating the objective reality of the event in chapter 9, isolating Paul as sole recipient of the words in chapter 22.

A formatting note for downstream cleanup: the source reference reads "Acts.22:9" with a period rather than a space, a scraper artifact that should display as "Acts 22:9." Verdict: a genuine contradiction; the case-grammar harmonization is lexically unfounded and in any event addresses only the auditory half of a multi-detail discrepancy.', 'Fitzmyer, Joseph A. *The Acts of the Apostles*. Anchor Bible 31. New York: Doubleday, 1998, pp. 423-433, 704-708, 758-762 (on the three Damascus road retellings).

Haenchen, Ernst. *The Acts of the Apostles: A Commentary*. Philadelphia: Westminster, 1971, pp. 318-322, 624-630 (classic critical treatment).

Wallace, Daniel B. *Greek Grammar Beyond the Basics*. Grand Rapids: Zondervan, 1996, pp. 133-134 (on the unreliability of the *akouō* case distinction).

Bruce, F. F. *The Book of the Acts*, rev. ed. New International Commentary on the New Testament. Grand Rapids: Eerdmans, 1988, pp. 184-188 (conceding the difficulty).

Archer, Gleason L. *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties*. Grand Rapids: Zondervan, 1982, pp. 382-383 (representing the standard apologetic argument).

Note: The bibleReferences entry "Acts.22:9" uses non-standard period-separator formatting; should be "Acts 22:9" for downstream consistency.', 0, NULL, 9, 'NT_internal', 'Acts', 7, 2, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Apologists such as Archer and Geisler typically run a two-step harmonization, and it should be stated at full strength. Step one: the accounts describe different moments of a single dynamic event — all fell at the theophany''s onset (26:14), then the companions rose and stood while Paul remained down (9:7) — so the two postures are sequential, not simultaneous, and not contradictory. Step two: the verb in 9:7, *heistēkeisan*, is a pluperfect of *histēmi* with stative force and can be rendered "stood fixed / remained rooted," connoting paralysis rather than literally standing on the feet, which would soften any clash with 26:14.

Neither step holds. The sequential reading is read into the text, not out of it: Acts 9:7 narrates a state ("stood speechless, hearing... seeing"), not the second beat of a two-stage choreography, and nothing in either passage signals an interval of falling-then-rising. The reconstruction supplies a transition the narrative does not contain in order to relieve the tension. The lexical step fares no better. While *histēmi* in the perfect/pluperfect can carry a settled "be standing" sense, BDAG (s.v. ἵστημι) gives precisely the upright-on-the-feet meaning as the default for these stative forms, and the rhetorical contrast in 26:14 — *everyone fell* — is what throws 9:7''s standing into relief. To gloss *heistēkeisan* as merely "were transfixed" while bodies are flat on the ground in the parallel is special pleading.

The posture discrepancy is also one of a cluster. Across the three retellings (Acts 9, 22, 26) Luke varies who heard the voice (9:7 says the companions heard it; 22:9 says they did not), what they saw (9:7, no one; 22:9, the light), and now whether they stood or fell (9:7 versus 26:14). These are exactly the incidental details that drift when an author recomposes an episode for new audiences and feels no need to cross-check them.

The critical reading, from Haenchen and Conzelmann through Pervo and Fitzmyer, is that Luke reworked the conversion three times to suit three rhetorical settings — narrative report, defense before a Jewish crowd, forensic apology before a Roman client king — and adjusted surface details accordingly. This is consonant with the conventions of ancient historiography, in which an author shaped speeches and their framing freely (Thucydides 1.22 concedes as much for the genre). Pervo in particular reads the prostration in 26:14 as heightening the dramatic and visionary register appropriate to a courtroom *apologia* before Agrippa, where Paul presents himself as the recipient of a commissioning theophany; the standing companions of 9:7 serve the different aim of corroborating the event''s objectivity to the reader. The detail moves because it is doing different narrative work each time.

Verdict: a genuine contradiction. The companions cannot have been both standing (9:7) and fallen (26:14) at the same point of the same event, and the sequential and lexical harmonizations rescue the texts only by importing a stage direction Luke never wrote and a gloss the lexicon does not support.', 'Haenchen, Ernst. *The Acts of the Apostles: A Commentary*. Translated by Bernard Noble and Gerald Shinn. Philadelphia: Westminster, 1971, pp. 322-327, 625-627. — Conzelmann, Hans. *Acts of the Apostles*. Hermeneia. Translated by James Limburg, A. Thomas Kraabel, and Donald H. Juel. Philadelphia: Fortress, 1987, pp. 71-75. — Pervo, Richard I. *Acts*. Hermeneia. Minneapolis: Fortress, 2009, pp. 230-238, 623-629. — Fitzmyer, Joseph A. *The Acts of the Apostles*. Anchor Bible 31. New York: Doubleday, 1998, pp. 423-425, 753-757. — Bauer, Walter, Frederick W. Danker, William F. Arndt, and F. Wilbur Gingrich. *A Greek-English Lexicon of the New Testament and Other Early Christian Literature*. 3rd ed. Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 2000, s.v. ἵστημι. — Archer, Gleason L. *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties*. Grand Rapids: Zondervan, 1982, pp. 382-383. — Geisler, Norman L., and Thomas Howe. *When Critics Ask: A Popular Handbook on Bible Difficulties*. Wheaton: Victor Books, 1992, pp. 437-438.', 0, NULL, 9, 'NT_internal', 'Acts', 7, 2, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard harmonization, found from Calvin through Douglas Moo, turns on the observation that the key terms carry different freight in each author. The Greek verb dikaioō can denote a forensic declaration (Paul''s typical sense: God reckons the believer righteous) or a demonstration/vindication (James''s sense: faith is shown genuine by its fruit). Likewise "works": Paul characteristically attacks erga nomou, "works of the Law" — circumcision, food laws, Sabbath, the boundary markers of Jewish covenant identity — whereas James means concrete deeds of mercy and obedience (feeding the destitute, 2:15-16; offering Isaac, 2:21). On this reading Paul attacks legalism and James attacks dead orthodoxy, and the two are complementary: Paul addresses how one enters righteousness, James how one evidences it. The New Perspective on Paul (Dunn, Sanders) reinforces half of this by showing that Paul''s polemic targets ethnic-covenantal markers, not good deeds in general — Paul too insists faith "works through love" (Gal 5:6) and that God judges "according to works" (Rom 2:6).

This semantic case is genuine and resolves much of the surface friction; hence the verdict of partial harmonization. But it does not dissolve the contradiction, for three reasons. First, both authors invoke the identical Abrahamic narrative and reach grammatically opposite verdicts on it — and James 2:21 grounds justification in the Aqedah (Gen 22), an event the rabbinic tradition (and Jubilees, 1 Maccabees 2:52) already read as the supreme test that "fulfilled" Gen 15:6. James is operating within a Jewish-Christian reading of Abraham that integrates faith and deed; Paul deliberately severs them. Second, the formula "not by faith alone" is most naturally read not as an abstract clarification but as a polemical rejoinder to a slogan in circulation. Luke Timothy Johnson and Dale Allison argue that James engages, and corrects, a misapplied Paulinism — perhaps not Paul himself but a libertine reading of "justified by faith apart from works of Law" (Rom 3:28) flattened into "faith alone." Third, the two letters emerge from different streams of earliest Christianity with different relationships to Torah observance: the Jerusalem-Jacobite tradition behind James and the gentile-mission theology of the undisputed Paulines. Richard Bauckham situates James within Jewish wisdom paraenesis where faith and obedience are inseparable, a milieu where Paul''s antithesis would itself sound dangerous.

The canonical editors placed both letters side by side, and later harmonizers smoothed over what was, historically, a live dispute about the relationship of faith and deed in the wake of Paul''s gentile mission. The semantic distinction is real and the authors are not crudely contradicting each other word for word — but they are not merely complementary either. Verdict: partially harmonizable at the level of authorial intent, but a genuine theological tension over the same proof text that the standard distinction softens rather than eliminates.', 'Martin Luther, "Preface to the New Testament" and "Preface to the Epistles of St. James and St. Jude" (1522), in *Luther''s Works* 35, ed. E. Theodore Bachmann (Philadelphia: Muhlenberg, 1960), pp. 357-362, 395-398, on the James/Romans tension.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *Romans* (Anchor Bible 33; Doubleday, 1993), pp. 372-380, on Romans 4:2-5.
James D. G. Dunn, *Romans 1-8* (Word Biblical Commentary 38A; Word, 1988), pp. 197-205, on ''works of the Law'' in Romans 4.
Luke Timothy Johnson, *The Letter of James* (Anchor Bible 37A; Doubleday, 1995), pp. 58-64, 237-252, on James and Paulinism.
Dale C. Allison Jr., *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Epistle of James* (International Critical Commentary; Bloomsbury T&T Clark, 2013), pp. 426-462, on James 2:14-26.
Richard Bauckham, *James: Wisdom of James, Disciple of Jesus the Sage* (Routledge, 1999), pp. 113-140, on faith and works in James.
Douglas J. Moo, *The Letter of James* (Pillar New Testament Commentary; Eerdmans, 2000), pp. 134-145, on harmonizing James and Paul.', 0, NULL, 4, 'NT_internal', 'Romans, James', 5, 3, 5, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The traditional harmonization, articulated by Wayne Grudem, Andreas Köstenberger, and Thomas Schreiner, holds that the two texts illuminate different facets of one event. Romans speaks of representative or "federal" headship: Adam acted on behalf of humanity, so sin and its penalty are imputed through him as covenant head, regardless of who sinned first chronologically. 1 Timothy speaks of the order and manner of the fall: the woman was created second (2:13) and deceived first (2:14), and that sequence furnishes the rationale for the teaching restriction. On this reading the two passages are complementary — one assigns juridical responsibility, the other notes chronological and psychological priority — and a careful reader need not choose between them. This is a real distinction and accounts for some of the difference, which is why the tension is partially, not fully, resolved.

The distinction nevertheless strains at a precise point. 1 Timothy 2:14 does not simply say Eve was deceived; it explicitly contrasts her with Adam, who "was not deceived," thereby easing Adam off the deception hook in a way Romans never anticipates and Genesis 3 does not warrant — Genesis 3:6 has Adam standing "with her" and eating without protest, and 3:17 holds him accountable for heeding his wife. The author of 1 Timothy is selectively reworking the narrative to serve a rhetorical end. That observation dovetails with the dominant critical judgment that 1 Timothy is deutero-Pauline. On linguistic grounds (a distinctive vocabulary with dozens of hapax legomena, a more developed ecclesiology of bishops and deacons, the "sound doctrine" formulae) and historical grounds (an institutionalized church order absent from the undisputed letters), Bart Ehrman, Margaret MacDonald, and Raymond Collins place the Pastorals a generation or more after Paul, written in his name to consolidate a patriarchal household-church structure. On that view the contradiction is not two complementary facets of a coordinated apostolic doctrine but two authors — Paul in Romans, a later Paulinist in 1 Timothy — reading Genesis 3 toward different ends, the one for cosmic soteriology, the other for the regulation of women in the assembly.

Thus the harmonization works at the level of abstract dogmatics (responsibility versus chronology of deception) while leaving a residue the abstraction cannot absorb: the explicit exoneration of Adam from deception in 1 Timothy, and the probable difference of authorship and agenda. It is worth noting that Romans 5 and 1 Timothy 2 are not strictly answering the same question — Paul asks how death came to all, the Pastor asks why women must not teach — so they are not point-blank contradictory propositions. But neither are they the seamless single doctrine the harmonizers describe. Verdict: partially harmonizable as differing emphases, but most plausibly two authors with divergent purposes whose statements about Adam and Eve do not fully cohere.', 'Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *Romans* (Anchor Bible 33; Doubleday, 1993), pp. 405-428, on Romans 5:12-21.
Robert Jewett, *Romans: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 374-385, on Romans 5:12.
Bart D. Ehrman, *Forgery and Counterforgery: The Use of Literary Deceit in Early Christian Polemics* (Oxford University Press, 2013), pp. 192-222, on the deutero-Pauline Pastorals.
Raymond F. Collins, *I & II Timothy and Titus* (New Testament Library; Westminster John Knox, 2002), pp. 65-82, on 1 Timothy 2:13-14.
Margaret Y. MacDonald, *The Pauline Churches* (SNTSMS 60; Cambridge University Press, 1988), pp. 159-202, on institutionalization in the deutero-Paulines.
I. Howard Marshall, *The Pastoral Epistles* (International Critical Commentary; T&T Clark, 1999), pp. 462-467, on 1 Timothy 2:14.
Wayne Grudem, *Evangelical Feminism and Biblical Truth* (Multnomah, 2004), pp. 213-220, on federal headship and Eve''s deception.
Andreas J. Köstenberger and Thomas R. Schreiner, eds., *Women in the Church*, 3rd ed. (Crossway, 2016), pp. 117-167, on 1 Timothy 2:9-15.', 0, NULL, 4, 'NT_internal', 'Romans, 1 Timothy', 5, 3, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The apologetic harmonization — developed by Murray Harris and F. F. Bruce on Colossians, and consistent with the Hebrew usage — observes precisely this: bekor/prototokos is a title of preeminence and inheritance that by its nature can be conferred on different figures within different frames of reference. There is no logical contradiction in Israel being God''s firstborn nation, Ephraim the firstborn tribe of the north, the Davidic king the firstborn among earthly rulers, and Christ the firstborn over creation and resurrection, because each claim operates in its own domain and none asserts exclusive biological priority. This case is strong, and it is reinforced by the Bible''s recurring "younger displaces elder" motif (Isaac over Ishmael, Jacob over Esau, Ephraim over Manasseh, David the youngest of Jesse''s sons), which shows that the canon treats "firstborn" status as an elective gift God assigns rather than a fact of birth. Given that the texts themselves use the word non-literally and in mutually exclusive domains, this is best classified as harmonized, not as a contradiction.

What critical scholarship adds is not a logical contradiction but a diachronic observation: the repeated transfer of the title is evidence of a developing theology that recoded chosen-ness for successive political and religious situations. Jon Levenson traces how the "beloved/firstborn son" motif migrates across the tradition and is ultimately christologized. The Ephraim claim in Jeremiah reflects northern self-understanding and the hope of reunification; the Israel claim in Exodus encodes national election against Egypt; the christological claim represents the early Christian movement assigning Israel''s distinctives to its messiah. So the data carry real historical significance — they index how a community reused a loaded covenantal term — without amounting to incompatible propositions. The only place the harmonization meets resistance is rhetorical: if one insists that "firstborn" must mark a unique, singular, non-transferable status, then multiple bearers do conflict. But that insistence is not how the biblical authors used the word; they used it flexibly and self-consciously, applying it to younger sons and to corporate and royal entities alike. Verdict: overstated as a contradiction — the term is a transferable title of rank, and the multiple referents reflect theological development rather than logical incompatibility.', 'Brevard S. Childs, *The Book of Exodus* (Old Testament Library; Westminster, 1974), pp. 100-104, on Exodus 4:22.
Jack R. Lundbom, *Jeremiah 21-36* (Anchor Bible 21B; Doubleday, 2004), pp. 421-428, on Jeremiah 31:9.
Jon D. Levenson, *The Death and Resurrection of the Beloved Son* (Yale University Press, 1993), pp. 36-52, 173-199, on the firstborn-son motif.
