The strongest harmonization, advanced by Douglas Moo and Thomas Schreiner, distinguishes two different speech-acts. Romans 12:14 forbids personal, retaliatory cursing — answering a persecutor''s hostility with a counter-curse, repaying evil for evil (the very thing 12:17 goes on to prohibit). Galatians 1:8 and 1 Corinthians 16:22, by contrast, are formal pronouncements that consign false teachers and Christ-rejecters to divine judgment; anathema in this usage (well documented by David Aune) reflects the Septuagint herem, the devoting of something to God for destruction, and functions as a judicial or covenantal sanction rather than personal spite. On this reading Paul is not nursing a grudge; he is invoking God''s verdict on those who corrupt the gospel, which is a categorically different act from cursing one''s enemies. This distinction is real — the genre and object of the two utterances genuinely differ — and so the tension is partially relieved.

But the harmonization does not fully hold, for two reasons. First, it is not textually marked in Romans 12:14: the verb katarasthe is unqualified, and Paul provides no carve-out for doctrinal opponents or for "judicial" as opposed to "personal" cursing. The exception that rescues consistency is imported by the interpreter, not stated by Paul. Second, and more pointedly, Paul deploys the anathema precisely at the moment his own gospel and apostolic authority are under attack. Galatians is, as Hans Dieter Betz and J. Louis Martyn document, a white-knuckle polemical letter whose emotional register is unmistakably personal; Paul''s opponents are not abstract heretics but rival missionaries threatening his churches, and his rhetoric draws on the conventions of first-century juridical curse-language. The line between "invoking God''s judgment" and "cursing the people who are undermining me" is, in practice, exactly where Paul stands. The distinction between personal animosity and ecclesial sanction is a sophisticated later-theological refinement; on the ground, Paul curses those who endanger his gospel while instructing others not to curse those who endanger them.

So the contradiction is real but not crude. Paul almost certainly did not perceive himself as violating Romans 12:14, because in his own mind the anathema was a sacral verdict, not a vendetta. The genre distinction his defenders draw is defensible and accounts for the difference in kind. What it cannot do is square the absolute wording of Romans 12:14 with Paul''s own readiness to curse, or erase the fact that the principle is applied selectively the instant his theology is threatened. Verdict: partially harmonizable as a difference between personal cursing and formal anathema, but the unqualified command of Romans 12:14 and Paul''s self-interested application leave a genuine residue of inconsistency.', 'Robert Jewett, *Romans: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 765-770, on Romans 12:14.
J. Louis Martyn, *Galatians* (Anchor Bible 33A; Doubleday, 1997), pp. 111-114, on Galatians 1:8-9.
Hans Dieter Betz, *Galatians: A Commentary on Paul''s Letter to the Churches in Galatia* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1979), pp. 50-54, on Galatians 1:8-9.
Anthony C. Thiselton, *The First Epistle to the Corinthians* (New International Greek Testament Commentary; Eerdmans, 2000), pp. 1346-1352, on 1 Corinthians 16:22.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *First Corinthians* (Anchor Yale Bible 32; Yale University Press, 2008), pp. 626-628, on 1 Corinthians 16:22.
Douglas J. Moo, *The Letter to the Romans* (New International Commentary on the New Testament; Eerdmans, 1996), pp. 781-783, on Romans 12:14.
Thomas R. Schreiner, *Romans* (Baker Exegetical Commentary on the New Testament; Baker, 1998), pp. 668-672, on Romans 12:14.
David E. Aune, "Anathema," in *Anchor Bible Dictionary*, vol. 1 (Doubleday, 1992), pp. 220-221, on the anathema/herem background.', 0, NULL, 5, 'NT_internal', 'Romans, 1 Corinthians, Galatians', 6, 2, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard harmonization, articulated by F. F. Bruce and Richard Longenecker, locates the difference in object and motive. The two affirming texts are explicitly qualified: one pleases the neighbor "for his good, to build him up" (Rom 15:2), and "that they may be saved" (1 Cor 10:33) — this is other-directed accommodation in the service of the gospel, the same flexible self-adaptation Paul describes in 1 Corinthians 9:19-22 ("I have become all things to all people"). Galatians 1:10, by contrast, repudiates a self-serving people-pleasing: courting human approval to secure one''s own standing or to dodge the offense of the cross (cf. Gal 5:11). On this reading Paul opposes pleasing people as a substitute for obedience to God, while commending pleasing people as a mode of loving service — two different acts that share a verb. This distinction is genuinely present in the qualifiers Paul attaches, which is why the tension is partially resolved rather than left raw.

What the distinction does not fully absorb is the rhetorical surface. 1 Corinthians 10:33 states the principle without restriction — "I please all people in all things" — and Galatians 1:10 makes pleasing men and being Christ''s servant structurally opposed, not merely differently motivated. The qualifying phrase that does the harmonizing work in Romans and 1 Corinthians has no counterpart in Galatians, where the verb is used absolutely and negatively. The most economical historical explanation is the polemical Sitz im Leben of Galatians 1. As Hans Dieter Betz and J. Louis Martyn observe, the letter''s opening is forensic self-defense: Paul is rebutting a specific charge of being a crowd-pleasing, second-hand apostle dependent on Jerusalem, and his denial is framed as sharply as the accusation. Under attack he overcorrects, casting all "pleasing of men" in the worst light; in the calmer pastoral registers of Romans and 1 Corinthians he can commend accommodating one''s neighbor as an expression of love. J. Paul Sampley and others have documented this very flexibility as a feature of Paul''s missionary method, which is precisely what makes the Galatians denial sound, on its face, like a contradiction of his own practice.

So the contradiction is real at the level of unqualified wording and rhetorical stance, while dissolving substantially at the level of intent: Paul is consistent in subordinating all human approval to faithfulness to God, and his "pleasing" of neighbors is always instrumentally aimed at their salvation, never at his own reputation. The harmonization succeeds in showing that Paul is not endorsing and forbidding the identical act, but it succeeds by supplying from Romans and 1 Corinthians a motive-qualifier that Galatians 1:10 itself does not state. Verdict: partially harmonizable — a coherent underlying ethic obscured by Paul''s polemically heightened rhetoric in Galatians, leaving a verbal but not a substantive contradiction.', 'J. Louis Martyn, *Galatians* (Anchor Bible 33A; Doubleday, 1997), pp. 137-142, on Galatians 1:10.
Hans Dieter Betz, *Galatians* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1979), pp. 54-57, on Galatians 1:10.
Robert Jewett, *Romans: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 877-880, on Romans 15:2.
Anthony C. Thiselton, *The First Epistle to the Corinthians* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 2000), pp. 793-797, on 1 Corinthians 10:33.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *First Corinthians* (Anchor Yale Bible 32; Yale University Press, 2008), pp. 405-407, on 1 Corinthians 10:33.
F. F. Bruce, *The Epistle to the Galatians* (New International Greek Testament Commentary; Eerdmans, 1982), pp. 84-86, on Galatians 1:10.
Richard N. Longenecker, *Galatians* (Word Biblical Commentary 41; Word, 1990), pp. 19-23, on Galatians 1:10.
J. Paul Sampley, ed., *Paul in the Greco-Roman World: A Handbook* (Trinity Press International, 2003), pp. 240-262, on Pauline accommodation.', 0, NULL, 5, 'NT_internal', 'Romans, 1 Corinthians, Galatians', 6, 2, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

Apologists such as Norman Geisler and Thomas Howe make exactly this point, and on the lexical merits they are correct: Genesis describes linguistic scattering, 1 Corinthians 1 describes rhetorical shaming, and 1 Corinthians 14 describes liturgical disorder, and a charitable reading can hold all three without contradiction. The harmonization is genuine and should be acknowledged plainly.

What the harmonization does not fully dissolve is a substantive theological residue. Even granting the three distinct words, the underlying portrait is of a deity who actively deploys cognitive and social disruption as an instrument of judgment and reversal — confounding the builders at Babel, shaming the wise through the cross, overturning human estimations of strength and weakness. Source critics read Genesis 11:1-9 as a J-source etiology of linguistic diversity, of a piece with J''s other primeval stories (Eden, Cain, the flood, the sons of God) that explain present human conditions through divine intervention; Speiser and Westermann both treat the scattering as Yahweh checking human hubris, the same theme as the expulsion from Eden. Paul, meanwhile, reworks an Isaianic reversal motif (Isa 29:14, which he quotes in 1 Cor 1:19) in which God overturns the wisdom of the wise. So the OT and Pauline texts agree that God disrupts human understanding when human understanding becomes a rival to him.

The 1 Corinthians 14:33 statement, by contrast, is a narrow pastoral claim: God''s character does not produce tumult in the worshipping assembly. It says nothing about whether God may scatter languages or shame the proud in the wider economy of judgment. The two are therefore reconcilable, but only because they operate at different levels — one a lexical-pastoral claim about worship order, the others claims about divine action in history and salvation. A reader working from the KJV alone, encountering "confound" in Genesis, "confound" in 1 Cor 1:27, and "confusion" in 1 Cor 14:33, would reasonably perceive a flat contradiction; that perception is an artifact of translation rather than of the underlying texts. Verdict: largely harmonizable as a translation artifact, with a real but lesser theological tension about divine cognitive disruption remaining.', 'E. A. Speiser, *Genesis* (Anchor Bible 1; Doubleday, 1964), pp. 75-76, on the Babel etiology and the balal/Babel wordplay.
Claus Westermann, *Genesis 1-11: A Continental Commentary*, trans. John J. Scullion (Fortress, 1994), pp. 542-557, on the J-source primeval history and divine response to human hubris.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *First Corinthians* (Anchor Yale Bible 32; Yale University Press, 2008), pp. 161-167, 526-528, on kataischynō and akatastasia.
Anthony C. Thiselton, *The First Epistle to the Corinthians* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 2000), pp. 184-194, 1146-1151, on Paul''s reversal rhetoric and akatastasia.
Walter Bauer, Frederick W. Danker, William F. Arndt, and F. Wilbur Gingrich, *A Greek-English Lexicon of the New Testament and Other Early Christian Literature*, 3rd ed. (University of Chicago Press, 2000), s.v. ἀκαταστασία, καταισχύνω.
Norman L. Geisler and Thomas Howe, *When Critics Ask* (Victor Books, 1992), pp. 451-452, distinguishing the three senses of ''confusion.''', 0, NULL, 2, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Genesis, 1 Corinthians', 5, 3, 2, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

Ephesians 2:19-20 redirects the metaphor. The household of God is built ἐπὶ τῷ θεμελίῳ τῶν ἀποστόλων καὶ προφητῶν, "upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets," with Christ Jesus himself as the ἀκρογωνιαῖος — a term debated between "cornerstone" and "capstone/keystone," but in either case distinguished from the foundation rather than identified with it. The exegetical hinge is the genitive τῶν ἀποστόλων καὶ προφητῶν. Read as a genitive of source or possession, the apostles and prophets ARE the foundation, and Christ is a separate architectural element. Read as an epexegetical or appositional genitive ("the foundation that consists of the apostles and prophets"), it still makes them the foundation, but apologetic readers gloss this as shorthand for their Christ-proclaiming witness, leaving Christ the deeper substance.

The strongest harmonizing case, advanced by Harold Hoehner, Peter O''Brien, and Markus Barth, runs as follows: "foundation" and "cornerstone" are complementary, not competitive, architectural roles; the apostles and prophets are foundational only as bearers of the gospel about Christ, who as cornerstone determines the whole structure''s alignment and bears its decisive weight; therefore Christ remains substantively foundational in Ephesians just as in 1 Corinthians, with no contradiction. This reading is grammatically possible and theologically coherent, and it deserves to be taken seriously. The cornerstone image itself draws on Isaiah 28:16 ("a tested stone, a precious cornerstone") and Psalm 118:22, a christological stone-testimonium widespread in early Christianity (cf. 1 Pet 2:6-8; Rom 9:33), which supports reading the Ephesian Christ as the controlling, foundational stone.

Nevertheless the harmonization is incomplete. The more natural reading of the genitive in context is source or possession — the apostles and prophets as the actual foundation layer — precisely because Ephesians elsewhere treats the apostles and prophets as constitutive, historically anchored offices (Eph 3:5; 4:11). That marks a shift from the ecclesiology of the undisputed Paul. Critical scholarship widely judges Ephesians deutero-Pauline (Best, Lincoln, MacDonald), composed after Paul''s death by an author developing Pauline themes into a more institutional, retrospective ecclesiology in which the founding generation of apostles and prophets has become a fixed historical stratum on which the later church is built. Markus Barth and Hoehner dissent in favor of authenticity, but even on Pauline authorship the metaphor''s center of gravity has moved. In 1 Corinthians 3, written into a live charismatic congregation, Christ alone is foundation and human teachers are merely builders who can build well or badly on it; in Ephesians, the apostles and prophets have themselves become foundational. The tension is therefore best described as a genuine development in ecclesiology rather than a flat logical contradiction: the same building metaphor is being used to make two different points about where the church''s grounding lies. Verdict: harmonizable at the level of intent (Christ remains decisive in both) but reflecting a real shift from Christ-as-sole-foundation to apostles-and-prophets-as-foundation, consistent with the deutero-Pauline trajectory.', 'Ernest Best, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on Ephesians* (International Critical Commentary; T&T Clark, 1998), pp. 280-285, on Ephesians 2:20.
Andrew T. Lincoln, *Ephesians* (Word Biblical Commentary 42; Word, 1990), pp. 153-156, on Ephesians 2:19-20.
Margaret Y. MacDonald, *Colossians and Ephesians* (Sacra Pagina 17; Liturgical Press, 2000), pp. 250-255, on the ecclesiology of Ephesians.
Anthony C. Thiselton, *The First Epistle to the Corinthians* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 2000), pp. 308-315, on 1 Corinthians 3:9-11.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *First Corinthians* (Anchor Yale Bible 32; Yale University Press, 2008), pp. 194-198, on 1 Corinthians 3:11.
Harold W. Hoehner, *Ephesians: An Exegetical Commentary* (Baker, 2002), pp. 397-405, on Ephesians 2:20.
Peter T. O''Brien, *The Letter to the Ephesians* (Pillar New Testament Commentary; Eerdmans, 1999), pp. 213-217, on Ephesians 2:20.
Markus Barth, *Ephesians 1-3* (Anchor Bible 34; Doubleday, 1974), pp. 314-320, on Ephesians 2:20 and the cornerstone tradition.', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', '1 Corinthians, Ephesians', 5, 3, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

Against this, 1 Corinthians 7 shows the historical Paul explicitly grading his own authority. In 7:10 he gives a command "not I but the Lord" — appealing to a dominical saying on divorce (cf. Mark 10:9-12). In 7:12 he shifts: "to the rest I say — I, not the Lord." In 7:25 he goes further: περὶ δὲ τῶν παρθένων ἐπιταγὴν κυρίου οὐκ ἔχω, γνώμην δὲ δίδωμι — "concerning virgins I have no command of the Lord, but I give my opinion (gnōmē)." Paul distinguishes levels of authority precisely because he does not regard his own pastoral ruling as carrying the same weight as a saying traceable to Jesus. The author of 2 Maccabees closes (15:38-39) by asking pardon if his composition is poorly done — a frank authorial self-disclaimer that sits awkwardly with theopneustic inspiration, and which matters specifically for the Catholic and Orthodox canons in which 2 Maccabees is scripture.

The classic apologetic harmonization, from B. B. Warfield onward, makes two moves. First, Paul''s "I, not the Lord" distinguishes which traditions stem from Jesus'' earthly teaching from Paul''s own Spirit-guided apostolic judgment, but both, on this view, are inspired in the canonical sense; indeed in 7:40 Paul adds "and I think that I too have the Spirit of God." Second, theopneustos describes the divine origin of the texts without erasing the human authors'' literary self-awareness, so an authorial humility-formula need not negate inspiration. The first move is partly successful: Paul is plainly distinguishing dominical command from apostolic counsel, not disclaiming all authority, and 7:40 shows he still claims the Spirit. So the relation between 2 Tim 3:16 and 1 Cor 7 need not be a flat contradiction at the level the authors intended.

But the harmonization overreaches when it flattens Paul''s gradient into uniform plenary inspiration. Paul''s own surface meaning is that his ruling on virgins is gnōmē, opinion, expressly not an ἐπιταγὴ κυρίου; reading that as functionally identical in authority to a divine command imposes a later doctrinal grid on the text. Critical scholars (Ehrman, Marshall as a moderating voice, Collins) note further that 2 Timothy is most likely deutero-Pauline, written after Paul''s death; if so, its sweeping inspiration claim cannot retroactively bind the historical Paul''s self-understanding, and certainly was not framed to cover the very NT documents and the deuterocanonical 2 Maccabees that later inerrantists include. The 2 Maccabees disclaimer is then doubly awkward, since it explicitly invites the reader to pardon imperfection. Verdict: harmonizable at the level of the authors'' own claims (2 Tim 3:16 concerns the Jewish scriptures; Paul grades authority without denying it), but a genuine contradiction against a later doctrine of plenary verbal inerrancy applied to the whole canon.', 'Bart D. Ehrman, *Forgery and Counterforgery* (Oxford University Press, 2013), pp. 192-222, on the pseudepigraphy of the Pastoral Epistles.
James D. G. Dunn, *The Theology of Paul the Apostle* (Eerdmans, 1998), pp. 174-179, on Paul''s appeal to and distinction from dominical tradition.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *First Corinthians* (Anchor Yale Bible 32; Yale University Press, 2008), pp. 287-296, 314-318, on 1 Corinthians 7:12, 25.
Anthony C. Thiselton, *The First Epistle to the Corinthians* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 2000), pp. 519-526, 568-579, on Paul''s distinction of command from counsel.
I. Howard Marshall, *The Pastoral Epistles* (International Critical Commentary; T&T Clark, 1999), pp. 786-797, on theopneustos and the syntax of 2 Timothy 3:16.
Raymond F. Collins, *I & II Timothy and Titus* (New Testament Library; Westminster John Knox, 2002), pp. 257-263, on the scope of ''scripture'' in 2 Timothy 3:15-16.
Jonathan A. Goldstein, *II Maccabees* (Anchor Bible 41A; Doubleday, 1983), pp. 502-504, on the epilogue and authorial disclaimer.
Benjamin B. Warfield, *The Inspiration and Authority of the Bible* (Presbyterian and Reformed, 1948), pp. 131-166, on theopneustos and plenary inspiration.', 0, NULL, 2, 'OT_vs_NT', '2 Maccabees, 1 Corinthians, 2 Timothy', 5, 3, 3, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The strongest pastoral harmonization, articulated by Murray Harris among others, distinguishes the scenarios: 1 Corinthians 7 addresses marriages already in existence before one partner''s conversion, where the believer is to remain for the sake of peace and possible salvation of the spouse (7:16); 2 Corinthians 6 prohibits forming new compromising entanglements with unbelievers — and on some readings is not primarily about marriage at all but about partnership with idolatry and with Paul''s opponents. Stated this way the directives need not collide: stay in the marriage you have; do not contract fresh alliances that yoke you to idolatry. This is a coherent pastoral logic and deserves a fair hearing.

Its limits are two. First, 2 Cor 6:14-7:1 is framed broadly enough ("what partnership... what fellowship... what agreement") that it resists confinement to new marriages, and its cultic dualism of clean/unclean stands in direct conceptual tension with Paul''s sanctification-by-contact theology in 1 Cor 7:14, where the unbeliever is positively sanctified through the believer. Second, and decisively for the authorship level, a large strand of critical scholarship judges 2 Cor 6:14-7:1 a non-Pauline interpolation. Hans Dieter Betz argued it is positively anti-Pauline; Furnish and Thrall note its concentration of hapax legomena, its sharply dualistic, Qumran-like vocabulary (Beliar/Belial, light vs. darkness, the temple imagery, the catena of OT quotations), its theology of separation foreign to Paul''s surrounding argument, and the smooth join that results when 6:13 is read directly into 7:2 ("make room for us"). Fitzmyer''s classic study highlighted the passage''s affinities with the Dead Sea Scrolls'' sectarian separatism. If that judgment holds, the contradiction is not within Paul at all but between Paul and an inserted fragment.

That text-critical solution, however, only relocates the problem. Within the canonical text as transmitted and read by communities, two opposed directives stand side by side: the believer sanctifies the unbelieving spouse, and the believer must not touch the unclean thing or be yoked to unbelievers. Verdict: partially harmonizable — eased by a scenario distinction and very plausibly dissolved at the authorial level if 2 Cor 6:14-7:1 is an interpolation, but a real tension remains in the received text.', 'Victor Paul Furnish, *II Corinthians* (Anchor Bible 32A; Doubleday, 1984), pp. 359-383, on 2 Corinthians 6:14-7:1 as a possible interpolation.
Margaret E. Thrall, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Second Epistle to the Corinthians*, vol. 1 (International Critical Commentary; T&T Clark, 1994), pp. 25-36, 472-481, on the integrity of 2 Cor 6:14-7:1.
Hans Dieter Betz, "2 Cor. 6:14-7:1: An Anti-Pauline Fragment?" *Journal of Biblical Literature* 92 (1973): 88-108.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, "Qumran and the Interpolated Paragraph in 2 Cor 6:14-7:1," *Catholic Biblical Quarterly* 23 (1961): 271-280.
Anthony C. Thiselton, *The First Epistle to the Corinthians* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 2000), pp. 526-540, on 1 Corinthians 7:12-14.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *First Corinthians* (Anchor Yale Bible 32; Yale University Press, 2008), pp. 297-302, on 1 Corinthians 7:12-14.
Murray J. Harris, *The Second Epistle to the Corinthians* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 2005), pp. 498-512, on 2 Corinthians 6:14-7:1.', 0, NULL, 5, 'NT_internal', '1 Corinthians, 2 Corinthians', 6, 2, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

Within that frame Paul''s affirmations of tongues are genuine, not grudging. In 14:2 ὁ γὰρ λαλῶν γλώσσῃ οὐκ ἀνθρώποις λαλεῖ ἀλλὰ θεῷ — the tongues-speaker addresses God, not people; in 14:4 he ἑαυτὸν οἰκοδομεῖ, "edifies himself"; in 14:5 Paul says θέλω δὲ πάντας ὑμᾶς λαλεῖν γλώσσαις, "I want you all to speak in tongues," immediately qualified by "but rather that you prophesy"; and in 14:18 he claims εὐχαριστῶ τῷ θεῷ, πάντων ὑμῶν μᾶλλον γλώσσαις λαλῶ, "I thank God that I speak in tongues more than all of you." These are not the words of someone discouraging the practice.

The 14:8-9 trumpet image, on which the "No" answer rests, is therefore being misread when taken as a blanket rejection. The figure — an indistinct bugle call leaves soldiers unprepared, so unintelligible words are "spoken into the air" — targets *uninterpreted* tongues in corporate worship specifically. Paul''s solution is not to ban tongues but to require interpretation: "one who speaks in a tongue should pray that he may interpret" (14:13), and in the assembly tongues are permitted in order, "two or at most three, and each in turn, and let one interpret" (14:27). The whole regulatory apparatus presupposes the legitimacy of the gift it is regulating.

This reading is not a confessional special pleading; it is the consensus of critical and apologetic commentators alike. Gordon Fee, Anthony Thiselton, and Joseph Fitzmyer all treat the chapter as internally coherent once Paul''s public/private and interpreted/uninterpreted distinctions are respected, and Christopher Forbes situates the phenomenon within its Hellenistic environment without finding Paul self-contradictory. The decisive datum against the SAB framing is the chapter''s own conclusion: 14:39, ὥστε... τὸ λαλεῖν μὴ κωλύετε γλώσσαις — "so then, desire earnestly to prophesy, and do not forbid speaking in tongues." An author closing a passage by explicitly prohibiting any ban on tongues is not the same author elsewhere forbidding tongues; he is calibrating their use.

Where a residual tension can be located, it is not the yes-versus-no of this entry but the gap between Paul''s evident personal enthusiasm for tongues (14:5, 14:18) and his firm subordination of them to intelligible speech in worship — a tension of priority, which Paul himself acknowledges and manages. Reading the chapter as a flat contradiction flattens a deliberate, pastorally calibrated rhetoric of "both/and, but in order." Verdict: overstated — the chapter is a regulated endorsement of tongues, not a self-contradiction.', 'Gordon D. Fee, *The First Epistle to the Corinthians*, rev. ed. (New International Commentary on the New Testament; Eerdmans, 2014), pp. 690-740, on 1 Corinthians 14.
Anthony C. Thiselton, *The First Epistle to the Corinthians* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 2000), pp. 1074-1110, on 1 Corinthians 14:8-9.
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *First Corinthians* (Anchor Yale Bible 32; Yale University Press, 2008), pp. 510-528, on 1 Corinthians 14.
Christopher Forbes, *Prophecy and Inspired Speech in Early Christianity and Its Hellenistic Environment* (WUNT 2/75; Mohr Siebeck, 1995), pp. 75-94, on glossolalia in its environment.
Clint Tibbs, *Religious Experience of the Pneuma: Communication with the Spirit World in 1 Corinthians 12 and 14* (WUNT 2/230; Mohr Siebeck, 2007), pp. 121-180, on glossolalia in 1 Corinthians.', 0, NULL, 5, 'NT_internal', '1 Corinthians', 6, 2, 2, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard apologetic harmonization, defended by Gleason Archer and Norman Geisler, observes that Acts 9:3-7 narrates the actual encounter — a blinding light and a voice — while 9:8 narrates only the subsequent physical blindness. On this reading Paul did "see" Jesus in the light at the moment of the encounter, and 9:8 simply reports that afterward his eyes registered nothing. As an account of Acts'' own internal logic this is reasonable: nothing in 9:8 denies that something was seen earlier. But it does not dissolve the underlying tension, because Acts never actually narrates Paul seeing the risen *Christ*. The Lukan conversion accounts (told three times, with telling variation: Acts 9, 22, 26) describe a light from heaven and an auditory exchange; in 9:7 the companions hear the voice but see no one, while in 22:9 they see the light but do not hear the voice — a discrepancy in its own right. What none of the three accounts supplies is a visual perception of Jesus. Only in the speech before Agrippa (26:16) does the risen Lord say "I have appeared to you" (*ōphthēn soi*), and even there the seeing is framed as a heavenly vision (26:19, *optasia*).

Critical scholars including John Knox, Hans Conzelmann, Joseph Fitzmyer, and Alan Segal note that Paul''s *ōphthē* in 1 Cor 15 belongs to the vocabulary of theophanic and revelatory disclosure rather than ordinary optical sight; in the Septuagint the passive of *horaō* with a dative regularly renders God or angels "appearing" to the patriarchs (Gen 12:7; 17:1; Exod 3:2). Paul reinforces this in Galatians 1:16, where he describes the event as God being pleased "to reveal his Son in me" (*apokalypsai ton huion autou en emoi*). Segal situates the experience within Jewish merkabah and apocalyptic visionary tradition, and Dale Allison and Bart Ehrman likewise treat it as visionary. The significance is that Paul lists his experience as the same *kind* of event as the appearances to the Twelve — "last of all" in a single series — which means that whatever category Paul''s Damascus experience belongs to, the earlier appearances arguably belong there too. This is why the contradiction matters beyond a surface discrepancy: it bears on whether the foundational resurrection appearances were understood, by the earliest witness we possess, as visionary disclosures or as encounters with a physically present body. Luke and Paul are working with different conceptions of the event — Paul names it seeing the risen Lord; Luke renders a Christophany-without-visible-Christ followed by blindness — and the two cannot be flattened into a single coherent account of what Paul''s eyes did or did not register. Verdict: harmonizable for Acts'' internal narrative, but a genuine conceptual tension between Paul''s first-person claim to have seen Jesus and Luke''s account in which he sees only light and then nothing.', 'Alan F. Segal, *Paul the Convert: The Apostolate and Apostasy of Saul the Pharisee* (New Haven: Yale University Press, 1990), pp. 34-71, on the Damascus experience as Jewish visionary tradition.
Dale C. Allison Jr., *Resurrecting Jesus: The Earliest Christian Tradition and Its Interpreters* (London: T&T Clark, 2005), pp. 269-299, on the visionary character of the appearances.
Bart D. Ehrman, *How Jesus Became God: The Exaltation of a Jewish Preacher from Galilee* (New York: HarperOne, 2014), pp. 132-174, on Paul''s experience as a vision.
