The strongest apologetic harmonization, framed by D. A. Carson and reflected in Thomas Schreiner''s treatment of divine hardening in Romans 9, distinguishes ultimate from proximate causation within a compatibilist frame: God sovereignly ordains hardening as a judicial response to prior human rejection (note John 12:37, where the blinding follows refusal to believe), Satan serves as the executing instrument, and human beings remain responsible for their own unbelief. On this layered reading all three statements can stand simultaneously — God ordains, Satan effects, humans choose — and there is real biblical precedent for God using a malign agent to accomplish judgment (1 Kings 22:19-23, the lying spirit; the hardening of Pharaoh in Exodus, where both "God hardened" and "Pharaoh hardened" appear). This framework genuinely dissolves the formal logical contradiction.

The difficulty is that the resolution is imported rather than exegeted. None of the three texts articulates a doctrine of layered causation; each names a single agent for the single act of blinding, and they name different ones. Critical scholarship — Raymond Brown on John, Victor Furnish and Margaret Thrall on 2 Corinthians, Abraham Malherbe and Charles Wanamaker on Thessalonians — reads these as independent theodicies generated in different communities facing different pastoral problems. The Johannine community had to explain massive Jewish rejection of Jesus and reached for Isaiah''s hardening commission, the same scriptural resource Paul uses in Romans 9-11. The Corinthian correspondence had to explain rejection of Paul''s own preaching and reached for the apocalyptic image of a cosmic adversary who blinds (consonant with the dualism Neil Forsyth traces in Second Temple Satan traditions). 2 Thessalonians — widely regarded as deutero-Pauline precisely on grounds of its developed, schematized eschatology — builds an end-time scenario in which God''s judgment actively includes delusion. These are three distinct etiologies of unbelief, drawn from distinct theological streams (prophetic hardening, apocalyptic cosmic dualism, eschatological judgment), later bound into one canon. The compatibilist synthesis rescues canonical coherence, and it is not unreasonable as systematic theology, but it is a later harmonizing construct, not what any one of the texts asserts on its own terms. Verdict: harmonizable only by importing a layered-causation framework the texts themselves do not state; the surface tension is real and reflects independent theodicies.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John I-XII* (AB 29; New York: Doubleday, 1966), pp. 484-487, on John 12:40 and Isaiah 6:10.
