Acts 9:26-28 narrates what is presumably the same visit very differently. Paul tries to join the disciples, is feared, is vouched for by Barnabas "to the apostles" (plural, *pros tous apostolous*), and then is "with them, going in and out at Jerusalem," preaching boldly in the name of the Lord until a plot drives him to Caesarea and Tarsus. The picture is of open, public integration into the Jerusalem community.

The strongest apologetic harmonization, advanced by F. F. Bruce and Darrell Bock, runs along two lines. First, Acts writes in summary mode and is demonstrably loose with collective nouns, so "the apostles" can stand for the only two apostles Paul names (Peter and James), especially since Gal 1:19 itself appears to class James among "the apostles." Second, the narratives serve different rhetorical functions: Luke compresses, Paul itemizes, and a compressed summary need not contradict an itemized defense. Grammatically the first point is defensible — Acts does generalize. But the harmonization strains as the divergences accumulate. The hardest datum is Galatians 1:22: Paul swears he remained personally unknown to the Judean churches, which sits very awkwardly with Luke''s portrait of him moving "in and out" publicly in Jerusalem and preaching openly there. One can attempt to limit Luke''s "Jerusalem" against Paul''s wider "Judea," but the irenic, integrative thrust of Acts is the opposite of Paul''s insistence on distance.

Critical scholars — John Knox, Robert Jewett, Gerd Lüdemann, J. Louis Martyn, Hans Dieter Betz — read the divergence as flowing from incompatible programs. Luke''s overarching aim across Acts is to demonstrate the continuity and unity of the early movement: Paul and the Jerusalem apostles are partners, vouched for by Barnabas, in fundamental concord. Paul''s aim in Galatians is the reverse — to prove that his gospel and commission owe nothing to Jerusalem. The details diverge because the theological intentions diverge. The same fault line reappears at the next visit, where Acts 11:30/12:25 and 15 give a famine relief trip and an apostolic council that are difficult to reconcile with Paul''s own account of only two Jerusalem visits in Galatians 1-2, generating the long-running "how many visits" chronology debate that Lüdemann and Jewett treat at length. On standard source-critical principles, Paul — a participant writing within roughly two decades, under oath, to people who could contradict him — carries evidential priority over Luke, writing decades later with a demonstrable irenic agenda. The harmonization that Acts'' "apostles" means just Peter and James is possible for that one phrase; it does not neutralize Gal 1:22 or the larger clash of portraits. Verdict: genuine historical tension, best explained by Luke''s irenic redaction over against Paul''s firsthand polemical testimony.', 'Gerd Lüdemann, *Paul, Apostle to the Gentiles: Studies in Chronology*, trans. F. Stanley Jones (Philadelphia: Fortress, 1984), pp. 59-77, on the visit chronology.
John Knox, *Chapters in a Life of Paul*, rev. ed. (Macon: Mercer University Press, 1987), pp. 47-67, on the priority of Paul''s letters.
Robert Jewett, *A Chronology of Paul''s Life* (Philadelphia: Fortress, 1979), pp. 52-78, on reconstructing the Jerusalem visits.
