Darrell L. Bock, *Acts* (BECNT; Grand Rapids: Baker Academic, 2007), pp. 369-373, on summary vs. detail.', 0, NULL, 9, 'NT_internal', 'Acts, Galatians', 7, 2, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The verbs and tenses sharpen the point further. In 6:2 *bastazete* is a present imperative — an ongoing communal practice. In 6:5 *bastasei* is future indicative — "each will carry," pointing forward, most interpreters argue, to the day of judgment, when each person stands alone before God for their own works (cf. Rom 14:12, "each of us will give an account of himself to God"; 2 Cor 5:10). The intervening verses confirm the logic: in 6:3-4 Paul warns against self-deception and tells each to "test his own work" (*to ergon heautou dokimazetō*) so that the ground for boasting will be "in himself alone and not in his neighbor." The flow of 6:1-5 is therefore a single coherent argument: restore the one caught in sin gently (6:1), share each other''s crushing burdens (6:2), but do not measure yourself by comparison with others (6:3-4), because in the end each will answer for his own assigned load (6:5).

The apologetic harmonization articulated by F. F. Bruce, Richard Longenecker, and Ben Witherington leverages exactly this lexical and contextual distinction — share the crushing weights of life communally, but own your individual responsibility and final accountability, which no one else can discharge for you. What is notable is that critical scholarship concurs: Hans Dieter Betz and J. Louis Martyn read the passage the same way, with Martyn and Betz both stressing the eschatological-judgment reference of the future *bastasei* and James Dunn likewise treating the two clauses as complementary rather than contradictory. There is, in other words, near-consensus across the apologetic and critical divide here, which is unusual.

A genuine residue remains, but it is pastoral and conceptual rather than logical: the New Testament holds together communal solidarity and individual moral accountability, and Galatians 6:1-5 deliberately voices both within a few lines. That is a creative tension Paul intends, not a self-contradiction he failed to notice. The contradiction as posed is largely an artifact of the King James Version''s uniform rendering of two distinct Greek words; once *barē* and *phortion* are distinguished, the paragraph reads as a single, internally consistent exhortation. Verdict: overstated — a translation artifact, not a substantive contradiction.', 'J. Louis Martyn, *Galatians* (AB 33A; New York: Doubleday, 1997), pp. 546-554, on the eschatological sense of 6:5.
Hans Dieter Betz, *Galatians* (Hermeneia; Philadelphia: Fortress, 1979), pp. 297-303, on barē vs phortion.
Richard N. Longenecker, *Galatians* (WBC 41; Dallas: Word, 1990), pp. 273-278, on 6:2 and 6:5 as complementary.
F. F. Bruce, *The Epistle to the Galatians* (NIGTC; Grand Rapids: Eerdmans, 1982), pp. 260-263, on the lexical distinction.
Walter Bauer, Frederick W. Danker, William F. Arndt, and F. Wilbur Gingrich, *A Greek-English Lexicon of the New Testament*, 3rd ed. (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 2000), s.v. βάρος, φορτίον.
James D. G. Dunn, *The Epistle to the Galatians* (BNTC; Peabody: Hendrickson, 1993), pp. 322-329, on the complementary clauses.
Ben Witherington III, *Grace in Galatia: A Commentary on St. Paul''s Letter to the Galatians* (Grand Rapids: Eerdmans, 1998), pp. 423-432, on the lexical and pastoral harmonization.', 0, NULL, 5, 'NT_internal', 'Galatians', 6, 2, 2, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

The prohibitions come from a different scenario. 2 John 1:10 — "if anyone comes to you and does not bring this teaching, do not receive him into your house or give him any greeting" — is not about evangelistic dialogue with the unconverted at all. Its target is itinerant teachers carrying a deviant Christology into believing households; the surrounding letters define the error precisely as the denial that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh (1 John 4:2-3; 2 John 7), i.e., an early docetic or proto-Gnostic teaching. The "house" in view is very likely the house-church, and "receiving" connotes offering hospitality and a platform. Similarly, the Pastoral injunctions to avoid "profane chatter" (*bebēlous kenophōnias*, 1 Tim 6:20) and the "falsely-named knowledge" (*pseudōnymou gnōseōs*) and to "shun profane and empty babblings" (2 Tim 2:16) target speculative teaching circulating within or pressing upon the congregation — language that scholars from I. Howard Marshall to Raymond Collins read against an emergent proto-Gnostic milieu.

The apologetic harmonization, voiced by Karen Jobes and George Knight, draws exactly this distinction: genuine outsiders who inquire receive a courteous *apologia*; insiders or itinerants peddling false doctrine receive disengagement. This is sound exegesis as far as it goes, and the texts do operate in different registers — public witness versus protection of community boundaries. So the contradiction as the skeptic frames it — "engage outsiders or shun them" — is partly a category error, since the shunning texts are aimed at heterodox teachers, not at non-believers being witnessed to.

That said, the resolution is not airtight. The texts themselves do not crisply mark the in-group/out-group line the harmonization needs. 2 John''s "if anyone comes" is unqualified, and the docetists in question presumably claimed to be Christians, so the boundary between "false insider" and "outsider with a different view" is precisely what is contested. The Pastorals'' commands to "avoid" and "shun" speculative talk have, throughout Christian history, been read as warrants against any external doctrinal engagement, and Brown, Lieu, and Collins note that the Johannine and Pastoral communities were drawing sharp sectarian boundaries that sit uneasily beside 1 Peter''s irenic, dialogical posture. The deeper issue is therefore not whether to speak with non-believers — on that the texts are reconcilable — but how consistent the New Testament is on engaging dissent, where a genuinely different communal ethos shows through: 1 Peter and Colossians encourage open, gracious engagement, while the Johannine and Pastoral letters counsel separation from doctrinal opponents. Verdict: overstated as a flat contradiction (the texts address different audiences), but a real residual tension remains between the dialogical and the boundary-drawing strands of the New Testament.', 'Karen H. Jobes, *1 Peter* (BECNT; Grand Rapids: Baker, 2005), pp. 227-235, on the apologia to outsiders.
Paul J. Achtemeier, *1 Peter* (Hermeneia; Minneapolis: Fortress, 1996), pp. 231-238, on the defense in a minority setting.
Raymond E. Brown, *The Epistles of John* (AB 30; New York: Doubleday, 1982), pp. 692-696, on the docetist target of 2 John 10.
Judith M. Lieu, *I, II, & III John: A Commentary* (NTL; Louisville: Westminster John Knox, 2008), pp. 256-260, on Johannine boundary-drawing.
I. Howard Marshall, *The Pastoral Epistles* (ICC; Edinburgh: T&T Clark, 1999), pp. 678-681, 750-754, on profane babblings as proto-Gnostic speculation.
Raymond F. Collins, *I & II Timothy and Titus* (NTL; Louisville: Westminster John Knox, 2002), pp. 175-180, 232-236, on shunning false teaching.
James D. G. Dunn, *The Epistles to the Colossians and to Philemon* (NIGTC; Grand Rapids: Eerdmans, 1996), pp. 265-269, on speech toward outsiders.
George W. Knight III, *The Pastoral Epistles* (NIGTC; Grand Rapids: Eerdmans, 1992), pp. 277-283, 416-420, on distinguishing outsiders from false teachers.', 0, NULL, 5, 'NT_internal', 'Colossians, 1 Timothy, 2 Timothy, 1 Peter, 2 John', 6, 2, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

2 Thessalonians 2:1-3 reverses the rhetorical posture. The author warns the community not to be shaken "either by spirit or by word or by letter, as though from us, to the effect that the day of the Lord is already here" (*hōs hoti enestēken hē hēmera tou kyriou*). The day cannot come, he insists, until "the apostasy comes first and the lawless one is revealed" (2:3), and until "the one who now restrains" (*to katechon* / *ho katechōn*, 2:6-7) is removed. This is a developed eschatological program with prerequisite signs — exactly the kind of delay-theology that surfaces in late first-century texts negotiating the non-arrival of the end, most explicitly 2 Peter 3:8-9 ("with the Lord one day is like a thousand years").

The strongest harmonization, pressed by F. F. Bruce and Gene Green, makes two moves. First, 1 Thessalonians never fixes a date; the "we" is a stylistic identification with whichever believers happen to be alive, not a claim that Paul personally will survive. Second, 2 Thessalonians does not deny imminence but corrects a specific error — the claim that the day had *already arrived* (note *enestēken*, perfect tense, "is present") — by clarifying the sequence of events. On this reading the two letters address different errors and are complementary, not contradictory. The argument is grammatically respectable: *enestēken* does point to a "realized" eschatology error, not merely an "imminent" one, and Paul''s "we" elsewhere can be inclusive rather than autobiographical.

Yet the harmonization underplays the cumulative force of Paul''s authentic language and the literary profile of 2 Thessalonians. Wolfgang Trilling, Maarten Menken, and Margaret MacDonald have documented that 2 Thessalonians stands unusually close to 1 Thessalonians in wording and structure — closer than any other pair of Pauline letters — while diverging in eschatology and in a cooler, more formal tone. That combination (heavy verbal dependence plus theological correction) is the signature of imitative pseudepigraphy: a later writer borrows the authority and idiom of the genuine letter precisely in order to redirect it. The self-referential warning against a forged "letter as though from us" (2:2) and the closing authentication mark (3:17, "this is the mark in every letter of mine") read, on this view, as a pseudonymous author preempting suspicion. Bart Ehrman situates this within the broader phenomenon of "counter-forgery," in which a document is fabricated to neutralize the implications of an authentic or rival text. Charles Wanamaker, who defends authenticity, nonetheless concedes the eschatological data is the hardest problem for that position and proposes reversing the order of composition to relieve it — an admission of how real the tension is. The most economical reading remains that 1 Thessalonians reflects Paul''s own imminent expectation and 2 Thessalonians reflects a later hand managing its disappointment. Verdict: genuine contradiction, and a leading indicator of 2 Thessalonians'' pseudonymity.', 'Wolfgang Trilling, *Der zweite Brief an die Thessalonicher* (EKKNT 14; Benziger, 1980), pp. 22-46, on 2 Thessalonians 2:2-3
Maarten J. J. Menken, *2 Thessalonians* (New Testament Readings; Routledge, 1994), pp. 27-43
Margaret Y. MacDonald, *The Pauline Churches* (SNTSMS 60; Cambridge University Press, 1988), pp. 158-202
Abraham J. Malherbe, *The Letters to the Thessalonians* (AB 32B; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 269-285, 411-434, on 1 Thessalonians 4:15-17 and 2 Thessalonians 2
Bart D. Ehrman, *Forgery and Counterforgery* (Oxford University Press, 2013), pp. 156-171, on 2 Thessalonians 2:2; 3:17
Charles A. Wanamaker, *The Epistles to the Thessalonians* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 1990), pp. 17-37, 235-265, on 2 Thessalonians 2:1-7
F. F. Bruce, *1 & 2 Thessalonians* (WBC 45; Word, 1982), pp. xxxiv-xliv
Gene L. Green, *The Letters to the Thessalonians* (PNTC; Eerdmans, 2002), pp. 59-64, 304-318, on the rhetorical ''we'' and 2 Thessalonians 2:2', 0, NULL, 4, 'NT_internal', '1 Thessalonians, 2 Thessalonians', 2, 3, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

James 4:7 is by contrast lexically transparent: *antistēte de tō diabolō, kai pheuxetai aph'' hymōn* — "resist the devil, and he will flee from you." The verb *antistēte* (aorist imperative of *anthistēmi*) is the ordinary word for standing one''s ground, and the promise is unconditional within the believing community James addresses. Dale Allison and Peter Davids note that this reflects a Jewish-wisdom and Two Ways tradition (cf. Testament of Naphtali 8:4; 1 Peter 5:8-9) in which moral resistance reliably repels the adversary.

The strongest harmonization, articulated by Davids and Douglas Moo, deploys two complementary points. First, even on the traditional reading, 2 Timothy describes those *outside* repentance — opponents "caught in the devil''s snare," people who have not yet "come to their senses" — whereas James addresses believers actively submitting to God ("Submit yourselves therefore to God," James 4:7a). The captive state and the resisting state are simply two different conditions, not contradictory claims about the same person. Second, on Marshall''s pronoun reading, even the captivity is provisional and oriented to God''s overriding will. Taken together, the in/out distinction and the pronoun ambiguity substantially defuse the contradiction.

Where the tension survives is at the level of underlying anthropology, and here the harmonization is only partial. Raymond Collins and Luke Timothy Johnson observe that the Pastorals lean toward a more pessimistic estimate of unaided human capacity against demonic power — captivity is broken by God-granted repentance (2:25), not by the captive''s own effort — while James presupposes that a believer''s resistance is itself effective. These are different theological emphases drawn from different streams of early Christianity: a Pauline-deutero-Pauline determinism on one side, a Jewish-wisdom voluntarism on the other. They are not strictly logically contradictory once the contexts are separated, but they do not reduce to a single doctrine of how the devil holds and loses people. Verdict: harmonizable at the propositional level (the contradiction is partly a translation artifact in the KJV''s handling of *autou*/*ekeinou* and partly an in/out category difference), but reflecting a real divergence in demonology between the two corpora.', 'I. Howard Marshall, *The Pastoral Epistles* (ICC; T&T Clark, 1999), pp. 768-771, on 2 Timothy 2:26
Raymond F. Collins, *I & II Timothy and Titus* (NTL; Westminster John Knox, 2002), pp. 244-247, on 2 Timothy 2:25-26
Luke Timothy Johnson, *The First and Second Letters to Timothy* (AB 35A; Doubleday, 2001), pp. 408-411, on 2 Timothy 2:26
Peter H. Davids, *The Epistle of James* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 1982), pp. 165-167, on James 4:7
Dale C. Allison Jr., *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Epistle of James* (ICC; Bloomsbury T&T Clark, 2013), pp. 616-624, on James 4:7
Douglas J. Moo, *The Letter of James* (PNTC; Eerdmans, 2000), pp. 192-194, on James 4:7
Walter Bauer, Frederick W. Danker, William F. Arndt, and F. Wilbur Gingrich, *A Greek-English Lexicon of the New Testament and Other Early Christian Literature*, 3rd ed. (University of Chicago Press, 2000), s.v. ζωγρέω, on 2 Timothy 2:26', 0, NULL, 4, 'NT_internal', '2 Timothy, James', 5, 3, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The apparent reversal is in 11:13: *kata pistin apethanon houtoi pantes, mē labontes tas epangelias* — "in accordance with faith these all died, not having received the promises." The pivot is the antecedent of *houtoi pantes* ("these all"). The decisive evidence that it does not reach back to Enoch is the relative clause that immediately qualifies it: "not having received the promises, but having seen them and greeted them from afar, and having confessed that they were strangers and exiles on the earth" (11:13), which 11:14-16 expands into the search for a homeland and a heavenly city. That description matches precisely the patriarchal cluster of 11:8-12 — Abraham, Sarah, Isaac, Jacob — who are characterized throughout as sojourners awaiting a promised land and city (cf. 11:9-10, "living in tents… he looked forward to the city that has foundations"). It does not match Abel (11:4) or Enoch (11:5), neither of whom is framed in terms of awaited land-promises, and one of whom the author has just said did not die at all. William Lane and Harold Attridge therefore read *houtoi pantes* as restrictive to the immediately preceding patriarchal section, not to the whole chapter from verse 4 onward.

This restrictive reading is not a special pleading to rescue the author; it is the default Greek reading of a demonstrative whose qualifying clause specifies its scope. Craig Koester and Luke Timothy Johnson concur, noting both the internal logic and the implausibility that a writer would flatly contradict in 11:13 a point he had made the centerpiece of 11:5. The phrase *kata pistin* ("in accordance with faith") in 11:13, slightly different from the anaphoric *pistei* ("by faith") that opens most exempla, further signals a summarizing comment on the patriarchs who died as resident aliens, not a fresh universal claim about everyone listed.

The residual tension is literary rather than logical. The chapter opens with a rapid sequence — Abel, Enoch, Noah — before settling into the extended Abraham material, so a reader arriving at "these all died" naturally tends to gather the whole roster, including Enoch, into the statement. That reflexive misreading is what the SAB framing exploits. But the moment the qualifying clause about unreceived promises and exile is taken seriously, Enoch (and Abel) fall outside its scope. James VanderKam''s survey of the Enoch traditions confirms that the non-death of Enoch was a fixed and well-known datum in Second Temple Judaism (developed extensively in 1 Enoch, Jubilees, and Sirach 44:16), making it all the more unlikely that the author of Hebrews — steeped in those traditions — would casually negate it eight verses after affirming it. Verdict: overstated; the contradiction dissolves once *houtoi pantes* is read with its own qualifying clause as referring to the patriarchs of 11:8-12.', 'Harold W. Attridge, *The Epistle to the Hebrews* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1989), pp. 316-332, on Hebrews 11:5, 13
Craig R. Koester, *Hebrews* (AB 36; Doubleday, 2001), pp. 471-492, on Hebrews 11:8-16
Luke Timothy Johnson, *Hebrews: A Commentary* (NTL; Westminster John Knox, 2006), pp. 281-295, on Hebrews 11:5, 13
William L. Lane, *Hebrews 9-13* (WBC 47B; Word, 1991), pp. 332-353, on Hebrews 11:13
Claus Westermann, *Genesis 1-11: A Continental Commentary*, trans. John J. Scullion (Fortress, 1994), pp. 357-360, on Genesis 5:24
James C. VanderKam, *Enoch: A Man for All Generations* (University of South Carolina Press, 1995), pp. 11-33, on the Enoch traditions', 0, NULL, 4, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Genesis, Hebrews', 7, 2, 2, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

That is the crux. James 2 is not merely emphasizing good behavior in general; it is explicitly polemicizing against a "faith alone" position: "a person is justified by works and not by faith alone" (2:24, *ouk ek pisteōs monon*), and "faith without works is dead" (2:26). Whether James targets Paul directly or a misapplication of Pauline teaching is debated — Dale Allison and Richard Bauckham favor a corrective aimed at a distorted Paulinism circulating in James''s milieu — but in either case James deliberately deploys the same triad (faith, works, justification) and the same scriptural exemplars (Abraham in 2:21-23, Rahab in 2:25) to reach the opposite conclusion from the one those exemplars carry in the Pauline and the Hebrews traditions.

The standard harmonization, articulated by Douglas Moo and Peter Davids, runs through the "faith demonstrated by works" synthesis: Rahab''s reception of the spies was the outward enactment of an inward faith (she confesses YHWH''s supremacy in Joshua 2:9-11), so Hebrews names the root and James names the fruit, and both describe one reality. At the level of systematic theology this is coherent and has a long pedigree. Its weakness is historical and exegetical: it imports a reconciling framework — the scholastic distinction between the faith by which one believes and the works that evidence it, or the Reformation synthesis of justification and sanctification — that neither author states. Luke Timothy Johnson, Harold Attridge, and Craig Koester locate Hebrews 11 within Hellenistic-Jewish exemplar catalogues (Sirach 44-50; 4 Maccabees 16:20-23; Wisdom 10) in which "faith" and "faithful deeds" are fused and not analytically separated, while James belongs to a distinct, deliberately anti-"faith-alone" trajectory. The two writers are not collaborating on a single doctrine viewed from two angles; they are participants in an intra-Christian dispute about how salvation is appropriated, and the canon preserves both unreconciled.

Thus while the propositions can be synthesized at the level of later dogmatics, the texts themselves stand in unresolved juxtaposition: Hebrews says Rahab was preserved *by faith*; James says she was justified *by works and not by faith alone*; and James says so using the precise vocabulary Hebrews and Paul would have answered differently. Verdict: genuine theological contradiction at the level of the texts'' own formulations, harmonizable only by importing a later synthetic framework neither author shares.', 'Dale C. Allison Jr., *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Epistle of James* (ICC; Bloomsbury T&T Clark, 2013), pp. 487-507, on James 2:24-25
Luke Timothy Johnson, *The Letter of James* (AB 37A; Doubleday, 1995), pp. 245-252, on James 2:25
Harold W. Attridge, *The Epistle to the Hebrews* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1989), pp. 348-352, on Hebrews 11:31
Craig R. Koester, *Hebrews* (AB 36; Doubleday, 2001), pp. 502-505, on Hebrews 11:31
William L. Lane, *Hebrews 9-13* (WBC 47B; Word, 1991), pp. 379-383, on Hebrews 11:31
Peter H. Davids, *The Epistle of James* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 1982), pp. 132-135, on James 2:25
Richard Bauckham, *James* (New Testament Readings; Routledge, 1999), pp. 113-140, on James 2:24-26
