The pivot is Ephesians 4:26, which quotes Psalm 4:4 in its Septuagint form, *orgizesthe kai mē hamartanete*. The Greek imperative *orgizesthe* is most plausibly read not as a command to generate anger but as a concessive or conditional construction — "in your anger, do not sin," or "be angry [if you must], and do not sin." The clause that follows, "let not the sun go down upon your wrath," treats anger as a state to be discharged quickly rather than a virtue to be cultivated, and the next verse warns against giving "place to the devil" (Ephesians 4:27). Andrew Lincoln in the Word Biblical Commentary and Harold Hoehner both read the verse as a regulation of an emotion presumed dangerous, not an endorsement. Read this way, Ephesians sits near, not opposite, the wisdom pole: it concedes that anger arises in human experience and insists it be contained and short-lived.

That brings most of the texts into substantial agreement. Ecclesiastes 7:9 warns against being hasty to anger because "anger resteth in the bosom of fools" — the Hebrew *ka''as* (vexation/anger) is repeatedly a mark of folly in Qoheleth. Proverbs 22:24 counsels avoiding the *ba''al ''ap* ("lord of anger," the hot-tempered man) as a corrupting companion. James 1:20, in a letter saturated with wisdom-tradition idiom, says "the wrath of man worketh not the righteousness of God," pairing it with the call to be "slow to wrath" (James 1:19) — itself an echo of the divine self-description as slow to anger in Exodus 34:6. All four texts converge on the proposition that human anger is spiritually hazardous. Ephesians differs only in tone: it acknowledges anger as a reality to be managed rather than treating it as pure vice. That is a difference of emphasis, not a logical contradiction.

Where a genuine tension does live is one the entry does not foreground: the asymmetry between divine and human anger. Yahweh''s wrath is celebrated across the Hebrew Bible and affirmed in the New Testament (e.g., Romans 1:18, Revelation), while James 1:20 specifically disqualifies human anger because it does not produce God''s righteousness. The implicit theology — that wrath is fitting in God but not in his creatures — is internally coherent (it rests on the prerogatives of the just judge versus the partiality of the sinner), but it does mean an affect praised in the deity is restricted in humanity. The Hebrew phrase *''erek ''appayim*, "long of nostrils" (slow to anger), is applied to God as praise precisely while human quickness to anger is condemned. That contrast is the sharper datum.

A second, smaller residue is the case of righteous human anger — prophetic indignation, or Jesus'' anger in the temple and at hardness of heart (Mark 3:5). The wisdom and Jamesian material would seem to leave little room for it, yet the canon clearly endorses some instances. This suggests the biblical view is not "anger good" versus "anger bad" but a graded ethic: anger is presumptively dangerous, occasionally warranted, and must be brief and unselfish. Ephesians 4:26 articulates exactly that graded position.

As framed, then, the contradiction is overstated. Ephesians 4:26 regulates rather than commands anger, and once that lexical point is granted the four texts largely agree. A real but narrower tension survives in the divine/human asymmetry and in the canon''s allowance for occasional righteous anger, neither of which this entry actually cites. Verdict: overstated; largely harmonizable via the concessive reading of orgizesthe, with a smaller genuine tension over divine versus human wrath.', 'Andrew T. Lincoln, *Ephesians* (Word Biblical Commentary 42; Word Books, 1990), pp. 300-305, on orgizesthe as concessive
Harold W. Hoehner, *Ephesians: An Exegetical Commentary* (Baker Academic, 2002), pp. 619-625, on the Psalm 4:4 LXX background
Scot McKnight, *The Letter of James* (NICNT; Eerdmans, 2011), pp. 144-150, on James 1:19-20 and wrath
C. L. Seow, *Ecclesiastes* (Anchor Bible 18C; Doubleday, 1997), pp. 240-244, on anger as folly in Qoheleth
Bruce K. Waltke, *The Book of Proverbs: Chapters 15-31* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 2005), pp. 217-220, on the hot-tempered man', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Proverbs, Ecclesiastes, Ephesians, James', 6, 2, 2, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

The harmonizations are ingenious. For Luke 22:36, Joel Green and others read the sword saying as ironic or symbolic — Jesus signals that a season of hostility and rejection is coming (fulfilling Isaiah 53:12, "reckoned among the transgressors," which Luke quotes in 22:37), not that the disciples should arm for combat. The decisive internal clue is that when the disciples produce two swords, Jesus says "It is enough" (Luke 22:38, *hikanon estin*) — and two swords are absurdly insufficient for any real fight, which many read as Jesus breaking off a conversation the disciples have misunderstood. Yet the same datum cuts the other way: the swords presented are literal physical weapons, and at the arrest one disciple actually strikes with one (Luke 22:49-50; cf. all four Gospels), so the tradition plainly remembers armed followers. Whatever Luke intends rhetorically, the historical residue is a Jesus movement that carried weapons.

For John 2:15 the favored move limits the whip to the animals. The Greek is genuinely ambiguous: *kai pantas exebalen ek tou hierou ta te probata kai tous boas* — "and he drove them all out of the temple, both the sheep and the oxen." Defenders such as D. A. Carson argue *pantas* (masculine plural, "all") is then specified by "the sheep and the oxen," so the whip targeted livestock. But *pantas* is masculine while *probata* (sheep) is neuter, which makes a reference limited strictly to animals grammatically strained; the more natural antecedent of "all" includes the people. Raymond Brown takes the action as directed at the merchants and moneychangers, whose tables Jesus overturns and whose coins he scatters — acts of force against persons and property, not against sheep. Even the most charitable reading concedes Jesus used physical coercion to clear a religious space.

Both poles of the contradiction also need qualifying, which narrows but does not close the gap. Matthew 5:39''s "resist not evil" is widely read by critical scholars (Dale Allison, John Meier) within an honor-culture frame: a blow to the right cheek is a backhanded insult, not a lethal assault, so the saying addresses retaliation in personal honor-disputes rather than legislating absolute pacifism. And the sword and whip episodes are situational, not programmatic endorsements of violence. So the resistance teaching may be narrower than pacifism, and the force episodes narrower than a doctrine of force.

Still, the tension is real and arguably reflects two streams of tradition and two communities'' concerns. Mark frames Jesus'' movement against the live question of armed revolt — one of the Twelve is "Simon the Zealot," Barabbas is a violent insurrectionist released in Jesus'' place, and Jesus is crucified between *lēstai* (a term Josephus uses for bandit-rebels) under a charge of sedition. The Gospels work hard to present Jesus as non-seditious while preserving memories — armed disciples, a forcible temple demonstration — that point the other way. The non-resistance sayings likely reflect later ecclesial articulation of an ethic for a vulnerable community; the sword and whip episodes preserve harder, less domesticated tradition. The harmonization succeeds only by letting each text qualify the other until neither stands at full strength. Verdict: genuine tension between two tradition-streams, only partially harmonizable.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison Jr., *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew* (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988), vol. 1, pp. 540-545, on the honor-culture reading of Matthew 5:39
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 1-7: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 273-280, on non-retaliation
Joel B. Green, *The Gospel of Luke* (NICNT; Eerdmans, 1997), pp. 773-778, on the sword saying as symbolic
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (Anchor Bible 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1430-1435, on Luke 22:36-38
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John I-XII* (Anchor Bible 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 114-125, on the temple cleansing
D. A. Carson, *The Gospel According to John* (Pillar New Testament Commentary; Eerdmans, 1991), pp. 175-183, on the whip and livestock
John P. Meier, *A Marginal Jew: Rethinking the Historical Jesus, Vol. 4: Law and Love* (Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 525-551, on non-retaliation', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Luke, John', 7, 2, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard harmonization distinguishes fear from strategy. D. A. Carson and Andreas Köstenberger argue that John 7:1 reports tactical timing, not dread. This reading is anchored in one of the Fourth Gospel''s most pervasive motifs: the divinely appointed "hour." In the immediate context Jesus tells his brothers "My time (*kairos*) is not yet come" (John 7:6, 7:8), and throughout John no one can seize him "because his hour was not yet come" (John 7:30; cf. 8:20), until at last "the hour is come, that the Son of man should be glorified" (John 12:23, 13:1, 17:1). On this Johannine theology Jesus is never a victim of circumstance; he lays down his life of his own accord (John 10:18). Avoiding Judea prematurely would short-circuit a passion that must occur at Passover and on Jesus'' own terms. Read within John''s frame, 7:1 is patience, not panic, and there is no inconsistency with Luke 12:4 — indeed John''s Jesus is supremely unafraid of death precisely because he controls its timing.

This is a coherent and probably correct reading of John on its own terms. The weakness is that it leans wholly on Johannine high Christology, and the question "did Jesus fear death?" cannot be confined to John. The Synoptic data complicate matters. In Mark, Jesus'' march to Jerusalem reads as deliberate confrontation punctuated by passion predictions (Mark 8:31, 9:31, 10:33-34), which fits the fearlessness model. But Gethsemane cuts the other way: Jesus is "sore amazed" and "very heavy" (*ekthambeisthai kai adēmonein*, Mark 14:33), "exceeding sorrowful unto death," and prays "let this cup pass from me" (Mark 14:34-36); Luke''s own tradition (in the contested longer text, Luke 22:43-44) intensifies this with sweat "as it were great drops of blood." Raymond Brown, in The Death of the Messiah, treats Gethsemane as preserving authentic tradition of genuine anguish before death. That anguish is at least in tension with the serene fearlessness Jesus commends in Luke 12:4.

So the contradiction has two layers. The narrow form — Luke 12:4 versus John 7:1 — largely dissolves under the "hour" reading: John 7:1 need not report fear at all, and the verb of the verse simply describes Jesus'' movements, with the causal clause stating others'' intent to kill him rather than Jesus'' emotional state. The broader form — did Jesus himself embody the fearlessness he taught? — is harder, because the Synoptic Gethsemane portrays a Jesus visibly distressed by impending death. Even here a partial harmonization is available: dread of suffering and abandonment is not the same as the cowering fear of human persecutors that Luke 12:4 forbids, and a teaching about courage under threat is not falsified by sorrow in the face of the cross. But the equation is not clean, and the texts foreground different theological emphases — Luke''s call to martyr-courage, John''s sovereign timetable, Mark''s anguished obedience.

Verdict: largely harmonizable in the narrow Luke-versus-John form (John 7:1 reflects the ''hour'' motif, not fear), with a genuine but smaller residual tension once Gethsemane is admitted — the database wording overstates the John 7:1 problem while understating the deeper Synoptic one.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John I-XII* (Anchor Bible 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 305-310, on the ''hour'' motif and John 7:1
D. A. Carson, *The Gospel According to John* (Pillar New Testament Commentary; Eerdmans, 1991), pp. 304-308, on strategic timing
Andreas J. Köstenberger, *John* (BECNT; Baker Academic, 2004), pp. 226-230, on the Johannine ''hour''
Craig S. Keener, *The Gospel of John: A Commentary* (Hendrickson, 2003), vol. 1, pp. 700-708, on John 7:1-9
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (Anchor Bible 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 957-960, on Luke 12:4
Raymond E. Brown, *The Death of the Messiah* (Anchor Bible Reference Library; Doubleday, 1994), vol. 1, pp. 146-234, on Gethsemane and anguish before death', 0, NULL, 4, 'NT_internal', 'Luke, John', 5, 3, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

Luke, by contrast, twice attaches parables to prayer whose explicit lesson is to keep asking. The friend at midnight (Luke 11:5-8) culminates in a man who rises and gives "because of his importunity" — the Greek *anaideia*, "shamelessness" or persistent boldness — not because of friendship. In the Lukan sequence this is immediately generalized: "ask... seek... knock" (Luke 11:9-10), with present-tense imperatives that connote continued action. The persistent widow (Luke 18:1-8) is even more pointed: Luke prefaces it editorially with the moral "that men ought always to pray, and not to faint" (18:1), and the widow prevails precisely by wearing down an unjust judge through relentless petition (the judge fears she will, literally, give him a black eye, *hypōpiazē*, 18:5). Both Lukan parables argue a fortiori — if a grudging friend and an unjust judge yield to persistence, how much more will God — but the rhetorical engine of both is that persistence works.

The usual harmonization, advanced by Craig Blomberg and D. A. Carson, distinguishes the objects of the two teachings: Matthew condemns only mindless, mechanical pagan repetition (the magical piling-up of words to compel a deity), whereas Luke commends sincere, faith-filled persistence. There is real exegetical substance here. Matthew''s target is explicitly the *ethnikoi* and their theory that volume secures a hearing — a critique of prayer-as-incantation, not of asking more than once. And the Lukan parables are about perseverance in trust, not about coercing God through verbiage. So the two are not strict logical contradictories: one can both pray persistently and avoid empty babble.

Yet the harmonization smooths over a genuine difference in posture. Matthew''s rationale — God already knows your needs before you ask — pushes against the very mechanism the Lukan parables exploit. If the Father knows in advance, the function of repeated asking becomes unclear, and Matthew''s emphasis falls squarely on brevity and restraint. Luke''s emphasis falls on tenacity and the refusal to give up. The friend at midnight in particular makes the granting depend on *anaideia*, persistence itself, which is awkward to reconcile with "your Father knoweth before ye ask." The two evangelists are deploying overlapping Jesus tradition (the ask-seek-knock logion is shared Q material, Matthew 7:7-11 // Luke 11:9-13) toward different pastoral ends — Matthew toward a critique of ostentatious, mechanical prayer, Luke toward an encouragement to persevere amid delayed answers and apparent divine silence (a concern visible in Luke 18:7-8''s eschatological framing about the Son of Man finding faith).

So the verbal contradiction softens under the pagan-repetition reading, but a real difference of theology and emphasis remains: Matthew relativizes the value of repeated asking by appeal to divine foreknowledge, while Luke makes persistence the very thing God rewards. Verdict: partially harmonizable on the surface (Matthew targets mechanical babble, not persistence), but a genuine tension of emphasis persists between Matthew''s brevity-and-foreknowledge logic and Luke''s reward-for-importunity logic.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison Jr., *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew* (ICC; T&T Clark, 1988), vol. 1, pp. 587-590, on Matthew 6:7-8 and battalogeo
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 1-7: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 304-308, on vain repetition
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (Anchor Bible 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 909-915 and 1175-1182, on the friend at midnight and the persistent widow
Craig L. Blomberg, *Interpreting the Parables*, 2nd ed. (IVP Academic, 2012), pp. 320-326, on persistence versus pagan repetition', 0, NULL, 5, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Luke', 6, 2, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The two rationales are not two facets of a single argument; each is grammatically presented as THE reason God gave the command, marked by the same causal connector ''al-kēn. The Exodus Sabbath imitates God; the Deuteronomic Sabbath liberates the household. P''s interest in cosmic order, the sanctification of time, and the sabbatical structure of creation runs through Genesis 1, the manna account in Exodus 16, and the festal calendars; D''s interest in the Exodus deliverance, the humane treatment of slaves and aliens, and the leveling memory of servitude runs through Deuteronomy 15, 16, and 24. Each motive clause is at home in its own source''s theological program, which is exactly what the Documentary Hypothesis predicts when a redactor preserves parallel traditions side by side without smoothing them into agreement.

The traditional harmonization, articulated by Gleason Archer, holds that a single commandment may rest on multiple compatible motives — God both rested at creation and redeemed Israel, so both clauses are true and complementary. In the abstract that is unobjectionable: motives are not zero-sum. But the harmonization does not engage the textual datum, which is not merely that two motives exist but that two transmitted versions of one verbatim law each assign a different single motive with the same causal particle. The question the contradiction poses is not "can God have two reasons" but "why does the same Decalogue, reproduced almost word for word, change the stated reason between Exodus and Deuteronomy." The most economical answer is that the two recensions descend from distinct literary traditions. Jewish interpreters noticed the tension early; the rabbinic tradition harmonized the parallel divergence over zākôr ("remember," Exod 20:8) versus šāmôr ("keep," Deut 5:12) by claiming both were spoken "in a single utterance" (b. Šebuʿot 20b, Rosh Hashanah 27a) — a device that concedes the surface difference precisely by appealing to miracle. Tigay, Weinfeld, and Propp all treat the Exodus/Deuteronomy motive divergence as a textbook example of competing Pentateuchal traditions, with Weinfeld emphasizing the characteristically Deuteronomic humanitarian recasting and Childs noting that the Deuteronomist deliberately substituted the historical for the creational ground. Friedman assigns Exodus 20:11 to P and the Deuteronomic frame to D, and the verbal dependence of Exodus 20:11 on the P creation account confirms the stratification. Verdict: genuine contradiction at the level of stated rationale, cleanly explained by source criticism; the multiple-motive harmonization is true in principle but does not account for the textual change of motive within a verbatim-repeated law.', 'Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1996), pp. 67-69, on Deuteronomy 5:15
Moshe Weinfeld, *Deuteronomy 1-11* (AB 5; Doubleday, 1991), pp. 301-305, on Deuteronomy 5:12-15
Brevard S. Childs, *The Book of Exodus: A Critical Theological Commentary* (OTL; Westminster, 1974), pp. 415-417, on Exodus 20:11
William H. C. Propp, *Exodus 19-40* (AB 2A; Doubleday, 2006), pp. 169-172, on Exodus 20:11
Richard Elliott Friedman, *The Bible with Sources Revealed* (HarperOne, 2003), pp. 153-154 (P) and pp. 332-333 (D), on Exodus 20:11 and Deuteronomy 5:15
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 119-121, on Exodus 20:11 and Deuteronomy 5:15', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Exodus, Deuteronomy', 6, 3, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Several features sharpen and several soften the tension. On the sharpening side, the triadic baptismal formula of Matthew 28:19 is unattested in this developed form anywhere else in the New Testament. Acts uniformly reports baptism "in the name of Jesus" or "of the Lord Jesus" (Acts 2:38; 8:16; 10:48; 19:5), and Paul likewise knows only baptism "into Christ" (Rom 6:3; Gal 3:27). The earliest extra-canonical witness, Didache 7, contains the triune formula but also preserves the older single-name practice in Didache 9:5, suggesting the trinitarian wording was crystallizing in the late first or early second century — the period most critical scholars (Davies-Allison, Luz) assign to Matthew''s final form, c. 80-90 CE. The Matthean commission, on this analysis, reflects the liturgy of Matthew''s community projected back onto the lips of the risen Jesus, a standard feature of post-resurrection appearance narratives.

On the softening side, the harmonization defended by Blomberg is genuinely strong and should not be caricatured. Paul did baptize; he simply did not regard administering the rite personally as the heart of his commission. His statement in 1 Corinthians 1:17 is occasioned by a specific pastoral problem — the Corinthians were forming personality cults around the persons who baptized them ("I am of Paul; and I of Apollos," 1:12), and Paul deliberately distances himself from baptism precisely to deflate those factions. The verb "sent" (apesteilen) places the remark within Paul''s division of apostolic labor, not within a theology that rejects the sacrament. Indeed Paul''s most sustained theological statement on baptism, Romans 6:3-4, treats it as the believer''s incorporation into Christ''s death and resurrection — hardly the language of a man who thinks baptism dispensable. So at the level of practice and theology of the rite, the two texts are reconcilable: both presuppose baptism; they differ on emphasis, and the difference is explained by Paul''s polemical setting (Fitzmyer, Conzelmann).

The residual, irreducible tension is narrower but real. If Paul had received and accepted a dominical command obliging him to baptize all nations, the rhetorical disavowal of 1 Corinthians 1:17 would be a strange way to argue, since it sets his own sending against a command of the risen Lord. The more economical reading is that Paul wrote in the mid-50s without knowledge of the Great Commission in its Matthean form, which represents a later development of the tradition (Ehrman). That conclusion is an inference from silence and remains a judgment about probabilities rather than a demonstration. Verdict: largely harmonizable at the level of mission and practice (hyperbole plus differing emphasis), with a genuine but narrower historical tension over whether the Matthean baptismal command was known to Paul.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 3 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 676-694, on Matthew 28:16-20
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 21-28: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2005), pp. 619-636, on Matthew 28:19
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *First Corinthians* (AYB 32; Yale University Press, 2008), pp. 140-148, on 1 Corinthians 1:14-17
Hans Conzelmann, *1 Corinthians* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1975), pp. 35-39, on 1 Corinthians 1:17
Bart D. Ehrman, *Jesus Before the Gospels* (HarperOne, 2016), pp. 215-220, on Matthew 28:19 as later tradition
Craig L. Blomberg, *Matthew* (NAC 22; Broadman, 1992), pp. 430-433, on Matthew 28:19', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, 1 Corinthians', 5, 3, 3, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The most natural source-critical placement is that Leviticus 20:21 belongs to the Holiness Code (H, Lev 17-26), whose incest catalogue in Leviticus 18 prohibits "uncovering the nakedness" of a long list of kin defined through a male relative — father''s wife, daughter-in-law, and at 18:16 "thy brother''s wife." H''s logic is genealogical and cultic: such unions defile the land. Deuteronomy 25 (D) operates from a different concern — the preservation of the dead man''s name, line, and property within the patrimony. Two legal traditions, two priorities, one act.

The strongest harmonization, defended by Archer and by John Walton, restricts the Levitical prohibition to a brother''s wife while the brother is alive, making it a prohibition of adultery and incest within a living union, and reserves Deuteronomy 25 for the widow of a brother who has died childless. This reading has real support: Leviticus 18:16''s parallel sits in a list otherwise concerned with relations that would constitute adultery or rivalry within the extended household, and Jacob Milgrom argues that 18:16 indeed contemplates the wife of a living brother. The reading thus resolves the parallel at 18:16 cleanly. The difficulty is that Leviticus 20:21 itself does not specify the brother''s state, and its penalty clause (childlessness) is most pointed precisely if the union in view is one undertaken after a brother''s death to produce children — which is exactly the levirate situation. If H simply meant adultery with a living brother''s wife, the death penalty applied elsewhere in the chapter to adultery (Lev 20:10) would be expected, not the distinctive sentence of childlessness.

The rabbinic tradition itself conceded a real conflict and resolved it by legislative arbitration rather than by claiming the laws never overlapped. The Mishnah''s entire tractate Yebamot is devoted to defining when the Deuteronomic levirate obligation overrides the general Levitical prohibition of a brother''s wife, concluding that the mitzvah of yibbum suspends the prohibition only in the precise case of a childless widow, and that outside that case the prohibition stands with full force. That is not the harmony of two laws that never touched; it is a ruling about which of two genuinely competing statutes prevails in a defined situation. The narrative tradition assumes levirate duty as normative and praiseworthy — Genesis 38 (Tamar, Judah, and the failure of Onan) and the book of Ruth both turn on the obligation to raise up seed for a dead kinsman. Verdict: a real legal tension between H and D that the case-distinction harmonization can partly but not fully dissolve; the rabbis themselves treated it as a conflict requiring one law to override the other.', 'Jacob Milgrom, *Leviticus 17-22* (AB 3A; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 1543-1546, on Leviticus 18:16 and 20:21
Jeffrey H. Tigay, *Deuteronomy* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1996), pp. 231-234, on Deuteronomy 25:5
Raymond Westbrook, "The Law of the Biblical Levirate," *Revue Internationale des Droits de l''Antiquité* 24 (1977), pp. 65-87, on Deuteronomy 25:5 and Leviticus 20:21
Eryl W. Davies, "Inheritance Rights and the Hebrew Levirate Marriage," *Vetus Testamentum* 31 (1981), pp. 138-144 and 257-268, on Deuteronomy 25:5
Israel Knohl, *The Sanctuary of Silence: The Priestly Torah and the Holiness School* (Fortress, 1995), pp. 102-110, on Leviticus 20:21
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 137-138, on Deuteronomy 25:5 and Leviticus 20:21', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Leviticus, Deuteronomy', 6, 3, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The principal harmonization is linguistic and is a real phenomenon, not an apologetic invention. In Semitic idiom the verb "hate" (Hebrew sane'', Greek miseo) frequently functions as a comparative meaning "to love less" or "to subordinate in preference." Genesis 29:31 calls Leah "hated" when 29:30 has just said Jacob loved Rachel "more than Leah"; Malachi 1:2-3 ("Jacob have I loved, but Esau have I hated"), quoted in Romans 9:13, expresses election, not emotional animus. Decisively, Matthew preserves the same dominical saying in comparative form: "He that loveth father or mother more than me is not worthy of me" (Matt 10:37). On the two-source hypothesis both evangelists draw a Q saying; Matthew renders the comparative sense explicitly while Luke retains the sharper Semitic "hate." Fitzmyer and Bovon both endorse this reading. To that extent the verbal contradiction with Ephesians is partially dissolved: Luke''s Jesus demands that allegiance to him outrank family loyalty, not that disciples cultivate malice toward parents.

The harmonization is nonetheless oversold when it reduces Luke 14:26 to mere prioritization. Luke''s wording is sharper than Matthew''s, and the inclusion of "his own life also" (kai ten heautou psychen) resists the simple "love less" gloss: one does not merely prefer Jesus over one''s own life in a comparative sense — the immediately following sayings (14:27, bearing the cross; 14:28-33, counting the cost and forsaking "all that he hath") demand literal, costly renunciation. The Lukan context envisions disciples who actually leave family and possessions behind, matching the social reality of itinerant early Christian missionaries, whom Gerd Theissen analyzed as "wandering charismatics" with parallels to Cynic itinerants. The radical break from household ties is the point, not a rhetorical flourish.

Ephesians belongs to a different stage of the movement. Most critical scholars regard it as deutero-Pauline, written c. 80-95 CE by a follower of Paul, and its household code (Haustafel, 5:21-6:9) addresses a second-generation church that had to live in stable households, stay married, raise children, and manage slaves. Lincoln and Best document the form as an adaptation of Greco-Roman household-management topoi baptized into Christian usage. The trajectory from the itinerant family-renunciation of the earliest Jesus tradition to the settled domestic ethic of the household codes is a well-attested sociological development. The two texts thus reflect two phases and two social settings: the radical wing that summoned disciples out of their families, and the institutionalizing wing that taught Christians how to be families. The verbal contradiction is partly an idiom artifact; the underlying ethical tension between renunciation and household affirmation is genuine. Verdict: partially harmonizable on the lexical level (comparative idiom), with a real residual tension between itinerant-renunciation ethics and the later domestic Haustafel.', 'Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (AB 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1060-1064, on Luke 14:26
François Bovon, *Luke 2: A Commentary on the Gospel of Luke 9:51-19:27* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2013), pp. 384-388, on Luke 14:26
Andrew T. Lincoln, *Ephesians* (WBC 42; Word, 1990), pp. 351-365 (Haustafel) and pp. 388-399, on Ephesians 5:21-6:9
Ernest Best, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on Ephesians* (ICC; T&T Clark, 1998), pp. 504-524, on Ephesians 5:21-6:9
Gerd Theissen, *Sociology of Early Palestinian Christianity*, trans. John Bowden (Fortress, 1978), pp. 8-16, on wandering charismatics
