Two harmonizations are worth steel-manning. The first, associated with Cranfield and Moo, restricts Paul''s command to legitimate authority acting within its God-ordained sphere of restraining evil; when a ruler commands sin, the authority has exceeded its mandate and the duty to submit lapses, with Acts 5:29 ("We ought to obey God rather than men") supplying the limiting principle. This reading coheres with the broader canon and is theologically attractive. Its weakness is that the qualification is imported: Paul writes pasa psyche without caveat and offers no "unless they command evil" clause, so the exception is supplied by the interpreter rather than the text. The second reading, associated with Käsemann and Wright, situates the passage within Paul''s apocalyptic frame — "the night is far spent, the day is at hand" (13:11-12) — so that submission is provisional and pragmatic, a counsel for a brief interim before the present order passes away, not a permanent political theology. Jewett and Neil Elliott add the concrete setting: Paul wrote c. 57 CE to a fragile house-church in the imperial capital, perhaps in the aftermath of disturbances over taxation and the recent expulsion of Jews under Claudius, with strong pastoral motive to keep the community inconspicuous and tax-compliant. On this reading Romans 13 is occasional counsel reflecting a specific danger rather than a timeless absolute.

The provisional/situational reading is the most defensible critically and reconciles Paul''s intent with the canon''s wider witness. But it harmonizes Paul''s purpose, not his wording. As written, Romans 13:1-3 states a universal duty and a universal empirical claim, and that empirical claim — that rulers terrorize only the wicked — is demonstrably false within the same canon, whose heroes are martyred and whose Messiah is executed by the state. The contradiction is real at the level of the text''s surface assertions even where the underlying theologies can be reconciled by appeal to context and the higher-allegiance principle of Acts 5:29. Verdict: partially harmonizable as situational, provisional counsel, but a genuine tension remains between Paul''s unconditional formulation and the canon''s repeated, celebrated defiance of rulers.', 'Ernst Käsemann, *Commentary on Romans*, trans. Geoffrey W. Bromiley (Eerdmans, 1980), pp. 350-359, on Romans 13:1-7
C. E. B. Cranfield, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Epistle to the Romans*, Vol. 2 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1979), pp. 651-673, on Romans 13:1-7
