Robert Jewett, *Romans: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 780-806, on Romans 13:1-7
N. T. Wright, "Romans," in *The New Interpreter''s Bible*, Vol. 10 (Abingdon, 2002), pp. 717-723, on Romans 13:11-12
Neil Elliott, *The Arrogance of Nations: Reading Romans in the Shadow of Empire* (Fortress, 2008), pp. 152-167, on Romans 13:1-7', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Exodus, Daniel, Mark, Luke, Acts, Romans', 6, 3, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The silencing texts are not uniform in pedigree, and this matters. Genesis 3:16 frames male rule as a consequence of the fall: the Hebrew *wehu yimshol-bak* ("and he shall rule over you") stands within the curse narrative, and Phyllis Trible argued that it describes a disordered, post-Edenic condition rather than a creational mandate — descriptive of fallen relations, not prescriptive of God''s intent. The Pastorals are a separate matter. Most critical scholars regard 1-2 Timothy and Titus as pseudonymous, composed c. 90-110 CE for a more institutionalized church; 1 Timothy 2:12''s prohibition (*didaskein de gynaiki ouk epitrepo*, "I do not permit a woman to teach") reflects a second-generation rollback of an earlier, more fluid practice. The most striking case is 1 Corinthians 14:34-35. These verses sit uneasily in their context — verse 33 and verse 36 read smoothly when the intervening command is removed — and a cluster of Western manuscripts (D, F, G, and the Old Latin) relocate them to follow verse 40. Gordon Fee took this floating placement as the classic signature of a marginal gloss that migrated into the text, and Philip Payne extended the case with a distigme-obelos marking in Codex Vaticanus. If 14:34-35 is non-Pauline, Paul''s authentic position appears in 1 Corinthians 11:5, where women pray and prophesy in the assembly with no prohibition — a flat contradiction of the silencing command attributed to him a few chapters later.

The agency texts are not isolated outliers. Deborah is called both *neviah* (prophetess) and judge (*shofet*); Judges 4:4 and 5:7 present her as Israel''s recognized civil and military authority, the one who summons and commissions Barak. The Joel oracle quoted at Acts 2:18 explicitly promises that daughters and female servants will prophesy, and Acts 21:9 reports Philip''s four daughters doing exactly that. The pattern continues in Paul''s own undisputed letters: Phoebe is a *diakonos* and *prostatis* (Romans 16:1-2), Junia is "prominent among the apostles" (Romans 16:7 — Eldon Epp showed the name was feminine and only later masculinized to "Junias"), and Priscilla instructs Apollos (Acts 18:26).

The standard harmonization restricts female authority to extraordinary, charismatic, or domestic spheres while reserving institutional teaching office for men: Deborah was a divinely raised exception, prophecy is not the same as authoritative teaching, and 1 Timothy regulates the ordered congregation. Schreiner and Köstenberger develop this with care, and it is internally coherent. But it works only by treating the subordination texts as the controlling norm and the agency texts as exceptions to be explained away — a hermeneutical decision, not one the texts themselves dictate. Reverse the priority, as egalitarians do, and the same canon underwrites women''s full ministry. The data do not resolve the question; they force a choice. The tension is real and the resolution is interpretive.

Verdict: genuine contradiction, partially mitigated by the strong text-critical case that 1 Corinthians 14:34-35 is a later interpolation and by the descriptive (not prescriptive) reading of Genesis 3:16.', 'Gordon D. Fee, *The First Epistle to the Corinthians* (NICNT; Eerdmans, 1987), pp. 699-708, on 1 Cor 14:34-35 as interpolation
Philip B. Payne, *Man and Woman, One in Christ* (Zondervan, 2009), pp. 217-267, on the manuscript case for interpolation
Phyllis Trible, *God and the Rhetoric of Sexuality* (Fortress, 1978), pp. 128-139, on Gen 3:16
Eldon Jay Epp, *Junia: The First Woman Apostle* (Fortress, 2005), pp. 23-78, on Rom 16:7
Craig S. Keener, *Paul, Women, and Wives* (Hendrickson, 1992), pp. 70-100
I. Howard Marshall, *The Pastoral Epistles* (ICC; T&T Clark, 1999), pp. 451-471, on 1 Tim 2:12
Elisabeth Schüssler Fiorenza, *In Memory of Her: A Feminist Theological Reconstruction of Christian Origins* (Crossroad, 1983), pp. 226-236
Thomas R. Schreiner and Andreas J. Köstenberger, eds., *Women in the Church*, 3rd ed. (Crossway, 2016), pp. 117-152, complementarian counter-position', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Genesis, Judges, Acts, 1 Corinthians, 1 Timothy, 1 Peter', 6, 3, 3, 1, 1, '1 Corinthians 14:34-35 is regarded by many critics (Fee, Payne) as a non-Pauline interpolation, with some Western witnesses (D F G) relocating the verses after 14:40.', 'NA28 apparatus at 1 Corinthians 14:34-35');

The pedigree of the texts matters here. Critical scholarship generally treats Colossians as deutero-Pauline and 1 Peter as pseudonymous, with the codes representing a second-generation accommodation to Roman social norms — precisely the kind of institutional consolidation one would expect as the movement''s eschatological urgency cooled and it sought a stable footing in the wider culture. The friction with the Jesus tradition is sharpest at Matthew 23:8-10, where Jesus forbids the very titles of hierarchical authority the codes inscribe: "call no man your father upon the earth... neither be ye called masters (*kathegetai*), for one is your Master, even Christ." Davies and Allison read this as a deliberate leveling of rank within the community.

The strongest harmonization is a two-spheres distinction, and it deserves a fair hearing. On this reading the commands operate on different planes: slaves owe ordered social obedience in the temporal household, while worship (*latreuo*, Matthew 4:10) and ultimate allegiance belong to God alone; "be not ye the servants of men" concerns spiritual bondage to human teaching or sin, not the social institution of slavery; and Matthew 23 forbids honorific religious titles within the assembly, not civic structures. The qualifying phrases reinforce this — Colossians 3:22 frames slave-obedience as done "as to the Lord," and 1 Peter grounds submission in conscience toward God. Each text, on its own terms, can be construed so as not to command the worship reserved for God.

But the distinction strains at two points. First, 1 Peter 2:18 specifically commands obedience to *harsh* masters (*skoliois*), which the qualifier cannot soften into a merely civic arrangement; it sanctifies submission to abuse. Second, the codes demand obedience "with all fear" (*meta pantos phobou*), and *phobos* in the LXX is the loaded term elsewhere reserved for reverence toward God; the codes thus do not merely permit slavery but fold the slave-master bond into Christian devotion, making servile obedience a religious duty. Jennifer Glancy and J. Albert Harrill have shown that early Christianity left the institution of slavery substantially intact and supplied it theological reinforcement rather than critique. Against that backdrop, the contradiction with the "call no man master" tradition is not dissolved by the two-spheres move; it is relocated. Whether one judges the conflict resolved depends on how much theological weight the religiously charged language of "fear" and "as to the Lord" is allowed to carry.

Verdict: partially harmonizable in principle by the two-spheres distinction, but the religiously loaded "fear" language and the command to obey harsh masters keep a genuine theological tension with the Jesus and undisputed-Pauline traditions.', 'David L. Balch, *Let Wives Be Submissive: The Domestic Code in 1 Peter* (SBLMS 26; Scholars Press, 1981), pp. 1-21, 119-121
James D. G. Dunn, *The Epistles to the Colossians and to Philemon* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 1996), pp. 251-265, on Col 3:22-23
Paul J. Achtemeier, *1 Peter* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1996), pp. 192-200, on 1 Pet 2:18
W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *Matthew*, Vol. 3 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1997), pp. 271-276, on Matt 23:8-10
Jennifer A. Glancy, *Slavery in Early Christianity* (Oxford University Press, 2002), pp. 142-156
J. Albert Harrill, *Slaves in the New Testament: Literary, Social, and Moral Dimensions* (Fortress, 2006), pp. 85-117', 0, NULL, 5, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, 1 Corinthians, Colossians, 1 Peter', 6, 2, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The universalizing clause of Genesis 41:57 ("all countries came into Egypt to Joseph for to buy corn") looks at first like it pushes back toward a genuinely global event, but in context it functions as narrative hyperbole describing the reach of Egypt''s grain trade, not a meteorological claim about every landmass. The same idiom recurs throughout the cycle precisely because the storyteller is emphasizing the magnitude of Joseph''s administrative triumph, not making a cosmological assertion.

More important is what the Genesis 8:22 promise actually guarantees. The verse pledges the persistence of the cosmic rhythms — seedtime/harvest, cold/heat, summer/winter, day/night — that is, the ongoing seasonal and cosmic order, not a guarantee against any localized crop failure. Westermann reads 8:22 as an assurance of the regular structure of nature against a return to the pre-creation chaos of the flood, not a promise of uninterrupted agricultural success everywhere at once. On that reading a regional famine, even a severe seven-year one, does not violate the promise: the seasons still turn, the cycle of sowing and reaping continues globally, and the structure of nature is intact even where a particular harvest fails. The famine is a failure within the system, not a cessation of the system.

So the contradiction softens considerably on the most defensible reading of both texts: 8:22 promises the durability of the natural order; the Joseph famine is a localized (or at most regional) catastrophe described in the hyperbolic idiom typical of Hebrew narrative. The residual friction is real only if one insists on a maximally literal, globally exhaustive reading of both *kol ha''aretz* in Genesis 41 and "shall not cease" in Genesis 8:22 — and pressing both maximally is what manufactures the conflict.

Source criticism adds a further layer without dissolving the harmonization. Genesis 8:22 is generally assigned to J (the Yahwist), while the Joseph narrative is a complex composite of J and E with later editorial shaping. Richard Friedman places 8:22 in J and treats the Joseph cycle as a distinct, intricately woven unit. The juxtaposition of an absolute-sounding promise and a near-global famine is the kind of unflattened tension the documentary model predicts when a redactor folds independent traditions into a continuous story. But here the redactional seam does not produce a logical contradiction so much as a difference of register: a covenantal promise about cosmic order standing a few chapters away from a hyperbolic famine narrative about regional disaster.

Verdict: overstated. The lexical breadth of *kol ha''aretz*, the hyperbolic conventions of Hebrew narrative, and the proper scope of the Genesis 8:22 promise (cosmic order, not local crop yield) together dissolve the apparent contradiction.', 'Claus Westermann, *Genesis 1-11: A Continental Commentary*, trans. John J. Scullion (Fortress, 1994), pp. 457-460, on Gen 8:22
Claus Westermann, *Genesis 37-50: A Continental Commentary*, trans. John J. Scullion (Fortress, 2002), pp. 87-104, on the Joseph famine
Gordon J. Wenham, *Genesis 1-15* (WBC 1; Word, 1987), pp. 190-191, on Gen 8:22
Victor P. Hamilton, *The Book of Genesis: Chapters 18-50* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 1995), pp. 510-515, on Gen 41:54-57
Richard Elliott Friedman, *The Bible with Sources Revealed* (HarperOne, 2003), p. 47 (Gen 8:22, J) and pp. 91-100 (Joseph cycle)
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 87-88, on the localized famine reading', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', 'Genesis', 7, 2, 1, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

Matthew, writing later (c. 80-90 CE) and editing Mark for a more Jewish-Christian readership, replaces *Syrophoinikissa* with *gyne Chananaia* — "a Canaanite woman" (Matthew 15:22). "Canaanite" was an archaic, biblical term by the first century; no one in the Roman provincial administration would have called a contemporary woman a Canaanite. The substitution is purposeful. Matthew reactivates the Hebrew Bible category of Israel''s paradigmatic ancient enemies — the Canaanites whom Joshua was commanded to dispossess and never fully did. Coupled with the woman''s address of Jesus as "Son of David" (a title Mark''s version lacks) and Jesus'' initial statement that he was sent "only to the lost sheep of the house of Israel" (15:24, also absent in Mark), Matthew reframes the episode as an encounter at the boundary of Israel and the nations, in which a Canaanite — of all people — models the faith Israel lacks. The redaction serves Matthew''s larger salvation-historical scheme that moves from Israel to the inclusion of the Gentiles.

The standard harmonization observes that the two terms describe overlapping populations: the Phoenicians were historically descended from the Canaanites of the Levantine coast, and the Septuagint sometimes renders the region and its inhabitants with *Chanaan/Chananaios*. This is correct as far as it goes, and it shows Matthew was not contradicting Mark about who the woman was. But the harmonization actually concedes the analytical point rather than refuting it. The two designations are not interchangeable in first-century usage — one is current Roman regional-ethnic terminology, the other a deliberately archaizing biblical label — and the only reason to prefer the archaic term is the theological work it performs. Davies and Allison treat the change as a clear, intentional Matthean reshaping of Mark''s narrative; Ulrich Luz reads it the same way.

The upshot is that this is not an error and not a claim that two different women existed; it is evidence that the evangelists were theologians editing their sources, not stenographers preserving them. The "contradiction" is a redactional substitution whose two terms do not match in contemporary register, undertaken to recast a Markan healing as a Matthean parable of Gentile faith over against unbelieving Israel.

Verdict: harmonizable as to historical referent (same woman, overlapping ethnic categories), but the divergence is real evidence of deliberate Matthean redaction of Mark for theological ends.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 2 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1991), pp. 541-557, on Matt 15:21-28
Joel Marcus, *Mark 1-8* (AB 27; Doubleday, 2000), pp. 461-471, on Mark 7:24-30
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 8-20: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2001), pp. 336-345
Gerd Theissen, *The Gospels in Context*, trans. Linda M. Maloney (Fortress, 1991), pp. 61-80, on the background of Syrophoenicia
R. T. France, *The Gospel of Mark* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 2002), pp. 296-301, on Mark 7:24-30', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, Mark', 10, 2, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

Apologists such as I. Howard Marshall and Darrell Bock harmonize on both fronts. Geographically, Bethany sits on the eastern slope of the Mount of Olives, roughly two miles from Jerusalem, so the two place-names can plausibly designate the same general locale — Luke naming the village, Acts naming the ridge. This is a genuine and fair point of Judean topography. Chronologically, they argue that Luke 24 simply telescopes events without dating them: the Gospel never says the ascension occurred on Easter evening, so the forty-day interval of Acts can be silently inserted between Luke 24:43 and 24:50. Both moves are textually possible.

They are also strained. Luke 24:50 specifies Bethany, not the Mount of Olives in general, and Acts 1:12 specifies the Mount of Olives as the return point without mentioning Bethany; the named locations differ even if they are adjacent. The chronological harmonization is harder. Luke 24 reads as a continuous, same-day narrative with no seam where forty days could be inserted; the temporal adverbs and connectives drive straight from the resurrection morning to the ascension, and the Gospel''s ending has the disciples "continually in the temple, praising and blessing God" — a tidy closure that does not anticipate a forty-day teaching period. There is also a text-critical wrinkle at Luke 24:51: the clause "and was carried up into heaven" (*kai anephereto eis ton ouranon*) is absent from a few important witnesses (notably the original hand of Codex Sinaiticus and Codex Bezae), which led some earlier scholars to question whether Luke''s Gospel originally narrated an ascension at all, or only a departure — a variant that complicates any simple harmonization of the two accounts.

The more economical explanation, defended by A. W. Zwiep, Mikeal Parsons, and Richard Pervo, is literary. Luke composed his Gospel with an ascension-as-conclusion and then, when writing Acts, redeployed the ascension as the prologue and inciting incident of the second volume, reshaping its geography and chronology to fit each book''s rhetorical function: closure for the Gospel, a forty-day commissioning and a launch-point for the Spirit and the mission in Acts. The divergent details are the fingerprints of an author using the same event as a hinge between two works, telling it twice for two purposes.

Verdict: the geography is plausibly harmonizable (Bethany on the slope of Olivet), but the chronology is not; the doubled, divergent account is best explained as Lukan literary reshaping of the ascension to serve as the seam between his two volumes.', 'Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Acts of the Apostles* (AB 31; Doubleday, 1998), pp. 198-216, on Acts 1:9-12
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke X-XXIV* (AB 28A; Doubleday, 1985), pp. 1586-1593, on Luke 24:50-51
François Bovon, *Luke 3: A Commentary on the Gospel of Luke 19:28-24:53* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2012), pp. 408-419
Richard I. Pervo, *Acts: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2009), pp. 36-49
Mikeal C. Parsons, *The Departure of Jesus in Luke-Acts: The Ascension Narratives in Context* (JSNTSup 21; JSOT Press, 1987), pp. 113-149
A. W. Zwiep, *The Ascension of the Messiah in Lukan Christology* (NovTSup 87; Brill, 1997), pp. 80-117', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Luke, Acts', 4, 2, 3, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

Hebrews 11:8 reads, in the Greek, that Abraham *exēlthen mē epistamenos pou erchetai* — "went out, not knowing where he was going." Crucially, the author of Hebrews is commenting on the *moment of departure*, the response to the call, not on the eventual arrival. The verse is built on the participle of obedience: "when he was called … he obeyed; and he went out." Read against Genesis 12:1, where the destination is genuinely unstated, Hebrews 11:8 is an accurate and even acute reading of the Hebrew: at the instant of the command, Abram did not know the land would be Canaan. Genesis 12:5, by contrast, is the narrator''s retrospective summary of the journey''s outcome. The apologetic harmonization — that Hebrews comments on the departure under the open command while Genesis reports the disclosed destination en route — therefore rests on the text itself, not on special pleading. This is why the entry is best classed as partially harmonized rather than a clean contradiction.

What remains is not a factual collision but a difference of literary purpose, and that difference is illuminating. Hebrews 11 belongs to the rhetorical genre of the encomium or *exemplum*-catalogue, a "hall of faith" in which biblical figures are recast as models of *pistis* for a community under pressure (compare Sirach 44-50, 1 Maccabees 2:51-60, and Philo''s lists of the virtuous). The author''s controlling metaphor is pilgrimage: Abraham is the prototype of the believer who leaves a settled home for a destination known only by promise, ultimately the heavenly city (Heb 11:10, 16). The theology of journeying-by-faith shapes how the Genesis data are selected and framed; the author foregrounds the not-knowing because it serves the exhortation, not because he is correcting Genesis. Second Temple Jewish tradition had already amplified Abraham''s faith at the call (Jubilees 12; Philo, *De Abrahamo* 60-88; *De migratione Abrahami*), so Hebrews stands within an established interpretive stream rather than inventing the emphasis.

Source-critically, Genesis 12:1-5 is composite: most analysts assign vv. 1-4a to J (the call and Abram''s response) and v. 4b-5 to P (the itinerary and the names of those who accompanied him, with its characteristic interest in property and persons "gotten in Haran"). The naming of Canaan in v. 5 is thus the work of the priestly itinerary-style, layered over the older J call. This means the "known destination" of v. 5 and the "open command" of v. 1 derive from different compositional hands, which further explains why the two do not sit in tight logical sequence. The author of Hebrews, reading the received text, latched onto the J-layer''s open command.

The residual tension is therefore narrow. If one insists on reading both verses as flat historical reportage of Abraham''s state of knowledge at a single instant, a contradiction appears. But the more natural reading — Genesis 12:1 names no land, Hebrews comments on that very moment, Genesis 12:5 reports the outcome — dissolves most of it, leaving a difference of aim rather than fact. Verdict: overstated; largely harmonizable as a difference of narrative moment and purpose, with genuine residual tension only at the level of propositional flattening.', 'Claus Westermann, *Genesis 12-36: A Continental Commentary*, trans. John J. Scullion (Minneapolis: Augsburg, 1985), pp. 144-159, on the call of Abram and the J/P composition of Gen 12:1-5
Nahum M. Sarna, *Genesis* (JPS Torah Commentary; Philadelphia: Jewish Publication Society, 1989), pp. 88-91, on the open-ended command and the Hebrew of Gen 12:1
Harold W. Attridge, *The Epistle to the Hebrews* (Hermeneia; Philadelphia: Fortress, 1989), pp. 321-327, on Heb 11:8 and the encomium genre of Hebrews 11
Craig R. Koester, *Hebrews* (AB 36; New York: Doubleday, 2001), pp. 480-486, on the pilgrimage motif and Abraham as faith exemplar
William L. Lane, *Hebrews 9-13* (WBC 47B; Dallas: Word, 1991), pp. 343-352, on the moment of departure in Heb 11:8', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Genesis, Hebrews', 7, 3, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

Genesis 25:1-2 complicates this. "Then again Abraham took a wife, and her name was Keturah," and she bore him Zimran, Jokshan, Medan, Midian, Ishbak, and Shuah. The waw-consecutive opening of 25:1 (*wa-yosef Avraham*, "and Abraham added/again") places the Keturah marriage after the immediately preceding material — the death and burial of Sarah (Gen 23) and the marriage of Isaac (Gen 24). On the surface chronology, Abraham fathers six sons after the age of 137 (Sarah died at 127, Gen 23:1, when Abraham was ten years her senior, Gen 17:17), apparently without any noted miracle.

Apologists, including Calvin and Archer, offer two principal harmonizations. The first is chronological re-sequencing: the Keturah union preceded Sarah''s death, Genesis 25 being a topical appendix rather than a strict chronological notice, so the sons were fathered when Abraham was younger. The Hebrew resists this. The verb *yosef* with the perfect placement after chapters 23-24, and the rabbinic discussion in *Genesis Rabbah* 61 (which debates whether Keturah is to be identified with Hagar), both treat the marriage as subsequent to Sarah''s death; 1 Chronicles 1:32 likewise lists Keturah as a distinct concubine-wife. The narrative does not support pre-dating.

The second harmonization is stronger and is the basis for classing this as partially harmonized. It distinguishes the locus of the miracle: the intervention restored *Sarah''s* womb, not Abraham''s potency. Genesis 18:11-12 is explicit — "it had ceased to be with Sarah after the manner of women," i.e., she was post-menopausal, and she laughs at the absurdity of conceiving. The biological obstacle in the Isaac narrative is specifically Sarah''s age and prior lifelong barrenness (Gen 11:30), not merely Abraham''s. On this reading Abraham could retain residual fertility into great age — not biologically impossible for a man — while Sarah''s conception genuinely required divine action. Paul''s pairing of "his own body … and the deadness of Sarah''s womb" is then best taken as rhetorical hyperbole heightening the miracle, the "as good as dead" applied to Abraham functioning typologically rather than as a clinical claim of permanent sterility.

What the harmonization does not fully absorb is that Romans 4:19 *explicitly names Abraham''s body* alongside Sarah''s, and the whole rhetorical weight of "so many descendants from one as good as dead" rests on his incapacity. The Keturah cycle puts real pressure on that emphasis. Source- and tradition-critically, the Keturah list is an ethnographic genealogy: the six sons map onto Arabian and trans-Jordanian peoples (Midian most prominently, also Sheba and Dedan among Jokshan''s line), functioning as an ethnogenesis that affiliates surrounding tribes to Abraham. This material is older folkloric tradition with a different purpose from the Isaac promise-narrative, and Paul, reading Genesis theologically, builds his resurrection typology from the Isaac strand without integrating the Keturah strand. The result is genuine tension at the level of Paul''s rhetoric, considerably eased — though not erased — by the Sarah-centered reading. Verdict: partially harmonizable; the miracle plausibly centered on Sarah, but the Pauline rhetoric of Abraham''s own deadness remains in tension with the Keturah narrative.', 'Claus Westermann, *Genesis 12-36: A Continental Commentary*, trans. John J. Scullion (Minneapolis: Augsburg, 1985), pp. 393-398, on the Keturah genealogy and its ethnographic function
Nahum M. Sarna, *Genesis* (JPS Torah Commentary; Philadelphia: Jewish Publication Society, 1989), pp. 171-173, on Keturah and the Arabian/Midianite affiliations
Robert Jewett, *Romans: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Minneapolis: Fortress, 2007), pp. 336-340, on Rom 4:19, nenekrōmenon, and the resurrection typology
Harold W. Attridge, *The Epistle to the Hebrews* (Hermeneia; Philadelphia: Fortress, 1989), pp. 325-326, on Heb 11:12 and ''as good as dead''
John Calvin, *Commentaries on the First Book of Moses Called Genesis*, trans. John King (Edinburgh: Calvin Translation Society, 1847), Vol. 2, pp. 22-25, on the chronological harmonization of the Keturah marriage
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Grand Rapids: Zondervan, 1982), pp. 102-103, on the Sarah-centered miracle and Abraham''s continued fertility', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Genesis, Romans, Hebrews', 5, 4, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The most important datum for harmonization is internal to Genesis. Genesis 15:13-16 explicitly tells Abram that his seed will be "a stranger in a land that is not theirs" and afflicted four hundred years, and only "in the fourth generation they shall come hither again." The deferral of possession to the descendants is therefore written into the promise from the start; Abraham is promised the land *for his seed*, with fulfillment postponed past his death. Read this way, Acts 7:5 and Hebrews 11:13 are not contradicting Genesis but restating its own logic: the promise was given, the possession deferred. Hebrews 11:9 even uses the language of sojourning "in the land of promise, as in a strange country, dwelling in tabernacles," which is precisely the Genesis portrait of the patriarch as *ger* (resident alien). The one piece of Canaanite soil Abraham ever owned was the burial cave of Machpelah, purchased from Ephron the Hittite (Gen 23) — a detail that ironically underscores the point: he held a grave, not a kingdom.

Where the harmonization does not fully reach is the modifier *olam*, "everlasting/perpetual," attached to Abraham''s own possession in 17:8, and the maximalist "to thee will I give it … for ever" of 13:15. These phrases, taken at face value, promise the patriarch himself perpetual tenure, which he never had. The New Testament resolves this not by appeal to deferral but by *redefining the referent of the inheritance*. Hebrews 11:10 says Abraham "looked for a city which hath foundations, whose builder and maker is God," and 11:16 declares that the patriarchs "desire a better country, that is, an heavenly" one. The earthly land is reread as a type of the eschatological/heavenly homeland. This is a genuine theological reinterpretation, and it is honest about the historical situation — Hebrews 11:13 frankly concedes the promises were "seen afar off" and not received.

Source- and tradition-critically, the developed *achuzzat olam* language of Genesis 17 belongs to the Priestly stratum (P), whose covenant theology many scholars date to the exilic or post-exilic period, when Israel''s claim to the land needed grounding in an unconditional, pre-conquest divine grant precisely because possession had become precarious. The older J/E traditions preserve tribal land memories, but the perpetual-possession formula reflects a Second Temple ideology of land entitlement. Acts 7 and Hebrews 11 were composed in the post-70 CE diaspora, after Rome''s destruction of Jerusalem and the loss of the land, when a literal-territorial fulfillment had failed catastrophically. Their spiritualizing move — the inheritance is heavenly, the city has foundations God laid — keeps the promise operative when geopolitics had voided it. Brueggemann and Habel trace exactly this trajectory from land-as-gift to land-as-symbol.

The net assessment: the bulk of the apparent contradiction dissolves once Genesis 15''s deferral clause is read alongside the promise, since the New Testament merely affirms that deferral. The irreducible residue is the *olam* language applied to Abraham personally, which the New Testament does not deny but reinterprets by changing what "inheritance" denotes — from Canaanite territory to a heavenly homeland. That is a real shift in meaning, made under historical pressure, rather than a simple factual error. Verdict: partially harmonizable; the deferral is built into Genesis, but the ''everlasting possession'' language is resolved only by the New Testament''s reinterpretation of the inheritance.', 'Claus Westermann, *Genesis 12-36: A Continental Commentary*, trans. John J. Scullion (Minneapolis: Augsburg, 1985), pp. 178-184, 255-263, on the land promise and the P ''everlasting possession'' formula
Nahum M. Sarna, *Genesis* (JPS Torah Commentary; Philadelphia: Jewish Publication Society, 1989), pp. 100-101, 123-124, on achuzzat olam and the deferral of possession
Richard I. Pervo, *Acts: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Minneapolis: Fortress, 2009), pp. 178-184, on Acts 7:5 and Stephen''s speech
Harold W. Attridge, *The Epistle to the Hebrews* (Hermeneia; Philadelphia: Fortress, 1989), pp. 327-335, on Heb 11:9-16 and the heavenly-city reinterpretation of the inheritance
Walter Brueggemann, *The Land: Place as Gift, Promise, and Challenge in Biblical Faith*, 2nd ed. (Minneapolis: Fortress, 2002), pp. 14-35, on the trajectory from territorial to symbolic land theology
Norman C. Habel, *The Land Is Mine: Six Biblical Land Ideologies* (Minneapolis: Fortress, 1995), pp. 115-133, on the ancestral household land-grant ideology', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Genesis, Acts, Hebrews', 7, 3, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

Verses 38-45 then execute an abrupt and devastating reversal. The same God who swore the oath has "cast off and abhorred," "made void the covenant," "profaned his crown," broken down his hedges, and — in v. 44 — "made his glory to cease, and cast his throne down to the ground." The vocabulary deliberately inverts the promises: the throne sworn to stand "as the sun" is hurled to the earth. Verses 46-51 turn to lament and petition — "How long, LORD? wilt thou hide thyself for ever?" — and the psalm ends without resolution, on an unanswered question and a closing doxology (v. 52) that belongs to the editorial seam closing Book III of the Psalter rather than to the psalm''s own argument.

The juxtaposition is not a redactional accident or a careless contradiction. It is the psalm''s central rhetorical instrument. The poet sets the maximal oath against the catastrophic reality in order to *confront God with the breach of covenant* — the logic is forensic: "This is what you swore; this is what has happened; how can both stand?" The contradiction is the complaint. To harmonize it away is to dismantle the very mechanism by which the lament does its theological work.

Critical scholarship (Tate, Hossfeld-Zenger, Goldingay) reads the final form as exilic or early post-exilic, composed after the fall of the Davidic monarchy in 587 BCE, when the "everlasting" dynasty had visibly ended. The pre-exilic court theology preserved in vv. 1-37 — itself drawing on the ancient Near Eastern royal-grant covenant type, in which a deity guarantees a dynasty perpetually — is set against the lived disaster of vv. 38-45 to dramatize the apparent failure of the divine word. Mowinckel situated the royal psalmody in cultic enthronement settings; whatever its earlier liturgical use, the present composition reframes that royal ideology as the premise of an unresolved theodicy.

The standard harmonization is messianic and Christian: the throne was "cast down" temporarily for historical Israel but "restored" eschatologically in Christ, the eternal Davidic king. The New Testament makes exactly this move — Acts 2:30 has Peter cite the Davidic oath as fulfilled in the resurrection, and Hebrews 1:8 applies enthronement language to the Son. This is a coherent later theological resolution, but it is imported from outside the psalm; Psalm 89 itself offers no such resolution. Its final word is interrogative — the covenant promise and the historical catastrophe are left in unrelieved collision, which is the poet''s intent. Reading the messianic answer back into the psalm satisfies a canonical-theological harmony but does not remove the deliberate contradiction the psalmist constructed. Verdict: genuine and intentional contradiction, deployed as a rhetorical device of lament; resolved only by later messianic reinterpretation external to the psalm itself.', 'Marvin E. Tate, *Psalms 51-100* (WBC 20; Dallas: Word, 1990), pp. 411-435, on the tripartite structure and exilic dating of Psalm 89
Frank-Lothar Hossfeld and Erich Zenger, *Psalms 2: A Commentary on Psalms 51-100* (Hermeneia; Minneapolis: Fortress, 2005), pp. 399-415, on the covenant oath, its reversal, and the lament structure
John Goldingay, *Psalms 42-89* (BCOTWP; Grand Rapids: Baker Academic, 2007), pp. 660-688, on the deliberate juxtaposition of oath and catastrophe
Jon D. Levenson, *The Hebrew Bible, the Old Testament, and Historical Criticism* (Louisville: Westminster John Knox, 1993), pp. 67-69, on the tension within the Davidic covenant tradition
Sigmund Mowinckel, *The Psalms in Israel''s Worship*, Vol. 1, trans. D. R. Ap-Thomas (Nashville: Abingdon, 1962), pp. 67-78, on royal psalmody and the cultic setting of the Davidic ideology', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', 'Psalms', 7, 3, 2, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

The Johannine framing is set inside the Farewell Discourse (John 13-17), addressed to the gathered disciples. The death is presented as the supreme act of *agapē* understood as friendship: "Ye are my friends, if ye do whatsoever I command you. Henceforth I call you not servants … but I have called you friends" (John 15:14-15). The friendship language is relational and revelatory — the disciples are *philoi* because Jesus has disclosed to them what he received from the Father. The shepherd-and-sheep image likewise presupposes an in-group that already belongs to him ("my sheep," 10:14, 27). The death, in John, expresses and seals an existing bond.

Paul''s framing in Romans 5:6-10 is deliberately the opposite. He stacks the terms to maximize the moral asymmetry of grace: Christ died for the *asthenōn* (weak, v. 6), the *asebōn* (ungodly, v. 6), *hamartōlōn* (sinners, v. 8), and *echthroi* (enemies, v. 10). The rhetorical engine of the passage is that the death *precedes* and *causes* the reconciliation — therefore at the moment of the death the beneficiaries stood in hostility. "God commendeth his love toward us, in that, while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us." The emphasis serves Paul''s argument that justification is unmerited and unilateral.

The strongest harmonization observes that the two statements specify different temporal vantage points and answer different questions. Romans 5 describes the recipients'' status *prior to* reconciliation — their condition at the time of the cross. John 15 describes the relational status of the disciples *as it now stands*, after they have responded to Jesus. There is no logical contradiction in saying that Christ died for those who were enemies and who, through that death, became friends; "friends-who-were-formerly-enemies" is a coherent single referent. Systematic theology routinely integrates the two: the objects of the atonement were enemies by status and are constituted friends by its effect. On this reading the entry is best classed as partially harmonized, since the statements are not mutually exclusive claims about the same instant.

What the harmonization cannot fully smooth is the genuinely different soteriological models underlying the two corpora. Paul''s reconciliation-of-enemies framework (Romans 5; 2 Corinthians 5:18-21) is forensic and substitutionary — it presupposes alienation, wrath, and a transaction that changes legal-relational status. John''s laying-down-life-for-friends framework is relational and revelatory — the cross manifests and consummates love within a covenant fellowship and is light on forensic categories. The texts are not answering the same question: Paul asks how the hostile are reconciled; John asks how divine love is supremely displayed to those drawn into Jesus'' circle. Notably, John never describes the cross''s beneficiaries as *former enemies* the way Paul does; the categories simply do not overlap in his account. So while the propositions can be reconciled at the systematic level, the harmonization flattens two distinct theologies of the atonement that the evangelist and the apostle each developed for their own pastoral ends. Verdict: harmonizable at the propositional level as differing temporal and theological vantage points, while reflecting a real divergence between Johannine and Pauline atonement theology.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John XIII-XXI* (AB 29A; Garden City: Doubleday, 1970), pp. 681-684, on John 15:13 and the friendship-love motif
Craig S. Keener, *The Gospel of John: A Commentary*, Vol. 2 (Peabody: Hendrickson, 2003), pp. 1003-1007, on philoi and the disciples'' relational status
Robert Jewett, *Romans: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Minneapolis: Fortress, 2007), pp. 357-365, on Rom 5:6-10 and the echthroi rhetoric
C. E. B. Cranfield, *Romans 1-8* (ICC; Edinburgh: T&T Clark, 1975), pp. 263-269, on the temporal logic of reconciliation while enemies
Ernst Käsemann, *Commentary on Romans*, trans. Geoffrey W. Bromiley (Grand Rapids: Eerdmans, 1980), pp. 136-145, on the unilateral, unmerited character of Pauline reconciliation
James D. G. Dunn, *The Theology of Paul the Apostle* (Grand Rapids: Eerdmans, 1998), pp. 207-233, on Pauline atonement and reconciliation categories', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'John, Romans', 5, 3, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The historical question is whether the Sanhedrin had in fact lost the ius gladii — the right of the sword — in this period. The dominant scholarly reconstruction, set out at length by Raymond Brown in The Death of the Messiah, is that Rome reserved capital cases for the prefect''s court, which is precisely why the authorities need Pilate at all. A.N. Sherwin-White''s classic study of Roman provincial administration reaches the same conclusion: local councils retained wide latitude in civil and religious matters, but the death penalty was a guarded Roman prerogative in a directly governed province like Judea after 6 CE. The rabbinic tradition preserves a memory consistent with this: the Talmud (b. Sanhedrin 41a) recalls that capital jurisdiction was removed "forty years before the destruction of the Temple," and the Jerusalem Talmud (y. Sanhedrin 1:1, 18a) reports the Sanhedrin''s exile from the Chamber of Hewn Stone. These traditions are late and stylized, but they corroborate the general picture that John 18:31 reflects. The stoning of Stephen (Acts 7) and of James (Josephus, Antiquities 20.200, where Ananus is censured for convening a court without the governor''s leave) are sometimes cited as counter-evidence, but both look like lynchings or actions taken in a jurisdictional vacuum rather than regular sentences, which is why they could be challenged.

The harmonization is therefore historically defensible, and on the narrow legal question the verdict is that there is no contradiction. The deeper critical problem lies elsewhere. John deploys both statements rhetorically to construct his Passion theology. The Fourth Gospel is invested in maximizing the culpability of "the Jews" (hoi Ioudaioi — John''s hostile collective term for the religious authorities) while presenting Pilate as a vacillating figure who repeatedly pronounces Jesus innocent (John 18:38; 19:4, 6). The note in 18:32 makes the agenda explicit: the matter went to Pilate "to fulfill the word of Jesus, signifying what death he should die" — that is, crucifixion (being "lifted up," John 3:14; 12:32-33), a Roman penalty, not the stoning that Jewish blasphemy law prescribed. The legal handoff is thus narratively engineered to deliver the theologically required mode of death. Brown, Bond, and Reinhartz all stress that John''s Pilate is a literary-theological construction rather than a transcript, and that the Gospel''s anti-Jewish framing — read by Adele Reinhartz and Amy-Jill Levine as a serious historical and ethical liability — is the real interpretive issue. The surface contradiction resolves cleanly into a coherent juridical picture; what survives is not a logical problem but a polemical one. Verdict: harmonizable on the legal mechanics; the genuine difficulty is John''s tendentious framing.', 'Raymond E. Brown, *The Death of the Messiah*, vol. 1 (ABRL; Doubleday, 1994), pp. 363-372, 711-722, on John 18:31 and the Sanhedrin''s loss of capital jurisdiction
A. N. Sherwin-White, *Roman Society and Roman Law in the New Testament* (Clarendon, 1963), pp. 35-43, on Rome''s reservation of capital jurisdiction
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John XIII-XXI* (AB 29A; Doubleday, 1970), pp. 849-852, 875-879, on the Johannine trial narrative
Helen K. Bond, *Pontius Pilate in History and Interpretation* (SNTSMS 100; Cambridge University Press, 1998), pp. 165-186, on John''s literary-theological Pilate
Adele Reinhartz, *Befriending the Beloved Disciple: A Jewish Reading of the Gospel of John* (Continuum, 2001), pp. 71-98, on John''s anti-Jewish framing of the Passion
Amy-Jill Levine, *The Misunderstood Jew: The Church and the Scandal of the Jewish Jesus* (HarperOne, 2006), pp. 87-119, on the ethical problem of the Passion''s polemic
Flavius Josephus, *Jewish Antiquities* (LCL; Harvard University Press), 20.200, on Ananus executing James without the governor''s leave', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'John', 7, 3, 2, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

The New Testament texts relocate the reckoning to the eschaton. Revelation 20:12 stages a post-mortem assize: the dead stand before the throne, "the books were opened," and judgment proceeds "according to their works." Matthew 16:27 has the Son of Man come "in the glory of his Father with his angels" to "reward every man according to his works" — a future, climactic event. 2 Corinthians 5:10 places all believers before the bēma, the "judgment seat of Christ," to receive recompense for deeds done in the body. The Greek of these texts is consistently forward-looking and forensic (apodōsei, "he will repay," in Matt 16:27; the future passive in Revelation''s courtroom). The frame is no longer the lifespan but the end of history.

The pivot between the two models is visible inside the Hebrew Bible itself. Daniel 12:2 — "many of them that sleep in the dust of the earth shall awake, some to everlasting life, and some to shame and everlasting contempt" — is the first unambiguous Hebrew Bible text positing differentiated post-mortem fates, and the critical consensus, developed in detail by John J. Collins, dates it to the Antiochene persecution (c. 167-164 BCE). The reason is theological necessity: when the righteous were being killed precisely for their covenant fidelity, the immanent-justice model collapsed, and a resurrection-and-judgment scheme was required to vindicate God''s justice. 2 Maccabees 7 and 1 Enoch witness the same development in the same period. The New Testament inherits this apocalyptic eschatology wholesale.

The standard harmonization invokes a "both/and": God dispenses some recompense in this life and reserves final judgment for the next, so Proverbs and Revelation describe two phases of one coherent scheme. As a synthetic systematic theology this is internally consistent, and it is how most Christian traditions read the texts. But it flattens precisely the development the texts record, and it ignores the canon''s own internal protest. The wisdom tradition does not merely lack an afterlife — large parts of it actively contest the immanent-justice doctrine. The book of Job is built around this dispute: the friends recite the Proverbs 11:31 theology (Job 4:7-9; 8:11-22; 20:4-29), and Job''s whole complaint is that the wicked manifestly prosper and the righteous suffer (Job 21:7-13). Ecclesiastes 9:2-3 states flatly that one fate befalls the righteous and the wicked alike. As James Crenshaw and Roland Murphy show, the wisdom corpus is not a unified witness but an argument, and apocalyptic eschatology arose in part because the older theology had failed the test of experience. Jon Levenson and N. T. Wright trace how resurrection belief grew out of, rather than always coexisting with, that crisis. To read Proverbs and Revelation as two coordinated phases is to harmonize away a genuine theological evolution. Verdict: genuine contradiction reflecting the canon''s own development from immanent to eschatological justice.', 'John J. Collins, *Daniel* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1993), pp. 390-398, on Dan 12:2 as the breakthrough resurrection text
John J. Collins, *The Apocalyptic Imagination*, 3rd ed. (Eerdmans, 2016), pp. 110-129, on the emergence of judgment eschatology
Roland E. Murphy, *Proverbs* (WBC 22; Thomas Nelson, 1998), pp. 84-87, on the immanent retribution of Prov 11:31
James L. Crenshaw, *Old Testament Wisdom: An Introduction*, 3rd ed. (Westminster John Knox, 2010), pp. 76-94, on the wisdom tradition''s dispute over retribution
Jon D. Levenson, *Resurrection and the Restoration of Israel* (Yale University Press, 2006), pp. 181-200, on resurrection belief and theodicy
Richard Bauckham, *The Theology of the Book of Revelation* (Cambridge University Press, 1993), pp. 102-108, on the judgment of Revelation 20
N. T. Wright, *The Resurrection of the Son of God* (Fortress, 2003), pp. 85-128, on the development of Jewish resurrection belief', 0, NULL, 4, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Proverbs, Matthew, 2 Corinthians, Revelation', 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

The eternal-torment texts come from an entirely different theological world. Matthew 25:46 contrasts "everlasting punishment" (kolasin aiōnion) with "life eternal"; Revelation 20:10 has the devil, beast, and false prophet "tormented day and night for ever and ever (eis tous aiōnas tōn aiōnōn)"; Revelation 14:11 has "the smoke of their torment" ascending "for ever and ever" with "no rest day nor night"; Revelation 20:15 consigns the unlisted to the lake of fire. Daniel 12:2 again marks the hinge inside the Hebrew Bible, introducing an awakening of the dead "some to everlasting life, and some to shame and everlasting contempt." The transition to differentiated, conscious post-mortem fates occurred in the Second Temple period under converging pressures: the theodicy crisis of the Maccabean martyrdoms (2 Maccabees 7; 1 Enoch 22, 27), contact with Persian Zoroastrian dualism, and Hellenistic body-soul dualism. By the first century, eternal conscious torment had become a developed strand, and the Synoptic Gospels and Revelation carry it fully.

Two harmonizations are usually offered. The first is progressive revelation: the earlier texts give partial or accommodated truth, and the later texts disclose the full picture, so there is no contradiction, only unfolding. This is theologically coherent on its own terms but historically tendentious, since it must treat the considered anthropology of Job and Ecclesiastes as defective rather than as a distinct and internally argued tradition. The second harmonization is more interesting because it concedes the data: conditional immortality, or annihilationism, defended by Edward Fudge and adopted in John Stott''s later work, holds that the wicked are ultimately destroyed rather than endlessly tormented, and reads even the "eternal" language as denoting the permanence of the destruction (the "eternal fire" of Jude 7 consumed Sodom once and for all). On this reading the older Hebrew anthropology and a strand of the NT agree, and the eternal-conscious-torment texts are the outliers. Critically, this apologetic does not dissolve the contradiction; it sides with one pole of it. Fudge''s case actually concedes the SAB''s central claim — that the canon contains conflicting pictures of the fate of the dead — and resolves the tension by choosing annihilation over endless misery. As Levenson, Bauckham, and Seow document, the texts cannot be flattened into a single doctrine without suppressing either the holistic Hebrew anthropology or the apocalyptic torment imagery. Verdict: genuine contradiction; the canon records an evolving and ultimately unreconciled anthropology of death.', 'Jon D. Levenson, *Resurrection and the Restoration of Israel: The Ultimate Victory of the God of Life* (Yale University Press, 2006), pp. 35-66, on holistic Hebrew anthropology and Sheol
C. L. Seow, *Ecclesiastes* (AB 18C; Doubleday, 1997), pp. 300-307, on the finality of death in Eccl 9:5, 10
John J. Collins, *Daniel* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1993), pp. 390-398, on Dan 12:2 and differentiated post-mortem fates
Edward W. Fudge, *The Fire That Consumes*, 3rd ed. (Cascade, 2011), pp. 60-89, 200-247, presenting the conditional-immortality case
Richard Bauckham, *The Fate of the Dead: Studies on the Jewish and Christian Apocalypses* (NovTSup 93; Brill, 1998), pp. 132-159, on Second Temple afterlife developments
N. T. Wright, *The Resurrection of the Son of God* (Fortress, 2003), pp. 85-128, 367-398, on the development of post-mortem belief
John H. Walton, *Job* (NIVAC; Zondervan, 2012), pp. 124-138, on Job 3 and progressive revelation', 0, NULL, 4, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Job, Ecclesiastes, Daniel, Matthew, Revelation', 5, 3, 3, 1, 0, NULL, NULL);

Matthew 11:28-30 is widely recognized as drawing on the Wisdom tradition, and specifically on Sirach 51:23-27, where personified Wisdom summons the unlearned to "put your neck under the yoke" and promises, "I have labored little and found for myself much rest." The verbal and structural parallels — invitation, yoke, labor, rest — are close enough that Davies and Allison, Celia Deutsch, and M. Jack Suggs read the Matthean Jesus as casting himself in the role of personified Wisdom. The term "yoke" (Greek zygos, Hebrew ol) is a standard rabbinic metaphor for the discipline of obligation: the rabbis spoke of taking on "the yoke of the kingdom of heaven" and "the yoke of the commandments" (m. Berakhot 2:2). Crucially, Matthew sets the easy yoke against a heavy one. Matthew 23:4 condemns the scribes and Pharisees who "bind heavy burdens and grievous to be borne, and lay them on men''s shoulders." The contrast shows that "easy" in 11:30 is comparative: Christ''s yoke is restful relative to the crushing legal burden it replaces, not absolutely effortless.

The persecution-discipleship texts operate on a different axis. John 16:33 promises "in the world ye shall have tribulation (thlipsin)" — external pressure from a hostile world, immediately balanced by "in me ye might have peace." 2 Timothy 3:12 generalizes that "all that will live godly in Christ Jesus shall suffer persecution" — again external opposition. Hebrews 12:6-8 concerns divine paideia, the discipline a father administers to legitimate sons; lack of chastisement marks one as illegitimate (nothoi, "bastards"). None of these describes the inner experience of devotion to Christ as itself onerous; they describe what the surrounding world and the disciplining Father impose. Carson and France harmonize precisely here: the yoke is light in the sense that fellowship with Christ is life-giving rest, while the road through a hostile world is hard. The two frames are compatible because they answer different questions — what is it like to belong to Christ (restful) versus what does the world do to those who belong to him (persecute them).

This harmonization is exegetically grounded and largely succeeds; even critical commentators such as Ulrich Luz note that the Wisdom background frames the "easy yoke" relatively rather than absolutely. What remains is a residual rhetorical tension rather than a logical contradiction. Matthew 11:30 makes a blanket, unqualified assertion — the yoke is easy, the burden light — without the comparative qualifier the harmonization supplies, and the same Gospel records Jesus warning his followers in Matthew 10:16-39 that they will be delivered up to councils, scourged, hated of all men, and divided from their own households, even "take his cross and follow me." Read flat against that material, the saying does sit in some tension with the broader picture of costly discipleship. The contradiction is therefore real at the level of unqualified wording but dissolves substantially once genre (Wisdom invitation), idiom (the Torah-yoke metaphor), and the internal/external distinction are taken into account. Verdict: largely harmonizable; the leftover tension is rhetorical, lodged in the unqualified form of Matt 11:30 rather than in an actual incompatibility of claims.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, vol. 2 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1991), pp. 282-294, on Matt 11:28-30 and its Sirach background
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 8-20: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2001), pp. 169-178, on the comparative sense of the easy yoke
Celia Deutsch, *Lady Wisdom, Jesus, and the Sages: Metaphor and Social Context in Matthew''s Gospel* (Trinity Press International, 1996), pp. 130-156, on Jesus as personified Wisdom
M. Jack Suggs, *Wisdom, Christology, and Law in Matthew''s Gospel* (Harvard University Press, 1970), pp. 71-97, on the Wisdom Christology of the yoke saying
R. T. France, *The Gospel of Matthew* (NICNT; Eerdmans, 2007), pp. 446-454, harmonizing the easy yoke with costly discipleship
D. A. Carson, *Matthew*, in *The Expositor''s Bible Commentary*, vol. 9 (Zondervan, 2010), pp. 320-323, on internal rest versus external hardship', 0, NULL, 3, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, John, 2 Timothy, Hebrews', 5, 3, 3, 4, 0, NULL, NULL);

Luke 6:26 inverts the valuation: "Woe unto you, when all men shall speak well of you! for so did their fathers to the false prophets." This is the fourth and final woe of the Sermon on the Plain (Luke 6:24-26), and it functions as the structural mirror of the fourth beatitude in Luke 6:22-23, where the disciples are "blessed" when men hate them, exclude them, and "cast out your name as evil, for the Son of man''s sake." Luke''s blessing and woe deliberately reverse conventional valuations of reputation. The reasoning is prophetic and eschatological, not sapiential: in a corrupt order, universal approval is itself a danger sign, because it is exactly what the false prophets received. The intertext is concrete — the court prophets who flattered Ahab against the lone dissenting Micaiah ben Imlah (1 Kings 22), and the prophets denounced by Jeremiah (Jeremiah 5:31; 23:16-17) and Micah (Micah 2:11) for prophesying what the people wished to hear. Joseph Fitzmyer, François Bovon, and John Nolland all locate the woe within this prophetic-critique tradition, where popularity correlates with telling comforting lies.

Gleason Archer''s harmonization, representative of the apologetic line, distinguishes between a good name earned through genuine righteousness and the spurious approval won through flattery and compromise: the sages commend the former, Jesus condemns the latter, and so the texts do not actually collide. The distinction is real and exegetically sound — Luke''s woe is plainly aimed at sycophantic universal acclaim, not at the honest esteem that follows integrity, and on that reading the referents diverge. This is why the status here is partial rather than no harmonization. But the harmonization is something the interpreter supplies, not something the wisdom texts state. Proverbs 22:1 and Ecclesiastes 7:1 praise reputation flatly, with no clause carving out the case of unrighteous popularity, and they assume a stable social order in which a good name reliably tracks a good life. Luke''s Jesus assumes the opposite — a world so inverted that broad approval is presumptive evidence of compromise. The tension is therefore not merely nominal; it reflects a genuine shift from the wisdom tradition''s confidence in the social order to the apocalyptic-prophetic suspicion of it. Verdict: partially harmonizable on a real conceptual distinction the wisdom texts themselves never draw; the residual tension is a genuine difference of ethical outlook.', 'C. L. Seow, *Ecclesiastes* (AB 18C; Doubleday, 1997), pp. 235-240, on the valuation of a good name in Eccl 7:1
Michael V. Fox, *A Time to Tear Down and a Time to Build Up: A Rereading of Ecclesiastes* (Eerdmans, 1999), pp. 250-253, on shem tov in Qoheleth
Michael V. Fox, *Proverbs 10-31* (AB 18B; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 700-702, on the honor-shame logic of Prov 22:1
Joseph A. Fitzmyer, *The Gospel According to Luke I-IX* (AB 28; Doubleday, 1981), pp. 635-637, on the Lukan beatitudes and woes
François Bovon, *Luke 1: A Commentary on the Gospel of Luke 1:1-9:50* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2002), pp. 226-229, on the false-prophet intertext of the fourth woe
