Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 257-258, on the distinction between righteous reputation and flattery', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Proverbs, Ecclesiastes, Luke', 6, 2, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

The vocabulary is worth pausing on. Biblical Hebrew distinguishes several grades of fool — the petî (the naive or open-minded), the kesîl (the dull, complacent fool), the ʾewil (the morally obstinate fool), and the nabal (the boorish, godless fool). Michael Fox notes that the ʾewil in particular is portrayed throughout Proverbs as resistant to correction, whereas the petî is precisely the one whom instruction can still reach. On that reading the two proverbs are not even talking about the same human type: 22:15 addresses formative folly in the young, 27:22 the entrenched folly of the confirmed ʾewil. This is the strongest form of the harmonization, and it is the one Bruce Waltke develops: the rod works on a child whose character is still plastic, while the adult fool''s character has set. The argument has real merit and should not be brushed aside.

It also has a cost. Neither proverb supplies the developmental marker the harmonization needs. Proverbs 22:15 does specify a naʿar, so a youth is in view there; but 27:22 names a generic ʾewil with no age restriction, and elsewhere in the same Solomonic core the kesîl and ʾewil are themselves declared beyond reach (e.g., 17:10, where rebuke penetrates a discerning man more than a hundred blows penetrate a kesîl; 26:11; 27:22 itself). In other words, the optimism of 22:15 about the rod is in tension not only with 27:22 but with a recurring pessimism running across collections. The genre context reinforces the point. Proverbs belongs to the Near Eastern Instruction tradition — Egyptian works such as the Instruction of Amenemope (with which 22:17-24:22 has demonstrable literary contact) and the Instruction of Ptahhotep — where corporal discipline of the young is standard pedagogy, and where the maxim and the counter-maxim coexist without systematic reconciliation. As R. N. Whybray and William McKane argued, the proverb is a situational utterance: it claims to be true in its setting, not as a universal law, and an anthology can hold two situationally true sayings that point in opposite directions. The redactors who assembled Proverbs made no attempt to harmonize the malleability of character through the rod with the fixity of folly under the pestle. Verdict: partially harmonizable along developmental/lexical lines, but the harmonization is imported rather than stated, and the underlying anthology preserves two genuinely different verdicts on whether folly can be cured.', 'Michael V. Fox, *Proverbs 10-31* (AB 18B; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 704-705, 818-819, on the grades of fool and the two verdicts in 22:15 and 27:22
Bruce K. Waltke, *The Book of Proverbs, Chapters 15-31* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 2005), pp. 217-220, 393-395, on the developmental reading of 22:15 versus 27:22
R. N. Whybray, *Proverbs* (NCB; Eerdmans, 1994), pp. 322-324, 386-388, on the proverb as situational utterance
William McKane, *Proverbs: A New Approach* (OTL; Westminster, 1970), pp. 569-571, 619-621, on the distinct sentence-literature collections
Richard J. Clifford, *Proverbs: A Commentary* (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 1999), pp. 199-201, 240-241, on the rod and the incorrigible fool', 0, NULL, 5, 'OT_internal', 'Proverbs', 6, 2, 1, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

The standard harmonization, defended by Douglas Moo and D. A. Carson, is that the word is used in two different registers: James means the external trial that proves faith, Matthew the inner solicitation to sin. Grammatically this is entirely possible — peirasmos genuinely carries both senses, and a single lexeme can mean different things in different authors. But the move is exegetically thinner than it first appears, for two reasons. First, James himself does not hold the two senses apart: within the same paragraph he pivots at 1:13 to the seductive sense, insisting "let no one say when he is tempted (peirazomenos), ''I am being tempted by God,'' for God cannot be tempted by evil and he himself tempts no one" — there the agent of enticement is explicitly not God but one''s own epithumia (1:14). James thus knows and deploys both senses deliberately; the celebration in 1:2 is real, and it is celebration of trial, not of seduction. Second, Matthew 6:13 carries no qualifier restricting its peirasmos to enticement alone. The petition is general and asks not for strength to resist temptation from within a trial but for non-entry into the situation itself, which is why it raised an early difficulty: it appears to make God the one who could "lead" believers into peirasmos.

A stronger and more historically grounded harmonization reads Matthew''s peirasmos eschatologically. Joachim Jeremias and Raymond Brown argued that the petition, like the Prayer as a whole, has an eschatological horizon: ho peirasmos with reference to the great final trial or tribulation (cf. Rev 3:10, "the hour of trial that is coming on the whole world"), so that the prayer asks not to be brought into the climactic apostasy-inducing ordeal. On that reading Matthew and James are not even speaking of the same event — James of the ordinary refining trials of life, Matthew of the eschatological testing that could break faith — and the celebration/avoidance contrast dissolves. This is the most defensible reconciliation, and it is grammatically and contextually credible given the Prayer''s Q provenance and Jewish-eschatological matrix (Davies and Allison weigh it seriously).

What remains, however, is a real difference of posture toward trial in early Christianity. James embraces hardship as paideia, divine pedagogy modeled on Jewish wisdom (cf. Sir 2:1-6, "my son, if you come to serve the Lord, prepare yourself for testing"), and his community is told to rejoice precisely when they fall into it. The Lord''s Prayer, by contrast, petitions for exemption. Pope Francis''s 2017-2019 endorsement of the revised rendering "do not let us fall into temptation" (over "lead us not into temptation") is itself an admission that the plain causative reading of mē eisenenkēs is theologically uncomfortable, since it implies God as the agent who leads into peirasmos — the very thing James 1:13 denies. Verdict: partially harmonizable, most successfully on the eschatological-trial reading; but the shared vocabulary, James''s own dual usage, and the unqualified breadth of Matthew''s petition mean the two texts still register genuinely different stances toward trial.', 'Peter H. Davids, *The Epistle of James* (NIGTC; Eerdmans, 1982), pp. 67-71, on James 1:2-4 and the dokimion of pistis
Luke Timothy Johnson, *The Letter of James* (AB 37A; Doubleday, 1995), pp. 175-180, on James''s deployment of both senses of peirasmos
Dale C. Allison, *James: A Critical and Exegetical Commentary* (ICC; Bloomsbury T&T Clark, 2013), pp. 159-172, on peirasmos and the testing of faith
