Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 1-7: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2007), pp. 320-322, on the sixth petition of the Lord''s Prayer
Raymond E. Brown, "The Pater Noster as an Eschatological Prayer," *Theological Studies* 22 (1961), pp. 175-208, on peirasmos as the final eschatological trial
Joachim Jeremias, *The Prayers of Jesus* (SBT 2.6; SCM Press, 1967), pp. 104-107, on the eschatological sense of the temptation petition
Douglas J. Moo, *The Letter of James* (PNTC; Eerdmans, 2000), pp. 53-56, on distinguishing external trial from inner enticement
D. A. Carson, *Matthew* (EBC 8; Zondervan, 1984), pp. 173-175, on the sixth petition and the meaning of peirasmos', 0, NULL, 4, 'NT_internal', 'Matthew, James', 6, 2, 2, 3, 0, NULL, NULL);

This conditional model is woven through the Deuteronomistic and prophetic traditions. It is the logic Jonah exploits when he complains in 4:2 that he fled to Tarshish precisely because he knew Yahweh to be "gracious and merciful, slow to anger, abounding in steadfast love, and relenting (nacham) from disaster" — a direct citation of the divine self-revelation in Exodus 34:6-7. It is the logic of Amos 7:1-6, where the prophet''s intercession twice moves Yahweh to "relent" of announced judgment ("the LORD relented concerning this; ''It shall not be,'' said the LORD"). It is the theology of the Chronicler''s Manasseh and of the deferral granted to Hezekiah and to Ahab. Walter Kaiser''s harmonization — distinguishing unconditional prophecies (the messianic and new-covenant promises) from conditional ones (oracles of judgment against nations) — is therefore not an ad hoc dodge; it tracks a real distinction in the texts, and it lowers the temperature considerably, because 2 Peter''s bebaioteros is precisely about the promise-stream while Jeremiah 18 is precisely about the judgment-stream. To that extent the two passages are not a flat yes/no on "is prophecy sure."

What the harmonization cannot dissolve is that the Hebrew Bible disagrees with itself about whether Yahweh relents at all. Against the relenting texts stand two emphatic denials. Numbers 23:19: "God is not a man (ʾish), that he should lie, nor a son of man, that he should change his mind (nacham); has he said, and will he not do it?" And 1 Samuel 15:29: "the Glory of Israel will not lie or relent (nacham), for he is not a man, that he should relent" — a verse that sits, with some irony, in the same chapter where the narrator twice says Yahweh did relent / was sorry that he had made Saul king (15:11, 15:35), using the same verb. The canon thus preserves two prophetic anthropologies side by side: one in which divine announcement is contingent and responsive (Jer 18; Jonah; Amos 7; Exod 32:14), and one in which it is fixed and irreversible (Num 23:19; 1 Sam 15:29; and the later apocalyptic trajectory toward sealed, determined ends). 2 Peter, working within the fixed-promise stream, calls prophecy "more sure"; Jeremiah, within the conditional-oracle stream, makes it contingent on repentance. The apologists are right that the conflict is not a clean contradiction once the referents are sorted; they are wrong to imply that the biblical view of prophecy is monolithic. Verdict: partially harmonizable by distinguishing promise from threat, but the underlying disagreement over whether Yahweh relents is internal to the Hebrew Bible and not eliminable.', 'Richard J. Bauckham, *Jude, 2 Peter* (WBC 50; Word, 1983), pp. 223-228, on 2 Peter 1:19 and the ''more sure'' prophetic word
Jerome H. Neyrey, *2 Peter, Jude* (AB 37C; Doubleday, 1993), pp. 175-183, on 2 Peter 1:16-19 and the validation of prophecy
Jack R. Lundbom, *Jeremiah 1-20* (AB 21A; Doubleday, 1999), pp. 814-823, on Jeremiah 18:1-12 and conditional announcement
William L. Holladay, *Jeremiah 1* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 1986), pp. 514-518, on Jeremiah 18 and Yahweh''s relenting
Jack M. Sasson, *Jonah* (AB 24B; Doubleday, 1990), pp. 245-252, 295-300, on conditional prophecy and Yahweh''s relenting over Nineveh
Walter C. Kaiser Jr., *The Old Testament Documents: Are They Reliable & Relevant?* (IVP, 2001), pp. 145-148, on the conditional/unconditional distinction in prophecy
Abraham J. Heschel, *The Prophets* (Harper & Row, 1962), pp. 194-198, on the conditional and responsive character of prophecy', 0, NULL, 2, 'OT_vs_NT', 'Jeremiah, 2 Peter', 5, 3, 2, 2, 0, NULL, NULL);

Proverbs 30:30 sits in an entirely different literary world. It belongs to the numerical sayings of Agur (30:24-31), a catalogue of creatures noted for some admirable or striking quality — the small-but-wise (ants, badgers, locusts, lizards in 30:24-28) and then the "stately in stride" (30:29-31): the lion "mightiest among beasts, which turns back before none," the strutting rooster (or greyhound), the male goat, and the king with his army. This is observational sapiential poetry, cataloguing the lion as the paradigm of fearless bearing. It is not a polemic against Genesis and almost certainly has no awareness of the P creation theology at all; it is praising a quality, not legislating a zoological exception.

Gleason Archer''s harmonization is, in this instance, the correct reading rather than special pleading. He notes that môrāʾ in Genesis 9:2 expresses a general principle of the post-flood order — humanity''s net dominance over the animal kingdom — rather than an absolute guarantee that no individual animal will ever display aggression, and that Proverbs 30:30 is poetic observation about a particular creature''s demeanor, not a denial of human dominion. The two texts are simply not making commensurable claims. Genesis 9:2 speaks of a structural, covenantal relationship; Proverbs 30:30 of an observed behavioral trait. The totalizing language of Genesis ("every beast … every bird … every fish") is the idiom of a programmatic blessing, comparable to the equally sweeping "every moving thing" of 9:3, and biblical Hebrew regularly uses kol ("all/every") in such global-rhetorical, not exhaustively literal, ways.

It is worth adding that the broader canon itself treats animal fear of humanity as something other than a fixed empirical law. Hosea 2:18 envisions a future covenant "with the beasts of the field" in which God will "abolish the bow, the sword, and war," implying the present order is not one of settled peace; Job 5:22-23 promises the blessed man that "the beasts of the field shall be at peace with you," and Job 39 dwells at length on creatures (the wild ox, the war-horse, the eagle, Behemoth, Leviathan) precisely beyond human control — the same observational register as Agur. These texts show that the Hebrew writers held the Genesis dominion-motif and the recognition of dangerous, untamable beasts together without strain, because they understood them as different kinds of statement. The only way to manufacture a contradiction here is to press Genesis 9:2 into a wooden, exhaustive literalism that the genre never intended — a demand that the inerrantist framework, ironically, imposes more than the text does. Verdict: harmonizable; the conflict is a genre and register difference, not a substantive disagreement.', 'Claus Westermann, *Genesis 1-11: A Continental Commentary* (Fortress, 1994), pp. 461-464, on Genesis 9:1-7 and the post-flood blessing
Gordon J. Wenham, *Genesis 1-15* (WBC 1; Word, 1987), pp. 192-194, on Genesis 9:2 and the language of fear and dread
Nahum M. Sarna, *Genesis* (JPS Torah Commentary; Jewish Publication Society, 1989), pp. 60-62, on Genesis 9:2 and the post-flood order
Victor P. Hamilton, *The Book of Genesis: Chapters 1-17* (NICOT; Eerdmans, 1990), pp. 311-314, on Genesis 9:2 and the dominion mandate
Michael V. Fox, *Proverbs 10-31* (AB 18B; Yale University Press, 2009), pp. 877-880, on Agur''s numerical sayings in 30:24-31
Richard J. Clifford, *Proverbs: A Commentary* (OTL; Westminster John Knox, 1999), pp. 265-267, on the catalogue of stately creatures in Agur
Gleason L. Archer, *Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties* (Zondervan, 1982), pp. 80-81, 263-264, on the general-principle reading of Genesis 9:2', 0, NULL, 9, 'OT_internal', 'Genesis, Proverbs', 7, 2, 1, 5, 0, NULL, NULL);

The counter-stream is equally canonical and arguably more theologically careful. Exodus 7:10-12 places the Egyptian ḥarṭummim (''magicians'') on the same evidentiary footing as Aaron — they too throw down rods that become serpents — so the bare sign-event cannot adjudicate whose deity is real; only the swallowing of their rods does, and that is a contest of power, not a demonstration that signs authenticate. Deuteronomy 13:1-3 (13:2-4 in the Hebrew) raises this to legal principle: a prophet may give a sign or wonder (ʾôt or môpēt), the wonder may actually come to pass, and Israel is still commanded to reject him if he counsels apostasy — for ''the LORD your God is testing you.'' This is the decisive text. It explicitly stipulates that a genuine, fulfilled wonder does not authenticate the messenger; content trumps spectacle. Luke 11:19, embedded in the Beelzebul controversy (Q material paralleled in Matthew 12:27), has Jesus concede that ''your sons'' — Jewish exorcists outside his movement — also cast out demons, so exorcistic power is not by itself proof of his unique commission.

The standard harmonization, advanced by Norman Geisler and echoed by traditional commentators, distinguishes genuine from counterfeit miracles: Pharaoh''s magicians worked lesser, demonic, or illusory feats, and Deuteronomy 13 engages only when the message is heretical. The Deuteronomy half of this is exegetically sound and is, in fact, the key to the whole problem — the text itself makes message-content the discriminator, which is a sophisticated epistemology, not a contradiction. The Egyptian-magician half is weaker: the narrative says the ḥarṭummim acted ''in like manner with their enchantments'' (belahaṭêhem) and offers no textual cue that their serpents were tricks rather than real transformations; the differentiator the text supplies is Aaron''s rod devouring theirs, i.e., superior power, not superior authenticity. Tigay and Propp both note the polemical thrust: the contest concedes the rivals'' efficacy in order to assert Yahweh''s supremacy. So the harmonization succeeds where it follows Deuteronomy''s own logic and fails where it imports a later metaphysics of demonic counterfeits absent from the Exodus text.

Read together, the canon does not actually hold a flat ''miracles prove divine mission'' doctrine. It holds that signs are necessary but not sufficient — corroborating evidence to be weighed against the prophet''s message and fruit (Matthew 7:15-20; Deuteronomy 13). The tension is genuine only against the crude proof-text version; against the layered version both streams cohere. Verdict: partially harmonizable — the proof-texts overstate, but Deuteronomy 13 supplies the canon''s own qualifying principle, and the residual friction is real chiefly in the Exodus magicians'' parity.', 'W. D. Davies and Dale C. Allison, *A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Gospel According to Saint Matthew*, Vol. 2 (ICC; T&T Clark, 1991), pp. 241-247, on Matthew 11:2-6
Ulrich Luz, *Matthew 8-20: A Commentary* (Hermeneia; Fortress, 2001), pp. 132-137, on Matthew 11:2-6
Raymond E. Brown, *The Gospel According to John I-XII* (AB 29; Doubleday, 1966), pp. 126-130, on John 3:2 and Johannine sēmeia theology
